"use strict";(self.webpackChunk_N_E=self.webpackChunk_N_E||[]).push([[947],{5947:(e,t,a)=>{a.r(t),a.d(t,{ARTICLES:()=>r,ARTICLE_COUNT:()=>d});let o=JSON.parse('{"1":{"id":"b1-u1","book":1,"unit":1,"title":"A Month in Amalfi","level":"B2","topic":"Travel and small-town life","generatedAt":"2026-09-23","wordCount":402,"paragraphs":["Last [[august|August]] my sister and I finally [[agree|agreed]] on something. For three summers we had argued about where to go, and this time we settled it in a single evening: a small town on the Amalfi Coast, for a whole [[month]]. We booked the flights the next morning before either of us could change her mind.","We [[arrive|arrived]] in Naples late and hungry. The [[weather]] was hotter than we had expected, so we ate [[breakfast]] on a bench outside the station and watched the traffic crawl past. In the afternoon we walked down to the harbour and [[catch|caught]] the last [[boat]] of the day. Seen from the water, the cliffs looked far too steep to build on, and yet there were houses all the way to the top, stacked one above another like boxes.","The town itself was tiny. Our room had a balcony, and every morning I stood on it with a [[camera]] in one hand and a cup of coffee in the other, waiting for the light to reach the water. Our host, a woman called Rosa, [[invite|invited]] us to eat with her family on our second evening. There was a [[duck]] on the terrace that followed her everywhere, and a long table under the vines. Rosa kept no [[alcohol]] in the house at all; the [[wine]] came from her brother\'s field, and it was better than anything we had drunk at home.","We spent the first [[week]] walking. That is a [[typical]] holiday for us — no plans, no timetables, just long walks and longer meals. We [[visit|visited]] Ravello, Positano, and a village whose name I have already forgotten. On the hottest day we [[travel|travelled]] inland to see the old [[capital]] of the region, a city of narrow streets and cold fountains, where the air smelled of stone and the buses were always late.","By the end of the [[month]] I had almost stopped taking photographs. I had begun to [[enjoy]] the mornings more than the sights, and to [[love]] the ordinary noise of the place: the shutters, the scooters, the man who shouted the same three words at the same time every day. On our last evening Rosa asked whether we would come back. We said yes at once, though we both knew it would be years, and that the town would have changed by then. It always does. That is part of what we were agreeing to."],"exercises":[{"id":"b1u1-e1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"Why did the sisters decide on the Amalfi Coast?","options":["Rosa had recommended it to them","They finally agreed on a destination after years of arguing","It was the cheapest place they could find","They had been there together as children"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 1 says that after three summers of arguing they settled the question in a single evening and booked the flights the next morning."},{"id":"b1u1-e2","type":"multiple-choice","question":"How did they reach the town on the day they arrived?","options":["By bus from the station","By car along the coast road","By boat from the harbour","On foot from the next village"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"Paragraph 2 states that they walked to the harbour and caught the last boat of the day."},{"id":"b1u1-e3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What does the passage tell us about Rosa?","options":["She owned the harbour caf\xe9","She had never left the town","She invited the sisters to eat with her family","She sold wine from her own field"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"Paragraph 3 says Rosa invited them to eat with her family on their second evening. The wine came from her brother\'s field, not her own."},{"id":"b1u1-e4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What did the narrator do on the hottest day?","options":["Stayed on the balcony with a camera","Travelled inland to see the old capital","Swam from the rocks below the town","Cooked dinner with Rosa\'s family"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 4 says that on the hottest day they travelled inland to see the old capital of the region."},{"id":"b1u1-e5","type":"multiple-choice","question":"In paragraph 5, the phrase \\"the ordinary noise of the place\\" suggests that the narrator…","options":["had grown tired of the town\'s constant noise","had come to value everyday life over sightseeing","was annoyed by the man shouting every day","wanted to move there permanently"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"She had stopped taking photographs and begun to enjoy the mornings and the daily sounds, which shows a shift from sightseeing towards ordinary life."},{"id":"b1u1-e6","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The ferry was the only ___ of reaching the island.\\"","options":["boat","camera","month","breakfast"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"A boat is a vehicle that moves across water — the only option that provides a means of reaching an island."},{"id":"b1u1-e7","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"We ___ at the hotel long after midnight, exhausted.\\"","options":["visited","arrived","invited","travelled"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"To arrive is to get somewhere, which fits reaching a hotel at the end of a journey."},{"id":"b1u1-e8","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"She ___ us to stay for dinner, and we could hardly refuse.\\"","options":["agreed","enjoyed","invited","caught"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"To invite is to ask someone to come to a place or event — exactly what she did with the dinner."},{"id":"b1u1-e9","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"It was a ___ Sunday: nothing unusual happened at all.\\"","options":["typical","capital","alcohol","weather"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"If something is typical, it is normal or what usually happens, which matches a day on which nothing unusual occurs."},{"id":"b1u1-e10","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"He does not drink ___ of any kind, not even at celebrations.\\"","options":["weather","wine","alcohol","duck"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"Alcohol is the general category of drink that can make people drunk; \\"of any kind\\" points to the general term rather than one specific drink."}]},"2":{"id":"b1-u2","book":1,"unit":2,"title":"The Green Stain on Bench Four","level":"B2","topic":"School science and discovery","generatedAt":"2026-09-23","wordCount":432,"paragraphs":["The science fair was three weeks away when Mr Alder told us that each of us would be working alone. Until that morning I had imagined the whole thing as an [[adventure]], a chance to build something strange and set it going. Instead I was handed a key to the [[laboratory]] at the end of the corridor, along with a printed set of rules, and told to keep my [[project]] a [[secret]] until the day of the fair. The room smelled of dust and old glass, and for the first two days I was the only person in it.","My plan was simple: I wanted to [[create]] a liquid that changed colour when it grew cold. The first attempt went badly. I had not measured the [[chemical|chemicals]] [[carefully]], and by the second afternoon a [[terrible]] [[smell]] was drifting from my bench, one that no amount of soap would remove. It was, everyone agreed, [[worse]] than the day before. I threw the mixture away and started again, twice, and each time the smell came back.","By the end of that week I was ready to give up. Then, quite by accident, I knocked over two beakers at once. There was a [[loud]] crack, a flash of green, and a [[noise]] like a door slamming somewhere above me. I heard someone [[shout]] in the corridor, and then, unexpectedly, a [[laugh]]; Mr Alder was standing in the doorway with his arms folded, and he was not angry at all. He asked me to write down exactly what I had done, in order, before I forgot it.","What I had made was not what I had intended, but it was interesting. The liquid turned from green to black as it cooled and back again when it warmed. Mr Alder explained that reactions of this kind can be used to [[kill]] bacteria in water, or to show that food has gone bad. He liked to remind us that science is neither good nor [[evil]]; it is only powerful, and it is what people choose to do with it that matters.","On the morning of the fair I set out my jars and waited for the judges to [[approach]]. I was [[nervous]], of course, and I kept checking the lids. When the first judge leaned over the bench I nearly dropped a jar on his shoes. In the end I was given second place, and a warning not to [[scare]] the younger students by demonstrating it too close to their faces. It remains the only [[experiment]] I have ever repeated simply for the pleasure of watching it work."],"exercises":[{"id":"b1u2-e1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What is the passage mainly about?","options":["A student who makes an unexpected discovery while preparing a school project","A teacher who is disappointed with the standard of his class","A competition in which the narrator wins first prize","A laboratory that is closed after a dangerous accident"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"The passage follows the narrator\'s failed attempts, the accidental spill, and the discovery that follows. Second place, not first, is mentioned, so option 3 is wrong."},{"id":"b1u2-e2","type":"multiple-choice","question":"Why did the narrator\'s first attempt go badly?","options":["The teacher had given the wrong instructions","The chemicals were not measured carefully","The liquid was left in the cold overnight","Another student knocked over the beakers"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 2 states that the chemicals had not been measured carefully, which produced the terrible smell. The beakers were knocked over later, by the narrator."},{"id":"b1u2-e3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"How did Mr Alder react when the beakers broke?","options":["He shouted at the narrator in the corridor","He sent the narrator out of the laboratory","He laughed and asked for a written record","He told the narrator to withdraw from the fair"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"Paragraph 3 says that Mr Alder stood in the doorway, was not angry, and asked the narrator to write down exactly what had been done."},{"id":"b1u2-e4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What does the narrator suggest about science in paragraph 4?","options":["It is usually too dangerous for students to attempt","It is neither good nor evil in itself, but depends on how it is used","It can only be trusted when a trained expert is watching","It is more useful in medicine than in any other field"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Mr Alder\'s remark, reported in paragraph 4, is that science is only powerful and that what matters is what people choose to do with it."},{"id":"b1u2-e5","type":"multiple-choice","question":"As used in paragraph 5, the word \\"approach\\" most nearly means…","options":["move towards","ask a question","judge carefully","begin to leave"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"\\"Waited for the judges to approach\\" means the narrator waited for them to come closer to the bench."},{"id":"b1u2-e6","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The path was narrow and icy, so we climbed ___ and said very little.\\"","options":["carefully","loud","nervous","worse"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Carefully means with great attention to safety, which is exactly how one would climb an icy path."},{"id":"b1u2-e7","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The children were told not to ___ the younger ones with stories about the dark.\\"","options":["scare","kill","create","smell"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To scare someone is to make them feel afraid, which is what frightening stories do to small children."},{"id":"b1u2-e8","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"I could ___ burning from the kitchen long before I saw any smoke.\\"","options":["smell","shout","laugh","approach"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To smell something is to sense it with your nose, and burning is something you notice by smell."},{"id":"b1u2-e9","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"In the story the magician is not ___ at all; he is simply lonely.\\"","options":["evil","loud","terrible","worse"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Evil describes someone cruel or bad. The sentence contrasts cruelty with loneliness, so evil is the right choice."},{"id":"b1u2-e10","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Her latest ___ involves building a small robot that can fold clothes.\\"","options":["project","noise","secret","adventure"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"A project is a piece of work undertaken over time for school or a job, which fits building a robot."}]},"3":{"id":"b1-u3","book":1,"unit":3,"title":"What I Learned From Looking Up","level":"B2","topic":"Weather and learning to observe","generatedAt":"2026-09-23","wordCount":428,"paragraphs":["Until I was twelve I had never really looked at a [[cloud]]. I knew that they existed, in the way that one knows [[several]] other countries exist without being able to name them. Then our geography teacher asked us to write a [[report]] on the weather of our own region, and I realised that I could not [[describe]] a single thing about the sky above my own house.","I went to the [[library]] on a Saturday and came home with a book of [[photograph|photographs]] taken from satellites. I spread them across the floor. Seen from above, the clouds had [[shape|shapes]] I had never noticed: long ribbons, thick grey sheets, and round towers that looked as if they had been built rather than blown. The book included a [[chart]] showing which of them bring rain and which simply pass over.","It took me a week to [[understand]] what I was looking at. Clouds form when warm air rises and cools, and their shape depends on how quickly that happens. I learned to [[view]] the sky the way a sailor does, reading it for information rather than decoration. On the third evening I sat on the roof and watched a front move in from the west: first thin lines, then a wall of grey, then rain.","Not everything went well. I [[fail|failed]] my first attempt at the report completely. My [[grade]] was low, because I had copied long sentences out of the book [[instead]] of using my own words. My teacher wrote one line at the bottom of the page: \\"You have told me what other people [[suppose]]. Now tell me what you have seen.\\"","So I began again. For a month I recorded the sky every morning, [[among]] the ordinary business of school and buses, and wrote down only what I had actually observed. [[suddenly|Suddenly]], on the last day, something appeared that I could not name: a flat grey sheet with a clean hole of blue in the middle. It is a gap punched through a layer of cloud, and I have never been able to [[solve]] the puzzle of how it forms; nobody has [[ever]] given me an answer that satisfied me.","The last page of my second report was a satellite picture of the Earth, wrapped in white. Beside it I had copied a line from the book: the same process happens on any [[planet]] with air and water. From that distance the weather looks less like a local event and more like something [[alien]], a slow enormous machine that we live inside without ever noticing it."],"exercises":[{"id":"b1u3-e1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What is the passage mainly about?","options":["A student who learns to observe the weather while writing a report","A teacher who explains how satellites are built","A family who move to a different region","A sailor who teaches children to read the sky"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"The passage describes the narrator\'s first report, the failed attempt, and the month of daily observation that followed."},{"id":"b1u3-e2","type":"multiple-choice","question":"Why did the narrator\'s first report receive a low grade?","options":["It was handed in after the deadline","It contained no pictures or charts","It copied sentences from a book instead of using the narrator\'s own words","It described the wrong region of the country"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"Paragraph 4 says the grade was low because long sentences had been copied out of the book instead of being written in the narrator\'s own words."},{"id":"b1u3-e3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What did the narrator see on the last day of the month?","options":["A round tower of cloud that looked as if it had been built","A flat grey sheet with a hole of blue in the middle","A wall of grey cloud moving in from the west","A satellite passing directly overhead"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 5 describes the unexpected sight: a flat grey sheet with a clean hole of blue in the middle."},{"id":"b1u3-e4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"How does the narrator feel about the unexplained hole in the cloud?","options":["Annoyed that the teacher could not explain it","Certain that it was caused by a satellite","Still curious, and not fully satisfied by any explanation","Embarrassed at having recorded it wrongly"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"The narrator says that nobody has ever given a satisfying answer, which shows continuing curiosity rather than annoyance or certainty."},{"id":"b1u3-e5","type":"multiple-choice","question":"As used in paragraph 3, the word \\"view\\" most nearly means…","options":["look at","paint","imagine","measure"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"\\"I learned to view the sky the way a sailor does\\" means the narrator learned to look at it in that way."},{"id":"b1u3-e6","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Could you ___ the man you saw at the station to the police?\\"","options":["describe","suppose","solve","view"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To describe is to say what someone or something is like, which is what the police would need."},{"id":"b1u3-e7","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The bus was full, so we took the train ___ .\\"","options":["instead","ever","among","suddenly"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Instead means in place of something else, which is exactly the contrast being made between the train and the bus."},{"id":"b1u3-e8","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Have you ___ visited the museum on the hill?\\"","options":["ever","instead","among","several"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Ever means at any time, and it is used in questions about whether something has happened at any point."},{"id":"b1u3-e9","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"She hid the letter ___ the papers on the desk.\\"","options":["among","instead","ever","suddenly"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Among means surrounded by other things, which fits hiding a letter inside a pile of papers."},{"id":"b1u3-e10","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The teacher drew a ___ on the board to show how the numbers had changed.\\"","options":["chart","cloud","planet","photograph"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"A chart is a list or display of information, and a drawing on the board that shows changing numbers is exactly that."}]},"4":{"id":"b1-u4","book":1,"unit":4,"title":"The Bell Nobody Wanted","level":"B2","topic":"A village fable about patience","generatedAt":"2026-09-23","wordCount":420,"paragraphs":["In the [[village]] of Kestrel Bottom there lived a black dog with no name and no owner. It was not a dangerous animal. It had simply one [[habit]] that nobody could break: it barked at every cart, every stranger and every hen that crossed the lane. The noise came so [[frequently]] that the shopkeeper claimed he had not slept properly in a year, and by spring the dog had become the most discussed creature in the valley. Visitors learned to take the other road into the village, and the baker kept her door shut even on warm days.","At the spring meeting the matter was raised as an [[issue]] of public safety. Three villagers spoke. The first said that the dog should be [[punish|punished]]. The second said that it should be sent away to the coast. The third, an old woman who had once worked with horses, said that [[none]] of this was [[appropriate]], because the dog was only doing what dogs do when nobody has ever taught them anything else.","Her proposal was modest. The dog, she argued, should be [[instruct|instructed]] rather than punished, and given a bell. If people could hear it coming, they could [[avoid]] it; and if the dog learned that the bell meant food, it would follow them instead of frightening them. Several people laughed at the idea. Nevertheless, the meeting agreed to let her try. The vote was close, and at least two of the villagers were quietly hoping that she would fail.","For six weeks she worked with the animal every morning, [[patient]] and unhurried. She never [[shake|shook]] it or raised her voice. She waited, and rewarded it whenever it stayed [[calm]]. The change was slow, but it was [[positive]]. By midsummer the dog would [[stroll]] beside her through the market without so much as a growl, and the bell on its collar had become a sound that people were [[content]] to hear.","What nobody had [[expect|expected]] was the second effect. The bell rang as the dog walked the fields, and the birds learned to leave before it arrived. In this way, although it could not [[represent]] anyone and had never been formally taught to [[behave]], the dog did more for the harvest than any scarecrow. Word [[spread]] to the neighbouring farms, and within two seasons three other villages had bells of their own. [[concern|Concern]] about the dog had turned, in a single summer, into something much closer to affection. The bell itself is still there, they say, hanging above the shop door."],"exercises":[{"id":"b1u4-e1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What is the passage mainly about?","options":["How a village dealt with a noisy dog by training it instead of punishing it","Why the villagers decided to send a dog away to the coast","How a shopkeeper lost a year of sleep","Why scarecrows are no longer used on farms"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"The passage follows the meeting, the old woman\'s proposal, her training of the dog, and the results. Sending the dog away is only one rejected suggestion."},{"id":"b1u4-e2","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What did the old woman suggest?","options":["That the dog should be given away to a farmer","That the dog should be trained and given a bell","That the dog should be kept indoors at night","That the villagers should meet again in the spring"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"In paragraph 3 she argues that the dog should be instructed rather than punished, and that a bell would let people hear it coming."},{"id":"b1u4-e3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What was the unexpected second effect of the bell?","options":["The dog stopped eating and grew thin","Other villages complained about the noise","The birds left the fields before the dog arrived","The shopkeeper finally began to sleep well"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"Paragraph 5 explains that the bell warned the birds as well as the people, which helped the harvest."},{"id":"b1u4-e4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"Which statement best describes the villagers\' attitude at the end of the passage?","options":["They still regarded the dog as a danger to children","They had grown fond of the dog they had once wanted removed","They believed the old woman had wasted six weeks","They planned to send the dog away the following spring"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"The closing line says that concern had turned into something closer to affection, and other villages copied the idea."},{"id":"b1u4-e5","type":"multiple-choice","question":"As used in paragraph 2, the word \\"issue\\" most nearly means…","options":["a subject for discussion","a printed document","a small problem with a machine","a sum of money"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"\\"The matter was raised as an issue of public safety\\" means it was put forward as a subject for the meeting to discuss."},{"id":"b1u4-e6","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Wearing jeans to the ceremony was hardly ___ .\\"","options":["appropriate","patient","content","positive"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Appropriate means right or suitable for the situation, which is what formal ceremonies demand."},{"id":"b1u4-e7","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"We left the motorway early to ___ the roadworks ahead.\\"","options":["avoid","punish","instruct","spread"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To avoid something is to stay away from it, which is why the driver left the motorway."},{"id":"b1u4-e8","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Learning a language takes years, so try to be ___ with yourself.\\"","options":["patient","frequent","appropriate","calm"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Patient means not becoming angry or upset easily, which is the advice given about a slow process."},{"id":"b1u4-e9","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The school will ___ any student caught cheating in the examination.\\"","options":["punish","avoid","represent","behave"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To punish is to make someone suffer for breaking the rules, which is what a school does to a cheating student."},{"id":"b1u4-e10","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Trains run ___ along this line, sometimes only ten minutes apart.\\"","options":["frequently","patiently","positively","appropriately"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Frequently means often, and ten minutes apart is a clear example of a frequent service."}]},"5":{"id":"b1-u5","book":1,"unit":5,"title":"Fourteen Weeks to the Start Line","level":"B2","topic":"Training for a first triathlon","generatedAt":"2026-09-23","wordCount":420,"paragraphs":["January. The [[doctor]] was blunt with me. My [[weight]] had risen again, my resting [[heart]] rate was higher than it should be for a man of my [[age]], and if I changed nothing, he said, the next ten years would be [[bad]] ones. I have never been an [[active]] person. I played [[football]] badly as a boy and gave up [[golf]] after a single summer, so the idea of entering a race of any kind had never once entered my head. I am forty-six, and until that morning I had not run anywhere for twenty years.","February. I [[choose|chose]] the smallest possible target: a sprint triathlon, which is short enough that an ordinary [[adult]] can finish it. It means swimming, then cycling, then running, in that order. I bought a second-hand [[bike]] for the price of a good dinner and began to train [[during]] my lunch hour, which is the only free time I have. The first session lasted nine minutes and left me unable to climb the stairs.","March. What I had not expected was the problem of [[balance]]. Three sports demand three kinds of fitness, and there are only seven days in a week. Running is simple enough; swimming is not, and after twenty lengths my arms stop working altogether. My coach tells me that [[plenty]] of beginners [[increase]] their distance far too quickly and then lose confidence. She is [[often]] right about this, and I have learned to write down what she says before I forget it. On the bad days I remind myself that she has coached hundreds of people like me, and that most of them finish.","April. A [[kilometer]] no longer feels endless. I have begun to enjoy the [[fun]] of small improvements and to treat the whole business as a [[game]] rather than a punishment. On Sunday I cycled forty kilometres and then walked the last two, because my legs had nothing left in them. My times are still slow, but they are mine, and they are getting better.","June, race week. I finished in two hours and eleven minutes, in the last third of the field, and nobody watching would have called it impressive. But at the finish line I understood something about my own [[life]] that the doctor\'s warning had not told me. The body responds to almost anything, provided that you ask it consistently, and give it time. Two hours and eleven minutes is not a record. It is, however, the first thing I have ever trained for and finished."],"exercises":[{"id":"b1u5-e1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What is the passage mainly about?","options":["A man who trains for his first triathlon after a warning from his doctor","A professional athlete who wins a national race","A doctor who advises patients to take up golf","A coach who trains beginners to swim"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"The diary records the narrator\'s decision, his training between January and June, and his completion of a sprint triathlon."},{"id":"b1u5-e2","type":"multiple-choice","question":"Why did the narrator decide to enter a race?","options":["He had always wanted to compete as a boy","His doctor warned him that his health would get worse","A friend had entered the same event","He had been given a bike as a present"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 1 reports the doctor\'s warning about weight, heart rate and age, and says the idea of racing had never entered his head before."},{"id":"b1u5-e3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What did the narrator find most difficult in March?","options":["Finding a free hour in which to train","Persuading his coach to help him","Balancing three sports in a single week","Buying equipment he could afford"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"Paragraph 3 begins by naming the problem of balance: three sports demand three kinds of fitness and there are only seven days in a week."},{"id":"b1u5-e4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"How does the narrator regard his finishing time?","options":["As a disappointment that proves he started too late","As proof that he should have chosen a longer race","As unremarkable to onlookers, but personally valuable","As evidence that his coach\'s advice was wrong"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"He admits nobody would have called the result impressive, yet says he learned something important about his own life at the finish line."},{"id":"b1u5-e5","type":"multiple-choice","question":"As used in paragraph 2, the word \\"during\\" most nearly means…","options":["throughout the whole of","at some point within","just before","instead of"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"\\"I began to train during my lunch hour\\" means he trained within that period, the only free time he had."},{"id":"b1u5-e6","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The price of fuel has ___ twice since the spring.\\"","options":["increased","chosen","balanced","kept"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To increase is to make something larger, and a price that rises twice has increased."},{"id":"b1u5-e7","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"There is ___ of time before the train leaves, so let us have coffee.\\"","options":["plenty","weight","balance","age"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Plenty of something means there is a lot of it, which is why having coffee is possible."},{"id":"b1u5-e8","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"You may ___ either the soup or the salad, but not both.\\"","options":["choose","increase","balance","attend"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To choose is to pick one thing rather than another, which is what the sentence offers."},{"id":"b1u5-e9","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"She cycles to work ___ , even in winter.\\"","options":["often","badly","actively","plenty"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Often means many times or regularly, which is what \\"even in winter\\" suggests about her habit."},{"id":"b1u5-e10","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The nearest village is about a ___ from here, so we can easily walk.\\"","options":["kilometer","weight","heart","game"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"A kilometre is a unit of distance equal to a thousand metres, and it is the only option that measures distance."}]},"6":{"id":"b1-u6","book":1,"unit":6,"title":"Two Ways of Listening","level":"B2","topic":"A profile of twin cousins","generatedAt":"2026-09-23","wordCount":424,"paragraphs":["My cousins were born eleven minutes [[apart]], and for the first twenty years of their lives they were treated as a single person. It is a common mistake. Because they share a face, people assume that they share everything else, and that assumption made both of them [[tense]] whenever a stranger asked which one was which. It took them years to convince people that they were not two halves of one person.","In fact they are [[totally]] different. Mira is quick, impatient and [[fashionable]]; she can [[dash]] across a room and be out of the door before anyone has finished a sentence. Her brother Tam is slower and more [[natural]] in his manner, and he dislikes being rushed. The only real [[attribute]] they share is a habit of standing in front of the [[mirror]] in the hall, arguing about whose turn it is. Mira once crossed the city in twenty minutes to return a library book on the last day it was due.","Language divided them early. Their mother is from one country and their father from another, so both children grew up [[bilingual]]. Mira treats the two languages as one mixed system and switches between them in the middle of a sentence; Tam keeps them strictly separate. His [[spoken]] English is careful and rather formal, and he says that his other language now feels [[foreign]] to him, although he has used it since childhood.","[[nowadays|Nowadays]] they live in different cities, and the [[gulf]] between their lives is wider than either of them expected. Mira competes seriously in a [[sport]] that takes her abroad most months, while Tam is a [[participant]] in a local choir, which requires him to be in the same place at the same time every week. When the two of them meet, they fall back into an old [[ritual]]: a long argument about nothing, followed by complete agreement. Neither is [[surprised]] by this any longer.","What interests me is that other people still cannot see it. At a family wedding last summer I watched them across a crowded room and realised how [[completely]] the pair had drifted. They disagree about music, clothes and how to spend a Sunday, and yet I have never once seen either of them show [[disgust]] at the other\'s choices. When Mira describes her plans she is deliberately [[vague]]; Tam gives exact times and places. Both of them are, in their own way, entirely clear about who they are. It is a way of being close without being identical, and neither of them would exchange it."],"exercises":[{"id":"b1u6-e1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What is the passage mainly about?","options":["Two people who look alike but differ in almost every other way","A family that moved between two countries","The difficulties of learning a second language","A wedding at which a family argument took place"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"The passage describes the twins\' shared appearance and then sets out their differences in manner, language, work and habits."},{"id":"b1u6-e2","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What do the twins regularly argue about in the hall?","options":["Which of them should learn another language","Whose turn it is to use the mirror","Which sport is more worth watching","How much time they should spend abroad"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 2 says the only attribute they genuinely share is a habit of standing in front of the mirror, arguing about whose turn it is."},{"id":"b1u6-e3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"How do the twins differ in their use of language?","options":["Mira mixes her two languages, while Tam keeps them separate","Mira speaks only one language, while Tam speaks two","Tam refuses to speak the language of his mother","Both of them use only formal written English"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Paragraph 3 explains that Mira switches between languages mid-sentence, whereas Tam keeps them strictly separate and is careful and formal."},{"id":"b1u6-e4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What is the writer\'s attitude towards the two cousins?","options":["Critical of the way they treat each other","Puzzled that they still speak to one another","Affectionate and closely observant","Indifferent, since they now live far apart"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"The writer notices small details and praises the absence of disgust at each other\'s choices, which shows warmth rather than criticism."},{"id":"b1u6-e5","type":"multiple-choice","question":"As used in paragraph 4, the word \\"gulf\\" most nearly means…","options":["a large area of water","a wide difference","a shared memory","a long journey"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"The word describes the widening gap between the two lives, so it means a wide difference rather than a physical stretch of water."},{"id":"b1u6-e6","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"His directions were so ___ that we had no idea which road to take.\\"","options":["vague","fashionable","natural","spoken"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"If something is vague it is not clear and gives very few details, which explains why the road could not be chosen."},{"id":"b1u6-e7","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The smell from the bins filled her with ___ .\\"","options":["disgust","ritual","attribute","mirror"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Disgust is a feeling of distaste caused by something unpleasant, and rotting rubbish is exactly that."},{"id":"b1u6-e8","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"He grew up in a ___ household, so he switched between two languages without thinking.\\"","options":["bilingual","fashionable","tense","foreign"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"A bilingual person can speak two languages, which is why switching between them came naturally to him."},{"id":"b1u6-e9","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"It is not considered ___ to wear a hat indoors in this country.\\"","options":["fashionable","natural","vague","apart"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Fashionable describes what people like to wear now, and the sentence is about whether a hat is accepted by current custom."},{"id":"b1u6-e10","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"She looked ___ before the interview, twisting her hands together.\\"","options":["tense","content","natural","vague"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"When someone is tense they are uncomfortable and unhappy, which fits the twisting of hands before an interview."}]},"7":{"id":"b1-u7","book":1,"unit":7,"title":"The Test of the Three Brothers","level":"B2","topic":"A fable about choosing a ruler","generatedAt":"2026-09-23","wordCount":420,"paragraphs":["When the old king died, his three sons each [[claim|claimed]] the throne, and no law existed that could settle the question. The council, [[therefore]], had to invent a test. Their decision was [[announce|announced]] throughout the city: whichever brother could show that he had [[protect|protected]] the people best would rule. It was the first time in the kingdom\'s history that a [[prince]] had been chosen by any means other than birth.","The eldest was a [[famous]] soldier. He had won battles and could [[lay]] a bridge across a river in a single day. The second was a scholar, an [[expert]] in law and trade, who could [[divide]] a disputed field into exact halves and prove that each part was equal. The third was the youngest, and he had done nothing that anyone could point to. Each of them believed, quite sincerely, that he was the obvious choice.","Each was given a month. The soldier returned with a treaty, signed after a long campaign, that promised [[peace]] on the northern border. He said that only [[force]] had made it possible, and that a ruler without an army could not protect anyone. His argument was strong, and many people were satisfied with it.","The scholar returned with a new set of rules for the market. Prices were fixed, weights were checked, and the [[condition]] of the roads had improved. He said that the [[difference]] between a good kingdom and a poor one was administration, and that he could [[contribute]] more than his brother, because his work would last longer. The council noted that the roads had been repaired without any increase in taxes.","The youngest returned with nothing at all. He had spent the month walking through the villages and had made one small change: he had ordered the grain stores to be opened in winter and had paid for it out of his own allowance. When the council asked what he had to say, he answered that he could not [[sense]] what the people needed from a palace, and that he had [[allow|allowed]] himself to be told. There was no [[sudden]] change in his manner as he spoke.","The council chose the youngest, and the city was surprised. The reasoning, once it was explained, was simple. A ruler who seeks power may cause [[harm]]; a ruler who is willing to stand [[beside]] his people and accept the [[challenge]] of listening will do less damage. The two older brothers were given posts that suited them, and the kingdom was quiet for a generation."],"exercises":[{"id":"b1u7-e1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What is the passage mainly about?","options":["How three brothers were judged before one of them was chosen to rule","How a soldier won a war on the northern border","How a scholar rewrote the laws of a kingdom","Why the grain stores were closed in winter"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"The passage sets out the test invented by the council, what each brother did in his month, and why the youngest was chosen."},{"id":"b1u7-e2","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What did the youngest brother do during his month?","options":["He signed a treaty with a neighbouring kingdom","He wrote a new set of rules for the market","He arranged for grain to be given out in winter","He trained a small army of his own"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"Paragraph 5 says he ordered the grain stores to be opened in winter and paid for it from his own allowance."},{"id":"b1u7-e3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What was the scholar\'s main argument?","options":["That only soldiers could keep a kingdom safe","That good administration would last longer than any victory","That the roads should be left to the villages","That the market should be closed to outsiders"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"In paragraph 4 he argues that the difference between a good kingdom and a poor one is administration, and that his work would last longer."},{"id":"b1u7-e4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"Why did the council finally choose the youngest brother?","options":["Because he was the eldest son\'s favourite","Because he had spent the most money","Because he was willing to listen to the people","Because he had won the most battles"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"The closing paragraph explains that a ruler willing to stand beside his people and accept the challenge of listening would do less damage."},{"id":"b1u7-e5","type":"multiple-choice","question":"As used in paragraph 1, the word \\"claim\\" most nearly means…","options":["assert a right to something","demand payment","take by accident","describe in detail"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"\\"Each claimed the throne\\" means each brother said the position was rightfully his."},{"id":"b1u7-e6","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The museum does not ___ visitors to touch the paintings.\\"","options":["allow","announce","protect","contribute"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To allow something to happen is to let it happen, which is what the museum refuses to do."},{"id":"b1u7-e7","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The lamp stood ___ the bed, just within reach.\\"","options":["beside","among","instead","therefore"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Beside means next to, which is where a bedside lamp stands."},{"id":"b1u7-e8","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Smoking does far more ___ to the lungs than most people realise.\\"","options":["harm","force","peace","sense"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Harm is injury or damage, which is what smoking causes to the lungs."},{"id":"b1u7-e9","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Everyone in the team was asked to ___ ideas for the new design.\\"","options":["contribute","divide","claim","lay"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To contribute is to give something that helps a shared effort, which is what the team members were asked to do."},{"id":"b1u7-e10","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"She is a leading ___ on the birds of the northern coast.\\"","options":["expert","challenge","condition","difference"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"An expert is someone who is very good at or deeply knowledgeable about something, which fits a leading authority on birds."}]},"8":{"id":"b1-u8","book":1,"unit":8,"title":"The Two Lamps","level":"B2","topic":"A folk tale about day and night","generatedAt":"2026-09-23","wordCount":426,"paragraphs":["Long ago, the people say, there was neither sun nor moon. The whole world was lit by a [[single]] lamp, [[hang|hung]] in the middle of the sky, and it was [[necessary]] to keep it burning, because without it nothing could be seen and nothing could grow. Water had to be carried up to it, and the wick had to be trimmed before it smoked. The people below arranged their days around it: they worked while it burned and slept when it dimmed.","The task of guarding it was given to two sisters. One was told to [[attend]] the lamp in the morning, the other at night. The [[pattern]] was simple, and for many years they kept to it. Each was [[satisfied]] with her half of the day, and neither was jealous of the other, although the night sister had far less company. The elder liked the cool mornings; the younger preferred the long silence of the dark.","Then came a winter when the wind would not stop. The lamp began to fail. The elder sister tried to [[grab]] the flame with her bare hands, and it [[tear|tore]] into two pieces, one [[huge]] and one small. She cried out, and the larger piece rose into the sky and stayed there, burning steadily. That, they say, is the sun. The wind took the smaller piece east, over the hills, and it was a long time before anyone saw it again.","Her sister ran after it, and in her hurry she stumbled. It is said that she tried to [[chase]] the light for a whole night and never caught it. When morning came she was still running, and the smaller flame had begun to [[release]] its light more gently, so that the fields were silver rather than gold. That is the moon, and this is why it follows the sun across the sky. Children are still told to look for the moon in the evening and to notice how far it has travelled by morning.","The village elders [[propose|proposed]] other explanations, and there are many versions. One says that the sisters had quarrelled; another says that they were [[encourage|encouraged]] to [[arrange]] the sky in this way on [[purpose]]. The [[contrast]] between the two lights, the old people insist, was never an accident. Whichever [[theory]] you [[accept]], the story makes one point. Night and day [[require]] each other, and the two lamps are [[familiar]] to everyone who has ever looked up. It is, in the end, a story about sharing, told by people who had only one lamp between them."],"exercises":[{"id":"b1u8-e1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What is the passage mainly about?","options":["A folk tale that explains how the sun and the moon came to be","An account of how lamps were made in ancient times","The story of two sisters who argued about a field","A description of the northern winter winds"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"The passage tells the story of a single lamp that was torn in two, producing the sun and the moon."},{"id":"b1u8-e2","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What were the two sisters asked to do?","options":["Carry water to the villages each morning","Look after the lamp, one in the morning and one at night","Trim the wick of every lamp in the village","Find a new place for the lamp in the sky"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 2 states that one sister was told to attend the lamp in the morning and the other at night."},{"id":"b1u8-e3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What happened to the lamp during the windy winter?","options":["It was blown out completely","It was carried away by the night sister","It was torn into two pieces","It was hidden behind the mountains"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"Paragraph 3 describes the elder sister grabbing the flame, which tore it into a huge piece and a small one."},{"id":"b1u8-e4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What do the different versions told by the elders have in common?","options":["They all agree that the sisters were punished","They all treat the contrast between the lights as intentional","They all say that the moon was made first","They all deny that the lamp ever existed"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 5 says the old people insist the contrast between the two lights was never an accident, whatever version is told."},{"id":"b1u8-e5","type":"multiple-choice","question":"As used in paragraph 5, the word \\"contrast\\" most nearly means…","options":["a sharp difference","a repeated sound","a slow movement","a written record"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"The contrast is the sharp difference between the strong light of the sun and the gentler light of the moon."},{"id":"b1u8-e6","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"It is ___ to book a table if you want to eat there on Friday.\\"","options":["necessary","familiar","satisfied","single"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"If something is necessary, you must do it, which is the case with booking a popular restaurant on a Friday."},{"id":"b1u8-e7","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The dog began to ___ the postman down the lane.\\"","options":["chase","release","arrange","attend"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To chase is to run after someone or something, which is what the dog does to the postman."},{"id":"b1u8-e8","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"She did not ___ the bird until she had checked that it was unhurt.\\"","options":["release","grab","require","propose"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To release something is to stop holding it, which is what happens after the bird is checked."},{"id":"b1u8-e9","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"He did not break the window by accident; he did it on ___ .\\"","options":["purpose","theory","pattern","contrast"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Doing something on purpose means doing it deliberately, which is the opposite of doing it by accident."},{"id":"b1u8-e10","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Her voice sounded ___ to me, though I could not remember where we had met.\\"","options":["familiar","necessary","huge","satisfied"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Something familiar is something you know well, which explains the feeling of recognition without a clear memory."}]},"9":{"id":"b1-u9","book":1,"unit":9,"title":"Working With Four Legs","level":"B2","topic":"How assistance animals are trained","generatedAt":"2026-09-23","wordCount":430,"paragraphs":["A guide [[dog]] is not simply a pet with a job. From the moment it leaves the training centre it becomes the eyes of a person who cannot [[see]], and the relationship between them is closer than most [[friend|friendships]]. The handler must [[depend]] on the animal completely, and the animal on the handler.","The work begins young. Puppies are placed with volunteer families for a year, where they are taught basic manners and taken onto buses, into shops and through crowded stations. This early exposure matters, because a working animal must stay calm in places where an ordinary pet would panic. Not every puppy qualifies, and those that do not are found homes as ordinary pets. Volunteers are chosen with care, because a puppy that is nervous at eight months rarely becomes confident at eighteen.","Formal training takes several months. A trainer will [[command]] the dog to stop at a kerb, to find a [[door]], to [[pull]] its handler away from danger. Some tasks are more specific: a dog may be trained to [[open]] a fridge, to fetch a telephone, or to warn its owner before a [[medical]] emergency begins. The animal is never asked to think for itself in a crisis; it is asked to follow a routine that has been practised a thousand times. Consistency matters more than praise: a rule that applies on Monday must also apply on Friday. Some handlers say the animal notices a change in their breathing before they do.","Dogs are not the only [[animal|animals]] used in this way. Miniature [[horse|horses]] can guide a handler who is blind, and they live longer than most dogs. Some people who cannot [[hear]] work with a trained [[cat]], which will touch its owner\'s hand when the doorbell rings. [[rabbit|Rabbits]], however, are rarely used, despite their gentle nature, because a rabbit is easily frightened and cannot be relied upon in a busy street. The choice of animal depends on the work required, not simply on which species is most appealing.","The [[service]] these animals provide is practical, but the [[help]] they give goes further. A person who has spent months in [[hospital]], or who has lost a [[leg]] in an accident, may find that the hardest thing to recover is confidence. A dog that walks beside you onto a [[bus]] and lies quietly under the seat gives that back. At [[school]], children with assistance animals are asked the same question by every visitor, which is why their handlers learn to answer it briefly and move on. The animal, after all, has no idea that it is working."],"exercises":[{"id":"b1u9-e1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What is the passage mainly about?","options":["How assistance animals are trained and what they provide for their owners","Why rabbits make better pets than dogs","The cost of running a guide-dog training centre","How to teach a puppy basic manners at home"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"The passage covers early socialisation, formal training, the range of animals used, and the wider benefits for the handler."},{"id":"b1u9-e2","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What happens to puppies that do not qualify as working animals?","options":["They are returned to the training centre for a second year","They are found homes as ordinary pets","They are trained for a different kind of work","They are kept by the volunteer families free of charge"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 2 says that not every puppy qualifies, and that those which do not are found homes as ordinary pets."},{"id":"b1u9-e3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"Why are rabbits rarely used as assistance animals?","options":["They are too small to be seen on a bus","They cannot be trained to follow any routine","They are easily frightened in busy places","They live for a shorter time than dogs"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"Paragraph 4 explains that a rabbit is easily frightened and cannot be relied upon in a busy street."},{"id":"b1u9-e4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What does the final paragraph suggest about assistance animals?","options":["They are mainly useful for people who have lost a leg","They provide confidence as well as practical help","They are only allowed in hospitals and schools","They cause extra work for their handlers"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"The paragraph says the practical help is only part of it, and that the animal gives back the confidence a person may have lost."},{"id":"b1u9-e5","type":"multiple-choice","question":"As used in paragraph 3, the word \\"command\\" most nearly means…","options":["an instruction","a loud noise","a reward","a kind of collar"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"A command is an order given to a person or animal, so here it means an instruction the trainer gives the dog."},{"id":"b1u9-e6","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Many island communities ___ on the fishing boats that visit each week.\\"","options":["depend","pull","hear","open"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To depend on something is to need it for support, which is the situation of an island community without other supplies."},{"id":"b1u9-e7","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The horse was too weak to ___ the cart up the hill.\\"","options":["pull","command","depend","see"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To pull is to hold something and move it towards you, which is what a horse does with a cart."},{"id":"b1u9-e8","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"She was given ___ treatment at the scene before the ambulance arrived.\\"","options":["medical","service","open","friend"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Medical means related to the treatment of injury or disease, which is exactly the treatment given at the scene of an accident."},{"id":"b1u9-e9","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The hotel offers a free bus ___ to and from the airport.\\"","options":["service","command","leg","animal"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Service is the act of helping or serving someone, and a shuttle to the airport is precisely that kind of help."},{"id":"b1u9-e10","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Speak more clearly, please; I can hardly ___ you.\\"","options":["hear","see","open","pull"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To hear is to be aware of sound through your ears, which is what the speaker is struggling to do."}]},"10":{"id":"b1-u10","book":1,"unit":10,"title":"The Tail and the Trouble It Causes","level":"B2","topic":"How the peacock\'s display evolved","generatedAt":"2026-09-23","wordCount":422,"paragraphs":["Standing in front of a peacock with its [[tail]] fully open, it is hard to believe that the bird is in any kind of [[trouble]]. The fan is more than a metre across, and every feather carries an [[image]] that looks like an eye. Yet the display is also a risk. A bird that cannot fly well and is bright enough to be seen from a great distance is, in [[certain]] places, an easy target.","The [[primary]] [[function]] of the tail, however, is not defence but courtship. On a flat piece of ground known as a lek [[site]], males gather and display, and females visit, inspect and choose. The [[immediate]] [[effect]] of this arrangement is that only a few males do most of the mating, while the [[rest]] wait for another season. Females may visit several sites before they decide, and they rarely return to a male they have already rejected.","Why should a female prefer a male with a larger tail? One explanation is that the tail is an honest signal of health. A bird that carries a heavy, awkward fan and still manages to find food and escape predators must be strong. The [[benefit]] to the female is practical: her chicks are more likely to survive if their father was genuinely healthy. Females, it seems, are reading a signal that is difficult to fake.","Not every feature of the display is [[essential]], however. The feathers can be damaged, and a male will [[remain]] in the open even when the [[chance]] of being caught is high. The tail also costs a great deal of energy to grow, and it must be [[guard|guarded]] from rivals, who will bite at it whenever the owner is looking elsewhere. A torn feather cannot be repaired; it must be carried until the next moult, which may be a year away.","A peacock standing on open [[grass]], with nothing to hide behind, seems an unlikely survivor. Yet the species has done well, partly because it has learned to live close to people. From [[far]] away the bird looks [[proud]] and entirely in control; up close, the tail is a complicated machine that requires constant attention. The [[separate]] strands must be combed, dried and eventually replaced. It is worth remembering that the bird cannot [[focus]] on food or danger while it is displaying, and that this is precisely the point. What looks like vanity is, in fact, a calculated risk. The display is, in the end, a gamble that has paid off often enough to be worth repeating."],"exercises":[{"id":"b1u10-e1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What is the passage mainly about?","options":["Why the peacock\'s tail evolved and what it costs the bird","How peacocks are kept in gardens as ornamental birds","The different colours found in a peacock\'s feathers","Why peacocks are unable to fly at all"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"The passage explains the courtship function of the tail, the theory behind female preference, and the risks and costs of the display."},{"id":"b1u10-e2","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What is the primary function of the peacock\'s tail?","options":["To frighten predators away","To attract a mate","To help the bird balance while running","To keep the bird warm in winter"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 2 states that the primary function of the tail is not defence but courtship."},{"id":"b1u10-e3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What is the immediate effect of the way peacocks gather to display?","options":["Females avoid the area entirely","Only a few males do most of the mating","All the males mate equally often","The birds move to a new site each year"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 2 says the immediate effect of the arrangement is that a few males do most of the mating while the rest wait."},{"id":"b1u10-e4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What does the writer suggest about the peacock\'s display?","options":["It is a costly risk that nevertheless brings a real advantage","It is a harmless feature with no real purpose","It developed mainly to protect the bird from rivals","It is gradually disappearing from the species"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"The writer stresses both the danger and the energy the tail costs, and explains the benefit it brings in courtship."},{"id":"b1u10-e5","type":"multiple-choice","question":"As used in paragraph 2, the word \\"function\\" most nearly means…","options":["the purpose that something serves","a formal social ceremony","a single feather of a bird","a fixed period of time"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"The sentence contrasts the function of the tail with defence, so function here means the purpose the tail serves."},{"id":"b1u10-e6","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Water is ___ for life, so the expedition carried a large supply.\\"","options":["essential","proud","certain","separate"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"If something is essential it is very important and necessary, which explains why so much was carried."},{"id":"b1u10-e7","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Please ___ in your seats until the aircraft has stopped moving.\\"","options":["remain","guard","focus","rest"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To remain somewhere is to stay there, which is what passengers are asked to do."},{"id":"b1u10-e8","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"There is a small ___ that it will snow tomorrow, but it is unlikely.\\"","options":["chance","benefit","effect","function"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"A chance is a possibility that something may happen, and the sentence describes a small possibility."},{"id":"b1u10-e9","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Two soldiers were left to ___ the entrance through the night.\\"","options":["guard","remain","rest","focus"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To guard something is to take care of it and keep it safe, which is what the soldiers were doing at the entrance."},{"id":"b1u10-e10","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"It is hard to ___ on reading when the room is so noisy.\\"","options":["focus","remain","guard","rest"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To focus on something is to give it your full attention, which noise makes difficult."}]},"11":{"id":"b1-u11","book":1,"unit":11,"title":"The Street That Went Rubbish-Free","level":"B2","topic":"Environment and community habits","generatedAt":"2026-09-23","wordCount":415,"paragraphs":["In the spring of 2019, one narrow [[street]] in the north of the [[city]] began an experiment that would [[eventually]] be copied across the whole region. The residents had a single [[problem]]: their bins were overflowing, and nobody could agree on what to do about it. Then a retired teacher who lived at number 14 suggested something unusual. Why not try, for three months, to throw almost nothing away?","She had spent years studying the [[history]] of waste in her [[country]], and she started with a [[fact]] that most [[people]] preferred to ignore: nearly half of what a household throws out could be used again. Her neighbors were doubtful. They had heard promises before, and they were not yet [[aware]] of how much their own habits were costing them. One of them, a mechanic in his sixties, said he would join only if the boxes were collected on a day when he could watch them go. Even so, the street agreed to the trial.","The rules were simple. Every kitchen received four boxes. Into the first went paper and card, into the second [[glass]] and metal, into the third anything made of [[plastic]], and into the fourth food waste. Once a week a small [[electric]] van collected them. Its [[battery]] lasted only forty kilometres, which was enough. Anything that could not be sorted had to be explained to the group at the monthly meeting, and nobody wanted that.","At first the street looked worse than before. Piles of [[black]] bags sat outside the doors while families argued about labels. Then something shifted. The teacher began to [[advertise]] the scheme at the market, and visitors came to see it for themselves. Local shops started to [[develop]] their own versions of it, and the council, which had ignored three earlier requests, sent an official to take notes. By the end of the year, the amount of rubbish leaving the street had fallen by two thirds.","What surprised the residents most was how quickly the new habits became invisible. Children who had grown up surrounded by packaging now rinsed jars without being asked. The teacher, who had [[never]] expected so much, said the real lesson was not about bins at all. It was about [[nature]] — and about the discovery that a community can [[clean]] its own street without waiting for permission from anyone. \\"I used to [[think]] that the planet was somebody else\'s responsibility,\\" one neighbor admitted. \\"Now I see that it begins at my own gate.\\""],"exercises":[{"id":"b1u11-e1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What is the passage mainly about?","options":["A council plan to build a new recycling factory","One street\'s three-month experiment in reducing waste","A retired teacher\'s career in local government","The history of rubbish collection in one country"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"The passage follows a single street from its overflowing bins to the lasting change in its habits, which is the thread that holds every paragraph together."},{"id":"b1u11-e2","type":"multiple-choice","question":"How often were the four boxes collected?","options":["Every day","Twice a week","Once a week","Once a month"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"Paragraph 3 states that a small electric van collected the boxes once a week."},{"id":"b1u11-e3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"Why did the street look worse than before at the beginning of the trial?","options":["The vans often failed to arrive","Families left piles of bags outside while they learned to sort","The council removed the old bins too early","The teacher had not explained the rules to anyone"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 4 says that piles of black bags sat outside the doors while families argued about labels."},{"id":"b1u11-e4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What had happened by the end of the year?","options":["The street had stopped producing rubbish altogether","The scheme had been cancelled after complaints","The amount of rubbish leaving the street had fallen by two thirds","Every shop in the region had joined the scheme"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"Paragraph 4 ends by reporting that the rubbish leaving the street had fallen by two thirds."},{"id":"b1u11-e5","type":"multiple-choice","question":"As used in paragraph 5, the word \\"clean\\" most nearly means…","options":["make tidy or free of rubbish","wash with soap and water","correct someone\'s behaviour","become completely empty"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"In the phrase \\"a community can clean its own street\\", the verb refers to removing rubbish and making the place tidy, not to washing with soap."},{"id":"b1u11-e6","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The council paid to ___ the new recycling service on local radio.\\"","options":["advertise","clean","develop","think"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To advertise is to draw public attention to a product or service through a medium such as radio."},{"id":"b1u11-e7","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Most shoppers are not ___ of how much packaging their food contains.\\"","options":["aware","clean","electric","glass"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"If you are aware of something, you know that it exists; the sentence says shoppers do not realise how much packaging there is."},{"id":"b1u11-e8","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"My phone needs a new ___ because it dies after two hours.\\"","options":["battery","glass","street","fact"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"A battery is the object inside a device that supplies it with electricity."},{"id":"b1u11-e9","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"It is a well-known ___ that the city produces more rubbish in December than in any other month.\\"","options":["problem","fact","nature","history"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"A fact is a piece of information that is true, and \\"a well-known fact\\" is a common collocation."},{"id":"b1u11-e10","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The village slowly ___ into a busy town over thirty years.\\"","options":["developed","advertised","cleaned","thought"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To develop is to grow larger or more advanced, which describes a village turning into a town."}]},"12":{"id":"b1-u12","book":1,"unit":12,"title":"The Studio on the Third Floor","level":"B2","topic":"Art and the people around it","generatedAt":"2026-09-23","wordCount":433,"paragraphs":["Nobody in the building knew what Mira did all day. She was a [[thin]] woman in her fifties who lived [[alone]] in a small [[apartment]] on the third floor, and the only sign of her work was the noise that came down the [[stair|stairs]] each evening — a slow, patient scraping, like someone filing metal. Her [[neighbor|neighbors]] assumed she was quietly repairing furniture.","They were wrong. Mira was an [[artist]], and for eleven years she had been making sculptures out of discarded [[material|materials]]: bent bicycle frames, broken tiles, sheets of rusted steel. A local [[magazine]] had once sent a journalist to interview her, and the resulting [[article]] described her studio as \\"a cathedral of useful rubbish\\". Mira disliked the phrase, but she kept the cutting pinned above her desk. It was, she admitted, the most accurate thing anyone had ever written about her work.","Her [[method]] is stubbornly simple. She collects, she sorts, and then she waits. \\"I never [[judge]] a piece of metal on the day I find it,\\" she says. \\"I give it a month. If it still interests me, it belongs to something.\\" Visitors often [[compare]] her finished works to the figures of dancers, though she insists that they are only shapes. What she cares about is the [[quality]] of a line — whether the eye is led somewhere and then released.","The apartment is barely big enough for a table and a bed. A narrow [[space]] between the window and the wall serves as her workshop, and she eats most of her [[meal|meals]] standing up, with a chisel in her free hand. She has never made a [[profit]] from a sale large enough to move. She is not a [[professional]] in any sense that a bank would recognise, and she finds the question boring. \\"You are asking me to be sensible,\\" she told the journalist. \\"I am busy being accurate.\\" She keeps her tools in an old biscuit tin and sharpens them herself on Sunday afternoons.","Yet the work has changed the people around her. A neighbor who once complained about the scraping now brings her offcuts from a factory. Children on the stairs have started leaving small objects outside her door — a bottle cap, a bent spoon — as if making an offering. Mira calls this the true [[symbol]] of what she does. [[beauty|Beauty]], she says, is not something an artist adds to the world; it is something already lying in it, waiting for an [[attitude]] that is willing to look twice. The building, which was once a factory, has slowly become something else because of her."],"exercises":[{"id":"b1u12-e1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What is the passage mainly about?","options":["A magazine\'s investigation into a local factory","An artist who builds sculptures from discarded materials","A dispute between neighbours about noise","The training of professional sculptors"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Every paragraph describes Mira\'s working life and the effect her sculptures have on the building she lives in."},{"id":"b1u12-e2","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What did Mira\'s neighbors assume she was doing?","options":["Teaching art classes in her apartment","Repairing furniture","Writing articles for a magazine","Running a small shop from home"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 1 says her neighbors assumed she was quietly repairing furniture."},{"id":"b1u12-e3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What does Mira do after she finds a piece of metal?","options":["She sells it as soon as possible","She uses it the same day","She waits a month to see whether it still interests her","She compares it with other artists\' work"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"In paragraph 3 she explains that she gives a piece a month and only uses it if it still interests her."},{"id":"b1u12-e4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What does the passage suggest about Mira\'s income?","options":["She earns a comfortable salary from teaching","She has never made a profit large enough to move","She refuses to sell any of her work","She depends on money from her family"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 4 states that she has never made a profit from a sale large enough to move."},{"id":"b1u12-e5","type":"multiple-choice","question":"As used in paragraph 3, the word \\"judge\\" most nearly means…","options":["form an opinion about the value of something","decide an official case in a court","criticise someone harshly","compare one thing with another"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Mira means that she does not decide on the day she finds a piece whether it is any good; she gives it time."},{"id":"b1u12-e6","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Her latest ___ about city gardens appeared in a design magazine last month.\\"","options":["article","material","profit","stair"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"An article is a piece of writing published in a newspaper or magazine."},{"id":"b1u12-e7","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"This chair is made from a recycled ___ that looks and feels like wood.\\"","options":["material","method","symbol","quality"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"A material is what something is made from, which is exactly what the sentence describes."},{"id":"b1u12-e8","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The two brothers are so alike that strangers often ___ them with each other.\\"","options":["compare","judge","profit","space"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To compare two things is to say how they are the same and how they differ, which is what strangers do with the brothers."},{"id":"b1u12-e9","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"After a long day on her feet, she was glad to sit down to a hot ___.\\"","options":["meal","space","stair","symbol"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"A meal is an occasion when food is eaten, such as dinner at the end of a working day."},{"id":"b1u12-e10","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"He lives ___ in a small flat and rarely has visitors.\\"","options":["alone","thin","professional","local"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Someone who is alone is not with another person, which fits a man who rarely has visitors."}]},"13":{"id":"b1-u13","book":1,"unit":13,"title":"Three Months Behind the Wheel","level":"B2","topic":"Work, money and unexpected chances","generatedAt":"2026-09-23","wordCount":418,"paragraphs":["When I graduated, I [[assume|assumed]] that a degree in [[accounting]] would lead straight to a comfortable desk and a steady income. Instead I spent eight months sending applications and hearing nothing at all. My [[former]] professor, who had once written me a [[scholarship]] reference, was kind but honest with me: the market was crowded, and being good was not enough. So in October I took a [[temporary]] job driving a taxi [[downtown]].","The work was not [[dull]], as my friends had predicted; it was exhausting and strange. Every night brought a different passenger, and every passenger had a firm opinion about the correct route. I learned that the [[fare]] on the meter is only half of what a driver earns, and that the other half is patience. I also learned how much of my own city I had never actually seen.","One regular [[client]] changed everything. He was an elderly man who ran a small chain of bakeries and who always sat in the front seat, as if he were travelling with a friend rather than paying for a service. He asked about my studies, and when I admitted that I had stopped looking for work in my field, he looked genuinely disappointed. \\"You are sitting on a [[treasure]] and complaining that the ground is hard,\\" he said. Then he made me an offer.","He wanted someone [[practical]] to look at the books of his shops one afternoon a week. He could not pay a full [[salary]], he explained, but he would [[invest]] his own time in teaching me, and he would cover the cost of the software I needed. At first the arrangement did not [[appeal]] to me; I wanted a proper job, not a favour. It would also mean [[borrow|borrowing]] against my savings to survive the months ahead. I said yes before I had time to [[embarrass]] myself by hesitating.","Within a [[quarter]] of a year I understood more about money than I had learned in three years of lectures. The old man had [[found|founded]] his first shop in 1978 with almost nothing, and he treated every decision as a small experiment. He showed me the [[loan]] he had arranged for new ovens and explained, in detail, why he had refused three better offers. When I [[urge|urged]] him to open a fourth branch, he said no, and gave his reason in a single sentence: \\"Growth that you cannot pay for is not growth.\\" I still write that on the first page of every notebook I own."],"exercises":[{"id":"b1u13-e1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What is the passage mainly about?","options":["How to pass a professional accounting exam","A graduate who found an unexpected career through driving a taxi","The dangers of borrowing money from a bank","The history of a family bakery business"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"The passage describes how a graduate who could not find work in accounting ended up learning business from a passenger in his taxi."},{"id":"b1u13-e2","type":"multiple-choice","question":"Why did the narrator start driving a taxi?","options":["He had always wanted to be a driver","His former professor recommended the company","He could not find work in his field after graduating","He needed to repay a scholarship"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"Paragraph 1 explains that after eight months of unsuccessful applications he took a temporary driving job."},{"id":"b1u13-e3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What did the regular client offer the narrator?","options":["A part-time job looking at the books of his shops","Money to open his own bakery","A reference for a university scholarship","A share in one of his shops"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Paragraph 4 says he wanted someone practical to look at the books of his shops one afternoon a week."},{"id":"b1u13-e4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What did the old man mean by \\"Growth that you cannot pay for is not growth\\"?","options":["He thought the narrator was too inexperienced to advise him","He believed a business should not expand with money it does not have","He wanted to sell the business and retire","He thought bakeries were no longer profitable"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"He refused to open a fourth branch because he had already explained that he would not expand beyond what the business could afford."},{"id":"b1u13-e5","type":"multiple-choice","question":"As used in paragraph 5, the word \\"quarter\\" most nearly means…","options":["a coin worth twenty-five cents","a period of three months","one district of a city","a place where someone lives"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"The phrase is \\"within a quarter of a year\\", so quarter here means a fourth of a year, that is three months."},{"id":"b1u13-e6","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The bank refused to give the company another ___ until the first one had been repaid.\\"","options":["loan","fare","salary","profit"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"A loan is money lent to someone, which is what a bank refuses to give a second time until the first is repaid."},{"id":"b1u13-e7","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Her first job after university was only ___, so she kept looking for something permanent.\\"","options":["temporary","dull","former","practical"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"If something is temporary, it lasts only a short time, which contrasts with the permanent job she wanted."},{"id":"b1u13-e8","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The old man was the ___ mayor of the town, having held the post for twelve years.\\"","options":["former","temporary","downtown","dull"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Former describes something that used to be but is no longer, which fits a post he no longer holds."},{"id":"b1u13-e9","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"He was so ___ by the mistake that he apologised three times.\\"","options":["embarrassed","urged","assumed","appealed"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To embarrass people is to make them feel ashamed or foolish, which explains the repeated apology."},{"id":"b1u13-e10","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The teacher ___ us to read the whole chapter before the discussion.\\"","options":["urged","borrowed","invested","founded"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To urge someone is to try hard to get them to do something, which matches a teacher pressing a class to prepare."}]},"14":{"id":"b1-u14","book":1,"unit":14,"title":"Three Questions on a Sheet of Paper","level":"B2","topic":"Learning, teaching and books","generatedAt":"2026-09-23","wordCount":424,"paragraphs":["My [[coach]] in those years was not a sportsman but a retired schoolteacher called Mr Adeyemi, who ran the [[local]] library. I was seventeen, I was failing mathematics, and I had decided that I was simply not clever. He disagreed with me, and he did so without ever once raising his voice. He wore a jacket with leather patches on the elbows and read three newspapers a day.","Once a week he would [[print]] out a single [[sheet]] of paper and hand it to me across the desk. On it were three questions, never more. \\"You cannot [[control]] what an [[exam]] asks you,\\" he said. \\"You can only [[limit]] the damage.\\" That was his whole philosophy, and it worked far better than the expensive textbooks my parents had bought.","He also gave me a [[novel]] every month. They were not the kind of books a teenager is supposed to read; some of them were written by a [[poet]] from the next town who had died before I was born. Mr Adeyemi would ask me to write an [[outline]] of the story, then a [[description]] of one [[scene]], and finally an [[example]] of a sentence I wished I had written myself. Then we would argue about it for an hour. He never told me what the books meant; he waited for me to say it first.","I thought the whole exercise was [[silly]] until the summer I sat my final examinations. In the middle of the hardest paper, I found myself doing exactly what he had taught me: reading the question twice, choosing a [[direct]] route to the answer, and refusing to be rushed by the sound of other people turning their pages. I passed. It was not a [[magical]] transformation — I had simply been shown a method and then made to practise it until it belonged to me.","Years later I met him again, standing in the queue at the little [[store]] beside the library with a bundle of [[mail]] under one arm. He had retired, and the library had closed. I told him that I had begun to write, and that I still kept his sheets of paper in a drawer. He laughed and said that the [[technology]] had changed, but the method had not. What I remember most is that he never once mentioned the years I had [[suffer|suffered]] over those exercises. He only asked whether I was still reading. I never asked him why he had chosen to help me, and I am not sure he could have explained it."],"exercises":[{"id":"b1u14-e1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What is the passage mainly about?","options":["How to pass a mathematics examination","A retired teacher who changed a student\'s approach to learning","The history of a small town library","The career of a poet from a nearby town"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"The passage describes Mr Adeyemi\'s method and the effect it had on the narrator, both at the time and years later."},{"id":"b1u14-e2","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What did Mr Adeyemi hand the narrator each week?","options":["A novel written by a local poet","A sheet of paper with three questions on it","A list of textbooks to buy","An old examination paper"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 2 says he printed a single sheet of paper with three questions, never more."},{"id":"b1u14-e3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What did Mr Adeyemi ask the narrator to write about each novel?","options":["An outline of the story and a description of one scene","A review for the local newspaper","A list of unfamiliar words","A letter to the author"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Paragraph 3 lists the outline, the description of a scene, and an example of a sentence."},{"id":"b1u14-e4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What does the narrator mean by \\"It was not a magical transformation\\"?","options":["The improvement came from method and practice rather than magic","The examination results were disappointing","Mr Adeyemi had used very unusual techniques","The narrator never really improved at mathematics"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"The narrator immediately explains that he had simply been shown a method and made to practise it."},{"id":"b1u14-e5","type":"multiple-choice","question":"As used in paragraph 4, the word \\"direct\\" most nearly means…","options":["honest and open with people","going straight to a point without turning aside","official and formally approved","controlled by one person"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"A \\"direct route to the answer\\" means the shortest and straightest one, with no detours."},{"id":"b1u14-e6","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The postman delivers our ___ before eight o\'clock every morning.\\"","options":["mail","sheet","store","scene"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Mail is letters and other things sent to people, which is what a postman delivers."},{"id":"b1u14-e7","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The library sets a ___ of five books per person.\\"","options":["limit","description","outline","example"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"A limit is the largest amount of something that is allowed, which fits a rule about how many books may be borrowed."},{"id":"b1u14-e8","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Her ___ of the accident was so clear that we could picture the whole thing.\\"","options":["description","limit","technology","exam"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"A description says what someone or something is like, and a clear one allows the listener to picture it."},{"id":"b1u14-e9","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Before writing the essay, he made a short ___ of his main points.\\"","options":["outline","sheet","scene","poet"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"An outline is a plan for a piece of writing, which is what you produce before drafting an essay."},{"id":"b1u14-e10","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The corner ___ sells fresh bread and vegetables until late in the evening.\\"","options":["store","mail","novel","coach"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"A store is a place where you can buy things, which fits a shop selling bread and vegetables."}]},"15":{"id":"b1-u15","book":1,"unit":15,"title":"The Woman in the Red Cap","level":"B2","topic":"Sport, endurance and helping a stranger","generatedAt":"2026-09-23","wordCount":419,"paragraphs":["I had trained for the river [[race]] for two years, and I was still not ready. The [[length]] of the course was eleven kilometres, and the water was [[extremely]] cold for the time of year. On the morning of the event I stood on the bank and tried to [[breathe]] slowly, the way my coach had taught me, while a hundred other swimmers did exactly the same. My coach had told me to forget the other swimmers and think only about my own stroke, but that advice is much easier to repeat than to follow.","What I had not expected was the noise. A race of that size does not begin so much as erupt, and for the first kilometre I could not [[observe]] anything except the arms churning around me. I [[consume|consumed]] half my energy in that opening stretch — a [[mistake]] that almost cost me everything. By the third kilometre my shoulders had begun to burn and my stroke had fallen apart.","Then something [[happen|happened]] that I still [[wonder]] about. A swimmer to my left, an older woman in a red cap, slowed down and matched my pace. She did not speak, which is impossible in open water anyway, but she stayed level with me for the next four kilometres, and somehow that steadiness was enough. I stopped thinking about the [[prize]] and started thinking about the next hundred metres.","I was [[fortunate]] in one respect: I had been warned about the current at the bend. A [[characteristic]] of this river is that it turns sharply near the old bridge, and swimmers who drift wide lose several minutes. I had studied the map so often that I could [[respond]] to the turn without hesitating, and the woman in the red cap followed me through it. Neither of us said so afterwards, but I understood that we had been helping each other.","I finished eleventh. I did not [[realize]] until the following day that I had beaten my own best time by six minutes, and that the [[opportunity]] to do so had come from a stranger. There is always a [[risk]] in a long swim, and there is always [[fear]]; what I had not understood [[yet]] was that both of them can be shared. The result [[excite|excited]] me far less than the simple fact of finishing. To swim [[across]] eleven kilometres of moving water is to learn something about yourself. At the finish line the woman in the red cap raised one hand, and then she was gone."],"exercises":[{"id":"b1u15-e1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What is the passage mainly about?","options":["A swimmer\'s experience of a long river race","The rules of open-water racing","How to train for a marathon","A disagreement between two competitors"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"The passage recounts one swimmer\'s race from the start on the bank to the finish line."},{"id":"b1u15-e2","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What mistake did the narrator make early in the race?","options":["He used half his energy in the opening kilometre","He swam in the wrong direction","He forgot to wear his red cap","He stopped to rest at the first bend"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Paragraph 2 says he consumed half his energy in the opening stretch, a mistake that almost cost him everything."},{"id":"b1u15-e3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What did the older woman in the red cap do?","options":["She shouted advice across the water","She slowed down and matched the narrator\'s pace","She overtook the narrator at the bridge","She asked the narrator to help her"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 3 says she slowed down, stayed level with him, and did not speak."},{"id":"b1u15-e4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"Why was the narrator fortunate at the bend?","options":["The current carried him forward without effort","He had studied the map and knew how to react to the turn","The other swimmers moved out of his way","The race was shortened because of the weather"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 4 explains that he had studied the map and could respond to the sharp turn without hesitating."},{"id":"b1u15-e5","type":"multiple-choice","question":"As used in paragraph 5, the word \\"risk\\" most nearly means…","options":["a chance that something bad may happen","a strong feeling of excitement","an unfair advantage over others","a long period of waiting"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"The narrator is talking about the danger involved in a long swim, which is the chance that something may go wrong."},{"id":"b1u15-e6","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The mountain air was so thin that it was hard to ___.\\"","options":["breathe","consume","observe","respond"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To breathe is to let air go in and out of your body, which thin mountain air makes difficult."},{"id":"b1u15-e7","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"She was ___ lucky to find a seat on the last train of the night.\\"","options":["extremely","yet","across","fortunate"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Extremely means very, very, and here it intensifies the adjective \\"lucky\\"."},{"id":"b1u15-e8","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The two brothers share one unusual ___: neither of them can hear in the left ear.\\"","options":["characteristic","opportunity","prize","mistake"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"A characteristic is something that shows what a person or thing is like, which fits an unusual shared trait."},{"id":"b1u15-e9","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"I ___ whether she ever received the letter I sent her.\\"","options":["wonder","observe","consume","realize"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To wonder is to ask yourself questions because you need to know something."},{"id":"b1u15-e10","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The athletes had trained for months for the ___ of competing at this level.\\"","options":["opportunity","fear","length","risk"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"An opportunity is a chance to do something, which is what months of training are for."}]},"16":{"id":"b1-u16","book":1,"unit":16,"title":"Seven Days at the End of the Year","level":"B2","topic":"A winter cultural festival","generatedAt":"2026-09-23","wordCount":425,"paragraphs":["Every [[december|December]], in cities across North America, thousands of [[family|families]] set aside a week to celebrate something that has nothing to do with shopping. The festival is called Kwanzaa, and it runs [[from]] the twenty-sixth of December to the first of [[january]]. It is a cultural celebration rather than a religious one, and its purpose is simple: to [[start]] the new [[year]] by remembering where you came from.","The festival was created in 1966 by a [[university]] professor who wanted people of African descent to have a moment of reflection at the [[end]] of the year. He drew on harvest traditions from several parts of the continent and organised them around [[seven]] principles, one for each day of the festival. Families light a candle for each principle in turn, and then discuss what it means in their own lives. Each principle is named in Swahili, and the words are explained aloud so that everybody in the room can follow.","The colours of the festival are [[red]], black and [[green]]. Red stands for the struggle of ancestors, black for the people themselves, and green for the land and for hope. Many people [[wear]] traditional [[clothes]] on the final evening, and the table is set with fruit and with a shared [[dinner]] that everyone has helped to cook [[together]]. In some [[home|homes]], a different person is asked to speak about each principle before the meal begins.","Gifts are [[exchange|exchanged]], but they are usually small and often handmade. A [[book]] is a common choice, as is a piece of [[art]] made by a member of the family. The emphasis is not on spending but on creating something between you. In many [[community|communities]], the celebration is public as well as private: libraries hold readings, and neighbourhood halls open their doors to anyone who wants to join in. Some families also set aside one evening for a shared project: a quilt, a photo album, or a recording of an older relative\'s memories.","Critics have sometimes asked whether a festival invented in the 1960s can honestly be called traditional. Supporters answer that all traditions were invented at some point, and that what matters is whether a practice still does useful work. For the families who gather each [[december|December]], the answer is visible in the room: several generations, one table, and a week set aside to remember that a [[community]] is something you build rather than something you inherit. That, perhaps, is the strongest argument for the festival: a tradition survives not because it is old, but because it is still needed."],"exercises":[{"id":"b1u16-e1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What is the passage mainly about?","options":["The religious beliefs of a university professor","A cultural festival that lasts seven days in December","How to cook a traditional winter dinner","The history of gift-giving in North America"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"The passage explains the origins, colours, customs and purpose of one winter festival."},{"id":"b1u16-e2","type":"multiple-choice","question":"When does the festival begin?","options":["On the first of January","On the twenty-sixth of December","On the twenty-fifth of December","At the end of November"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 1 states that the festival runs from the twenty-sixth of December to the first of January."},{"id":"b1u16-e3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What do the three colours of the festival stand for?","options":["Red for the struggle of ancestors, black for the people, green for the land and hope","Red for fire, black for night, green for trees","Red for love, black for wisdom, green for money","Red for the past, black for the future, green for the present"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Paragraph 3 gives exactly these meanings for red, black and green."},{"id":"b1u16-e4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"How do supporters answer critics who say the festival is not really traditional?","options":["They say the festival is much older than people think","They say that all traditions were invented at some point","They say the critics do not understand the language","They say the festival is a religious duty"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 5 reports that supporters reply that every tradition was invented at some point, and that what matters is whether it still does useful work."},{"id":"b1u16-e5","type":"multiple-choice","question":"As used in paragraph 4, the word \\"exchanged\\" most nearly means…","options":["given one thing and receiving another in return","argued about the price of something","bought from a shop in a hurry","wrapped carefully for a journey"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"The passage says gifts are exchanged but are usually small and handmade, so the meaning is giving and receiving in return."},{"id":"b1u16-e6","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"She bought a new winter coat because her old ___ were too thin for the cold.\\"","options":["clothes","art","book","dinner"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Clothes are what people wear to cover their bodies, which is what a winter coat belongs to."},{"id":"b1u16-e7","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The whole ___ came to the wedding, including cousins from abroad.\\"","options":["family","community","university","year"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"A family is a group of people who are related to each other, which fits relatives gathering for a wedding."},{"id":"b1u16-e8","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The concert will ___ at eight o\'clock and finish at about ten.\\"","options":["start","end","wear","exchange"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To start is to begin doing something, and eight o\'clock is the moment the concert begins."},{"id":"b1u16-e9","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"She is studying medicine at a ___ in the north of the country.\\"","options":["university","community","home","dinner"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"A university is a school where people study for a degree, which is where medicine is studied."},{"id":"b1u16-e10","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"After a long day of travelling, there is nothing better than a hot ___ with your family.\\"","options":["dinner","art","book","clothes"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Dinner is the main meal eaten in the evening, which is what you would want at the end of a long day."}]},"17":{"id":"b1-u17","book":1,"unit":17,"title":"Where the Water Went","level":"B2","topic":"Water, drought and simple engineering","generatedAt":"2026-09-23","wordCount":420,"paragraphs":["On a [[bright]] morning in the dry [[season]], a class of schoolchildren stood at the top of a bare [[hill]] and looked down at a village that had once had a river. The river was still there on old maps, but in reality it had [[disappear|disappeared]] many years earlier. Where it used to [[flow]] past the mill, there was now a single [[lone]] tree and a shallow [[puddle]] that shrank a little further every afternoon.","Their teacher had asked them to [[decide]] [[whether]] the village could solve its own problem. The question was not a sentimental one. Clean water was simply not [[available]] for most of the day; women walked four kilometres and returned with containers that had to last until evening. Everyone agreed that something had to be done, but nobody could agree on what.","The first [[response]] from the regional office was disappointing. Officials proposed a pipeline from the next town, which would take years to build and would cost more than the village earned in a decade. \\"That is not a [[solution]],\\" one farmer said. \\"That is a promise.\\" The children wrote down every suggestion, however impractical, and pinned the papers to the classroom wall. Nothing [[else]] seemed possible. The teacher kept the letters they wrote and read them aloud at the end of the term.","Then a student noticed something that everyone else had overlooked. Every roof in the village was sloped, and during the short rains a great deal of water ran off them and was [[waste|wasted]]. A simple gutter and a tank could catch it. The idea was not new, but it had never been tested here, and the older villagers were sceptical. Eventually a local builder offered to construct one system for a single household as a trial. The student had measured the roof with string and a ruler, and the numbers were hard to argue with.","A year later the village gathered to [[celebrate]] the results. The tank had supplied drinking water for six months, and two more had been built beside it. Nobody claimed that the problem was finished, and nobody pretended that the arrangement was perfectly [[fair]] — the family with the largest roof still collected the most. But the [[level]] of the wells had stopped falling, and that alone was worth [[appreciate|appreciating]]. \\"We cannot [[beat]] the weather,\\" the teacher told her class. \\"We can only move [[forward]] one roof at a time.\\" The class presented their measurements to the regional office, and this time somebody came out to look."],"exercises":[{"id":"b1u17-e1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What is the passage mainly about?","options":["A village that found a practical way to collect rainwater","A school trip to study a river","The failure of a government pipeline project","How to dig a well in dry ground"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"The passage follows a class and a village from the loss of their river to the rainwater tanks that replaced it."},{"id":"b1u17-e2","type":"multiple-choice","question":"Why was clean water difficult to obtain in the village?","options":["The wells had been poisoned","Water was not available for most of the day and had to be carried from far away","The river had flooded the village","The village had no roofs to collect water from"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 2 says water was not available for most of the day and that women walked four kilometres to fetch it."},{"id":"b1u17-e3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What did the regional office propose?","options":["A pipeline from the next town","A new well in the centre of the village","A water tank for every household","Moving the village closer to the river"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Paragraph 3 reports that officials proposed a pipeline that would take years and cost far too much."},{"id":"b1u17-e4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"Why did the student\'s idea suit this particular village?","options":["The village had many sloped roofs and short, heavy rains","The government agreed to pay for the tanks","The river returned the following year","The village had plenty of money to spend"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Paragraph 4 explains that every roof was sloped and that a great deal of water ran off them during the short rains."},{"id":"b1u17-e5","type":"multiple-choice","question":"As used in paragraph 5, the word \\"fair\\" most nearly means…","options":["reasonable and just","light in colour","ordinary and unremarkable","equal in size"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"The sentence says nobody pretended the arrangement was fair because one family still collected the most, so the meaning is just or reasonable."},{"id":"b1u17-e6","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"After the storm, children were jumping in every ___ on the road.\\"","options":["puddle","hill","season","response"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"A puddle is a pool of liquid on the ground, which is what a storm leaves behind."},{"id":"b1u17-e7","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The company promised to reply to every complaint, but no ___ ever arrived.\\"","options":["response","solution","level","flow"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"A response is an answer to a question or a message, which is what the company failed to send."},{"id":"b1u17-e8","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"It is a mistake to ___ money on things you will never use.\\"","options":["waste","beat","celebrate","appreciate"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To waste something is to use it carelessly and unnecessarily, which fits spending on useless things."},{"id":"b1u17-e9","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The path climbs steeply up the ___ before reaching the village.\\"","options":["hill","puddle","season","level"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"A hill is a raised area of land higher than the land around it, which is what a path can climb."},{"id":"b1u17-e10","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Nobody could decide ___ to travel by train or by car.\\"","options":["whether","else","forward","however"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Whether is used when you must choose between two possibilities, here the train and the car."}]},"18":{"id":"b1-u18","book":1,"unit":18,"title":"The Kitchen That Started Asking Questions","level":"B2","topic":"Food, health and changing habits","generatedAt":"2026-09-23","wordCount":419,"paragraphs":["For thirty years, the little [[restaurant]] on the corner served the same menu every single day: fried [[chicken]], white [[bread]], and a slice of [[cake]] for anyone who finished everything on the plate. It was cheap, it was [[delicious]], and it was slowly making its most loyal customers ill. Then the owner\'s doctor gave him a warning that he could not ignore, and he sat down to think about what he had built. He had inherited the place from his father and had never once questioned a line of the menu.","He began by [[ask|asking]] his customers a question that no restaurant had ever asked him: what do you actually [[eat]] at home, and why? The answers surprised him. Most people said they wanted to change their [[diet]], but they had no idea where to begin. They assumed that healthy [[food]] meant something expensive, unfamiliar and dull.","His first experiment was a [[special]] board that changed every day of the week. Monday was [[carrot]] soup with ginger; Tuesday, grilled fish with lemon. He put a bowl of [[fruit]] by the door and charged nothing at all for it. He replaced the sweet drinks with cold [[water]] and the fried chicken with a roasted version that [[contain|contained]] far less oil. Half of his regulars complained bitterly. The other half came back twice a week. One customer told him he was ruining a good business; another said it was the first time in years that she had eaten there without feeling guilty.","The turning point arrived when a local nurse brought him a [[recipe]] for a bean stew that her grandmother had made for fifty years. He cooked it on a quiet afternoon, tasted it twice, and put it on the menu the same evening. It cost a third of what the chicken cost and sold better than anything else on the board. \\"The mistake everyone makes,\\" he says now, \\"is thinking that [[health]] is a punishment. It is not. It is just cooking, done properly.\\"","Three years later the restaurant has a waiting list on Friday nights. The [[great]] surprise, the owner says, is how little he actually had to change. A [[banana]] instead of a bar of [[chocolate]], a few more vegetables on every plate, one glass of water before the meal — none of it was dramatic. What changed was the habit of thinking about it at all. He still serves cake on Sundays, and he [[always]] will. \\"A diet without pleasure,\\" he says, \\"is just a list.\\""],"exercises":[{"id":"b1u18-e1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What is the passage mainly about?","options":["A restaurant that changed its menu to become healthier","The history of fried chicken as a popular dish","Why sweet food should be avoided completely","How to open a successful restaurant"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"The passage follows one owner as he replaces an unhealthy menu with a healthier one and sees the business improve."},{"id":"b1u18-e2","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What question did the owner start asking his customers?","options":["How much are you willing to pay for a meal?","What do you actually eat at home, and why?","Do you prefer fish or chicken?","Where did you learn to cook?"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 2 says he asked customers what they ate at home and why."},{"id":"b1u18-e3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"How did customers react to the new menu?","options":["Every regular customer left and never returned","Half complained, while the other half came back twice a week","The restaurant had to close for a year","The prices had to be doubled"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 3 says half of his regulars complained bitterly while the other half came back twice a week."},{"id":"b1u18-e4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"Why did the bean stew matter so much?","options":["It cost a third of the chicken and sold better than anything else","A famous chef had recommended it","It was the only dish without meat","It won a prize at a local competition"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Paragraph 4 says it cost a third of what the chicken cost and sold better than anything else on the board."},{"id":"b1u18-e5","type":"multiple-choice","question":"As used in paragraph 4, the word \\"punishment\\" suggests that many people believe healthy food is…","options":["something unpleasant that has to be endured","too expensive to buy regularly","difficult to cook at home","only suitable for children"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"The owner is rejecting the idea that eating well is a penalty rather than a pleasure, so the word points to something unpleasant that must be endured."},{"id":"b1u18-e6","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"This soup ___ no salt at all, so it is suitable for everyone.\\"","options":["contains","eats","asks","serves"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To contain something is to hold or have it, which fits a soup that has no salt in it."},{"id":"b1u18-e7","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"My grandmother\'s ___ for apple cake has been in the family for a hundred years.\\"","options":["recipe","diet","health","restaurant"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"A recipe is a set of instructions for cooking a particular dish."},{"id":"b1u18-e8","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"He eats a slice of ___ with his coffee every morning.\\"","options":["cake","carrot","chicken","water"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Cake is a sweet food made from flour, sugar and eggs, which can be cut into slices."},{"id":"b1u18-e9","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The market sells fresh ___ and vegetables from farms in the area.\\"","options":["fruit","cake","bread","chocolate"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Fruit is healthy food that grows on trees and plants, and it is naturally paired with vegetables here."},{"id":"b1u18-e10","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Doctors say that a balanced ___ and regular exercise are the best medicine.\\"","options":["diet","recipe","restaurant","banana"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"A diet is the food a person regularly eats, so \\"a balanced diet\\" refers to the overall pattern of eating."}]},"19":{"id":"b1-u19","book":1,"unit":19,"title":"Twenty-One Days in the Atlantic","level":"B2","topic":"Survival at sea and a later inquiry","generatedAt":"2026-09-23","wordCount":411,"paragraphs":["The [[captain]] of the cargo ship had been at sea for thirty-one years and had never lost a vessel. He was an [[old]] man by the standards of his trade, and he had a [[policy]] that he repeated to every new member of his [[staff]]: \\"The weather is never wrong. We are.\\" In March a storm broke his ship in two, and eighteen people spent twenty-one days in a lifeboat made of [[wood]].","What kept them [[alive]] was not courage but routine. Every morning somebody checked the water ration; every evening somebody counted the others twice. The captain insisted on this even when there was nothing to count. \\"It gives people something to do,\\" he explained afterwards. \\"[[however|However]], you must never let them [[doubt]] the plan.\\" He did not [[bother]] to add that he had no plan at all.","On the ninth day a young sailor with a broken [[bone]] in his arm asked whether they would ever be found. The captain pointed [[toward]] the horizon and said only that ships passed this way. He did not [[mention]] the fact that the shipping lane lay eighty kilometres to the north. Later he would admit that this was the closest he ever came to lying.","An [[international]] rescue operation found them on the twenty-first day, and the story might have ended there. Instead it became a legal case. The shipping company argued that the captain had waited too long to abandon ship; a [[lawyer]] representing the families argued the opposite. The [[conclusion]] of the inquiry took two years and produced a report four hundred pages long, most of which nobody outside the industry ever read. Two of its recommendations were adopted within a decade, and neither of them mentioned the captain by name.","The captain was cleared of blame, but he never returned to sea. He spent his last years giving [[speech|speeches]] at maritime schools, and he was [[glad]] to do it. What troubled him was not the storm but the [[injustice]] he saw in its aftermath: crews from poor countries were held to standards that the owners of the ships were never asked to meet. He began to [[explore]] the idea of a [[social]] fund for sailors\' families, and although it was never created, the proposal changed the wording of one international [[policy]]. \\"I did not want to be remembered as a survivor,\\" he told a room of cadets. \\"I wanted to be remembered as somebody who asked a better question.\\""],"exercises":[{"id":"b1u19-e1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What is the passage mainly about?","options":["A captain\'s survival at sea and the inquiry that followed it","How to build a lifeboat from wood","The training of merchant sailors","A dispute between two shipping companies"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"The passage covers the shipwreck, the twenty-one days in the lifeboat, and the legal and personal consequences afterwards."},{"id":"b1u19-e2","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What policy did the captain repeat to new staff?","options":["Never sail during the month of March","The weather is never wrong; we are","Always keep the lifeboat ready","Never trust a young sailor"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 1 quotes his rule: \\"The weather is never wrong. We are.\\""},{"id":"b1u19-e3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"Why did the captain insist on daily routines in the lifeboat?","options":["To keep the water ration exactly correct","To give people something to do","To prepare the crew for a rescue","Because the company required it"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 2 quotes him saying that the routine gives people something to do."},{"id":"b1u19-e4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"Why did the captain tell the sailors that ships passed that way?","options":["It was true and he wanted to be honest with them","He wanted to give them hope, although the shipping lane was far to the north","He was planning to signal a passing ship that night","He had seen a ship earlier that same morning"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 3 says he did not mention that the lane lay eighty kilometres north, and that he later admitted this was close to lying."},{"id":"b1u19-e5","type":"multiple-choice","question":"As used in paragraph 5, the word \\"injustice\\" most nearly means…","options":["a lack of fairness","a legal punishment","a dangerous working condition","a disagreement between companies"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"The captain objects that crews were held to standards the owners were never asked to meet, which is a lack of fairness."},{"id":"b1u19-e6","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The ship\'s ___ had thirty years of experience and had never lost a vessel.\\"","options":["captain","staff","lawyer","policy"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"A captain is the person who controls a ship, and experience at sea belongs to that person."},{"id":"b1u19-e7","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The report\'s ___ was that the accident could easily have been prevented.\\"","options":["conclusion","speech","doubt","injustice"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"The conclusion is the final part of something, and a report\'s conclusion states what it decided."},{"id":"b1u19-e8","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"He did not ___ the fact that he had already been warned twice.\\"","options":["mention","explore","bother","regard"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To mention something is to talk about it, which is what he avoided doing with the earlier warnings."},{"id":"b1u19-e9","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The company has an ___ reputation and employs people in nine countries.\\"","options":["international","old","social","glad"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"If something is international, it involves more than one country, which fits a company working in nine of them."},{"id":"b1u19-e10","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"She was ___ to hear that her brother had arrived safely.\\"","options":["glad","alive","however","toward"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"If you are glad, you are happy about something, which fits relief at good news."}]},"20":{"id":"b1-u20","book":1,"unit":20,"title":"The Map That Was Wrong","level":"B2","topic":"A sixteenth-century expedition and how legends survive","generatedAt":"2026-09-23","wordCount":410,"paragraphs":["In 1540 an expedition of several hundred men set out from the coast of Mexico with a single [[goal]]: to find seven cities that were said to be paved with gold. The story had reached them through a priest who claimed to have seen one of the cities from a distance. Nobody in the party had [[consider|considered]] the simpler explanation — that he had seen a village of sunlit stone, and that memory had done the rest.","The journey was not a [[war]], though it was fought every day against thirst, distance and the flat indifference of the land. For weeks the men ate dried [[meat]] and whatever [[vegetable|vegetables]] they could find, and drank from rivers that had [[already]] dried up. The leader [[advise|advised]] his officers to keep the column together, and for a while they managed it. The youngest men sang to keep their spirits up, and the older ones saved their breath.","What they found, when they reached the first settlement, was a [[basic]] arrangement of mud houses around a well. There was no gold. There was not even a [[bit]] of silver. The priest, when he was questioned, insisted that this was not the city he had described and that the [[real]] one lay further north. Some of the officers believed him. Others had begun to suspect that he had [[lie|lied]] from the beginning.","The expedition pushed on for another five months. It [[destroy|destroyed]] nothing of value, because there was nothing of value to destroy, but it lost half its horses and a quarter of its men. The [[opinion]] of the camp turned against the priest, and when the leader finally ordered a return, the decision was recorded as [[worth]] nothing by one chronicler and as a rare act of good sense by another.","Historians still disagree about what the story means. One reading is that the cities were never there at all, and that the expedition was a machine for turning hope into ruin. A second reading is gentler: the men were not fools but ordinary people who had [[regard|regarded]] a rumour as a map. What is certain is that the legend outlived every explorer who chased it. It [[entertain|entertained]] readers for centuries, and it still [[serve|serves]] as a warning. The [[extra]] years the leader spent writing his report did not [[achieve]] much during his own lifetime, but the report survives — a document that lets us [[reflect]] on how easily a wish becomes a fact."],"exercises":[{"id":"b1u20-e1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What is the passage mainly about?","options":["An expedition that searched for seven golden cities and found nothing","How to read a sixteenth-century map","The life of a priest in Mexico","Why gold was valuable in the sixteenth century"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"The passage follows the expedition from its departure to its return, and then considers how the story has been interpreted."},{"id":"b1u20-e2","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What did the expedition find at the first settlement?","options":["A city paved with gold","A basic arrangement of mud houses around a well","A large silver mine","Nothing at all, because the settlement was empty"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 3 describes a basic arrangement of mud houses, with no gold and not even a bit of silver."},{"id":"b1u20-e3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What happened during the five extra months of travelling?","options":["The expedition found the real cities further north","It lost half its horses and a quarter of its men","It met another expedition travelling south","It turned back immediately without moving"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 4 says the expedition lost half its horses and a quarter of its men."},{"id":"b1u20-e4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What is the \\"gentler\\" reading of the story mentioned in paragraph 5?","options":["The men were ordinary people who mistook a rumour for a map","The cities were found but kept secret","The priest had deliberately been cruel to the men","The expedition was a complete success"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Paragraph 5 says this reading sees the men not as fools but as ordinary people who regarded a rumour as a map."},{"id":"b1u20-e5","type":"multiple-choice","question":"As used in paragraph 5, the word \\"reflect\\" most nearly means…","options":["think carefully about something","send back light from a surface","copy the behaviour of other people","record something in writing"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"The report lets us reflect on how a wish becomes a fact, so the verb here means to think carefully about something."},{"id":"b1u20-e6","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The hikers carried a little ___ of bread and cheese in their bags.\\"","options":["bit","meat","war","goal"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"A bit is a small amount of something, which fits a little bread and cheese."},{"id":"b1u20-e7","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"It is unwise to ___ that a train will always arrive exactly on time.\\"","options":["consider","destroy","entertain","serve"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To consider something can mean to think about it as true, which is what the sentence warns against doing."},{"id":"b1u20-e8","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The storm ___ three houses near the coast last winter.\\"","options":["destroyed","served","entertained","reflected"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To destroy is to damage something so badly that it cannot be used, which is what a severe storm does to houses."},{"id":"b1u20-e9","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"In my ___, the new library is a great improvement on the old one.\\"","options":["opinion","goal","bit","meat"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"An opinion is a thought about a person or thing, and \\"in my opinion\\" introduces a personal view."},{"id":"b1u20-e10","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The hotel ___ a hot breakfast between seven and ten every morning.\\"","options":["serves","achieves","advises","lies"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To serve someone is to give them food or drinks, which is what a hotel does at breakfast time."}]},"21":{"id":"b1-u21","book":1,"unit":21,"title":"The Quiet Ward","level":"B2","topic":"A nurse, one long night, and an ordinary kind of courage","generatedAt":"2026-09-23","wordCount":441,"paragraphs":["Katy did not [[appear]] nervous, [[though]] the ward was unusually still that evening. She had been a [[nurse]] for eleven years, and quiet evenings made her suspicious rather than calm. On her way in she passed the reception desk, where a [[clerk]] was sorting a pile of forms, and [[enter|entered]] the lift carrying a cup of tea she had no intention of drinking. The corridor smelled of disinfectant and, faintly, of somebody\'s late dinner.","Her [[career]] had begun almost by accident. At eighteen she wanted to study medicine, but the fees were far beyond her family\'s reach, so she trained as a nurse instead. For two years she was certain she had made a mistake. The [[various]] duties she was given — changing dressings, recording temperatures, [[inform|informing]] anxious relatives about delays that were nobody\'s fault — seemed to have nothing to do with the work she had imagined. [[later|Later]], and rather suddenly, she came to see that they were the [[base]] on which everything else rested. A doctor might diagnose; a nurse had to notice. Those are different skills, and only one of them can be learned from a book.","Shortly after nine, a young man was brought in who had fallen from a ladder. He was in obvious [[pain]] but could not [[locate]] its source; he only repeated that his shoulder felt wrong. A doctor examined him briefly, ordered a scan, and decided that an [[operation]] would be necessary. The man [[refuse|refused]] at first. He had a wedding to attend on Saturday, he said, and he could not see why a small fall should cost him that. It took twenty minutes of patient explanation before he agreed to stay.","By midnight Katy\'s [[brain]] had slowed to a crawl. There was still paperwork to finish, and she knew that if she did not [[hurry]] she would be there until dawn. She made an [[effort]] to concentrate. The surgeon\'s notes were [[excellent]] — clear, ordered, easy to follow — and she copied the essential details into the file before going to check on the patient. He was asleep, with his phone on the blanket beside him, showing a photograph of a woman in a yellow dress. She did not [[leave]] the ward until half past two.","People sometimes called her a [[hero]]. She [[refuse|refused]] the word, politely but firmly. What she did, she said, was ordinary work done carefully, and the fact that it mattered to [[various]] people did not make it extraordinary. [[Though]] she was tired, she walked home rather than take a taxi, because the cold air helped her think and because she liked the empty streets at that hour."],"exercises":[{"id":"b1u21-e1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What is the passage mainly about?","options":["A hospital\'s shortage of experienced staff","One night in the working life of a nurse","How a patient recovered after a serious fall","Why young people choose medical careers"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"The passage follows Katy through a single evening shift, describing her duties, her patient and her attitude to her work."},{"id":"b1u21-e2","type":"multiple-choice","question":"Why did Katy train as a nurse instead of studying medicine?","options":["Her family could not afford the fees","She failed the entrance examinations","A relative persuaded her to change her mind","She was frightened of performing operations"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Paragraph 2 says the fees were far beyond her family\'s reach, so she trained as a nurse instead."},{"id":"b1u21-e3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"How did the man who fell from the ladder respond to the news that he needed an operation?","options":["He asked to be sent to another hospital","He agreed immediately and thanked the doctor","He refused at first, then agreed after twenty minutes","He insisted on leaving before the scan"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"Paragraph 3 states that he refused at first and that it took twenty minutes of patient explanation before he agreed to stay."},{"id":"b1u21-e4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What does Katy\'s reaction to the word \\"hero\\" suggest about her?","options":["She dislikes being praised for doing her job","She believes nurses deserve higher pay","She thinks the doctor should have been praised instead","She regrets not having studied medicine"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"She refuses the word politely but firmly and describes her work as ordinary, which shows she rejects the praise rather than welcoming it."},{"id":"b1u21-e5","type":"multiple-choice","question":"In paragraph 4, \\"her brain had slowed to a crawl\\" most nearly means that Katy…","options":["had begun to feel unwell","was finding it hard to think quickly","had decided to work more slowly on purpose","could no longer remember her training"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"The phrase describes the effect of tiredness: she had to make an effort to concentrate because her thinking had become slow."},{"id":"b1u21-e6","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Rescuers used a map and a torch to ___ the missing walker in the dark.\\"","options":["locate","inform","hurry","refuse"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To locate something is to find it, which is exactly what the rescuers were trying to do."},{"id":"b1u21-e7","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Please ___ the office if your address changes.\\"","options":["enter","appear","inform","leave"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"To inform someone is to tell them something — here, to tell the office about a new address."},{"id":"b1u21-e8","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Finishing the report on time took a great deal of ___ .\\"","options":["effort","pain","career","base"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Effort is hard work or an attempt to do something, which fits completing a difficult task on time."},{"id":"b1u21-e9","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The hotel was ___ : clean, quiet and surprisingly cheap.\\"","options":["various","excellent","later","though"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Excellent means very good, and the list of qualities that follows all supports a strongly positive judgement."},{"id":"b1u21-e10","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Visitors must show a pass before they ___ the building.\\"","options":["enter","locate","inform","appear"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To enter a place is to go into it, so a pass would be checked before someone goes in."}]},"22":{"id":"b1-u22","book":1,"unit":22,"title":"The Silver Plate","level":"B2","topic":"A small shop, a missing object, and an unexpected kindness","generatedAt":"2026-09-23","wordCount":503,"paragraphs":["For forty years the bakery on King Street had been [[manage|managed]] by the same family, and in that time almost nothing had [[occur|occurred]] there that was worth reporting. Then, one Tuesday in March, a silver [[plate]] disappeared from the window.","The [[customer|customers]] who came in that morning noticed nothing unusual. Mrs. Aldridge, who had [[earn|earned]] her living from the shop since she was nineteen, was in the back, [[set|setting]] out trays for the afternoon delivery. The plate had been in the window at eight o\'clock; by ten it was gone. It was not valuable in the [[actual]] sense — it was thin, scratched, and probably worth very little — but it had belonged to her mother, and that made it a different matter entirely.","She did not want to [[contact]] the police. What would she have said? That a plate had vanished while six people were standing in the room? A [[thief]] would have needed to be invisible. The whole thing was a [[mystery]], and mysteries made her uncomfortable, because they usually turned out to have very ordinary explanations.","That evening her grandson, Daniel, came to [[deliver]] the bread order and found her sitting by the window with the lights off. He listened to the story, then asked a single question: had anyone come in who had bought nothing? She thought about it. Yes — a man in a grey coat had stood near the display for several minutes, had asked about the [[charge]] for a birthday cake, and had left without ordering.","Daniel did not go to the police either. Instead he waited outside the shop the next morning, and when the man in the grey coat appeared again he asked him, politely, whether he had perhaps taken something by mistake. The man went red. He had taken the plate, he admitted, because it looked exactly like one his own mother had owned, and he had wanted to [[comfort]] himself with it for a few days. He had not meant to [[steal]] it. He had meant to bring it back.","The plate was returned before lunch. Mrs. Aldridge chose not to press the matter further, and the man [[receive|received]] a box of cakes instead of a lecture. Her kindness did not [[include]] an apology from him, though he offered one anyway, twice. Daniel said afterwards that this was a better [[reward]] than any punishment would have been — the [[opposite]] of what most people would have done, perhaps, but the shop had never been like most shops.","The story [[amaze|amazed]] the regular customers when they heard it, and it travelled further than the family expected. There is a small iron [[gate]] at the side of the building, and for years afterwards people would stop at it and ask whether this was the bakery where the plate had been stolen and given back. Mrs. Aldridge always answered the same way: that nothing had been stolen, and that nothing had been returned either. It had simply gone out for a while and come home."],"exercises":[{"id":"b1u22-e1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What is the passage mainly about?","options":["How a family bakery was forced to close","A theft that was handled with forgiveness rather than punishment","The history of a silver plate owned by a baker","Why small shops find it hard to keep customers"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"The plate is taken and returned, and the shopkeeper chooses kindness instead of involving the police — that decision is the heart of the story."},{"id":"b1u22-e2","type":"multiple-choice","question":"Why did Mrs. Aldridge care about the plate?","options":["It was worth a great deal of money","It had been a gift from a regular customer","It had belonged to her mother","It was the oldest object in the shop"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"Paragraph 2 says the plate was not valuable in the actual sense but had belonged to her mother."},{"id":"b1u22-e3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What made Daniel suspect the man in the grey coat?","options":["He had been seen near the window the night before","He had asked about the price of a cake and bought nothing","He had asked Mrs. Aldridge where the plate was kept","He had been in the shop on several previous occasions"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"In paragraph 4 Mrs. Aldridge recalls that the man asked about the charge for a birthday cake and then left without ordering."},{"id":"b1u22-e4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What does the passage suggest about Mrs. Aldridge\'s decision?","options":["She regretted it almost immediately","It was typical of the way the shop had always been run","It was made because the police refused to help","It was criticised by her regular customers"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"The narrator notes that her choice was the opposite of what most people would have done, but that the shop had never been like most shops."},{"id":"b1u22-e5","type":"multiple-choice","question":"In paragraph 3, the word \\"mystery\\" is used to describe…","options":["a crime that the police could not solve","an event that Mrs. Aldridge found hard to explain","a secret that Daniel was keeping from his grandmother","a story that the customers invented"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"She calls the disappearance a mystery because a plate seemed to have vanished while six people were in the room."},{"id":"b1u22-e6","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The courier will ___ the parcel before noon.\\"","options":["deliver","include","manage","receive"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To deliver something is to take it from one place to another, which is what a courier does with a parcel."},{"id":"b1u22-e7","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"There is no extra ___ for luggage on this flight.\\"","options":["reward","charge","mystery","plate"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"A charge is the price you pay for something, so it fits a statement about luggage costing nothing extra."},{"id":"b1u22-e8","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The prize list will ___ three weekend trips to the coast.\\"","options":["include","occur","earn","steal"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To include something means to have it as part of a group, which is what a prize list does with its prizes."},{"id":"b1u22-e9","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"She sat with the crying child and tried to ___ him.\\"","options":["contact","comfort","manage","deliver"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"To comfort someone means to make them feel better, which is what you would do for a crying child."},{"id":"b1u22-e10","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Nobody expected the concert to be cancelled, but it did ___ in the end.\\"","options":["occur","receive","set","earn"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To occur means to happen, so it fits a sentence about something taking place after all."}]},"23":{"id":"b1-u23","book":1,"unit":23,"title":"Two Weeks at Pine Lake","level":"B2","topic":"A diary from a summer sports camp","generatedAt":"2026-09-23","wordCount":402,"paragraphs":["Day one. I was the last [[passenger]] off the bus, which is typical of me. The camp sits [[behind]] a line of tall pines, and the first thing I noticed was a flag [[pole]] near the water, with nothing on it.","There are thirty of us, and I am easily the most [[average]] [[athlete]] here. Most of the others have been training for years. I signed up for the swimming [[course]] because I can [[swim]] reasonably well, though not quickly. Our coach, a former [[member]] of a national team, told us on the first evening that speed is the last thing she cares about. \'Technique first,\' she said. \'Speed is a consequence.\'","Day four. We spent the morning on [[safety]] drills, which sound dull but were not. We learned how to [[remove]] a struggling swimmer from the water without being pulled under ourselves. Later, on the field, we tried archery. I had never [[shoot|shot]] an arrow before, and my first three attempts went nowhere near the target. The fourth [[sound|sounded]] as though it had hit something, and it had — the [[pole]] holding the target.","Day seven. Something has shifted in my [[mental]] state, though I could not say exactly when. I no longer count the hours until dinner. In the water I [[advance|advanced]] from the slow group to the middle group, which nobody noticed except me.","Day nine. There is a girl here called Rosa whose [[personality]] could not be more different from mine. She talks constantly, and at first I found this exhausting. Last night she read us a [[poem]] she had written about the lake. It was not good, but she read it as though it were, and that is a kind of courage I do not have.","Day twelve. The final race. My [[behavior]] in the water was calmer than it has ever been; my arms [[match|matched]] the rhythm I had been practising for a fortnight, and I finished fourth out of thirty. Fourth is not a medal. But I was [[behind]] the winner by less than two seconds, and at the start of the fortnight I would have been [[behind]] by half a minute.","Day fourteen. On the last morning I found a [[web]] stretched between two reeds, still wet with dew, and stood there for ten minutes doing nothing at all. Then I [[lower|lowered]] my bag to the ground and went back for one more swim."],"exercises":[{"id":"b1u23-e1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What is the writer\'s main purpose in this passage?","options":["To persuade readers to take up competitive swimming","To record personal progress over a two-week camp","To explain how a sports camp is organised","To compare two different swimming coaches"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"The passage is a dated diary that traces the writer\'s changing ability and attitude from day one to day fourteen."},{"id":"b1u23-e2","type":"multiple-choice","question":"Why did the writer choose the swimming course?","options":["The coach had invited her personally","It was the only course still available","She could already swim reasonably well","She wanted to compete at national level"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"Paragraph 2 says she signed up because she could swim reasonably well, though not quickly."},{"id":"b1u23-e3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What happened when the writer tried archery?","options":["She hit the pole that held the target","She hit the target with her first arrow","She was asked to leave the field","She decided not to try again"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Paragraph 3 says the fourth attempt sounded as though it had hit something, and it had — the pole holding the target."},{"id":"b1u23-e4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What does the writer\'s comment about Rosa\'s poem reveal about her?","options":["She is jealous of Rosa\'s popularity","She admires Rosa\'s willingness to perform despite the quality of the work","She thinks Rosa should stop writing poetry","She wishes she had written the poem herself"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"She says the poem was not good but that Rosa read it as though it were, and calls that a kind of courage she does not have."},{"id":"b1u23-e5","type":"multiple-choice","question":"In the final entry, the writer\'s decision to go back for one more swim suggests that she…","options":["wants to win the race next year","has grown to enjoy the place rather than just the training","is worried about losing her fitness","has not yet packed her bag"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"She stops to look at a spider\'s web and then returns to the water for its own sake, which shows a shift from measuring progress to simply enjoying the setting."},{"id":"b1u23-e6","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The referee asked the coach to ___ the injured player from the pitch.\\"","options":["remove","advance","match","lower"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To remove something is to take it away, which is what happens to an injured player who must leave the pitch."},{"id":"b1u23-e7","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The two sets of figures do not ___ , so one of them must be wrong.\\"","options":["sound","match","shoot","swim"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"To match is to be the same or similar, so figures that do not match cannot both be correct."},{"id":"b1u23-e8","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Please ___ your voice — the baby is asleep upstairs.\\"","options":["lower","remove","advance","behave"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To lower something is to make it go down, so lowering your voice means speaking more quietly."},{"id":"b1u23-e9","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The train was crowded, and every ___ was standing in the corridor.\\"","options":["member","athlete","passenger","course"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"A passenger is a person who rides in a train, car or aeroplane, so a crowded train is full of them."},{"id":"b1u23-e10","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Wearing a helmet is a basic ___ rule on any building site.\\"","options":["mental","average","safety","behind"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"Safety means being free from danger, so a safety rule is one designed to prevent injury."}]},"24":{"id":"b1-u24","book":1,"unit":24,"title":"Thirty Years of Disagreeing","level":"B2","topic":"Friendship, disagreement and one night on stage","generatedAt":"2026-09-23","wordCount":461,"paragraphs":["Marta and I met at a school concert, in a hall so badly designed that the sound reached the back row a full second after it left the stage. She was playing the violin; I was there because a friend had dragged me along. We spoke afterwards for perhaps four minutes. Neither of us could have predicted that those four minutes would turn into a [[friendship]] lasting thirty years.","What held it together was not similarity. On almost every subject we disagreed. She was a [[critic]] by temperament — of films, of books, of the way people behaved in queues — while I [[support|supported]] almost everything on principle. [[probably|Probably]] that is why it worked: she gave me [[direction]] when I drifted, and I gave her permission to enjoy things.","The [[event]] that tested us came in our late twenties. Marta had written a short play and had been offered a single night in a [[public]] theatre. She asked me to [[perform]] in it. I had no experience and, worse, no natural ability. Rehearsals were held in a cold room above a shop, and the script was [[complex]] — three timelines, six characters, and a final scene that made no sense to anyone until the week before we opened.","Under that kind of [[pressure]] people show what they are made of. I forgot my lines at the dress rehearsal and had to be prompted twice. Marta, who had every reason to panic, stayed calm. \'You are [[smart]] enough,\' she said. \'You do not trust the words.\' She had chosen every [[term]] in the script deliberately, she explained, and if I trusted the words, the meaning would follow.","We opened to a small audience. Somewhere in the second act I stopped being afraid. The play was not a triumph — one reviewer called it \'promising\', which is the politest way of saying disappointing — but the audience [[cheer|cheered]] at the end, and that was enough.","That night we made a promise. Whatever else happened, we would [[unite]] once a year to make something together, however small. We have kept to it. There have been [[block|blocks]] of years when we barely spoke, months when we each [[bury|buried]] ourselves in work and said nothing for weeks, long [[lack|lacks]] of time and patience. But we always came back.","I still [[exercise]] three times a week because of her, incidentally. She told me once that a friendship needs maintenance the way a body does, and that the hardest part is not the grand gesture but turning up when nothing much is happening. She was a [[guide]] in that respect, though she would have hated the word. She preferred to [[strike]] at ideas rather than accept them, and in doing so she taught me to do the same."],"exercises":[{"id":"b1u24-e1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What is the passage mainly about?","options":["The difficulty of writing a play for a small theatre","A long friendship built on difference rather than similarity","Why the writer gave up acting after one performance","The difference between critics and ordinary readers"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"The passage traces a thirty-year friendship and argues that disagreement, not similarity, held it together."},{"id":"b1u24-e2","type":"multiple-choice","question":"According to the writer, what did the two friends give each other?","options":["Practical help with money and work","Direction and permission to enjoy things","Honest reviews of each other\'s writing","Introductions to useful people"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 2 says she gave him direction when he drifted, and he gave her permission to enjoy things."},{"id":"b1u24-e3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"Why did the writer find the rehearsals difficult?","options":["The theatre was too cold to work in","He had no experience and no natural ability","Marta kept changing the script","The other actors refused to help him"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 3 states that he had no experience and, worse, no natural ability."},{"id":"b1u24-e4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What does the writer imply about the reviewer who called the play \\"promising\\"?","options":["The review was more positive than it appeared","The reviewer had not understood the play","The word was a polite way of expressing disappointment","The reviewer was a personal friend of Marta\'s"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"The writer glosses the word himself, saying it is the politest way of saying disappointing."},{"id":"b1u24-e5","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What does the writer mean when he says Marta preferred to \\"strike at ideas\\"?","options":["She attacked people who disagreed with her","She questioned ideas instead of accepting them","She wrote about violence in her plays","She refused to discuss anything seriously"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"The phrase is contrasted with accepting ideas, and the writer says her habit taught him to question things in the same way."},{"id":"b1u24-e6","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The choir will ___ in the town hall on Saturday evening.\\"","options":["perform","support","unite","exercise"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To perform is to do something in front of people who watch, which is what a choir does at a concert."},{"id":"b1u24-e7","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The report was so ___ that nobody could summarise it in one page.\\"","options":["smart","complex","public","probably"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"If something is complex it has many small parts and is hard to understand, which explains why it cannot be summarised briefly."},{"id":"b1u24-e8","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The walkers lost their ___ in the fog and had to stop for the night.\\"","options":["direction","pressure","term","event"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"A direction is the way to go, so losing it in fog means no longer knowing which way to walk."},{"id":"b1u24-e9","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"There was a serious ___ of skilled staff, so the project fell behind.\\"","options":["block","guide","lack","critic"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"A lack of something means there is not enough of it, which explains why the project fell behind."},{"id":"b1u24-e10","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The two small parties agreed to ___ against the larger one.\\"","options":["unite","bury","cheer","strike"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To unite is to get together to do something, which is what two small parties would do against a larger opponent."}]},"25":{"id":"b1-u25","book":1,"unit":25,"title":"The Pond Behind the Mill","level":"B2","topic":"How a village brought an old industrial pond back to life","generatedAt":"2026-09-23","wordCount":426,"paragraphs":["Ask anyone in the valley where the pond came from and you will get a different answer each time. Some say it was always there. Others say it was dug by hand, and that the men who dug it were paid in flour. The truth, according to the parish records, is less romantic: it was created in 1871 to supply water to a [[factory]] that made woollen cloth, and for sixty years that was all it was.","The factory closed in the 1930s. For a [[period]] of about twenty years the pond was used for nothing at all. People [[associate|associated]] it with the mill, and the mill with hard times, so they stayed away. [[trash|Trash]] was dumped at one end. Reeds [[populate|populated]] the shallows. By the 1950s it had become, in the words of one local newspaper, an eyesore of the first order.","What saved it was a schoolteacher named Joe Halloran, who moved to the village in 1958 and could not understand why nobody swam there. He [[organize|organized]] a clean-up. He [[involve|involved]] the children first, because children are easier to persuade than adults, and then their parents, because the children went home and complained. Within three summers the rubbish was gone and a [[sign]] had appeared at the entrance: SWIMMING PERMITTED.","Joe\'s greatest [[feature]] as a campaigner was his patience. He did not ask for money. He asked for an hour. In one [[instance]] he spent an entire winter writing to the council about the water quality, and the water quality, largely as a result of that correspondence, improved.","The pond now [[produce|produces]] more than anyone expected. There is a [[wide]] [[range]] of birds — herons, coots, at least four kinds of duck — and a [[mix]] of habitats that biologists from the university come to study. The [[environment]] is monitored [[regular|regularly]]: samples are taken every month, and the results are pinned up beside the car park.","Some [[tradition|traditions]] have grown up around it. On the first Sunday in June there is a race from one [[tip]] of the pond to the other, open to anyone who can swim. It is not a serious competition. People enter in fancy dress; a man once completed the distance in a full suit of armour made of cardboard.","I [[recognize]] that this is a small story, and that it will never appear in a history book. But small stories are how places keep themselves. The [[medicine]] for a dying village, Joe used to say, is not money but attention. He was right, and the pond is the proof."],"exercises":[{"id":"b1u25-e1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What is the passage mainly about?","options":["The decline of the woollen industry in one valley","How a neglected pond was restored and became useful again","The life of a schoolteacher who loved swimming","Why village traditions are difficult to maintain"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"The passage follows the pond from its creation for a factory, through decades of neglect, to its restoration and present use."},{"id":"b1u25-e2","type":"multiple-choice","question":"Why was the pond originally created?","options":["To provide a place for the village to swim","To supply water to a cloth factory","To store fish for the winter","To drain water away from the fields"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 1 says the parish records show it was created in 1871 to supply water to a factory that made woollen cloth."},{"id":"b1u25-e3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"Why did Joe Halloran involve children in the clean-up first?","options":["They were stronger than the adults","They were easier to persuade than adults","The council had asked him to do so","They had complained about the rubbish"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 3 says he involved the children first because children are easier to persuade than adults, and then their parents."},{"id":"b1u25-e4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What does the writer suggest about Joe\'s method of campaigning?","options":["It depended on raising large amounts of money","It relied on patience and small requests rather than grand gestures","It was successful only because of university support","It failed until the council changed its policy"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 4 says his greatest feature as a campaigner was his patience: he did not ask for money but for an hour."},{"id":"b1u25-e5","type":"multiple-choice","question":"In paragraph 2, the word \\"eyesore\\" is used to describe something that…","options":["is unpleasant to look at","is dangerous to swim in","is difficult to find","is expensive to repair"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"The word is applied to the pond when it was full of rubbish and reeds, and the newspaper\'s judgement is clearly a negative one about its appearance."},{"id":"b1u25-e6","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The region is home to a wide ___ of bird species.\\"","options":["range","sign","period","tip"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"A range is a set of similar things, so a wide range of species means many different kinds."},{"id":"b1u25-e7","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"A large ___ on the wall warned visitors not to feed the animals.\\"","options":["mix","sign","feature","tradition"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"A sign is an indication that gives information or a warning, which is exactly what such a notice does."},{"id":"b1u25-e8","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"You should take this ___ twice a day until the cough disappears.\\"","options":["medicine","trash","instance","range"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Medicine is something you take to treat an illness, and taking it twice a day is normal medical advice."},{"id":"b1u25-e9","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The committee will ___ a fundraising dinner for the school.\\"","options":["recognize","involve","organize","associate"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"To organize is to plan or get ready for an event, which is what a committee does with a fundraising dinner."},{"id":"b1u25-e10","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Her plan is to ___ every empty flat in the building.\\"","options":["populate","produce","associate","monitor"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"If people populate an area, they live there, so populating empty flats means filling them with residents."}]},"26":{"id":"b1-u26","book":1,"unit":26,"title":"Three Mornings a Week","level":"B2","topic":"A mountain postman, his donkey, and a village that looks after itself","generatedAt":"2026-09-23","wordCount":380,"paragraphs":["The village of Kestor is [[situated]] about nine hundred metres above sea level, at the end of a road that stops being a road some two kilometres before you reach it. There is no bus. There is a track, and on that track, three mornings a week, there is Archie.","Archie is seventy-one and has been carrying post [[along]] this route for forty years. His vehicle is a donkey named Bramble, who is [[neither]] fast nor especially obedient but who has never once fallen. \'A motorbike would be quicker,\' he admitted when I met him, \'and a helicopter would be quicker still. But you cannot [[ride]] a helicopter up a flight of steps.\'","The [[climb]] takes two and a half hours. Archie stops four times to let Bramble rest, and at each stop he [[drop|drops]] the bags, sits on a stone, and says nothing. He does not [[react]] well to questions during these pauses; he [[imply|implies]] by his silence that the mountain deserves your [[attention]] more than he does.","What [[attract|attracts]] people to Kestor is not the scenery, though the scenery is remarkable. It is the fact that a [[society]] of ninety people has somehow [[maintain|maintained]] itself on a slope too steep to farm properly. There is a school with eleven pupils. There is a shop that opens when the owner feels like it. There is no doctor, and the nearest hospital is two hours away by road, which is why the villagers keep a [[standard]] of self-reliance that visitors find either admirable or alarming, depending on how long they stay.","I asked Archie for [[advice]] about walking the route myself. He [[suggest|suggested]] that I start early, carry less than I thought I needed, and [[prove]] nothing to anybody. \'People come up here to test themselves,\' he said. \'That is fine. But the mountain does not care whether you succeed. [[otherwise|Otherwise]] you would not need to come at all.\'","The [[physical]] effort of the last hour is considerable. My legs ached for two days afterwards. But the [[final]] stretch, where the track turns and the whole valley opens beneath you, is worth every step. Archie was waiting for me at the top, [[further]] up than I had expected, and he handed me a letter that had been addressed to me."],"exercises":[{"id":"b1u26-e1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What is the passage mainly about?","options":["The dangers of walking in high mountain areas","A postman, his donkey and the isolated village they serve","Why young people are leaving mountain villages","The history of postal services in remote regions"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"The passage profiles Archie and the route he has walked for forty years, and describes the village at the top of it."},{"id":"b1u26-e2","type":"multiple-choice","question":"Why does Archie not use a motorbike?","options":["He cannot afford one","The track is too steep and stepped for a vehicle","He is not allowed to ride one","Bramble is faster than a motorbike"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Archie\'s remark that you cannot ride a helicopter up a flight of steps makes the point that the route is stepped and steep."},{"id":"b1u26-e3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"How does Archie behave when he stops to rest?","options":["He asks the walkers endless questions","He complains about the weight of the bags","He sits down and stays silent","He feeds and washes the donkey"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"Paragraph 3 says he drops the bags, sits on a stone and says nothing, and does not react well to questions."},{"id":"b1u26-e4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What does the writer find most striking about Kestor?","options":["The quality of the scenery","The number of tourists who visit it","The way a small community has kept going in a difficult place","The size of the village school"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"Paragraph 4 says what attracts people is not the scenery but the fact that a society of ninety people has maintained itself on a slope too steep to farm."},{"id":"b1u26-e5","type":"multiple-choice","question":"In paragraph 5, what does Archie imply about people who climb the mountain?","options":["They should train for several months first","They are wasting their time","They often come for the wrong reasons","They should always hire a guide"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"He says the mountain does not care whether you succeed, otherwise you would not need to come at all — a comment on the motives of those who climb it."},{"id":"b1u26-e6","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The two brothers were ___ willing to sell the farm.\\"","options":["neither","otherwise","further","along"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Neither connects two negative possibilities, so it fits a sentence in which both brothers refused."},{"id":"b1u26-e7","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Leave now, ___ you will miss the last train.\\"","options":["further","otherwise","neither","situated"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Otherwise means in another way if you do not do this, which is exactly the warning given here."},{"id":"b1u26-e8","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Regular servicing will ___ the machine in good condition for years.\\"","options":["maintain","react","drop","imply"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To maintain something means to make it stay the same, so servicing keeps the machine in good condition."},{"id":"b1u26-e9","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Her results ___ that she had worked extremely hard all year.\\"","options":["prove","attract","climb","ride"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To prove something is to show that it is true, which is what strong results do about hard work."},{"id":"b1u26-e10","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"I would ___ taking the earlier flight, in case of delays.\\"","options":["suggest","imply","maintain","react"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To suggest something is to give an idea or plan about it, which is what the speaker is doing about the flight."}]},"27":{"id":"b1-u27","book":1,"unit":27,"title":"The Trap in the Sand","level":"B2","topic":"Desert wildlife and the survival strategy of one small predator","generatedAt":"2026-09-23","wordCount":379,"paragraphs":["If you [[imagine]] a [[desert]], you probably picture sand and heat and very little else. This is not [[false]] exactly, but it is incomplete. Deserts contain more life than most people expect, and some of it is [[quite]] remarkable.","Consider the [[spider]]. More than forty thousand species are known, and they live almost everywhere: in forests, in gardens, on the [[coast]], and in deserts so dry that rain falls perhaps once in a decade. Their success is not a [[puzzle]] to biologists — they are simply extremely [[effective]] predators — but the details are worth [[examine|examining]].","A desert spider faces a [[specific]] problem. It needs water, and there is none. Some species solve this by staying underground during the day and emerging at night, when the air is cooler. Others have evolved a different strategy: they build a trap.","The trap is made of silk, which the spider produces from its own body. It is not sticky. Instead the threads are arranged like a funnel, and any insect that walks across them is guided towards a hidden entrance. The spider waits at the bottom. The system is so efficient that a single spider can capture enough food in one night to survive a month of [[hunger]].","I spent a week on a research [[trip]] to the Namib some years ago, and I still have not fully [[figure-out|figured out]] how they do it. What I can tell you is that the threads are finer than a human hair and stronger, weight for weight, than steel. A [[bite]] from most desert species is harmless to people.","[[actually|Actually]], the more I learned, the less the [[journey]] felt like a holiday. A [[tour]] group arrived on the third day and asked, reasonably enough, where the animals were. There were hundreds of them under our feet. They had simply been invisible.","There is a good [[deal]] of nonsense written about spiders — that they are aggressive, that they hunt people, that their bite is dangerous. [[rather|Rather]] than argue with any of it, I now take people outside at night with a torch. The [[gift]] of a good torch, it turns out, is that it changes what you can see. After twenty minutes nobody talks about danger any more. They ask what everything is called."],"exercises":[{"id":"b1u27-e1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What is the writer\'s main purpose in this passage?","options":["To warn readers about dangerous desert animals","To explain how a desert spider survives and to correct common beliefs about it","To describe a holiday taken in southern Africa","To argue that deserts contain no life at all"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"The passage explains the spider\'s trap and its water strategy, and ends by challenging popular fears about spiders."},{"id":"b1u27-e2","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What problem do desert spiders face, according to the writer?","options":["A lack of insects to eat","A shortage of water","Competition from other predators","Very cold night-time temperatures"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 3 says the spider needs water and there is none, which is the specific problem it has to solve."},{"id":"b1u27-e3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What is unusual about the trap the spider builds?","options":["It is made of stone rather than silk","It is built underground","It is not sticky, but guides insects towards a hidden entrance","It is large enough to catch birds"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"Paragraph 4 states that the trap is not sticky and that the threads are arranged like a funnel to guide insects towards a hidden entrance."},{"id":"b1u27-e4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"Why did the tour group fail to see the animals?","options":["The animals were active only in the middle of the day","The animals had moved away from the area","The animals were present but extremely hard to notice","The group had arrived at the wrong time of year"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"Paragraph 6 says there were hundreds of animals under their feet, but they had simply been invisible."},{"id":"b1u27-e5","type":"multiple-choice","question":"In the final paragraph, the writer says the gift of a good torch is that it…","options":["keeps people warm in the desert night","changes what people are able to notice","frightens dangerous animals away","helps people find their way back to camp"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"The writer explains that after twenty minutes with the torch nobody talks about danger and they start asking what things are called — their attention has shifted."},{"id":"b1u27-e6","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The label on the bottle turned out to be ___ , so the medicine was not what it claimed.\\"","options":["false","specific","effective","quite"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"If something is false it is not correct, which fits a label that misdescribes its contents."},{"id":"b1u27-e7","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The new training method proved far more ___ than the old one.\\"","options":["effective","specific","false","rather"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"If something is effective it works well, so an effective method produces better results than the old one."},{"id":"b1u27-e8","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The customs officer asked me to ___ the contents of my suitcase.\\"","options":["examine","imagine","figure out","puzzle"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To examine something is to look at it carefully, which is what an officer does with luggage."},{"id":"b1u27-e9","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"We had to ___ which bus to take, since none of the signs were in English.\\"","options":["figure out","imagine","examine","journey"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To figure something out is to come to understand it and find an answer, which is what was needed with the bus timetable."},{"id":"b1u27-e10","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The village lies on the ___ , about ten minutes\' walk from the sea.\\"","options":["desert","coast","journey","tour"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"The coast is the land beside an ocean, so a village a short walk from the sea is on the coast."}]},"28":{"id":"b1-u28","book":1,"unit":28,"title":"The Night the Power Went Out","level":"B2","topic":"A birthday party, a storm, and an accidental concert","generatedAt":"2026-09-23","wordCount":399,"paragraphs":["The [[storm]] had been [[predict|predicted]] for days, and by Friday afternoon it was clear that the forecast had been right. Wind tore at the trees along the [[driveway]]; rain came down in sheets. My neighbour, a retired [[professor]] of music, stood at her window and said, with some satisfaction, \'Well. The party will have to be [[cancel|cancelled]].\'","It was her birthday. Her family had been planning it for months. There was a [[band]] booked, a [[list]] of forty guests, and a [[schedule]] pinned to the fridge. By six o\'clock the power was gone and the [[garbage]] bins had blown over. We sat in the dark and [[share|shared]] a bottle of water.","Then somebody — I never found out who — went to the garage and came back with a guitar. The professor [[notice|noticed]] immediately, as musicians do. \'That is not an [[instrument]],\' she said. \'That is a piece of furniture.\' But she took it anyway.","What happened next is difficult to describe without sounding sentimental. Eight people who [[barely]] knew each other sat in a circle on the floor, and one by one they were handed the guitar. Some could play. Most could not. It was, technically, the most [[boring]] concert I have ever attended. It was also the best evening I have had in years.","The professor played last. She had not performed in public for a decade, and she [[rush|rushed]] through the first minute as though she wanted it over. Then she stopped, took a breath, and started again — slowly, [[within]] the limits of what her hands could still do. Nobody spoke. Outside, the wind continued.","The electricity came back at ten past eleven, and with it the temptation to return to the plan: the [[stage]], the speakers, the band that had been booked. We did not. The professor said that the evening had contained a kind of [[magic]] that no amount of planning would have produced, and I think she was right. It had also been entirely accidental.","I [[own]] a guitar now. It sits in the corner of my room and I play it badly, but I play it. When people ask why, I tell them about the [[message]] I received the following morning — a photograph of forty people sitting on the floor of a dark house, sent by a woman I had never met, with the caption: \'Look what the storm did.\'"],"exercises":[{"id":"b1u28-e1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What is the passage mainly about?","options":["The damage caused by a severe winter storm","An evening that turned out better than the original plan","A professor\'s career as a concert performer","How to organise a large family celebration"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"The planned party is cancelled by the storm, but the improvised evening in the dark becomes the best evening the writer has had in years."},{"id":"b1u28-e2","type":"multiple-choice","question":"How did the professor react to the storm at first?","options":["She was alarmed about the damage to her house","She seemed almost pleased that the party would be cancelled","She immediately suggested an alternative plan","She refused to speak to anyone"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 1 says she stood at the window and announced, with some satisfaction, that the party would have to be cancelled."},{"id":"b1u28-e3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What did the professor say about the guitar?","options":["That it was too old to be played","That it was not really an instrument","That it belonged to a former student","That it needed new strings"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 3 quotes her saying that it is not an instrument but a piece of furniture, though she took it anyway."},{"id":"b1u28-e4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"Why does the writer call the evening both boring and the best in years?","options":["The music was poor but the atmosphere was memorable","He was too tired to enjoy the party properly","The guests left early and he had the house to himself","He preferred the band that had been booked"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"He describes the concert as technically the most boring he has attended, yet also the best evening in years — the value lay in the shared experience rather than the skill."},{"id":"b1u28-e5","type":"multiple-choice","question":"In paragraph 6, the writer says the evening contained a kind of \\"magic\\". This suggests that it was…","options":["carefully organised in advance","surprisingly delightful and impossible to plan","created by a professional musician","over too quickly to be enjoyed"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"He adds that no amount of planning would have produced it and that it was entirely accidental, so the magic lay in its unplanned quality."},{"id":"b1u28-e6","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The airline had to ___ the flight because of heavy snow.\\"","options":["cancel","predict","notice","rush"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To cancel means to decide that an event will not happen, which is what an airline does with a flight it cannot operate."},{"id":"b1u28-e7","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Economists find it very difficult to ___ the exact date of a recession.\\"","options":["predict","own","share","notice"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To predict something is to say that it will happen, which is what economists attempt when they give a date."},{"id":"b1u28-e8","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Please put the empty bottles in the ___ , not beside it.\\"","options":["schedule","garbage","instrument","stage"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Garbage is waste material such as bottles, so empty bottles belong in the garbage."},{"id":"b1u28-e9","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The singer walked onto the ___ to a roar of applause.\\"","options":["stage","driveway","list","message"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"A stage is the place where musicians or actors perform, so it is where a singer would walk to applause."},{"id":"b1u28-e10","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The deadline falls ___ the same working week, so we must start immediately.\\"","options":["within","barely","boring","own"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Within means inside another thing, so a deadline within the same week falls inside that week."}]},"29":{"id":"b1-u29","book":1,"unit":29,"title":"The Mason of Vale\'s Crossing","level":"B2","topic":"A village legend and the ordinary bridge behind it","generatedAt":"2026-09-23","wordCount":477,"paragraphs":["Every village has one story that everybody knows and nobody can verify. In Vale\'s Crossing it is the story of the bridge. The [[usual]] version goes like this: the river was too wide to cross, the villagers could not afford an engineer, and one winter a stranger appeared and offered to build it in exchange for a [[payment]] that he never specified.","The stranger was [[talkative]] in a way that made people uneasy. He was [[sociable]] enough — he drank in the tavern, he remembered names — but he asked odd questions about how long people lived and what they were afraid of. He worked at night, and at a [[moderate]] pace; by morning there would be another stone in place, cut so precisely that there was no visible gap between it and the next.","The work took [[half]] a year. The villagers brought him food, which he accepted without thanks. Children followed him about, and he did not send them away; he seemed to find their [[innocence]] amusing. When the bridge was finished he named his price: the first living thing to cross it.","This is where the versions differ. In one, the villagers are [[overwhelm|overwhelmed]] by the demand and simply never use the bridge, and it stands empty for a generation. In another, an old woman [[lead|leads]] her dog across, and the stranger leaves without a word. In a third, he is never paid at all and simply disappears.","What interests me is not which version is true — none of them is — but why the story has survived. The bridge is real. It was built in 1740 by a mason whose name is recorded in the parish register, and his [[craft]] was learned in the ordinary way, from a master to an apprentice. There was no [[meeting]] with a stranger and no contract of any kind.","The likeliest explanation is that the mason was simply very good, and that his skill was [[overwhelm|overwhelming]] to people who had never seen anything like it. A [[perception]] of magic is easier to accept than an explanation of engineering. So the story [[merge|merged]] the man and the myth, and the two have never been separated since.","Local historians have tried to [[settle]] the question for a century, and have reached a [[compromise]]: the legend is taught as a legend, not as history. The [[burden]] of proof, they say, lies with whoever repeats it. I crossed the bridge last spring and will admit to a [[shiver]]. The stone looks [[fragile]] in places, though it has held for nearly three centuries, and I did not [[dive]] into the river beneath, although the day was hot enough to tempt me.","The [[speed]] of traffic on the new road has emptied the old crossing. Nobody uses it now except walkers. But the story keeps going, and I suspect it always will."],"exercises":[{"id":"b1u29-e1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What is the passage mainly about?","options":["How a stone bridge was repaired after three centuries","A local legend and the ordinary history it hides","Why villagers in remote places distrust strangers","The training of apprentice stonemasons in the 1700s"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"The writer recounts the legend, then shows that the bridge was built by an ordinary mason and asks why the story persists."},{"id":"b1u29-e2","type":"multiple-choice","question":"Why did the stranger make people uneasy?","options":["He refused to speak to anyone in the village","He asked strange questions and worked only at night","He demanded payment before starting work","He was known to have cheated other villages"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 2 says he asked odd questions about how long people lived and what they feared, and that he worked at night."},{"id":"b1u29-e3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What does the writer say about the different versions of the story?","options":["One version is supported by the parish register","They all end in the same way","None of them is true","The oldest version is the most reliable"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"Paragraph 5 states plainly that none of the versions is true, and that the bridge was built by a recorded mason."},{"id":"b1u29-e4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"According to the writer, why has the legend survived?","options":["The villagers were forbidden to record the truth","The mason\'s skill was so great that a magical explanation seemed easier","The bridge was never actually completed","Historians deliberately invented the story"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 6 argues that his skill was overwhelming to people who had never seen anything like it, and a perception of magic is easier to accept than engineering."},{"id":"b1u29-e5","type":"multiple-choice","question":"In paragraph 7, the writer\'s admission of a \\"shiver\\" suggests that he…","options":["was physically cold on the bridge","felt a moment of unease despite not believing the legend","was afraid of falling into the river","regretted crossing the bridge"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"He has just called the legend untrue, yet still feels a shiver — an emotional response that survives his scepticism."},{"id":"b1u29-e6","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"After a long discussion, the two sides finally reached a ___ .\\"","options":["compromise","burden","shiver","payment"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"A compromise is an agreement in which each side gives up something, which is what two sides reach after negotiation."},{"id":"b1u29-e7","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The extra paperwork has become a heavy ___ for the small team.\\"","options":["perception","burden","meeting","craft"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"A burden is a serious or difficult responsibility, so extra paperwork can be described as a heavy burden."},{"id":"b1u29-e8","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The parcel arrived in a box marked ___, so it was handled with great care.\\"","options":["fragile","moderate","sociable","usual"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"If something is fragile it can be damaged easily, which is exactly why it would be handled carefully."},{"id":"b1u29-e9","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"She is extremely ___ and will talk to anyone at a party.\\"","options":["talkative","fragile","usual","overwhelmed"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Someone who is talkative talks a lot, which fits a person who will speak to anyone."},{"id":"b1u29-e10","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The two companies decided to ___ into a single organisation.\\"","options":["merge","settle","craft","lead"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To merge two things is to combine them into one whole, which is what two companies do when they become a single organisation."}]},"30":{"id":"b1-u30","book":1,"unit":30,"title":"The Fossil in the Bookshop","level":"B2","topic":"Idioms, their doubtful origins, and why the stories survive","generatedAt":"2026-09-23","wordCount":477,"paragraphs":["There is a small shop near my flat that sells second-hand books. It has been for [[sale]] twice in ten years and has never sold. The owner, who is [[independent]] in every sense, sits [[inside]] behind a desk covered in paper and does not appear to want customers at all.","I go there because of one shelf. On it are about forty dictionaries of [[idiom]] — books that explain why English speakers say that it is raining cats and dogs, or that the cat is out of the bag. In the [[center]] of the shop, on a low table, are the volumes he cannot bring himself to price. Most people would find such books dull. I find them irresistible.","The shopkeeper explained the appeal to me once. \'An [[idiom]] is a fossil,\' he said. \'It is the [[memory]] of a sentence that people stopped saying for [[proper]] reasons and kept saying for no reason at all.\' He is a [[master]] of the throwaway remark, and that one stayed with me. He once told me that certain phrases ought to be locked up, and that if he had his way there would be a [[jail]] for clich\xe9s.","Take the cat out of the bag. The [[common]] explanation is that it comes from markets, where a dishonest trader would show a customer a pig in a sack and then hand over a cat instead. Once the cat was out, the trick was visible to everyone. It is a story about [[honesty]], or the absence of it.","The trouble is that this explanation, though widely repeated, is not supported by much [[evidence]]. The phrase appears in print in the eighteenth century, and no source from that period mentions pigs or sacks. The [[belief]] that it comes from the market is, in other words, a story about a story.","What the dictionaries [[demonstrate]], when you read enough of them, is that the origin of most idioms is unknown. There are exceptions, of course; some phrases [[cost]] us nothing to explain because their history is documented. But the [[amount]] of confident nonsense written about word origins is remarkable.","The shopkeeper has a theory about this too. He says that people prefer a good story to a true one, and that this is not a fault — it is how language survives. He keeps a notebook in his [[pocket]] in which he writes down phrases he has heard on the street, with the date and the name of the speaker. He has filled eleven notebooks.","I asked him once whether he would ever sell the collection. He said he would not, and that he had [[ahead]] of him at least another decade of collecting. Then he pointed [[above]] the shelf, where a second row of dictionaries stood, and said: \'That is a [[different]] problem. Those are the ones I have not read yet.\'"],"exercises":[{"id":"b1u30-e1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What is the passage mainly about?","options":["The history of English dictionaries","A bookshop owner\'s interest in idioms and their unreliable origins","Why second-hand bookshops are closing","How to sell a private book collection"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"The passage profiles the shopkeeper and his collection of idiom dictionaries, and discusses why explanations of idiom origins are often untrue."},{"id":"b1u30-e2","type":"multiple-choice","question":"Why does the writer keep visiting the shop?","options":["It is the cheapest place to buy books nearby","The owner is a close friend","He is drawn to the shelf of idiom dictionaries","He hopes to buy the shop one day"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"Paragraph 2 says plainly that he goes there because of one shelf, which holds about forty dictionaries of idiom."},{"id":"b1u30-e3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What does the shopkeeper mean when he calls an idiom \\"a fossil\\"?","options":["It is very old and difficult to understand","It is the surviving trace of a sentence people no longer say for any real reason","It belongs in a museum rather than a dictionary","It has been copied from another language"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"He explains it as the memory of a sentence that people stopped saying for proper reasons but kept saying anyway."},{"id":"b1u30-e4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What is wrong with the market explanation of \\"the cat is out of the bag\\"?","options":["It is supported by too many sources to be useful","The phrase is much older than the eighteenth century","No source from the period mentions pigs or sacks","The phrase was invented by dictionary writers"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"Paragraph 5 says the phrase appears in print in the eighteenth century and no source from that period mentions pigs or sacks."},{"id":"b1u30-e5","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What is the writer\'s attitude towards the shopkeeper?","options":["Impatient with his refusal to sell anything","Warmly appreciative of his way of thinking","Sceptical of everything he says","Concerned about his health"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"The writer calls the books irresistible, says the shopkeeper\'s remark stayed with him, and records his theories with obvious pleasure."},{"id":"b1u30-e6","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The two witnesses gave completely ___ accounts of the accident.\\"","options":["different","common","proper","independent"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"If something is different it is not the same as something else, which is what conflicting accounts are."},{"id":"b1u30-e7","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"There is no ___ that he was anywhere near the scene.\\"","options":["evidence","amount","sale","memory"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Evidence is a fact or thing used to prove something, so a lack of evidence means nothing can be proved."},{"id":"b1u30-e8","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"A clock that size will ___ a great deal to repair.\\"","options":["cost","demonstrate","own","sell"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To cost is to require payment, so a repair that requires a lot of payment costs a great deal."},{"id":"b1u30-e9","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The instructor will ___ the correct technique before you try it yourself.\\"","options":["demonstrate","cost","master","pocket"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To demonstrate something is to show how it is done, which is what an instructor does before learners practise."},{"id":"b1u30-e10","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Her ___ about the project turned out to be correct after all.\\"","options":["belief","amount","center","jail"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"A belief is a strong feeling that something is correct or true, so a belief turning out to be correct fits the sentence."}]}}'),n=JSON.parse('{"1":{"id":"b2-u1","book":2,"unit":1,"title":"The Village Above the Clouds","level":"B2","topic":"Tourism and how a mountain village changed","generatedAt":"2026-09-23","wordCount":416,"paragraphs":["For most of its history, Marrenbach was simply a [[place]] where farmers kept goats and grew just enough food for their own families. It lies high in the [[mountain|mountains]], a few kilometres [[east]] of the main valley road, and until a [[train]] line reached it in 1911 hardly anybody outside the region had heard of the village. The line had been built to carry timber, not visitors, but the visitors came anyway.","The first of them were walkers. They arrived in summer, [[walk|walked]] up from the valley, and stayed in farmhouses [[because]] there was no hotel. They came to [[watch]] the light change on the peaks and to breathe air that city dwellers found almost shockingly clean. Local people thought them odd — [[such]] behaviour is difficult to explain to anyone who has to work for a living — but they were happy to rent out rooms, and the money was welcome.","Then somebody discovered the snow. The slopes above the village hold snow from December to April, and by the 1930s Marrenbach had become one of the first resorts in the region where ordinary families could learn to [[ski]]. A [[tower]] was built to support a cable car, a second hotel opened, and the village began to change shape. The old wooden houses were repainted, window boxes appeared on every wall, and in summer the balconies were full of [[flower|flowers]].","Tourism made Marrenbach prosperous, but it also made it [[expensive]]. A small [[garden]] behind a house, which once grew vegetables, is now worth more than the house itself. [[many|Many]] of the young people who left for the cities in the 1970s have come back, because there is work again, although the work is no longer farming. The [[total]] number of beds in the village is now larger than the number of permanent residents.","Every year about two [[million]] people pass through Marrenbach. For a [[town]] of nine hundred, that is a remarkable figure, and it explains why the place sometimes feels less like a village than like a stage. Whether this is good or bad depends on whom you ask, and both answers can be defended. What is certain is that Marrenbach is now [[popular]] in a way nobody in 1911 could have imagined, and that its [[holiday|holidays]], once quiet weeks when the fields were empty, have become the busiest part of the year. Somewhere in the [[world]] there are still valleys that nobody has thought to visit. There are fewer of them every decade."],"exercises":[{"id":"b2u1-e1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"Why was the railway line built to Marrenbach?","options":["To bring tourists up from the valley","To carry timber out of the mountains","To connect two large cities","To serve the hotel that had just opened"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 1 says the line had been built to carry timber, not visitors, and that the visitors came anyway."},{"id":"b2u1-e2","type":"multiple-choice","question":"Where did the first visitors stay?","options":["In a hotel beside the cable car","In tents above the village","In farmhouses, because there was no hotel","In the old wooden houses they bought"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"Paragraph 2 states that they stayed in farmhouses because there was no hotel."},{"id":"b2u1-e3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What change took place in the 1930s?","options":["The railway finally reached the village","The village became a place where ordinary families could learn to ski","The last farm in the valley closed","The village was connected to the main road"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 3 says that by the 1930s Marrenbach had become one of the first resorts where ordinary families could learn to ski."},{"id":"b2u1-e4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"The detail that a small garden is \\"worth more than the house itself\\" is used to show that…","options":["vegetables are unusually expensive in the mountains","the houses in the village are badly built","land in the village has become extremely valuable","farming is more profitable than tourism"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"The garden\'s value is given as evidence that tourism has made the village expensive — land is now worth more than the buildings on it."},{"id":"b2u1-e5","type":"multiple-choice","question":"In the final paragraph, the comparison of the village to \\"a stage\\" suggests that…","options":["the village is used for theatrical performances","life there is shaped by being watched by visitors","the houses are only temporary structures","the residents earn their living as performers"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"The writer is describing a village whose daily life is performed for two million visitors a year, so it feels like a place on show rather than a working community."},{"id":"b2u1-e6","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The village is ___ with visitors in July, so book your room early.\\"","options":["popular","east","total","such"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"If a place is popular, it is liked by many people — which explains why rooms must be booked early."},{"id":"b2u1-e7","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Add the two figures together and tell me the ___.\\"","options":["tower","total","garden","flower"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"A total is the amount you get when everything has been added up."},{"id":"b2u1-e8","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"We decided to ___ up the hill instead of taking the bus.\\"","options":["ski","train","walk","watch"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"To walk is to move forward on your legs, which is the opposite of taking a bus."},{"id":"b2u1-e9","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Everything in that shop is far too ___ for me to afford.\\"","options":["many","popular","expensive","such"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"Expensive things cost a lot of money, so they are hard to afford."},{"id":"b2u1-e10","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"He stood at the window and ___ the rain for an hour.\\"","options":["walked","skied","trained","watched"],"answerIndex":3,"explanation":"To watch is to look at something for a period of time, which fits standing at a window as the rain falls."}]},"2":{"id":"b2-u2","book":2,"unit":2,"title":"Twelve Months on a Hill Farm","level":"B2","topic":"Seasons and the working year","generatedAt":"2026-09-23","wordCount":411,"paragraphs":["The year on our hill farm did not [[consist]] of four seasons so much as twelve separate months, each with its own work. My grandfather kept a notebook in which he [[remark|remarked]] on the weather every morning, and reading it now I can see how closely the family\'s plans followed the [[landscape]] around them. It was not a sentimental record. He noted frost, rain, the first swallow and the day the cows went out, and nothing else. Yet taken together it reads like a calendar of small decisions.","January was the month of waiting. Nothing grew, and the [[household]] lived on what had been stored in autumn. Everyone was [[anxious]] about the potatoes and the hay, and the mood in the kitchen could turn [[awful]] if the frost went on too long. My grandmother, however, refused to complain. She was always [[polite]] to the postman, even when he brought nothing but bills, and she [[desire|desired]] only one thing: a clear, cold sky.","By February we were [[eager]] for light. The days lengthened [[rapidly]], and by the end of the month there was enough sun to make the wet roofs [[shine]]. My father would [[lift]] sacks of seed onto the cart and [[load]] them one by one, and after an hour of that his [[lung|lungs]] ached in the cold air. He never mentioned it. His only [[intent]] that month was to have everything ready before the ground softened.","Spring arrived with a [[pace]] that surprised us. One week the fields were brown; the next they were green. There was a [[motion]] in the valley that had been missing all winter — tractors, birds, children running down the lane. We would [[seek]] out the first wild garlic in the wood, and because the family did not [[possess]] a greenhouse, the young plants lived on the kitchen windowsill, where they were always in danger of being knocked over.","Somebody always [[spill|spilled]] something — a bucket of milk, a tray of eggs — and there would be a brief silence, and then laughter, and then the work would continue. That is what I remember most about those twelve months: not the labour, but the noise of a house in which everyone had something to do. My grandfather\'s notebook ends in the middle of December, with a line about a hard frost and a reminder to check the pipes. He died the following spring. The farm is still there, and so, in a sense, is he."],"exercises":[{"id":"b2u2-e1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What did the grandfather write in his notebook every morning?","options":["His plans for the day\'s work","A note about the weather","The prices he had been paid for hay","A list of what the family had eaten"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 1 says he remarked on the weather every morning, noting frost, rain, the first swallow and the day the cows went out."},{"id":"b2u2-e2","type":"multiple-choice","question":"Why was January a difficult month for the household?","options":["There was too much rain to work outside","They had to sell the animals to buy food","Nothing grew and they lived on what had been stored","The postman brought news that worried them"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"Paragraph 2 explains that nothing grew and the household lived on what had been stored in autumn, and that everyone was anxious about the potatoes and the hay."},{"id":"b2u2-e3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What was the father\'s only intent in February?","options":["To repair the roof before the rain came","To sell the seed he had kept from last year","To plant the fields as early as possible","To have everything ready before the ground softened"],"answerIndex":3,"explanation":"Paragraph 3 states that his only intent that month was to have everything ready before the ground softened."},{"id":"b2u2-e4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"Why were the young plants kept on the kitchen windowsill?","options":["The family did not possess a greenhouse","The kitchen was the warmest room in the house","They were too valuable to leave outside","The greenhouse had been damaged by frost"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Paragraph 4 says that because the family did not possess a greenhouse, the young plants lived on the kitchen windowsill."},{"id":"b2u2-e5","type":"multiple-choice","question":"In paragraph 4, \\"Spring arrived with a pace that surprised us\\" — pace most nearly means…","options":["the direction in which something moves","the speed at which something happens","the amount of work involved","the length of time something lasts"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"The next sentence shows the fields turning from brown to green within a week, so pace refers to the speed of the change."},{"id":"b2u2-e6","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"He felt ___ before the interview and his hands were shaking.\\"","options":["polite","eager","anxious","awful"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"Anxious means worried or nervous, which fits shaking hands before an interview."},{"id":"b2u2-e7","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The soup was ___ — I could not finish it.\\"","options":["awful","polite","anxious","eager"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"If something is awful it is very bad, which is why the speaker could not finish it."},{"id":"b2u2-e8","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Please be ___ and thank your host before you leave.\\"","options":["eager","polite","awful","anxious"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Polite behaviour shows thoughtfulness towards other people, such as thanking a host."},{"id":"b2u2-e9","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The family did not ___ a car, so they walked everywhere.\\"","options":["seek","spill","lift","possess"],"answerIndex":3,"explanation":"To possess something is to own it — not owning a car explains why they walked."},{"id":"b2u2-e10","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Hold the jug with both hands or you will ___ the milk.\\"","options":["spill","lift","seek","shine"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To spill is to let something fall accidentally out of its container."}]},"3":{"id":"b2-u3","book":2,"unit":3,"title":"The Pass at Kaldra","level":"B2","topic":"A short history of a mountain battle","generatedAt":"2026-09-23","wordCount":409,"paragraphs":["For three days a small force held the [[entrance]] to the pass at Kaldra against an [[enemy]] many times its size. The road there is barely wide enough for two carts, with the sea on one side and a cliff on the other, and a hundred determined men in the right place can stop a thousand. Historians still argue about the numbers, but they agree on the essentials: a narrow road, a [[steady]] line of defenders, and a [[disadvantage]] that no amount of courage could remove.","The defenders were not a professional [[military]] force. They were farmers and shepherds who had been called up for a single summer, and most of them had never fought in a [[battle]] before. Their [[chief]], a man named Ordan, [[intend|intended]] to hold the pass for a week — long enough for the towns behind him to prepare. He [[hardly]] had time to explain this before the first scouts appeared on the ridge.","Their [[weapon|weapons]] were simple: spears, shields and a few [[bow|bows]]. An [[arrow]] shot from a [[bow]] could cross the whole valley, yet inside the pass there was rarely room to draw one, so the fighting came down to shields and short blades, and to who grew tired first.","Ordan\'s plan depended on [[trust]]. He had to [[trust]] that the men on the left would not move, and that the men on the right would [[obey]] without asking why. A soldier who [[twist|twisted]] round to see what was happening behind him left a gap, and a gap in a line of shields is the beginning of defeat.","On the fourth morning a local guide showed the attackers a path over the mountain — a route the defenders had not thought to [[secure]]. From that moment the position was impossible. There was no [[laughter]] in the camp that night, only the sound of men stacking [[log|logs]] on the fires and waiting. [[unless|Unless]] reinforcements arrived by dawn, they would be surrounded. None arrived. Ordan dismissed most of his force and stayed with a few hundred volunteers, and the pass fell before noon.","Whether that last stand was [[brave]] or merely stubborn is a question each generation answers differently. What is not in doubt is that it bought time, and that the towns behind the pass used it. The names of the dead were later carved into a stone at the top of the road, and travellers still stop there, mostly without knowing what happened."],"exercises":[{"id":"b2u3-e1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"Who were the defenders at the pass?","options":["A professional army sent from the towns","Farmers and shepherds called up for one summer","Volunteers who had fought many battles before","Sailors from the ships in the bay"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 2 says they were farmers and shepherds called up for a single summer, most of whom had never fought in a battle."},{"id":"b2u3-e2","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What did Ordan intend to do?","options":["Hold the pass for a week so the towns could prepare","Defeat the enemy army completely","Retreat to the sea and escape by boat","Capture the guide who knew the mountain path"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Paragraph 2 states that Ordan intended to hold the pass for a week — long enough for the towns behind him to prepare."},{"id":"b2u3-e3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"Why were bows of limited use inside the pass?","options":["The defenders had run out of arrows","There was hardly room to draw one","The enemy soldiers wore heavy armour","The wind made accurate shooting impossible"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 3 explains that although an arrow could cross the whole valley, inside the pass there was rarely room to draw a bow."},{"id":"b2u3-e4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What made the defenders\' position impossible on the fourth morning?","options":["The enemy attacked at night","Their supplies of food ran out","A local guide showed the attackers a path over the mountain","Ordan was wounded and could not give orders"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"Paragraph 5 says a local guide showed the attackers a path over the mountain, which the defenders had not secured."},{"id":"b2u3-e5","type":"multiple-choice","question":"In paragraph 1, \\"a disadvantage that no amount of courage could remove\\" — disadvantage most nearly means…","options":["a situation that makes success harder","a punishment given to soldiers","a disagreement between leaders","a mistake made in planning"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"A disadvantage is a condition that makes it difficult to do something; here it refers to the defenders\' small numbers."},{"id":"b2u3-e6","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The soldiers were ordered to ___ their commander without question.\\"","options":["obey","secure","trust","twist"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To obey is to do what a person in authority tells you to do, which fits an order given without question."},{"id":"b2u3-e7","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The company hopes to ___ a large contract before December.\\"","options":["obey","secure","twist","trust"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"To secure something is to get it after a lot of effort, which is how contracts are usually won."},{"id":"b2u3-e8","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"He ___ spoke to anyone after the argument.\\"","options":["hardly","unless","steady","brave"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Hardly means almost not at all, which matches someone who has stopped speaking to people."},{"id":"b2u3-e9","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"We will not finish the work ___ you help us.\\"","options":["hardly","unless","brave","steady"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Unless means if not, so the sentence means the work will not be finished if help does not come."},{"id":"b2u3-e10","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The narrow path ___ through the trees and then climbed steeply.\\"","options":["obeyed","trusted","twisted","secured"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"To twist is to turn around and around, which describes a path winding through a wood."}]},"4":{"id":"b2-u4","book":2,"unit":4,"title":"The Antlers and the Legs","level":"B2","topic":"A fable about looks and usefulness","generatedAt":"2026-09-23","wordCount":384,"paragraphs":["A young deer came down to the river at midday to drink. The water was still, and for the first time in his life he saw himself clearly: a [[narrow]] head, a [[pale]] coat, and above it a pair of antlers so fine that he forgot to drink. \\"I did not [[estimate]] that I was so handsome,\\" he said. He was not being vain, exactly; he simply had not known.","What [[disturb|disturbed]] him was the part below his [[chest]]. His legs were thin and [[rough|rough-looking]], and they did not match the rest of him at all. He did not want to [[impress]] anyone in particular. He simply could not accept that the two halves of his body belonged together. \\"Anyone would take me for a servant,\\" he thought, and the idea [[satisfy|satisfied]] him so little that he turned away from the water.","That afternoon he heard the dogs. The first sound was distant, and then it was not. [[terror|Terror]] — he had never felt anything like it — sent him running towards the trees, and whatever [[confidence]] he had possessed an hour earlier disappeared completely. His legs, the ones he had been ashamed of, carried him over the [[rough]] ground faster than he had believed possible.","But the wood was [[narrow]], and the antlers he had admired so much caught in the branches. He pulled and the branches held. Behind him the dogs came closer, and the [[threat]] grew until he [[scream|screamed]], a thin and helpless sound. With one final effort he tore himself free, leaving an antler behind, and ran until the barking faded.","He reached the river again at dusk, shaking, and lay down in the [[shade]] of an oak. Only then did he understand the [[consequence]] of what he had done. He had trusted his beauty and doubted his [[strength]], and the part of himself he had despised was the part that had saved him. The [[disaster]] had been entirely of his own making, and there had been no [[victim]] that day but himself. He did not [[honor]] his legs, exactly; he simply stopped insulting them.","The grass by the river was poor, and he had to [[supplement]] it with leaves from the lower branches. A [[sensitive]] animal learns quickly. He drank, and this time he looked only at the water."],"exercises":[{"id":"b2u4-e1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"Why did the deer stop drinking at the river?","options":["He was frightened by a sound in the trees","He saw his reflection and was struck by his antlers","The water was too rough to drink","He noticed that the river had stopped flowing"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 1 says the water was still, he saw himself clearly for the first time, and his antlers were so fine that he forgot to drink."},{"id":"b2u4-e2","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What did the deer dislike about himself?","options":["His narrow head","His pale coat","His thin, rough-looking legs","The size of his antlers"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"Paragraph 2 says the part below his chest disturbed him: his legs were thin and rough-looking and did not match the rest of him."},{"id":"b2u4-e3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What did the deer\'s legs do when the dogs appeared?","options":["They carried him over the rough ground faster than he had believed possible","They failed him and he could not move","They carried him straight into the branches of a tree","They were too thin to cross the river"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Paragraph 3 says his legs, the ones he had been ashamed of, carried him over the rough ground faster than he had believed possible."},{"id":"b2u4-e4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"Why was the deer caught in the wood?","options":["He was too tired to run any further","The wood was narrow and his antlers caught in the branches","The dogs had surrounded him on three sides","He stopped to drink from the river again"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 4 says the wood was narrow and the antlers he had admired caught in the branches, which held him fast."},{"id":"b2u4-e5","type":"multiple-choice","question":"In paragraph 4, \\"the threat grew until he screamed\\" — threat most nearly means…","options":["a loud noise made by the dogs","something bad that might happen to him","a warning given by another animal","the pain he felt in his legs"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"A threat is something bad that might happen; here it is the danger of the dogs catching him."},{"id":"b2u4-e6","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"After months of practice she has great ___ in her own ability.\\"","options":["confidence","consequence","strength","terror"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Confidence is a feeling of certainty about your own ability, which grows with practice."},{"id":"b2u4-e7","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Please do not ___ your sister while she is studying.\\"","options":["satisfy","disturb","honor","supplement"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"To disturb someone is to interrupt or upset them, which is exactly what you should not do during study."},{"id":"b2u4-e8","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Engineers ___ the cost of the bridge at around two million pounds.\\"","options":["estimated","disturbed","supplemented","honored"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To estimate is to make a guess about an amount, which is what engineers do before building begins."},{"id":"b2u4-e9","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"This plant is very ___ to cold and must be brought indoors.\\"","options":["pale","narrow","sensitive","rough"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"A sensitive plant is easily harmed, which is why it must be brought indoors in cold weather."},{"id":"b2u4-e10","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"He sat in the ___ of a large tree to escape the sun.\\"","options":["shade","chest","strength","threat"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Shade is the dark area made when something blocks the sun, so sitting in it keeps you out of the heat."}]},"5":{"id":"b2-u5","book":2,"unit":5,"title":"The Last Hundred Metres","level":"B2","topic":"A mountaineering account","generatedAt":"2026-09-23","wordCount":382,"paragraphs":["On the morning of 29 May, two climbers left their tent at 8,500 metres. They had slept badly, and the [[thick]] down jackets [[wrap|wrapped]] around them could not stop the cold from reaching their bones. Below them the valley was [[silent]]; above them the summit looked impossibly far away.","The [[senior]] of the two, Dawa, had climbed in these mountains since he was a boy, and his father had done the same before him. His [[ancestor|ancestors]] had lived in the valley below for generations, and not one of them had ever stood on the summit. His father, and every [[relative]] he could name, had been born within sight of the mountain. In his pocket he carried a photograph of that father, [[wrap|wrapped]] in cloth, and he had promised himself that if he reached the top he would leave it there.","The route above the camp was [[incredible]] in its steepness. They moved one step at a time, their [[boot|boots]] biting into the ice, and at every [[angle]] the mountain offered a new way to fall. After three hours they reached a [[border]] of rock and snow where the wind dropped, and for the first time they could hear each other breathe. In his pocket the small [[frame]] that held the photograph pressed against his chest.","The last hundred metres took longer than the whole of the day before. Twice Hallam stopped and thought about turning back; twice Dawa waited, saying nothing, until he [[proceed|proceeded]]. Above them the sky was a [[pure]], glassy blue, and the snow was so dry that it made no sound under their feet.","They stood on the top for fifteen minutes. There was no [[heaven]] waiting for them, no chorus of any kind — only wind, and a view that [[surround|surrounded]] them on every side and seemed to [[sink]] away into the distance. Dawa took out the photograph and [[praise|praised]] his father aloud, though the wind carried the words off before anyone could hear them.","Later, at base camp, the others came out of their tents to [[congratulate]] them, and somebody asked whether the [[legend|legends]] about the mountain were true. Dawa said that the mountain was simply a mountain, and that the only [[superior]] thing about it was its size. He was not being modest. He was tired."],"exercises":[{"id":"b2u5-e1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"Where did the two climbers begin the day described in the passage?","options":["At base camp, below the glacier","At a tent at 8,500 metres","At the border of rock and snow","On the summit, after a night there"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 1 says they left their tent at 8,500 metres on the morning of 29 May."},{"id":"b2u5-e2","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What had Dawa promised himself?","options":["To turn back if the weather changed","To leave his father\'s photograph on the summit","To bring his father with him on the climb","To reach the top before midday"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 2 says he carried a photograph of his father wrapped in cloth and had promised himself that if he reached the top he would leave it there."},{"id":"b2u5-e3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"Why did Hallam stop twice during the last hundred metres?","options":["He had injured his foot on the ice","He was waiting for the wind to drop","He was thinking about turning back","He wanted to take photographs of the view"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"Paragraph 4 says twice Hallam stopped and thought about turning back, and twice Dawa waited until he proceeded."},{"id":"b2u5-e4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"How did Dawa respond when asked about the legends of the mountain?","options":["He said the mountain was simply a mountain","He said the legends were entirely true","He refused to answer and went to sleep","He praised the mountain as a superior place"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Paragraph 6 says he replied that the mountain was simply a mountain, and that the only superior thing about it was its size."},{"id":"b2u5-e5","type":"multiple-choice","question":"In paragraph 4, \\"the sky was a pure, glassy blue\\" — pure most nearly means…","options":["extremely cold","clear and unspoiled","bright enough to hurt the eyes","unusual for that time of year"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"A pure sky is one that is completely clear, with nothing in it to spoil the colour."},{"id":"b2u5-e6","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The whole family came out to ___ her on winning the prize.\\"","options":["praise","congratulate","proceed","surround"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"To congratulate someone is to tell them you are happy about their success."},{"id":"b2u5-e7","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Her father is a ___ officer with thirty years of service.\\"","options":["silent","senior","superior","thick"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"A senior person is older or more experienced, which fits thirty years of service."},{"id":"b2u5-e8","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The boat began to ___ slowly beneath the waves.\\"","options":["sink","wrap","praise","proceed"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To sink is to go down slowly under water, which is what the boat did."},{"id":"b2u5-e9","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Please ___ the parcel in brown paper before you post it.\\"","options":["surround","praise","wrap","sink"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"To wrap is to cover something on all sides, as you do with a parcel before posting it."},{"id":"b2u5-e10","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"He wore ___ woollen socks to keep his feet warm in the snow.\\"","options":["silent","pure","senior","thick"],"answerIndex":3,"explanation":"A thick thing is wide and solid; thick socks keep the feet warmer than thin ones."}]},"6":{"id":"b2-u6","book":2,"unit":6,"title":"Ten Quiet Minutes","level":"B2","topic":"Everyday habits and reducing stress","generatedAt":"2026-09-23","wordCount":371,"paragraphs":["Most people who say they are too [[busy]] to rest are telling the truth as they see it. The problem is not that they have no time; it is that the time they do have is filled so completely that they no longer notice it. [[stress|Stress]] of this kind builds quietly, and by the time it becomes obvious the habits that caused it already feel [[normal]].","One simple experiment is to sit still for ten minutes. Not to [[sleep]], not to [[study]], not to [[write]] a list of things that still need doing — simply to sit. At first this feels strange, and the mind [[automatically]] produces reasons why it is a waste of time. You [[can]] ignore them. You [[can]] [[also]] decide to keep going, and that decision is the whole exercise. The first two minutes are the hardest, and it is worth knowing that in advance.","The point of it is to [[clear]] the mind, or at least to stop adding to it. When you [[close]] the door and leave your phone in another room, you give your attention nothing to hold on to, and it gradually settles. If a thought arrives, you do not have to follow it. Some people find it helps to [[listen]] to [[music]] and do nothing else — not as a background to work, but as the only thing happening.","Talking helps too, provided it is real talking. To [[discuss]] a problem with a friend, or simply to [[talk]] about how the week has gone, is often more useful than thinking about it alone. It also matters how you [[feel]] when you [[meet]] somebody: if you arrive already planning your reply, you are not really listening. [[quiet|Quiet]] company is better than lively company when you are tired.","None of this is complicated and none of it requires equipment. What it requires is repetition, because the ability to [[relax]] is a skill like any other, and skills improve only with practice. Ten minutes that you actually take is worth more than an hour that you merely intend to take. People who practise it do not become lazy. They [[work]] better, they make fewer mistakes, and they recover faster from the days when everything goes wrong."],"exercises":[{"id":"b2u6-e1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"According to the passage, why do busy people fail to rest?","options":["They believe that rest is a waste of time","Their time is so completely filled that they stop noticing it","They have no free time at all","They prefer the company of lively people"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 1 says the problem is not a lack of time but that the time they have is filled so completely that they no longer notice it."},{"id":"b2u6-e2","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What does the writer suggest doing for ten minutes?","options":["Writing a list of things that need doing","Listening to music while working","Sitting still and doing nothing else","Sleeping in the middle of the day"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"Paragraph 2 recommends sitting still for ten minutes, and specifically says this does not mean sleeping, studying or writing a list."},{"id":"b2u6-e3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What is the purpose of the exercise described?","options":["To clear the mind, or at least to stop adding to it","To prove that the mind cannot be controlled","To make people more productive at work","To help people sleep better at night"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Paragraph 3 states that the point of the exercise is to clear the mind, or at least to stop adding to it."},{"id":"b2u6-e4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What does the writer say about listening to music?","options":["It should always be combined with work","It is the best way to fall asleep","It works better as the only thing happening than as a background","It should be avoided when you are tired"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"Paragraph 3 says some people find it helps to listen to music and do nothing else — not as a background to work, but as the only thing happening."},{"id":"b2u6-e5","type":"multiple-choice","question":"In paragraph 2, \\"the mind automatically produces reasons\\" — automatically most nearly means…","options":["with the help of a machine","without thinking or planning","in a slow and careful way","because somebody has asked it to"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"If something happens automatically it happens without deliberate thought, which is the point being made about the excuses the mind produces."},{"id":"b2u6-e6","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"I need to ___ my desk before I can start any new work.\\"","options":["close","clear","discuss","relax"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"To clear a place is to remove everything from it, which is what you do to a desk before starting work."},{"id":"b2u6-e7","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Could you ___ the window? It is very cold in here.\\"","options":["close","clear","meet","talk"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To close something is to shut it or cover an opening, which is what you do to a window to keep the cold out."},{"id":"b2u6-e8","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"We should ___ the plan with the whole team before Friday.\\"","options":["listen","feel","discuss","sleep"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"To discuss something is to talk about it with other people, which is what a team does with a plan."},{"id":"b2u6-e9","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"A good manager takes the time to ___ to the staff.\\"","options":["listen","write","work","study"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To listen is to pay attention to what you can hear, which is what a manager does when staff speak."},{"id":"b2u6-e10","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"After a long week she likes to ___ with a hot bath.\\"","options":["work","relax","study","discuss"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"To relax is to rest or do something enjoyable, which fits a hot bath at the end of a long week."}]},"7":{"id":"b2-u7","book":2,"unit":7,"title":"Field Notes from a Small Island","level":"B2","topic":"Scientific fieldwork and a colony of birds","generatedAt":"2026-09-23","wordCount":386,"paragraphs":["We [[depart|departed]] at four in the morning, which is the only sensible hour for the [[route]] we had chosen. The road climbs for two hours and then simply stops, and from there the island is reached on foot. My [[colleague]] Ana slept for most of the journey. I watched the light come up and tried to remember why I had agreed to come.","The [[basis]] of our project is simple. We are counting a bird that nests only on this island, in a [[colony]] of a few hundred pairs. Nobody knows whether the numbers are falling or rising, and without that [[factual]] information nobody can decide what to do about it. Our [[mission]] is to spend six weeks counting, and to come back with data that somebody else can check.","Ana is a [[scholar]] of [[biology]] who has worked [[overseas]] for most of her career, and she has an [[occupation]] that sounds glamorous and is not. Her days consist of sitting behind a telescope, writing numbers, and repairing a [[cage]] that a previous team left behind. [[nevertheless|Nevertheless]] she says it is the best work she has ever done, and I believe her.","What [[fascinate|fascinates]] me is how little the island has changed. There are [[ruins]] on the ridge above the bay — the walls of a house, a well, the outline of a garden — and the birds nest in them without any regard for what they once were. The island also has a [[volcano]], long asleep, whose slopes are the reason the soil is so rich. Some of the most [[significant]] discoveries here have been made by accident, usually by somebody who was looking for something else.","There is a [[debate]] among the older researchers about whether the colony is in decline. Some argue that the counts made in the 1980s were unreliable; others insist the birds have simply moved to another part of the island. The argument is not easy to settle, and it can [[depress]] a young researcher who wants a clear answer. I have learned not to [[persuade]] anyone of anything before the numbers are in.","We leave in ten days. I will miss the noise of the colony at dusk, and the cold, and the long walk back in the dark. I will also be glad to sleep in a bed."],"exercises":[{"id":"b2u7-e1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"How do the researchers reach the island?","options":["By boat from the nearest harbour","On foot, after the road comes to an end","By helicopter from the mainland","Along a track that runs beside the volcano"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 1 says the road climbs for two hours and then simply stops, and from there the island is reached on foot."},{"id":"b2u7-e2","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What is the team\'s mission?","options":["To repair the ruins on the ridge","To count a bird that nests only on the island","To study the volcano on the island","To build a permanent research station"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 2 states that their mission is to spend six weeks counting a bird that nests only on this island."},{"id":"b2u7-e3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What does the writer say about Ana\'s occupation?","options":["It sounds glamorous but is not","It involves a great deal of travelling","It is better paid than most academic work","It requires her to work mostly at night"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Paragraph 3 says she has an occupation that sounds glamorous and is not, describing days of sitting behind a telescope and writing numbers."},{"id":"b2u7-e4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"Why is the island\'s soil so rich?","options":["Because of the birds that nest there","Because of the ruins on the ridge","Because of the slopes of a sleeping volcano","Because the island is never farmed"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"Paragraph 4 explains that the island has a volcano, long asleep, whose slopes are the reason the soil is so rich."},{"id":"b2u7-e5","type":"multiple-choice","question":"In paragraph 5, \\"the counts made in the 1980s were unreliable\\" — unreliable most nearly means…","options":["carried out too quickly","not to be trusted as accurate","made by people from another country","impossible to find in the records"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"If a count is unreliable it cannot be trusted to be accurate, which is why the researchers disagree about whether the colony is declining."},{"id":"b2u7-e6","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"She works ___ for a company based in another country.\\"","options":["overseas","nevertheless","factual","significant"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Overseas means in another country, across an ocean, which matches a company based abroad."},{"id":"b2u7-e7","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The team will ___ at dawn and reach the camp by noon.\\"","options":["persuade","depart","debate","fascinate"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"To depart is to leave a place in order to go somewhere else, which fits setting off at dawn."},{"id":"b2u7-e8","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"My ___ and I share a small office on the third floor.\\"","options":["colleague","scholar","colony","mission"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"A colleague is somebody you work with, so sharing an office fits exactly."},{"id":"b2u7-e9","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"We spent the afternoon walking among the ___ of an ancient city.\\"","options":["cages","colonies","ruins","routes"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"Ruins are the remains of old buildings that are no longer used, which is what you walk among at an ancient site."},{"id":"b2u7-e10","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"There was a ___ improvement in her health after the operation.\\"","options":["factual","significant","overseas","senior"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"A significant improvement is an important or noticeable one, which is what the sentence describes."}]},"8":{"id":"b2-u8","book":2,"unit":8,"title":"The Shortest Way Down","level":"B2","topic":"A walk in the hills and a question of honesty","generatedAt":"2026-09-23","wordCount":379,"paragraphs":["The [[path]] down to the [[valley]] is eight kilometres long and takes about four hours. Everyone in the group had been given the same [[instructions]] at breakfast, and everyone had agreed to meet at the bridge by five, which was our agreed [[destination]]. What happened between those two moments is still argued about whenever the six of us get together. It is not a difficult walk, but it is a long one, and the weather in the valley changes faster than any forecast admits.","Four of us walked together. We [[concentrate|concentrated]] on the ground in front of us, because the [[edge]] of the path drops away sharply and the stones are loose. The [[bush|bushes]] on either side were high enough to block the view, so we could not tell whether anybody was ahead of us or behind.","We reached the bridge at ten past five, [[confident]] that we had done well. Daniel was already there, sitting in the [[shadow]] of a tree with his boots off, and he had been there, he said, for an hour. This was a [[considerable]] claim. To be an hour ahead of us he would have had to run, and Daniel is not [[capable]] of running for four hours downhill.","Nobody said anything at first. Then Maria, who is never afraid to [[convey]] an unpopular opinion, asked him which way he had come. Daniel described a [[broad]] track that none of us had seen. We began to [[suspect]] that he had taken a shortcut through the fields, and that he had done so deliberately.","The [[definite]] proof came later, when we compared photographs. In one of them, taken from a high point, there is a clear line across the meadow where somebody had walked through the grass. To [[conclude]] from this that Daniel [[cheat|cheated]] may be going too far; he might simply have lost his way and been too proud to admit it. But he had taken no [[delight]] in the walk and no interest in the view, and when the subject came up again he changed it.","Some people [[resort]] to winning because they cannot imagine [[succeed|succeeding]] in any other way. That is a sadder thought than a lost afternoon, and it is the reason none of us has ever asked him directly."],"exercises":[{"id":"b2u8-e1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What had the group agreed at breakfast?","options":["To walk in pairs rather than as a group","To follow the same instructions and meet at the bridge by five","To take a shortcut through the fields if it rained","To photograph the whole route for the record"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 1 says everyone had been given the same instructions and had agreed to meet at the bridge by five."},{"id":"b2u8-e2","type":"multiple-choice","question":"Why did the walkers concentrate on the ground in front of them?","options":["The view was hidden by high bushes","They were trying to walk as quickly as possible","The edge of the path drops away and the stones are loose","They were looking for the line through the grass"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"Paragraph 2 explains that the edge of the path drops away sharply and the stones are loose, so they had to watch where they were stepping."},{"id":"b2u8-e3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"Why was Daniel\'s claim difficult to believe?","options":["He was not carrying a watch","The bridge was not where they had agreed to meet","He is not capable of running four hours downhill","He had arrived at the bridge after the others"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"Paragraph 3 says that to be an hour ahead he would have had to run, and Daniel is not capable of running for four hours downhill."},{"id":"b2u8-e4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What did the photographs show?","options":["Daniel running along a broad track","A clear line through the grass in the meadow","The group resting beside a high point","That the bridge had been reached at ten past five"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 5 says one photograph, taken from a high point, shows a clear line across the meadow where somebody had walked through the grass."},{"id":"b2u8-e5","type":"multiple-choice","question":"In paragraph 3, \\"This was a considerable claim\\" — considerable most nearly means…","options":["large in extent or significance","carefully considered before being made","polite and well expressed","difficult to hear in the wind"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"The claim of having been at the bridge for an hour was a big one, given that the others had just arrived."},{"id":"b2u8-e6","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"She was ___ that she would pass the exam without any difficulty.\\"","options":["capable","confident","definite","considerable"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Confident people believe they can do something without failing, which matches expecting to pass easily."},{"id":"b2u8-e7","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The ___ at the edge of the field is full of berries.\\"","options":["bush","shadow","path","valley"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"A bush is a woody plant smaller than a tree, and berries commonly grow on one."},{"id":"b2u8-e8","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Always read the ___ before you use the machine.\\"","options":["instructions","destinations","delights","shadows"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"A set of instructions explains how to do something, which is what you read before using equipment."},{"id":"b2u8-e9","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"It is not fair to ___ when the other players are following the rules.\\"","options":["succeed","cheat","convey","suspect"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"To cheat is to be dishonest in order to win, which is unfair to players who keep to the rules."},{"id":"b2u8-e10","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"From the evidence, we can ___ that the fire started in the kitchen.\\"","options":["resort","convey","conclude","concentrate"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"To conclude is to reach a logical answer by looking at the evidence."}]},"9":{"id":"b2-u9","book":2,"unit":9,"title":"Twenty Arms and No Brain","level":"B2","topic":"The starfish and life on the ocean floor","generatedAt":"2026-09-23","wordCount":373,"paragraphs":["On a warm evening, if you [[step]] carefully over the [[rock|rocks]] where the [[beach]] meets the water, you may see something the size of a plate, orange or purple, pressed [[against]] the stone. It looks like a decoration. It is a starfish, and it is hunting. Nothing about it suggests an animal that can open a shell, which is exactly why it is worth looking at closely. Most people walk past without seeing it at all.","A starfish usually has five arms, though a few species have twenty or more. It has no brain and no blood, and it moves so slowly that a single [[wave]] can carry it back to where it started. Nevertheless it is one of the most effective hunters on the [[ocean]] floor, because it can open a shell that almost nothing else can open. Its only real sense is a kind of chemical [[taste]], which tells it whether the shell in front of it is worth the effort.","If you [[throw]] one back into the water it will [[still]] be alive, and starfish recover from injuries that would kill most animals. Scientists [[discover|discovered]] some years ago that a starfish can grow a whole new arm, and in some cases a whole new body from a single arm. This makes them unusually hard to [[damage]] permanently, and it is one reason they have survived for hundreds of millions of years, through changes that wiped out almost everything else.","That toughness is not always welcome. On certain reefs one species has multiplied until it has eaten almost everything, and divers now go down to [[identify]] and remove them by hand. It is hard work and it is not [[pleasant]]. The aim is to [[prevent]] the reef from being destroyed and to [[save]] the corals that remain. Whether such efforts can [[fix]] the problem in the long term is [[perhaps]] too early to say.","There is also the question of how we feel about them. A starfish cannot [[smile]], and it is difficult to know what [[emotion]], if any, lies behind its slow movement. It has no face to read. Yet visitors to any [[island]] in the region will tell you the same thing: the first one you see, you remember."],"exercises":[{"id":"b2u9-e1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"Where is a starfish most likely to be seen, according to the passage?","options":["Floating on the surface far out at sea","On the rocks where the beach meets the water","Buried in the sand at the top of the beach","Among the corals of the deepest reefs"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 1 describes stepping over the rocks where the beach meets the water and seeing something pressed against the stone."},{"id":"b2u9-e2","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What is surprising about the starfish as a hunter?","options":["It hunts only at night","It moves very slowly yet is extremely effective","It feeds on other starfish","It has no way of sensing its food"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 2 says it moves so slowly that a single wave can carry it back, yet it is one of the most effective hunters on the ocean floor."},{"id":"b2u9-e3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"Why are starfish difficult to damage permanently?","options":["They can grow new arms, and sometimes a whole new body","They hide in rocks where nothing can reach them","They are protected by a hard outer shell","They move too quickly to be caught"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Paragraph 3 explains that a starfish can grow a whole new arm, and in some cases a whole new body from a single arm."},{"id":"b2u9-e4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"Why do divers remove some starfish by hand?","options":["To sell them to visitors on the island","To study how they grow new arms","Because one species has multiplied and eaten almost everything on certain reefs","Because they are dangerous to swimmers"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"Paragraph 4 says that on certain reefs one species has multiplied until it has eaten almost everything, so divers go down to identify and remove them."},{"id":"b2u9-e5","type":"multiple-choice","question":"In paragraph 4, \\"That toughness is not always welcome\\" — toughness most nearly means…","options":["the ability to survive and recover","a rough and uneven surface","a willingness to fight","the strength of a hard shell"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"The word refers back to the starfish\'s ability to recover from injuries and regrow parts of its body."},{"id":"b2u9-e6","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Do not ___ stones at the birds on the water.\\"","options":["step","throw","save","prevent"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"To throw something is to make it go through the air with your hand."},{"id":"b2u9-e7","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"She is ___ waiting for an answer to her letter.\\"","options":["perhaps","pleasant","still","against"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"Still means that a situation keeps going on, so she is continuing to wait."},{"id":"b2u9-e8","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Firefighters worked through the night to ___ the fire from spreading.\\"","options":["prevent","damage","identify","throw"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To prevent something is to stop it from happening, which is what the firefighters were trying to do."},{"id":"b2u9-e9","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Doctors are working to ___ his sight after the accident.\\"","options":["throw","damage","save","fix"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"To save something is to keep it from being hurt or lost, which fits the effort to protect his sight."},{"id":"b2u9-e10","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"He could not hide his ___ when he heard the news.\\"","options":["taste","emotion","rock","island"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"An emotion is how you feel, and a strong feeling is hard to hide."}]},"10":{"id":"b2-u10","book":2,"unit":10,"title":"The Captain Who Was Never Caught","level":"B2","topic":"A coastal legend and what the records show","generatedAt":"2026-09-23","wordCount":386,"paragraphs":["For two hundred years the [[tale]] has been told in almost the same words. A [[giant]] of a man with a beard down to his chest, a [[sword]] in one hand and a pistol in the other, appeared out of the [[fog]] off the coast and took whatever he wanted. He was [[violent]] when it suited him and generous when it did not. Children were told to behave, or he would come for them. The story has been printed in books, repeated in songs, and used for two centuries as a warning to children along that coast.","The records, however, tell a smaller story. The man was of ordinary height, and the crew he led numbered fewer than forty. He was [[extraordinary]] mainly in his sense of timing: he attacked ships in narrow channels, where their size became a disadvantage, and he rarely killed anybody. His [[intention]] was not destruction but profit. A frightened crew, he understood, would hand over its cargo without a fight, and a [[mad]] captain with a reputation was cheaper than a real battle.","He did work hard at the reputation. Witnesses described an [[enormous]] hat, slow-burning candles fixed in his beard, and a manner that made the [[citizen|citizens]] of the port towns unwilling to [[resist]] him. The [[impression]] he left was of a man who could not be reasoned with. It was a performance, and it worked.","In the end, the [[council]] of one town grew tired of losing ships and paid a captain to [[rid]] the coast of him. Officials were quick to [[declare]] the coast safe, although the plan itself was a [[trap]]: a ship that appeared helpless, a shallow channel, and a boarding party hidden below deck. He was captured and put on [[trial]], and the proceedings [[reveal|revealed]] very little that the town did not already know. The [[funeral]] was reported in every newspaper along the coast.","And yet the legend did not end there. A [[tale]] that is told often enough stops needing to be true, and within a century the man had become a character in a story rather than a person. Historians [[ought]] to correct such stories, and mostly they do. But they also know that a plain account of a small-time thief is harder to remember than a [[giant]] with candles in his beard."],"exercises":[{"id":"b2u10-e1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"According to the records, what was the captain\'s crew like?","options":["It numbered fewer than forty men","It was larger than any navy of the time","It consisted entirely of foreign sailors","It was made up of citizens from the port towns"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Paragraph 2 says the crew he led numbered fewer than forty, in contrast to the legend of a giant and his great force."},{"id":"b2u10-e2","type":"multiple-choice","question":"Why did he attack ships in narrow channels?","options":["The fog was thicker there","Larger ships could not use their size to advantage there","There were no towns nearby to send help","It was the quickest route along the coast"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 2 says he attacked in narrow channels where the size of larger ships became a disadvantage."},{"id":"b2u10-e3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"Why did the captain work hard at his reputation?","options":["He wanted the citizens to vote for him","A frightened crew would hand over its cargo without a fight","He was afraid of being caught by the council","He hoped to be remembered in a famous tale"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 2 explains that a frightened crew would surrender its cargo without a fight, and a mad captain with a reputation was cheaper than a real battle."},{"id":"b2u10-e4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What was the plan that finally caught him?","options":["A fleet of warships sent by the council","A ship that appeared helpless, with a boarding party hidden below deck","A reward offered to any citizen who named him","A blockade of every channel along the coast"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 4 describes the trap: a ship that appeared helpless, a shallow channel, and a boarding party hidden below deck."},{"id":"b2u10-e5","type":"multiple-choice","question":"In the final paragraph, \\"A tale that is told often enough stops needing to be true\\" suggests that…","options":["people repeat stories even when the facts do not support them","stories are only worth telling if they are invented","the captain eventually became an honest man","newspapers of the time could not be trusted at all"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"The sentence explains why the legend survived: the story was repeated so often that accuracy stopped mattering."},{"id":"b2u10-e6","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The committee will ___ the winner tomorrow morning.\\"","options":["declare","resist","reveal","rid"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To declare is to say something officially, which is what a committee does when it announces a winner."},{"id":"b2u10-e7","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The town ___ meets on the first Monday of every month.\\"","options":["trial","council","funeral","tale"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"A council is a group of people who run a town, and such a group holds regular meetings."},{"id":"b2u10-e8","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"She made a strong ___ on everyone she met at the interview.\\"","options":["intention","impression","fog","sword"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"An impression is the way somebody thinks about you, and a strong impression is one that lasts."},{"id":"b2u10-e9","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"He could not ___ the temptation to open the letter.\\"","options":["resist","declare","trap","rid"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To resist something is to fight against it, so being unable to resist temptation means giving in to it."},{"id":"b2u10-e10","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"We ___ to leave early if we want to avoid the traffic.\\"","options":["resist","declare","ought","rid"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"Ought to is used when something is the right thing to do, which fits the sensible plan of leaving early."}]},"11":{"id":"b2-u11","book":2,"unit":11,"title":"The Hall of Bones","level":"B2","topic":"A school trip to a museum","generatedAt":"2026-09-23","wordCount":440,"paragraphs":["Our [[principal]] announced in assembly that Year 10 would spend a Friday at the city museum. She read the details from a sheet of paper in a flat, careful voice: the [[admission]] fee had been paid by the parents\' association, so we needed only a packed lunch and a coat. The museum had been open since 1897, and none of us had ever been inside it. \\"This is not a day off,\\" she said, although nobody in the hall believed her.","I ended up in a [[row]] of eight students behind Mr Alvarez, who taught [[geography]] and walked as though the floor might give way beneath him. The first room held a [[dinosaur]] skeleton so large that its head hung above the doorway, and we all stopped talking at once. [[despite|Despite]] the fact that the creature had been dead for millions of years, it still looked capable of stepping on us.","[[exhibit|Exhibits]] like that one had made the museum famous, although its [[fame]] owed more to a film than to science. In the next hall a guide explained that the week\'s [[forecast]] had almost cancelled our visit. \\"We were worried about rain,\\" she said, \\"because a [[wet]] floor is dangerous around the fossils.\\" She spoke [[lightly]] about it, but I noticed that she kept one hand on the rope the whole time.","My favourite thing was a small case of drawings on a [[shelf]] behind glass. A Victorian [[genius]] had sketched the bones by candlelight, and the sketches were [[super]] — livelier, honestly, than any photograph. There was a queue to look at them, which surprised me. The labels beside them said that he had studied [[astronomy]] first, then [[chemistry]], before turning to fossils, and that he had never been paid for any of it.","At lunch somebody blamed the teachers for the cold sandwiches, and the [[blame]] spread along the bench within minutes. Mr Alvarez, who was [[gentle]] by nature and rarely raised his voice, did not [[interfere]]. He sat with his back against a pillar and let us argue, and I am almost certain that he was smiling. In [[spite]] of our complaints, he had chosen the museum himself, and he knew exactly which room we would remember.","On the bus home the rain finally arrived, hard and sudden, and the windows turned to steam. Nobody minded. For an hour we were quiet, and the quiet felt deliberate, as though each of us were checking that the others had seen the same thing. Mr Alvarez was asleep before we reached the motorway. It was still there in my head that night, enormous and patient, waiting behind the glass."],"exercises":[{"id":"b2u11-e1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What had the parents\' association paid for?","options":["The admission fee","The coach that took them there","Lunch for every student","A guide to show them round"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Paragraph 1 states that the admission fee had been paid by the parents\' association, so the students only needed a packed lunch and a coat."},{"id":"b2u11-e2","type":"multiple-choice","question":"How did the students react to the dinosaur skeleton?","options":["They took photographs of it","They stopped talking all at once","They asked the guide how old it was","They argued about whether it was real"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 2 says that when they saw the skeleton, whose head hung above the doorway, they all stopped talking at once."},{"id":"b2u11-e3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"According to the guide, why was rain a problem?","options":["It would have forced the museum to close early","The roof above the fossil hall leaked","A wet floor is dangerous around the fossils","The students would have had nowhere to eat lunch"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"Paragraph 3 quotes the guide saying that a wet floor is dangerous around the fossils, which is why the forecast had nearly cancelled the trip."},{"id":"b2u11-e4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What does the narrator suggest about Mr Alvarez in paragraph 5?","options":["He was annoyed by the students\' complaints","He regretted choosing the museum","He was quietly amused rather than upset","He wanted the students to stop arguing immediately"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"He did not interfere, let them argue, and was almost certainly smiling — so he seems quietly amused. The narrator adds that he had chosen the museum himself and knew which room they would remember."},{"id":"b2u11-e5","type":"multiple-choice","question":"As used in paragraph 3, the word \\"lightly\\" most nearly means…","options":["in a quiet voice","without much force","without seriousness","at a quick pace"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"She spoke lightly about the danger of a wet floor, yet kept her hand on the rope — so she treated the subject casually, without seriousness."},{"id":"b2u11-e6","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The manager refused to ___ anyone for the mistake until all the facts were clear.\\"","options":["blame","exhibit","interfere","forecast"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To blame someone is to say they did something bad. The manager would not say who was responsible until the facts were known."},{"id":"b2u11-e7","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"She is a ___ person who would never raise her voice at a child.\\"","options":["wet","gentle","super","stiff"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Someone gentle is kind and calm, which fits a person who never raises her voice at a child."},{"id":"b2u11-e8","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The evening news gave a ___ for the following week: cold and wet.\\"","options":["shelf","fame","forecast","row"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"A forecast is an idea about what the weather will be like in the future, which is exactly what the news gave."},{"id":"b2u11-e9","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"He put the jar back on the top ___ and closed the cupboard door.\\"","options":["row","shelf","spite","blame"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"A shelf is a flat place on a wall where things are kept, so a jar goes back on a shelf."},{"id":"b2u11-e10","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"___ the heavy rain, the match went ahead exactly as planned.\\"","options":["Despite","Spite","Lightly","Super"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Despite introduces a difference from what is expected: rain would normally stop a match, but it did not."}]},"12":{"id":"b2-u12","book":2,"unit":12,"title":"The Bakery on Cardamom Street","level":"B2","topic":"Small business and food","generatedAt":"2026-09-23","wordCount":392,"paragraphs":["When Mira took over the bakery on Cardamom Street, the neighbours warned her that she would never [[afford]] the rent. The previous owner had left behind a [[debt]] that nobody wanted to discuss, a broken oven, and a kitchen that was little more than a [[mess]] of flour, cracked tiles and one rusting [[metal]] shelf. She signed the papers anyway.","She did not [[oppose]] the warnings, but she did not accept them either. Instead she spent a month learning the old recipes by heart. She would [[bake]] from four in the morning, weighing every [[ingredient]] twice, and she [[insist|insisted]] that the ovens be cleaned before and after each batch. The regulars noticed. A [[generous]] customer lent her a small [[fund]] to replace the counter, and she repaid it within a year.","Not everyone was kind. A food writer who had never eaten there described her bread as worthy but dull, and for a week she was so [[passive]] that she barely spoke to anyone. Then she did something unexpected. She added a spoonful of coffee to the dough and a small [[quantity]] of dark honey, and the loaves began to disappear before ten o\'clock.","She also learned to [[monitor]] her costs. Rather than [[convert]] the old storeroom into a second dining room, she turned it into a shop selling [[bean|beans]], oil and [[candle|candles]], a decision that [[decrease|decreased]] her waste and doubled her income. \\"It was nobody\'s [[fault]] but mine,\\" she says of the early years, \\"that I believed a good recipe was enough.\\"","The turning point involved a supplier who had been quietly [[abuse|abusing]] his workers and mixing cheap flour into the sacks he delivered. When Mira discovered what he was doing, she did not [[sue]] him, although she could have. She refused his next delivery, wrote a short letter to the other bakeries on the street, and let the evidence do the rest. Within a month he had no customers left in the district.","Today the bakery employs six people and opens at seven. Mira still arrives first, still tastes the dough with a fingertip, and still refuses to sell anything she would not serve at her own table. She keeps the old flour scoop above the door as a reminder of how little she started with. \\"A recipe is a promise,\\" she says. \\"You can break it, but people remember.\\""],"exercises":[{"id":"b2u12-e1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What was Mira warned about before she took over the bakery?","options":["That the neighbours would not welcome her","That she would never be able to afford the rent","That the recipes had been lost","That no customers came to that street"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 1 says the neighbours warned her that she would never afford the rent."},{"id":"b2u12-e2","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What did the food writer\'s criticism lead Mira to do?","options":["Close the bakery for a week","Change the recipe by adding coffee and honey","Write an angry letter to the newspaper","Reduce the number of loaves she baked"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 3 describes how she became passive for a week and then added a spoonful of coffee and some dark honey to the dough."},{"id":"b2u12-e3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What was the effect of turning the storeroom into a shop?","options":["It reduced waste and doubled her income","It brought more customers into the dining room","It allowed her to raise the price of bread","It gave her somewhere to store flour"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Paragraph 4 states that this decision decreased her waste and doubled her income."},{"id":"b2u12-e4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"Why did Mira not take the supplier to court?","options":["She could not prove what he had done","She had no money for lawyers","She chose other means, and he soon lost his customers","The other bakeries asked her not to"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"Paragraph 5 says she did not sue him although she could have; instead she refused his delivery and wrote to the other bakeries, and within a month he had no customers left."},{"id":"b2u12-e5","type":"multiple-choice","question":"As used in paragraph 3, the word \\"passive\\" most nearly means…","options":["unwilling to take action","unusually rude to customers","careful about spending money","slow at baking bread"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"She was so passive that she barely spoke — that is, she did not act or respond, which matches a person who does not take action."},{"id":"b2u12-e6","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The recipe asks for only one unusual ___: a spoonful of vinegar.\\"","options":["fund","ingredient","debt","fault"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"An ingredient is something that forms part of a dish, so an unusual one would be listed in a recipe."},{"id":"b2u12-e7","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Please ___ the temperature of the oven every twenty minutes.\\"","options":["monitor","bake","oppose","convert"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To monitor something is to watch it closely and regularly, which is what checking an oven temperature every twenty minutes means."},{"id":"b2u12-e8","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"She left them a ___ sum of money in her will, far more than anyone expected.\\"","options":["passive","generous","metal","wet"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"A generous person likes to give, so a generous sum is a large and freely given one."},{"id":"b2u12-e9","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The landlord will ___ the garage into a small office next spring.\\"","options":["convert","insist","sue","decrease"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To convert something is to change it into something else, which is exactly what turning a garage into an office involves."},{"id":"b2u12-e10","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The factory had to ___ its output after orders fell.\\"","options":["decrease","afford","bake","oppose"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To decrease something is to make it less than it was, so output would be reduced when orders fall."}]},"13":{"id":"b2-u13","book":2,"unit":13,"title":"The Ridge Above Valmore","level":"B2","topic":"Mountains and rescue","generatedAt":"2026-09-23","wordCount":416,"paragraphs":["The [[initial]] plan was simple. Luca, a mountain guide with twenty years of experience, would lead four climbers to the [[peak]] above Valmore and be back before dark. He had walked the route twice that month and the weather looked stable. The group had booked in March, when the snow was still deep, and none of them had climbed above three thousand metres before. What Luca had not checked was whether his clients had brought [[adequate]] clothing.","By mid-afternoon the wind had turned [[intense]], and one of the climbers, a [[tiny]] woman in a thin jacket, began to [[spin]] slowly on the spot, unable to feel her fingers. Luca did not [[quit]]. He [[lend|lent]] her his spare gloves, tied the whole group together with a single rope, and started down. \\"We are going down now,\\" he said, and nobody argued.","That was when the [[emergency]] began in earnest. A rockfall had blocked the main path, and there was no [[guarantee]] that the alternative route was safe. The path they needed lay somewhere below the ridge, and cloud had swallowed it completely. Luca had to [[consult]] a map he had barely opened in years, and for a moment his [[anxiety]] was visible to everyone behind him.","What saved them was [[fortune]] as much as skill: a [[potential]] route that a shepherd had described years earlier, and a radio signal just strong enough for one short message. There was no [[proof]] that the path would hold, but they took it anyway, and by nine o\'clock they could see the lights of the valley. It was the only message they sent, and it was enough.","The rescue team that met them at the road was small — not an [[army]], just six volunteers with torches and flasks of tea — but it was enough. One of them, a retired [[tutor]] who had taught half the village, wrapped the climbers in blankets and asked no questions. Luca felt no [[pride]] that night; he felt only a cold, enormous tiredness. In the morning he apologised to each of them personally, which none of them had expected.","The mountain, he said afterwards, does not care how many [[billion]] people have climbed it. Every ascent begins again from nothing, and every climber is a beginner at the top. On the wall of his workshop he [[carve|carved]] a single line: count your layers, not your summits. He kept the rope that had held them together, coiled on a shelf, and never used it again."],"exercises":[{"id":"b2u13-e1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What was Luca\'s mistake before the climb?","options":["He chose a route he had never walked","He did not check whether his clients had suitable clothing","He set off too late in the day","He ignored the weather forecast completely"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 1 says he had walked the route twice and checked the weather, but had not checked whether his clients had brought adequate clothing."},{"id":"b2u13-e2","type":"multiple-choice","question":"Why did the group have to leave the main path?","options":["A rockfall had blocked it","It was too steep for the weakest climber","The map showed it led nowhere","The shepherd had told them to avoid it"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Paragraph 3 says a rockfall had blocked the main path and there was no guarantee the alternative was safe."},{"id":"b2u13-e3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What does the writer suggest was most important in saving the group?","options":["The strength of the radio signal alone","A combination of luck and skill","The experience of the four climbers","The blankets brought by the volunteers"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 4 opens by saying that what saved them was fortune as much as skill, and then lists the shepherd\'s route and the one radio message."},{"id":"b2u13-e4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"How did Luca feel when he reached the road?","options":["Proud of how he had handled the descent","Angry with his clients","Exhausted rather than proud","Relieved that nobody had been hurt but eager to climb again"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"Paragraph 5 says he felt no pride that night, only a cold, enormous tiredness."},{"id":"b2u13-e5","type":"multiple-choice","question":"As used in paragraph 3, the word \\"consult\\" most nearly means…","options":["to ask someone for help or information","to fold something carefully","to draw a new map","to abandon a plan"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Luca consulted a map — he turned to it for information he needed, which matches asking for help or information."},{"id":"b2u13-e6","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The room was ___ — you could not have fitted a second chair in it.\\"","options":["tiny","intense","potential","adequate"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"A tiny thing is very small, which fits a room with no space for another chair."},{"id":"b2u13-e7","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Could you ___ me your umbrella until tomorrow evening?\\"","options":["quit","lend","spin","carve"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"To lend something is to give it to someone for a short time, which is exactly what is being asked here."},{"id":"b2u13-e8","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The doctor asked whether the pain was mild or ___ enough to keep her awake.\\"","options":["initial","intense","tiny","adequate"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"An intense feeling is very strong, so intense pain could keep someone awake."},{"id":"b2u13-e9","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"His ___ of the situation was clear from the way he kept checking the door.\\"","options":["pride","proof","anxiety","fortune"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"Anxiety is a feeling of worry and fear, which explains repeatedly checking the door."},{"id":"b2u13-e10","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The shop promises to ___ that every repair is finished within three days.\\"","options":["guarantee","consult","quit","interfere"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To guarantee is to promise that something will happen, so a shop can guarantee a repair time."}]},"14":{"id":"b2-u14","book":2,"unit":14,"title":"The Girl Who Measured Light","level":"B2","topic":"Science and education","generatedAt":"2026-09-23","wordCount":398,"paragraphs":["Nobody expected Nadia to become a physicist. At school she sat at the back in a slightly too-large [[uniform]] and read books that were not on the syllabus. Her teachers thought she was lazy. It was [[apparent]] to them that she would never pass the examinations, and they told her so, kindly, more than once. Her report cards were unremarkable, and she kept every one of them.","What they could not see was that she was teaching herself [[physics]] from second-hand books. She had a [[talent]] for [[calculate|calculating]] things in her head, and she had [[commit|committed]] herself to a single question: what happens to light when it passes through glass? She wrote that question on the inside cover of every notebook she owned.","Her route to university was not smooth. A [[severe]] illness in her final year [[exhaust|exhausted]] her so completely that she could barely read for a month. She moved into a [[dormitory]] because it was cheaper than a flat, shared a room with three other students, and worked at night in a [[greenhouse]] on the roof, where the warmth kept her awake. The greenhouse belonged to the biology department, and she never told them she was using it.","Her [[thesis]] began as a small idea and grew into something larger. She would [[compose]] experiments out of broken equipment, [[chat|chatting]] with the technicians until they agreed to help her. The apparatus was old, but she knew exactly how much error each instrument could hide. A [[secretary]] in the department, who had watched her struggle, quietly [[remind|reminded]] her of every deadline she was close to missing.","Her interest had begun at home. Her grandmother had gone [[blind]] slowly, and as her [[vision]] faded the family described light to her as though it were a language. \\"You cannot [[ignore]] what you have to explain,\\" Nadia says. \\"I had to make light simple enough for someone who would never see it.\\" Her grandmother died before the first paper was published.","The final [[portion]] of the work took eight years. The result was a set of measurements so precise that other laboratories spent a decade trying to repeat them. The paper she published was only eleven pages long, and it is still cited today. What had once been [[obvious]] to her teachers now looked like something else entirely. \\"They were not wrong,\\" she says. \\"They were simply measuring the wrong thing.\\""],"exercises":[{"id":"b2u14-e1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"Why did Nadia\'s teachers think she would fail?","options":["She was often absent from lessons","She seemed lazy and did not follow the syllabus","She refused to sit the examinations","She spent all her time in the greenhouse"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 1 says she sat at the back reading books that were not on the syllabus, and her teachers thought she was lazy and would never pass."},{"id":"b2u14-e2","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What single question did Nadia set out to answer?","options":["How fast light travels in a vacuum","What happens to light when it passes through glass","Why some people are colour blind","How to make a greenhouse warmer at night"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 2 says she had committed herself to a single question: what happens to light when it passes through glass."},{"id":"b2u14-e3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"Why did Nadia work in the greenhouse at night?","options":["She was employed by the biology department","The warmth there kept her awake","It was the only place with the right equipment","Her roommates asked her to leave the dormitory"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 3 says she worked at night in a greenhouse on the roof, where the warmth kept her awake."},{"id":"b2u14-e4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What does the passage suggest about the origin of Nadia\'s interest in light?","options":["It came from her grandmother\'s blindness","It came from a teacher who encouraged her","It began when she moved into the dormitory","It began with the equipment she found in the greenhouse"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Paragraph 5 explains that her interest had begun at home, where her grandmother had gone blind and the family described light to her."},{"id":"b2u14-e5","type":"multiple-choice","question":"As used in paragraph 3, the word \\"severe\\" most nearly means…","options":["very long-lasting but mild","very bad or serious","difficult to diagnose","easily treated"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"A severe illness left her barely able to read for a month, so it must have been very bad or serious."},{"id":"b2u14-e6","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The nurse took a small ___ of the sample and sent the rest to the laboratory.\\"","options":["portion","talent","thesis","uniform"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"A portion is a part of something, so a small portion of a sample is a small part of it."},{"id":"b2u14-e7","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Can you ___ how much paint we will need for two rooms?\\"","options":["ignore","calculate","chat","compose"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"To calculate is to work out an answer using numbers, which is what estimating the amount of paint requires."},{"id":"b2u14-e8","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Please ___ me to call the dentist before the office closes.\\"","options":["remind","exhaust","compose","ignore"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To remind someone is to tell them to remember to do something, which is what the speaker is asking for."},{"id":"b2u14-e9","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The whole class wore the same ___ on the first day of term.\\"","options":["thesis","uniform","dormitory","portion"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"A uniform is clothing worn by members of the same group, so a whole class would wear the same one."},{"id":"b2u14-e10","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"He has a real ___ for languages and learned three of them before he was twenty.\\"","options":["talent","greenhouse","vision","thesis"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"A talent is a natural ability to do something well, which fits learning three languages quickly."}]},"15":{"id":"b2-u15","book":2,"unit":15,"title":"Twelve Weeks at Bracken Field","level":"B2","topic":"Archaeology and discovery","generatedAt":"2026-09-23","wordCount":398,"paragraphs":["Day one. The [[crew]] arrived at Bracken Field before six, when the [[soil]] was still cold and the [[mud]] from the previous week\'s rain had not yet dried. Our [[boss]], a patient woman named Dr Okonjo, handed out trowels and warned us that we would spend the first fortnight doing almost nothing at all. She had been excavating for thirty years, and she had learned to distrust anyone in a hurry.","She was right. We [[dig|dug]] slowly, in careful squares, lifting the earth in thin [[layer|layers]] and sieving every bucket. Pottery came up first: [[smooth]] brown pieces, some with a [[unique]] pattern pressed into the clay before it was fired. Then, on the ninth day, a small iron blade appeared, still fixed to a wooden [[handle]].","The [[committee]] that funds the site operates under a [[contract]] requiring us to record everything, so we photographed the knife from six angles before we moved it. Dr Okonjo [[devote|devoted]] an entire evening to cleaning it. \\"Someone made this,\\" she said. \\"Someone sharpened it and then lost it, and that was probably the worst day of their week.\\"","The laboratory work was slower than the digging. A colleague who could [[absorb]] the details of forty reports in a week took charge of the samples, and she found traces of grain and of something sweet. We would never recover the [[flavor]] of a meal eaten four thousand years ago, but we could name its ingredients, and that felt like a kind of recovery.","The [[foundation]] that pays for the excavation asked us to share what we had found, so we built a small display for the village hall. A whole [[generation]] of children who had grown up walking over that field came to see what had been underneath it all along. Two of them stayed behind to help us wash the pottery, and one of them is now studying archaeology.","On the last weekend we held a dinner in the field itself, at a long table borrowed from the hall. We had planned to [[dine]] simply, but the local baker arrived with [[double]] the food we had ordered. The surplus went to a [[charitable]] group in the next town, and we [[donate|donated]] our tools to the school. Dr Okonjo said the knife had been lucky after all, not because of what it was worth, but because of what it had made people do."],"exercises":[{"id":"b2u15-e1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What did Dr Okonjo tell the crew on the first day?","options":["That they would find gold within a fortnight","That they would spend the first fortnight doing almost nothing","That the site had already been excavated","That they would work only at night"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 1 says she warned them that they would spend the first fortnight doing almost nothing at all."},{"id":"b2u15-e2","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What was found on the ninth day?","options":["A wooden bowl with a pattern on it","An iron blade still attached to a wooden handle","A set of coins from the village hall","Traces of a wooden table"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 2 says that on the ninth day a small iron blade appeared, still fixed to a wooden handle."},{"id":"b2u15-e3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"Why did the team photograph the knife before moving it?","options":["Because the contract required everything to be recorded","Because the committee wanted to sell the photographs","Because the knife was too fragile to lift","Because Dr Okonjo was not present"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Paragraph 3 explains that the committee operates under a contract requiring them to record everything."},{"id":"b2u15-e4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What does the writer say about the laboratory work?","options":["It was quicker and easier than digging","It was slower, and revealed what the object had been used with","It proved the knife was extremely valuable","It was carried out by Dr Okonjo alone"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 4 begins by saying the laboratory work was slower than the digging and then describes the traces of grain and something sweet found on the samples."},{"id":"b2u15-e5","type":"multiple-choice","question":"As used in paragraph 4, the word \\"absorb\\" most nearly means…","options":["to take in and understand","to soak up a liquid","to copy exactly","to write down slowly"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"The colleague could absorb the details of forty reports in a week — that is, take in and process a great deal of information."},{"id":"b2u15-e6","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Thick ___ stuck to our boots as we crossed the field after the storm.\\"","options":["mud","flavor","handle","layer"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Mud is soft, wet dirt, which is exactly what sticks to boots after heavy rain."},{"id":"b2u15-e7","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The cupboard door has a small brass ___ that has come loose.\\"","options":["handle","foundation","committee","soil"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"A handle is the part of an object that people hold, so a loose brass handle is a natural problem for a cupboard door."},{"id":"b2u15-e8","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Every ___ of the cake was wrapped separately for the fair.\\"","options":["layer","crew","contract","boss"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"A layer is one of several pieces lying on top of each other, so each layer of a cake could be wrapped separately."},{"id":"b2u15-e9","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The school asked parents to ___ old books for the new library.\\"","options":["donate","dine","absorb","devote"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To donate is to give something to a charity or organisation, which fits giving old books to a school library."},{"id":"b2u15-e10","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"This workshop is ___ to any other in the region — nothing else is quite like it.\\"","options":["unique","smooth","double","charitable"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"A unique thing is not like any other, which is exactly what the sentence says about the workshop."}]},"16":{"id":"b2-u16","book":2,"unit":16,"title":"The Map Under the Keys","level":"B2","topic":"Local history and legend","generatedAt":"2026-09-23","wordCount":392,"paragraphs":["For most of a [[century]], people in Adams [[county]] told the same story: somewhere beneath the [[flat]] fields north of the river, a fortune in gold lay [[hidden]]. Nobody could prove it. Nobody could even agree on who was supposed to have buried it. Every decade or so, someone would arrive with a spade and a theory, and every decade or so the field would be left with a new hole in it.","The story began with a [[gentleman]] named Elias Ward, who had arrived in the 1880s with a [[dictionary]], a piano and no visible [[wealth]]. He taught music at a small [[academy]] and gave [[concert|concerts]] in the town [[theater]]. By every account he was entirely ordinary, which is precisely why the story survived. He was said to have been generous with his time and careless with his money.","When Ward died, his piano was sold. Forty years later, a family moving the instrument found a folded [[board]] of paper wedged beneath the keys. The paper was thin, brittle and stained, and the numbers on it made no obvious sense. Along one edge, though, there was a rough drawing of the river and a single word: below.","The paper reached a local [[officer]] of the historical society, who [[publish|published]] a photograph of it in the newspaper. [[maybe|Maybe]] it was a hoax; maybe it was genuine. Either way, the [[process]] of checking it took three years and produced a file thick enough to fill a shelf. Two local historians disagreed publicly about the handwriting, and the argument lasted far longer than the investigation.","The [[original]] document now sits in the county archive. Its ink looks [[ancient]], though tests suggest it is not as old as it pretends. That is the only real [[clue]] anyone has, and it points in two directions at once. Even the archive keeps it in a plain folder, filed under the name of the county rather than the man. As for the gold, it may never have [[exist|existed]] at all.","The story has one more chapter. In 1998 a team used ground-penetrating radar on the field and found nothing whatsoever: no cavity, no metal, no trace. Six years later a metal detector turned up a single coin, worn almost smooth, its date unreadable. Rain [[pound|pounded]] the tents that night and nobody slept. They never found a second one."],"exercises":[{"id":"b2u16-e1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What is the local story about?","options":["A piano that plays by itself","Gold hidden somewhere under the fields","A gentleman who never paid his debts","A river that changed its course"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 1 states that people in the county told the same story about a fortune in gold hidden beneath the flat fields north of the river."},{"id":"b2u16-e2","type":"multiple-choice","question":"How was the paper discovered?","options":["It was found in the county archive","A family moving Ward\'s piano found it beneath the keys","A metal detector located it in the field","It was published in the newspaper first"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 3 says that forty years after Ward died, a family moving the piano found a folded board of paper wedged beneath the keys."},{"id":"b2u16-e3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What does the passage say about the document\'s age?","options":["Tests prove it is genuinely from the 1880s","It is definitely a modern forgery","Its ink looks ancient but tests suggest it is younger","Nobody has ever examined it"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"Paragraph 5 says its ink looks ancient, though tests suggest it is not as old as it pretends."},{"id":"b2u16-e4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What was the result of the 1998 radar survey?","options":["It found a large metal object","It found nothing at all","It found a single coin","It was abandoned because of rain"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 6 says the radar found nothing whatsoever — no cavity, no metal, no trace."},{"id":"b2u16-e5","type":"multiple-choice","question":"As used in paragraph 5, the word \\"original\\" most nearly means…","options":["the first one of its kind, not a copy","unusual and surprising","written by hand","difficult to read"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"The original document sits in the archive — that is, the first and genuine one rather than the newspaper photograph of it."},{"id":"b2u16-e6","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The police found a ___ that finally explained where the missing car had gone.\\"","options":["clue","century","board","wealth"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"A clue is a fact or object that helps solve a mystery, which is exactly what the police needed."},{"id":"b2u16-e7","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Heavy rain ___ the roof all night and nobody in the house could sleep.\\"","options":["pounded","existed","published","greeted"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To pound something is to hit it many times with a lot of force, which describes heavy rain on a roof."},{"id":"b2u16-e8","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The manuscript is kept in a locked room because it is so ___.\\"","options":["ancient","flat","hidden","formal"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"If something is ancient it is very old, which is a good reason to keep a manuscript locked away."},{"id":"b2u16-e9","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The writer could not find a publisher willing to ___ her second novel.\\"","options":["publish","pound","exist","greet"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To publish a book is to get it printed and ready to sell, which is what a publisher does."},{"id":"b2u16-e10","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The keys must have been ___ under the newspapers all along.\\"","options":["hidden","flat","ancient","original"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"If something is hidden it is not easily noticed or found, which explains why the keys were not seen."}]},"17":{"id":"b2-u17","book":2,"unit":17,"title":"The Line That Changed Everything","level":"B2","topic":"Invention and industry","generatedAt":"2026-09-23","wordCount":381,"paragraphs":["In 1908, most people travelled short distances by [[carriage]] or on foot, and long distances by train. A car was an [[expense]] that only the rich could justify, and a [[mechanic]] in a small town might see two in an entire year. Owning one was less a convenience than a public statement about who you were.","A young engineer named Hana Ito had a different [[aim]]. She was [[junior]] to everyone in the workshop and was told, repeatedly, that her ideas would never work. She did not [[criticize]] the people who said so. She simply kept a notebook, and she recorded every failure with the same care as every success.","Her insight was not a new machine but a [[shift]] in how work was organised. The idea itself was not new: it was a [[classic]] method from the pottery towns, where a line of workers passed bowls from hand to hand. Rather than one person building a whole engine, each worker [[attach|attached]] a single part and passed the frame along. The [[labor]] was divided, a bell gave the [[signal]] to move, and the [[prime]] cost of all — time — fell by more than half.","The method was not universally welcomed. Skilled workers complained that their craft had been broken into pieces, and newspapers [[criticize|criticized]] the factories as inhuman. Hana [[confirm|confirmed]] that the work was repetitive; she had done it herself for two years. She also pointed out that a car which had once cost as much as a house now cost about as much as a [[carriage]], and that this, rather than any machine, was the real [[invent|invention]].","Others had tried something similar and failed. What made her system [[differ|different]] was an attention to [[height]] and reach: every part was placed so that a worker could pick it up without bending or stretching. She [[bet]] her savings on the idea, and for once the bet was a good one.","By 1924 the factory was producing a car every ninety seconds. [[commute|Commuting]] by road had stopped being a [[formal]] privilege of the wealthy and had quietly become ordinary. Hana never claimed to have changed the world. In a [[sincere]] interview late in life, she said only that she had tried to make one thing slightly easier for a great many people."],"exercises":[{"id":"b2u17-e1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What does paragraph 1 suggest about cars in 1908?","options":["They were common in small towns","They were an expensive luxury for the wealthy","They were used mainly for long journeys","They were cheaper than carriages"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 1 says a car was an expense only the rich could justify, and a mechanic in a small town might see two in a year."},{"id":"b2u17-e2","type":"multiple-choice","question":"How did Hana respond to people who said her ideas would fail?","options":["She argued with them publicly","She wrote down her failures carefully and continued","She left the workshop immediately","She asked her manager to support her"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 2 says she did not criticise them; she kept a notebook and recorded every failure with the same care as every success."},{"id":"b2u17-e3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What was Hana\'s actual insight?","options":["A completely new kind of engine","A change in the way work was organised","A cheaper kind of metal","A method of training young workers"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 3 states that her insight was not a new machine but a shift in how work was organised."},{"id":"b2u17-e4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What did Hana admit about the new system?","options":["That the work was repetitive","That it could not be used for engines","That it made cars more expensive","That skilled workers were right to leave"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Paragraph 4 says she confirmed that the work was repetitive, having done it herself for two years."},{"id":"b2u17-e5","type":"multiple-choice","question":"As used in paragraph 3, the word \\"prime\\" most nearly means…","options":["the most important","the first of several","the most expensive","the easiest to measure"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"The prime cost of all — time — fell by more than half. Here prime marks the most important cost of the ones being discussed."},{"id":"b2u17-e6","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Please ___ the form to your passport photograph before posting it.\\"","options":["attach","bet","shift","differ"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To attach is to put two things together, which is what you do with a form and a photograph."},{"id":"b2u17-e7","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"She had to ___ her chair closer to the window to read.\\"","options":["shift","invent","confirm","criticize"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To shift is to move into a different place or direction, which fits moving a chair nearer to the light."},{"id":"b2u17-e8","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The two brothers ___ in almost every way: one is loud, the other silent.\\"","options":["differ","attach","commute","confirm"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To differ is to not be the same as another person or thing, which is what the contrast between the brothers shows."},{"id":"b2u17-e9","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"He gave a ___ apology and everyone believed he meant it.\\"","options":["sincere","formal","junior","classic"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"A sincere person is honest about their feelings, so a sincere apology would be believed."},{"id":"b2u17-e10","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The bridge was designed to be the same ___ on both sides of the river.\\"","options":["height","expense","labor","signal"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Height is how tall something is, which is the measurement you would compare on both sides of a bridge."}]},"18":{"id":"b2-u18","book":2,"unit":18,"title":"Pages from a Valley Diary","level":"B2","topic":"Village life and work","generatedAt":"2026-09-23","wordCount":374,"paragraphs":["3rd March. The new [[priest]] arrived today, and half the village went to the square to [[greet]] him. He is younger than I expected, and he came on a bicycle with a single bag. My grandmother says he will not last a winter here. She says that about everyone, and she is usually right.","11th March. He came to the farm. He wanted to know about [[agriculture]] — how we choose the [[grain]], when we cut it, what the soil does after heavy rain. It was an [[odd]] question from a man in a black coat, and my father was [[curious]] enough to answer it for a whole hour.","2nd April. A [[monk]] who visits twice a year is staying in the old room under the [[ceiling]]. He and the priest sit outside in the evenings and argue about books. I do not follow half of it, but I like listening. There is a kind of [[joy]] in the way they disagree that I have never heard between two adults before.","19th May. I asked the priest what a [[profession]] was really for. He [[pause|paused]] for a long time and said that some people choose a job and some are chosen by one. Then he asked me what I was good at. I could not answer, which I suspect was the point of the question. He has an [[ability]] to leave a thought sitting in your head for weeks.","7th June. My uncle tried to [[convince]] me to leave school and take work on the road. The priest did not [[delay]] his answer when I asked him about it. He said that a person needs more than one [[element]] in their life, and that leaving a place is not the same thing as escaping it.","30th September. I have started drawing [[cartoon|cartoons]] in the margins of this [[diary]], small figures, badly done. I showed one to the priest and he laughed. \\"Keep them,\\" he said. \\"One day you will want to [[investigate]] what you were thinking.\\" He has never once asked me what I believe. Perhaps that is why I trust him. He has [[faith]] in people without needing proof, and he [[label|labels]] his jars in exactly the same careful handwriting as his letters."],"exercises":[{"id":"b2u18-e1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"How did the priest arrive in the village?","options":["By train, with several cases","On a bicycle with a single bag","In a car sent by the village","On foot from the next valley"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 1 says he came on a bicycle with a single bag."},{"id":"b2u18-e2","type":"multiple-choice","question":"Why was the writer surprised by the priest\'s questions at the farm?","options":["Because he had never visited a farm before","Because they were about farming rather than religion","Because he asked them in a foreign language","Because he wanted to buy the harvest"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 2 calls it an odd question from a man in a black coat, and the questions were all about grain, cutting and soil."},{"id":"b2u18-e3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What does the writer enjoy about the evenings in April?","options":["Listening to the priest and the monk disagree about books","Learning to read the monk\'s old books","Working under the ceiling of the old room","Talking to the monk about the farm"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Paragraph 3 says they sit outside and argue about books, and the writer likes listening, finding joy in the way they disagree."},{"id":"b2u18-e4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What did the priest say when the writer\'s uncle wanted him to leave school?","options":["That the writer should take the work on the road","That a person needs more than one element in their life","That leaving the valley is always a mistake","That the uncle should be the one to decide"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 5 says the priest answered without delay that a person needs more than one element in their life, and that leaving is not the same as escaping."},{"id":"b2u18-e5","type":"multiple-choice","question":"As used in paragraph 4, the word \\"ability\\" most nearly means…","options":["a natural skill or power to do something","a formal qualification","a habit that annoys other people","a duty given by someone else"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"He has an ability to leave a thought sitting in your head for weeks — that is, a skill or power he possesses."},{"id":"b2u18-e6","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The farm has moved from ___ to tourism over the last twenty years.\\"","options":["agriculture","profession","ceiling","cartoon"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Agriculture is the growing of plants and raising of animals, so moving away from it to tourism makes sense."},{"id":"b2u18-e7","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"She wrote in her ___ every night before switching off the light.\\"","options":["diary","label","grain","element"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"A diary is a book in which people write their personal experiences, typically each night."},{"id":"b2u18-e8","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The soup was thick with ___ — barley, wheat and oats.\\"","options":["grain","joy","faith","profession"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Grain is a food crop such as wheat or oats, all of which are named in the sentence."},{"id":"b2u18-e9","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"It took him two hours to ___ his mother that the plan was safe.\\"","options":["convince","greet","pause","delay"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To convince someone means to make that person sure of something, which is what the two hours were spent doing."},{"id":"b2u18-e10","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"There was a small ___ on the jar saying \'raspberry jam\'.\\"","options":["label","ceiling","diary","monk"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"A label is a tag that tells you about something, which is exactly what was on the jar."}]},"19":{"id":"b2-u19","book":2,"unit":19,"title":"Nine to Five, More or Less","level":"B2","topic":"Work and unusual careers","generatedAt":"2026-09-23","wordCount":343,"paragraphs":["Ask people what a [[company]] does and most will describe a [[product]]. Ask them who makes it, and the answer becomes vaguer. In fact, a surprising number of jobs exist to [[test]] things that nobody ever thinks about: the [[bottom]] of a shoe, the surface of a [[ball]], the temperature of a cup of [[tea]].","Take professional tasters. [[they|They]] work in quiet rooms, and their job is to [[drink]] and to describe. A taster\'s [[tongue]] is a trained instrument, and a single sip is enough to tell whether a batch should be [[sell|sold]] or poured away. It sounds pleasant until you learn that the same person may taste forty samples before lunch.","Then there are the animal handlers. Some look after [[pet|pets]] in hotels; others work with reptiles. One handler I spoke to keeps a [[snake]] in a room with a [[very]] warm floor and a locked door. Her family has never visited her workplace. \\"It is a [[strange]] job,\\" she admits, \\"but somebody has to [[stand]] there and [[wait]] for the animal to calm down.\\"","Other jobs are built on [[few]] simple skills used with great precision. Along a [[line]] in a factory, each worker may perform one movement thousands of times a day. The person [[responsible]] for quality is often the least visible and the most important, because a single error can ruin an entire batch of goods.","What unites these roles is temperament rather than pay. Most of the workers I met said the same thing: the strangeness wears off within a month, and what remains is the routine. One described her job as a [[type]] of meditation. Another said simply that she liked finishing something every single day.","That may be the real lesson. A job does not have to be exciting to be worth doing, and the [[company]] that employs you is not the same thing as the work itself. Somewhere tonight, someone is [[wait|waiting]] for a machine to cool, checking a [[product]] against a list, and taking a quiet pride in the fact that nothing went wrong."],"exercises":[{"id":"b2u19-e1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What is the main point of the first paragraph?","options":["Most people know exactly how products are made","Many jobs exist to test things nobody thinks about","Companies rarely describe their products clearly","Tea and shoes are made in the same factories"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 1 says a surprising number of jobs exist to test things that nobody ever thinks about, and gives examples such as the bottom of a shoe."},{"id":"b2u19-e2","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What does the writer say makes the taster\'s job harder than it sounds?","options":["The rooms are very cold","They may taste forty samples before lunch","They must write long reports","They work alone with no training"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 2 says it sounds pleasant until you learn that the same person may taste forty samples before lunch."},{"id":"b2u19-e3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What does the handler\'s family do?","options":["They help her feed the snake","They have never visited her workplace","They work in the same hotel","They keep pets of their own at home"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 3 states that her family has never visited her workplace."},{"id":"b2u19-e4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"According to the passage, what do most of these jobs have in common?","options":["They pay unusually well","They require long training","The strangeness wears off and routine takes over","They are all done outdoors"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"Paragraph 5 says most of the workers agreed that the strangeness wears off within a month and what remains is the routine."},{"id":"b2u19-e5","type":"multiple-choice","question":"As used in paragraph 5, the word \\"type\\" most nearly means…","options":["a particular kind","a printed form","a written record","a machine part"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"One worker described her job as a type of meditation — that is, a particular kind of meditation."},{"id":"b2u19-e6","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"He works for a small ___ that repairs old clocks.\\"","options":["company","tongue","line","bottom"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"A company is a business that makes or sells goods or services, which fits a clock repair business."},{"id":"b2u19-e7","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The name of the beach is printed at the ___ of the map.\\"","options":["bottom","line","type","pet"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"The bottom is the lowest part of something, and maps often carry names along the lower edge."},{"id":"b2u19-e8","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"She could not ___ still while the photographer adjusted the light.\\"","options":["stand","drink","sell","wait"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To stand is to hold the body upright on the legs, so being unable to stand still means being unable to keep still."},{"id":"b2u19-e9","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"A vet is ___ for the health of every animal in her care.\\"","options":["responsible","strange","few","very"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"A responsible person is in charge of someone or something, so a vet is responsible for the animals she treats."},{"id":"b2u19-e10","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"They decided to ___ the old furniture rather than take it with them.\\"","options":["sell","drink","stand","wait"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To sell is to give something to someone in exchange for money, which is what they did instead of moving it."}]},"20":{"id":"b2-u20","book":2,"unit":20,"title":"The Patent Office Years","level":"B2","topic":"Science and biography","generatedAt":"2026-09-23","wordCount":375,"paragraphs":["In 1902 a young [[immigrant]] stepped off a train in Bern with a degree he could not use and a [[duty]] to support himself. His name was Albert Einstein. He had applied for academic posts and had been rejected by every one of them. [[poverty]] was not far away, and for a while he gave private lessons to pay the [[rent]].","The job he finally found was at the patent office, and it suited him better than he was willing to admit. The work was [[elementary]] enough to leave his mind free. He would [[approve]] applications, [[insert]] corrections in the margins, and then, in the evenings, return to physics with a clear head.","His early years had been marked by [[failure]] and by something harder to name: a habit of questioning what everyone else accepted without thought. At school he had been slow to [[pretend]] interest in subjects he found dull, and one teacher told him he would never [[accomplish]] anything. Whether the story is true or not, he repeated it for the rest of his life.","In 1905 he published four papers in a single year. Their conclusions arrived not as one [[instant]] of insight but through [[gradual]] work, checked and rechecked against every case he could imagine. One paper suggested that light behaves as both a wave and a particle; another showed that time itself could not be treated as absolute. It took the scientific world nearly two decades to give him full [[recognition]].","Even then, the [[barrier]] between his ideas and the public was considerable. He was often asked to explain relativity in a single sentence and always refused. He preferred, he said, to [[detect]] the error in his own thinking rather than to defend a conclusion in public.","He disliked [[rank]] and ceremony, although the highest honours were offered and he accepted them. He had once helped design a refrigerator that needed no electricity to [[refrigerate]] its contents, and he was prouder of that than of several of his theories. He [[retire|retired]] from his post in 1945 and spent his last years on a problem he never solved. The [[statistic]] he cared about was not his own fame but the [[approximate]] number of hours he had left in which to think."],"exercises":[{"id":"b2u20-e1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What was Einstein\'s situation in 1902?","options":["He had just been given a university post","He had been rejected for academic posts and needed money","He had already published four papers","He had left Europe for good"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 1 says he had applied for academic posts and been rejected by all of them, that poverty was not far away, and that he gave lessons to pay the rent."},{"id":"b2u20-e2","type":"multiple-choice","question":"Why did the patent office suit him?","options":["It paid more than a university","The work was simple enough to leave his mind free","It allowed him to publish without permission","He could work entirely from home"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 2 says the work was elementary enough to leave his mind free, so he returned to physics in the evenings."},{"id":"b2u20-e3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"How does the passage describe the arrival of his 1905 conclusions?","options":["As a single sudden moment of inspiration","As the result of gradual, repeated checking","As something he had planned since childhood","As a direct response to his teacher\'s remark"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 4 says the conclusions arrived not as one instant of insight but through gradual work, checked and rechecked."},{"id":"b2u20-e4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What did Einstein prefer to doing in public?","options":["Explaining relativity in one sentence","Finding the error in his own thinking","Defending his conclusions in debates","Accepting the highest honours"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 5 says he preferred to detect the error in his own thinking rather than defend a conclusion in public."},{"id":"b2u20-e5","type":"multiple-choice","question":"As used in paragraph 5, the word \\"barrier\\" most nearly means…","options":["something that blocks the way","a fence around a building","a formal agreement","a scientific instrument"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"The barrier between his ideas and the public was considerable — that is, something that made understanding difficult, a block in the way."},{"id":"b2u20-e6","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Please ___ your card into the machine and wait for the green light.\\"","options":["insert","detect","retire","approve"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To insert something is to put it into something else, which is what you do with a card and a machine."},{"id":"b2u20-e7","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The committee will ___ the new plans at its next meeting.\\"","options":["approve","refrigerate","pretend","accomplish"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To approve of something is to like or agree with it, and a committee formally agrees to plans."},{"id":"b2u20-e8","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The change was ___ — you could hardly notice it from one week to the next.\\"","options":["gradual","approximate","elementary","instant"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Something gradual happens slowly, which matches a change too small to notice week by week."},{"id":"b2u20-e9","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The device can ___ very small amounts of gas in the air.\\"","options":["detect","pretend","accomplish","insert"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To detect means to notice or find something, which is what a sensitive device does with tiny amounts of gas."},{"id":"b2u20-e10","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"It is your ___ to lock the doors before you leave the building.\\"","options":["duty","rank","barrier","poverty"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"A duty is something a person has to do, which fits a task that must be carried out before leaving."}]},"21":{"id":"b2-u21","book":2,"unit":21,"title":"Above the Old Copper Mine","level":"B2","topic":"A mining town turns to astronomy","generatedAt":"2026-09-23","wordCount":482,"paragraphs":["The town of Kestrel Ridge was built for a single purpose, and when that purpose ended, almost everyone left. For nearly a century its families had lived on what lay beneath them: every morning the [[miner|miners]] went down before dawn and came back after dark, cutting tunnels [[underground]] in search of copper, silver and other [[mineral|minerals]]. The work was hard, the pay was good, and nobody in the town had ever seriously imagined doing anything else.","The closure, when it came, was less a decision than an [[accident]] that everyone had been warned about and had chosen to ignore. Within two years half the families had gone. The old shafts filled with water, and today, if you lower a lamp into one of them, a forgotten bucket can still be seen [[float|floating]] on the black surface far below.","Then a university arrived with a proposal. The ridge, its surveyors explained, was one of the few places left in the country where the night sky was still genuinely dark. They asked [[permission]] to build an observatory on the summit, and after a long and bad-tempered public meeting the council agreed to [[grant]] it. Local people were invited to [[participate]] in the construction, and many did; the same hands that had once dug for metal now dug foundations.","The [[telescope]] that was finally installed is not the largest in the world, but on a clear night the [[scale]] of what it reveals is difficult to put into words. From the control room you can watch a [[satellite]] cross the field of view in silence, or follow a star that burns like a distant [[jewel]]. The air up there is thin and dry, which is precisely why the site was chosen in the first place.","The observatory also trains people who will never leave the ground. Would-be [[astronaut|astronauts]] come for a month at a time to learn how instruments behave in low [[gravity]] and how to stay [[awake]] through a night that seems to have no end. The routine is strict. Trainees [[stretch]] before every session, and they are not allowed to [[skip]] the safety briefings, however tired they are. One of them told me that the hardest part is not the science but the [[courage]] required to sit still while a machine you cannot touch does the work for you.","What surprises visitors most is how little the town has changed in spirit. The ore that the [[miner|miners]] once lifted out of the ground was [[raw]] material, sold by weight and shipped away down the valley road; the data gathered on the summit leaves by the same road today. On my last evening, a retired office clerk [[pour|poured]] tea for both of us and said that her father had never once looked up. He would have called it an accident of history, she said, that a mine should end its life as a window."],"exercises":[{"id":"b2u21-e1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What did the people of Kestrel Ridge originally live on?","options":["Tourism during the summer months","Farming the thin soil of the valley","Copper, silver and other minerals dug from beneath the town","A university grant paid to the town council"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"Paragraph 1 explains that miners cut tunnels underground in search of copper, silver and other minerals, and that the town\'s families lived on what lay beneath them."},{"id":"b2u21-e2","type":"multiple-choice","question":"Why did the university want to build on the ridge?","options":["The land was cheap after the mine closed","The night sky there was still genuinely dark","The tunnels could be used as laboratories","The summit was easy to reach by road"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 3 states that the ridge was one of the few places left in the country where the night sky was still genuinely dark."},{"id":"b2u21-e3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"How did local people react to the observatory project?","options":["They refused to allow building on the summit","They demanded that the mine be reopened instead","They were indifferent and took no part in it","Many of them took part in the construction work"],"answerIndex":3,"explanation":"Paragraph 3 says local people were invited to participate in the construction and many did, with the same hands that once dug for metal digging foundations."},{"id":"b2u21-e4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What does the retired clerk\'s remark at the end suggest?","options":["She thinks the observatory is an unlikely but fitting continuation of the town\'s history","She believes her father would have been ashamed of the town","She regrets that the mine ever closed","She has never visited the observatory herself"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"She describes the change as an \'accident of history\' and says a mine should end its life as a window, which shows she sees the observatory as a surprising but natural next chapter."},{"id":"b2u21-e5","type":"multiple-choice","question":"As used in paragraph 6, \\"raw material\\" most nearly means material that is…","options":["dangerous to handle","in its natural, unprocessed state","extremely valuable","imported from another region"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"The ore is described as sold by weight and shipped away, which shows raw here means unprocessed rather than dangerous or valuable in itself."},{"id":"b2u21-e6","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The committee decided to ___ the request, though it set several conditions.\\"","options":["skip","grant","float","stretch"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"To grant something is to allow someone to have it, which fits a committee approving a request with conditions attached."},{"id":"b2u21-e7","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"He was still ___ at three in the morning, reading by the light of a single lamp.\\"","options":["awake","raw","underground","solitary"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Awake means not asleep, which describes someone reading in the middle of the night."},{"id":"b2u21-e8","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"It takes ___ to admit in public that you were wrong.\\"","options":["gravity","permission","courage","scale"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"Courage is the quality of not being afraid, and admitting a mistake in public requires exactly that."},{"id":"b2u21-e9","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Runners should ___ before a race to avoid injury.\\"","options":["stretch","pour","skip","participate"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To stretch is to extend the arms or legs, which is the standard preparation before running."},{"id":"b2u21-e10","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"She ___ the milk carefully into a clean glass.\\"","options":["granted","poured","floated","stretched"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"To pour a liquid is to make it flow out of a container, which is what happens when milk goes from a bottle into a glass."}]},"22":{"id":"b2-u22","book":2,"unit":22,"title":"The Missing Trophy","level":"B2","topic":"A country fair and a false alarm","generatedAt":"2026-09-23","wordCount":452,"paragraphs":["Every August the village holds a [[festival]] on the field behind the church, and for three days the place is louder than it has any right to be. There are stalls, a sheep [[contest]], a tent where farmers show off the best examples of each [[breed]], and a rule that only local entries [[qualify]] for the main prizes. This year a young [[journalist]] from the regional paper was sent to cover it, which is how I came to spend a Saturday standing in the rain.","The morning began well. A farmer named Doreen had spent six months [[repair|repairing]] an old tractor in her [[garage]], and it won first prize — a silver cup that had been presented as an [[award]] every year since 1954. She parked it on a table in the main tent and went to find a [[tissue]] for her nose, because the wind had turned cold.","Twenty minutes later the cup was gone. The first sign of trouble was the [[alarm]] on the tent\'s side gate, which nobody had expected to work. A small group gathered round the empty table. Someone suggested that a stranger had [[slip|slipped]] in while the steward was carrying a [[bucket]] of water to the sheep pens, and the steward, [[somewhat]] embarrassed, admitted that he had left the gate open.","The police were called, and a constable arrived who clearly did not think a missing cup was urgent. By the afternoon, however, a second piece of news changed the mood: the cash box from the raffle stall had also disappeared. It was now possible, though far from certain, that someone had come to the festival intending to [[rob]] it. Nobody had been [[arrest|arrested]]; nobody had even been accused. The constable began to take statements, and the journalist began to take notes.","The cup was found at six o\'clock, in the [[yard]] behind the village hall, wrapped in a coat. It had not been stolen at all. A boy of eleven had carried it there to show his grandmother, with a [[pup]] following at his heels, and had then been unable to remember where it belonged. As for the cash box, it was discovered in a [[stable]] at the edge of the field, untouched, where the wind had blown it off a bench.","The festival [[resume|resumed]] on Sunday with a much smaller programme. Doreen got her cup back and said, rather generously, that she was glad the boy had liked it. The constable went home. And the journalist, who had come hoping to write about prize vegetables, filed a story about a stranger who was never [[convict|convicted]] of anything at all — a story, she admitted later, that was better than the one she had planned."],"exercises":[{"id":"b2u22-e1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"Why was the journalist in the village?","options":["She had been sent by her paper to cover the festival","She was investigating a series of thefts in the area","She had grown up there and was visiting family","She was judging the sheep contest"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Paragraph 1 says a young journalist from the regional paper was sent to cover the festival."},{"id":"b2u22-e2","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What won first prize on the Saturday morning?","options":["A sheep from a local breed","A tractor that Doreen had repaired","A jar of homemade jam","A pup belonging to the steward"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 2 states that Doreen had spent six months repairing an old tractor in her garage and that it won first prize."},{"id":"b2u22-e3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What made people think the festival had been deliberately targeted?","options":["The side gate alarm had been switched off","A stranger had been seen carrying a bucket","The raffle stall\'s cash box had also disappeared","The steward refused to make a statement"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"Paragraph 4 explains that the disappearance of the raffle stall\'s cash box made it possible that someone had come intending to rob the festival."},{"id":"b2u22-e4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"Where was the silver cup eventually found?","options":["In the stable at the edge of the field","In a garage on the far side of the village","In the yard behind the village hall","Under a bench in the main tent"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"Paragraph 5 says the cup was found at six o\'clock in the yard behind the village hall, wrapped in a coat. The cash box, not the cup, was found in the stable."},{"id":"b2u22-e5","type":"multiple-choice","question":"As used in paragraph 3, \\"somewhat embarrassed\\" most nearly means…","options":["extremely angry","slightly ashamed","completely confused","quite relieved"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Somewhat means to some degree but not to a large degree, so the steward was mildly ashamed of having left the gate open."},{"id":"b2u22-e6","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Two officers were sent to ___ the man at his home on Tuesday morning.\\"","options":["arrest","repair","resume","qualify"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To arrest someone means to take that person into custody for doing something bad, which is what officers do at a suspect\'s home."},{"id":"b2u22-e7","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Only swimmers who finish the heat in under a minute will ___ for the final.\\"","options":["rob","slip","qualify","convict"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"To qualify is to meet the standard needed for the next stage, which is exactly what a fast enough time allows."},{"id":"b2u22-e8","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The chair felt perfectly ___ — it did not rock or creak at all.\\"","options":["stable","somewhat","delicate","raw"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"A stable thing does not move or fall over, which matches a chair that neither rocks nor creaks."},{"id":"b2u22-e9","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Thieves broke into the depot at night and ___ three lorries of their loads.\\"","options":["arrested","robbed","qualified","resumed"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"To rob is to take property by force, which describes thieves emptying lorries that do not belong to them."},{"id":"b2u22-e10","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Be careful on the wet steps — it is very easy to ___ there.\\"","options":["slip","skip","stretch","swear"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To slip means to slide and fall down, which is the risk on wet steps."}]},"23":{"id":"b2-u23","book":2,"unit":23,"title":"Two Days, One City","level":"B2","topic":"A short city break, told as a diary","generatedAt":"2026-09-23","wordCount":429,"paragraphs":["I had [[two]] days in the city and no real [[plan]] beyond eating as well as I could. My [[hotel]] was a single room on the fourteenth floor of a building that also contained a clinic, a language school and at least three restaurants. From the window I could see the harbour, and after dark the [[light]] from the towers on the far side seemed to hang in the air rather than shine from any particular place. I had arrived late, on a flight delayed twice, and by the time I reached the room it was already dark.","On the first morning I took a [[taxi]] to the [[station]] and then walked, which turned out to be the [[best]] decision of the whole trip. The streets were already busy at eight, but the [[crowd]] moved with a purpose that I found [[easy]] to follow. I bought a travel [[card]] from a machine, though I never fully understood the [[system]] behind it, and then spent a full [[hour]] standing on a footbridge simply watching the traffic move below me. Nobody hurried, and nobody seemed to be late for anything.","Lunch was a bowl of noodles in a [[short]], noisy street behind a [[market]]. The [[price]] was low, the [[dish]] was simple, and it was better than anything I ate for the rest of the week. Afterwards I went to [[shop]] for a while, mainly to stay out of the heat, and was [[surprise|surprised]] by how quickly an afternoon can disappear inside a building with seven floors of small stalls.","By the second [[day]] I had stopped trying to see everything, which is the only sensible approach. I took a ferry across the water, walked through a park where elderly men were practising something slow and precise, and drank tea in a shop that had belonged to the same family for decades. What I remember is not any single sight but the [[experience]] of being somewhere that never quite pauses. I had not expected to like it as much as I did.","On my last evening I ate alone in a place with six tables and a menu written on a board. The owner asked where I was from, and when I told her she nodded as if she had expected it. Then she brought me something I had not ordered and refused to charge me for it. We talked for twenty minutes about nothing in particular, in two languages and a great deal of gesture. That, rather than the towers or the harbour, is the reason I would go back."],"exercises":[{"id":"b2u23-e1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What had the writer planned before arriving in the city?","options":["A detailed schedule of museums and galleries","A list of restaurants recommended by friends","Nothing beyond eating as well as possible","A two-day walking route around the harbour"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"The first paragraph says the writer had two days and no real plan beyond eating as well as possible."},{"id":"b2u23-e2","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What does the writer describe as the best decision of the trip?","options":["Taking a taxi rather than a bus","Choosing to walk instead of using transport","Buying a travel card from a machine","Eating in a restaurant with only six tables"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 2 says the writer took a taxi to the station and then walked, which turned out to be the best decision of the whole trip."},{"id":"b2u23-e3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"Why did the writer go shopping on the first afternoon?","options":["To buy a present for the owner of the tea shop","To find somewhere to eat lunch","Mainly to escape the heat","To replace a travel card that had stopped working"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"Paragraph 3 says the writer went to shop for a while, mainly to stay out of the heat."},{"id":"b2u23-e4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What does the writer say they remember most about the visit?","options":["The view of the towers from the hotel window","The food in the noodle street","The experience of being in a place that never quite pauses","The ferry crossing to the other side of the harbour"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"Paragraph 4 ends by saying that what the writer remembers is not any single sight but the experience of being somewhere that never quite pauses."},{"id":"b2u23-e5","type":"multiple-choice","question":"In paragraph 2, \\"a purpose that I found easy to follow\\" suggests the crowd…","options":["was moving in a direction the writer could understand","was following the writer along the street","was unusually small for that time of day","was easy to photograph from the footbridge"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"The writer is describing the movement of the crowd as something simple to read and go along with, not something that followed the writer."},{"id":"b2u23-e6","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"A large ___ had gathered outside the theatre long before the doors opened.\\"","options":["card","crowd","price","system"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"A crowd is a large group of people in one place, which is what forms outside a theatre before a performance."},{"id":"b2u23-e7","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Living abroad for a year was the most valuable ___ of my life.\\"","options":["experience","station","market","taxi"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"An experience is something you live through or take part in, which fits a year spent living in another country."},{"id":"b2u23-e8","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The ___ of coffee has risen sharply since last winter.\\"","options":["dish","plan","price","light"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"The price of something is the amount of money needed to buy it, and it is prices that rise over time."},{"id":"b2u23-e9","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The heating ___ in this building is old and difficult to control.\\"","options":["system","card","hour","crowd"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"A system is a group of related parts that work together, which describes the machinery that heats a building."},{"id":"b2u23-e10","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"It did not ___ me at all to find him already there — I had half expected it.\\"","options":["plan","shop","surprise","experience"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"To surprise is to cause something unexpected, so not being surprised means the writer was not caught off guard."}]},"24":{"id":"b2-u24","book":2,"unit":24,"title":"The Ward with the Open Windows","level":"B2","topic":"A hospital ward and an argument about cleanliness","generatedAt":"2026-09-23","wordCount":467,"paragraphs":["In the winter of 1861 a young surgeon took charge of a hospital ward in a city that I will not name, and within a month she had lost more patients than she cared to count. She could [[recall]] every one of them. What troubled her was that the operations themselves had gone well: her hands were steady, the [[surgery]] was quick — by every measure the hospital used, she was a [[fantastic]] doctor — and yet the fever came afterwards, almost every time. Most of the people in her care were already [[disabled]] when they arrived, and the operations were meant to help them, not to save them.","The hospital\'s senior physician did not welcome questions. He believed that bad air caused infection, and he had spent years writing in a medical [[journal]] that the answer was rest, warmth and a great deal of beef tea. Above the ward door hung a [[flag]] given by a grateful patient thirty years earlier, and it was never taken down. He had never once [[inspect|inspected]] the instruments between operations, because in his view there was nothing there to see.","The young surgeon began to keep notes. She [[marvel|marvelled]] at how little anyone seemed to care about the water in the ward. The [[tube|tubes]] used to drain wounds were rinsed in the same [[liquid]] that the nurses used to wash the floor, and the basin kept for a patient\'s [[bath]] was used afterwards for the next patient\'s dressings. Patients who could not [[chew]] were fed on thin soup. Nothing was clean, and nobody thought cleanliness was worth discussing.","Her proposal was modest and it cost almost nothing. She asked for [[sufficient]] soap, a separate basin for every bed, and a rule that anyone touching a wound should first wash. The senior physician called the idea sentimental. He said that patients died because they were weak, not because they were dirty, and that her statistics were a work of [[fiction]].","She published anyway. The paper was rejected twice, then accepted by a journal in another country, and slowly — much too slowly — the argument was won. Within twenty years, washing hands between patients had become ordinary practice in most European hospitals. It is difficult, reading her notes now, not to feel [[regret]] at the delay; thousands of people died while the debate continued.","What strikes me most is how [[tough]] that argument was, and how little it depended on genius. She did not [[overcome]] the problem with a brilliant discovery; she simply looked at the [[value]] of clean water and refused to be quiet. She wrote that a patient\'s [[soul]] was not her business, but a patient\'s bandage certainly was, and that a doctor who could not [[bend]] down to wash a basin had no right to call herself careful."],"exercises":[{"id":"b2u24-e1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What puzzled the young surgeon about her work?","options":["Her patients were too weak to survive any operation","The senior physician refused to let her operate","The operations went well but fever followed almost every time","Her instruments kept breaking during surgery"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"Paragraph 1 says her hands were steady and the surgery was quick, yet the fever came afterwards almost every time — that contradiction is what troubled her."},{"id":"b2u24-e2","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What did the senior physician believe caused infection?","options":["Dirty instruments","Bad air","Weakness in the patients","The water used to wash the ward"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 2 states that he believed bad air caused infection and that the answer was rest, warmth and beef tea."},{"id":"b2u24-e3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"Why did the senior physician reject her proposal?","options":["He thought it was too expensive for the hospital","He thought her figures had been invented","He believed soap harmed the skin","He wanted to publish the idea himself"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 4 says he called the idea sentimental and described her statistics as a work of fiction, meaning he thought she had made them up."},{"id":"b2u24-e4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What does the writer express regret about?","options":["The surgeon\'s decision to publish her findings","The number of operations the surgeon performed","The delay before her ideas were widely accepted","The closure of the hospital where she worked"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"Paragraph 5 says it is difficult not to feel regret at the delay, since thousands of people died while the debate continued."},{"id":"b2u24-e5","type":"multiple-choice","question":"In paragraph 4, calling her idea \\"sentimental\\" suggests the physician thought it was…","options":["based on feeling rather than evidence","too complicated to put into practice","unnecessarily cruel to patients","borrowed from another hospital"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"The physician follows this remark by dismissing her statistics, which shows he regarded the proposal as emotional rather than evidence-based."},{"id":"b2u24-e6","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"She had to ___ a fear of water before she could learn to sail.\\"","options":["inspect","overcome","marvel","regret"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"To overcome a problem is to deal with it successfully, which is what she does with her fear of water."},{"id":"b2u24-e7","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"We did not have ___ chairs for everyone, so three of us stood.\\"","options":["sufficient","tough","fantastic","raw"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Sufficient means enough in quantity, which is precisely what was missing when three people had to stand."},{"id":"b2u24-e8","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Experts disagree about the ___ of the painting, some placing it far higher than others.\\"","options":["tube","bath","value","flag"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"The value of something is what it is worth, and disagreements about how much a painting is worth are disagreements about its value."},{"id":"b2u24-e9","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"I cannot ___ where I put the keys, though it was only a minute ago.\\"","options":["recall","chew","bend","marvel"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To recall something is to remember it, which is exactly what the speaker is unable to do with the keys."},{"id":"b2u24-e10","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"He lived to ___ the day he turned down that offer.\\"","options":["regret","inspect","overcome","recall"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To regret something is to wish it had not happened, which fits a decision someone wishes they had made differently."}]},"25":{"id":"b2-u25","book":2,"unit":25,"title":"Why the Sky Wanderer Grew a Tail","level":"B2","topic":"A tale about a comet and how it changes","generatedAt":"2026-09-23","wordCount":438,"paragraphs":["Long before anyone thought to count them, the sky was full of lights that moved. Some held their patterns for thousands of years and were easy to name; others [[roam|roamed]], appearing in one part of the sky and then another, as though they had nowhere in particular to be. One of these wanderers was small and dim, and the other lights did not think much of him.","By the standards of the sky he was, frankly, [[ugly]]. He had no steady place, no fixed [[status]] among the constellations, and no circle of companions. He was [[solitary]] by nature, and on the coldest nights he admitted to himself that what he felt was close to [[despair]]. \\"I am a [[fragment]],\\" he said. \\"I am a piece of something that broke, and I will never be anything more than that.\\"","The [[moon]], who had seen a great deal and was not easily impressed, heard him. \\"You are not a fragment,\\" she said. \\"You are a [[sphere]] of ice and dust, and ice remembers everything it has ever touched.\\" She told him that every [[atom]] of him had once belonged to a [[star]], that the [[galaxy]] he crossed so slowly was in a sense his family, and that he carried its history in his body whether he liked it or not.","Then she explained how the light would work. When he came near the sun, the ice on his surface would warm and turn to gas, and that gas would [[form]] a cloud around him. The cloud would [[cover]] him like a coat and then be pushed away by the solar wind, [[radiate|radiating]] outward in a long, curved stream. \\"It will not be [[beautiful]] at first,\\" she warned. \\"It will look like smoke, and you will think it is a mistake.\\"","She was right about the smoke. As he drew closer to the sun, [[gloom]] gathered behind him and a pale stream of gas poured out into the dark. At first it was only a smudge. Then it grew, and the [[breadth]] of it surprised even the moon: a tail so [[large]] that it could be seen from the ground, glowing across the whole visible [[spectrum]], from deep red at the edges to a faint violet at its heart.","So the wanderer became a [[comet]], and the lights that had ignored him learned his name. He never gained a fixed place in the sky, and he never wanted one. But travellers who saw him hanging above the horizon understood something that the constellations, for all their order, could not teach: that a thing may be brief and still be worth watching."],"exercises":[{"id":"b2u25-e1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"Why did the other lights in the sky pay no attention to the wanderer?","options":["He travelled too quickly for them to see","He was small, dim and had no fixed place","He refused to speak to any of them","He had once been part of the moon"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 1 describes him as small and dim, and paragraph 2 adds that he had no steady place, no fixed status and no companions."},{"id":"b2u25-e2","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What did the moon tell him about his atoms?","options":["That they had once belonged to a star","That they would eventually become a moon","That they were colder than anything else in the sky","That they could never be seen from the ground"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Paragraph 3 says she told him that every atom of him had once belonged to a star and that the galaxy he crossed was in a sense his family."},{"id":"b2u25-e3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"According to the moon, what causes the tail to form?","options":["The gravity of the nearest planet pulling dust away","Collisions with fragments of other comets","Ice warming into gas and being pushed by the solar wind","Light from the stars reflecting off his surface"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"Paragraph 4 explains that the ice warms and turns to gas, which forms a cloud that is then pushed away by the solar wind into a long curved stream."},{"id":"b2u25-e4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What does the writer say the comet never wanted?","options":["To be seen from the ground","A fixed place in the sky","To be given a name by travellers","To travel near the sun at all"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"The final paragraph states that he never gained a fixed place in the sky, and he never wanted one."},{"id":"b2u25-e5","type":"multiple-choice","question":"In paragraph 2, the wanderer felt \\"close to despair\\". This means he felt…","options":["almost entirely without hope","angry with the other lights","proud of his independence","confused about where he was going"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Despair is the feeling of having no hope, and \'close to despair\' means he had almost reached that state."},{"id":"b2u25-e6","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Sheep are allowed to ___ freely across the upper slopes in summer.\\"","options":["form","roam","radiate","cover"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"To roam is to move around without a fixed plan or purpose, which describes sheep wandering over open hillside."},{"id":"b2u25-e7","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"He has led a rather ___ life since retiring, with few visitors and no fixed routine.\\"","options":["solitary","beautiful","large","ugly"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Solitary means lonely or alone, which fits a life with few visitors and no fixed routine."},{"id":"b2u25-e8","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Dust had begun to ___ every surface in the empty house.\\"","options":["roam","radiate","cover","form"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"To cover something is to put or spread something over it, and here the dust spreads across each surface."},{"id":"b2u25-e9","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"If the two groups agree to work together, they could ___ a very strong team.\\"","options":["form","cover","roam","despair"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To form is to make or shape something, which is what two groups do when they join into one team."},{"id":"b2u25-e10","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"A wood stove will ___ heat for hours after the fire has died down.\\"","options":["radiate","cover","form","roam"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To radiate is to send out energy or heat, which is what a stove continues to do after the flames are gone."}]},"26":{"id":"b2-u26","book":2,"unit":26,"title":"The Reef and the Rival","level":"B2","topic":"Two rival captains and a storm at sea","generatedAt":"2026-09-23","wordCount":429,"paragraphs":["The two captains had known each other for twenty years and had never once agreed about anything. One sailed a [[grand]] vessel with three masts and a polished [[deck]]; the other preferred a small, [[plain]] boat that he could handle alone. They were both [[strict]] with their crews, both excellent sailors, and both entirely convinced that the other was a fool. Neither of them would ever have admitted it, but they had once been friends.","Their [[task]] that season was to chart a stretch of water near a [[reef]] that had already cost several ships. It was a difficult [[zone]]: the charts were old, the [[coral]] grew close to the surface, and the weather changed without warning. The older captain, whose name was Aldis, had [[engage|engaged]] a local pilot; the younger, whose name was Renn, had refused, saying that a man should read the sea for himself.","For a month they worked within sight of one another, and their rivalry [[amuse|amused]] everyone in the small harbour. Then, on the last afternoon of the survey, the barometer began to fall. What arrived that night was not an ordinary storm but a [[hurricane]], and it caught both ships away from shelter with very little [[fuel]] and nowhere to run.","Renn was the first to [[crash]] against the reef. He had refused to [[adjust]] his course, and the impact tore open his hull. Aldis, seeing the lights go out, [[shut]] down his engine and turned towards the wreck, although every instinct told him to keep clear of the rocks.","The rescue took four hours. Aldis\'s crew, working in darkness with a rope and a [[cotton]] sail used as a sling, brought eleven men off the reef. Two of them had broken arms, and one had been in the water for more than an hour. Renn was the last to leave, and he was carried, still arguing, across the water. The [[loss]] of his ship was total; the loss of his crew was nothing at all, which was the only thing that mattered. The vessel broke up within an hour of the last man leaving it.","Afterwards, in the harbour, Renn did something that surprised everybody. He did not [[accuse]] Aldis of anything, and he did not pretend that the storm had simply been bad luck. He stood on the quay, [[firm]] on his feet for the first time in a week, and said that he had been wrong for twenty years. The two of them went on to [[surf]] the same beaches in summer and to argue, as always, about everything else."],"exercises":[{"id":"b2u26-e1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What was the two captains\' task that season?","options":["To rescue a ship that had sunk near the reef","To chart a stretch of water near a reef","To carry cotton and fuel to a distant harbour","To train a new crew of local sailors"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 2 says their task was to chart a stretch of water near a reef that had already cost several ships."},{"id":"b2u26-e2","type":"multiple-choice","question":"How did the two captains differ in the way they prepared?","options":["Aldis engaged a local pilot, while Renn refused to","Aldis refused to sail, while Renn set off at once","Aldis bought new charts, while Renn trusted the old ones","Aldis worked at night, while Renn worked only in daylight"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Paragraph 2 states that Aldis had engaged a local pilot, while Renn had refused, saying a man should read the sea for himself."},{"id":"b2u26-e3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What happened to Renn\'s ship?","options":["It ran out of fuel and drifted for two days","It reached shelter just before the storm arrived","It crashed against the reef and was completely lost","It was towed back to harbour by Aldis\'s crew"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"Paragraph 4 says Renn was the first to crash against the reef and the impact tore open his hull; paragraph 5 confirms the loss of his ship was total."},{"id":"b2u26-e4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What surprised people about Renn\'s behaviour afterwards?","options":["He blamed Aldis for the accident","He refused to sail again","He admitted that he had been wrong","He sold his boat and left the harbour"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"Paragraph 6 says he did not accuse Aldis and did not blame bad luck; instead he said he had been wrong for twenty years."},{"id":"b2u26-e5","type":"multiple-choice","question":"In paragraph 5, \\"the loss of his crew was nothing at all\\" means that…","options":["the crew had been poorly paid","no member of the crew died","the crew had already left the ship","the crew were not important to him"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"The sentence contrasts the total loss of the ship with the crew, and the writer adds that this was the only thing that mattered — meaning everyone was saved."},{"id":"b2u26-e6","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The report did not ___ anyone by name, though it was clear who was meant.\\"","options":["accuse","adjust","amuse","engage"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To accuse someone is to blame them for doing something, which is what naming them in a report would do."},{"id":"b2u26-e7","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"You may need to ___ the mirror before you can see the whole road behind you.\\"","options":["adjust","accuse","crash","surf"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To adjust something is to change it so that it works better, which is what you do to a mirror to get a proper view."},{"id":"b2u26-e8","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The mattress was pleasantly ___, neither too hard nor too soft.\\"","options":["firm","plain","strict","grand"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"A firm thing is solid but not too hard, which is exactly the description given of the mattress."},{"id":"b2u26-e9","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"They served the meal on ___ white plates, without any decoration at all.\\"","options":["plain","grand","strict","firm"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"A plain thing is simple and undecorated, which matches plates with no decoration."},{"id":"b2u26-e10","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Our teacher was ___ about deadlines and would accept no excuses.\\"","options":["strict","plain","firm","grand"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"A strict person makes sure that rules are followed, which fits a teacher who refuses to accept late work."}]},"27":{"id":"b2-u27","book":2,"unit":27,"title":"The Minister\'s Garden","level":"B2","topic":"A duke, a suspicion and a greenhouse","generatedAt":"2026-09-23","wordCount":463,"paragraphs":["The [[duke]] had a habit of listening at doors. He was not a cruel man, but he was a curious one, and he had learned more about his own household by standing quietly in corridors than by asking direct questions. On the evening the trouble began, he was in the garden, hidden behind a hedge, when he heard the [[minister]] talking to someone he could not see.","The conversation was in a [[language]] the duke did not know well, but he understood three words, and one of them was a name. The other voice belonged to a woman who had been [[hire|hired]] the previous spring as a gardener\'s assistant. She was [[ordinary]] in every way — unremarkable, quiet, easy to overlook — which, the duke reflected later, is exactly what makes a good spy.","By morning the duke had convinced himself of a plot. He told the minister what he had heard, expecting an [[apology]] and a confession. Instead the minister laughed. \\"You have been listening to my sister,\\" he said. \\"She is not a spy. She is a botanist, and we were discussing [[tobacco]] plants, which is the only subject she finds genuinely interesting.\\"","The duke was not satisfied. He had a [[bold]] nature and a suspicious one, and he decided to [[expose]] whatever was really happening. He had the gardener watched. He had her letters read. He even had a [[bug]] collected from the leaves in the greenhouse and sent to a laboratory, hoping that it would prove something; it proved only that the greenhouse had insects. His [[twin]] sister, who had warned him about this exact weakness since childhood, refused to speak to him for a month.","The truth, when it came out, was duller than any plot. The minister\'s sister was [[innocent]] of everything except an interest in plants. The duke, however, had gone too far to withdraw quietly. He had written a letter accusing her of theft, and now he had to explain why. It was a painful week. He was not [[guilty]] of anything worse than pride, but pride in his position was enough to make the matter serious, and the king — who [[resemble|resembled]] his father in temper if not in patience — made it clear that it would be remembered.","The duke\'s punishment of himself was to [[preserve]] the greenhouse exactly as it was. He did not [[capture]] the woman or send her away; he promoted her, and the arrangement was not [[permanent]], because gardens never are. When, years later, he was asked to [[pronounce]] judgement on a similar case, he refused to convict on evidence as thin as his own had been. \\"A [[symptom]] is not a proof,\\" he wrote. \\"And a [[witch]] is usually a woman whom somebody else has failed to understand.\\""],"exercises":[{"id":"b2u27-e1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What did the duke overhear in the garden?","options":["The minister discussing a plan to leave the country","The minister speaking in a language he did not know well","Two gardeners arguing about the greenhouse","His sister warning the minister about him"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 2 says the conversation was in a language the duke did not know well, though he recognised three words, one of which was a name."},{"id":"b2u27-e2","type":"multiple-choice","question":"Who was the woman the duke suspected?","options":["A foreign agent hired to watch the household","The minister\'s sister, a botanist","The duke\'s own twin sister","A former servant dismissed the previous spring"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 3 reveals that the woman was the minister\'s sister, a botanist interested in tobacco plants."},{"id":"b2u27-e3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What did the duke do with the bug he found?","options":["He had it collected and sent to a laboratory","He used it to frighten the gardener","He kept it as evidence in a sealed box","He asked the minister to identify it"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Paragraph 4 says he had a bug collected from the leaves and sent to a laboratory, hoping it would prove something."},{"id":"b2u27-e4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What did the duke eventually do about the gardener?","options":["He had her arrested and tried","He dismissed her without a reference","He promoted her","He ordered her to leave the greenhouse"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"Paragraph 6 states that he did not capture her or send her away; instead he promoted her."},{"id":"b2u27-e5","type":"multiple-choice","question":"In paragraph 6, the duke\'s remark that \\"a symptom is not a proof\\" means that…","options":["an illness cannot be treated without evidence","a sign of something is not evidence that a person is responsible","witnesses are more reliable than documents","a single letter can never be admitted in court"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"He is refusing to convict on thin evidence, drawing on his own mistake of treating a suspicious sign as if it settled the question."},{"id":"b2u27-e6","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"It was a ___ move to ask for a pay rise in her first week, but it worked.\\"","options":["bold","ordinary","permanent","innocent"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"A bold person is not afraid of doing something risky, which fits making such a request so early."},{"id":"b2u27-e7","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The jury decided that he was ___ of all the charges against him.\\"","options":["guilty","innocent","bold","ordinary"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"An innocent person is not guilty of a crime, which is what the jury concluded about the charges."},{"id":"b2u27-e8","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"It was an entirely ___ Tuesday: nothing memorable happened at all.\\"","options":["ordinary","bold","permanent","guilty"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Ordinary means normal or not special in any way, which matches a day on which nothing memorable occurred."},{"id":"b2u27-e9","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The trust has spent forty years working to ___ the old theatre.\\"","options":["expose","preserve","capture","pronounce"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"To preserve is to protect something from harm so that it lasts, which is what a trust does with an old building."},{"id":"b2u27-e10","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"People often say that she does not ___ her mother at all, though the voices are similar.\\"","options":["resemble","expose","capture","hire"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To resemble someone is to look like them, which is what is being denied here despite the similar voices."}]},"28":{"id":"b2-u28","book":2,"unit":28,"title":"The Man Who Counted Nothing","level":"B2","topic":"A portrait of an old fisherman","generatedAt":"2026-09-23","wordCount":436,"paragraphs":["The old fisherman lived at the end of a [[bridge]] in a small [[inn]] that had once been a boathouse. He had no family nearby and no [[electronic]] devices of any kind — no radio, no telephone, nothing with a screen. Visitors who asked how he managed without them were told, politely, that he had never found a use for a machine that could not be repaired with a knife.","His boat was small and his hands were older than the rest of him. Every morning he rowed out past the [[branch|branches]] of a fallen tree that marked the edge of the shallows, and every morning he [[cast]] his [[net]] in a single wide arc, without hurrying, and waited. He would [[weigh]] the catch in his hands rather than on a scale, and if it seemed light he would say so and go home.","What people remembered about him was the way he spoke. He never raised his voice. When he had something important to say, he [[whisper|whispered]] it, which had the strange effect of making everyone lean in. His [[philosophy]] was simple and he repeated it often: that a man should [[subtract]] from his life rather than add to it, and that the [[sort]] of person who owns too much is the sort who is owned.","He had a [[sharp]] eye and a [[bare]] kitchen: a table, two chairs, a [[pot]] that had been mended three times, and a shelf of [[seed]] packets arranged by the month in which they should be planted. He [[dare|dared]] anyone who visited to sit for an hour without speaking, and most of them failed. He took a long [[breath]] before answering any question, and his answers were usually shorter than the pause that came before them.","One summer a young researcher came to study the bay and asked to [[accompany]] him on his rounds. She brought instruments, a [[tight]] waterproof case and a great deal of equipment, and for three weeks she recorded everything. At the end of it she asked him what he thought of her work. He said that she had measured the water very well and had not yet looked at it once.","The bay has changed since then. A marina was built, the water is warmer, and the [[virtual]] silence of those early mornings has gone. The fisherman died some years ago, and the inn is now a caf\xe9 with a menu and a licence. But the story about him survives, and it survives mostly among people who never met him — which is, in its own way, exactly what he would have found funny."],"exercises":[{"id":"b2u28-e1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"How did the fisherman measure his catch?","options":["With an old brass scale kept in the inn","By weighing it in his hands","By counting the number of fish","By comparing it with the previous day\'s catch"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 2 says he would weigh the catch in his hands rather than on a scale."},{"id":"b2u28-e2","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What was the fisherman\'s philosophy?","options":["A man should work as hard as his body allows","A man should subtract from his life rather than add to it","A man should never speak unless he is asked a question","A man should keep records of everything he does"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 3 states that he repeated his philosophy often: a man should subtract from his life rather than add to it."},{"id":"b2u28-e3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What did the fisherman do before answering a question?","options":["He wrote the question down","He walked to the bridge to think","He took a long breath","He asked the visitor to repeat it"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"Paragraph 4 says he took a long breath before answering any question, and his answers were shorter than the pause."},{"id":"b2u28-e4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What did the fisherman say about the researcher\'s work?","options":["That she had measured the water but never looked at it","That she had chosen the wrong part of the bay to study","That her instruments were too modern for the harbour","That she should return in the winter to see the difference"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Paragraph 5 ends with his verdict: she had measured the water very well and had not yet looked at it once."},{"id":"b2u28-e5","type":"multiple-choice","question":"In paragraph 3, \\"the sort of person who owns too much is the sort who is owned\\" suggests that…","options":["wealthy people are usually unhappy","possessions can end up controlling their owners","fishermen own less than other people","it is wrong to sell anything you have inherited"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"The remark reverses the usual relationship between a person and their possessions, implying that the owner becomes controlled by what they own."},{"id":"b2u28-e6","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The walls were completely ___, with no pictures and no paint.\\"","options":["bare","sharp","tight","virtual"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"A bare thing is plain and not covered, which describes walls with nothing on them."},{"id":"b2u28-e7","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"I would not ___ to swim in that river in winter.\\"","options":["dare","weigh","cast","subtract"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To dare is to be brave enough to try something, so not daring means being unwilling to attempt it."},{"id":"b2u28-e8","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"You will need a ___ knife to cut through that rope.\\"","options":["sharp","bare","tight","virtual"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"A sharp object has a thin edge that cuts easily, which is what is needed for a thick rope."},{"id":"b2u28-e9","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The lid was so ___ that I could not turn it at all.\\"","options":["tight","bare","sharp","virtual"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"A tight thing is hard to move because it is firmly in place, which explains why the lid would not turn."},{"id":"b2u28-e10","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"She leaned over and ___ something I could not quite hear.\\"","options":["whispered","weighed","cast","dared"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To whisper is to say something very quietly, which is why the speaker could not hear it."}]},"29":{"id":"b2-u29","book":2,"unit":29,"title":"The River That Ran on a Calendar","level":"B2","topic":"How the Nile flood shaped farming life","generatedAt":"2026-09-23","wordCount":427,"paragraphs":["For thousands of years the farmers of the Nile valley lived by a single [[annual]] event. Each summer the river rose, spread across the land, and then withdrew, leaving behind a layer of dark, wet soil. That soil was [[fertile]] as no other soil in the region was, and everything depended on it: the wheat, the animals, the towns and the taxes.","The [[flood]] was not a disaster but a system. Farmers learned to read its height against marked stones, and officials recorded it every year. Too little water meant hunger; too much could carry away houses and [[furniture]] and drown the animals. An [[ideal]] season was one in which the river rose high enough to reach the fields but not the villages.","The land itself shaped what people could build with. There was little wood, so houses were made of mud brick, and household goods were made of [[clay]] — pots, plates, lamps and storage jars. [[wooden|Wooden]] objects were rare and valuable, and so were [[feather|feathers]], which were used to decorate fans and headdresses. Linen was woven from flax, and [[cloth]] was used for everything from clothing to the [[curtain|curtains]] that hung in doorways to keep out the dust.","Because the river flooded the same fields year after year, the same families worked the same land, and their calendar grew around it. They divided the year into three seasons, and their [[religious]] festivals marked the rising and the falling of the water. These ceremonies were not separate from farming; they were the farming year expressed in another form.","The river also made possible things that had nothing to do with agriculture. The [[wheel]] arrived late, but the boat did not, and the river carried stone from the south to the building sites of the north. Along the [[shore]] you could [[obtain]] almost anything: fish, reeds, [[shell|shells]] for jewellery, and news from three hundred miles away.","Modern visitors are often surprised by how much of this was written down. The Egyptians recorded the height of the flood, the size of the harvest, the number of workers and the movement of the stars. Their [[intelligence]] was not [[abstract]]; it was practical, and it [[deserve|deserved]] the credit it eventually received. Later writers turned the river into something [[romantic]] — a subject for paintings and poetry — but the people who lived beside it were more interested in its height. What they built there, from the fields to the [[grave|graves]] of their kings, was a way of living with a force they could not control but could at least predict."],"exercises":[{"id":"b2u29-e1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What did the summer flood leave behind?","options":["A layer of dark, wet soil","A wide channel that had to be refilled","A deposit of clay used for making pots","A natural wall of reeds along the shore"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Paragraph 1 says the river rose, spread across the land and withdrew, leaving behind a layer of dark, wet soil that was unusually fertile."},{"id":"b2u29-e2","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What could happen if the flood was too high?","options":["The wheat would grow too quickly to harvest","The marked stones would be washed away","Houses and furniture could be carried off and animals drowned","The river would change course and never return"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"Paragraph 2 says too much water could carry away houses and furniture and drown the animals."},{"id":"b2u29-e3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"Why were wooden objects rare in the region?","options":["There was very little wood available","Wood was reserved for the graves of kings","Wooden objects broke too easily in the heat","The river carried all the timber away each year"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Paragraph 3 explains that there was little wood, which is why houses were built of mud brick and wooden objects were rare and valuable."},{"id":"b2u29-e4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What does the writer say about the religious festivals?","options":["They were held only in years when the flood failed","They were the farming year expressed in another form","They were introduced long after the calendar was fixed","They took place far from the fields, in the towns"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 4 states that the ceremonies were not separate from farming; they were the farming year expressed in another form."},{"id":"b2u29-e5","type":"multiple-choice","question":"In paragraph 6, saying that Egyptian intelligence was \\"not abstract\\" means that it was…","options":["never written down at all","concerned with practical problems rather than pure ideas","borrowed from neighbouring peoples","limited to a small group of officials"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"The writer contrasts abstract thinking with what the Egyptians actually recorded — flood heights, harvests, workers and stars — and calls their intelligence practical."},{"id":"b2u29-e6","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"After ten years of unpaid work for the charity, she ___ a proper thank-you.\\"","options":["deserves","obtains","floods","decorates"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To deserve is to be worthy of something as a result of one\'s actions, which fits long unpaid service earning gratitude."},{"id":"b2u29-e7","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"You can ___ a copy of the certificate from the registry office for a small fee.\\"","options":["obtain","deserve","decorate","flood"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To obtain is to get something you want or need, which is what happens when you apply to the registry office."},{"id":"b2u29-e8","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The valley soil is so ___ that farmers can grow two crops a year.\\"","options":["fertile","abstract","wooden","annual"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Fertile land produces good crops, and soil rich enough for two harvests a year is clearly fertile."},{"id":"b2u29-e9","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"A cool, quiet room with a desk is the ___ place to study.\\"","options":["ideal","annual","romantic","religious"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"An ideal thing is the best it can possibly be, which fits a description of the perfect study conditions."},{"id":"b2u29-e10","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The company publishes an ___ report on its environmental impact.\\"","options":["annual","ideal","fertile","abstract"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"An annual event happens once a year, which describes a report published every year."}]},"30":{"id":"b2-u30","book":2,"unit":30,"title":"Small, Green, and Very Interesting","level":"B2","topic":"A kitten, a caterpillar and a hole in the wall","generatedAt":"2026-09-23","wordCount":435,"paragraphs":["The kitten had been in the house for eleven days and had already inspected every [[appliance]], every [[cupboard]] and every corner of the kitchen floor. Nothing escaped her. She had knocked a [[basin]] off a shelf, she had unrolled an entire basket of [[laundry]], and she had pulled a coat down from its [[hook]], knocked over a [[broom]] and dragged the coat under the table.","On the twelfth day she found the [[caterpillar]]. It was on the floor near the back door, moving slowly across the tiles in a way that suggested it had a destination. It was green, it was small, and it had more legs than anything she had ever seen. She crouched, [[swing|swinging]] her tail, and watched it for a full minute before she [[pursue|pursued]] it.","The caterpillar was not fast, but it was clever. Every time a paw came down, it changed direction. It crawled along a [[strip]] of skirting board and disappeared into a [[hole]] in the wall where the plaster had crumbled. The kitten could not follow it, which she found deeply unfair, and she sat in front of the hole and complained.","I was washing up at the time, with my [[sleeve|sleeves]] rolled up, and I made the mistake of laughing. She turned, gave me a look of pure betrayal, and then — because she was [[reluctant]] to give up — went back to the hole and waited. She waited for forty minutes. I have never seen a cat concentrate so hard on anything.","The caterpillar [[emerge|emerged]] the following morning, on the other side of the wall, in the garden. It was on a leaf, and it was eating, and it did not appear to have noticed that anything unusual had happened. The [[delicate]] little creature had, in its own quiet way, outwitted her completely. I [[swear]] she knew it. For the rest of the week she checked the hole every morning, and every morning she [[hop|hopped]] back to the door looking offended, stopping to inspect a [[stain]] on the tiles as though it might have moved.","There is a lesson here, and I am aware that it is a small one. My neighbour, who has a [[handicap]] that keeps her at home, watched the whole business from her window and laughed harder than I did. She told me that the caterpillar had one advantage over the kitten and only one: it had no [[spine]], and therefore no pride. I am not sure that is true, but the kitten has never gone near the back door again, and I have not seen a caterpillar in the kitchen since."],"exercises":[{"id":"b2u30-e1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What had the kitten done before she found the caterpillar?","options":["She had slept behind the buckets all morning","She had knocked a basin off a shelf and unrolled the laundry","She had been shut in the cupboard by mistake","She had caught a bird in the garden"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 1 lists the damage she had already caused, including knocking a basin off a shelf and unrolling a basket of laundry."},{"id":"b2u30-e2","type":"multiple-choice","question":"How did the caterpillar escape from the kitten?","options":["It climbed onto a leaf and was carried away","It hid under the edge of the cupboard","It went through a hole in the wall","It stayed completely still until she lost interest"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"Paragraph 3 says it crawled along a strip of skirting board and disappeared into a hole in the wall where the plaster had crumbled."},{"id":"b2u30-e3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"Why did the kitten give the narrator a look of pure betrayal?","options":["The narrator had laughed at her","The narrator had taken the caterpillar outside","The narrator had shut the back door","The narrator had picked her up and carried her away"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Paragraph 4 says the narrator made the mistake of laughing, and the kitten turned and gave a look of pure betrayal."},{"id":"b2u30-e4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What did the neighbour say about the caterpillar?","options":["That it would soon turn into something else","That it had no spine and therefore no pride","That it had been living in the wall for years","That the kitten would catch it in the end"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 6 reports the neighbour\'s joke that the caterpillar had only one advantage: no spine, and therefore no pride."},{"id":"b2u30-e5","type":"multiple-choice","question":"In paragraph 4, \\"reluctant to give up\\" most nearly means…","options":["unwilling to stop trying","unable to reach the hole","too tired to continue","angry with the narrator"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Reluctant means not wanting to do something, so being reluctant to give up means not wanting to stop."},{"id":"b2u30-e6","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The glass in that window is extremely ___ and must be handled with care.\\"","options":["delicate","reluctant","stained","handicapped"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Delicate things are easy to break or harm, which is why the glass needs careful handling."},{"id":"b2u30-e7","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The sun ___ from behind the clouds just as the ceremony began.\\"","options":["emerged","pursued","hopped","stained"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To emerge means to come out of something, which is what the sun does from behind cloud."},{"id":"b2u30-e8","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"He was ___ to lend the car, but in the end he agreed.\\"","options":["reluctant","delicate","spiny","stained"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Reluctant means not wanting to do something, which fits someone who agrees only after hesitating."},{"id":"b2u30-e9","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Reporters continued to ___ the story for weeks after the announcement.\\"","options":["pursue","hop","emerge","stain"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To pursue something is to follow or chase it, which is what reporters do with a story over time."},{"id":"b2u30-e10","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Red wine left a dark ___ on the carpet that would not come out.\\"","options":["stain","sleeve","hook","spine"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"A stain is a dirty mark that is difficult to clean, which is exactly what the wine left behind."}]}}'),i=JSON.parse('{"1":{"id":"b3-u1","book":3,"unit":1,"title":"What the Sand Kept Hidden","level":"B2","topic":"Ancient engineering and archaeology","generatedAt":"2026-09-23","wordCount":484,"paragraphs":["Drive west from the river and the city thins out within minutes. After a few kilometres there is only sand, and then the sand rises into shapes that cannot possibly be natural. The site covers several hundred [[acre|acres]], yet the first impression is of how little there is to see: a few pyramids, a handful of smaller tombs, and a stone figure with the body of a [[lion]] and the calm face of a king.","Up close, a pyramid is not a shape but a machine. Beneath the outer [[surface]] lies a [[core]] of rough blocks, and through that core run passages cut with astonishing accuracy. A long [[corridor]] climbs from the entrance; a narrow [[channel]] draws fresh air down to the deepest rooms; and at the centre sits the burial [[chamber]], where a ruler was laid to rest with everything he was expected to need.","None of this was accidental. To [[engineer]] a structure on that scale, the builders had to solve problems of weight, slope and timing that would still trouble a modern contractor. They levelled the ground until the [[gap]] between the highest and lowest corners was almost too small to measure, and they cut [[stairs]] into the stone so that workers could move between levels without ropes. Even the [[interior]] — the parts that no one was ever meant to enter again — was finished as carefully as the outside.","Who did the work? For a long time the answer printed in guidebooks was that slaves had built the pyramids for an [[elite]] ruling family. The evidence now points elsewhere. [[archaeology|Archaeology]] has uncovered graffiti in which labourers record their teams, their nicknames and their [[distinct]] handwriting, and the bones of those workers show that they were fed well and treated by doctors when they were injured. The [[sole]] purpose of the whole project, it appears, was not to display power but to serve the king in the [[afterlife]]: the pyramid was a machine for turning a dead man into a god.","[[royal|Royal]] building projects of this kind did not appear from nowhere. Earlier kings had been buried under flat platforms of mud brick, and the step from those platforms to a true pyramid was taken over several generations. The [[role]] of the architect was central to that change; one official was so proud of his part in it that he filled the walls of his own tomb with a list of the monuments he had designed.","What survives today is mostly the stone. Wood, rope, bread, cloth and people have all gone, and that is why the [[glory]] of the place can feel oddly cold to a modern visitor: we are admiring the part that was never meant to matter. The kings who [[found|founded]] this cemetery chose their ground well. Four thousand years later, their works are still the first thing a traveller sees from the ridge above the valley."],"exercises":[{"id":"b3u1-e1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What is the writer\'s main purpose in this passage?","options":["To explain how the pyramids were engineered and who actually built them","To argue that the pyramids should be closed to visitors","To describe a journey along the river","To compare Egyptian and Greek architecture"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"The passage describes the structure of a pyramid, the problems its builders solved, and the evidence about who did the work."},{"id":"b3u1-e2","type":"multiple-choice","question":"According to paragraph 2, what is the function of the narrow channel?","options":["It marks the main entrance to the monument","It draws fresh air down to the deepest rooms","It holds the possessions of the dead ruler","It supports the weight of the ceiling above"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 2 says the channel draws fresh air down to the deepest rooms."},{"id":"b3u1-e3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What does the graffiti described in paragraph 4 suggest?","options":["The workers were prisoners from another country","The monument was abandoned before it was finished","The workers had names, teams and recognisable handwriting","The builders were paid in bread rather than money"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"The graffiti records teams, nicknames and distinct handwriting, which points to organised, named labourers rather than anonymous slaves."},{"id":"b3u1-e4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What does the writer suggest in the final paragraph?","options":["The pyramids are less impressive than they first appear","What remains is only the stone, not the parts that mattered most to the builders","Modern visitors understand the site better than ancient people did","The cemetery was abandoned shortly after it was built"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"The writer says the wood, rope, bread, cloth and people have gone, so we admire the part that was never meant to matter."},{"id":"b3u1-e5","type":"multiple-choice","question":"As used in paragraph 3, the word \\"gap\\" most nearly means…","options":["a space between two things","a difference of opinion","a pause in a conversation","a hole in a wall"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"The sentence describes the distance between the highest and lowest corners of the levelled ground, so \\"gap\\" means a physical space between two points."},{"id":"b3u1-e6","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The mine has a vast underground ___ where the tools were once stored.\\"","options":["chamber","acre","surface","lion"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"A chamber is a closed space or room used for a special purpose, which fits a large underground room."},{"id":"b3u1-e7","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"There is a ___ difference between the two cloths: one is clearly heavier.\\"","options":["distinct","sole","royal","elite"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"A distinct thing stands out or is clearly different, which matches a difference you can notice at once."},{"id":"b3u1-e8","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The team had to ___ a bridge that could survive the winter floods.\\"","options":["engineer","found","overlook","venture"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To engineer something is to plan skilfully how to make it, which is what designing a bridge involves."},{"id":"b3u1-e9","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"She played a key ___ in persuading the council to fund the project.\\"","options":["role","gap","core","acre"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"A role is a part or position in something; \\"played a key role\\" is the standard phrase for an important contribution."},{"id":"b3u1-e10","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The school was ___ in 1871 by a group of local farmers.\\"","options":["founded","engineered","levelled","trimmed"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To found something is to start or establish it, which is exactly what happened to the school in 1871."}]},"2":{"id":"b3-u2","book":3,"unit":2,"title":"The Merchant Who Left No Name","level":"B2","topic":"A quiet benefactor in a northern port","generatedAt":"2026-09-23","wordCount":476,"paragraphs":["In the winter of 1652 a quiet man took a room above a [[blacksmith]]\'s workshop in a small port on the northern coast. He paid for a month in advance, asked for a fire and a shelf for his ledgers, and gave no explanation of where he had come from. The [[province]] had seen plenty of traders pass through, but few of them arrived with so little luggage.","His name, according to the town register, was Nicolas. He was in his thirties, [[modest]] in dress and [[humble]] in manner, and he [[occupy|occupied]] himself with the dull business of buying and selling wool. Within two years his affairs had begun to [[prosper]]; within five he owned three warehouses and a share in a ship. His neighbours assumed that he would soon move to the capital and forget them. Most people in the port, after all, worked with [[iron]], rope or tar, and they had seen what money did to a man.","He did not forget them. Instead he began to give money away, and he did it in a way that made it almost impossible to thank him. A family whose rent was about to [[exceed]] their income would find an envelope under the door. A widow who could not [[sustain]] her household through the winter would discover that her coal had already been paid for, down to the last [[penny]]. He never [[preach|preached]] about generosity, and he refused to have his name attached to any gift.","Why he did it, nobody knew. Some said he was [[compensate|compensating]] for a fortune made unfairly. Others believed that he had once [[encounter|encountered]] real hunger himself and could not forget the experience. Whatever the truth, his behaviour drew attention. A local official demanded to know whether such giving was part of some [[agreement]] with the town council. Nicolas replied that there was no agreement at all, and that he expected no [[satisfaction]] from it beyond the act itself.","He was, in the language of the time, a [[benefactor]], although he disliked the word. When he died, the town learned the full scale of what he had done: a school, a bridge, and a fund for the widows of sailors. It also learned where part of his money had come from. His first fortune had been [[forge|forged]] in a trade that was legal but not kind, and his later giving may have been an attempt to balance an account that could not be balanced.","Stories [[arise]] easily in small towns, and this one grew taller every year. In the version that children liked best, he climbed a [[ladder]] to the roof of a poor family\'s house and dropped coins down the [[chimney]]. There is no evidence for any of it. There is, however, a great deal of evidence for the quiet envelopes, and that is a better story in any case."],"exercises":[{"id":"b3u2-e1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What is the passage mainly about?","options":["A merchant who gave money away without taking credit for it","A blacksmith who taught a stranger his trade","A town council that refused to help the poor","A sailor who made a fortune in a distant port"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"The whole passage follows Nicolas, who prospered in the wool trade and then gave his money away anonymously."},{"id":"b3u2-e2","type":"multiple-choice","question":"How did Nicolas help a family whose rent was about to exceed their income?","options":["He found them work in his warehouses","He left an envelope of money under their door","He spoke to the town council on their behalf","He paid for their children to attend school"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 3 says such a family would find an envelope under the door."},{"id":"b3u2-e3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What did the town discover after Nicolas died?","options":["That he had never actually been wealthy","That he had left the country without telling anyone","That part of his fortune had come from a trade that was legal but not kind","That he had been writing to the town council for years"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"Paragraph 5 says the town learned both the scale of his gifts and that his first fortune had been forged in a trade that was legal but not kind."},{"id":"b3u2-e4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What does the writer suggest about the story of the ladder and the chimney?","options":["It happened exactly as the children describe it","It is a later invention with no evidence behind it","It was invented by the town council","It is the only story the town still tells"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"The final paragraph says there is no evidence for any of it, though there is plenty of evidence for the envelopes."},{"id":"b3u2-e5","type":"multiple-choice","question":"As used in paragraph 2, the word \\"modest\\" most nearly means…","options":["simple and not showy","shy and unwilling to speak","not very large in size","poorly educated"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"The phrase \\"modest in dress\\" contrasts with the wealth he later accumulated, so it means plain or not showy."},{"id":"b3u2-e6","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"An anonymous ___ paid for the new library wing.\\"","options":["benefactor","blacksmith","province","penny"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"A benefactor is a person who gives money to help someone, which fits paying for a library wing."},{"id":"b3u2-e7","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The cost of the repairs will ___ our budget unless we find cheaper materials.\\"","options":["exceed","sustain","occupy","compensate"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To exceed is to be more than something, so the repairs would be more than the budget allows."},{"id":"b3u2-e8","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Problems often ___ when nobody has written down what was agreed.\\"","options":["arise","prosper","preach","forge"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To arise is to happen or come about, which is what problems do when there is no written agreement."},{"id":"b3u2-e9","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The charity could not ___ its work without regular donations.\\"","options":["sustain","compensate","encounter","exceed"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To sustain something is to keep it going, which is exactly what donations allow a charity to do."},{"id":"b3u2-e10","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Smoke poured from the ___ above the roof.\\"","options":["chimney","ladder","penny","iron"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"A chimney is the tall pipe that carries smoke out of a building."}]},"3":{"id":"b3-u3","book":3,"unit":3,"title":"The Newcomer on the Hill","level":"B2","topic":"A shepherd\'s fable about trust and forgiveness","generatedAt":"2026-09-23","wordCount":433,"paragraphs":["An old [[shepherd]] had kept his flock on the same hillside for forty years. His [[hut]] stood where the [[wheat]] fields ended and the rough grass began, and from its doorway he could count every animal he owned without moving his head.","He was no longer quick, and he knew it. So when a [[newcomer]] to the valley offered to help, he accepted. The young man was cheerful, strong and full of plans, and for the first month he worked well. The shepherd began to speak of him as a [[caretaker]] rather than a hired hand, which in that valley counted as a promotion.","Then the trouble started. Sheep went missing, and the caretaker had an answer for each one — a fox, a broken fence, a gate left open by a child. The shepherd wanted to believe him, because the other possibility was too painful to consider. But the answers did not fit together, and a man who has watched animals for forty years learns to notice when something is slightly wrong.","One evening he [[venture|ventured]] up the hill alone and found the truth. Half the flock had been sold at a market three valleys away, and in their place the caretaker had brought back a dozen animals of an [[inferior]] breed, bought cheaply and passed off as the shepherd\'s own. It was a clumsy [[fake]], and only a man who had begun to [[overlook]] small details would have missed it.","The shepherd did not shout. He had spent a lifetime avoiding [[offense]] and had no wish to [[discourage]] the young man completely. He asked one question instead, and the answer told him everything. What followed was an [[awkward]] silence, and then an offer: the caretaker would work without pay until the loss had been [[repay|repaid]].","He kept his word. It took two years, and by the end of them the shepherd had grown used to the arrangement, and even, in a cautious way, to the man. He never quite lost his [[hatred]] of being [[deceive|deceived]] — a shepherd\'s whole life depends on trust — but he learned to separate the fault from the person who had committed it.","Not everyone approved. The neighbours thought his decision [[ridiculous]]. Some said he had [[neglect|neglected]] his own interests by forgiving a stranger; others, who knew him better, said he had simply [[acquire|acquired]] something worth more than sheep. He did not argue with either group. He moved to a small hunting [[lodge]] further up the valley, where he could still watch the fields, and told anyone who asked that the arrangement had proved entirely [[satisfactory]]."],"exercises":[{"id":"b3u3-e1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What is the passage mainly about?","options":["An old shepherd who discovers that his helper has cheated him and responds in an unexpected way","A young man who learns the skills of sheep farming from a neighbour","Two farmers who argue about the border between their fields","A village that decides to sell its sheep at a distant market"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"The passage follows the shepherd from hiring the newcomer to discovering the fraud and choosing to let him repay the loss through work."},{"id":"b3u3-e2","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What had the caretaker actually done with half the flock?","options":["He had hidden them in a lodge further up the valley","He had sold them at a market three valleys away","He had given them away to his own family","He had lost them to a disease"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 4 says half the flock had been sold at a market three valleys away and replaced with inferior animals."},{"id":"b3u3-e3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"How did the shepherd react when he learned the truth?","options":["He drove the caretaker out of the valley at once","He reported the matter to the village elders","He asked a single question instead of shouting","He sold the remaining sheep and retired immediately"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"Paragraph 5 says he did not shout, asked one question, and then accepted the caretaker\'s offer to work without pay."},{"id":"b3u3-e4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"Why does the writer mention that \\"a shepherd\'s whole life depends on trust\\"?","options":["To explain why the deception was so serious to him","To show that shepherds rarely speak to their neighbours","To suggest that the flock was difficult to count","To argue that young workers should not be trusted"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"The comment explains why he never lost his hatred of being deceived, even after he forgave the man."},{"id":"b3u3-e5","type":"multiple-choice","question":"As used in paragraph 4, the word \\"inferior\\" most nearly means…","options":["not as good as something else","smaller than expected","more expensive than usual","unwilling to work"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"The animals were bought cheaply and used to replace the shepherd\'s own flock, so \\"inferior\\" means of poorer quality."},{"id":"b3u3-e6","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"She ___ a small collection of old maps over twenty years of travelling.\\"","options":["acquired","neglected","ventured","deceived"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To acquire something is to get or gain possession of it, which fits building up a collection over time."},{"id":"b3u3-e7","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The mechanic said the repairs were ___ and the car was safe to drive.\\"","options":["satisfactory","ridiculous","awkward","inferior"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"A satisfactory thing is good enough, which matches repairs that make the car safe."},{"id":"b3u3-e8","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The first bad review did not ___ her from finishing the book.\\"","options":["discourage","repay","overlook","deceive"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To discourage someone is to make them feel less excited about something, which is what a bad review might have done."},{"id":"b3u3-e9","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"If you ___ a garden for a whole summer, the weeds will take it over.\\"","options":["neglect","acquire","venture","lodge"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To neglect something is to fail to take care of it properly, which allows weeds to spread."},{"id":"b3u3-e10","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"It seemed ___ to carry an umbrella on a cloudless day.\\"","options":["ridiculous","satisfactory","inferior","awkward"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"A ridiculous thing is silly or strange, which describes carrying an umbrella when there is no sign of rain."}]},"4":{"id":"b3-u4","book":3,"unit":4,"title":"The Winter I Fixed the Sled","level":"B2","topic":"A childhood memory of a broken sled","generatedAt":"2026-09-23","wordCount":494,"paragraphs":["The winter I turned eleven, my father handed me an [[ax]] and a job. The job was splitting kindling, which I hated, and the tool was old and blunt, which did not help. Every Saturday morning I stood behind the house with a [[bunch]] of [[pine]] logs and a growing sense of injustice. My brother, who was nine, was allowed to watch.","My [[sled]] was the real problem. It had belonged to a cousin, and by the time it reached me the runners were [[loose]] and the rope had been replaced twice. Down the long hill behind our house it would start straight and then swing sideways, as if it had decided to go somewhere else.","I [[persist|persisted]] anyway. Every afternoon after school I dragged it up the hill and threw myself down again, and every evening I came home with a new [[scar]] on my [[elbow]] or a fresh tear in my coat. My sister called this [[disgraceful]] and stood at the top of the hill to [[tease]] me, and I pretended not to hear.","In February a boy from the next [[alley]] offered to look at it. He was two years older, and I assumed he was [[kid|kidding]] when he said the problem could be fixed in a single evening. It was [[irritate|irritating]] to be treated like a child, but I was tired of falling off, so I carried the sled to his father\'s workshop and left it there [[overnight]].","He did a [[decent]] job. The runners were straightened, the rope was replaced with something that did not cut your hands, and the whole thing was sanded smooth. When I asked what I owed him, he said nothing. He had done it because he was bored, and because the sight of me crashing into the fence every afternoon had begun to [[offend]] his sense of how a sled ought to behave.","I was [[grateful]] in the way that eleven-year-olds are grateful, which is to say loudly and briefly. Then I took the sled up the hill and went down faster than I had ever gone, straight as a ruled line. The [[sensation]] was closer to flying than to falling, and I shouted the whole way.","Years later my mother told me something I had never noticed. The boy\'s family had very little that winter, and the [[chore|chores]] he did for us — clearing the path, carrying coal, mending the gate — had been arranged quietly by my father, who wanted to pay him without calling it charity. I had thought the whole business was about me. It usually is, at eleven.","That February my sister gave me a [[valentine]] she had made herself, with a drawing of me flying off the sled. It was the first time she had admitted that the whole thing was funny rather than shameful, and I kept it for years. I still have the sled, too, though nobody in the house is small enough to use it."],"exercises":[{"id":"b3u4-e1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"How did the narrator feel about splitting kindling?","options":["He resented having to do it","He was proud of his skill with the ax","He preferred it to playing outside","He was afraid of the tool"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Paragraph 1 says he hated the job and stood there with a growing sense of injustice."},{"id":"b3u4-e2","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What was wrong with the narrator\'s sled?","options":["It was too small for him","The runners were loose and the rope had been replaced twice","It had been left outside all summer","The seat had broken in the middle"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 2 describes the loose runners and the twice-replaced rope, which made the sled swing sideways."},{"id":"b3u4-e3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"Why did the older boy repair the sled?","options":["He wanted to be paid for the work","His father had ordered him to do it","He was bored and disliked watching the narrator crash","He hoped to borrow the sled himself"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"Paragraph 5 says he did it because he was bored and because the crashes offended his sense of how a sled ought to behave."},{"id":"b3u4-e4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What did the narrator learn years later?","options":["That the older boy had moved away without saying goodbye","That his father had arranged the chores to help the boy\'s family without calling it charity","That the sled had originally belonged to his father","That his sister had paid for the repairs herself"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 7 explains that his father arranged the chores so that he could pay the boy without calling it charity."},{"id":"b3u4-e5","type":"multiple-choice","question":"As used in paragraph 5, the word \\"decent\\" most nearly means…","options":["good enough","kind and generous","expensive","carefully hidden"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"\\"He did a decent job\\" describes the quality of the repair, so it means acceptable or good enough."},{"id":"b3u4-e6","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"I did not mean to ___ anyone by leaving the party so early.\\"","options":["offend","tease","irritate","kid"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To offend someone is to make them angry or upset, which is the risk when you leave a social event early."},{"id":"b3u4-e7","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"If the pain continues to ___ for more than a week, see a doctor.\\"","options":["persist","bounce","vanish","creep"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To persist means to keep going even when it is difficult; here it describes pain that does not go away."},{"id":"b3u4-e8","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"We were ___ for everything the neighbours did after the storm.\\"","options":["grateful","disgraceful","loose","decent"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"A grateful person feels thankful, which is the natural response to help after a storm."},{"id":"b3u4-e9","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The parcel should arrive ___ if you order before noon.\\"","options":["overnight","shortly","aloud","upwards"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"An overnight action happens during the night, so a parcel sent before noon would arrive the next morning."},{"id":"b3u4-e10","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"One of the screws had come ___ and the shelf was wobbling.\\"","options":["loose","decent","grateful","sore"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"A loose thing is not held tightly in place, which explains why the shelf wobbled."}]},"5":{"id":"b3-u5","book":3,"unit":5,"title":"The House at the End of Marlow Lane","level":"B2","topic":"A Halloween visit that ends quietly","generatedAt":"2026-09-23","wordCount":426,"paragraphs":["Every October the children on our street began planning their costumes in the first week of the month, and by the last week the plans had usually collapsed. This year, however, my friend Ines had a serious idea. She wanted us to visit the empty house at the end of Marlow Lane, where, according to a [[publication]] that nobody could actually produce, a [[wicked]] old man had once lived.","The house had stood empty for years. It backed onto the [[cemetery]], and its garden had been left to [[rot]] until the gate would no longer open. Local [[inhabitant|inhabitants]] disagreed about almost everything except this: nobody went in.","We prepared carefully. I wore a [[hood|hooded]] coat, because it was cold, and carried a [[flashlight]] that my father had checked twice. Ines came as a [[pirate]], which was not much of a [[disguise]] but was at least recognisable, and her brother wore a [[skeleton]] suit that glowed faintly in the dark. We were ten years old and entirely convinced of our own courage.","The first surprise was that the garden had not been abandoned at all. Someone had been [[nourish|nourishing]] it: the beds were neat, and there were tomatoes still on the stems. The second surprise was the light in an upstairs window. For a moment the [[thrill]] of the evening turned into something closer to fear, and we stood still in the dark, listening.","Then a voice asked us what we wanted. It belonged to a woman of about eighty who had lived in the house for fifty years and had never once answered the door at Halloween. She invited us in, gave us tea, and set us a [[riddle]] that she had been saving for a child clever enough to solve it. We failed. She seemed delighted.","We stayed for an hour. The [[curse]] the neighbours talked about turned out to be nothing more than a quarrel about a fence in 1974. She had stopped speaking to the street, and the street had [[acquaint|acquainted]] itself with a version of her that was far more interesting than the truth. She claimed she had been left to [[starve]] by her neighbours, which was not true, but she said it with such pleasure that we let it pass.","[[shortly|Shortly]] afterwards, half the street knew her name. She was not a [[creature]] of the graveyard at all; she was a retired music teacher with firm opinions and a very [[fancy]] teapot. Nothing about that evening went the way we had planned, and nothing was [[spoil|spoiled]] by it. It was better."],"exercises":[{"id":"b3u5-e1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What is the passage mainly about?","options":["A Halloween visit that turns into a friendly meeting rather than a frightening one","A group of children who get lost on their way home","A woman who refuses to leave an empty house","A neighbourhood quarrel about an overgrown garden"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"The children expect something frightening but end up drinking tea and talking with the woman who lives there."},{"id":"b3u5-e2","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What did the children notice about the garden?","options":["It was full of broken furniture","It had been neatly kept, with tomatoes still growing","It had been completely paved over","It was guarded by a large dog"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 4 says the beds were neat and there were tomatoes still on the stems."},{"id":"b3u5-e3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What was the truth behind the story of the curse?","options":["It came from a quarrel about a fence in 1974","It came from a newspaper report about the house","It was invented by the woman herself","It began when the cemetery was built"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Paragraph 6 explains that the curse was nothing more than a quarrel about a fence in 1974."},{"id":"b3u5-e4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"Why does the writer say \\"It was better\\" at the end of the passage?","options":["Because the house was warmer than the children expected","Because the real woman was more interesting than the frightening story","Because they had solved the riddle after all","Because the neighbours finally apologised to her"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"The writer contrasts the invented curse with the real person — a retired music teacher with a fancy teapot — and says nothing was spoiled by the truth."},{"id":"b3u5-e5","type":"multiple-choice","question":"As used in paragraph 3, the word \\"disguise\\" most nearly means…","options":["clothing worn so that people cannot tell who you are","a costume worn only at parties","a mask that covers the whole face","an untrue story about someone"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"The writer describes Ines dressed as a pirate, so \\"disguise\\" refers to clothing that hides her identity."},{"id":"b3u5-e6","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The old ___ behind the church is the oldest part of the village.\\"","options":["cemetery","closet","district","lodge"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"A cemetery is a place where people are buried, which is why it is found beside a church."},{"id":"b3u5-e7","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"A good breakfast will ___ you through a long morning.\\"","options":["nourish","starve","spoil","rot"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To nourish is to give the food needed to live and work, which is what a good breakfast does."},{"id":"b3u5-e8","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The children spent the whole journey trying to solve the ___.\\"","options":["riddle","bulletin","rhyme","disguise"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"A riddle is a difficult question meant to be solved for fun."},{"id":"b3u5-e9","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The ___ of scoring in the final minute was unlike anything else.\\"","options":["thrill","curse","complaint","relief"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"A thrill is an exciting feeling, which matches scoring in the last minute of a game."},{"id":"b3u5-e10","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The island has only one permanent ___, a lighthouse keeper.\\"","options":["inhabitant","newcomer","opponent","benefactor"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"An inhabitant is a person who lives in a certain place, which fits the only permanent resident of an island."}]},"6":{"id":"b3-u6","book":3,"unit":6,"title":"A Worm, a Jar and a Very Small Town","level":"B2","topic":"A local news story about a discovery that was not one","generatedAt":"2026-09-23","wordCount":510,"paragraphs":["The strangest story to come out of our [[district]] last spring began with a salad. A woman named Ana was washing lettuce at her kitchen sink when she noticed something moving [[underneath]] the leaves. It was a [[worm]], pale and long and unexpectedly heavy, and it was very much alive.","Ana did what any sensible person would do. She put it in a jar, [[tap|tapped]] the lid shut, and carried it to her neighbour, a retired [[senator]] who now spent his days growing vegetables. He examined the jar for a long moment, [[stir|stirred]] the contents with a pencil, and admitted that in forty years of gardening he had never seen one that size.","Word travelled fast. Within a week the local radio station had put out a [[bulletin]] about the giant worm, and a longer [[broadcast]] followed on the Sunday programme. A reporter came to photograph the jar. Ana, who had expected nothing at all from her lettuce, found herself explaining the situation to strangers in the street.","The science, it turned out, was less exciting than the story. A biologist from the university [[alert|alerted]] everyone to a simple fact: the worm belonged to an ordinary species that had been growing in unusually rich soil. There was no new discovery, no record broken, nothing [[tremendous]]. The [[rear]] of Ana\'s garden, where she had been dumping vegetable waste for twenty years, was simply the best-fed patch of ground in the county.","The retired senator was disappointed, but not for long. He had spent a career watching small events become large ones, and he recognised the pattern. A [[closet]] in his hallway was full of newspaper cuttings about similar cases: a potato shaped like a face, a [[bump]] on a tree that a whole village had decided was a miracle, a horse\'s [[skull]] dug up in a field and reported for three days as a human one. He kept them, he said, as a reminder of how quickly a community can talk itself into a belief. One cutting recorded that a man had been [[execute|executed]] in the town square in 1789 for stealing a sheep.","Ana kept the jar, and later the worm, in a labelled box in a kitchen [[drawer]], next to the [[chop|chopping]] board. She had to [[endure]] a certain amount of teasing about it. People asked whether she had given it a name. She had. The name was Gerald.","The ending was quieter than the beginning. The biologist took the worm away for study and returned it a month later, along with a report that Ana could not [[grasp]]. What she understood was that the creature had spent its entire life in the dark and had done nothing more remarkable than eat.","The retired senator tried to [[console]] her. He pointed out that most of the great discoveries of his own career had been small ones, and that a good story rarely survives contact with the facts. Then he asked whether she had any more lettuce, and Ana said she had, and that was the end of the matter."],"exercises":[{"id":"b3u6-e1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What is the passage mainly about?","options":["A small discovery that became a local news story and then turned out to be ordinary","A biologist who discovers a new species in a garden","A radio station that refuses to report local news","A town that quarrels about the correct way to grow lettuce"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"The worm causes a bulletin and a broadcast, but the biologist explains that it is an ordinary species in unusually rich soil."},{"id":"b3u6-e2","type":"multiple-choice","question":"Why was the worm so large?","options":["It belonged to a species nobody had seen before","It had been living in unusually rich soil","It had been fed by the senator for years","It had grown in a jar rather than in the ground"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 4 says the worm was an ordinary species that had grown in unusually rich soil at the rear of Ana\'s garden."},{"id":"b3u6-e3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"How did the story reach the wider public?","options":["Through a radio bulletin and a Sunday broadcast","Through a scientific journal published by the university","Through a letter written to the town council","Through a photograph displayed in the senator\'s hallway"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Paragraph 3 describes the local radio bulletin and the longer Sunday broadcast."},{"id":"b3u6-e4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"Why does the writer describe the senator\'s newspaper cuttings?","options":["To prove that the worm was a genuine scientific discovery","To show that the town had a habit of turning small events into big stories","To explain how the senator had spent his career","To suggest that Ana should have kept the worm a secret"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"The cuttings — a potato, a bump, a horse\'s skull — all illustrate how quickly a community talks itself into a belief."},{"id":"b3u6-e5","type":"multiple-choice","question":"As used in paragraph 4, the word \\"tremendous\\" most nearly means…","options":["very large or impressive","extremely frightening","surprising but untrue","expensive to prove"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"The sentence lists the things that did not happen — no new discovery, no record broken — so \\"tremendous\\" means very large or impressive."},{"id":"b3u6-e6","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The nurse ___ the doctor as soon as the test results arrived.\\"","options":["alerted","consoled","endured","grasped"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To alert someone is to tell or warn them about something, which is what the nurse did with the results."},{"id":"b3u6-e7","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The bridge was built to ___ a century of storms.\\"","options":["endure","stir","chop","tap"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To endure means to experience and survive something difficult, which fits a bridge standing through many storms."},{"id":"b3u6-e8","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"I could not ___ how the trick was done, however slowly he explained it.\\"","options":["grasp","alert","console","stir"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To grasp something is to understand and hold it in your mind, which is what the speaker could not do."},{"id":"b3u6-e9","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"There is a small ___ in the road where the tree roots have pushed up the surface.\\"","options":["bump","drawer","closet","skull"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"A bump is a small raised area on a surface, which is what tree roots create in a road."},{"id":"b3u6-e10","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The cat had hidden ___ the bed and would not come out.\\"","options":["underneath","rear","district","bulletin"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Underneath means below or under, which describes the cat\'s position beneath the bed."}]},"7":{"id":"b3-u7","book":3,"unit":7,"title":"The Dog at the End of the Pier","level":"B2","topic":"A harbour town and the animal it remembers","generatedAt":"2026-09-23","wordCount":505,"paragraphs":["The town sits at the head of a wide [[bay]], where the river meets the sea and the fishing boats return twice a day. It is not a large place. In winter the wind comes straight off the water, and the only people on the dock are those with work to do.","In the spring of 1998 a puppy was [[abandon|abandoned]] there by a family leaving for the mainland. She was small, [[overweight]] and completely [[deaf]], and for the first week nobody could get near her.","That changed when a [[veterinarian]] named Marta began leaving food out. Within a month the dog had decided that the dock belonged to her. She was not [[ambitious]]. She did not want a home, or a basket, or a name — although she acquired all three in the end. What she wanted was to be present.","The fishermen were not [[enthusiastic]] at first. Then they noticed something odd. Her hearing was gone, but her other [[senses]] were extraordinary, and every morning she made the same short [[expedition]] to the end of the pier and sat facing the [[horizon]]. When a boat was two hours out, she would already be there. The fishermen began to trust her judgement. If the dog was on the pier, they said, someone was coming in.","She was [[loyal]] to everyone and owned by no one. Her [[mutual]] arrangement with the town was simple: the fishermen brought her fish, and she gave people a reason to stand together in bad weather. When the [[mayor]] raised a [[complaint]] about dogs on the dock, the meeting lasted four minutes. Nobody was willing to be the person who suggested removing her.","She was [[brilliant]] in a way that had nothing to do with obedience. She never learned to [[bark]] on command; in fact she rarely barked at all. But she could tell a returning boat from a departing one by the way it sat in the water, and she could tell a friend from a stranger by the sound of a foot on the boards — which, for a deaf animal, is not easy to explain.","She had spent her life offering a kind of [[refuge]] to anyone who needed one: a warm animal to sit beside when the season had been bad or the news from home was worse. Her last winter was hard. The [[chin]] that had once rested on every fisherman\'s knee now rested mostly on the ground, and Marta [[rub|rubbed]] her ears in the evenings and told her she had done enough.","She died in March, in the vet\'s back room, with two fishermen waiting outside. The town did something unusual: it paid for a small bronze statue at the end of the pier. Every few years money is raised to [[restore]] it, and the children repaint the collar. Visitors sometimes ask why a fishing port would put up a monument to a dog. The answer is not sentimental. For eleven years, she was the only thing on that dock that everybody agreed about."],"exercises":[{"id":"b3u7-e1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What is the passage mainly about?","options":["A deaf dog that became an important part of a fishing town","A veterinarian who opens a clinic beside a harbour","A town that argues about whether dogs belong on a dock","A fisherman who trains a puppy to find boats"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"The passage follows the abandoned puppy from her arrival to her death and the statue the town built for her."},{"id":"b3u7-e2","type":"multiple-choice","question":"How did the fishermen use the dog\'s behaviour?","options":["They took her presence on the pier as a sign that a boat was coming in","They followed her to find the best fishing grounds","They used her to warn them about bad weather","They relied on her to keep strangers off the dock"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Paragraph 4 says that if the dog was on the pier, they believed someone was coming in."},{"id":"b3u7-e3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What happened when the mayor raised a complaint about dogs on the dock?","options":["The dog was moved to the veterinarian\'s clinic","The meeting lasted only four minutes because nobody wanted to remove her","The fishermen agreed to keep her away from the boats","The town voted to ban dogs from the pier"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 5 says the meeting lasted four minutes, as nobody was willing to suggest removing her."},{"id":"b3u7-e4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"Why does the writer say that the town\'s answer \\"is not sentimental\\"?","options":["Because the statue was paid for by visitors","Because the dog had a practical role that everyone valued","Because the fishermen disliked the animal","Because the statue was removed after a few years"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"The final sentence says she was the only thing on the dock that everybody agreed about, which points to her practical value rather than mere affection."},{"id":"b3u7-e5","type":"multiple-choice","question":"As used in paragraph 7, the word \\"refuge\\" most nearly means…","options":["a place or source of comfort and safety","a hiding place from bad weather","a home for unwanted animals","a temporary shelter for boats"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"The writer describes a warm animal to sit beside in hard times, so \\"refuge\\" means a source of comfort and safety."},{"id":"b3u7-e6","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"He remained ___ to the club even after it was relegated.\\"","options":["loyal","enthusiastic","ambitious","overweight"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Loyal people always support a certain person or thing, which is what he did even when the club did badly."},{"id":"b3u7-e7","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The crew had to ___ the ship when the engine room flooded.\\"","options":["abandon","restore","rub","bark"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To abandon something is to leave it, which is what a crew must do when a ship is flooding."},{"id":"b3u7-e8","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The sun sank slowly towards the ___.\\"","options":["horizon","refuge","bay","chin"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"The horizon is where the sky appears to meet the ground, so it is where the sun seems to sink."},{"id":"b3u7-e9","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The ___ said the horse needed two weeks of rest.\\"","options":["veterinarian","mayor","benefactor","caretaker"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"A veterinarian is a doctor who treats animals, which fits advice about a horse."},{"id":"b3u7-e10","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Their friendship was based on ___ respect rather than shared interests.\\"","options":["mutual","loyal","brilliant","distinct"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"A mutual feeling is one that two people share in the same way, which describes respect felt on both sides."}]},"8":{"id":"b3-u8","book":3,"unit":8,"title":"The Chest in the Corner","level":"B2","topic":"A family story about a homemade anniversary gift","generatedAt":"2026-09-23","wordCount":478,"paragraphs":["For their twenty-fifth [[anniversary]] my grandparents received, between them, four teapots, three photograph frames and a very large clock. What my grandmother remembered, though, was what my grandfather gave her: a wooden chest that he had made himself, in the [[shed]] behind the house, over a single winter.","He was not a [[carpenter]]. He was a miner, and in those years the [[coal]] he cut paid for everything the family owned. But he could measure, and he could [[polish]], and he had watched a joiner in the village work often enough to copy the way a lid should sit. The shed roof [[drip|dripped]] all winter, and he worked with a bucket beside him.","The family came from the [[highlands]], where his mother had worked at the [[mill]] on the river, in a house with too many children and not enough chairs. He had never owned a piece of furniture that was not second-hand. That, I think, was the point of the chest.","The lining took longest. My grandmother had kept a length of dark [[fabric]] for years, and among her possessions was a small carved [[ivory]] bird that her own mother had brought from another country. He wanted both inside the chest, where only she would see them. He cut the cloth with her old scissors, [[sew|sewed]] it with a [[needle]] and [[thread]] borrowed from a neighbour, and [[trim|trimmed]] the edges so neatly that she assumed for a while that a professional had done it.","There was a mistake, of course. One corner was a centimetre short, and he never stopped noticing it. He was [[ashamed]] of that corner for the rest of his life, and he kept the offcuts stacked [[upwards]] in a corner of the shed for years, on the theory that they would one day be useful. She, on the other hand, used the chest every day, and when a seam finally [[burst]] she mended it herself, sitting on the [[couch]] with the lid open beside her.","I once asked her whether the mistake bothered her. She said it did not, and then she did some quick [[arithmetic]] to prove the point. Twenty-five years of marriage, she said, was roughly nine thousand days, and a single centimetre on one corner worked out at almost nothing per day. The chest was never [[elegant]] — it was too heavy and too plain for that — but it was used every day for fifty years, and no argument I have heard since has been better.","The chest is in my parents\' house now. It is not a beautiful object; the wood has darkened unevenly and one hinge has been replaced. But the corner is still short by a centimetre, and every time I open it I think of a man in a leaking shed who could not measure quite well enough and decided to finish the job anyway."],"exercises":[{"id":"b3u8-e1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What is the passage mainly about?","options":["A miner who built a wooden chest as a gift for his wife","A family that moved from the highlands to a village","A carpenter who taught his trade to a neighbour","A grandmother who collected carved birds"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"The passage describes how the grandfather, a miner rather than a carpenter, made the chest for his twenty-fifth anniversary."},{"id":"b3u8-e2","type":"multiple-choice","question":"Why did the corner of the chest bother the grandfather?","options":["It was made from a different kind of wood","It was a centimetre short and he noticed it for the rest of his life","It had been damaged when a seam burst","It did not fit the lid properly"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 5 says one corner was a centimetre short and he never stopped noticing it."},{"id":"b3u8-e3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What did the grandmother do when a seam finally burst?","options":["She asked a neighbour to repair it","She replaced the lining with new cloth","She mended it herself while sitting on the couch","She moved the chest to her parents\' house"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"Paragraph 5 says she mended it herself, sitting on the couch with the lid open beside her."},{"id":"b3u8-e4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What does the grandmother\'s arithmetic show about her attitude to the chest?","options":["She was more interested in the years of use than in the flaw","She had never noticed the short corner","She thought the chest should be sold","She believed the measurements were correct after all"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"By dividing the flaw across nine thousand days she reduces it to almost nothing, showing that its usefulness mattered more to her."},{"id":"b3u8-e5","type":"multiple-choice","question":"As used in paragraph 4, the word \\"trimmed\\" most nearly means…","options":["cut a little to make it neater","folded over and fixed in place","replaced with new material","decorated with a pattern"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"The trimming followed the sewing of the cloth lining and produced neat edges, so it means cutting a little to make something tidier."},{"id":"b3u8-e6","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The pipe ___ during the frost and flooded the kitchen.\\"","options":["burst","dripped","trimmed","polished"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To burst is to break open suddenly, which is what a pipe does in freezing weather."},{"id":"b3u8-e7","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"It takes practice to ___ a straight seam by hand.\\"","options":["sew","trim","polish","drip"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To sew is to join pieces of cloth with thread, which is what makes a seam."},{"id":"b3u8-e8","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"He felt ___ of the way he had spoken to his brother.\\"","options":["ashamed","grateful","elegant","satisfactory"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Ashamed means feeling upset and embarrassed about a bad action, which fits regretting how he spoke."},{"id":"b3u8-e9","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"They spent the morning trying to ___ the brass handles until they shone.\\"","options":["polish","trim","sew","burst"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To polish something is to rub it in order to make it shiny, which is what the handles needed."},{"id":"b3u8-e10","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The path climbed ___ along the side of the valley.\\"","options":["upwards","overnight","shortly","aloud"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"An upwards movement goes vertically towards the direction above, which describes a path climbing a valley side."}]},"9":{"id":"b3-u9","book":3,"unit":9,"title":"The Last Match of the Season","level":"B2","topic":"A school wrestling season and a surprising final","generatedAt":"2026-09-23","wordCount":509,"paragraphs":["The wrestling team had lost every match that season, and by March the [[entire]] squad had stopped pretending otherwise. We were not bad. We were simply younger than everyone else, and in a sport where weight and reach matter, youth is expensive. We trained three evenings a week in a hall that smelled of old mats, and we learned to [[wrestle]] badly but with enthusiasm.","Our problem had a name, and the name was Dalton. He was two years older, he had never lost, and he liked to [[boast]] about it in the corridor. He was not a [[bully]] in the obvious sense — he never hit anyone — but he had a way of making a room feel smaller when he entered it, and he was unusually good at finding the one thing you were [[sore]] about.","I was his [[opponent]] that spring, which meant I had four weeks to prepare. My coach, who also taught maths, took a scientific approach. She put me on a diet that involved a great deal of [[carbohydrate]] and very little sugar, and she made me watch every match Dalton had ever wrestled. He was not my only [[rival]] that season, but he was the only one I thought about.","Training was [[torture]] in the ordinary sense of the word: two hours of drilling, then sprints until my legs would not [[bounce]] any more, then a final round in which I had to [[strain]] against a partner who was stronger than me and simply refuse to move.","My [[ally]] in all this was a boy called Sam, who had lost to Dalton twice and had decided that helping me was the next best thing to beating him himself. Sam was not strong, but he was patient. He timed every hold with the clock on the wall, his eyes fixed on its [[dial]], and he wrote the results in a notebook.","The final was not dramatic. Dalton scored first and then made a mistake, and I did what Sam had told me to do, which was to hold on and wait. By the third round he was [[crawl|crawling]] back to the centre of the mat after every break, and I was still standing. When the whistle went, the referee raised my hand. I had never seriously expected to [[defeat]] him.","I had expected to feel triumph. What I felt was mostly relief, and something less comfortable. Dalton, seen up close and out of breath, was not a [[dominant]] figure at all; he was a tired seventeen-year-old who had [[quarrel|quarrelled]] with his coach that morning. He had never known the [[sting]] of losing before, and it showed.","In the corridor afterwards he did not speak to me, and I did not speak to him. We simply [[nod|nodded]] at each other, which was more than either of us had expected. He asked for no [[mercy]] and I offered none; there was nothing to forgive, and both of us knew it. Sam wrote the result in his notebook, and that was the end of the season."],"exercises":[{"id":"b3u9-e1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What is the passage mainly about?","options":["A wrestler who beats a stronger opponent and finds the victory less satisfying than he expected","A coach who is forced to leave the school team","Two friends who fall out over a notebook","A school that decides to close its wrestling programme"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"The narrator prepares for and wins the final, then discovers that his opponent is far less impressive in person than he had imagined."},{"id":"b3u9-e2","type":"multiple-choice","question":"How did the coach change the narrator\'s preparation?","options":["She made him train with the older students","She changed his diet and made him study Dalton\'s matches","She told him to avoid watching his opponent","She moved the training sessions to the morning"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 3 says she put him on a high-carbohydrate diet and made him watch every match Dalton had wrestled."},{"id":"b3u9-e3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What did Sam contribute to the narrator\'s preparation?","options":["He trained as a sparring partner every evening","He persuaded the coach to change the tactics","He timed every hold and recorded the results in a notebook","He spoke to Dalton on the narrator\'s behalf"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"Paragraph 5 describes Sam timing each hold with the clock and writing the results down."},{"id":"b3u9-e4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"Why was the victory less satisfying than the narrator had expected?","options":["He felt he had won by luck rather than skill","Dalton turned out to be an ordinary, tired teenager rather than a monster","The referee\'s decision was unpopular with the crowd","His coach was not there to see it"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 7 says Dalton was not a dominant figure at all but a tired seventeen-year-old who had quarrelled with his coach that morning."},{"id":"b3u9-e5","type":"multiple-choice","question":"As used in paragraph 2, the word \\"sore\\" most nearly means…","options":["painful or sensitive","angry and resentful","physically injured","easily tired"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Dalton was good at finding the thing you were sore about, that is, a subject that felt painful or sensitive to you."},{"id":"b3u9-e6","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The champions were ___ by a team that had not won a game all year.\\"","options":["defeated","boasted","nodded","crawled"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To defeat someone is to beat them, which is what the weaker team did to the champions."},{"id":"b3u9-e7","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"In the argument about the new road, the farmers found an unexpected ___ in the local doctor.\\"","options":["ally","opponent","rival","bully"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"An ally is someone who agrees to help or support you, which is what the doctor became."},{"id":"b3u9-e8","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"He likes to ___ about how quickly he can solve these puzzles.\\"","options":["boast","quarrel","strain","crawl"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To boast is to talk about how great one is, which fits someone praising his own speed."},{"id":"b3u9-e9","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"She read the ___ report in one sitting.\\"","options":["entire","sore","dominant","mutual"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Entire means the whole thing, so reading the entire report means reading all of it."},{"id":"b3u9-e10","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The ball will ___ higher if you drop it on a hard surface.\\"","options":["bounce","crawl","sting","wrestle"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To bounce is to move up and away from a surface after hitting it, which is what a ball does."}]},"10":{"id":"b3-u10","book":3,"unit":10,"title":"Three Days on Foxglove Road","level":"B2","topic":"A babysitter\'s diary of an eventful weekend","generatedAt":"2026-09-23","wordCount":404,"paragraphs":["Friday. The Millers\' front door opened before I had finished knocking. Mrs Miller was already in her coat, holding a list, and the baby was already crying. She is in the middle of a [[divorce]], which is why she needs help three evenings a week. \\"He has a [[nap]] at two,\\" she said, \\"and if he will not sleep, read to him [[aloud]]. He likes the one with the [[rhyme]].\\" Then she was gone.","The [[infant]] is seven months old. He has almost no hair — his head is nearly [[bald]] — and a deep [[wrinkle]] between his eyebrows that makes him look permanently worried. He is also, I discovered within an hour, much stronger than he appears.","Saturday. It rained all day, so we stayed inside. I read the rhyming book nine times. I [[pat|patted]] his back until my arm ached. I sang, badly. At four o\'clock he fell asleep on a [[blanket]] in the middle of the floor, and I sat on the sofa and did not move for forty minutes.","The trouble came on Sunday afternoon. I had put him in his [[wagon]] in the garden while I carried the washing in, and when I turned round the wagon was empty. He had [[vanish|vanished]]. For about four seconds I could not think of a single sensible thing to do.","Then I heard him. He was behind the hedge, [[creep|creeping]] along the fence with the speed of an animal that has been practising. I picked him up. The [[relief]] was enormous and entirely physical, like putting down something heavy.","It was not a [[kidnap]], obviously. Nobody was taking him anywhere. But for those four seconds I understood why parents use the word [[urgent]] about small things: the world narrows to [[nowhere]] but the missing person, and everything else stops.","Monday. Mrs Miller came home and told me that her son\'s [[absence]] from the garden was normal, that he had been crawling for three weeks, and that I should not worry. I said I had not worried. Then I described the four seconds, and she laughed until she had to sit down.","He [[imitate|imitates]] everything now. If I [[suck]] air through my teeth, he does it. If I sigh, he sighs. He can even [[reproduce]] the exact sound of the doorbell, which is impressive and slightly alarming. I have agreed to go back next weekend. I am not sure I was actually asked."],"exercises":[{"id":"b3u10-e1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What is the passage mainly about?","options":["A babysitter\'s account of a weekend looking after a baby","A mother who returns to work after a long absence","Two neighbours who argue about a garden","A child who is afraid of the doorbell"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"The passage is written as a diary covering Friday to Monday and follows the babysitter\'s care of a seven-month-old baby."},{"id":"b3u10-e2","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What did Mrs Miller tell the babysitter to do if the baby would not sleep?","options":["Take him out in the wagon","Read to him aloud, especially the book with the rhyme","Give him a warm blanket","Leave him alone until he settled"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"In paragraph 1 she says to read to him aloud and mentions that he likes the one with the rhyme."},{"id":"b3u10-e3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What happened on Sunday afternoon?","options":["The baby refused to eat his lunch","The baby crawled out of the wagon and disappeared behind the hedge","The washing was blown into the garden","The wagon broke while it was being pushed"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 4 says the wagon was empty and the baby had vanished; paragraph 5 reveals he was behind the hedge."},{"id":"b3u10-e4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"Why did Mrs Miller laugh when she heard the story?","options":["Because the babysitter had lost the wagon","Because the panic had been unnecessary — the baby had been crawling for weeks","Because the baby had learned to say a new word","Because she had forgotten to leave the list"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 7 says she explained that the baby\'s absence from the garden was normal, as he had been crawling for three weeks."},{"id":"b3u10-e5","type":"multiple-choice","question":"As used in paragraph 6, the word \\"urgent\\" most nearly means…","options":["needing immediate attention","extremely frightening","difficult to explain","lasting only a short time"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"The sentence describes the world narrowing to the missing person, so \\"urgent\\" means important and needing to be dealt with now."},{"id":"b3u10-e6","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The children usually have a short ___ after lunch.\\"","options":["nap","rhyme","wagon","blanket"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"A nap is a short sleep during the day, which is what children often take after lunch."},{"id":"b3u10-e7","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"My keys seem to ___ whenever I am in a hurry.\\"","options":["vanish","creep","pat","suck"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To vanish is to go away suddenly, which is what the keys appear to do."},{"id":"b3u10-e8","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"It was a ___ to hear that everyone had arrived safely.\\"","options":["relief","thrill","absence","wrinkle"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Relief is the good feeling that comes after something worrying has ended, which fits news that everyone is safe."},{"id":"b3u10-e9","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Parrots can ___ human speech surprisingly well.\\"","options":["imitate","vanish","suck","pat"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To imitate someone is to act in the same way they do, which is what parrots do with human speech."},{"id":"b3u10-e10","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"He began to go ___ in his early thirties.\\"","options":["bald","wrinkled","urgent","aloud"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"A bald person has no hair, which is what \\"go bald\\" describes."}]},"11":{"id":"b3-u11","book":3,"unit":11,"title":"The Summer My Cousin Came to Stay","level":"B2","topic":"Family, museums and a summer visit","generatedAt":"2026-09-23","wordCount":418,"paragraphs":["My [[cousin]] arrived by [[plane]] in the middle of [[july|July]], and for three weeks our house belonged to her. Her own family was moving to another city, so my parents had agreed to [[host]] her until the last of the boxes had been unpacked. On the first evening we did what we always do with a new [[guest]]: we [[welcome|welcomed]] her with far too much food and a long list of places she simply had to see before she left.","That summer the [[temperature]] rarely fell below twenty-five, even at night, and the old [[refrigerator]] in the corner of the kitchen hummed as though it were complaining. In the garden the roses were in full [[bloom]], and every second morning my mother cut an armful of them and carried them indoors in a wicker [[basket]] that had once belonged to her grandmother. My cousin said the flowers made the house feel as if something were still happening in it.","On the Saturday we took the train into the city to see the [[museum]]. A decade earlier it had been rebuilt, and the new [[architecture]] had almost nothing in common with the photographs in my father\'s old guidebook: glass where there had once been stone. We climbed to the [[upper]] floor, where a long [[carpet]] in faded red ran the whole length of the hall, and spent a slow hour in the [[gallery]] of nineteenth-century landscapes. One of the paintings was almost entirely [[pink]], which my cousin found ridiculous and I found rather brave.","Near the entrance to that room stood a small [[desk]], and behind it an elderly attendant who had clearly been watching visitors for most of his working life. The [[modern]] wing, which had opened only that spring, was crowded and loud; this room was empty apart from the two of us. \\"People come for the new building,\\" he said, \\"and then they walk straight past the paintings.\\"","On the Sunday my uncle lent us his [[canoe]], and we spent the afternoon on the river, arguing about nothing in particular and stopping whenever we felt like it. On her last evening we went to the [[theater]] in the square and watched a comedy we barely understood, laughing at the wrong moments. It was a silly play about a hotel inspector and a missing dog, and I have never laughed so hard at anything since. She flew home the following week. We still write to each other, though not nearly as often as either of us intends."],"exercises":[{"id":"b3u11-e1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"Why did the cousin come to stay with the family?","options":["She was studying at a university in the city","Her own family was moving to another city","She had been invited to an exhibition","She wanted to learn how to use a canoe"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 1 explains that her family was moving to another city, so the narrator\'s parents agreed to host her until the boxes were unpacked."},{"id":"b3u11-e2","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What did the narrator\'s mother do with the roses?","options":["Sold them at the market","Left them in the garden to bloom","Cut them and carried them indoors in a basket","Gave them to the museum attendant"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"Paragraph 2 says she cut an armful of them every second morning and carried them indoors in a wicker basket."},{"id":"b3u11-e3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"How did the room with the landscapes compare with the modern wing?","options":["It was busier and much louder","It was empty while the modern wing was crowded","It had been closed for repairs","It contained only recently painted works"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 4 states that the modern wing was crowded and loud, while the landscape room was empty apart from the narrator and her cousin."},{"id":"b3u11-e4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What can be inferred about the elderly attendant?","options":["He had painted the landscapes himself","He thought visitors overlooked the paintings","He wanted the museum to be modernised","He disliked the narrator and her cousin"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"His remark that people come for the new building and then walk straight past the paintings shows he had noticed how visitors ignored the older collection."},{"id":"b3u11-e5","type":"multiple-choice","question":"As used in paragraph 4, the word \\"crowded\\" most nearly means…","options":["full of people","badly designed","newly opened","difficult to reach"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"\\"Crowded\\" is contrasted with the empty landscape room, so it means full of people."},{"id":"b3u11-e6","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The new library is a striking piece of ___, all glass and sharp angles.\\"","options":["architecture","temperature","basket","carpet"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Architecture is the design and form of a building, which is exactly what a glass-and-angles library would be praised for."},{"id":"b3u11-e7","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"She arranged the apples neatly in a ___ and carried them to the market.\\"","options":["desk","basket","gallery","theater"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"A basket is a woven container used for carrying things, which fits apples being taken to market."},{"id":"b3u11-e8","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The lilies will ___ in late spring if the weather stays mild.\\"","options":["host","welcome","bloom","plane"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"To bloom is to produce an open flower, which is what lilies do in late spring."},{"id":"b3u11-e9","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The ___ in the room dropped sharply once the windows were opened.\\"","options":["temperature","architecture","guest","carpet"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"A temperature is a measure of how hot or cold something is, and opening windows makes it drop."},{"id":"b3u11-e10","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"We paddled the ___ gently along the edge of the lake.\\"","options":["museum","gallery","refrigerator","canoe"],"answerIndex":3,"explanation":"A canoe is a long, light boat with pointed ends, and it is paddled rather than driven."}]},"12":{"id":"b3-u12","book":3,"unit":12,"title":"The Demonstration","level":"B2","topic":"A school science demonstration about pressure","generatedAt":"2026-09-23","wordCount":401,"paragraphs":["Mr. Alvarez taught chemistry with the calm of a man who had already seen everything, and his [[casual]] manner had a strange effect on us: the more relaxed he looked, the more carefully we listened. On the morning of the demonstration he told us, without any drama at all, that what we were about to watch would demand genuine [[caution]].","The equipment was ordinary. On a [[steel]] bench at the front of the room stood a sealed container about the size of a small [[barrel]], and beside it lay a cardboard [[kit]] of tubes, clips and a thermometer. Above the bench a wooden [[beam]] ran the length of the ceiling, and he glanced up at it once before he began. Into a jug of warm water he stirred several spoons of sugar and a spoonful of dried yeast — a living [[germ]], he reminded us, not so very different in principle from the ones that make bread rise. Given time, the sugar would [[dissolve]] completely, and then the yeast would begin to feed on it.","We had [[anticipate|anticipated]] something slow and quiet. For ten minutes nothing happened at all. Then a thin [[puff]] of gas escaped from under the lid, followed by another, and the room filled with the warm [[scent]] of bread. [[contrary|Contrary]] to everything we had assumed, the change was [[swift]]: the pressure climbed faster than the dial could follow, and a few of us began to edge towards the door.","That, of course, was the point of the lesson. Mr. Alvarez had [[fasten|fastened]] the lid himself the previous evening, and the choice had been entirely [[deliberate]] — he wanted us to see what happens when a gas is given nowhere to go. Nothing [[explode|exploded]] in the way that films suggest. There was no broken glass and no fire. The lid simply left, and a wave of foam [[scatter|scattered]] across the first two rows of desks. He wiped the table with a [[rag]], [[toss|tossed]] it into a bucket, and asked us to write down what we had observed.","The whole event lasted less than a second, yet he was plainly delighted. His small [[triumph]] lasted all morning, and nobody in that class has ever forgotten the difference between a reaction that is fast and a reaction that is dangerous. Pressure, he told us as the bell rang, is not a force. It is a force with nowhere to go."],"exercises":[{"id":"b3u12-e1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What effect did Mr. Alvarez\'s casual manner have on the class?","options":["They ignored his instructions","They listened more carefully","They asked him to cancel the demonstration","They found him difficult to take seriously"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 1 says that the more relaxed he looked, the more carefully they listened."},{"id":"b3u12-e2","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What did he add to the jug of warm water?","options":["Sugar and dried yeast","Salt and vinegar","Soap and sugar","Flour and cooking oil"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Paragraph 2 describes him stirring in several spoons of sugar and a spoonful of dried yeast."},{"id":"b3u12-e3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"Why had he fastened the lid himself the night before?","options":["He had forgotten to do it earlier","He wanted the pressure to build up on purpose","He wanted to keep the smell inside the container","He was afraid that a student would damage the kit"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 4 explains that the choice was deliberate: he wanted the class to see what happens when a gas is given nowhere to go."},{"id":"b3u12-e4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What does the writer suggest about the demonstration?","options":["It was far more dangerous than the teacher had expected","It was carefully planned to make a particular point","It failed because the yeast had stopped working","It lasted much longer than a single second"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"The teacher fastened the lid himself deliberately and the writer says this was the point of the lesson, so the whole event was planned."},{"id":"b3u12-e5","type":"multiple-choice","question":"As used in paragraph 3, \\"swift\\" most nearly means…","options":["fast","gentle","unexpected","dangerous"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"The pressure climbed faster than the dial could follow, so \\"swift\\" here means fast."},{"id":"b3u12-e6","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Take great ___ when you carry that tray; the glasses are extremely thin.\\"","options":["caution","scent","triumph","kit"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Caution is care and attention taken in order to avoid danger or damage, which is exactly what a thin glass tray requires."},{"id":"b3u12-e7","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Stir the powder until the grains ___ completely in the water.\\"","options":["fasten","dissolve","toss","scatter"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"To dissolve is to mix into a liquid until the substance disappears, which is what grains of powder do in water."},{"id":"b3u12-e8","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"She ___ the buttons of her coat before stepping out into the wind.\\"","options":["scattered","dissolved","fastened","exploded"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"To fasten something is to close or attach it, and buttons are fastened before going out."},{"id":"b3u12-e9","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"A thin ___ of smoke rose from the chimney and disappeared into the grey sky.\\"","options":["puff","kit","barrel","rag"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"A puff is a small amount of smoke or steam, which matches a thin trail rising from a chimney."},{"id":"b3u12-e10","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"He ___ the keys onto the table and sat down without a word.\\"","options":["fastened","dissolved","anticipated","tossed"],"answerIndex":3,"explanation":"To toss something is to throw it lightly, which describes dropping keys carelessly onto a table."}]},"13":{"id":"b3-u13","book":3,"unit":13,"title":"The Man Who Kept the Harbour Quiet","level":"B2","topic":"A port town and the man who brought it rules","generatedAt":"2026-09-23","wordCount":422,"paragraphs":["For most of the nineteenth century the town lived off its [[harbor]]. Ships arrived from every direction, and a [[vessel]] that had survived a long [[voyage]] would tie up at the [[port]] to unload timber, salt and coffee. Anyone [[aboard]] one of those ships had a story to sell, and the taverns along the waterfront were never quiet. Half the families in the town had a relative on one of those vessels, and the other half had a relative who had sailed away and never come back.","The people who [[inhabit|inhabited]] the town were not, on the whole, fond of rules. Sailors, miners and traders had little in common except the water, and disagreements were settled in the street as often as in a courtroom. In the middle of the square stood a stone [[fountain]] paid for by a local [[millionaire]] who wanted his name remembered, and it was there, every morning, that the town\'s only [[sheriff]] began his round.","His name was Dan. He had arrived with nothing, [[march|marched]] into the square on his first morning, and announced that from that day the law would be the same for everybody. It was an unpopular promise, and he kept it. He did not [[worship]] the rich and he did not fear the poor; he simply [[enforce|enforced]] what had been written down, whether or not he happened to agree with it.","Not everyone was grateful. A [[bitter]] merchant once told him that no man could [[unify]] a town with a notebook. Dan agreed, and went on writing in it. He rarely carried anything more threatening than a pen; the famous [[trigger]] on his belt was, according to the local children, mostly decoration, and there is no record of a single [[bullet]] ever being fired in his time.","He died in his sixties, in bed, of nothing more dramatic than old age. He had [[startle|startled]] the town on his very first morning by fining its richest man, and nobody had entirely forgiven or forgotten it. Some said the storm that followed his funeral was the work of the [[devil]]; Dan, who had [[sweat|sweated]] through every summer of his working life without complaint, would have said it was simply the sea. Boats still [[drift]] past the fountain on their way in, and the harbour he looked after for thirty years is busier now than it has ever been. The children who once said his gun was decoration now bring their own children to sit on the steps of that fountain and tell them about him."],"exercises":[{"id":"b3u13-e1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What is the passage mainly about?","options":["How a port town grew rich from the timber trade","How one sheriff brought consistent rules to a disorderly town","Why sailors avoided the town\'s waterfront taverns","The history of a stone fountain in a town square"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"The passage describes the harbour town, the sheriff Dan and the single set of rules he enforced for everyone."},{"id":"b3u13-e2","type":"multiple-choice","question":"Who paid for the fountain in the square?","options":["The sheriff","A local millionaire","A group of shipowners","The miners of the town"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 2 says the fountain was paid for by a local millionaire who wanted his name remembered."},{"id":"b3u13-e3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What is said about Dan\'s gun?","options":["He used it to arrest the richest man","It was mostly decoration and no bullet was ever fired","It was given to him by the merchant","He kept it locked in a courtroom"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 4 says the trigger was, according to local children, mostly decoration, and that there is no record of a bullet being fired in his time."},{"id":"b3u13-e4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What can be inferred about the merchant in paragraph 4?","options":["He thought a notebook was a weak instrument for keeping order","He wanted to become sheriff himself","He had been fined by Dan the previous week","He had never met Dan before that morning"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"His remark that no man could unify a town with a notebook shows he doubted written rules were enough to keep order."},{"id":"b3u13-e5","type":"multiple-choice","question":"As used in paragraph 3, \\"unpopular\\" most nearly means…","options":["not liked by most people","illegal at the time","expensive to keep","difficult to understand"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"The promise was unpopular, meaning most people did not like it, yet he kept it anyway."},{"id":"b3u13-e6","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The fishing ___ returned to the bay just before dark.\\"","options":["vessel","harbor","fountain","trigger"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"A vessel is a large ship or boat, which is what returns to a bay."},{"id":"b3u13-e7","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Every passenger ___ the plane was asked to remain seated until the engines stopped.\\"","options":["ashore","aboard","apart","awake"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Someone aboard a plane is in or on it, which is the only option that fits passengers on an aircraft."},{"id":"b3u13-e8","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The police exist to ___ the law, not to make it.\\"","options":["drift","unify","enforce","worship"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"To enforce means to make people follow a rule, which is what police do with the law."},{"id":"b3u13-e9","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"After weeks at sea with no crew, the empty boat began to ___ slowly towards the rocks.\\"","options":["march","sweat","startle","drift"],"answerIndex":3,"explanation":"To drift means to be moved slowly by wind or water, which describes an unmanned boat."},{"id":"b3u13-e10","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"It was a ___ disappointment, and he never spoke of the matter again.\\"","options":["bitter","swift","shallow","tender"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"A bitter person feels upset or angry about something, and a bitter disappointment is one that leaves lasting resentment."}]},"14":{"id":"b3-u14","book":3,"unit":14,"title":"The Boy in the Emperor\'s Kitchen","level":"B2","topic":"A palace kitchen and a determined apprentice","generatedAt":"2026-09-23","wordCount":424,"paragraphs":["When he was twelve, Emi was sent to the palace to work as an [[apprentice]]. His father had been a [[chef]] in a small town and had taught him to chop an onion without once looking at it, and that, it turned out, was enough to get him through the gate. He was given a thin [[robe]], a corner to sleep in, and a list of duties longer than he could read.","The kitchen was [[massive]], and in summer it was a [[horrible]] place to be: four fires, no windows, and a head cook who believed that shouting was a form of teaching. Emi was [[diligent]], which is not the same thing as talented. He was not clever, and he knew it, which is a kind of intelligence in itself. He arrived first and left last, he wrote down everything he was told, and he learned to judge meat by the [[fiber|fibers]] alone — whether it had been hung long enough, and whether it would be tough or tender.","In his third week the head cook cut his hand badly and began to [[bleed]] through the cloth. Everyone else started to [[panic]]; the [[emperor|Emperor\'s]] supper was already late, and a late supper at court was a serious matter. Emi did not panic. He pressed a clean [[bandage]] against the wound, [[kneel|knelt]] beside the older man, and quietly [[assure|assured]] him that the soup could wait ten minutes and that so, for that matter, could the court.","That evening the head cook did something he had never done before: he sat down with Emi and asked what he thought of the soup. It was the beginning of a [[bond]] that lasted thirty years. Emi learned to cook for the [[crown|Crown]], which meant cooking without salt, without spice, and without any of the small [[luxury|luxuries]] that make a dish memorable — the body of a ruler, he was told, is a [[priority]], not a pleasure.","Years later, when the old cook retired, Emi took his place. He was strict, and he could be [[impolite]] when he was tired, but he never [[scold|scolded]] anyone for asking a question. The night before his own [[departure]], he walked through the kitchen one last time and told the newest boy the same thing his father had told him: that a good cook is not someone who never makes a mistake, but someone who notices. He never became famous, and his name appears in no history of the palace kitchens, but for thirty years the food that reached the table was his."],"exercises":[{"id":"b3u14-e1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"Why was Emi accepted at the palace?","options":["His family was wealthy and well known","He could chop an onion without looking at it","He had studied at a famous cooking school","The head cook was a friend of his father"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 1 says his father had taught him to chop an onion without looking at it, and that this was enough to get him through the gate."},{"id":"b3u14-e2","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What was the kitchen like in summer?","options":["Cool and spacious","Hot, airless and unpleasant","Empty because the court had left","Newly rebuilt and well lit"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 2 describes four fires, no windows and a shouting head cook, which the writer calls a horrible place to be."},{"id":"b3u14-e3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What did Emi do when the head cook was injured?","options":["He ran to fetch the emperor","He bandaged the wound and reassured him","He finished the soup himself","He panicked and left the kitchen"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 3 says he pressed a clean bandage against the wound, knelt beside the man and assured him the soup could wait."},{"id":"b3u14-e4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"Why was the food cooked for the crown plain?","options":["The cooks had never learned to use spices","Luxuries were thought unsuitable for a ruler\'s body","The emperor disliked the head cook","Salt was too expensive for the palace to buy"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 4 explains that he cooked without salt or spice because the body of a ruler was a priority rather than a pleasure."},{"id":"b3u14-e5","type":"multiple-choice","question":"As used in paragraph 2, \\"diligent\\" most nearly means…","options":["hard-working and careful","naturally gifted","quiet and shy","quick to lose his temper"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"The paragraph says he arrived first, left last and wrote everything down, and contrasts this with being talented — so diligent means hard-working."},{"id":"b3u14-e6","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Every young ___ spends years learning the trade from a master.\\"","options":["apprentice","luxury","priority","bandage"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"An apprentice is a person who learns a job from a skilled person, which fits someone spending years with a master."},{"id":"b3u14-e7","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The deep cut on his arm would not stop ___.\\"","options":["bonding","bleeding","kneeling","scolding"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"To bleed is to lose blood, which is what a deep cut does."},{"id":"b3u14-e8","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"She ___ us that the parcel would arrive well before Friday.\\"","options":["scolded","panicked","assured","knelt"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"To assure people is to tell them something is true so they stop worrying, which fits a promise about a delivery."},{"id":"b3u14-e9","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The hotel offers every ___ imaginable, from a private pool to a personal driver.\\"","options":["fiber","robe","departure","luxury"],"answerIndex":3,"explanation":"A luxury is an expensive thing that is pleasant but not necessary, which describes private pools and personal drivers."},{"id":"b3u14-e10","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The teacher ___ the class for arriving without their books.\\"","options":["scolded","assured","bonded","knelt"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To scold means to criticise someone angrily for doing something wrong, which fits a teacher\'s reaction to forgotten books."}]},"15":{"id":"b3-u15","book":3,"unit":15,"title":"How Monkey Lost His Home","level":"B2","topic":"A folk tale about a famine and a clever plan","generatedAt":"2026-09-23","wordCount":423,"paragraphs":["In the days of the [[pharaoh|Pharaohs]], in a country of wide fields and a slow river, there lived a monkey who had no home of his own. He slept in the branches of a fig tree at the edge of a village and watched the farmers go out to work. His [[routine]] never varied: he woke at first light, ate whatever the tree gave him, and spent the afternoon watching other people be busy. He was [[bless|blessed]] with excellent eyes and a very poor memory for consequences.","That year the [[harvest]] was poor. The river rose late, the [[cereal]] crops came in thin, and by the following spring the village was facing a [[famine]]. The elders called an [[assembly]] in the square and talked for a long time about what should be done. The monkey, sitting on the wall, listened to every word and understood perhaps half of it.","What he did understand was that the storehouses were now guarded, and so he made a [[scheme]]. In the back wall there was a hole no wider than the [[diameter]] of a bowl. Every night, while the guards were at their most tired, he squeezed through it, filled his cheeks with grain and carried it back to the tree. For a while the [[theft]] went unnoticed. The monkey grew fat and the villagers grew thin, and he began to believe that he had [[exploit|exploited]] them cleverly rather than simply stolen from them.","It might have gone on for years if he had not grown careless. One evening a woman came out to [[roast]] grain over the fire and saw him. By morning the whole village knew. The elders did not shout, which was worse. They asked him, very quietly, whether a [[slim]] monkey with full cheeks looked like a [[merry]] sight in a year of hunger. He had no answer. They did not [[pardon]] him, and they did not drive him out; they simply stopped speaking to him.","The [[affair]] ended as such things do. The village survived the winter, partly because a trader arrived with sacks of grain and a new [[stove]] on his cart, and partly because the next [[harvest]] was generous enough to make everyone [[cheerful]] again. The monkey was allowed to stay, but the fig tree was cut down for firewood, and he never found another place that felt like his own. And that is why, even today, a monkey will sit on the ground to eat a [[ripe]] [[nut]] — and why he never stays anywhere for very long."],"exercises":[{"id":"b3u15-e1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"Why did the monkey begin stealing grain?","options":["He wanted to sell it to a trader","The village was facing a famine and the storehouses were guarded","The elders had asked him to guard the storehouse","He had been driven out of the fig tree"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 3 says the storehouses were now guarded and that this is why he made his scheme."},{"id":"b3u15-e2","type":"multiple-choice","question":"How did the monkey get into the storehouse?","options":["Through the main door while the guards slept","Through a small hole in the back wall","By climbing down onto the roof","With the help of one of the guards"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 3 describes a hole in the back wall, no wider than the diameter of a bowl, which he squeezed through."},{"id":"b3u15-e3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"How did the elders react when they discovered what he had done?","options":["They beat him in the square","They asked him a quiet question and then stopped speaking to him","They forgave him immediately","They sent him away to the pharaoh"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 4 says they did not shout, asked him a quiet question, did not pardon him and simply stopped speaking to him."},{"id":"b3u15-e4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What is the passage mainly about?","options":["How a village survived a long famine","How a monkey\'s cleverness cost him his home","Why monkeys prefer to live in fig trees","The trade routes of the ancient world"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"The tale follows the monkey\'s scheme and its result: he keeps his life but loses the tree that was his home."},{"id":"b3u15-e5","type":"multiple-choice","question":"As used in paragraph 3, \\"scheme\\" most nearly means…","options":["a plan","a hiding place","a type of grain","an argument"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"He makes a scheme — a plan — for getting into the guarded storehouse."},{"id":"b3u15-e6","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The farmers celebrated a record ___ of wheat that autumn.\\"","options":["harvest","famine","diameter","routine"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"A harvest is the act of collecting food from farming, so a record harvest is a very large one."},{"id":"b3u15-e7","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"A long ___ left the whole region with almost nothing to eat.\\"","options":["assembly","famine","stove","nut"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"A famine is a long period with little or no food, which is exactly what leaves a region without anything to eat."},{"id":"b3u15-e8","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The ___ of the pipe is only five centimetres, so it blocks very easily.\\"","options":["routine","theft","diameter","scheme"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"The diameter of a round thing is the length across its centre, which is how the width of a pipe is measured."},{"id":"b3u15-e9","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The children were ___ as they opened their presents on the kitchen floor.\\"","options":["cheerful","ripe","slim","stove"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"A cheerful person is happy and pleasant, which fits children opening presents."},{"id":"b3u15-e10","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Only fruit that is fully ___ should be picked and taken to market.\\"","options":["merry","slim","cheerful","ripe"],"answerIndex":3,"explanation":"Ripe fruit is ready to be eaten, which is the condition fruit must reach before it is picked for market."}]},"16":{"id":"b3-u16","book":3,"unit":16,"title":"The Name on the Form","level":"B2","topic":"A young writer learns about privacy","generatedAt":"2026-09-23","wordCount":420,"paragraphs":["At fifteen, Maya had decided that she would spend her life in [[journalism]]. Like most [[adolescent|adolescents]], she was entirely certain about it, and her certainty, far from [[hinder|hindering]] her, carried her forward. She had the [[aptitude]] for it — she noticed things that other people walked past, and she could describe a room in three sentences — but she had not yet learned that the most important question a reporter asks is not \\"what happened\\" but \\"whose business is this\\".","Her room was [[tidy]] in a way that slightly worried her parents. On the wall above her desk she kept a [[literary]] map of the town: not streets but stories, pinned to the places where they had happened. She had marked the [[pharmacy]] on the corner, the school hall, and the courthouse where the [[jury|juries]] met, outside which she liked to sit and listen to lawyers argue about nothing. She had already interviewed a bus driver, a retired teacher and a man who repaired bicycles, and she had written about all three of them.","That spring she wrote a piece about the pharmacy. A [[sensible]] piece, she thought: the owner had recently been [[compliment|complimented]] in the local paper for keeping his prices low, and Maya wanted to know how he managed it. She interviewed him, took a [[slice]] of his time — forty minutes, no more — and drank the tea he offered through a paper [[straw]] while he explained the mathematics of very small margins.","Then she made her mistake. A classmate had been ill, and Maya had seen a prescription waiting behind the counter with his name on it. She [[presume|presumed]] that because she had seen it, it was hers to use. She mentioned it in a single line and thought nothing more about it. The classmate\'s family did not agree. His mother telephoned the school, and then the complaints began to [[swell]]; the word she used, over and over, was [[privacy]].","The [[punishment]] was mild — two weeks away from the paper — but the lesson was not. The editor, a calm woman who taught history, did not shout. She asked Maya one question: if the story had been about a [[pill]] and a name, whose [[sorrow]] was being described? Maya took the line out, and the piece ran a week later without it. It was, she said afterwards, the first time she had understood that [[liberty]] and [[justice]] are not the same thing, and that a writer\'s freedom ends exactly where another person\'s private life begins."],"exercises":[{"id":"b3u16-e1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"Which quality made Maya suited to journalism?","options":["She was unusually good at mathematics","She noticed details that other people missed","She had many friends at school","She could read very quickly"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 1 says she noticed things other people walked past and could describe a room in three sentences."},{"id":"b3u16-e2","type":"multiple-choice","question":"Why did Maya interview the pharmacy owner?","options":["She wanted to know how he kept his prices low","She suspected him of breaking the law","He had asked her to write about his shop","She needed medicine for a sick classmate"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Paragraph 3 says he had been complimented for keeping prices low and Maya wanted to know how he managed it."},{"id":"b3u16-e3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What was Maya\'s mistake?","options":["She arrived late for the interview","She used a classmate\'s private medical information in her article","She printed the pharmacy owner\'s name without permission","She refused to correct a spelling error"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 4 says she saw a prescription with a classmate\'s name and mentioned it in a single line of her article."},{"id":"b3u16-e4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What did Maya learn from the incident?","options":["That newspapers should avoid medical subjects altogether","That a writer\'s freedom ends where another person\'s private life begins","That editors should not question young writers","That interviews should always last longer than forty minutes"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"The final paragraph gives Maya\'s own conclusion that liberty and justice are not the same, and that freedom ends where private life begins."},{"id":"b3u16-e5","type":"multiple-choice","question":"As used in paragraph 4, \\"presumed\\" most nearly means…","options":["assumed without being certain","hoped","proved","denied"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"She presumed the information was hers to use simply because she had seen it, which is an assumption rather than a proven fact."},{"id":"b3u16-e6","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The magazine has published serious ___ for more than a century.\\"","options":["journalism","privacy","sorrow","punishment"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Journalism is the work of collecting news for newspapers or broadcast, which is what a magazine publishes."},{"id":"b3u16-e7","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"A judge must decide every case with fairness and ___.\\"","options":["liberty","justice","sorrow","straw"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Justice is fairness in the way people are treated, which is what a judge is expected to apply."},{"id":"b3u16-e8","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"She was careful never to ___ that everyone shared her opinions.\\"","options":["presume","compliment","hinder","swell"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To presume is to believe something is true without being certain, which fits assuming that others agree with you."},{"id":"b3u16-e9","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"He ___ her on the clarity of her presentation.\\"","options":["hindered","presumed","complimented","swelled"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"To compliment is to say something nice about someone, which is what praising the clarity of a presentation does."},{"id":"b3u16-e10","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Reading widely is one of the best habits for a ___ student.\\"","options":["literary","tidy","sensible","adolescent"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"A literary person or thing is involved with literature, and reading widely is what such a student does."}]},"17":{"id":"b3-u17","book":3,"unit":17,"title":"The Island That Is Still Being Built","level":"B2","topic":"A journey to a volcanic island","generatedAt":"2026-09-23","wordCount":400,"paragraphs":["The ferry to the island takes two hours, and for most of that time there is nothing to look at but water. Then the island appears, low and grey at first, and you begin to understand that you are looking at the [[remainder]] of something considerably larger. The whole place [[originate|originated]] in a series of eruptions, and it is still being built: on a clear day you can see steam rising from the [[slope]] of the northern mountain.","Our guide, Ana, had grown up in the only village. She [[navigate|navigated]] the narrow road up the hillside with the confidence of someone who had done it a thousand times, and she explained, without a trace of [[superstition]], what happens when the mountain decides to [[erupt]]. \\"People imagine it is sudden,\\" she said. \\"It is not sudden. The ground [[vibrate|vibrates]] for weeks beforehand. The instruments know long before we do.\\"","At the top we walked across a field of black [[ash]] that [[span|spanned]] several kilometres and was still warm in places. [[lava|Lava]] from an eruption forty years earlier had cooled into a surface so rough that walking on it felt like walking on broken glass. In the [[shallow]] pools between the rocks, rainwater had collected, and a few stubborn plants had somehow taken hold.","Ana had real [[affection]] for the mountain, which I found difficult to share. It had, after all, destroyed her grandmother\'s house, and the family had returned to the ruins to [[retrieve]] what they could before spending two [[miserable]] years in temporary housing in the city, until an [[agency]] found them somewhere permanent. When I asked whether she had ever wanted to leave for good, she [[dismiss|dismissed]] the question with a wave of her hand. \\"Everyone leaves,\\" she said. \\"Then everyone comes back.\\"","That evening I [[wander|wandered]] down to the harbour alone and sat watching the lights of the ferry cross the water towards the mainland. The island is [[confine|confined]] to a small space on the map, yet it has more than enough weather, history and opinion for one afternoon. I had gone there expecting to be impressed by the geology, and I came away thinking mostly about the people, with a [[sympathy]] I had not expected and could not quite explain. It is odd, I thought on the ferry home, how often [[fate]] turns out to be nothing more than the place a person happens to be born."],"exercises":[{"id":"b3u17-e1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"According to Ana, how does the mountain give warning of an eruption?","options":["The sea around the island turns grey","The ground vibrates for weeks beforehand and instruments detect it","The plants in the village begin to die","The steam disappears from the slope"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 2 quotes her saying the ground vibrates for weeks and that the instruments know long before people do."},{"id":"b3u17-e2","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What happened to Ana\'s grandmother\'s house?","options":["It was sold to a hotel company","It was destroyed by the volcano","It was moved to the city in pieces","It is now the village museum"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 4 says the mountain destroyed the house and the family returned to the ruins to retrieve what they could."},{"id":"b3u17-e3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"How did Ana respond when asked whether she had wanted to leave?","options":["She became angry and changed the subject","She dismissed the question and said everyone leaves and then comes back","She said she had never once considered it","She admitted she was planning to move to the mainland"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 4 says she dismissed the question with a wave of her hand and made the remark about everyone leaving and returning."},{"id":"b3u17-e4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What does the writer suggest in the final paragraph?","options":["The geology of the island was disappointing","The people mattered to him more than the landscape","The island should not be inhabited","The ferry service is unreliable in winter"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"He says he had expected to be impressed by the geology but came away thinking mostly about the people."},{"id":"b3u17-e5","type":"multiple-choice","question":"As used in paragraph 4, \\"dismissed\\" most nearly means…","options":["rejected as not worth discussing","answered in an angry voice","explained in great detail","remembered with affection"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"She waved her hand and gave a brief answer, showing that she treated the question as not worth discussing."},{"id":"b3u17-e6","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Scientists warn that the volcano could ___ again at any time.\\"","options":["erupt","vibrate","wander","retrieve"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To erupt is to explode or blow apart, which is what a volcano does."},{"id":"b3u17-e7","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"He spoke with real ___ about the family he had lost.\\"","options":["affection","superstition","fate","sympathy"],"answerIndex":3,"explanation":"Sympathy is a feeling of sadness for another person who feels bad, which fits speaking about someone\'s loss."},{"id":"b3u17-e8","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Thick grey ___ covered the whole town after the fire.\\"","options":["lava","ash","slope","remainder"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Ash is the grey or black powder left when something has burned, which is what covers a town after a fire."},{"id":"b3u17-e9","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The children paddled happily in the ___ water close to the shore.\\"","options":["miserable","confined","shallow","dismissed"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"A shallow thing is not deep, and shallow water near a shore is safe for children to paddle in."},{"id":"b3u17-e10","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"I had to go back upstairs to ___ the keys I had left on the table.\\"","options":["dismiss","wander","span","retrieve"],"answerIndex":3,"explanation":"To retrieve something is to find it and get it back, which is what going back for forgotten keys involves."}]},"18":{"id":"b3-u18","book":3,"unit":18,"title":"The Plan at the Pass","level":"B2","topic":"A mountain pass and a battle that was never fought","generatedAt":"2026-09-23","wordCount":395,"paragraphs":["The pass was the only road into the valley, and everybody in the kingdom knew it. On one side the rock rose in a [[cliff]] so [[steep]] that nobody had ever climbed it; on the other, the ground fell away into a forest of black pines. Whoever held the pass held the valley, and for two hundred years nobody had needed to prove it.","Then the [[troops]] came. They arrived from the north in the last week of summer, and by the first storm they had taken the outer villages. It was not a [[revolution]] — nothing so noble — but an [[invasion]], plain and simple, and the kingdom had perhaps four hundred soldiers with which to answer it. Their commander, a young [[knight]] named Alda, had never fought a battle of this size.","She did not intend to. Her plan, which she explained to her officers by [[flame|flame]]light in a cave above the road, was to let the pass itself do the fighting. The valley\'s defenders would hold the high ground and allow the enemy to come up the slope. When the attackers finally reached the [[summit]], they would find no army waiting for them — only rocks, and the sound of [[thunder]] rolling between the walls of the pass. It was, she admitted, a plan that depended entirely on the weather and on the enemy\'s patience.","The first night was the worst. The sky turned white with [[lightning]], and the rain came down so hard that the fires would not [[blaze]]. Alda\'s [[armor]] was soaked through by midnight and her [[spear]] had been lost somewhere on the path. What she did have was patience and a very clear idea of what the enemy would do next. An army that has nowhere to [[retreat]] to is an army that will eventually [[yield]].","By dawn the pass was quiet. The invaders had begun to [[withdraw]] before first light, and by the following evening not one of them remained in the valley. Alda was given a [[boom|booming]] welcome in the square, and songs were written about the [[warrior]] who had won a battle without fighting one. Later historians would call it the beginning of the valley\'s [[independence]]; Alda herself, who disliked the word [[rebel]] and distrusted the word hero, said only that she had been lucky, and that the mountain had done most of the work."],"exercises":[{"id":"b3u18-e1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"Why was the pass so important?","options":["It was the only road into the valley","It contained the kingdom\'s treasury","It was where the kingdom\'s leader lived","It led directly to the sea"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"The first paragraph states that the pass was the only road into the valley, and that whoever held it held the valley."},{"id":"b3u18-e2","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What was Alda\'s plan?","options":["To attack the enemy camp in the middle of the night","To let the pass and the high ground do the fighting","To retreat into the forest and wait for winter","To ask the neighbouring kingdom for soldiers"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 3 says her plan was to let the pass itself do the fighting by holding the high ground and letting the enemy climb."},{"id":"b3u18-e3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"How does the passage describe the conflict?","options":["As a revolution fought for noble ideals","As a straightforward invasion","As a dispute between two families","As a disagreement over trade routes"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 2 says it was not a revolution but an invasion, plain and simple."},{"id":"b3u18-e4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What can be inferred about Alda from the final paragraph?","options":["She greatly enjoyed being celebrated","She was modest about her own part in the outcome","She wished she had become the ruler","She regretted not attacking the enemy directly"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"She disliked the word rebel, distrusted the word hero, and said only that she had been lucky and the mountain had done most of the work."},{"id":"b3u18-e5","type":"multiple-choice","question":"As used in paragraph 2, \\"plain and simple\\" suggests that the invasion was…","options":["straightforward and without any noble excuse","small and badly organised","carried out by farmers rather than soldiers","easy for the kingdom to defend against"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"The phrase is contrasted with the idea of a noble revolution, so it means the invasion was simply an invasion, with no grand justification."},{"id":"b3u18-e6","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The soldiers were ordered to ___ from the city before dawn.\\"","options":["withdraw","blaze","boom","spear"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To withdraw means to leave a place, which is what troops ordered out of a city would do."},{"id":"b3u18-e7","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"After three weeks without supplies, the fortress had no choice but to ___.\\"","options":["blaze","yield","boom","spear"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"To yield means to give up control or give something away, which is what a starving fortress must do."},{"id":"b3u18-e8","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The ___ was so sheer that we had to use ropes to get down.\\"","options":["flame","spear","cliff","thunder"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"A cliff is a high wall of rock, which fits somewhere that requires ropes to descend."},{"id":"b3u18-e9","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"A sudden flash of ___ lit up the whole field for a second.\\"","options":["lightning","thunder","flame","armor"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Lightning is the bright light seen during a storm, whereas thunder is the sound."},{"id":"b3u18-e10","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"He wore heavy ___ that weighed almost as much as he did.\\"","options":["spear","summit","troops","armor"],"answerIndex":3,"explanation":"Armor is metal worn by soldiers to protect the body, which is what would weigh a great deal."}]},"19":{"id":"b3-u19","book":3,"unit":19,"title":"The Pear Tree and the Empty Bowl","level":"B2","topic":"A folk tale about a quarrel over a tree","generatedAt":"2026-09-23","wordCount":426,"paragraphs":["An old woman lived at the edge of a village with her granddaughter, who was an [[orphan]] and had been with her since she was three days old. They had a small house, a goat, and a pear tree that grew beside the wooden [[bench]] outside the door. In spring a few [[daisy|daisies]] came up through the grass at its foot; in autumn the tree gave them enough fruit to sell a little and [[spare]] a little for the winter.","The trouble began with the neighbour. He claimed the tree stood on his land and he wanted it. His wife was [[pregnant]] with their first child, and he had decided that the family needed the extra ground. On the surface it was a small [[dispute]] about a few metres of soil, but everyone in the village understood that it was really about who had the right to be comfortable. The matter went to the elders, and the elders went home for [[supper]] and thought about it for a week.","While they were thinking, the neighbour began to [[plot]]. One night, long after the village had gone quiet, he [[sneak|sneaked]] into the garden with a saw and cut through the trunk. He did not take the wood. He left the tree lying where it fell, in [[mist]] and darkness, for the old woman to find at first light. It was not theft and it was not violence. It was something colder.","She found it, of course. She stood at the door, looked at the [[empty]] space where the tree had been, and [[sigh|sighed]] once — a long breath that seemed to come from somewhere below her ribs. Then she knelt and touched the cut [[stem]] with her fingers, and what she felt was not [[rage]] but a kind of [[horror]]: the particular horror of watching a thing you have loved for forty years be ended in a single night. Her granddaughter wanted [[revenge]]. The old woman said no.","Instead she did something the village talked about for years. She gathered every pear the fallen tree had been carrying — an armful of hard, unripe fruit — and she carried them to the neighbour\'s door in a [[object-n|basket]]. \\"They are not [[tender]] yet,\\" she said. \\"Keep them until they are.\\" And she went home. There was no [[incident]] after that. The neighbour never spoke of the tree again, and he never quite met her eye. Whatever he had expected, it was not generosity, and the [[shame]] of it stayed with him far longer than any punishment would have."],"exercises":[{"id":"b3u19-e1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What did the neighbour actually want?","options":["The pears growing on the tree","The ground on which the tree stood","The old woman\'s house and goat","The bench outside the door"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 2 says he claimed the tree stood on his land and that his wife was pregnant, so the family needed the extra ground."},{"id":"b3u19-e2","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What did the neighbour do at night?","options":["He picked all the pears","He cut through the trunk of the tree","He set fire to the wooden bench","He moved the boundary stones"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 3 says he sneaked into the garden with a saw and cut through the trunk."},{"id":"b3u19-e3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"How did the old woman respond to what he had done?","options":["She called the elders together immediately","She gave the neighbour the unripe pears","She demanded revenge on her granddaughter\'s behalf","She packed up and left the village"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 5 describes her gathering the hard pears and carrying them to his door in a basket."},{"id":"b3u19-e4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What is suggested about the neighbour at the end of the story?","options":["He was proud of what he had done","He felt ashamed of what he had done","He never understood why she acted as she did","He planted a new tree in the same place"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"The final sentence says the shame of it stayed with him longer than any punishment would have, and that he never quite met her eye."},{"id":"b3u19-e5","type":"multiple-choice","question":"As used in paragraph 3, \\"plot\\" most nearly means…","options":["make a secret plan","mark out a piece of land","tell a story to someone","write down a list of names"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"He began to plot while the elders were thinking — that is, he made a secret plan to act before they decided."},{"id":"b3u19-e6","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The two companies are involved in a long legal ___ over the use of the land.\\"","options":["dispute","shame","horror","mist"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"A dispute is an argument or disagreement, and a legal dispute is one settled formally."},{"id":"b3u19-e7","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"She ___ out of the room so as not to wake the sleeping baby.\\"","options":["sighed","sneaked","plotted","spared"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"To sneak is to move quietly so as not to be seen or heard, which is what she does to avoid waking the baby."},{"id":"b3u19-e8","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The vase fell and shattered, leaving the shelf completely ___.\\"","options":["tender","pregnant","empty","spare"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"An empty container has no things in it, so a shelf with nothing left on it is empty."},{"id":"b3u19-e9","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"He let out a long ___ and put the heavy box down on the floor.\\"","options":["rage","shame","horror","sigh"],"answerIndex":3,"explanation":"To sigh is to breathe out loudly to show tiredness, which fits putting down a heavy box."},{"id":"b3u19-e10","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The meat was so ___ that it fell apart under the fork.\\"","options":["tender","empty","spare","pregnant"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Tender food is soft and easy to chew, which is why it falls apart under a fork."}]},"20":{"id":"b3-u20","book":3,"unit":20,"title":"The First Winter","level":"B2","topic":"A wolf cub learns to survive","generatedAt":"2026-09-23","wordCount":413,"paragraphs":["The [[cub]] was born in a den [[beneath]] the roots of an old pine, in the last week of spring, along with three brothers and a sister. For the first month he knew nothing except warmth, milk and the smell of his mother\'s fur. By midsummer he had begun to explore the clearing, and by autumn he had learned the two lessons that would keep him alive: how to [[leap]] across a stream without falling in, and how to run when his mother made a certain low sound in her throat.","That winter was hard. The [[hail]] came early, and the herd of deer the pack depended on moved south, out of reach. For days the wolves went hungry. It was [[evident]] to the adults that something would have to change, though nobody could have said exactly what. The cub, who was [[dissatisfied]] with hunger but had no idea how to fix it, spent his time watching.","What he learned, he learned by watching. His mother did not teach him to [[seize]] a hare by the neck; she simply did it, repeatedly, while he sat at a distance and paid attention. Nor did she explain that in deep snow a small animal is easier to catch than a large one. That was a [[necessity]] of the landscape, and the landscape taught it. By February he could feed himself, though not well. He moved through the drifts with growing [[ease]], and he came back to the den at [[dawn]] with blood on his muzzle and something in his stomach.","The [[outcome]] of that first winter was that two of the litter did not survive it. This is not a sad story so much as a factual one: among wolves, the [[supreme]] test of any [[trait]] is whether it helps an animal reach spring. Speed matters. Patience matters. What matters most, perhaps, is the ability to [[squeeze]] through a narrow gap in the rocks when the alternative is a very long way round.","By his second summer he was [[magnificent]] — long-legged, heavy-chested, and able to [[howl]] in a voice that carried across the whole valley. He had also learned something more [[profound]] than hunting: that a wolf alone is a poor animal, and that a pack is not a [[pile]] of individuals but a single creature with many eyes. Whether he was ever happy, nobody can say. But he was alive, and among wolves that is a [[terrific]] achievement, and the only [[vital]] one."],"exercises":[{"id":"b3u20-e1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"Which two lessons did the cub learn by autumn?","options":["How to swim and how to hide from hunters","How to leap a stream and how to run at his mother\'s signal","How to howl and how to dig a den","How to hunt deer and how to find clean water"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 1 names the two lessons: leaping across a stream without falling in, and running when his mother made a certain low sound."},{"id":"b3u20-e2","type":"multiple-choice","question":"Why was that winter so hard for the pack?","options":["The herd of deer moved south out of reach","The other wolves left the valley","The den was flooded by the stream","The hail destroyed the pine tree"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Paragraph 2 says the hail came early and the deer herd the pack depended on moved south, out of reach."},{"id":"b3u20-e3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"How did the cub learn to hunt?","options":["His mother taught him step by step","By watching his mother do it repeatedly","By practising alone in the dark","By following the deer herd south"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 3 says his mother did not teach him but simply did it repeatedly while he watched and paid attention."},{"id":"b3u20-e4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What does the writer suggest in the final paragraph?","options":["The cub was unusually intelligent for his age","A pack works as a single unit rather than as separate animals","Wolves are happier than most other animals","Howling is more important than hunting"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"The writer says a pack is not a pile of individuals but a single creature with many eyes."},{"id":"b3u20-e5","type":"multiple-choice","question":"As used in paragraph 3, \\"necessity\\" most nearly means…","options":["something that is needed","an accident of weather","a serious difficulty","a rule made by the pack"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Catching small animals in deep snow was a necessity of the landscape — something the conditions made necessary."},{"id":"b3u20-e6","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The cat was hiding ___ the kitchen table, waiting for the door to open.\\"","options":["beneath","above","among","beyond"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Beneath means under or lower than, which is where a cat hides relative to a table."},{"id":"b3u20-e7","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"At ___ the birds begin to sing and the sky turns pale.\\"","options":["dusk","dawn","ease","hail"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Dawn is the time of day when the sun rises, which is when birds begin to sing."},{"id":"b3u20-e8","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"She solved the difficult puzzle with surprising ___.\\"","options":["ease","necessity","outcome","trait"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Ease is a condition without difficulty, so solving something with ease means doing it without trouble."},{"id":"b3u20-e9","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The lioness will ___ her prey and hold it tightly.\\"","options":["howl","leap","pile","seize"],"answerIndex":3,"explanation":"To seize something is to grab it quickly or strongly, which is what a lioness does to her prey."},{"id":"b3u20-e10","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"It was ___ that she had been crying; her eyes were red and swollen.\\"","options":["evident","supreme","profound","vital"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Evident means easy to see or understand, which fits a conclusion drawn from visible signs."}]},"21":{"id":"b3-u21","book":3,"unit":21,"title":"The Instruction Book Inside Every Cell","level":"B2","topic":"Genetics and health","generatedAt":"2026-09-23","wordCount":419,"paragraphs":["Inside almost every [[cell]] of the human body there is a set of instructions long enough to fill a small library. Scientists call it [[dna|DNA]], and it is written in a chemical [[code]] of just four letters. Reading that code has been one of the great projects of modern biology, and it has changed the way we think about illness.","A [[gene]] is a short section of the code. Genes determine how an organism grows, what it looks like, and how it develops. We [[inherit]] them from our parents, half from each, which is why members of the same family often resemble one another. [[furthermore|Furthermore]], the same inheritance explains why certain conditions appear again and again in the same family line.","Among those conditions is [[cancer]]. The disease begins when a cell stops obeying the instructions that control its growth. Instead of dividing in an orderly way, it multiplies without limit, and the [[result]] is a mass of tissue that damages the healthy organs around it. Cells of this kind are described as [[cancerous]]. Left untreated, they can spread, and the eventual [[destruction]] they cause may be fatal.","Doctors now understand that the [[link]] between daily habits and this disease is strong. Smoking [[cigarette|cigarettes]], for example, is among the best-known causes: the chemicals in the smoke [[attack]] the code inside the cells of the lungs. A diet heavy in [[sugar]] and processed food appears to raise the risk as well, although the evidence is less clear. The picture is not identical for [[female|female]] and [[male|male]] patients either; cancer of the [[breast]], for instance, is far more common in women, while several other forms are more frequent in men.","The effect on whole [[population|populations]] is [[extensive]]. Across the world, millions of people receive the diagnosis each year, and in many countries it remains a leading cause of death. Research is extensive too. There is still no single [[cure]], but treatment has improved enormously, and many patients now live for decades after their first diagnosis. In several countries, screening programmes find the disease at a stage when treatment is far more likely to succeed, and survival rates have risen as a direct consequence.","Genetics alone does not decide anyone\'s future. Most cases arise from a mixture of inherited tendency and the things we do, or fail to do, over a lifetime. Knowing the code helps, but it is not a verdict. The advice doctors give is still unglamorous: do not smoke, eat sensibly, and go for the checks that are offered."],"exercises":[{"id":"b3u21-e1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What is the passage mainly about?","options":["The history of a scientific research project","How genes and daily habits together influence a serious disease","Why some families resemble one another more than others","The reasons why smoking has become less common"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"The passage moves from DNA and genes to the development of cancer and then to the roles played by smoking, diet and inherited tendency, so it is about both inheritance and lifestyle."},{"id":"b3u21-e2","type":"multiple-choice","question":"According to the passage, what happens when a cell stops obeying the instructions that control its growth?","options":["It stops dividing altogether and slowly dies","It begins to store sugar instead of using it","It multiplies without limit and damages surrounding organs","It repairs the DNA of neighbouring cells"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"Paragraph 3 states that such a cell multiplies without limit and that the result is a mass of tissue which damages the healthy organs around it."},{"id":"b3u21-e3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What does the final paragraph suggest about inherited risk?","options":["It makes illness unavoidable in later life","It matters far less than doctors once believed","It is one factor among several and does not settle a person\'s future","It can be removed by changing one\'s diet"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"The writer says genetics alone does not decide anyone\'s future, that most cases arise from a mixture of inherited tendency and behaviour, and that knowing the code \'is not a verdict\'."},{"id":"b3u21-e4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"Which phrase best describes the writer\'s attitude towards medical research?","options":["Openly disappointed by the lack of progress","Carefully optimistic, while avoiding overstatement","Indifferent to the subject","Convinced that a complete cure is already available"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"The writer accepts that no single cure exists but stresses that treatment has improved enormously and that many patients now live for decades, which is hopeful without being exaggerated."},{"id":"b3u21-e5","type":"multiple-choice","question":"As used in paragraph 4, the word \\"attack\\" most nearly means…","options":["to criticise someone publicly","to damage or harm something","to copy something exactly","to divide something into parts"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Here the chemicals in cigarette smoke act on the DNA inside lung cells, so \'attack\' means to damage or harm rather than to criticise."},{"id":"b3u21-e6","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Eye colour is decided by more than one ___, which is why two brown-eyed parents can have a blue-eyed child.\\"","options":["gene","cure","code","cell"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"A gene is a section of DNA that controls a feature of an organism, which is exactly what determines eye colour."},{"id":"b3u21-e7","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The children ___ their grandfather\'s old watch and his love of long walks.\\"","options":["attacked","inherited","cured","counted"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"To inherit is to receive something passed down from a relative; the watch and the habit both came from the grandfather."},{"id":"b3u21-e8","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The ___ of the city has fallen by a fifth since the factory closed.\\"","options":["sugar","population","breast","destruction"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"A population is all the people living in an area, so it is the only option that can fall in size."},{"id":"b3u21-e9","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"He gave up ___ after thirty years, and his cough disappeared within a month.\\"","options":["cigarettes","results","cells","codes"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Only cigarettes are smoked and can be given up, and the disappearance of a cough points clearly to smoking."},{"id":"b3u21-e10","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The survey was so ___ that it took the team two years simply to read the answers.\\"","options":["extensive","cancerous","female","furthermore"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Extensive means large in size or amount, which explains why reading the answers took so long."}]},"22":{"id":"b3-u22","book":3,"unit":22,"title":"The Week the Big Top Came to Town","level":"B2","topic":"A travelling show and a small town","generatedAt":"2026-09-23","wordCount":446,"paragraphs":["Nobody in our town could remember exactly when the posters first appeared, but by Monday morning they were on every wall. A travelling [[circus]] was coming, and the field beside the river had been rented for a week. The [[headline]] in the local paper was modest enough — \'SHOW ARRIVES FRIDAY\' — yet the queue at the ticket office began on Wednesday.","The big top was designed to [[accommodate]] eight hundred people, though on the busiest night it probably held more. Workmen spent two days raising the canvas, and when it was finally up you could see the red and yellow [[stripe|stripes]] from the far side of the valley. The show itself had been [[commission|commissioned]] by a wealthy merchant more than a century earlier, and every piece of equipment, from the ropes to the cooking pots, still travelled with the family that owned it.","The animals were the part everyone talked about. The horses were [[tame]] enough for children to stroke, and the elephants, guided by a single handler, could [[steer]] a wooden cart around the ring without ever touching the barrier. In the interval an artist sat at a small table and drew [[portrait|portraits]] of anyone willing to [[pose]] for ten minutes. People would [[peer]] at the finished picture, half pleased and half disappointed, and then pay for it anyway. The performers were of every age and [[gender]], from a girl of nine to a man of seventy who had joined the show as a boy.","Two things about that week [[coincide|coincided]] with the town\'s own calendar: the harvest fair and the opening of the new bridge. To what [[extent]] the circus profited from the crowds is hard to say. A [[messenger]] arrived on Thursday with a telegram for the owner, and by Friday two reporters had begun to [[inquire]] about next year\'s tour. The answer, delivered in the [[informal]] manner that such families prefer, was simply: \'We\'ll see.\'","Behind the tent there was a small [[ranch]] where the animals were kept, and a kitchen that produced an extraordinary [[dose]] of strong coffee every morning. Visitors were [[tempt|tempted]] by the sweets and paper hats on sale at the entrance, and children queued to have their faces [[dye|dyed]] in bright colours that would not come off for days.","The show left on Sunday night, and by Monday the field was empty again. For years afterwards, people who had been there would argue about how large the tent had really been, and each year it grew a little bigger in the telling. That, I think, is the honest measure of a circus\'s power: it gives a small town something to exaggerate for the rest of its life."],"exercises":[{"id":"b3u22-e1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What is the main purpose of the passage?","options":["To persuade readers to visit a travelling show","To recall the week a circus visited the writer\'s town","To explain how elephants are trained for performances","To compare a harvest fair with a circus"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"The passage is a personal recollection of one week, from the posters appearing to the field being empty again on Monday."},{"id":"b3u22-e2","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What does the passage say about the show\'s history?","options":["It had been commissioned by a merchant over a century earlier","It had been started by the town council","It had been running for only three years","It had been bought by a newspaper owner"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Paragraph 2 states that the show had been commissioned by a wealthy merchant more than a century earlier."},{"id":"b3u22-e3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"How did people generally react to the portraits drawn in the interval?","options":["They refused to pay for them","They asked for a second drawing immediately","They were partly pleased and partly disappointed, but bought them anyway","They complained to the owner of the show"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"Paragraph 3 says people would peer at the finished picture, half pleased and half disappointed, and then pay for it anyway."},{"id":"b3u22-e4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What does the final sentence imply about the town?","options":["The town soon forgot that the circus had ever come","The story of the circus became more impressive each time it was retold","The town wanted the circus to return every year","The writer thinks people deliberately lied about the tent"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"The writer says the tent \'grew a little bigger in the telling\' each year, which shows how the memory was gradually exaggerated."},{"id":"b3u22-e5","type":"multiple-choice","question":"As used in paragraph 5, the word \\"tempted\\" most nearly means…","options":["forced to pay a higher price","attracted by something they wanted","warned about a danger","disappointed by what they saw"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"The visitors were tempted by the sweets and hats — that is, the goods attracted them and made them want to buy."},{"id":"b3u22-e6","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The new hall can ___ up to four hundred guests, so there is room for everyone.\\"","options":["accommodate","steer","inquire","tempt"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To accommodate is to have enough room for people, which is what a hall does for guests."},{"id":"b3u22-e7","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Their holidays ___ this year, so they decided to travel together.\\"","options":["tempted","coincided","peered","dyed"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"To coincide means to happen at the same time, which explains why the two holidays allowed joint travel."},{"id":"b3u22-e8","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"She ___ at the small print on the contract for several minutes before signing.\\"","options":["steered","posed","peered","accommodated"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"To peer at something is to look at it carefully, which is what one does with small print."},{"id":"b3u22-e9","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The farmer keeps two hundred cattle on his ___ in the hills.\\"","options":["dose","ranch","stripe","headline"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"A ranch is a large farm where animals are kept, which fits cattle in the hills."},{"id":"b3u22-e10","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The nurse measured out the correct ___ of medicine and handed it to the patient.\\"","options":["extent","dose","portrait","messenger"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"A dose is a certain amount of medicine taken at one time, so it is the only option that fits measuring medicine."}]},"23":{"id":"b3-u23","book":3,"unit":23,"title":"The Boy Who Would Not Mow the Lawn","level":"B2","topic":"A folk tale about idleness","generatedAt":"2026-09-23","wordCount":443,"paragraphs":["There was once a young man called Hans who lived with his mother in a [[cottage]] at the edge of a forest. The cottage had a [[lawn]] in front of it, and that lawn was the great sorrow of Hans\'s life. Every Saturday his mother asked him to [[mow]] it. Every Saturday he found something else that urgently needed doing.","Hans was not wicked, only idle. The cottage itself was old and cold, and the roof leaked in three places, which he regarded as one more argument against doing anything about anything. He would lie in the grass through the whole [[daytime]], watching the clouds, and he [[seldom]] lifted a finger before evening. The forest, people said, still sheltered an [[outlaw]], and his mother never let him stay outside after dark. She had also long ago [[ban|banned]] cards and dice from the house, because his father had lost money at them, and she was [[cautious]] about anything that looked like a [[gamble]].","One afternoon a [[wizard]] came down the road, carrying a thin wooden [[rod]] and a leather [[purse]] that never seemed to open. He offered Hans a bargain. If the whole lawn were finished by sunset, the purse would be his. If not, Hans would work for the wizard for a year. Hans, who had never yet refused a [[prospect]] of easy money, agreed at once.","The work was [[exhausting]]. By noon his arms ached and the sun had burned the back of his neck. In a [[desperate]] attempt to finish, he pushed the mower faster and faster, until he slipped and cut a long scar across the grass. When the wizard returned at dusk he was not angry. He was simply [[fierce]] in a quiet way, and Hans, [[terrified]], [[confess|confessed]] that he had done barely half the job.","So Hans went away with the wizard for a year. He learned to [[shave]] with cold water, to cook, and to work from first light until the colours began to [[fade]] from the hills. He was given no magic and was never once allowed to touch the purse. What he was given instead was the habit of finishing things, which is a far more useful gift. He also learned that a task done slowly is usually done once, while a task done quickly is very often done twice.","When Hans came home, the lawn stood waist-high. He mowed it that same afternoon without being asked, and afterwards he sat on the step and looked at the cottage as though he had never really seen it before. His mother said nothing at all. She simply brought out two cups and sat down beside him."],"exercises":[{"id":"b3u23-e1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"Why did Hans agree to the wizard\'s bargain so quickly?","options":["He wanted to leave home for a year","He was attracted by the prospect of easy money","He hoped to learn magic from the wizard","He wanted to prove his mother wrong"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 3 says Hans had never yet refused a prospect of easy money, and the purse would be his if he finished the lawn."},{"id":"b3u23-e2","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What was the result of Hans\'s attempt to finish the lawn faster?","options":["He finished just before sunset","He cut a long scar across the grass after slipping","He broke the mower and had to buy a new one","He persuaded a neighbour to help him"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 4 describes how he pushed the mower faster until he slipped and cut a long scar across the grass."},{"id":"b3u23-e3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What does the passage say Hans gained from his year with the wizard?","options":["A share of the wizard\'s money","A knowledge of magic","The habit of finishing what he started","A new cottage of his own"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"Paragraph 5 states that he was given no magic but acquired the habit of finishing things."},{"id":"b3u23-e4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What is the writer\'s attitude towards Hans at the end of the story?","options":["Amused but approving, since Hans has changed","Angry that Hans was never punished","Sorry for Hans because he lost the purse","Uncertain whether Hans learned anything at all"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"The closing scene shows Hans mowing unprompted and his mother joining him silently, which suggests the writer sees the change as real and pleasing."},{"id":"b3u23-e5","type":"multiple-choice","question":"As used in paragraph 4, the word \\"fierce\\" most nearly means…","options":["extremely tired","quietly but intensely severe","friendly and forgiving","confused and uncertain"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"The wizard is described as \'fierce in a quiet way\' rather than angry, so the word suggests an intense but controlled severity."},{"id":"b3u23-e6","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The council decided to ___ cars from the market square on Saturdays.\\"","options":["ban","mow","fade","confess"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To ban something is to not let people do it, which is what the council did with cars in the square."},{"id":"b3u23-e7","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"He is a ___ driver: he checks the mirrors twice before pulling out.\\"","options":["fierce","desperate","cautious","exhausting"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"A cautious person is careful to avoid danger, which matches a driver who checks everything twice."},{"id":"b3u23-e8","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"She finally ___ that she had borrowed the book and never returned it.\\"","options":["confessed","mowed","gambled","faded"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To confess is to admit a bad or embarrassing truth, exactly what she did about the borrowed book."},{"id":"b3u23-e9","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"They rented a small ___ by the lake for the summer.\\"","options":["purse","rod","cottage","prospect"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"A cottage is a small old house in the countryside, which fits a summer rental by a lake."},{"id":"b3u23-e10","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"He was ___ of the dark water and would not go near the edge.\\"","options":["terrified","exhausting","cautious","seldom"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"A terrified person is extremely scared, which explains his refusal to go near the water."}]},"24":{"id":"b3-u24","book":3,"unit":24,"title":"The Woman Who Writes the Guidebooks","level":"B2","topic":"The working life of a travel writer","generatedAt":"2026-09-23","wordCount":422,"paragraphs":["Marta Reyes writes guidebooks for a living, though she insists that it began as a [[hobby]]. She was working as a translator when friends started asking her, every summer, where they should go and what they should avoid. Eventually she wrote the answers down, and a publisher asked for more.","Her method is far less romantic than her readers imagine. She spends four months a year [[abroad]], and on a good day she will visit nine caf\xe9s, three museums and two hotels before dinner. She [[gather|gathers]] prices, opening hours and the small details that no [[official]] website bothers to mention. Back in her flat she turns the [[data]] into a first [[draft]], and the draft into a chapter. A whole [[volume]] takes about eighteen months. She keeps two notebooks: one for prices, which she fills in as she goes, and one for impressions, which she writes up at night before she forgets them.","Money is a constant subject. She records the [[currency]] of every country she visits, along with the rate at which it changed while she was there, and she is ruthless about what counts as a [[bargain]]. \'A cheap meal that ruins your afternoon is not a bargain,\' she says. She also [[refer|refers]] constantly to her own earlier editions, because a hotel that was excellent five years ago is often merely adequate now.","Travel itself is the least enjoyable part of the job. She boards [[jet|jets]] the way other people board buses, and she has taught herself to sleep sitting upright. Even so, a long flight leaves her [[sleepless]] for a day or two afterwards. She books the [[maximum]] legroom she can afford, prefers smaller [[airline|airlines]] where the staff still remember her name, and does most of her [[domestic]] travel by train, which she claims is the only way to see a country properly. She has missed three flights in ten years, all of them because a taxi was late, and she still counts each one as a personal failure.","Her [[audience]] has grown slowly and steadily. The [[income]] is modest but reliable. What she enjoys most are the [[remote]] places: a village four hours from the nearest railway, a guesthouse with no sign and eleven rooms. She will [[recommend]] such a place only if she has slept in it herself. A [[brief]] stay is never enough, she says — you have to be bored somewhere before you can describe it honestly. Her publisher would prefer her to cover more cities and fewer villages, but so far she has refused."],"exercises":[{"id":"b3u24-e1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"How did Marta\'s career as a guidebook writer begin?","options":["She was hired directly by a large publisher","She answered friends\' travel questions, which turned into writing","She won a competition for young travel writers","She inherited the work from a relative"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 1 explains that friends asked her for travel advice each summer, she wrote the answers down, and a publisher then asked for more."},{"id":"b3u24-e2","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What does the passage say about the time a guidebook takes?","options":["About three months from first draft to publication","About eighteen months for a whole volume","Four months, all of it spent travelling","It varies too much to estimate"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 2 states that a whole volume takes about eighteen months."},{"id":"b3u24-e3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What is Marta\'s opinion of what counts as a bargain?","options":["Anything that costs less than the local average","Only meals that are both cheap and enjoyable","A low price is always worth having","Bargains are rare and usually not worth the effort"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"She says a cheap meal that ruins your afternoon is not a bargain, showing that low price alone is not enough."},{"id":"b3u24-e4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"Why does Marta prefer a longer stay in a place?","options":["Because shorter trips cost her more money","Because she believes boredom helps her describe a place truthfully","Because airlines charge less for longer bookings","Because her publisher requires a minimum stay"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"In the final paragraph she says a brief stay is never enough and that you have to be bored somewhere before you can describe it honestly."},{"id":"b3u24-e5","type":"multiple-choice","question":"As used in paragraph 3, the word \\"refer\\" most nearly means…","options":["to mention or consult","to send someone to a specialist","to refuse politely","to write something from memory"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"She refers to her own earlier editions, that is, she consults or mentions them when checking whether a place is still good."},{"id":"b3u24-e6","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The bank charges a fee for changing foreign ___ at the airport.\\"","options":["currency","volume","audience","data"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Currency is the type of money used in a country, so it is what travellers exchange."},{"id":"b3u24-e7","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"We found a beautiful little hotel in a ___ valley, two days\' walk from any road.\\"","options":["domestic","remote","official","brief"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"A remote place is distant or far away, which fits a valley two days from the nearest road."},{"id":"b3u24-e8","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Could you ___ a good dentist near the station? I have never been to this city before.\\"","options":["refer","recommend","gather","coincide"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"To recommend is to give advice based on experience, which is what one does when suggesting a dentist."},{"id":"b3u24-e9","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The ___ for tonight\'s concert was smaller than the band had hoped.\\"","options":["income","audience","draft","maximum"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"An audience is a group of people who gather to watch or listen, so it is what a concert can attract."},{"id":"b3u24-e10","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"After a ___ pause he continued reading the report aloud.\\"","options":["brief","sleepless","extensive","domestic"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"A brief action lasts a short time, which matches a short pause before continuing."}]},"25":{"id":"b3-u25","book":3,"unit":25,"title":"A Green Name for a White Island","level":"B2","topic":"The naming of Greenland and the Norse settlements","generatedAt":"2026-09-23","wordCount":460,"paragraphs":["Greenland is covered by an ice sheet that holds roughly one tenth of the world\'s fresh water. Only a small [[fraction]] of the island — a narrow strip along the coast — is green in summer, and even there the growing season is short. So why does a place of [[polar]] ice carry a name that promises grass?","The usual explanation [[derive|derives]] from the sagas, the stories that Norse settlers told about themselves. According to them, a man known as Erik the Red was exiled from Iceland after a quarrel and sailed west until he found land. He spent three years exploring it, then returned home to persuade others to follow. He called the island Greenland — a piece of marketing, his critics have always said, rather than a description.","The story [[circulate|circulated]] quickly, and within a few years several hundred settlers had made the crossing. Whether Erik deliberately created an [[illusion]] of fertile land is impossible to prove. What is clear is that he needed followers, and a name is the cheapest advertisement available.","The climate of the period explains the rest. Between the tenth and fourteenth centuries the North Atlantic was warmer than it is today, and the [[frost]] came later in the year. [[marine|Marine]] records taken from sediment show that the sea itself was unusually mild. As a [[consequent]] result, the coastal pastures supported cattle for a few generations. But the settlements were always fragile. Boats were small, and a sudden storm could [[drown]] a whole crew within sight of land. The settlers must have [[tremble|trembled]] through their first winters, when a pale [[ray]] of light appeared for barely an hour and the wind never stopped.","The colony did not last. No [[dynasty]] of families survived into the modern period; the last written record of the Norse settlers dates from the early fifteenth century, and after that the silence is complete. Later European powers did not so much [[invade]] the island as claim it. In 1721 a Danish [[navy]] ship was sent north to re-establish contact with the descendants of the Norse, none of whom were found. The [[lieutenant]] in command kept a detailed journal, and it is still read today.","Some historians defend the old name on its own [[merit|merits]]: the sheltered fjords really are greener than the ice sheet suggests. Others argue that the [[underlying]] reason was simple ambition. What is certain is that anyone travelling [[via]] the open sea to reach Greenland knew what they were risking. The sagas record that many settlers [[resign|resigned]] themselves to hard work and early death, and the coldest years hint at a despair that no source fully explains — at least one settler is said to have taken his own life, a [[suicide]] the records never account for."],"exercises":[{"id":"b3u25-e1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What question does the passage set out to answer?","options":["Why Greenland\'s ice sheet is shrinking","Why an icy island has a name that suggests green land","How the Norse built their boats","Why the Danish navy sailed north in 1721"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"The opening paragraph contrasts the ice sheet with the name Greenland and asks why such a place carries a name that promises grass."},{"id":"b3u25-e2","type":"multiple-choice","question":"According to the sagas, why did Erik call the island Greenland?","options":["Because he genuinely believed it was fertile","Because he wanted to persuade others to settle there","Because the local people already used that name","Because he was required to do so by law"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 2 explains that after exploring for three years he returned home to persuade others to follow, and the passage describes the name as marketing."},{"id":"b3u25-e3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What does the passage say about the climate of the tenth to fourteenth centuries?","options":["It was colder than today, which made farming impossible","It was warmer than today, so coastal pastures could support cattle","It was exactly the same as it is now","It changed so suddenly that no records exist"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 4 says the North Atlantic was warmer than today, frost came later, and as a consequent result the coastal pastures supported cattle for a few generations."},{"id":"b3u25-e4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What happened when the Danish ship reached Greenland in 1721?","options":["It found the Norse settlers still living there","It found no descendants of the Norse settlers","It was turned back by ice before landing","It established a new colony immediately"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 5 states that the ship was sent to re-establish contact with the descendants of the Norse, none of whom were found."},{"id":"b3u25-e5","type":"multiple-choice","question":"As used in paragraph 3, the word \\"illusion\\" most nearly means…","options":["a false impression of how things are","a detailed map of a coastline","an official agreement between two countries","a legal punishment for a crime"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"The writer asks whether Erik deliberately created a false impression of fertile land, which is exactly what an illusion is."},{"id":"b3u25-e6","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Only a small ___ of the money raised was actually spent on the project.\\"","options":["fraction","dynasty","ray","merit"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"A fraction is a small part of something, so it fits money that was mostly not spent."},{"id":"b3u25-e7","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Many English words ___ from Latin and Greek.\\"","options":["circulate","derive","resign","invade"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"To derive means to come or originate from a thing or place, which is how words come from older languages."},{"id":"b3u25-e8","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"In the early morning the grass was white with ___ and the path was slippery.\\"","options":["illusion","frost","navy","merit"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Frost is a white layer of ice that forms in very cold weather, which explains white grass and a slippery path."},{"id":"b3u25-e9","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"After twelve years in the same post, she decided to ___ and travel.\\"","options":["resign","circulate","tremble","derive"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To resign means to quit a job, which is what leaving a post after twelve years means."},{"id":"b3u25-e10","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"We flew to the island ___ the mainland, changing planes twice.\\"","options":["via","underlying","consequent","polar"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Via introduces a route or means of travel, which is what the mainland was on the way to the island."}]},"26":{"id":"b3-u26","book":3,"unit":26,"title":"The Bakery on the Corner","level":"B2","topic":"A family bakery and an autumn festival","generatedAt":"2026-09-23","wordCount":425,"paragraphs":["My grandmother ran a bakery in a narrow street, and every [[autumn]] the whole neighbourhood helped her prepare for the festival. The recipe never changed: [[flour]], butter, eggs, and a spoonful of ground [[ginger]] that she refused to measure. \'You measure with your hand,\' she said, \'not with a cup.\'","On the first morning we [[blend|blended]] the spices into the dough and worked it until it was smooth. She showed me how to [[crush]] the seeds with the back of a knife, and then how to turn them into a dark [[paste]] with a little water. Every surface had to be [[wipe|wiped]] down afterwards, and the [[drain]] in the corner, which had been blocked for years, had to be cleared before we could wash anything at all. Bowls were stacked [[aside]] on the floor, well out of the way of our feet. By mid-afternoon the whole street smelled of butter and spice, and neighbours began to find reasons to walk past the window.","In the evening the town let off [[fireworks]] over the river. A boy from the next street made them himself, which was probably illegal. He lit the [[fuse]] with a length of slow-burning [[wire]], and the first rocket [[curve|curved]] up over the water before breaking into a hundred small lights. One of the wooden frames [[collapse|collapsed]] under the weight of the crowd, and everyone laughed, and nobody was hurt.","I was fourteen that year, and I [[envy|envied]] the boy with the fireworks more than I have ever envied anyone since. He had something to show. I was [[jealous]], plainly, and I knew it. My grandmother noticed. She put her arm around me — she was not a woman who [[embrace|embraced]] easily, so it surprised me — and told me that a baker\'s work is invisible: it disappears by nine in the morning.","She kept every [[receipt]] in a tin box, and she would not [[alter]] a single line of the recipe, even when the flour changed from year to year. Once I tried to substitute margarine for butter. The result was, by general agreement, [[disgusting]], and she made me eat a whole slice of it before she would speak to me again.","She died in the spring, and the bakery closed. What I remember is not the cakes but the noise: the radio, the whisk, the door. When people say that everyone is special, I think of her hands. She never believed that she was special. She believed that the work was. I still make the cakes myself, badly, once a year."],"exercises":[{"id":"b3u26-e1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What is the passage mainly about?","options":["How to bake a traditional festival cake","The narrator\'s memories of a grandmother and her bakery","The dangers of home-made fireworks","Why small shops close when their owners retire"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"The passage recalls the grandmother, her working methods and the narrator\'s feelings, using the festival as a setting rather than a subject."},{"id":"b3u26-e2","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What does the passage tell us about the grandmother\'s attitude to measuring?","options":["She weighed every ingredient carefully","She trusted her hands rather than a cup","She followed a written recipe exactly","She asked the narrator to do the measuring"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 1 quotes her as saying \'You measure with your hand, not with a cup.\'"},{"id":"b3u26-e3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What happened during the fireworks display?","options":["The rocket failed to leave the ground","A wooden frame collapsed but nobody was hurt","The river flooded part of the street","The boy was arrested for making fireworks"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 3 says one of the wooden frames collapsed under the weight of the crowd, everyone laughed, and nobody was hurt."},{"id":"b3u26-e4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"Why did the grandmother embrace the narrator?","options":["To congratulate him on helping with the baking","To comfort him because he envied the boy with the fireworks","To warn him about the danger of fireworks","To say goodbye before the bakery closed"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"She noticed his jealousy, and the passage presents her embrace as a response to it, along with her remark about invisible work."},{"id":"b3u26-e5","type":"multiple-choice","question":"As used in paragraph 5, the word \\"alter\\" most nearly means…","options":["to explain in detail","to change","to copy exactly","to write down"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"She would not alter a single line of the recipe, that is, she would not change any part of it."},{"id":"b3u26-e6","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The soup was so hot that I burned my tongue and pushed the bowl ___.\\"","options":["aside","blended","curved","crushed"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"An action done aside is done toward the side, which is what pushing a hot bowl away from you means."},{"id":"b3u26-e7","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"To make the sauce, ___ the garlic and the herbs together until the mixture is smooth.\\"","options":["wipe","blend","alter","envy"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"To blend is to mix two or more things together so that they become one, which is what a sauce requires."},{"id":"b3u26-e8","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The water would not go down the sink, so we called someone to clear the ___.\\"","options":["wire","drain","paste","receipt"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"A drain is a pipe that carries water away from a building, so a blocked one stops water leaving the sink."},{"id":"b3u26-e9","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Keep the ___ in case the machine turns out to be faulty and you need to return it.\\"","options":["fuse","paste","receipt","flour"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"A receipt is a paper that proves something was bought, which is what a shop asks for when goods are returned."},{"id":"b3u26-e10","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The old bridge ___ during the flood and had to be rebuilt.\\"","options":["collapsed","curved","blended","wiped"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To collapse is to fall down suddenly, which is what the bridge did and why it needed rebuilding."}]},"27":{"id":"b3-u27","book":3,"unit":27,"title":"The Room Filled with Gold","level":"B2","topic":"A turning point in the history of the Andes","generatedAt":"2026-09-23","wordCount":421,"paragraphs":["In 1532 a small group of Spanish soldiers reached the northern coast of South America. They had come, they said, to explore. Their real interest, however, was gold. The empire they had entered was vast, wealthy and remarkably well organised, and its ruler had never seen a European before. It stretched along the western edge of the continent, from high mountain valleys down to a desert coast, and it was linked by thousands of kilometres of paved road.","An [[ambassador]] was sent to greet the strangers with gifts. The Spanish commander was [[suspicious]] from the first day. He had heard [[rumor|rumors]] of cities roofed with gold, and like many of his countrymen he was ready both to believe them and to [[exaggerate]] them further. The local [[noble|nobles]], for their part, could not [[acknowledge]] that a handful of foreigners in strange clothes represented any serious threat.","What followed has often been described as the most expensive ransom in history. To buy their ruler\'s freedom, his people filled a large room with objects of [[precious]] metal: plates, cups, a [[necklace]] of heavy links, [[vase|vases]] shaped like animals, and a [[stack]] of thin sheets that had once decorated a temple wall. Much of it was not gold at all but silver or [[tin]], which the Spanish rejected. Wagons had to [[drag]] the metal across the mountains to the coast, where it was melted down and shipped away. [[meanwhile|Meanwhile]], the craftsmen who had made these objects watched their work disappear into the furnaces.","For the Spanish it was a [[spectacle]]; for the empire it was the end of a world. The pieces that survived are now scattered across museums in Europe, and their loss is still spoken of in the region as a wound to its [[heritage]]. Some chroniclers wrote that melting down the metal was a [[sin]] against the people who had made it. Others were less generous: one soldier described the local population in terms that were pure [[insult]], and another noted, almost in passing, that the only [[blonde]] in the party was treated as a curiosity wherever he went.","Within two years the Spanish had [[conquer|conquered]] the empire. Their weapons, their horses and, above all, the diseases they carried made the outcome almost inevitable. The gold they took changed the economy of Europe; the society they displaced left behind terraces, roads and textiles that are still admired today. The story has usually been told with [[prejudice]] — some writers praised the invaders, others condemned them, and very few asked the people themselves."],"exercises":[{"id":"b3u27-e1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What is the passage mainly about?","options":["The geography of the Andes","The Spanish arrival in a wealthy empire and the taking of its gold","How European museums acquire their collections","The techniques used by Andean craftsmen"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"The passage follows the Spanish arrival, the ransom of precious metal, the melting down of the objects and the conquest that followed."},{"id":"b3u27-e2","type":"multiple-choice","question":"Why were the local nobles not alarmed by the Spanish at first?","options":["They had already heard of Spanish weapons","They did not believe a few foreigners could be a serious threat","They had been warned by an ambassador","They had signed a treaty with the Spanish commander"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 2 says the nobles could not acknowledge that a handful of foreigners in strange clothes represented any serious threat."},{"id":"b3u27-e3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"Why was part of the ransom rejected by the Spanish?","options":["It was damaged beyond repair","It was silver or tin rather than gold","It was too heavy to transport","It had belonged to a temple"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 3 states that much of the metal was not gold at all but silver or tin, which the Spanish rejected."},{"id":"b3u27-e4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What does the final paragraph suggest about accounts of the conquest?","options":["They have generally been written from one side or the other","They agree on almost every detail","They were written by the craftsmen themselves","They are no longer of any interest to historians"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"The writer says the story has usually been told with prejudice, with some praising the invaders and others condemning them."},{"id":"b3u27-e5","type":"multiple-choice","question":"As used in paragraph 4, the word \\"spectacle\\" most nearly means…","options":["an amazing sight","a legal document","a quiet ceremony","a serious mistake"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"The word contrasts the Spanish view of the event as an astonishing sight with the empire\'s view of it as the end of a world."},{"id":"b3u27-e6","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The two countries exchanged ___ last year, and trade between them has grown since.\\"","options":["ambassadors","rumors","necklaces","vases"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"An ambassador is a government worker who serves in another country, so two countries exchange them as representatives."},{"id":"b3u27-e7","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Do not ___ the size of the problem; it is smaller than it looks.\\"","options":["exaggerate","acknowledge","drag","insult"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To exaggerate is to say something is bigger or better than it really is, which is what the sentence warns against."},{"id":"b3u27-e8","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The lorry had to ___ the broken tractor all the way back to the farm.\\"","options":["drag","conquer","insult","acknowledge"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To drag something is to pull it across the ground, which is what the lorry did with a broken tractor."},{"id":"b3u27-e9","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Her grandmother\'s ring is the most ___ thing she owns, and she never takes it off.\\"","options":["precious","suspicious","noble","blonde"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"A precious thing is valuable and important, which fits an irreplaceable family ring."},{"id":"b3u27-e10","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Please ___ that you received this letter by signing at the bottom.\\"","options":["acknowledge","conquer","exaggerate","stack"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To acknowledge something is to accept that it is true or that it exists, which is what a signature at the foot of a letter does."}]},"28":{"id":"b3-u28","book":3,"unit":28,"title":"The Night the Water Came","level":"B2","topic":"A flood, a warning, and a town meeting","generatedAt":"2026-09-23","wordCount":434,"paragraphs":["Our town sits at the end of a [[canal]], and everyone who lives here learns early to watch the [[tide]]. On ordinary days the water rises quietly and falls again a few hours later. On the night I am describing, it did not.","Sam was fifteen and lived in a [[suburb]] on the higher side of the canal. He [[jog|jogged]] along the towpath every evening, whatever the weather. That December an [[arctic]] wind had been blowing for a week, and there was a permanent [[chill]] in the air that no amount of running could shift. He knew the towpath better than anyone in his family, and he could tell from the colour of the water what the river had been doing further upstream.","On the Thursday he stopped because the ground felt wrong under his feet. Where the path met the old stone wall, water was coming through in a thin, steady line. He [[poke|poked]] at the mud with a stick, and a whole section of the bank slid away. The bank had been patched with concrete the previous summer, and the patch had lasted less than a year. He [[hesitate|hesitated]] for perhaps five seconds. Then he turned and ran. Behind him the water [[splash|splashed]] through the gap and began to [[descend]] towards the lower streets.","The shops were still open. He shouted at the [[grocer]], who was locking up, and at a [[merchant]] loading a van outside the hardware store. The [[chemist]] on the corner heard him and rang the fire bell. A [[dairy]] lorry stood across the road, and its driver used it to block the lowest entrance. The [[institution]] at the end of the street — a home for elderly residents — was the hardest to reach and the last to be warned. Two of the shopkeepers ran up the hill to knock on doors, and a group of children carried the message along the terrace where the oldest houses stood.","The town [[congress]] met the following morning. Someone proposed that the repairs should be [[postpone|postponed]] until spring, and the mayor supported the idea, because he was a [[stubborn]] man who disliked spending money in winter. Sam\'s father, whose legs still [[ache|ached]] from an old fall, spoke for two minutes and changed the vote. Had the work waited until spring, the next high tide would have taken the lower half of the town. What was avoided that winter was not an inconvenience. It was a [[tragedy]]. The repairs took eleven weeks and cost more than the town had spent on anything in a decade, and afterwards nobody complained about the price."],"exercises":[{"id":"b3u28-e1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What is the passage mainly about?","options":["The history of a canal built in the nineteenth century","A boy who noticed a danger and warned his town in time","A dispute between a mayor and a shopkeeper","The daily work of a dairy lorry driver"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"The passage follows Sam\'s discovery on the towpath, his warnings to the shops, and the meeting at which the repairs were approved."},{"id":"b3u28-e2","type":"multiple-choice","question":"Why did Sam stop on the towpath?","options":["He was too cold to continue running","The ground felt wrong beneath his feet","He wanted to watch the tide come in","He had been called by the grocer"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 3 says he stopped because the ground felt wrong under his feet, and then he saw water coming through the wall."},{"id":"b3u28-e3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"How did the fire bell come to be rung?","options":["The mayor ordered it after the meeting","The chemist heard Sam shouting and rang it","The dairy driver rang it from his lorry","Sam himself ran to the fire station"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 4 says the chemist on the corner heard him and rang the fire bell."},{"id":"b3u28-e4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What does the passage suggest about the mayor?","options":["He acted quickly to protect the lower town","He preferred to delay the repairs to save money","He had warned the town years earlier","He refused to attend the meeting"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"The mayor supported postponing the repairs because he was stubborn and disliked spending money in winter."},{"id":"b3u28-e5","type":"multiple-choice","question":"As used in paragraph 5, the word \\"tragedy\\" most nearly means…","options":["a very sad and serious event","an expensive repair","a long delay","a public argument"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"The writer contrasts an inconvenience with what would have happened if the tide had flooded the lower half of the town, so \'tragedy\' means a serious and sad event."},{"id":"b3u28-e6","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"He ___ before answering, choosing his words with unusual care.\\"","options":["hesitated","jogged","splashed","poked"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To hesitate is to wait for a short time before doing something, which fits a careful pause before answering."},{"id":"b3u28-e7","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The path ___ steeply towards the beach, and the return climb took an hour.\\"","options":["descended","postponed","hesitated","ached"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To descend is to go downward, which is what a path towards a beach does."},{"id":"b3u28-e8","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The ___ on the corner sells vegetables, bread and almost everything else the street needs.\\"","options":["grocer","chemist","congress","tide"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"A grocer is a person who sells food, which matches a shop offering vegetables and bread."},{"id":"b3u28-e9","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"They had to ___ the wedding until the spring because of the storm damage.\\"","options":["postpone","jog","poke","descend"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To postpone something is to make it happen later than planned, which is what the storm damage forced them to do."},{"id":"b3u28-e10","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The wind was so ___ that even the birds had disappeared from the sky.\\"","options":["arctic","dairy","stubborn","institutional"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Arctic describes something of the cold far north, so it conveys extreme cold wind — the only option that fits weather."}]},"29":{"id":"b3-u29","book":3,"unit":29,"title":"The Woman Who Mapped the Deep","level":"B2","topic":"A profile of an underwater archaeologist","generatedAt":"2026-09-23","wordCount":432,"paragraphs":["Her friends call her Nora, and her working life has been spent at the bottom of the sea. For thirty years she has explored shipwrecks from a small [[submarine]], and she has probably seen more of the ocean floor than almost anyone alive. She works from a research vessel that spends six weeks at sea and then returns to port for repairs that always take longer than anyone planned.","She began, unexpectedly, with a toy [[microscope]] and a [[certificate]] in marine biology that she earned at night school while working in a laboratory. Her first proposal, to study a wreck in the North Sea, attracted nothing but [[criticism]]: the site was too deep, the currents too strong, the funding too small. The night school course took four years, and she failed the final examination once before she passed it. She obtained her diving [[license]] anyway and went.","The vessel she wanted to reach had been sunk by a [[bomb]] in 1944 and had lain undisturbed ever since. Its hull had been [[devastate|devastated]] by corrosion, but the cargo hold was largely intact. She spent four seasons [[trace|tracing]] the outline of the deck with her hands, working in near-total darkness. On the surface she would [[rotate]] the images on a screen and [[gaze]] at them for hours, hunting for a door, a name, a single object that would identify the ship. Sometimes she would [[glance]] at a photograph and know at once that a theory was wrong.","In 1998 a colleague died on a dive, and the loss almost ended her career. She has described the [[grief]] of that year in interviews, and the way she learned to [[cope]] by working. She attended the funeral, watched the [[coffin]] carried in, and afterwards went straight back to the boat. \'You do not choose the [[circumstance]],\' she says. \'You choose what you do inside it.\' She still [[frown|frowns]] whenever the subject comes up.","Since then her work has been [[portray|portrayed]] in three television documentaries, and a museum in her home city keeps a permanent exhibition of the objects she has recovered. Her favourite is not the most valuable one: it is a brass button, a [[souvenir]] of a sailor\'s uniform, found in the mud of a hold. She has never married. She says, half joking, that she was once engaged to a man she calls the [[groom]] who got away, and that the sea was the reason. Her former students, who now number forty, are working on wrecks from the Baltic to the Pacific, several of them using the [[nuclear]] dating techniques she helped to develop."],"exercises":[{"id":"b3u29-e1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What is the passage mainly about?","options":["How underwater corrosion destroys shipwrecks","The career and character of a deep-sea researcher","The history of a warship sunk in 1944","The training of television documentary makers"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"The passage follows Nora from her early studies through her wreck research, her personal loss and her later recognition."},{"id":"b3u29-e2","type":"multiple-choice","question":"How did Nora respond to the criticism of her first proposal?","options":["She abandoned the project and changed fields","She got her diving licence and went anyway","She rewrote the proposal for a different wreck","She complained publicly about the reviewers"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 2 says her proposal attracted only criticism but that she obtained her diving licence anyway and went."},{"id":"b3u29-e3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What was the condition of the wreck when Nora reached it?","options":["It had been raised and taken to a museum","The hull was badly corroded but the cargo hold was largely intact","It had been destroyed by fishing nets","It had already been fully excavated by another team"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 3 states that the hull had been devastated by corrosion but the cargo hold was largely intact."},{"id":"b3u29-e4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What does the quotation in paragraph 4 tell us about Nora\'s attitude?","options":["She blames others for what happened","She believes we cannot control events, only our response to them","She regrets ever having started diving","She thinks grief should be kept completely private"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"She says \'You do not choose the circumstance. You choose what you do inside it,\' which distinguishes events from how one responds."},{"id":"b3u29-e5","type":"multiple-choice","question":"As used in paragraph 3, the word \\"trace\\" most nearly means…","options":["to follow the outline of something","to write down in a notebook","to remove carefully","to measure the depth of"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"She traces the outline of the deck with her hands in the dark, so the word means to follow over something."},{"id":"b3u29-e6","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"After the accident he found it hard to ___ with the constant pain.\\"","options":["cope","gaze","rotate","trace"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To cope with a difficult situation means to deal with it, which is what living with constant pain requires."},{"id":"b3u29-e7","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"She kept a small stone from the beach as a ___ of that holiday.\\"","options":["souvenir","license","certificate","coffin"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"A souvenir is something kept to remember a place or event, which is exactly what the stone was."},{"id":"b3u29-e8","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The manager\'s report was full of ___ , but he offered no practical suggestions.\\"","options":["criticism","expertise","grief","optimism"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Criticism is the act of saying that something is not correct or good, which fits a report that finds fault without offering solutions."},{"id":"b3u29-e9","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"You must pass a test before you can get a ___ to drive a taxi in this city.\\"","options":["license","microscope","groom","circumstance"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"A license is an official document giving permission to do something, which is required to drive a taxi."},{"id":"b3u29-e10","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"He ___ at the empty page for a long time before he began to write.\\"","options":["gazed","frowned","rotated","glanced"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To gaze at something is to look at it for a long time, which matches the long look at an empty page; a glance would be much shorter."}]},"30":{"id":"b3-u30","book":3,"unit":30,"title":"Eleven Days on the Reef","level":"B2","topic":"A sailing holiday that went wrong","generatedAt":"2026-09-23","wordCount":433,"paragraphs":["My [[spouse]] and I had planned the crossing for two years. We would sail along a remote [[coastline]], anchor each evening, and be home within ten days. We were [[proficient]] sailors, or believed we were, and we had completed the route twice before in settled weather. We had checked the forecast, the engine, the liferaft and the water tanks, and everything had passed inspection.","On the fourth night the wind turned. A wave [[shove|shoved]] the boat sideways and threw me against the cabin roof; the impact [[fracture|fractured]] two ribs and dislocated my [[shoulder]]. Another wave [[thrust]] the hull onto a [[ridge]] of rock, where a [[limb]] of a half-submerged tree caught the rudder and held it fast. We spent the rest of the night in the dinghy, waiting for daylight. The radio aerial had come down with the first wave, so there was no way of calling for help.","At dawn we could see an island, but the [[distance]] was deceptive — perhaps three kilometres, perhaps five. Our dinghy had been damaged, so we [[devise|devised]] a [[raft]] from two hatch covers and a length of rope, and [[implement|implemented]] the plan while the sea was still calm. We loaded the last of the drinking water and pushed off, watching the boat tilt a little further with every swell. It took four hours to reach the beach.","We had no water and no shelter. What we did have was a certain [[expertise]]: my spouse is a trained nurse, and I had spent a summer working on a survey ship, which gave me an unexpected [[insight]] into finding fresh water on a rocky shore. We collected rain in a plastic sheet and ate shellfish. On the third day I had a permanent [[headache]] from thirst, and there were hours when I could not [[tolerate]] the sun at all. Looking back, what surprises me most is the [[optimism]] we felt on that first morning — we were certain we would be picked up by lunchtime.","Nothing about that week [[deter|deterred]] us from sailing again, though perhaps it should have. We were found on the eleventh day by a fishing boat whose engine had barely enough [[might]] to make headway against the current, and the crew, with the quiet confidence of men who had grown up on that water, told us we had been lucky. The island, they added, was not even named on the local charts. We have returned to that [[coastline]] since, but only in summer, and never at night. We now carry a second radio, and I check it every morning before we leave harbour."],"exercises":[{"id":"b3u30-e1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What is the passage mainly about?","options":["How to prepare a boat for a long crossing","A sailing trip that ended in eleven days on an island","The wildlife of a remote coastline","A disagreement between two married sailors"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"The passage describes the storm, the damage to the boat, the journey by raft and the eleven days before the couple were rescued."},{"id":"b3u30-e2","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What injuries did the narrator suffer?","options":["A broken arm and a head injury","Two fractured ribs and a dislocated shoulder","A broken leg and a damaged knee","A deep cut to the hand and back pain"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 2 states that the impact fractured two ribs and dislocated the narrator\'s shoulder."},{"id":"b3u30-e3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"Why did the couple build a raft?","options":["They wanted to explore the island more quickly","Their dinghy had been damaged","The boat was too heavy to move","They needed somewhere to store their water"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 3 explains that the dinghy had been damaged, so they devised a raft from hatch covers and rope."},{"id":"b3u30-e4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What surprised the narrator about the first morning on the island?","options":["How cold the water was","How hopeful they both felt","How close the fishing boat was","How much food they had with them"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"The final paragraph of the body says what surprises the narrator most is the optimism they felt on the first morning, expecting rescue by lunchtime."},{"id":"b3u30-e5","type":"multiple-choice","question":"As used in paragraph 5, the word \\"might\\" most nearly means…","options":["strength or power","a possibility","permission","a loud noise"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Here \'might\' refers to the engine having barely enough power to make headway, so it means strength or force."},{"id":"b3u30-e6","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"It took us three attempts to ___ a route through the mountains that would not need any climbing.\\"","options":["devise","tolerate","deter","shove"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To devise something is to come up with an idea or plan, which is what planning a route involves."},{"id":"b3u30-e7","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The new safety rules were ___ across all the company\'s factories last month.\\"","options":["implemented","devised","tolerated","fractured"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To implement something is to ensure that what has been planned is actually done, which is what happened to the new rules."},{"id":"b3u30-e8","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"She can ___ a lot of noise while she is reading, but not music with words.\\"","options":["tolerate","devise","deter","thrust"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To tolerate something is to be able to accept it even when it is unpleasant, which fits putting up with background noise."},{"id":"b3u30-e9","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The high fence was put up to ___ anyone from walking onto the track.\\"","options":["deter","implement","shove","tolerate"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To deter means to prevent or discourage someone from doing something, which is the purpose of a fence around a track."},{"id":"b3u30-e10","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"After twenty years in the same job, he has remarkable ___ in the field of water engineering.\\"","options":["expertise","optimism","distance","fracture"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Expertise is the knowledge and skills to do something well, which is what twenty years in a field produces."}]}}'),s=JSON.parse('{"1":{"id":"b4-u1","book":4,"unit":1,"title":"The Price of the Scroll","level":"B2","topic":"Social media and wellbeing","generatedAt":"2026-09-23","wordCount":512,"paragraphs":["Ten years ago a team of researchers set out to measure something the rest of us had only guessed at. Did the hours we spend on social [[media]] have anything to do with how we feel? The team designed a [[survey]] of forty thousand adults, asking how often they used each major [[platform]], how many close friends they could call in a crisis, and whether they had ever been treated for [[depression]].","The results refused to fall into a neat pattern. Heavy users reported far more [[loneliness]] than light users, and the two measures appeared to [[correlate]] closely. Yet the direction of the effect was impossible to pin down. It was just as plausible that unhappy people retreat online as that long evenings online make people unhappy, and the authors were careful to say so: a close statistical link is not the same thing as a cause.","What did seem clear was the [[importance]] of how the time was used. Scrolling silently past other people\'s holidays produced one set of effects; sending a message to a friend produced quite another. The researchers also concluded that the design of the apps themselves was far from neutral. An endless feed is deliberately [[addictive]], because a company\'s [[agenda]] is to keep you on the site for as long as possible, and every extra minute is another chance to show you an advertisement.","The study had obvious weaknesses. Self-reported answers are often [[poor]] evidence, since people misremember their own habits. When the same volunteers were [[subject|subjected]] to a second round of questions a year later, a third of them had changed their estimates by more than two hours a day. Critics seized on this, arguing that the whole [[trend]] might be an artefact of the method rather than a fact about the first generation to grow up entirely in a [[digital]] world.","Since then, a [[surge]] in similar research has followed, and the picture has grown more detailed rather than simpler. Some platforms now [[target]] teenagers with nightly reminders to stop; others have removed the counter that shows how many people liked a post. Legislators in several countries have debated whether to [[coin]] a new legal term, persuasive design, and to hold the largest firms [[accountable]] for the features they build.","Teachers, meanwhile, have developed their own small remedies. One I spoke to keeps a box by the door of her classroom; every lesson begins with thirty phones going into it. She does not lecture her students about willpower. Instead, she tries to [[validate]] their feelings, the boredom and the fear of missing out, and then asks them to describe in writing what the [[presence]] of a phone on the desk actually does to their attention. Her pupils usually notice, without being told, that the device in front of them is at once a [[keyboard]] and a doorway.","Whether such lessons change anything over a lifetime is unknown. They do, however, suggest a shift in how the question is framed: not whether technology is good or bad, but who benefits from the way it is designed."],"exercises":[{"id":"b4u1-e1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What was the aim of the survey described in the first paragraph?","options":["To find out how many adults own a smartphone","To test whether time on social media is connected to how people feel","To compare the profits of the largest technology firms","To discover which platform is the most popular with teenagers"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 1 says the team wanted to know whether hours spent on social media had anything to do with how people feel."},{"id":"b4u1-e2","type":"multiple-choice","question":"Why were the researchers unwilling to say that social media causes unhappiness?","options":["The survey did not include enough participants","The connection could equally run in the opposite direction","The results were kept secret by the companies involved","They had not been allowed to publish their findings"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 2 explains that it was equally plausible that unhappy people retreat online, so the direction of the effect could not be established."},{"id":"b4u1-e3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"According to the passage, what mattered most about the time people spent online?","options":["The device they used to go online","The time of day at which they went online","The way in which the time was spent","The number of accounts they had opened"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"Paragraph 3 states that what did seem clear was the importance of how the time was used, contrasting passive scrolling with messaging a friend."},{"id":"b4u1-e4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"Why does the writer mention the second round of questions a year later?","options":["To show that the study\'s evidence had a serious weakness","To prove that the original findings were correct","To explain why the survey was repeated in other countries","To illustrate how quickly habits changed during the year"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"The second round revealed that a third of participants had changed their own estimates by more than two hours, which critics used to question the reliability of self-reported data."},{"id":"b4u1-e5","type":"multiple-choice","question":"In paragraph 6, the writer describes the device as being \'at once a keyboard and a doorway\'. What does this suggest?","options":["That it is difficult to use without training","That it is useful and risky at the same time","That it is now cheaper than it used to be","That it will soon be replaced by something else"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"A keyboard is a practical tool, while a doorway suggests something that leads you somewhere else. The image presents the phone as both useful and a possible way of losing attention."},{"id":"b4u1-e6","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The report found that income and life expectancy ___ strongly in every region studied.\\"","options":["correlate","validate","coin","target"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To correlate means to have a close connection with something, which is what the sentence describes between two measurements."},{"id":"b4u1-e7","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The manager was held ___ for the failure, because the final decision had been hers.\\"","options":["addictive","poor","accountable","digital"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"When someone is accountable, they are responsible for the effects of their actions, which fits a person blamed for a failure."},{"id":"b4u1-e8","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Rather than dismissing his worries, she listened carefully and ___ them.\\"","options":["subjected","validated","surged","targeted"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"To validate someone is to make them feel that their ideas and feelings are respected, which is the opposite of dismissing them."},{"id":"b4u1-e9","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"After the announcement, there was a sudden ___ in demand for tickets.\\"","options":["surge","agenda","presence","trend"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"A surge is a sudden, large increase in something, and the sentence describes a sudden rise in demand."},{"id":"b4u1-e10","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The advertisement was clearly ___ at people under twenty-five.\\"","options":["subjected","targeted","coined","correlated"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"To target something is to aim it at a particular person or group, which is exactly what the sentence says about the advertisement."}]},"2":{"id":"b4-u2","book":4,"unit":2,"title":"Spreading Rock Dust on Fields","level":"B2","topic":"Climate science and farming","generatedAt":"2026-09-23","wordCount":537,"paragraphs":["Every year, human activity [[emit|emits]] roughly thirty-seven billion [[ton|tons]] of [[carbon-dioxide|carbon dioxide]] into the atmosphere. Most of it comes from burning coal, oil and gas, and much of it will remain there for centuries unless something removes it. That something is the subject of a fast-growing field of research known as enhanced weathering.","The idea borrows from geology. Rain absorbs a little [[carbon-dioxide|carbon dioxide]] as it falls and becomes a weak acid. Over millions of years, that acid slowly dissolves certain rocks, and the chemical reaction locks the gas into solid minerals. It is one of the Earth\'s oldest cooling mechanisms, and it is far too slow to help us now. Enhanced weathering simply speeds the process up. Rock is dug from a quarry, [[grind|ground]] into a fine [[powder]], and spread across farmland. Because the grains are tiny, an enormous surface area is exposed to rain and soil, and a reaction that would take millennia in a mountain takes months in a field.","The rock of choice is usually basalt, a dark volcanic [[substance]] rich in calcium and magnesium. When it dissolves, it also releases nutrients that plants need, which is one reason farmers are interested. Trials in several countries have reported that yields rise and that the soil holds water better. [[moreover|Moreover]], crushed rock can be spread with the same machinery that farmers already use to add lime, so no new equipment is required.","The numbers are encouraging. A single [[ton]] of basalt powder can capture a few hundred kilograms of gas, depending on grain size and climate. If the practice were [[integrate|integrated]] into ordinary farming across a large region, the total could be measured in millions of tons. Crushing and spreading the rock requires energy, and the [[transport]] of heavy material from quarry to field adds more, but most studies suggest that the gas saved still outweighs the gas produced.","Not everyone is convinced. At one of the [[leading]] laboratories in the field, the researchers themselves are the first to list the uncertainties. A [[skeptic|sceptic]] might point out that the early trials were small and short, and that the long-term fate of the captured [[carbon]] is not fully understood. Some groups [[object-v|object]] on principle, arguing that the technique offers an excuse to keep burning fuel. Others worry about the [[pollutant|pollutants]] that mining and grinding might release, or about the [[dust]] carried on the wind from a newly spread field. Such doubts have done more than anything else to [[prompt]] better experiments.","The economics are the harder problem. Governments would have to fund the work, since no farmer will pay for a benefit that the whole world shares, and every scheme depends on political decisions that can be reversed. A proposal that worked in one country could be [[reject|rejected]] in the next. [[enable|Enabling]] all three conditions at once, money, evidence and public consent, is a political task as much as a scientific one.","The ambition, in any case, is deliberately modest. Nobody claims that spreading rock on fields will [[heal]] the climate on its own. Yet if the method [[substantially]] reduces the damage done by industry while improving soil at the same time, it would be one of the rare climate measures that pays for itself."],"exercises":[{"id":"b4u2-e1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What is enhanced weathering, as described in the passage?","options":["A way of capturing gas by speeding up a natural rock reaction","A method of filtering dust out of factory chimneys","A technique for growing crops without any fertiliser","A system for moving heavy rock between quarries"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Paragraph 2 explains that enhanced weathering speeds up the natural process by which certain rocks absorb carbon dioxide."},{"id":"b4u2-e2","type":"multiple-choice","question":"Why is the rock ground into a fine powder?","options":["So that it can be transported more cheaply","So that it mixes easily with animal feed","So that a large surface area is exposed to rain and soil","So that it can be stored in the same buildings as lime"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"Paragraph 2 says that because the grains are tiny, an enormous surface area is exposed, which makes the reaction happen much faster."},{"id":"b4u2-e3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What extra benefit do farmers gain from using basalt?","options":["It removes the need for machinery on the farm","It releases nutrients and helps the soil hold water","It makes the land warmer in winter","It allows them to grow crops without rain"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 3 states that the rock releases nutrients plants need and that the soil holds water better."},{"id":"b4u2-e4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"Which criticism of the technique is mentioned in the passage?","options":["It can only be carried out in very cold climates","It produces more gas than it removes in every case","It may be used as an excuse to continue burning fuel","It has never been tested outside a laboratory"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"Paragraph 5 says some groups object on principle, arguing that the technique offers an excuse to keep burning fuel."},{"id":"b4u2-e5","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What is the writer\'s overall attitude towards enhanced weathering?","options":["Dismissive, because the science is still unproven","Cautiously hopeful, while recognising the difficulties","Certain that it will solve the climate problem","Indifferent, because the costs are too high to measure"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"The writer accepts the uncertainties and the political obstacles but concludes that it could be one of the rare measures that pays for itself, which is a cautiously positive position."},{"id":"b4u2-e6","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The factory was ordered to stop ___ harmful gases into the air.\\"","options":["emitting","grinding","integrating","healing"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To emit is to send out gas, heat or light, which is what the factory was told to stop doing."},{"id":"b4u2-e7","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The committee voted to ___ the proposal, saying it was far too expensive.\\"","options":["enable","prompt","reject","object"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"To reject is to refuse to accept something, which matches a committee turning a proposal down because of its cost."},{"id":"b4u2-e8","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The discovery of the letters ___ her to write the biography she had long postponed.\\"","options":["prompted","emitted","integrated","rejected"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To prompt is to cause someone to do something, which fits a discovery that led her to begin writing."},{"id":"b4u2-e9","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The cost of the repairs rose ___ after the second survey was completed.\\"","options":["substantially","moreover","leading","legitimate"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Substantially means by a large amount, which describes a sharp rise in the cost of repairs."},{"id":"b4u2-e10","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Buses are the main form of public ___ in the city, carrying two million people a day.\\"","options":["substance","transport","powder","pollutant"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Transport is the system or method of carrying passengers or goods, which is exactly what the buses provide."}]},"3":{"id":"b4-u3","book":4,"unit":3,"title":"The Plateau That Kept a Secret","level":"B2","topic":"A village\'s history of shelter","generatedAt":"2026-09-23","wordCount":594,"paragraphs":["On a high plateau in southern France, the winters are long and the wind is relentless. The soil is thin, the farms are small, and the population has always been scattered across isolated hamlets. These are not the conditions in which one expects to find a legend. Yet the villages around one small town are known for something [[exceptional]]: during the Second World War, the people of this plateau sheltered thousands of men, women and children who were [[flee|fleeing]] persecution, and they did so for years without being stopped.","The area was largely Protestant, in a country that was mostly [[catholic|Catholic]]. Its inhabitants had themselves been persecuted in earlier centuries, and they remembered it. According to the accounts of those who were there, the local [[pastor|pastors]] taught that sheltering a stranger was not a matter of choice. Their reading of the [[bible|Bible]] was straightforward: when somebody asks for help, you give it, and you do not ask who they are or where they came from.","The [[parish|parishes]] on the plateau formed a loose [[network]]. When word reached one village that people were hiding in the woods, families in the next village would be told, and beds would be found. Farmers hid strangers in a [[barn]], in a cellar, or in a room behind a kitchen. Children arrived at school under new names and were told, simply, not to talk about it.","The whole undertaking depended on a decision that every household had to make separately. To shelter a stranger was to risk everything, and the penalty for being caught was severe. A few people did [[betray]] their neighbours, and the records of those cases make painful reading. But the majority [[cooperate|cooperated]], and they did so quietly, without a central organisation and without written lists, which is precisely why the [[authority|authorities]] never managed to break the system.","That is not to say the plateau was never threatened. Police and soldiers [[raid|raided]] the area more than once, searching farm buildings and stopping people on the roads. A [[patrol]] could appear at any hour. Young men who had escaped forced labour went into the forests, where they waited for a chance to cross the mountains. Others were [[detain|detained]] and sent away, and some of them never returned.","Escape routes ran in several directions. One followed the [[rail]] line south; another used a disused [[tunnel]] under a hill, where people walked in single file for a kilometre with no light at all. From the edge of the plateau, a narrow [[passage]] between two rocks led down towards a river. Guides took small groups at night and were back before dawn. The [[exit]] from the region was often nothing more than a gap in a hedge.","After the war, the story spread slowly. The people of the plateau had not been [[align|aligned]] with any organised resistance movement, and they had kept few records, so the full numbers will never be known. When journalists finally arrived, some villagers were puzzled by the attention. They had not thought of themselves as remarkable. When the news of the camps reached them, the reaction was not triumph but [[outrage]] and grief.","Today the plateau is quiet again. The farms are larger and fewer, and many of the old buildings have been turned into guest rooms. Visitors come to walk the paths, and a small museum displays photographs of the families who once hid here. The lesson the villagers left behind is not complicated: courage is usually a series of small, unremarkable decisions, made by ordinary people who never expected anyone to be watching."],"exercises":[{"id":"b4u3-e1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What makes the plateau described in the passage unusual?","options":["Its farms produce most of the region\'s food","Its inhabitants sheltered people fleeing persecution for years","It was the site of a major battle during the war","It has been home to the same families for centuries"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 1 says the villages are known for sheltering thousands of people who were fleeing persecution, and for doing so for years without being stopped."},{"id":"b4u3-e2","type":"multiple-choice","question":"Why does the writer mention the earlier history of the area\'s inhabitants?","options":["To explain why they were willing to help strangers","To show that the plateau was wealthy","To prove that the local farms were successful","To explain why so few people lived there"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Paragraph 2 says the inhabitants had themselves been persecuted in earlier centuries and remembered it, which helps explain their willingness to shelter others."},{"id":"b4u3-e3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"Why was the network difficult for the authorities to break?","options":["It was protected by soldiers stationed in the area","It had no central organisation and kept no written lists","Its members all lived inside one large building","It had been officially approved by the government"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 4 explains that the operation was carried out quietly, without a central organisation and without written lists."},{"id":"b4u3-e4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What does the passage suggest about the escape routes?","options":["They were marked clearly on local maps","They were used only during the daytime","They were improvised and often little more than gaps or old tunnels","They ran entirely along the main railway line"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"Paragraph 6 describes a disused tunnel, a narrow passage between rocks and an exit that was nothing more than a gap in a hedge."},{"id":"b4u3-e5","type":"multiple-choice","question":"In the final paragraph, what does the writer suggest was the villagers\' real achievement?","options":["They won official recognition for their district","They built a museum that attracts many visitors","They made a series of small decisions that ordinary people can imitate","They kept their farms running through the war years"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"The closing sentence says courage was a series of small, unremarkable decisions made by ordinary people, which presents their achievement as something others could follow."},{"id":"b4u3-e6","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The two companies agreed to ___ on the design of the new bridge.\\"","options":["betray","cooperate","detain","flee"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"To cooperate is to work with someone towards something you both want, which is what the two companies agreed to do."},{"id":"b4u3-e7","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"He would never ___ a friend\'s secret, whatever the pressure.\\"","options":["betray","raid","align","outrage"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To betray is to be disloyal to someone who trusts you, which is what revealing a friend\'s secret would do."},{"id":"b4u3-e8","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The police were not allowed to ___ a suspect for more than twenty-four hours.\\"","options":["detain","flee","betray","cooperate"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To detain is to officially prevent someone from leaving a place, which is what the police do with a suspect."},{"id":"b4u3-e9","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Her performance in the final was ___; nobody in the audience had expected anything like it.\\"","options":["exceptional","catholic","aligned","detained"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"If something is exceptional, it is outstanding, which fits a performance that astonished the audience."},{"id":"b4u3-e10","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The village was cut off for a week because the only ___ through the mountain had collapsed.\\"","options":["patrol","tunnel","outrage","barn"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"A tunnel is an underground passage that vehicles or people travel through, and its collapse would cut a village off."}]},"4":{"id":"b4-u4","book":4,"unit":4,"title":"The Teacher Who Rewrote the Timetable","level":"B2","topic":"A career in technology","generatedAt":"2026-09-23","wordCount":497,"paragraphs":["The first time Nadia walked into a university lecture hall, she counted four women in a room of ninety. She was seventeen, the first in her family to go on to higher [[education]], and she had won a place on a course in software engineering because she was, in her own words, stubborn. I did not know that I was supposed to feel out of place, she says. Nobody had told me.","That ignorance was, in a sense, her greatest [[tool]]. She asked questions that more cautious students kept to themselves, and she refused to accept that a [[mere]] difference in background should decide what she could learn. What she lacked was not ability but [[guidance]]. Nobody in her family had ever applied for a job in the [[field]], and she had no idea how to read the codes and conventions of an office she had never seen.","Two things changed her course. The first was a teacher who took her aside and offered the kind of practical [[encouragement]] that is easy to underestimate: a list of companies that hired graduates, a reminder that a rejection is not a verdict, and a standing invitation to ask for help. The second was a student society where she met other women in the same [[position]]. For the first time she felt [[comfortable]] enough to admit what she did not know.","After graduating she joined a small company and discovered how quickly a career can [[soar]]. Within six years she was leading a team of thirty. She was also, by then, the most [[vocal]] person in the building on the subject of hiring. She argued that a fair process does not happen by accident, and that [[inclusion]] is a practice rather than a slogan. When she was offered a senior [[position]] with the title of director, she accepted on one condition: that she be allowed to [[hold]] the budget for recruitment herself.","Her approach is unglamorous. She rewrites job advertisements to remove the long lists of requirements that [[inspire]] nobody and discourage good candidates. She insists that interviews be scored against fixed criteria, so that [[power]] in the room belongs to the evidence rather than to the loudest voice. She has made it a rule that every new team must [[face]] the question of who is missing from it. And she refuses to [[endorse]] the fashionable claim that the problem has already been solved.","Progress is real but uneven. The proportion of women in technical roles has risen steadily, and pay gaps have narrowed, though neither has closed. In some companies the culture of the [[workplace]] has changed more slowly than the numbers. Nadia is careful with the word [[equal]]: she points out that equal opportunity at the door means little if the building itself was designed for somebody else. The [[responsibility]], she says, lies with those who [[foster]] the habits of a team, which is to say with managers, not with the people who arrive and find themselves alone."],"exercises":[{"id":"b4u4-e1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What does the passage say about Nadia\'s first lecture?","options":["She was one of very few women in a large group","She arrived late and missed the introduction","She had already studied the subject for a year","She decided to leave the course immediately"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Paragraph 1 says she counted four women in a room of ninety."},{"id":"b4u4-e2","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What did Nadia lack most at university, according to the passage?","options":["The ability to understand technical subjects","The money to pay for her course","Practical advice about how the professional world works","Friends who shared her interest in software"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"Paragraph 2 states that what she lacked was not ability but guidance, since nobody in her family had applied for a job in the field."},{"id":"b4u4-e3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"Why did Nadia accept the director\'s position on a condition?","options":["She wanted a higher salary than was offered","She wanted control of the recruitment budget","She wanted to move to a different city","She wanted to work part-time"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 4 says she accepted on one condition: that she be allowed to hold the budget for recruitment herself."},{"id":"b4u4-e4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"Which of the following is NOT part of Nadia\'s approach to hiring?","options":["Shortening the list of requirements in job advertisements","Scoring interviews against fixed criteria","Asking who is missing from every new team","Interviewing only candidates from her own university"],"answerIndex":3,"explanation":"The passage describes the first three practices. It says nothing about limiting interviews to graduates of one university, and the rest of the passage suggests the opposite."},{"id":"b4u4-e5","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What does Nadia mean when she says she is careful with the word \'equal\'?","options":["That equal treatment at the door is not enough on its own","That women and men have different abilities","That pay gaps have now been closed completely","That the word is too old-fashioned to use"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Paragraph 6 explains that equal opportunity at the door means little if the building itself was designed for somebody else."},{"id":"b4u4-e6","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The scheme was designed to ___ new talent in the region\'s smaller towns.\\"","options":["foster","endorse","face","hold"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To foster is to help a skill or feeling develop over time, which is what a scheme for new talent would aim to do."},{"id":"b4u4-e7","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Several former players have publicly ___ the campaign to improve facilities for young athletes.\\"","options":["endorsed","fostered","soared","held"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To endorse is to express formal support or approval for something, which is what the former players have done publicly."},{"id":"b4u4-e8","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"House prices in the district have ___ since the new rail link opened.\\"","options":["soared","fostered","endorsed","inspired"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To soar is to increase quickly to a high level, which describes what happened to house prices."},{"id":"b4u4-e9","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Her speech ___ a generation of young scientists to apply for the programme.\\"","options":["inspired","faced","held","soared"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To inspire is to encourage someone by making them feel confident and eager to do something, which is what the speech did."},{"id":"b4u4-e10","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"He spent a ___ ten minutes on the report, and it showed.\\"","options":["mere","vocal","equal","comfortable"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Mere is used to emphasise how small or unimportant something is, which fits a report written in only ten minutes."}]},"5":{"id":"b4-u5","book":4,"unit":5,"title":"Painting the Hour, Not the Story","level":"B2","topic":"Art history and Impressionism","generatedAt":"2026-09-23","wordCount":566,"paragraphs":["In 1874 a group of painters in Paris did something that the official art world found unforgivable. They rented a studio, hung their work themselves and invited the public in. The show was not a success. A critic picked up one painting, a harbour at dawn rendered in loose grey and orange [[stroke|strokes]], and used its title, Impression, as an insult. The painters, who had chosen the name for themselves, kept it. Within thirty years the [[movement]] they started had changed what a picture could be.","To understand why the work seemed so shocking, you have to know what the [[rule|rules]] of the [[academic]] salon required. A serious painting was expected to be large, smooth and historical: a scene from myth or legend, composed in a studio, with every edge carefully finished. Its subject mattered more than its surface, and [[traditional]] taste placed history above portrait, and portrait above a bowl of fruit. The young painters who gathered in the rooms along the river had begun to doubt all of this. What they wanted to record was not a story but an hour of the day.","Their [[practice]] was unusual, and the [[distinctive]] look of the finished work followed directly from it. They carried their materials outdoors and worked quickly, because the light changes. A single [[canvas]] might be completed in one sitting, in front of the subject, with the paint laid on in thick patches rather than blended smooth. This produces a surface that looks unfinished at close range. Step back, however, and the patches combine, and the eye does the mixing. The result is more [[realistic]] in one sense than any studio painting, because it records how we actually see: not as a camera, but as a moving observer whose attention keeps shifting.","Light was the [[key]] obsession. Snow in the afternoon, steam in a station, water at sunset: these were subjects because they allowed the painter to show how [[sunlight]] alters colour. Water was especially useful, since it offers both a surface and a [[reflection]] of everything above it. The [[width]] of a river might be implied by nothing more than three horizontal bands of blue and grey. Faces were left deliberately vague, and edges were allowed to [[blur]] into one another, so that a crowd becomes an [[array]] of hats and shoulders rather than a set of portraits.","The painters were also, in a quiet way, observers of social change. Their pictures include railway stations, new apartment blocks and the growing middle [[class]] at leisure. They painted the [[cafe|caf\xe9s]] where they met, and the theatres and dance halls of a city being rebuilt. [[unlike|Unlike]] the salon painters, they found the ordinary evening interesting. A woman standing at a bar, a pair of dancers, a bowl of fruit on a tablecloth: nothing in these scenes would have seemed worth a large canvas to the generation before them.","The early years were hard. Sales were poor, and the group depended on a handful of loyal buyers and dealers. The [[acceptance]] they wanted came slowly, and by the time it arrived several of the founders were dead. Today the [[cinema]] has taught us to read their compositions almost without effort: the cropped edge, the sudden change of focus, the sense that the camera has caught a moment already in progress. The visual vocabulary of the films we watch was invented, in part, with a brush."],"exercises":[{"id":"b4u5-e1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"How did the group of painters get their name?","options":["They took it from a critic\'s mocking review of one painting","They invented it after their first exhibition was a great success","It was given to them by the official salon","It was the name of the studio they rented in 1874"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Paragraph 1 says a critic used the title of one painting, Impression, as an insult; the painters had chosen the name for themselves and kept it."},{"id":"b4u5-e2","type":"multiple-choice","question":"According to paragraph 2, what did the official salon expect from a serious painting?","options":["That it should be painted outdoors in a single sitting","That it should be large, smooth and historical","That it should show ordinary people at work","That it should use only three colours"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 2 lists the requirements: large, smooth and historical, composed in a studio with every edge finished."},{"id":"b4u5-e3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"Why does the writer say the new paintings were in one sense more realistic?","options":["Because they showed scenes exactly as photographs do","Because they used cheaper and more natural materials","Because they recorded how a moving observer actually sees","Because they included more accurate details of clothing"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"Paragraph 3 explains that the result records how we actually see, not as a camera but as a moving observer whose attention keeps shifting."},{"id":"b4u5-e4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"Why was water such a useful subject for these painters?","options":["It was easy and cheap to paint","It showed both a surface and a reflection of what was above it","It allowed them to work indoors during the winter","It never changed from one hour to the next"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 4 says water offers both a surface and a reflection of everything above it."},{"id":"b4u5-e5","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What does the final sentence of the passage suggest?","options":["That cinema borrowed visual techniques from these painters","That the painters were influenced by early films","That painting has been replaced by film","That the group\'s work is now almost forgotten"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"The writer says the visual vocabulary of the films we watch was invented in part with a brush, meaning cinema inherited techniques from these painters."},{"id":"b4u5-e6","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"From the top of the hill, the lake was perfectly still and showed a clear ___ of the mountains.\\"","options":["reflection","stroke","width","rule"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"A reflection is an image seen in a mirror or other shiny surface, which is what the still water produced."},{"id":"b4u5-e7","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Tears ___ her vision as she read the final page of the letter.\\"","options":["blurred","marked","blur","stroked"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To blur is to make something unclear or difficult to see, and the sentence needs the past tense form blurred."},{"id":"b4u5-e8","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The shop displayed a dazzling ___ of cakes, jams and imported cheeses.\\"","options":["array","reflection","canvas","practice"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"An array is a large group or number of things, which suits a display of many different products."},{"id":"b4u5-e9","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The two brothers share a ___ sense of humour that everyone recognises at once.\\"","options":["distinctive","academic","realistic","key"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"If something is distinctive, it has a special quality that is easy to recognise, which fits a shared sense of humour."},{"id":"b4u5-e10","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"___ her sister, Maya has never enjoyed large parties.\\"","options":["Unlike","Beyond","Moreover","Thus"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Unlike is used to introduce someone completely different from the person mentioned, which is the contrast the sentence makes."}]},"6":{"id":"b4-u6","book":4,"unit":6,"title":"Before the Sun Came Up","level":"B2","topic":"Remembering a wartime landing","generatedAt":"2026-09-23","wordCount":583,"paragraphs":["My grandmother never talked about the war until she was eighty. Then, one winter, she began to tell me about her father, who had gone away as a young man and come back with a [[wound]] that never properly healed. He had been [[originally]] from a small town inland, and he had lied about his age in order to enlist. She kept his letters in a tin. The paper was thin, and the handwriting grew worse as the months went on.","The story began, as these stories do, with an [[outbreak]] of fighting far from home. Troops were [[mobilize|mobilized]] in a hurry and sent across the sea. On a beach in April, before dawn, they went ashore in small boats. The sea was [[wild]] and the boats were difficult to steer. The [[landing]] was a failure by any military measure: the position was never taken, and the losses were terrible. Yet in the years that followed, that morning came to stand for something [[beyond]] the battle itself, for endurance, for loyalty, and for the cost of both.","I once asked my grandmother why people [[mark]] the day with such solemnity. She thought for a moment and said that a [[nation]] needs to remember what it has asked of its young people. Her father had [[sacrifice|sacrificed]] his health, his youth and eventually his peace of mind. He had spent months as a [[prisoner]], and he came home thinner and quieter than the boy who had left. He never spoke of a [[victory]], because for him there had not been one.","The ceremonies began in a small way while the soldiers were still abroad. Families who had lost someone wanted a [[joint]] occasion on which to grieve together, since the graves were on the other side of the world. They gathered at memorials, sang an [[anthem]] and stood in [[silence]] at the hour when the boats had gone in. There was no attempt to glorify the [[fighting]]. The mood was closer to grief than to pride, and the [[tribute]] they offered was simple: names read aloud, flowers laid, a promise not to forget.","The customs have grown and changed. Today the day begins before first light. Crowds stand in the cold, and the service opens with a short address and a period of reflection. Some people [[pray]]; others simply stand with their thoughts. There is a bugle call, and then the silence, and [[thus]] the day is formally begun. Later there are marches, and families pin medals to the chests of grandchildren who never met the men who earned them.","What strikes me now is the [[determination]] behind it. The old soldiers I met as a child were not sentimental men, and they were not, on the whole, eager to talk. Yet they turned up every year, in the rain, in their eighties and nineties, because they believed that the dead deserved an audience. My grandmother called it a duty; her father called it paying respects. Both meant the same thing.","When she died, we found the tin of letters in her cupboard. Some were cheerful and full of jokes, which surprised me. Others were written in a hand I could barely read, on paper that had gone soft with age. We read them aloud at the kitchen table, one by one, and the room went very quiet. That, I think, is what the day is for. It is not really about the past at all. It is about what we choose to carry forward."],"exercises":[{"id":"b4u6-e1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"How does the writer describe the landing itself?","options":["As a quick and complete success","As a failure that cost many lives","As an operation that was cancelled at the last moment","As a plan that had never been attempted before"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 2 says the landing was a failure by any military measure, since the position was never taken and the losses were terrible."},{"id":"b4u6-e2","type":"multiple-choice","question":"Why did the first ceremonies take place?","options":["Families wanted an occasion to grieve together","Soldiers demanded a public parade","The government required every town to hold one","Veterans wanted to celebrate their victory"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Paragraph 4 says families who had lost someone wanted a joint occasion on which to grieve together, because the graves were far away."},{"id":"b4u6-e3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What was the mood of those early ceremonies, according to the passage?","options":["Closer to grief than to pride","Loud and celebratory","Formal and entirely silent","Cheerful and full of jokes"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Paragraph 4 states that the mood was closer to grief than to pride, and that there was no attempt to glorify the fighting."},{"id":"b4u6-e4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"Why does the writer mention that some of the letters were cheerful?","options":["To show that the soldiers did not understand the war","To suggest that the family had misread the letters","Because it was unexpected and made the letters more human","To prove that the letters had been written by someone else"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"The writer says the cheerfulness surprised them, which shows the letters revealed an unexpected side of a man remembered mainly for his suffering."},{"id":"b4u6-e5","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What is the main point of the final paragraph?","options":["That the day is more about what we carry forward than about the past itself","That the letters should be given to a museum","That the family should stop attending the ceremony","That the war is now too distant to be remembered"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"The closing sentences say the day is not really about the past at all, but about what we choose to carry forward."},{"id":"b4u6-e6","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Her ___ to finish the race, despite the injury, impressed everyone who watched.\\"","options":["determination","silence","tribute","landing"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Determination is what you have when you keep trying to do something difficult, which is what finishing the race despite an injury required."},{"id":"b4u6-e7","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The concert was organised as a ___ to the conductor, who had led the orchestra for forty years.\\"","options":["tribute","outbreak","prisoner","anthem"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"A tribute is something said, done or given to express respect for someone, which is what the concert was."},{"id":"b4u6-e8","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"He had to ___ his place on the team in order to care for his father.\\"","options":["sacrifice","mark","pray","mobilize"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To sacrifice is to give up something in order to do something for someone else, which is what he did with his place on the team."},{"id":"b4u6-e9","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The two universities run a ___ programme in which students spend a year at each institution.\\"","options":["joint","wild","original","beyond"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"When something is joint, it is done by or involves two or more groups, which describes a programme run by two universities."},{"id":"b4u6-e10","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The building was closed for six months, and ___ the whole project was delayed by a year.\\"","options":["thus","beyond","originally","wild"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Thus means as a result of what was just mentioned, which links the six-month closure to the year-long delay."}]},"7":{"id":"b4-u7","book":4,"unit":7,"title":"When a Language Stops Being Spoken","level":"B2","topic":"Language loss and revival","generatedAt":"2026-09-23","wordCount":625,"paragraphs":["There are roughly seven thousand languages in the world, and the number is falling quickly. Linguists [[generally]] agree that at least half of them are [[endangered]], and that somewhere a language loses its last fluent speaker about once a fortnight. When that happens, the loss is not merely sentimental. A language carries knowledge of plants, weather, medicine and family relationships that may exist nowhere else, and once it goes it is gone [[forever]].","The most common [[reason]] for the decline is not hostility but practicality. A community that speaks a minority language usually finds that schools, courts and offices operate in a larger one. Parents who want their children to do well encourage them to speak the dominant language at home. Within two or three generations the children are fluent in the majority tongue and merely competent in their own, and the grandchildren may speak it not at all. In such [[case|cases]], nobody decides to abandon the language. It is simply [[lose|lost]] through a series of small, sensible choices.","The written record makes the problem harder to study. In many cases the first [[reference]] to a minority language comes from a traveller or an official rather than from its speakers, and such accounts are often brief and inaccurate. A language with a long oral tradition can therefore look insignificant in the archive, and a community that has been misdescribed for two centuries may begin to believe the description.","Moving to the city speeds the process up. When people leave a village for the capital, they leave behind the community in which the language was used all day. [[urban|Urban]] life is conducted in the majority language, and the minority tongue becomes a private thing, spoken with older relatives and on visits home. Some families make a deliberate [[attempt]] to keep it alive at the dinner table; many do not, and the language [[cease|ceases]] to be used in the household at all.","The [[diversity]] at stake is difficult to overstate. Languages differ not only in vocabulary but in what they oblige their speakers to notice. Some describe direction with great precision; a few have no words for left and right at all. Some encode the source of every statement, so that a speaker must indicate whether a claim was seen, heard or merely guessed. When such a language [[lose|loses]] its last speakers, an entire way of organising experience disappears with it.","Some losses are sudden rather than gradual. War, disease and natural disaster can [[hit]] a small community with [[devastating]] effect: a single [[earthquake]], or an illness that kills the elderly, may leave too few speakers for the language to continue. In the worst historical cases, and here the word [[genocide]] is the only accurate one, the speakers themselves were killed, and the language went with them. For most languages, however, the road to [[extinction]] is slow and quiet, and its final stage is simply a generation that never learned to speak.","Historically, the pressure was often deliberate. Children were punished for speaking their [[native]] language at school, and families were encouraged to [[assimilate]] as quickly as possible. Policies of that kind have mostly been abandoned, but their effects last for generations. In some regions, fewer than five [[percent]] of children now speak the language their grandparents used every day.","The picture is not uniformly gloomy. Language-revival programmes have had real success in a number of countries, from Wales to New Zealand. In each successful case, three conditions seem to matter: official recognition, education in the language, and a community that wants it. Where all three are present, the decline can be slowed and sometimes reversed. Where one is missing, the [[danger]] is that the effort fades into a footnote rather than becoming part of ordinary life."],"exercises":[{"id":"b4u7-e1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"Why is the loss of a language described as more than sentimental?","options":["Because it makes international communication more difficult","Because it carries knowledge that may exist nowhere else","Because it reduces the number of books that are published","Because governments lose tax revenue as a result"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 1 says a language carries knowledge of plants, weather, medicine and kinship that may exist nowhere else."},{"id":"b4u7-e2","type":"multiple-choice","question":"According to the passage, what is the usual cause of language decline?","options":["Practical decisions made by families who want their children to succeed","Laws that forbid minority languages outright","A lack of interest among older speakers","The absence of any written form of the language"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Paragraph 2 says the most common reason is not hostility but practicality, and that parents encourage the dominant language so their children will do well."},{"id":"b4u7-e3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"Why does the writer mention travellers and officials in paragraph 3?","options":["To show that minority languages were often poorly documented by outsiders","To prove that such languages were widely studied","To explain how the languages were first taught in schools","To argue that oral traditions are unreliable"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"The paragraph says the first reference to a minority language often comes from a traveller or an official, and that such accounts are often brief and inaccurate."},{"id":"b4u7-e4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What happens when a language encodes the source of every statement?","options":["Speakers must indicate whether a claim was seen, heard or guessed","Speakers cannot describe the past at all","The language has no words for numbers","The language is easier for outsiders to learn"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Paragraph 5 gives exactly this example to show how languages differ in what they oblige speakers to notice."},{"id":"b4u7-e5","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What is the writer\'s attitude in the final paragraph?","options":["Complete despair about the future of minority languages","Total confidence that the problem has been solved","Cautious optimism, since revival is possible under certain conditions","Indifference, because the process cannot be influenced"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"The writer says the picture is not uniformly gloomy, gives examples of success and lists three conditions that make revival possible."},{"id":"b4u7-e6","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The charity is working to protect ___ species in the national park.\\"","options":["endangered","urban","native","devastating"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"If a species is endangered, it has become very rare and may die out, which is what a conservation charity would work to prevent."},{"id":"b4u7-e7","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The storm was ___ for the coastal villages, destroying almost every road in the area.\\"","options":["devastating","endangered","urban","native"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"If something is devastating, it causes great harm or damage, which matches the destruction described."},{"id":"b4u7-e8","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"After two failed interviews he made a third ___ to find work.\\"","options":["attempt","reference","percent","danger"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"An attempt is an act of trying to do something, which fits a third try at finding work."},{"id":"b4u7-e9","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The factory will ___ production at the end of the year, and four hundred jobs will go.\\"","options":["cease","hit","lose","assimilate"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To cease is to stop doing something or to stop it happening, which is what the factory will do with production."},{"id":"b4u7-e10","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The school takes pride in the ___ of its students, who come from more than thirty countries.\\"","options":["diversity","extinction","earthquake","reference"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Diversity is the quality of having many different forms or types, which suits a student body from many countries."}]},"8":{"id":"b4-u8","book":4,"unit":8,"title":"The Diet That Started as Medicine","level":"B2","topic":"Nutrition and metabolism","generatedAt":"2026-09-23","wordCount":510,"paragraphs":["Most diets are invented to help people lose weight. The ketogenic diet is unusual in that it began as a treatment for a serious illness. In the 1920s, doctors noticed that fasting reduced the number of seizures in patients with [[epilepsy]], and they wondered whether the effect could be reproduced by changing what a patient ate. The result was a regime extremely [[high]] in [[fat]] and very [[low]] in carbohydrate. It worked for some patients, particularly children whose seizures had not responded to medication.","The biology is well [[known]], at least in outline. Normally the body runs on [[glucose]], a simple sugar produced when [[starch|starches]] and sugars are digested. When carbohydrate is restricted sharply, the [[liver]] begins converting fat into molecules called ketones, and the brain, which cannot use fat directly, starts to [[burn]] ketones instead. This shift is a [[metabolic]] state, and it is the reason the diet has to be followed consistently: a single large portion of bread or rice can interrupt it.","For decades the diet was used mainly as a medical tool, supervised by doctors and dietitians. Then, in the 1990s, it was taken up by people who simply wanted to lose weight. The logic was straightforward: if the body is running on fat, it may draw on stored fat more readily. Whether this produces better results than an ordinary diet with the same number of calories is still debated, and the [[summary]] of the evidence is mixed. What is clear is that people on such a diet often [[gain]] nothing, largely because fat is filling and the menu is monotonous.","Advocates claim other benefits. Some report better mental clarity and steadier [[performance]] on long tasks. Some describe improvements in blood sugar [[management]] for people with type 2 diabetes. The mechanisms are plausible, since a [[powerful]] reduction in glucose intake does change the way the body handles insulin. However, the trials are usually small and short, and their results are difficult to compare with one another.","The practical difficulties are considerable. The diet is restrictive, and it is easy to [[keep]] to it for a month and very hard for a decade. Fruit, most bread, potatoes and rice all disappear from the plate, and the [[oil|oils]] and fats that replace them must be chosen with care. [[olive|Olive]] oil, [[coconut]] oil, butter, nuts, eggs and fish do most of the work; olive oil is the most versatile, while coconut oil is popular because it is easy to measure. Some people also report digestive problems, headaches and tiredness during the first weeks.","Anyone considering the diet should [[check]] with a doctor first, particularly if they take medication. It can affect blood pressure, digestion and the balance of minerals in the body, and in rare cases it can be genuinely dangerous. It is a clinical tool with a clinical history, not a general recommendation. Before making changes, it is worth asking what the diet is actually for: a specific medical condition, a short-term goal, or a permanent way of eating. The answer decides whether the effort is worth it."],"exercises":[{"id":"b4u8-e1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"How did the ketogenic diet begin?","options":["As a treatment for patients with epilepsy","As a plan to help athletes run faster","As a wartime solution to food shortages","As a way of preserving food without refrigeration"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Paragraph 1 explains that in the 1920s doctors noticed fasting reduced seizures in patients with epilepsy and tried to reproduce the effect through diet."},{"id":"b4u8-e2","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What happens in the body when carbohydrate is sharply restricted?","options":["The liver converts fat into ketones, which the brain can use","The brain begins to use fat directly as fuel","The body stops producing glucose altogether","The liver stops working until carbohydrate returns"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Paragraph 2 says the liver converts fat into ketones and the brain, which cannot use fat directly, starts to burn ketones instead."},{"id":"b4u8-e3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"Why does the diet have to be followed consistently?","options":["Because a single large portion of bread or rice can interrupt the metabolic state","Because the body forgets how to digest fat after a day","Because doctors must check the patient every week","Because the diet only works in cold climates"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Paragraph 2 states that one large portion of bread or rice can interrupt the state, which is why consistency matters."},{"id":"b4u8-e4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What does the passage say about weight loss on this diet?","options":["It is guaranteed to be faster than on any other diet","It is debated whether it is better than an ordinary diet with the same calories","It has been proved to be permanent in every case","It happens only in patients with epilepsy"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 3 says it is still debated whether the diet produces better results than an ordinary diet with the same number of calories."},{"id":"b4u8-e5","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What is the writer\'s advice to anyone considering the diet?","options":["Start it immediately without telling anyone","Consult a doctor first and consider what the diet is for","Follow it for at least ten years to see results","Replace all fat with fruit and rice"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 6 advises checking with a doctor first and asking whether the diet is for a medical condition, a short-term goal or a permanent way of eating."},{"id":"b4u8-e6","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The doctor asked him to ___ his blood sugar every morning before breakfast.\\"","options":["check","burn","gain","keep"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To check is to ask or find out whether something is correct, which is what measuring blood sugar does each morning."},{"id":"b4u8-e7","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Athletes who train at altitude often see an improvement in their ___ during the following season.\\"","options":["performance","management","summary","starch"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Performance is how well someone functions or works, which is what improves after altitude training."},{"id":"b4u8-e8","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The report gave a clear ___ of the findings in fewer than three hundred words.\\"","options":["summary","incentive","subsidy","liver"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"A summary is a short statement containing the main information, which is what a report of fewer than three hundred words provides."},{"id":"b4u8-e9","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Over the winter she ___ confidence in her own judgement.\\"","options":["gained","burned","checked","kept"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To gain is to gradually get more of a quality or feeling, which is what happened to her confidence."},{"id":"b4u8-e10","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The medicine had a ___ effect on the pain, easing it within minutes.\\"","options":["powerful","metabolic","high","low"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"If something is powerful, it has a strong effect, which fits a medicine that eased pain within minutes."}]},"9":{"id":"b4-u9","book":4,"unit":9,"title":"The Man Who Knew the Theory","level":"B2","topic":"A lesson in practical judgement","generatedAt":"2026-09-23","wordCount":609,"paragraphs":["Ellis had been told all his life that he was clever. He had a first-class [[diploma]] from a good university, where he had chosen to specialise in [[geology]] because the rocks, at least, did not argue. His marks were the highest in his year. He had [[excel|excelled]] in every written examination he had ever sat, and his tutor had described him in a letter as the most [[keen]] undergraduate of his generation. What the letter did not mention was that Ellis had never done a day of practical work in his life.","The interview was for a junior post at a research station in the north. The building was old and cold, and the man who met him was small, untidy and apparently unimpressed. He introduced himself as Dr Halloran and led Ellis into a room where a table was covered with instruments. You have a fine [[intellect]], Halloran said, reading the letter. So do many people. What I need to know is whether you are [[competent]]. Those are not the same quality.","The test was not written. Halloran pointed to a sealed box on the table and explained that it contained a sample of rock that might or might not be [[radioactive]]. The task was to establish which, and to do it safely. Ellis reached for the box at once. Halloran stopped him. Think first, he said. Every [[collision]] between a careless hand and the wrong material has a cost. What is the [[minimum]] you need to know before you open anything?","Ellis hesitated, then began to work through the problem aloud. He considered the instruments available and the [[area]] they covered. He asked whether the sample had been stored near photographic film, and what previous tests had shown. He sketched a rough calculation, using the [[theoretical]] framework he had learned, and found that it gave him two possible answers. Instead of choosing between them, he said so. Halloran nodded slowly. Good, he said. A [[weak]] answer that admits its limits is worth more than a confident one that hides them.","They talked for two hours. Halloran had worked in the field for thirty years and had once published a paper on the chemistry of deep rock that was still cited. He spoke about the habits of mind the work required: careful observation, patience, and a willingness to be wrong. Ellis, who was still a [[bachelor]] and lived alone with his books, mentioned that he had also read [[psychology]] and [[sociology]] in his spare time, and that he had once tried to teach himself the mathematics of [[relativity]]. Halloran was not impressed by the list. Reading is not the same as knowing, he said. The [[analytic]] mind is a fine instrument, but it will not tell you whether a rock is hot.","What Ellis learned that afternoon was not a technique but a habit. At university he had been rewarded for producing answers, and he had come to believe that a [[job]] was simply a place where answers were required. He saw now that judgement was [[integral]] to the work, and that judgement could only be built from experience. Theory and practice were not rivals; they were two halves of the same [[cognitive]] process, and neither was sufficient on its own.","He was offered the post, and took it. Years later, when he was asked to interview candidates himself, he used the same sealed box, though by then it contained nothing more dangerous than an ordinary lump of stone. The point of the exercise was never the rock. It was to find out whether a young person, having been taught to be quick, could learn to be careful."],"exercises":[{"id":"b4u9-e1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What was missing from Ellis\'s education, according to the first paragraph?","options":["Any practical experience of the work","A good academic record","A letter of recommendation","Knowledge of the theory behind the subject"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Paragraph 1 ends by saying that Ellis had never done a day of practical work in his life."},{"id":"b4u9-e2","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What distinction does Halloran draw in paragraph 2?","options":["Between having a fine intellect and being competent","Between working in the north and working in the south","Between reading a letter and writing one","Between theoretical geology and practical geology"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Halloran says Ellis has a fine intellect but that what he needs to know is whether he is competent, and that these are not the same quality."},{"id":"b4u9-e3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"Why did Halloran stop Ellis from opening the box?","options":["Because the box was locked and the key was missing","Because Ellis had not yet thought about the risk","Because the sample belonged to another researcher","Because Ellis had not read the correct textbook"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Halloran tells him to think first, warns of the cost of a careless collision with the wrong material, and asks what the minimum he needs to know is."},{"id":"b4u9-e4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"How did Halloran react to Ellis\'s calculation giving two possible answers?","options":["He was pleased that Ellis admitted the uncertainty","He was annoyed that Ellis had made a mistake","He insisted that Ellis choose one answer immediately","He refused to discuss the calculation at all"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Halloran nods slowly and says that a weak answer which admits its limits is worth more than a confident one that hides them."},{"id":"b4u9-e5","type":"multiple-choice","question":"Why does the writer mention that the box later contained only ordinary stone?","options":["To show that the exercise was always about the candidate\'s habits of mind, not the rock","To explain that the station had run out of dangerous samples","To prove that Halloran had been dishonest with Ellis","To suggest that geology is a less serious subject than it appears"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"The final paragraph says the point of the exercise was never the rock, but whether a young person could learn to be careful."},{"id":"b4u9-e6","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The two drivers were shaken but unhurt after the ___ at the junction.\\"","options":["collision","diploma","intellect","area"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"A collision is the act of two things hitting each other, which is what happened at the junction."},{"id":"b4u9-e7","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"A ___ of two hours\' notice is required if you wish to cancel the booking.\\"","options":["minimum","bachelor","relativity","geology"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"The minimum is the smallest amount, which fits the shortest period of notice that is accepted."},{"id":"b4u9-e8","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"She is highly ___ at her job and has never missed a deadline.\\"","options":["competent","analytic","theoretical","integral"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"If someone is competent, they are able to think or act successfully, which is confirmed by never missing a deadline."},{"id":"b4u9-e9","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"He is ___ on birdwatching, and can name every species in the valley.\\"","options":["keen","weak","integral","cognitive"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"If someone is keen, they are eager and enthusiastic about something, which fits his detailed knowledge of local birds."},{"id":"b4u9-e10","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Trust is ___ to any long partnership; without it nothing else works.\\"","options":["integral","theoretical","radioactive","weak"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Something that is integral is an important part of the whole, which is what trust is to a long partnership."}]},"10":{"id":"b4-u10","book":4,"unit":10,"title":"The Ledger in the Basement","level":"B2","topic":"An inquiry into public money","generatedAt":"2026-09-23","wordCount":599,"paragraphs":["The building that housed the city\'s transport authority had been admired when it opened. From the outside it looked like a statement of confidence: glass, steel and a plaza with a fountain. Inside, on the fourth floor, sat the [[administrator]] in charge of a budget larger than that of some small countries. His name appeared on no ballot, and most residents could not have named him. Yet his signature controlled the buses, the roads, the bridges and the depots, and therefore a great deal of daily life.","For years the accounts looked healthy. The city was [[affluent]] by regional standards, and its [[revenue]] from fares and property taxes grew steadily. Then a routine [[audit]], ordered because a new finance law required one, began to find small inconsistencies. Payments had been made to companies that appeared to have no employees and no equipment. Contracts for [[infrastructure]] repairs had been awarded without competition. The sums were not enormous individually, but there were a great many of them.","The pattern that emerged was simple. A handful of officials had arranged for public money to flow to private firms, and a share had flowed back. In one [[transaction]], a supplier received a contract to resurface twelve kilometres of road and then submitted an invoice for fifteen. In another, a company with a single van won the contract to supply [[merchandise]] for the transport museum\'s shop, although it had never sold anything at [[retail]] in its life. When questioned, the officials said the arrangements were entirely [[legitimate]] and the paperwork was complete. In a narrow sense, it was.","The methods were not subtle. Investigators found evidence that the administrator had tried to [[manipulate]] the tender process by writing specifications so detailed that only one firm could possibly qualify. There were suggestions that he had accepted gifts from contractors, and that on at least one occasion money had changed hands to secure a signature. To [[bribe]] a public official is a criminal offence, and the [[incentive]] was obvious: a contract worth millions, and nobody watching closely.","The inquiry also uncovered waste that was, strictly speaking, not criminal. Because repairs were never carried out properly, the city was spending more each year on patching roads than it would have spent on rebuilding them. Money intended to support public transport as a [[subsidy]] had been diverted to cover the shortfall. Equipment that could easily have been [[automate|automated]] was operated by hand, with staff paid overtime to do work a machine could have done. And the [[headquarters]] itself had been renovated twice in five years, at a cost nobody could justify.","None of this was illegal by accident. Rules existed, but they had been written in a way that made them easy to [[violate]] without breaking. Officials were allowed to [[dispose]] of documents after three years, and many of the relevant files had gone out with the [[rubbish]]. Contracts below a certain value did not require approval, so contracts had been split into smaller pieces. The result was a system that was [[corrupt]] in substance while appearing correct on paper.","The response, when it came, was slow and partial. Several officials resigned, and one was prosecuted. The city council promised to [[legislate]] new rules on disclosure, and a new administrator was appointed with a mandate to publish every contract above a modest threshold. Whether the change will last is uncertain. What the episode showed, more clearly than any report could, is that oversight is not a luxury. Where nobody is watching, the rules tend to bend, and the cost is paid by everyone who depends on the service."],"exercises":[{"id":"b4u10-e1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What was unusual about the administrator\'s position?","options":["He controlled a very large budget although nobody had elected him","He had been elected by a large majority","He was also in charge of the city\'s schools","He had no responsibility for spending decisions"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Paragraph 1 says his name appeared on no ballot and most residents could not have named him, yet his signature controlled buses, roads, bridges and depots."},{"id":"b4u10-e2","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What first drew attention to the accounts?","options":["A routine audit required by a new finance law","A complaint from a member of the public","A newspaper investigation","The resignation of a senior official"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Paragraph 2 says a routine audit, ordered because a new finance law required one, began to find small inconsistencies."},{"id":"b4u10-e3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"How did the administrator try to influence the tender process?","options":["By writing specifications so detailed that only one firm could qualify","By cancelling all contracts and starting again","By publishing every bid on the city\'s website","By hiring an independent panel of engineers"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Paragraph 4 says investigators found evidence he tried to manipulate the tender process by writing specifications so detailed that only one firm could possibly qualify."},{"id":"b4u10-e4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"According to paragraph 6, why was so much of this activity not strictly illegal?","options":["The rules had been written in a way that made them easy to avoid breaking","The officials had been given written permission by the council","The sums involved were too small to be covered by any law","The contracts had all been approved by the public"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"The paragraph says rules existed but were written so that they were easy to violate without breaking, for example by splitting contracts into smaller pieces."},{"id":"b4u10-e5","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What is the writer\'s conclusion in the final paragraph?","options":["That oversight is essential because rules bend when nobody is watching","That the city should stop running public transport","That the new administrator has solved the problem completely","That such episodes are rare and unlikely to be repeated"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"The writer says the episode showed that oversight is not a luxury, and that where nobody is watching the rules tend to bend."},{"id":"b4u10-e6","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The accountant was asked to ___ the charity\'s records going back five years.\\"","options":["audit","bribe","violate","manipulate"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To audit is to inspect financial records, which is exactly what the accountant was asked to do."},{"id":"b4u10-e7","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Drivers who ___ the speed limit in a school zone face a heavy fine.\\"","options":["violate","legislate","dispose","audit"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To violate a law or rule is to break it, which is what exceeding the speed limit does."},{"id":"b4u10-e8","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Please ___ of these old batteries at the recycling centre, not in the bin.\\"","options":["dispose","violate","automate","bribe"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To dispose of something is to get rid of it, which is what the sentence asks the reader to do with the batteries."},{"id":"b4u10-e9","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The company\'s annual ___ rose by eight per cent, mostly from overseas sales.\\"","options":["revenue","subsidy","incentive","rubbish"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Revenue is the income made by a company, which is what rose by eight per cent."},{"id":"b4u10-e10","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The council offered a tax ___ to firms that moved into the old industrial district.\\"","options":["incentive","transaction","audit","infrastructure"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"An incentive is something that makes a person or organisation want to do something, which is the purpose of the tax offer."}]},"11":{"id":"b4-u11","book":4,"unit":11,"title":"The Spilled Beaker","level":"B2","topic":"Science and accidental discovery","generatedAt":"2026-09-23","wordCount":443,"paragraphs":["The laboratory where the accident happened was small, badly lit, and shared by three departments. In the winter of 1893 a young researcher named Marta Reiner was asked to [[assess]] a batch of coal tar that another team had sent up from the basement. The task was dull, and she carried it out with the [[haste]] of someone who wanted to be somewhere else. What followed would [[astonish]] her colleagues, and in [[subsequent]] years it would change the way hospitals treated infection.","Reiner had been trying to [[extract]] a colourless compound from the tar. Late one evening she left a beaker uncovered on the bench and went home. When she returned the next morning, the liquid had turned a deep blue — not the dull grey she expected, but something almost [[fabulous]]. Colleagues who saw it assumed she had used a [[synthetic]] dye bought from a supplier. She had not. The colour had appeared by itself.","For months she worked with [[precise]] measurements, recording every step. The [[essence]] of the problem was simple: she could not tell whether the blue came from a single [[molecule]] or from several reacting together. Her first idea was that the air itself had caused the change; a second experiment suggested otherwise. The [[latter]] explanation — that a trace of iron in the glass was responsible — turned out to be correct.","What made the discovery more than a curiosity was what happened next. A doctor at the nearby hospital, hearing about the blue liquid, wondered whether a related compound might [[inhibit]] the growth of bacteria. He was right. The substance did not kill the bacteria outright, but it slowed them enough for the body\'s own defences to finish the job. It became a [[remedy]] for infections that had previously been [[terminal]].","Reiner never sought [[publicity]], and she disliked the [[ongoing]] attention that followed. She was, by all accounts, a careful and rather unexciting person who distrusted sudden ideas; an [[impulse]] to leave a beaker uncovered was, she insisted, the only reckless thing she ever did. Yet her accidental discovery mattered because of its [[proximity]] to a real medical need — the hospital stood a hundred metres away, and its surgeons were desperate.","Looking back, historians of science like to point out that the work had [[commence|commenced]] as routine quality control, and that the [[significance]] of what Reiner found was not obvious for years. She had been hired to check other people\'s materials, not to make discoveries. The lesson her story teaches is not that accidents are useful, but that a trained eye can recognise one when it happens — and that recognition takes patience rather than luck."],"exercises":[{"id":"b4u11-e1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What is the passage mainly about?","options":["The career of a researcher who spent years studying coal tar","An accidental discovery that led to a treatment for infection","The competition between three university departments","A dispute between a laboratory and a nearby hospital"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"The passage follows an unplanned laboratory result that was later turned into a medical remedy for infection."},{"id":"b4u11-e2","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What was Reiner originally asked to do?","options":["Develop a synthetic dye for a supplier","Test the safety of a new medicine","Assess a batch of coal tar sent from the basement","Teach students how to record measurements"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"Paragraph 1 says she was asked to assess a batch of coal tar that another team had sent up from the basement."},{"id":"b4u11-e3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What did Reiner finally conclude had caused the blue colour?","options":["A trace of iron in the glass of the beaker","The action of the air overnight","A synthetic dye left on the bench by a colleague","A reaction between several different molecules"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Paragraph 3 says her second explanation — a trace of iron in the glass — turned out to be correct."},{"id":"b4u11-e4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What does the passage suggest about Reiner\'s character?","options":["She was ambitious and eager for recognition","She was careless and often repeated her mistakes","She was cautious and uncomfortable with attention","She was impatient with slow, detailed work"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"Paragraph 5 describes her as careful, unexciting, distrustful of sudden ideas, and unwilling to seek publicity."},{"id":"b4u11-e5","type":"multiple-choice","question":"In paragraph 3, the word *latter* most nearly means…","options":["the more complicated of two explanations","the second of the two explanations mentioned","the explanation that was eventually abandoned","the explanation supported by the hospital"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"*Latter* refers to the last of two things just mentioned — here, the idea that iron in the glass was responsible."},{"id":"b4u11-e6","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"A doctor must ___ a patient\'s condition before deciding on any treatment.\\"","options":["assess","inhibit","extract","astonish"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To assess something is to judge its quality or nature, which is what a doctor does with a patient\'s condition."},{"id":"b4u11-e7","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"In their ___ to catch the train, they left the tickets on the kitchen table.\\"","options":["essence","haste","proximity","publicity"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Haste is speed in movement or action, and rushing is exactly why the tickets were forgotten."},{"id":"b4u11-e8","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The discovery received a great deal of ___ in the national newspapers.\\"","options":["molecule","impulse","publicity","remedy"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"Publicity is the public attention given to something by the media, which is what newspapers provide."},{"id":"b4u11-e9","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"There is no simple ___ for a broken friendship.\\"","options":["remedy","essence","haste","molecule"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"A remedy is a cure for a problem, so it fits a problem that has no easy solution."},{"id":"b4u11-e10","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Scientists believe the reaction is ___ and will continue for several years.\\"","options":["precise","synthetic","terminal","ongoing"],"answerIndex":3,"explanation":"If something is ongoing, it is still happening; the phrase \\"will continue for several years\\" signals this meaning."}]},"12":{"id":"b4-u12","book":4,"unit":12,"title":"The Notebook Nobody Read","level":"B2","topic":"Music and a composer\'s reputation","generatedAt":"2026-09-23","wordCount":438,"paragraphs":["For most of her life, the composer Ilse Marchetti was known less for her music than for her silence. The [[biography]] published last year — the first serious attempt to describe her career — opens with a scene that has become almost [[notorious]] among musicians: a concert hall in 1954, a full orchestra, and a woman in the front row who refused to [[clap]].","Marchetti had written [[multiple]] works by then, and at least one of them, the Second Quartet, is now widely regarded as a [[masterpiece]]. At the time, however, audiences found her music difficult. It was too [[classical]] in structure to please the modernists, yet too strange in harmony to please the traditionalists. [[overall|Overall]], the critics were puzzled rather than hostile. One reviewer went so far as to call the Second Quartet unplayable, a judgement that now looks merely careless.","What [[compel|compelled]] her to keep writing was not ambition but habit. She worked every morning, and the pieces arrived slowly. Her [[creation|creations]] rarely came from a single burst of feeling; she distrusted [[spontaneous]] ideas and preferred to test a phrase for weeks before trusting it. When something finally satisfied her, she would say that it had earned its place.","The [[dominance]] of a few male conductors in that period made her career unusually hard. She had a [[partiality]] for small ensembles, which gave her fewer chances to be heard, and she was [[acute|acutely]] aware that a single bad review could end a season. Yet her chief [[virtue]] as a composer — patience — was exactly what the fashion of the day lacked. Audiences wanted novelty; she offered something slower.","Her [[legacy]] is now secure, though it took decades. The turning point was [[inevitable]] in hindsight: younger musicians began to play her quartets, and the recordings sold steadily. The slow movement, which is [[gorgeous]] and entirely unhurried, became the part concert-goers remembered. A festival devoted to her work [[boost|boosted]] her reputation further, and by the 1990s she was being performed alongside composers she had once been compared to unfavourably.","Marchetti herself died long before any of this happened. In an interview late in life she was asked whether the years of neglect had made her bitter. She said no, and added that [[aggression]] had never interested her; what interested her was the next bar of music. She [[narrate|narrated]] her own life as though it belonged to somebody else, and perhaps that is why the biography, for all its detail, still feels incomplete. The notebook she kept for forty years, which nobody has yet published in full, may be the only place where the answer is written down."],"exercises":[{"id":"b4u12-e1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What is the passage mainly about?","options":["A composer whose reputation grew long after her death","A concert that ended in a public argument","The training of orchestral conductors in the 1950s","The rivalry between two groups of modern composers"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"The passage traces Marchetti\'s difficult career and the later recognition of her work, including a biography published last year."},{"id":"b4u12-e2","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What happened at the concert in 1954?","options":["The orchestra refused to play Marchetti\'s music","Marchetti walked out before the performance ended","Marchetti sat in the front row and did not applaud","The critics left the hall in the middle of the piece"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"Paragraph 1 describes a woman in the front row who refused to clap, which is the scene the biography opens with."},{"id":"b4u12-e3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"Why did audiences of the time find Marchetti\'s music difficult?","options":["It was too short and too repetitive","It was too classical in structure yet strange in harmony","It was written for instruments that no one could play","It changed style completely in every movement"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 2 says it was too classical in structure for the modernists and too strange in harmony for the traditionalists."},{"id":"b4u12-e4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What does the passage suggest about Marchetti\'s working method?","options":["She wrote quickly and rarely revised anything","She relied on sudden inspiration and distrusted planning","She worked steadily and tested ideas slowly before keeping them","She preferred to compose at night with other musicians"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"Paragraph 3 says she worked every morning, distrusted spontaneous ideas, and tested a phrase for weeks before trusting it."},{"id":"b4u12-e5","type":"multiple-choice","question":"As used in paragraph 4, *partiality* most nearly means…","options":["an unfair judgement of other composers","a strong preference for one thing over another","an inability to work with large groups","a refusal to accept criticism"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"*Partiality* means a tendency to prefer one thing to another — here, a preference for small ensembles."},{"id":"b4u12-e6","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"A single good review can ___ an unknown writer\'s career.\\"","options":["clap","narrate","boost","compel"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"To boost something is to increase or improve it, which is what a favourable review does to a career."},{"id":"b4u12-e7","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The audience began to ___ as soon as the final note faded.\\"","options":["boost","clap","compel","narrate"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"To clap is to hit one\'s hands together to show pleasure, which is what an audience does at the end of a performance."},{"id":"b4u12-e8","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"She has a strong ___ for dark chocolate over milk chocolate.\\"","options":["partiality","legacy","virtue","dominance"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"A partiality is a tendency to prefer one thing to another, which matches the comparison between two kinds of chocolate."},{"id":"b4u12-e9","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"It was ___ that the two old rivals would eventually meet again.\\"","options":["spontaneous","notorious","gorgeous","inevitable"],"answerIndex":3,"explanation":"If something is inevitable, it is certain to happen and cannot be avoided, which fits a meeting that was bound to occur."},{"id":"b4u12-e10","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The painting is considered a ___ of the early twentieth century.\\"","options":["masterpiece","biography","legacy","virtue"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"A masterpiece is a very good work of art, so it describes a painting of outstanding quality."}]},"13":{"id":"b4-u13","book":4,"unit":13,"title":"Twenty-Four Hours Without Sight","level":"B2","topic":"Perception and a student experiment","generatedAt":"2026-09-23","wordCount":462,"paragraphs":["Our tutor gave us the [[assignment]] on a Friday afternoon, and most of the class groaned. We were to [[undertake]] a single day with our eyes covered, from waking until bedtime, and then write up what we had learned. The [[premise]] was simple: if we could not see, we would begin to notice how much of ordinary life depends on vision.","I began at seven on a Tuesday. The first hour was the hardest. I had underestimated how many small decisions require sight — where the cup is, whether the milk is still fresh, which way a door opens. I had to [[specify]] every movement to myself before making it. My [[awareness]] of the room changed completely: the table, the [[rack]] of coats by the door, the window — all of them became obstacles rather than furniture.","By midday I had begun to [[coordinate]] my hands and ears instead of my eyes. I could hear which [[aspect]] of a room I was in, because a tiled kitchen sounds quite different from a carpeted hall. My [[optic]] memory was useless, but my sense of [[spatial]] distance improved quickly. I [[tie|tied]] a length of string between two chairs to mark a safe path and followed it back and forth like a child.","The most surprising discovery was how quickly I stopped thinking about it. Blindness, for a day, was not the constant struggle I had expected. It was tiring and occasionally frightening, but it was also quiet. I noticed [[bud|buds]] on the apple tree by the gate — I could feel them, hard and small, where a week earlier there had been nothing. That felt like a gain rather than a loss, and I began to suspect that patience was the true [[asset]] in the exercise, not eyesight.","I had expected to come away with a grand conclusion. Instead I came away with a question I could not answer and no way to [[disprove]] it. My [[hypothesis]] — that a day without sight would make me permanently more careful — turned out to be false. Within a week I was walking into doors again.","One thing did change. The following term I volunteered for a [[humanitarian]] project that arranged reading for people with limited vision, and I found I was better at it than I had any right to be. I do not want [[credit]] for that; the small [[renaissance]] of my interest in the subject came from a single day, and a single day is not much to be proud of. The write-up I handed in was longer than it needed to be and probably not very [[informative]]. But I still keep the string in a drawer by my desk, together with the lump of [[wax]] I used to hold it in place."],"exercises":[{"id":"b4u13-e1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What is the passage mainly about?","options":["A scientific study of how blind people navigate rooms","What a student learned from spending a day without sight","The history of reading services for people with limited vision","Why the human sense of hearing is stronger than eyesight"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"The narrator describes an assignment to spend one day blindfolded and reflects on what the experience taught him."},{"id":"b4u13-e2","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What exactly did the tutor ask the class to do?","options":["Interview someone with limited vision","Read a book about blindness in a single day","Cover their eyes for one day and then write about it","Avoid using their hands for twenty-four hours"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"Paragraph 1 states they were to undertake a single day with their eyes covered, from waking until bedtime, and then write up what they had learned."},{"id":"b4u13-e3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"Why did the narrator tie a string between two chairs?","options":["To mark a safe path he could follow","To test how strong his sense of hearing was","To keep the coats from falling off the rack","To measure the width of the kitchen"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Paragraph 3 says he tied a length of string between two chairs to mark a safe path and followed it back and forth."},{"id":"b4u13-e4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What surprised the narrator most about the day?","options":["How angry he became when he could not find things","How quickly he stopped thinking about being unable to see","How much better his memory became without sight","How much help he needed from other people"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 4 opens with the statement that the most surprising discovery was how quickly he stopped thinking about it."},{"id":"b4u13-e5","type":"multiple-choice","question":"As used in paragraph 4, *asset* most nearly means…","options":["a possession of great financial value","something that makes a task harder","a quality that is useful or valuable","a skill that must be taught formally"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"An asset here is a useful quality — patience — rather than property or money."},{"id":"b4u13-e6","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Please ___ which colour you would prefer, and we will order it for you.\\"","options":["specify","coordinate","disprove","undertake"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To specify is to state something clearly and exactly, which is what naming a colour does."},{"id":"b4u13-e7","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"She refused to take the ___ for the discovery, saying the whole team had contributed.\\"","options":["awareness","credit","asset","premise"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Credit is something good in your favour, so taking the credit means accepting praise for an achievement."},{"id":"b4u13-e8","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The lecture was ___ and well organised, and I learned a great deal from it.\\"","options":["optic","spatial","informative","humanitarian"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"If something is informative, it provides a lot of information, which matches learning a great deal."},{"id":"b4u13-e9","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The company agreed to ___ the repairs at its own expense.\\"","options":["undertake","coordinate","specify","disprove"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To undertake a task is to take on responsibility for doing it, which fits agreeing to carry out repairs."},{"id":"b4u13-e10","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Travelling abroad broadens your ___ of how other people live.\\"","options":["bud","awareness","rack","wax"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Awareness is knowledge or perception of a situation or fact, and travel increases such knowledge."}]},"14":{"id":"b4-u14","book":4,"unit":14,"title":"The Sketch on the Wall","level":"B2","topic":"Archaeology and a disputed discovery","generatedAt":"2026-09-23","wordCount":513,"paragraphs":["In 1968 a small [[team]] of researchers began digging in the [[courtyard]] of an old country [[estate]] in the south of France. They were not looking for treasure. Their interest was [[archeological]]: the estate had been built on the site of an earlier settlement, and the [[archeology]] of the valley was still poorly understood. The [[budget]] was tiny, and the first season lasted only six weeks.","What they found was, at first, disappointing. The site was [[chaotic]] — layers of rubble, broken tiles, and the remains of at least four buildings constructed on top of one another. Nothing [[correspond|corresponded]] neatly to the plans in the local archive. Beneath the later layers, however, lay a much older one, and it was there, if anywhere, that the [[prehistoric]] story of the valley could be read. The director, a patient woman in her sixties, liked to [[cite]] the same sentence at every meeting: we are not looking for objects, we are looking for behaviour. One member of the team was a genuine [[addict]] of the archive and read excavation diaries for pleasure.","The discovery came in the third season, and it came from a student who had been sent to clean a wall. Behind the plaster she found a drawing: a figure with arms spread wide, and above it a shape like an open umbrella. The director was cautious. A single sketch proves nothing, she said, and she was right to be cautious. But the shape was hard to explain in any other way. It looked like a [[parachute]]. That evening the whole team sat up late to [[brainstorm]] explanations, filling a blackboard with possibilities and crossing most of them out again.","The claim remained controversial for years. [[proponent|Proponents]] of the idea argued that the drawing showed a person descending deliberately rather than falling. Critics replied that the [[outlook]] of the period — its assumptions about the body, about air, about weight — made such a device unlikely. Someone suggested the drawing was a [[fraud]], added later by a worker hoping to make the site famous. The debate ran for a decade and produced more articles than the site had produced objects.","What settled it was chemistry rather than argument. A sample of the pigment was analysed, and the result was compared with the [[hydrogen]] content of the plaster around it. The two sets of numbers matched only if the drawing was original. The evidence did not prove that anyone had actually jumped; it proved only that the sketch was old. That was enough to change the argument.","The site is now a small museum. Its [[integrity]] as a record matters more to the staff than its fame, and they have chosen to [[restrict]] visitor numbers rather than build a larger car park. In winter, when the rooms are empty, you can stand in the [[courtyard]] and look at the wall through glass. I have been there twice. Both times I found myself trying to [[refine]] my own idea of what the drawing means, and both times I failed — which is, I suspect, the point."],"exercises":[{"id":"b4u14-e1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What is the passage mainly about?","options":["The daily routine of a modern archaeological museum","A disputed find at a dig and how the dispute was resolved","The history of parachute design in the seventeenth century","Why archaeological sites in France are poorly funded"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"The passage describes a drawing found at a dig, the arguments it provoked, and the chemical evidence that ended the argument."},{"id":"b4u14-e2","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What did the student find behind the plaster?","options":["A collection of broken tiles and rubble","A written diary belonging to an earlier excavator","A drawing of a figure with something like an umbrella above it","A set of tools buried in the floor of the room"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"Paragraph 3 describes a figure with arms spread wide and, above it, a shape like an open umbrella — which looked like a parachute."},{"id":"b4u14-e3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What finally settled the argument about the drawing?","options":["A comparison of the pigment with the plaster around it","The discovery of a second, identical drawing","A written record found in the local archive","The confession of the worker who had added it"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Paragraph 5 says the pigment was analysed and compared with the hydrogen content of the surrounding plaster, and the numbers matched only if the drawing was original."},{"id":"b4u14-e4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What does the director\'s reaction to the drawing suggest about her?","options":["She was eager to announce a dramatic discovery","She preferred evidence to exciting claims","She did not believe the student had found anything","She wanted the site to be closed to visitors"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"She insisted that a single sketch proves nothing, which shows a preference for evidence over dramatic claims."},{"id":"b4u14-e5","type":"multiple-choice","question":"As used in paragraph 4, *outlook* most nearly means…","options":["a view from a particular place","a prediction about the future","a way of thinking about the world","an official written record"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"Here *outlook* refers to the assumptions and way of thinking of the period, not to a physical view."},{"id":"b4u14-e6","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The two accounts of the accident do not ___ with each other at all.\\"","options":["correspond","brainstorm","refine","cite"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To correspond is to match or be similar to something, so accounts that do not correspond disagree with one another."},{"id":"b4u14-e7","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"He was accused of ___ after claiming expenses he had never paid.\\"","options":["integrity","fraud","outlook","budget"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Fraud is the crime of gaining money by lying or tricking people, which describes claiming false expenses."},{"id":"b4u14-e8","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The council voted to ___ the number of cars allowed into the old town.\\"","options":["refine","restrict","cite","correspond"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"To restrict something is to limit it, which is exactly what the council did with the number of cars."},{"id":"b4u14-e9","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Please ___ your sources at the end of the essay.\\"","options":["brainstorm","restrict","cite","refine"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"To cite something is to mention it as an example or as proof, which is what a list of sources does."},{"id":"b4u14-e10","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The team spent the afternoon trying to ___ new ways of solving the problem.\\"","options":["brainstorm","correspond","cite","restrict"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To brainstorm is to produce a lot of ideas about a topic, which fits generating possible solutions."}]},"15":{"id":"b4-u15","book":4,"unit":15,"title":"Smaller Than a Grain of Rice","level":"B2","topic":"Technology and medicine","generatedAt":"2026-09-23","wordCount":454,"paragraphs":["A modern [[microchip]] is one of the smallest objects most people will ever own and one of the most complicated. A single chip the size of a fingernail can contain several billion switches. Laid out flat, its [[circuit|circuits]] would stretch for several kilometres; [[compress|compressed]] onto silicon, they fit into a space smaller than a grain of rice.","The basic idea is not new. Engineers noticed long ago that when a wire is wound into a [[spiral]] and current is passed through it, the wire behaves in useful and slightly unexpected ways. What matters inside a chip is simpler still: electricity moves through a [[circuit]] only when it is given a complete path, and a switch can open or close that path. A [[magnet]] does something similar with force rather than current, which is why the earliest computers stored data on magnets and were later replaced by chips that do the same job invisibly.","The numbers involved are hard to imagine. The [[ratio]] of switches to people on the planet is now greater than a thousand to one. The [[aggregate]] computing power of all the chips produced in a single year is a figure that nobody can hold in their head. And at the scale where chips are designed, ordinary physics gives way to [[quantum]] effects, so the behaviour of individual particles has to be accounted for rather than ignored.","Chips matter most where they meet the body. A chip can [[pinch]] off a tiny sample of blood and test it within minutes. Sensors that follow a person\'s [[metabolism]] can warn of illness long before symptoms appear, and an [[antibiotic]] can then be chosen with far more precision than a doctor could manage alone. Some devices are designed to [[complement]] the [[immune]] system rather than replace it, prompting the body to respond faster to a [[viral]] infection.","All of this depends on data. A hospital [[database]] may hold millions of records, and the [[input]] to a chip is often nothing more than a single reading from a sensor. Yet the [[equivalent]] of a whole laboratory can now sit on a wrist. The relationship between the device and the person wearing it is surprisingly [[intimate]]: it learns a routine, notices when the routine changes, and reports the difference.","The technology is still in an early [[phase]]. Some problems remain stubborn — chips are difficult to recycle, and making them uses enormous quantities of water. It is also true that the most [[prevalent]] use of these devices so far has been rather trivial: counting steps, measuring sleep, reminding us to stand up. Whether they will do more than that is not really a question of engineering. It is a question of what we ask them to do."],"exercises":[{"id":"b4u15-e1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What is the passage mainly about?","options":["The history of computing from magnets to modern chips","What microchips are, how they work, and how they are used in medicine","Why hospitals should stop storing patient records electronically","The environmental cost of manufacturing silicon"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"The passage explains the scale and basic working of microchips and then focuses on their medical applications and limits."},{"id":"b4u15-e2","type":"multiple-choice","question":"According to the passage, how far would the circuits of a fingernail-sized chip stretch if laid out flat?","options":["A few centimetres","Several metres","Several kilometres","Several thousand kilometres"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"Paragraph 1 says that laid out flat the circuits would stretch for several kilometres."},{"id":"b4u15-e3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What can sensors that follow a person\'s metabolism do?","options":["Replace the need for any medicine","Warn of illness before symptoms appear","Measure how much water a person drinks","Repair damaged cells inside the body"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 4 states that such sensors can warn of illness long before symptoms appear."},{"id":"b4u15-e4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What does the writer suggest in the final paragraph?","options":["The technology has already reached its full potential","The value of the technology depends on how it is used","Chips will never be useful outside laboratories","Recycling problems will soon be solved by engineers"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"The writer notes that most current uses are trivial and concludes that the future depends on what we ask the devices to do."},{"id":"b4u15-e5","type":"multiple-choice","question":"As used in paragraph 5, *intimate* most nearly means…","options":["private and personal in a romantic sense","closely connected and well acquainted","expensive and difficult to replace","unpredictable and hard to control"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Here *intimate* describes a close connection between device and wearer — the device learns the wearer\'s routine."},{"id":"b4u15-e6","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The doctor prescribed an ___ to clear the infection.\\"","options":["antibiotic","equivalent","input","magnet"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"An antibiotic is a medical drug used to kill bacteria and treat infections."},{"id":"b4u15-e7","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Once you have had the illness, you are usually ___ to it for life.\\"","options":["prevalent","viral","immune","quantum"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"If someone is immune to a disease, they cannot be affected by it, which matches protection after an illness."},{"id":"b4u15-e8","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Please enter the customer\'s name and address in the ___ before continuing.\\"","options":["phase","spiral","circuit","database"],"answerIndex":3,"explanation":"A database is a collection of data stored in a computer, which is where records are entered and kept."},{"id":"b4u15-e9","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"This sauce is designed to ___ the flavour of the fish rather than hide it.\\"","options":["compress","complement","pinch","extract"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"To complement something is to make it better, which is what a well-chosen sauce does to a dish."},{"id":"b4u15-e10","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The company\'s ___ profits for the year were higher than anyone had predicted.\\"","options":["aggregate","intimate","immune","viral"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Aggregate means made up of smaller amounts added together, so aggregate profits are the total for the year."}]},"16":{"id":"b4-u16","book":4,"unit":16,"title":"The Label That Told the Truth","level":"B2","topic":"Food labelling and consumer trust","generatedAt":"2026-09-23","wordCount":460,"paragraphs":["The bottle sat on the shelf for two years before anyone read it properly. It was a fruit drink, sold in supermarkets, and its label was designed to look honest: a photograph of an orchard, a list of ingredients in small print, and the word [[nectar]] printed across the front in large letters. The [[notion]] behind the design was simple — that a product which looks [[straightforward]] must be telling the truth. It was [[manufacture|manufactured]] in a plant outside the city, where forty thousand bottles an hour came off the line.","A consumer group decided to test that assumption. They commissioned a laboratory to analyse twelve popular drinks, including this one. The results were [[explicit]] and, for the company, embarrassing. The drink contained almost no fruit. Its sweetness came from a sweetener, its sharpness from citric [[acid]], and its colour from a compound that also appeared in industrial dyes. The company\'s aim, its lawyers later admitted, had been to [[minimize]] the amount of fruit it needed while keeping the taste. None of this was illegal. Everything on the label was accurate in the narrow sense that it was not false. But the impression it created was [[deceptive]].","The company\'s [[administration]] responded carefully. In a statement, its [[administrative]] team argued that the drink was safe and the criticism unfair. What the critics wanted, they said, was a new [[framework]] for food labelling — a set of rules that would make the difference between contains fruit and tastes of fruit impossible to confuse. Under the current [[regime]], a producer may list ingredients in any order as long as they are all present, and a shopper has no way of knowing which one dominates.","The wider issue is not one product but a system. Modern food production relies on [[biotechnology]], and the [[mechanism|mechanisms]] it uses are genuinely useful: they can [[eliminate]] harmful bacteria, reduce [[cholesterol]] in cooking oil, and help people with [[diabetes]] manage their sugar intake. A [[coalition]] of scientists and consumer groups has argued for years that the problem is not the science but the gap between what the science does and what the label says it does.","Two things could narrow that gap. The first is [[ethics]]: a company that is open about its methods tends to be trusted, even when those methods are unglamorous. The second is simpler. Regulators could require a short, clear summary on the front of every packet, so that a shopper does not need a chemistry qualification to understand what is inside. Meanwhile the criticism has achieved something the label never could: it has slowed, though certainly not stopped, the [[erosion]] of trust in everyday food. The drink is still on sale. Its label has changed, slightly. The orchard photograph, I notice, is now smaller."],"exercises":[{"id":"b4u16-e1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What is the passage mainly about?","options":["How a laboratory tests soft drinks for safety","A misleading label and what could be done about labelling","The health benefits of modern food biotechnology","Why small producers cannot afford to redesign labels"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"The passage describes a drink whose label created a false impression and discusses possible reforms to food labelling."},{"id":"b4u16-e2","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What did the laboratory analysis of the drink show?","options":["It contained dangerous levels of bacteria","It was made entirely from imported fruit","It contained almost no fruit and used a sweetener, citric acid and dye","It contained more sugar than the label claimed"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"Paragraph 2 lists a sweetener, citric acid and a dye, and states that the drink contained almost no fruit."},{"id":"b4u16-e3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"According to the company\'s response, what did the critics really want?","options":["The drink to be removed from sale","A new framework of rules for food labelling","An apology printed on the front of the bottle","A ban on the use of the word natural"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 3 says the company argued that critics wanted a new framework for food labelling."},{"id":"b4u16-e4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What is the writer\'s attitude towards modern food biotechnology?","options":["Hostile, because it is used to deceive shoppers","Cautious, because its effects are not yet understood","Accepting of the science but critical of how it is described","Indifferent, because labelling is a matter for regulators"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"Paragraph 4 calls the mechanisms genuinely useful and states that the problem is the gap between what the science does and what the label says."},{"id":"b4u16-e5","type":"multiple-choice","question":"As used in paragraph 2, *deceptive* most nearly means…","options":["likely to encourage a belief that is false","illegal under current food regulations","deliberately harmful to the consumer","difficult for a shopper to read"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"If something is deceptive it encourages one to believe something that is false — here, that the drink contains fruit."},{"id":"b4u16-e6","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The new filters are designed to ___ bacteria from the water supply.\\"","options":["minimize","eliminate","manufacture","assess"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"To eliminate something unwanted means to remove it completely, which is what a filter does to bacteria."},{"id":"b4u16-e7","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The winding ___ of the old clock had not been oiled for years.\\"","options":["mechanism","framework","regime","notion"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"A mechanism is a part of a machine that performs a particular function, such as the winding mechanism of a clock."},{"id":"b4u16-e8","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Doctors advise patients with ___ to check their blood sugar regularly.\\"","options":["cholesterol","erosion","diabetes","biotechnology"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"Diabetes is a condition in which the body cannot control the level of sugar in the blood, so blood sugar must be monitored."},{"id":"b4u16-e9","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Several local groups formed a ___ to campaign for cleaner air.\\"","options":["coalition","nectar","acid","regime"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"A coalition is a group of people or organisations working for a common purpose, which fits a joint campaign."},{"id":"b4u16-e10","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The committee questioned the ___ of testing the product on young children.\\"","options":["ethics","nectar","cholesterol","acid"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Ethics are moral beliefs about right and wrong, which is what the committee is questioning here."}]},"17":{"id":"b4-u17","book":4,"unit":17,"title":"The Ceiling That Leaked","level":"B2","topic":"Renting a flat and reading the small print","generatedAt":"2026-09-23","wordCount":434,"paragraphs":["When I first saw the flat, I did not look at the ceiling. I looked at the windows, the light, and the space, and I signed the [[lease]] the same afternoon. The [[landlord]] was pleasant and slightly distracted. He mentioned that the building was old, that the [[plumbing]] had been replaced in the 1980s, and that a [[quote]] for repainting the hall had just come in. I did not ask what any of this meant.","It rained in November, and the ceiling above the kitchen began to weep. Water appeared in a line, then in a patch, then in a slow brown map that grew every day. I wrote to the landlord. He replied that he would send somebody in the [[forthcoming]] week. Nobody came. A second letter produced a promise of a [[mend|mended]] roof by Christmas, which also came to nothing.","Meanwhile I learned the rules of the building. Rent was payable on the first of the month, and insurance was [[mandatory]] for every [[tenant]]. The landlord [[employ|employed]] a caretaker for the common areas, and the caretaker was, in turn, responsible for [[hygiene]] in the stairwell. The arrangement was, on paper, thoroughly [[hygienic]] and well organised. In practice, the stairwell was cleaned twice a year.","By January I had become the sort of tenant who reads documents. I discovered that the building was owned by a small company with three [[personnel]] and a large [[mortgage]], and that the company was close to going [[bankrupt]]. I also discovered something far more useful: the lease contained a clause [[whereby]] the landlord was required to carry out repairs within twenty-one days of a written request.","I sent a third letter, this time quoting the clause. Within a week a roofer arrived, and within two the ceiling was dry. The flat was never properly [[furnish|furnished]] — I had bought two chairs and a bed, and that was all — but it was warm, and the roof held. I stayed another four years.","The lesson was not that landlords are dishonest. Mine was simply overwhelmed, and he had stopped believing that letters mattered. What changed the situation was not anger but precision: a date, a clause, and a demand that the other side [[conform]] to the agreement they had already signed. I never asked him to [[expel]] the caretaker, though I was tempted. I did ask once for an [[extension]] of the lease while I looked for somewhere else, and he agreed without hesitating. The [[utility|utilities]] were included in the rent, which was probably why the flat was so cold: there was no reason to heat it properly."],"exercises":[{"id":"b4u17-e1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What is the passage mainly about?","options":["How to find a cheap flat in an old building","How a tenant solved a problem by reading his lease carefully","Why landlords in old buildings are usually dishonest","The legal duties of a building caretaker"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"The narrator\'s problem is only solved when he finds a clause in the lease and quotes it in a letter."},{"id":"b4u17-e2","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What happened in November?","options":["The landlord raised the rent","The caretaker stopped cleaning the stairwell","The kitchen ceiling began to leak","The building was sold to a new company"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"Paragraph 2 says that in November the ceiling above the kitchen began to weep, and a brown patch grew every day."},{"id":"b4u17-e3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What did the narrator find in the lease?","options":["A clause requiring repairs within twenty-one days of a written request","A clause allowing the landlord to keep the deposit","A clause forbidding him from keeping pets","A clause making insurance the tenant\'s responsibility"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Paragraph 4 describes a clause whereby the landlord had to carry out repairs within twenty-one days of a written request."},{"id":"b4u17-e4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What does the writer suggest about his landlord?","options":["He was deliberately dishonest and ignored every letter","He was overwhelmed rather than dishonest","He had never intended to rent the flat out","He was unaware that the building had a mortgage"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 6 states that the lesson was not that landlords are dishonest, and that his landlord was simply overwhelmed."},{"id":"b4u17-e5","type":"multiple-choice","question":"As used in paragraph 4, *whereby* most nearly means…","options":["in spite of which","by which","at which time","for which reason"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"*Whereby* means by which or through which, introducing the means by which something is done."},{"id":"b4u17-e6","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The company went ___ after losing its two largest customers.\\"","options":["bankrupt","hygienic","mandatory","forthcoming"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"If someone or something is bankrupt, they are unable to pay their debts, which follows losing major customers."},{"id":"b4u17-e7","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Could you ___ this tear in my jacket before I wear it again?\\"","options":["expel","employ","mend","lease"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"To mend something is to fix it when it is broken or damaged, which is what a tear in a jacket needs."},{"id":"b4u17-e8","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Wearing a helmet is ___ on all building sites.\\"","options":["forthcoming","mandatory","bankrupt","hygienic"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"If something is mandatory, it is required by law or by rule, which is the case for helmets on building sites."},{"id":"b4u17-e9","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The manager decided to ___ two extra staff for the summer season.\\"","options":["employ","expel","conform","mend"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To employ someone is to give work to them, which is what hiring extra staff means."},{"id":"b4u17-e10","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Kitchen ___ is essential when you are preparing food for other people.\\"","options":["personnel","plumbing","hygiene","utility"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"Hygiene refers to the conditions and methods needed for health and cleanliness, which is critical in a kitchen."}]},"18":{"id":"b4-u18","book":4,"unit":18,"title":"The Operation Nobody Watched","level":"C1","topic":"Medical history","generatedAt":"2026-09-23","wordCount":423,"paragraphs":["In the spring of 1951, a [[surgeon]] at a small city hospital attempted an operation that most of his colleagues considered a [[fantasy]]. He intended to remove a diseased kidney from a young man and replace it with a healthy one taken from his twin brother. There was no press, no audience, and no announcement of any kind; the hospital had asked him to keep the attempt quiet.","The idea itself was not new. Doctors had understood the [[biological]] basis of rejection for decades, and the [[cellular]] details were becoming clearer year by year. What nobody had solved was the [[sequence]] of problems that followed a successful operation: the body\'s [[internal]] defences would begin to attack the new organ within days.","The surgeon was a [[pioneer]] in a literal sense — the first in his country to attempt it — but he was not reckless. He spent two years preparing. He learned to [[adapt]] existing instruments so that they could work on smaller vessels. He also visited four other hospitals to watch how their surgeons handled the same vessels, and filled two notebooks with their answers. He found a way to [[transfer]] blood without cooling the patient too far, and he kept the period without circulation to a [[minimal]] length.","The operation lasted nine hours. The kidney began to produce urine before the end of it, which the theatre staff took as a good sign. The patient recovered slowly. His brother, whose recovery was [[similar]] in almost every respect, was walking within a week. Both were given a course of [[therapy]] that would now be considered crude: a drug intended to reduce the immune response, given in doses that were [[rigid]] and rarely adjusted.","The kidney failed after eight months, and a second [[transplant]] was attempted, this time from an unrelated donor. It failed within days. The surgeon published his results honestly, including the failures, and the paper was read everywhere. What it established was not that the operation worked but that the [[transition]] from theory to practice was possible at all.","Modern medicine has travelled a long way from that theatre. Today a surgeon can [[substitute]] a damaged heart valve with one grown from the patient\'s own cells, and doctors can [[prescribe]] treatments matched to an individual\'s [[heredity]]. Two patients in the same ward may receive entirely different drugs for the same diagnosis, chosen according to their [[respective]] genetic profiles. The field is [[dynamic]] in a way it was not in 1951: techniques that were experimental five years ago are now routine."],"exercises":[{"id":"b4u18-e1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What is the passage mainly about?","options":["How twin brothers recover differently from the same operation","An early transplant operation and why it mattered","The training of surgeons in the 1950s","Why organ rejection can never be prevented"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"The passage describes a pioneering but only partly successful transplant and explains its significance for later medicine."},{"id":"b4u18-e2","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What did the hospital ask the surgeon to do?","options":["Publish his results immediately","Perform the operation on a different patient","Keep the attempt quiet","Wait until a second surgeon was available"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"Paragraph 1 says the hospital had asked him to keep the attempt quiet, so there was no press or audience."},{"id":"b4u18-e3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"How long did the transplanted kidney continue to work?","options":["A few days","Eight months","Nine hours","More than five years"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 5 states that the kidney failed after eight months."},{"id":"b4u18-e4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What does the writer suggest about the surgeon\'s published paper?","options":["It exaggerated the success of the operation","Its honesty about failure was what made it valuable","It was ignored because the operation had failed","It was written mainly to attract publicity"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"The paper included the failures, and the writer says it established that the transition from theory to practice was possible at all."},{"id":"b4u18-e5","type":"multiple-choice","question":"As used in paragraph 4, *rigid* most nearly means…","options":["carefully calculated for each patient","not easily changed or adjusted","unpleasant for the patient to take","expensive and difficult to obtain"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"*Rigid* here describes doses that were fixed and rarely adjusted, meaning not easily changed."},{"id":"b4u18-e6","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The doctor will ___ a stronger painkiller if the current one is not working.\\"","options":["prescribe","substitute","transfer","adapt"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To prescribe medicine means to tell someone to take it, which is what a doctor does with a stronger drug."},{"id":"b4u18-e7","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"You can ___ butter for oil in this recipe if you prefer.\\"","options":["prescribe","transfer","substitute","adapt"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"To substitute one thing for another is to have it take the place of the first, which is what swapping butter for oil means."},{"id":"b4u18-e8","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The nurse had to ___ the patient to a different ward overnight.\\"","options":["transfer","prescribe","adapt","substitute"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To transfer something means to move it from one place to another, which is what moving a patient to another ward involves."},{"id":"b4u18-e9","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"It takes time to ___ to a new country and its customs.\\"","options":["substitute","adapt","transfer","prescribe"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"To adapt means to change in order to deal with a new situation, which is what settling in a new country requires."},{"id":"b4u18-e10","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"She was a ___ of modern dance — nobody had moved like that before.\\"","options":["surgeon","therapy","pioneer","transition"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"A pioneer is a person who is the first to be involved in something, which fits someone who did something entirely new."}]},"19":{"id":"b4-u19","book":4,"unit":19,"title":"Two Degrees Off Course","level":"B2","topic":"Sailing and the value of checking your numbers","generatedAt":"2026-09-23","wordCount":413,"paragraphs":["The [[mariner]] who taught me navigation had a rule he repeated until I could say it in my sleep: the sea forgives nothing, but it does allow you to [[err]] once, provided you notice quickly. He had spent thirty years on the water, and he spoke about it the way other people speak about a difficult relative.","Navigation, he said, is mostly arithmetic. If you know your [[latitude]] and the time, you can find your position. The [[equation]] is not difficult. What is difficult is trusting it when your eyes tell you something else. A [[couple]] of degrees of error looks like nothing on paper, and it looks like nothing on the water either — until you find that the harbour you were aiming for is not there.","The mistake I made was small and entirely [[inadvertent]]. I had copied a figure from the log into the wrong column. For six hours we [[deviate|deviated]] two degrees north of where we should have been, and the [[erroneous]] position sat on the chart looking perfectly reasonable. When I finally checked the numbers I had to [[differentiate]] between two almost identical sets of figures to find the error. I found it. Then I panicked.","Panic on a boat is a [[nuisance]] at best. I became [[frantic]] for about ten minutes, which is long enough to make several more mistakes. The weather had turned, and a [[multitude]] of small problems appeared at once: a torn sail, a jammed winch, a radio that would not hold a signal. My companion, who had been asleep, came up and asked, quite calmly, what our position was.","We had to [[improvise]]. The [[technique]] we used was old: dead reckoning, combined with whatever the sky would give us. That night the clouds cleared and we found the north star. It [[soothe|soothed]] me more than it should have; I knew perfectly well that the [[permanence]] of the stars is not the same thing as the permanence of a plan. We corrected our course, and the coast appeared the next afternoon.","We were not [[stranded]] for long, and nothing dramatic happened. But the episode changed how I think about instruments. A chart is a claim, not a fact, and the whole business of sailing [[revolve|revolves]] around checking that claim. A single wrong figure can [[disrupt]] an entire passage. My teacher had no interest in [[astrology]] and very little patience for sailors who trusted luck; he wanted the numbers checked twice, and then checked again."],"exercises":[{"id":"b4u19-e1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What is the passage mainly about?","options":["A navigation error at sea and what the narrator learned from it","How to use the north star to find your latitude","A storm that nearly destroyed a small sailing boat","Why old navigation techniques are better than modern instruments"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"The passage recounts a copying error that put the boat off course and the lesson the narrator drew from it."},{"id":"b4u19-e2","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What caused the navigation error?","options":["A faulty compass that had not been checked","A figure copied from the log into the wrong column","Clouds that hid the stars for six hours","A radio signal that gave the wrong position"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 3 says the narrator had copied a figure from the log into the wrong column."},{"id":"b4u19-e3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"How did they correct their course?","options":["By radioing the harbour for directions","By turning back the way they had come","By using dead reckoning and the north star","By waiting for another boat to appear"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"Paragraph 5 describes using the old technique of dead reckoning together with the north star."},{"id":"b4u19-e4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What does the teacher\'s rule suggest about his attitude to mistakes?","options":["Mistakes are unavoidable and should simply be accepted","A single mistake is survivable only if it is noticed quickly","Mistakes at sea are always the captain\'s fault","Mistakes matter less than the weather does"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"The rule allows one error provided you notice quickly, which makes speed of detection the crucial factor."},{"id":"b4u19-e5","type":"multiple-choice","question":"As used in paragraph 3, *inadvertent* most nearly means…","options":["done without realising what you are doing","done in a deliberate and careless way","done in order to save time","done after careful consideration"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"*Inadvertent* describes an action done without realising what you are doing — here, an accidental copying mistake."},{"id":"b4u19-e6","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The climbers were ___ on the mountain for two days after the storm closed the path.\\"","options":["stranded","frantic","erroneous","inadvertent"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"If someone is stranded, they are prevented from leaving a place, which is exactly the climbers\' situation."},{"id":"b4u19-e7","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"It is easy to ___ when you are working quickly and not paying attention.\\"","options":["soothe","err","revolve","improvise"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"To err means to make a mistake, which is likely when work is done quickly and without care."},{"id":"b4u19-e8","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"She became ___ when she could not find her passport at the airport.\\"","options":["inadvertent","erroneous","frantic","permanent"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"If people are frantic, they behave in a wild way because they are frightened or upset, which fits losing a passport."},{"id":"b4u19-e9","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The concert was ___ by a power cut in the middle of the second half.\\"","options":["disrupted","soothed","revolved","deviated"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To disrupt something is to prevent it from continuing normally, which is what a power cut does to a concert."},{"id":"b4u19-e10","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Young children cannot always ___ between what is real and what is imagined.\\"","options":["improvise","differentiate","soothe","deviate"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"To differentiate things is to show or see the difference between them, which is what the sentence requires."}]},"20":{"id":"b4-u20","book":4,"unit":20,"title":"The Year the Hats Grew Tall","level":"B2","topic":"Fashion history and what clothing means","generatedAt":"2026-09-23","wordCount":463,"paragraphs":["Fashion has always produced [[craze|crazes]], but few have been as strange as the one that swept through the courts of Europe in the late seventeenth century. Within a decade, the hats worn by the [[aristocracy]] had grown from modest caps into structures a metre high. The fashion began at a single court and spread, through letters, portraits, and visiting relatives, to a dozen others.","The taller the hat, the more clearly it [[signify|signified]] rank. Height was not decoration; it was information. A hat could [[denote]] the wearer\'s position in the social [[hierarchy]] almost as precisely as a title, and because the order of rank was strict, the hats became a form of competition. If a duchess added ten centimetres, a countess added twenty. A visiting [[aristocrat]] from a neighbouring kingdom would order the same shape within a month of first seeing it.","The practical consequences were [[absurd]]. Doors had to be [[enlarge|enlarged]]; carriages were rebuilt; servants were hired whose only task was to carry a hat from one room to another. A [[strap]] under the chin was essential, and so was a [[pad]] of horsehair inside the crown to stop the whole structure from slipping sideways. By the 1680s the fashion had become almost entirely [[feminine]]: men had abandoned the tall hat for a low, practical one, and the excess now belonged to women\'s [[attire]]. The [[predominant]] colour changed with the season, which meant that a hat could be out of date before it was finished.","What ended it was not taste but arithmetic. An [[excess]] of height made movement impossible, and the [[protocol]] of the court required women to walk, to bow, and to dance. A hat that could not pass through a doorway was a problem that no amount of [[vanity]] could solve. By 1700 the tallest hats had been abandoned, and fashion had moved on to hair — which unfortunately produced difficulties of its own, since the elaborate styles of the following generation became a [[tangle]] of wire, powder, and borrowed hair, and were just as impractical as the hats had been.","It is tempting to laugh at the whole episode, and many historians have done so. But the mechanism was not unusual. A [[reputable]] dressmaker would not have invented such a thing on her own; she was responding to demand. The [[style]] of a period is rarely chosen by any single person, and the [[partner|partners]] in the trade — tailors, merchants, portrait painters — all had an interest in change.","The lesson is not that people were foolish. It is that clothing carries meaning, and meaning, once it can be displayed, will always be displayed at greater and greater cost. The hats were ridiculous, but the impulse behind them was entirely ordinary — and it has not gone away."],"exercises":[{"id":"b4u20-e1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What is the passage mainly about?","options":["How hats were manufactured in the seventeenth century","A fashion for very tall hats and the reasons it ended","The differences between men\'s and women\'s clothing at court","Why portrait painters influenced European fashion"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"The passage describes the craze for tall hats, why it developed, and the practical reasons it was abandoned."},{"id":"b4u20-e2","type":"multiple-choice","question":"Why did the hats keep getting taller?","options":["Taller hats were warmer in winter","Height signalled rank, so wearers competed with one another","Materials became cheaper and easier to obtain","Court rules required a new hat every season"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 2 explains that height signified rank and that the hats became a form of competition."},{"id":"b4u20-e3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"Which practical problems did the tall hats cause?","options":["Doors had to be enlarged and carriages rebuilt","They made the wearer\'s hair fall out","They could only be worn out of doors","They had to be replaced every month"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Paragraph 3 lists enlarged doors, rebuilt carriages, and servants hired to carry hats from room to room."},{"id":"b4u20-e4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What does the writer suggest in the final paragraph?","options":["People in the past were much sillier than people today","The impulse behind such fashions is ordinary and still exists","Fashion history is too trivial a subject to study","Clothing no longer carries any social meaning"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"The writer says the impulse behind the hats was entirely ordinary and has not gone away."},{"id":"b4u20-e5","type":"multiple-choice","question":"As used in paragraph 2, *denote* most nearly means…","options":["to limit the number of something","to stand as a sign of something","to criticise something publicly","to hide something from view"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"When one thing denotes another, it stands as a sign or substitute for it — here, a hat indicating rank."},{"id":"b4u20-e6","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Skateboarding became a national ___ in the 1970s and then faded just as quickly.\\"","options":["protocol","hierarchy","craze","excess"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"A craze is a brief and popular activity or object, which matches something that swept the country and then faded."},{"id":"b4u20-e7","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"We need to ___ this photograph so that it fits the frame.\\"","options":["enlarge","denote","signify","tangle"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To enlarge something is to make it bigger, which is what is needed to fit a larger frame."},{"id":"b4u20-e8","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"He cared far too much about his appearance; his ___ was obvious to everyone.\\"","options":["attire","vanity","strap","pad"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Vanity is excessive pride in one\'s own appearance, which fits someone obsessed with how he looks."},{"id":"b4u20-e9","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The manager refused to break ___ and greeted the visitors in the correct order.\\"","options":["hierarchy","protocol","craze","excess"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Protocol is a system of rules and customs that guides how things are done, which is what the manager is following."},{"id":"b4u20-e10","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"It is one of the most ___ firms in the city; its work is trusted everywhere.\\"","options":["absurd","reputable","feminine","predominant"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"If someone or something is reputable, they have a good reputation, which matches a firm whose work is trusted."}]},"21":{"id":"b4-u21","book":4,"unit":21,"title":"Notes from a Dragon Island","level":"B2","topic":"Field research and island wildlife","generatedAt":"2026-09-23","wordCount":502,"paragraphs":["We came [[ashore]] just after dawn, and the first thing we saw was a shape on the beach that seemed far too large to be a lizard. It was a monitor of the kind that a colonial [[gazette]] once described, with considerable excitement, as a land crocodile. We had come to this island to count them, and within an hour we had already lost track of how many we had seen.","These animals are not [[homogeneous]]. Two populations living only twenty kilometres apart [[diverge]] in ways that a casual visitor would never notice: the northern group is stockier, the southern one darker, and their hunting habits differ too. My [[counterpart]] at the university insists that the evidence is [[devoid]] of any real pattern and that the differences are accidents of sampling. My own measurements [[contradict]] his. The northern animals are consistently heavier, and no amount of statistical argument has yet persuaded either of us to give way.","What nobody disputes is that a [[major]] change is under way here. Deer were introduced decades ago, and the resulting [[surplus]] of prey has allowed the lizards to grow larger than they did before. Some researchers [[theorize]] that we are watching evolution in fast motion: from an [[embryo]] in a buried clutch to a three-metre adult, the journey now takes far less time than the old records suggest. An animal that would once have needed years to reach full size now grows [[vigorous|vigorously]] in a fraction of that time.","Tracking them is slow work. Fallen trees [[obstruct]] the paths, and the heat makes every step an effort. When one of the animals decides to move, however, it does not walk — it [[plunge|plunges]] forward with a speed that is genuinely alarming. We [[verify]] each sighting twice, photographing the tail and the throat, because those two features together are the only reliable way to tell one [[individual]] from another.","Our guide has lived on the island his whole life and has never needed a permit to watch these animals. He knows which trees they sleep under, and he finds them faster than our equipment does. When I asked him whether they were becoming more common, he thought for a while and said that there were more of them than when he was a boy, but that they were also less afraid of people. That answer, which I wrote down exactly as he gave it, may turn out to be the most useful observation of the whole trip.","Our permits allow us to stay for six weeks, but the rainy season may [[prolong]] our work or cut it short. [[finally|Finally]], after eleven days, we recorded the last of the marked animals. What we do with the data matters as much as collecting it. If we [[publicize]] the results carelessly, tour operators will advertise the island, and the very animals we are counting may not [[survive]] the attention. So we will publish slowly, in a journal rather than a newspaper, and hope that the lizards outlast our curiosity."],"exercises":[{"id":"b4u21-e1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What is the writer\'s main purpose in this passage?","options":["To argue that the island should be closed to visitors","To describe a research trip and the questions it raised","To explain how to apply for a research permit","To prove that his colleague\'s statistics are wrong"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"The passage follows the team from their arrival through their counting work, and it reports an open disagreement and a guide\'s observation rather than arguing a single case."},{"id":"b4u21-e2","type":"multiple-choice","question":"According to the passage, why have the lizards grown larger than before?","options":["The island has become steadily hotter","Visitors have been feeding them","Deer were introduced and provided extra prey","They no longer have any competitors"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"Paragraph 3 states that deer were introduced decades ago and that the resulting surplus of prey has allowed the lizards to grow larger."},{"id":"b4u21-e3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What can be inferred about the writer and his counterpart?","options":["They have stopped speaking to one another","They disagree but keep working on the same question","They have agreed on a shared set of measurements","They study populations on different islands"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"They hold opposite readings of the same data, yet the writer says no argument has persuaded either to give way — an ongoing professional disagreement, not a break."},{"id":"b4u21-e4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What is the writer\'s attitude towards publishing the findings quickly?","options":["Cautious","Enthusiastic","Indifferent","Confused"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"He says careless publicity would draw tour operators to the island, so the team will publish slowly — a deliberately restrained approach."},{"id":"b4u21-e5","type":"multiple-choice","question":"As used in paragraph 3, the word \\"surplus\\" most nearly means…","options":["a shortage","an extra amount","a decline","a substitute"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"The surplus of prey is the extra prey created by the introduced deer, which allowed the lizards to grow larger."},{"id":"b4u21-e6","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Very few plants can ___ in soil as dry as this.\\"","options":["survive","plunge","obstruct","verify"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To survive is to continue to live despite difficult conditions, which is exactly what dry soil makes hard for plants."},{"id":"b4u21-e7","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The two roads ___ a few miles north of the town and never meet again.\\"","options":["diverge","verify","publicize","prolong"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To diverge is to follow a different direction, which describes two roads separating and continuing apart."},{"id":"b4u21-e8","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The witness\'s second statement appeared to ___ everything he had said the day before.\\"","options":["contradict","verify","survive","prolong"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To contradict is to state the opposite of what has been said, so a statement that contradicts an earlier one cannot be reconciled with it."},{"id":"b4u21-e9","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The bridge is closed for repairs, and the delays are causing a ___ disruption to traffic.\\"","options":["major","vigorous","devoid","homogeneous"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"A major disruption is an important and serious one, which fits a closed bridge holding up traffic across a whole area."},{"id":"b4u21-e10","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The mixture looked completely ___ : every grain was the same size and colour.\\"","options":["homogeneous","vigorous","devoid","major"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"If something is homogeneous it is made up of parts that are all the same, which matches a mixture in which every grain is identical."}]},"22":{"id":"b4-u22","book":4,"unit":22,"title":"The Curator\'s Table","level":"B2","topic":"Museum archives and ancient manuscripts","generatedAt":"2026-09-23","wordCount":499,"paragraphs":["The room where Elena works has no windows and a constant temperature of nineteen degrees. On the table in front of her lie forty fragments of papyrus, each no bigger than a playing card, each carrying a few lines of Greek written by a hand that stopped moving some seventeen centuries ago. She has been [[interpret|interpreting]] them for eleven years. \'People imagine that this is a spiritual business,\' she says. \'It is mostly [[botany]] and patience.\'","The collection is a mixed one. Some pieces are medical: a recipe for a paste to ease [[arthritis]], a note on the diet of a patient recovering from [[pneumonia]]. Others are ritual. One fragment lists the equipment needed for a ceremony — a small [[altar]], a lamp, a bowl — while another gives instructions for a charm meant to make a rival fall silent. A third claims that its owner could see the future. Whether the writer genuinely believed he was [[psychic]], or was simply selling a service, is impossible to establish; what interests Elena is the [[motive]] behind the copying.","\'Every one of these [[text|texts]] was written down for a reason,\' she says. \'Somebody paid for them.\' That is why she is careful about the [[scope]] of her conclusions. A single [[document]] proves nothing on its own; forty of them, read together, begin to show a pattern. And the pattern she has found is less exotic than the public expects. Most of the spells were not [[sinister]] at all. They were medicine, legal advice, and the ordinary business of getting through a difficult life in a crowded city.","A few pieces, admittedly, are exercises in [[deception]]: charms designed to make a buyer believe he had been given something valuable. Elena files these separately, not because they are unusual but because they are the hardest to date. \'People lie in letters,\' she says. \'They exaggerate. Some of the writers were probably [[psychotic]], and some were simply tired.\' She is careful, all the same, not to make the people in the archive more [[credible]] than they deserve to be.","The most affecting item in the box is a letter. A woman writes to a friend about a man who has recently died — the [[deceased]], she says, owed her family money — and asks for help. Elena reads it as evidence of ordinary [[strife]] within a family rather than of anything magical. Work on the fragments begins at [[dusk]] and ends when her eyes give out. Over the years she has [[hone|honed]] a technique for reading damaged ink: photograph the surface under four different lights, then compare the shadows.","It is unglamorous work, and it rarely [[gratify|gratifies]] anyone but herself. The one substance she refuses to handle is the residue found on the oldest pieces, which turned out to be animal [[dung]] used in preparing the surface. \'You can tell a lot from what people put on their paper,\' she says. \'You can tell even more from what they chose to write on it.\'"],"exercises":[{"id":"b4u22-e1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What is the main subject of the passage?","options":["The market for ancient manuscripts","A curator\'s patient work on a collection of fragments","The history of Greek medicine","Why museums keep their rooms at fixed temperatures"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"The passage describes Elena, her forty fragments, what they contain and how she reads them — a profile of her working life rather than a market or medical history."},{"id":"b4u22-e2","type":"multiple-choice","question":"According to the passage, what does Elena say most of the spells actually were?","options":["Attempts to predict the future","Dangerous curses aimed at rivals","Medicine, legal advice and everyday practical help","Copies of older religious writings"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"Paragraph 3 says the pattern she found was less exotic than expected: the spells were medicine, legal advice, and the ordinary business of daily life."},{"id":"b4u22-e3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"Why is Elena cautious about the scope of her conclusions?","options":["Because the fragments are written in an unknown language","Because a single document on its own proves nothing","Because she has not been allowed to publish","Because the collection belongs to another museum"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 3 explains that one document proves nothing alone, and that only forty of them read together begin to show a pattern."},{"id":"b4u22-e4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What is Elena\'s attitude towards the people who wrote the documents?","options":["She assumes they were all honest","She treats their claims with measured scepticism","She believes they were mostly seriously ill","She thinks they were more educated than modern readers"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"She notes that people lie and exaggerate in letters and refuses to make them more credible than they deserve — sceptical, but not dismissive."},{"id":"b4u22-e5","type":"multiple-choice","question":"As used in paragraph 3, the word \\"sinister\\" most nearly means…","options":["evil or threatening","ancient","expensive","confusing"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Elena contrasts sinister spells with ordinary medicine and legal advice, so the word carries its sense of something evil or harmful."},{"id":"b4u22-e6","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The police found no clear ___ for the attack.\\"","options":["motive","altar","scope","text"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"A motive is the reason someone does something, which is what investigators look for when an action needs explaining."},{"id":"b4u22-e7","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"It took her years of practice to ___ her skills as a translator.\\"","options":["hone","gratify","document","altar"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To hone something is to improve it until it is very good, which is what years of practice do to a skill."},{"id":"b4u22-e8","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The witness\'s account was detailed, consistent and entirely ___.\\"","options":["credible","sinister","psychotic","deceased"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"If something is credible it can be believed, and a detailed, consistent account is exactly what makes a witness believable."},{"id":"b4u22-e9","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"A quiet evening with a good book is enough to ___ him.\\"","options":["gratify","hone","interpret","document"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To gratify someone is to please them, and a quiet evening with a book is presented here as the thing that pleases him."},{"id":"b4u22-e10","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The village had suffered years of ___ between two rival families.\\"","options":["strife","dusk","scope","motive"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Strife is disagreement or fighting between people or groups, which fits a long dispute between two families."}]},"23":{"id":"b4-u23","book":4,"unit":23,"title":"The Smallest Hunter","level":"B2","topic":"Insects, disease and public health","generatedAt":"2026-09-23","wordCount":502,"paragraphs":["A mosquito is not, by any reasonable standard, a [[savage]] animal. It weighs less than a grain of rice, it cannot bite through clothing, and its bite produces nothing worse than an itch. Yet measured by the number of human deaths it causes, it is the most dangerous creature on the planet. In the language of public health it is classified as a [[pest]] — an animal that harms people not by hunting them but by sharing their homes.","When a mosquito feeds, it pierces the skin and taps directly into a small blood vessel. If that vessel is an [[artery]], the pressure helps the insect draw blood quickly; if it is a vein, the meal takes longer. Either way, the insect\'s saliva enters the wound. That saliva contains proteins which stop blood from clotting, and it is these proteins that [[induce]] the familiar swelling and itching. For most people the reaction is [[minor]] and passes within a day.","The danger begins when the mosquito is carrying something else. Certain [[microbe|microbes]] — single-celled parasites in particular — use the insect as transport. Once inside a human host they multiply, and the [[deterioration]] in the patient\'s condition can be alarmingly fast. In the worst cases the parasites reach the brain, causing [[paralysis]] or death. Because the early symptoms resemble those of a common infection, they are easy to dismiss, and by the time the illness is obvious, treatment is far harder.","Control has therefore always depended on prevention rather than cure. Draining standing water removes the breeding sites; screens and nets [[ensure]] that people are not bitten while they sleep. These measures sound modest, and their effect on any single household is [[negligible]]. Applied across a whole region, however, they [[prevail]] over the disease. It is one of the few areas of public health in which dull, repetitive work has saved more lives than any drug.","Researchers in [[pursuit]] of a vaccine face a moving target. The parasite is [[elusive]]: it changes the proteins on its surface, so a vaccine that works this year may be [[invalid]] the next. Some scientists argue that a single universal vaccine is unrealistic, and that effort should go instead into a combination of tools. Others believe that the insect and the parasite are [[inseparable]] in practice, and that only by attacking both at once can real progress be made.","For decades the search remained outside the [[mainstream]] of medical research, funded by a handful of charities and dismissed as commercially pointless. That has changed. A [[rupture]] in the old assumptions came when a trial in three countries reported results that [[stun|stunned]] the field: protection of over seventy per cent, sustained for a year. Under a microscope it is possible to [[magnify]] a single parasite until it fills the screen, and yet what one sees explains very little about why one child falls ill and another does not. Children who were once [[susceptible]] to infection from their first birthday now have a reasonable chance of reaching adulthood untouched."],"exercises":[{"id":"b4u23-e1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What is the passage mainly about?","options":["How to identify different species of mosquito","Why a tiny insect is so dangerous and how the risk is managed","The commercial failure of vaccine research","The symptoms of a common childhood infection"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"The passage moves from the insect\'s bite, through the parasites it carries, to prevention and vaccine research — a single explanation of the danger and the response to it."},{"id":"b4u23-e2","type":"multiple-choice","question":"According to the passage, what is the effect of the proteins in mosquito saliva?","options":["They stop the blood from clotting","They carry the parasite into the wound","They make the bite completely painless","They speed up the patient\'s recovery"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Paragraph 2 says the saliva contains proteins that stop blood from clotting, and that these proteins cause the swelling and itching."},{"id":"b4u23-e3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"Why is the illness often missed in its early stages?","options":["It produces no symptoms at all for several weeks","Its early symptoms look like those of a common infection","It only affects people who have never been bitten before","Doctors are not trained to recognise it"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 3 explains that the early symptoms resemble a common infection, so they are easy to dismiss until the illness becomes obvious."},{"id":"b4u23-e4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What does the writer suggest about preventive measures?","options":["They are too expensive for most regions","They are less effective than new drugs","Applied widely, they have saved more lives than any drug","They only work in countries with cold winters"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"Paragraph 4 states that although their effect on one household is negligible, applied across a region they prevail, and that dull repetitive work has saved more lives than any drug."},{"id":"b4u23-e5","type":"multiple-choice","question":"As used in paragraph 4, the word \\"negligible\\" most nearly means…","options":["extremely small","harmful","expensive","temporary"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"The writer contrasts the negligible effect on a single household with the large effect across a whole region, so the word means extremely small."},{"id":"b4u23-e6","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Despite a long search, the bird remained ___ and was photographed only twice.\\"","options":["elusive","savage","minor","invalid"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"If something is elusive it is hard to find, which fits a bird that a long search has barely turned up."},{"id":"b4u23-e7","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Common sense usually ___ in the end, even in the noisiest arguments.\\"","options":["prevails","induces","magnifies","ruptures"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To prevail is to win out or be accepted in the end, which is what common sense does in an argument."},{"id":"b4u23-e8","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Nothing could ___ him to change his mind once it was made up.\\"","options":["induce","ensure","stun","magnify"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To induce someone to do something is to cause or persuade them to do it, which is exactly what failed here."},{"id":"b4u23-e9","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Young trees are especially ___ to frost in their first winter.\\"","options":["susceptible","elusive","negligible","mainstream"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"If something is susceptible to a harm, it is easily affected by it, which is the case for young trees and frost."},{"id":"b4u23-e10","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The pipe ___ under the pressure of the freezing water.\\"","options":["ruptured","induced","ensured","prevailed"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To rupture is to tear or burst open, which is what a pipe does under pressure it cannot withstand."}]},"24":{"id":"b4-u24","book":4,"unit":24,"title":"The King Who Asked the Wrong Question","level":"B2","topic":"An ancient kingdom and its fall","generatedAt":"2026-09-23","wordCount":427,"paragraphs":["For thirty years the kingdom prospered, and for thirty years its ruler believed that this was proof of his own good judgment. He had inherited a fertile valley, a river that carried gold dust, and a position on the trade routes between two continents. Grain, oil and metal moved through his markets, and every [[commodity]] that passed through them was taxed. The treasury was full, the granaries were fuller, and nobody at court had any reason to ask how long such an arrangement could reasonably be expected to last.","What the king could not do was [[marshal]] his advantages into a lasting settlement. His neighbours to the east were [[envious]] of the valley, and his own nobles were divided. When the harvest failed in the fourth year of his reign, the resulting [[unrest]] was suppressed quickly, but the [[morale]] of the army never fully recovered. A ruler who has once used force against his own people finds that the option is always available and never quite forgotten.","Then came the [[prophecy]]. A priestess at the temple announced that a great empire would fall if the king crossed the river. She did not say which empire. The king, whose [[mood]] that spring was buoyant after a good harvest, chose to hear the answer he wanted. His advisers [[advocate|advocated]] caution, [[albeit]] without much conviction, and the [[senate]] — an assembly of elders with the power to [[authorize]] war but not to conduct it — voted in favour by a narrow margin.","The campaign began well. Three cities fell, and the king [[communicate|communicated]] his [[conquest|conquests]] home in letters read aloud in the market square. A [[festive]] atmosphere settled on the capital, and even the [[civilian]] population, which had cheered the first victories, seemed to share in it. Yet the enthusiasm did not last. Merchants began to [[indicate]] that they would rather not extend further credit, and volunteers were slow to [[enroll]] in the new regiments. The [[dynamics]] of the court had shifted towards the young officers who had led the attacks, and the older advisers found themselves without influence.","The reversal, when it came, was total. The enemy avoided battle, cut the supply road and waited. Within a season the army was eating boiled [[lentil|lentils]] and little else. The king sent envoys to ask for terms, and for the first time he [[disclose|disclosed]] to the senate the true state of his finances. It was not enough. He had asked the oracle a question about the future and received an answer about the past: the empire that fell was his own."],"exercises":[{"id":"b4u24-e1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What is the passage mainly about?","options":["The trade routes of an ancient valley","How a prosperous kingdom lost everything through a series of decisions","The religious ceremonies of an ancient temple","How the senate elected its elders"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"The passage traces the kingdom from prosperity, through the prophecy and the campaign, to total reversal — a story of decline rather than of trade or religion."},{"id":"b4u24-e2","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What exactly did the prophecy say?","options":["That the king would die before the harvest","That a great empire would fall if he crossed the river","That the senate would refuse to fund a war","That the eastern neighbours would attack first"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 3 gives the prophecy as a warning that a great empire would fall if the king crossed the river, and stresses that it did not say which empire."},{"id":"b4u24-e3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What was the lasting effect of the failed harvest?","options":["The army\'s morale never fully recovered","The nobles divided the valley among themselves","The king abolished the senate","Trade with the east came to an end"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Paragraph 2 says the unrest was suppressed quickly but that the morale of the army never fully recovered."},{"id":"b4u24-e4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What does the final sentence of the passage suggest?","options":["The prophecy was deliberately invented after the war","The empire that fell was the king\'s own","The enemy kingdom was destroyed instead","The oracle had never spoken to the king"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"The king received an answer about the past rather than the future: the empire the prophecy referred to turned out to be his own."},{"id":"b4u24-e5","type":"multiple-choice","question":"As used in paragraph 2, the word \\"marshal\\" most nearly means…","options":["assemble and organise","hide from view","give away","argue against"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"The king could not marshal his advantages into a lasting settlement — he could not bring them together and organise them into something permanent."},{"id":"b4u24-e6","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"He was ___ of his younger brother\'s success and said nothing about it.\\"","options":["envious","festive","civilian","albeit"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"If someone is envious they want what another person has, which explains the silence about the brother\'s success."},{"id":"b4u24-e7","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Rising prices and food shortages led to ___ in several cities.\\"","options":["unrest","morale","prophecy","senate"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Unrest is a state of anger among people in a place, which is what shortages and high prices tend to produce."},{"id":"b4u24-e8","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Clean water is a ___ that no community can do without.\\"","options":["commodity","prophecy","lentil","mood"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"A commodity is something that can be bought or sold, which describes water treated as a resource to be supplied and paid for."},{"id":"b4u24-e9","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The company refused to ___ the terms of the agreement to the public.\\"","options":["disclose","marshal","authorize","enroll"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To disclose something is to tell it to someone else, so refusing to disclose means keeping the terms secret."},{"id":"b4u24-e10","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Over two hundred students ___ in the new course during its first week.\\"","options":["enrolled","advocated","disclosed","authorized"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To enroll in something is to put your name on a list as a member, which is what students do when they join a course."}]},"25":{"id":"b4-u25","book":4,"unit":25,"title":"The Last Case","level":"B2","topic":"A global vaccination campaign","generatedAt":"2026-09-23","wordCount":435,"paragraphs":["In 1967 [[smallpox]] was still [[widespread]]. It killed roughly two million people that year and left many survivors blind or badly scarred. Yet within a decade it would become the first human disease to be deliberately wiped out — and the campaign that achieved this was, by the standards of modern medicine, remarkably crude.","The weapon was vaccination, a technique discovered nearly two centuries earlier. The [[onset]] of protection came within days of a successful jab, and it lasted for years. The difficulty was not the science. It was logistics. [[compile|Compiling]] a reliable picture of where the disease still existed took years of fieldwork, because in some regions no accurate records had ever been kept. Teams travelled by boat, by jeep and on foot; in a single year one field worker might cover four thousand kilometres of river and track, carrying nothing but a cold box and a set of forms.","The campaign\'s organisers [[vow|vowed]] to break the chain of [[transmit|transmission]] rather than vaccinate entire populations. Where a case appeared, teams would [[diagnose]] it, isolate the patient, and vaccinate everyone within a wide radius. This approach required a [[coherent]] plan and extraordinary local knowledge, and it could not have worked without thousands of health workers who agreed to [[collaborate]] across borders.","Governments had to be persuaded to act as well. Some [[enact|enacted]] laws requiring vaccination; others, suspicious of outside interference, [[prohibit|prohibited]] foreign teams from entering certain districts. A [[federation]] of national agencies was eventually created to coordinate the work, although its authority was limited and its budget smaller than that of a single city hospital in a wealthy country.","To a modern reader the field accounts are [[gross]] in their detail — the fever, the rash, the smell — but they were written to shock officials into action, and they worked. Progress was not always [[humane]] in its methods. [[needy|Needy]] families were sometimes offered food in exchange for cooperation, a practice that many workers found uncomfortable but which [[curb|curbed]] resistance. [[pledge|Pledges]] of cooperation from local leaders were essential, and where they were absent the work stalled.","[[render|Rendered]] safe by decades of vaccination, the world gradually stopped noticing. The last natural case occurred in 1977. Four years later, a commission was able to [[certify]] that the disease had gone. It had been [[transform|transformed]] from a permanent threat into a chapter of medical history — a change so complete that most people now have no idea what it looked like. The vaccine is no longer produced for general use, and the virus itself survives only in two high-security laboratories, where its future is reviewed every few years."],"exercises":[{"id":"b4u25-e1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What is the passage mainly about?","options":["The invention of vaccination two centuries ago","How a worldwide campaign eliminated a disease","The budgets of international health agencies","Why some governments distrust foreign doctors"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"The passage covers the scale of the disease in 1967, the strategy adopted, the obstacles faced, and the certification of its disappearance — the story of one eradication campaign."},{"id":"b4u25-e2","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What strategy did the organisers of the campaign adopt?","options":["Vaccinating every person in every country","Waiting for the disease to disappear naturally","Breaking the chain of transmission around each case","Closing all borders to travellers"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"Paragraph 3 says they vowed to break the chain of transmission rather than vaccinate entire populations, isolating each case and vaccinating everyone nearby."},{"id":"b4u25-e3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"According to the writer, what was the real difficulty facing the campaign?","options":["The vaccine was unreliable","Logistics rather than science","A shortage of trained scientists","The disease changed too quickly"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 2 states plainly that the difficulty was not the science but logistics — compiling a reliable picture of where the disease still existed."},{"id":"b4u25-e4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What does the writer suggest about the shocking detail in the field accounts?","options":["It was exaggerated for personal fame","It was included deliberately to force officials to act","It was added later by historians","It was the reason some governments refused to help"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 5 says the accounts were gross in their detail because they were written to shock officials into action — and that this worked."},{"id":"b4u25-e5","type":"multiple-choice","question":"As used in paragraph 5, the word \\"curbed\\" most nearly means…","options":["restrained","rewarded","explained","increased"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Offering food curbed resistance, meaning it held resistance back or restrained it — not that it encouraged it."},{"id":"b4u25-e6","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Doctors were unable to ___ the illness until the test results came back.\\"","options":["diagnose","certify","curb","render"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To diagnose someone is to identify the condition they have, which is what test results allow doctors to do."},{"id":"b4u25-e7","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The new rules ___ the use of mobile phones during examinations.\\"","options":["prohibit","pledge","vow","compile"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To prohibit something is to not allow it, which is what rules do with phones in an exam room."},{"id":"b4u25-e8","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"An independent laboratory was asked to ___ that the results were genuine.\\"","options":["certify","collaborate","enact","curb"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To certify something is to confirm officially that it is true, which is the role of an independent laboratory."},{"id":"b4u25-e9","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The ___ of winter brought heavy snow and closed the mountain roads.\\"","options":["onset","pledge","federation","vow"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"The onset of something is its beginning, so the onset of winter is the point at which winter starts."},{"id":"b4u25-e10","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The two universities agreed to ___ on a study of coastal erosion.\\"","options":["collaborate","certify","prohibit","render"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To collaborate is to work together on something, which is what two institutions do when they run a joint study."}]},"26":{"id":"b4-u26","book":4,"unit":26,"title":"Six Weeks at the End of the Road","level":"B2","topic":"Volunteering at a rural health clinic","generatedAt":"2026-09-23","wordCount":434,"paragraphs":["Day one. The clinic stands at the end of a dirt road: four rooms, a generator, and a waiting area that becomes [[overcrowded]] by nine in the morning. There are two nurses, a [[practitioner]] who visits on Thursdays, and a single refrigerator that must not be allowed to fail. My job, for six weeks, is to help with the records.","Day three. A boy arrives with a high fever. The infection, the nurse explains, settles in the [[intestines]] and drains the body of water faster than a small child can afford. It [[manifest|manifests]] first as nothing more than tiredness. The source, almost certainly, is the storage tank. It is cracked, and whatever gets into it will [[contaminate]] the supply for the entire village. \'This is not a [[hazard]] we can fix with medicine,\' she says.","Day nine. Two more cases, then four. By the twelfth day we are seeing a dozen, and the word [[epidemic]] is used openly. An evening meeting is called, and the [[emphasis]] of our work shifts: we stop asking how to treat the sick and start asking how to keep them well. It is [[imperative]] that the tank be repaired before the rains, because rain will carry whatever is in the ground directly into the water.","Day fourteen. A team arrives with chlorine and a testing kit. They show us how to make water [[sterile]] and how to keep the equipment clean between uses. A [[deficiency]] in the mixture would leave the water as dangerous as before, so the measuring is done twice, every time. The senior nurse is the most [[compassionate]] person I have worked with; she [[administer|administers]] the treatment to every child under five, whether or not there are symptoms, and she [[adhere|adheres]] to the protocol even when she is exhausted.","Day twenty-two. The cases begin to fall. We cannot [[reverse]] the damage already done to the weakest children, but the trend is unmistakable. [[voluntary|Voluntary]] workers from the village have taken over the daily chlorination, which is the only [[viable]] arrangement once we leave, and we have begun to [[replenish]] the supplies we used in the first fortnight.","Day forty. In the end it was the [[provision]] of clean water, not medicine, that ended it. The cracked tank has been replaced and there is talk of an [[upgrade]] to the pump. The numbers in my notebook look ordinary again, which is exactly what they should look like. Six weeks is not long enough to know whether the repair will hold, but it is long enough to learn that most of what keeps a clinic useful happens outside it."],"exercises":[{"id":"b4u26-e1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What is the passage mainly about?","options":["How a rural clinic was designed and built","A volunteer\'s account of an outbreak and how it was brought under control","The training of nurses in remote areas","Why record-keeping in clinics is often inaccurate"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"The diary entries follow the outbreak from the first case, through the meeting and the arrival of the chlorine team, to the fall in cases — a first-hand account of controlling an outbreak."},{"id":"b4u26-e2","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What did the nurse identify as the source of the infection?","options":["Food brought in from the market","A cracked storage tank feeding the water supply","The clinic\'s single refrigerator","Contact with visitors from the city"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 2 says the source was almost certainly the storage tank, which was cracked and would contaminate the supply for the whole village."},{"id":"b4u26-e3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What changed after the evening meeting on day nine?","options":["The clinic stopped admitting children","The emphasis shifted from treating the sick to keeping them well","The practitioner began visiting every day","The volunteers were sent home"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 3 says the emphasis of the work shifted: the team stopped asking how to treat the sick and started asking how to keep them well."},{"id":"b4u26-e4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What does the writer mean by saying that \\"most of what keeps a clinic useful happens outside it\\"?","options":["Patients prefer to be treated at home","The decisive work was repairing the water supply, not treating patients","The clinic building was in poor condition","Nurses spend most of their time travelling"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"The writer concludes that the provision of clean water, rather than medicine, ended the outbreak, and that the tank and pump mattered more than anything done inside the clinic."},{"id":"b4u26-e5","type":"multiple-choice","question":"As used in paragraph 4, the word \\"deficiency\\" most nearly means…","options":["a lack","an error in the records","an excess","a delay"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"A deficiency in the mixture means too little chlorine, which is why the measuring is done twice to avoid leaving the water unsafe."},{"id":"b4u26-e6","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"We stopped at a village shop to ___ our supplies of water.\\"","options":["replenish","contaminate","administer","adhere"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To replenish something is to make it full or complete again, which is what buying more water does to a dwindling supply."},{"id":"b4u26-e7","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"It is difficult to ___ the damage caused by years of neglect.\\"","options":["reverse","replenish","administer","upgrade"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To reverse something is to turn it back to an earlier state, which is what the sentence says is difficult to do with long-term damage."},{"id":"b4u26-e8","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Solar power is now a ___ option for most households in the area.\\"","options":["viable","voluntary","sterile","imperative"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"If something is viable it is capable of doing what it is intended to do, so a viable option is one that will actually work."},{"id":"b4u26-e9","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Loose wires near water are a serious ___ in any workshop.\\"","options":["hazard","deficiency","provision","emphasis"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"A hazard is something that could be dangerous to a person\'s health or safety, which describes loose wires near water."},{"id":"b4u26-e10","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The staff were patient and ___ with every anxious family.\\"","options":["compassionate","sterile","voluntary","overcrowded"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Compassionate people feel pity and sympathy for others, which is the quality being praised alongside patience here."}]},"27":{"id":"b4-u27","book":4,"unit":27,"title":"What the Body Remembers","level":"B2","topic":"Living with a neurological condition","generatedAt":"2026-09-23","wordCount":428,"paragraphs":["The first time it happened I was fourteen, sitting at a kitchen table with a spoon in my hand. The [[utensil]] hit the floor before I did. I remember the sound of it, and then nothing for several minutes. When I woke, my mother was holding my head and someone was checking that my [[airway]] was clear.","For a long time, what I knew about my own condition came from other people. In the centuries before anyone understood the brain, a [[seizure]] was read as a sign of possession, or of a punishment, or of a gift. [[almighty|The Almighty]] was often [[invoke|invoked]] in explanations. Even after medicine took an interest, the [[ideology]] of the day shaped what doctors saw. Nineteenth-century writers were fond of [[equate|equating]] the condition with madness, and the first specialist wards were [[found|founded]] on that assumption.","Modern [[psychiatry]] has moved a long way from those ideas. The [[advent]] of reliable imaging in the 1970s allowed researchers to watch activity in a living brain, and the [[novelty]] of seeing it for the first time changed the questions that were asked. The old explanations did not disappear at once; [[outmoded]] ideas rarely do. But a [[definitive]] account of what happens during an attack gradually replaced them.","What happens, in plain terms, is a sudden surge of electrical activity. The body\'s [[reflex|reflexes]] take over, muscles tighten, and the person cannot respond. Breathing changes: the patient may [[inhale]] sharply, or not at all. The event usually follows a [[cycle]] that is different for every patient — some are woken by it, others by a particular pattern of light or a missed meal. Because the pattern is so personal, treatment has to be [[personalize|personalized]] rather than prescribed from a standard list.","For years I resisted the [[psychiatric]] label, partly out of pride and partly because it seemed to reduce me to a diagnosis. What changed my mind was a [[session]] with a specialist who drew the pattern of my attacks on a sheet of paper and asked whether it looked familiar. It did. The [[lunar]] calendar had nothing to do with it, she said, although patients have believed otherwise for centuries; stress, sleep and alcohol did.","I still have them, though far less often. What I have lost is the fear of the unknown — the sense that my body was doing something no one could explain. That is what a good explanation gives you: not a cure, but a description you can carry. It is a strange thing to be grateful for, and I am grateful for it anyway."],"exercises":[{"id":"b4u27-e1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What is the passage mainly about?","options":["The history of brain imaging techniques","A personal account of a condition and how explanations of it have changed","Why patients distrust specialists","The dangers of stress and alcohol"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"The writer describes his own experience and sets it against the shifting historical and medical understanding of the condition."},{"id":"b4u27-e2","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What happened when the writer was fourteen?","options":["He dropped a spoon and lost consciousness","He was taken to hospital by a specialist","He was told he would never work again","He was diagnosed after a brain scan"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Paragraph 1 describes the spoon hitting the floor before he did, and then nothing for several minutes until he woke with his mother holding his head."},{"id":"b4u27-e3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What changed in the 1970s, according to the passage?","options":["The condition became far more common","Reliable imaging allowed researchers to watch a living brain at work","Specialist wards were closed","Doctors stopped using the psychiatric label"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 3 credits the advent of reliable imaging in the 1970s with letting researchers watch activity in a living brain for the first time."},{"id":"b4u27-e4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"Why did the writer resist the psychiatric label for years?","options":["He did not believe the diagnosis was correct","He felt it reduced him to a diagnosis","He had been told it would cost him his job","He distrusted all forms of medicine"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 5 gives two reasons: pride, and the feeling that the label seemed to reduce him to a diagnosis rather than describe a person."},{"id":"b4u27-e5","type":"multiple-choice","question":"As used in paragraph 3, the word \\"outmoded\\" most nearly means…","options":["no longer in use","difficult to prove","widely accepted","carefully recorded"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"The writer says old explanations did not disappear at once because outmoded ideas rarely do — that is, ideas that have gone out of use."},{"id":"b4u27-e6","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Take a slow, deep breath and ___ through your nose.\\"","options":["inhale","invoke","equate","found"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To inhale is to take air into the lungs, which is what breathing in through the nose does."},{"id":"b4u27-e7","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Blinking when something moves towards your eye is a simple ___.\\"","options":["reflex","cycle","session","novelty"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"A reflex is an unconscious response by a body part to an event, which is exactly what blinking at a fast approach is."},{"id":"b4u27-e8","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Water ___ from cloud to rain to river and back again.\\"","options":["cycles","inhales","equates","invokes"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To cycle is to move through a series of stages and return to the beginning, which is what water does in this description."},{"id":"b4u27-e9","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The software can be ___ to suit the needs of each user.\\"","options":["personalized","equated","invoked","founded"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To personalize something is to design it for one person\'s particular needs, which is what the software allows."},{"id":"b4u27-e10","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"There is no ___ answer to the question; historians still disagree.\\"","options":["definitive","lunar","outmoded","psychiatric"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"A definitive answer is the most official and settled one, so the fact that historians still disagree shows there is none."}]},"28":{"id":"b4-u28","book":4,"unit":28,"title":"The Building That Kept Winter","level":"B2","topic":"An old ice house and how it worked","generatedAt":"2026-09-23","wordCount":429,"paragraphs":["From the road, the ice house looks like nothing: a low mound of earth with a door in one side. It was built in the 1790s to [[generate]] a supply of cold that would last through the summer, and for sixty years it did exactly that, without a single machine.","The [[structure]] is simpler than it appears. Beneath the mound is a brick chamber — a [[cavity]] about four metres deep, shaped like an egg. Around it run two [[concentric]] walls with a gap between them, and the gap is packed with straw. The entrance faces north, away from the sun. A drain at the bottom carries away meltwater, so that the ice does not sit in a pool and dissolve.","Filling it was a winter occupation. Workers would [[select]] blocks from the frozen lake, cut them into regular [[item|items]] of roughly equal size, and drag them up the slope. Each block was [[assign|assigned]] to a layer, and each layer was packed with straw. The work was done [[clockwise]] around the chamber, so that no side was loaded more heavily than another, and it was [[oversee|overseen]] by a foreman whose only qualification was that he had done it for twenty years.","Inside, the air is [[crisp]] even in August. If you [[pierce]] the surface of the ice at the top, you can see the [[wavy]] lines that mark the summers of two centuries ago — a [[visual]] record of which years were warm and which were not.","The ice house was never just a building. It was a social arrangement. A committee [[allocate|allocated]] a share to each household, and the [[interchange]] of ice for butter or eggs was part of ordinary village life. Records show occasional [[discord]] over the size of shares, but also a strict [[courtesy]]: nobody collected a portion before the oldest residents had theirs.","The estate itself was unusually [[multicultural]] for its time; the gardeners, the dairymaids and the foreman came from four different countries, and the kitchen produced food that visitors found strange and, on a good day, [[appetizing]]. The one serious [[omission]] in the surviving accounts is the ice house\'s cost. Nobody wrote it down.","Cheap mechanical refrigeration arrived in the 1860s, and the trade collapsed within a decade. The building was left to fill with leaves. It has since been repaired, and it now receives visitors, who mostly come for the cold. Standing inside on a hot afternoon, you can feel what the original customers were paying for: a piece of winter, kept in the dark, handed out fairly and shared by agreement."],"exercises":[{"id":"b4u28-e1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What is the passage mainly about?","options":["The history of refrigeration as an industry","An old ice house, how it worked and what it meant to a village","The design of eighteenth-century brick buildings","The working conditions of estate gardeners"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"The passage describes the building and its construction, then its role in village life, and finally its decline — a profile of the ice house rather than of refrigeration in general."},{"id":"b4u28-e2","type":"multiple-choice","question":"Why does the entrance of the ice house face north?","options":["To keep it away from the sun","Because the road runs that way","To make the blocks easier to drag","Because the wind blows from the south"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Paragraph 2 states that the entrance faces north, away from the sun — a way of keeping the interior cold."},{"id":"b4u28-e3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"How was the ice arranged inside the chamber?","options":["Loose, with no packing at all","In equal-sized blocks assigned to layers packed with straw","In a single block cut from the lake","Covered with water to freeze it solid"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 3 describes blocks cut to roughly equal size, each assigned to a layer, with every layer packed with straw and built up clockwise."},{"id":"b4u28-e4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What do the wavy lines in the ice record?","options":["The names of the workers who cut the blocks","Which summers were warm and which were not","The number of households sharing the ice","The year the building was repaired"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 4 describes the wavy lines as a visual record of which years were warm and which were not."},{"id":"b4u28-e5","type":"multiple-choice","question":"As used in paragraph 6, the word \\"omission\\" most nearly means…","options":["something left out","a mistaken figure","an unexpected cost","a private agreement"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"The writer notes that the cost of the ice house is the one serious omission — the single thing the surviving accounts failed to record."},{"id":"b4u28-e6","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The apple was fresh, cold and ___.\\"","options":["crisp","wavy","concentric","multicultural"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Crisp describes food with a pleasant hard surface, which is what a fresh cold apple has."},{"id":"b4u28-e7","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The dentist found a small ___ in one of my back teeth.\\"","options":["cavity","courtesy","item","structure"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"A cavity is a hole or space inside something, which is what a dentist finds in a decayed tooth."},{"id":"b4u28-e8","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"He showed great ___ by holding the door open for everyone.\\"","options":["courtesy","discord","omission","interchange"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Courtesy is good manners and considerate social conduct, of which holding a door open is a small example."},{"id":"b4u28-e9","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The decision caused ___ among the members of the committee.\\"","options":["discord","courtesy","cavity","interchange"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Discord is disagreement or conflict, which is what a divisive decision produces within a committee."},{"id":"b4u28-e10","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The council will ___ half of the budget to road repairs.\\"","options":["allocate","oversee","pierce","select"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To allocate something is to put it aside for a particular purpose, which is what the council does with part of its budget."}]},"29":{"id":"b4-u29","book":4,"unit":29,"title":"After the Oil Ran Slow","level":"B2","topic":"Economic change in a resource-dependent region","generatedAt":"2026-09-23","wordCount":430,"paragraphs":["For forty years the [[region]]\'s economy rested on a single product. [[petroleum]] was cheap to extract, easy to [[export]], and profitable enough to pay for schools, roads and hospitals without anyone having to think very hard. When the price fell by half in a single year, the thinking had to start.","The immediate problem was that nothing else was large enough to replace it. Manufacturing accounted for less than a tenth of national [[output]]. Agriculture employed a third of the workforce but produced very little, and many families in the interior [[subsist|subsisted]] on what they grew rather than on what they earned. [[overpopulation]] in the coastal cities, meanwhile, had pushed rents beyond what most workers could pay.","The government\'s response, published as a white paper, was a programme of [[conversion]]: turning an oil economy into something more varied. Three [[sector|sectors]] were chosen for investment — tourism, agriculture and light manufacturing. New rules were designed to [[penalize]] companies that exported raw materials without processing them at home, and a simplified [[patent]] system was introduced to encourage local invention.","Not everyone [[subscribe|subscribed]] to the plan. Critics argued that the targets were arbitrary and that the money set aside for them was a [[scrap]] of what would be needed. Supporters replied that the alternative was to wait until the reserves ran low, by which time there would be nothing left to invest. [[notwithstanding]] the objections, the first projects broke ground within eighteen months.","Two decisions proved more difficult than expected. The first concerned fuel subsidies, which had become so [[habitual]] that removing them was treated as a betrayal rather than an economic measure. The second was the decision to [[defer]] repayment of foreign loans, a step the finance ministry could [[justify]] only by pointing to the collapse in revenue. Both measures were unpopular, and both were [[suspend|suspended]] temporarily after protests in the capital.","The cities had changed as well. [[fume|Fumes]] from unregulated traffic hung over the main roads, and minibuses were [[cram|crammed]] far beyond their legal capacity. A [[synthesis]] of several proposals — cleaner buses, higher parking charges and a new tram line — became the programme\'s most visible success, if only because it was the easiest to photograph.","Ten years on, the results are mixed. Oil still provides most of the state\'s income, and the reserves are smaller than they were. But manufacturing has doubled, and for the first time the country exports more food than it imports. The lesson, if there is one, is that diversification is not a decision but a long habit, and habits are hard to acquire late."],"exercises":[{"id":"b4u29-e1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What is the passage mainly about?","options":["How oil is extracted and refined","How a region tried to diversify after relying on a single product","Why fuel subsidies are popular with voters","The design of a new tram system"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"The passage sets out the problem created by dependence on oil and then follows the government\'s attempts to build a more varied economy."},{"id":"b4u29-e2","type":"multiple-choice","question":"Why were new rules introduced to penalize certain companies?","options":["They employed too many foreign workers","They exported raw materials without processing them at home","They refused to pay their taxes","They had taken out foreign loans"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 3 says the rules targeted companies that exported raw materials without processing them domestically, so that more of the value would stay in the country."},{"id":"b4u29-e3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What was the critics\' main objection to the plan?","options":["The targets were arbitrary and the funding far too small","Tourism would damage the coastline","The plan would raise taxes on workers","Foreign companies would take over the farms"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Paragraph 4 reports that critics called the targets arbitrary and described the money set aside as a scrap of what would be needed."},{"id":"b4u29-e4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What does the writer mean by saying that \\"diversification is not a decision but a long habit\\"?","options":["Governments should avoid making economic plans","Changing an economy requires sustained effort over many years","Habits are impossible to change in old age","Only habits, not policies, can be measured"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"The closing sentence contrasts a single decision with something built up over time, which matches the mixed results after ten years of effort."},{"id":"b4u29-e5","type":"multiple-choice","question":"As used in paragraph 4, the word \\"scrap\\" most nearly means…","options":["a very small amount","a written record","a wasted payment","an unnecessary expense"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"The critics said the money set aside was a scrap of what would be needed — a small fraction of the sum required."},{"id":"b4u29-e6","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Nothing can ___ such rude behaviour.\\"","options":["justify","defer","cram","export"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To justify something is to show that it is reasonable or necessary, so saying nothing can justify it means there is no good reason for it."},{"id":"b4u29-e7","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"They decided to ___ the meeting until the following month.\\"","options":["defer","penalize","subsist","subscribe"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To defer something is to arrange for it to happen later, which is what happens to a meeting postponed to the next month."},{"id":"b4u29-e8","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Players who arrive late for training will be ___ by the coach.\\"","options":["penalized","exported","crammed","justified"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To penalize someone is to punish them, which is what a coach does to players who turn up late."},{"id":"b4u29-e9","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Six of us were ___ into a car built for four.\\"","options":["crammed","deferred","penalized","subscribed"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To cram things into a place is to force them into a space that can barely hold them, which is what happened to six people in a small car."},{"id":"b4u29-e10","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The league ___ the player for three matches after the incident.\\"","options":["suspended","crammed","deferred","subsisted"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To suspend someone is to stop them taking part for a period, which is what a league does to a player as a punishment."}]},"30":{"id":"b4-u30","book":4,"unit":30,"title":"A Hundred Evenings","level":"B2","topic":"A physical theatre company at work","generatedAt":"2026-09-23","wordCount":453,"paragraphs":["The company occupies a converted warehouse on the edge of the city, and on a Tuesday afternoon it contains about thirty people, four of whom are hanging from the ceiling. They are preparing a show that has no dialogue at all. The director, a [[veteran]] of twenty years in physical theatre, calls it \'the simplest thing we have ever attempted and the hardest to get right\'.","The performers are an unusually mixed group. One trained as an [[acrobat]] in a circus; another spent a decade in [[mandarin|Mandarin]]-language television; a third arrived from a [[metropolitan]] dance school where the [[technical]] standards were, she says, far stricter than anything here. The company\'s [[advocacy]] of open casting has produced a troupe that looks like the city it performs in.","Rehearsals are long. The company [[rehearse|rehearses]] six hours a day for eleven weeks before a first performance, and much of that time is spent on material that will be cut. The work is [[industrious]] rather than glamorous. A single sequence may be repeated forty times until the movement becomes [[fluid]] and the group seems to breathe together. The aim is a [[communal]] rhythm — not one performer\'s skill, but a shared timing that the audience feels rather than notices.","The story is told entirely through the body. A figure [[mimic|mimics]] a bird, then a machine, then a person. The [[villain]] of the piece, if it has one, is a shape woven from [[vine|vines]] and cloth that slowly takes over the stage. There is no narration, so the audience must rely on [[sensory]] detail — the sound of feet, the smell of the smoke, the changing weight of the light.","Not everyone approves. One reviewer described the company\'s earlier work as [[misguided]], arguing that difficulty is not the same as depth, and that audiences are being [[scorn|scorned]] rather than entertained. The director takes the criticism seriously without accepting it. \'If we [[harmonize]] nothing,\' she says, \'we have failed. But if we harmonize everything too neatly, we have made a machine.\'","The company survives on a mixture of ticket sales, a small public grant, and the [[resourceful]] habits of people who are used to making props out of whatever is in the store cupboard. Under a city [[statute]] that requires any publicly funded venue to run education programmes, they also teach. The classes are [[inventive]] and loud, and half the children who attend have never been inside a theatre before.","Whether the show will be any good is not yet clear. What is clear, watching a run-through on a Tuesday afternoon, is that a hundred evenings of this kind of work have produced something rare: a group of people who can move as one without anyone counting out loud."],"exercises":[{"id":"b4u30-e1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What is the passage mainly about?","options":["How to train as a circus performer","A theatre company preparing a show without words","The funding of publicly owned venues","Why critics dislike modern dance"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"The passage visits the company during rehearsals and describes the performers, the method, the criticism it has received and its teaching work."},{"id":"b4u30-e2","type":"multiple-choice","question":"How much rehearsal does the company do before a first performance?","options":["Two hours a day for a month","Six hours a day for eleven weeks","Six days a week for a year","Four hours a day for three weeks"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 3 states that the company rehearses six hours a day for eleven weeks before a first performance."},{"id":"b4u30-e3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What did the reviewer object to?","options":["The lack of dialogue in the show","Difficulty being mistaken for depth and audiences being scorned","The company\'s use of children in its classes","The size of the public grant it receives"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 5 reports the reviewer\'s argument that difficulty is not the same as depth and that audiences are being scorned rather than entertained."},{"id":"b4u30-e4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What does the director mean by saying that harmonizing everything too neatly would make \\"a machine\\"?","options":["The company would need more technical equipment","Perfect smoothness would lose the human quality of the work","Rehearsals would have to become shorter","The performers would all have to be replaced"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"She sets harmony against mechanical perfection: a piece that fits together too neatly would stop being human, which is why she wants some friction left in."},{"id":"b4u30-e5","type":"multiple-choice","question":"As used in paragraph 3, the word \\"industrious\\" most nearly means…","options":["hard-working","creative","expensive","competitive"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"The writer contrasts industrious work with glamour, describing long repetition and effort rather than creative inspiration."},{"id":"b4u30-e6","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Parrots can ___ human speech surprisingly accurately.\\"","options":["mimic","rehearse","scorn","harmonize"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To mimic something is to copy the way it sounds, which is what parrots do with human speech."},{"id":"b4u30-e7","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Her Spanish is so ___ that listeners take her for a native speaker.\\"","options":["fluid","technical","communal","misguided"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"If speech is fluid it is smooth and graceful, which is why listeners mistake her for a native speaker."},{"id":"b4u30-e8","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The children were ___ , building an entire castle out of cardboard boxes.\\"","options":["inventive","misguided","metropolitan","sensory"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Someone inventive is good at creating new things, which describes children turning boxes into a castle."},{"id":"b4u30-e9","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"She was ___ enough to repair the engine with a piece of wire.\\"","options":["resourceful","communal","sensory","misguided"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"A resourceful person is clever at using whatever is available, which is exactly what repairing an engine with wire requires."},{"id":"b4u30-e10","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"He is a ___ of three previous championships and knows every course.\\"","options":["veteran","villain","acrobat","statute"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"A veteran is someone with a great deal of experience in something, which fits a competitor who has taken part in three championships."}]}}'),h=JSON.parse('{"1":{"id":"b5-u1","book":5,"unit":1,"title":"The Bitter Drink That Conquered the World","level":"C1","topic":"The history of chocolate","generatedAt":"2026-09-23","wordCount":471,"paragraphs":["For most of its history, chocolate was not a sweet bar but a bitter drink. The story begins in the [[humid]] forests of Central America, where the cacao tree grows in the shade of taller trees. Its pods hang directly from the trunk in heavy [[cluster|clusters]], and inside each pod lie the seeds that would one day travel the world.","The peoples who first [[cultivate|cultivated]] cacao did not treat it as a snack. They ground the roasted seeds into a paste, mixed it with water, chilli and spices, and drank the result from tall vessels. The [[beverage]] was thick, bitter and often foamy, and it was usually drunk cold. The land that produced it was a kind of [[paradise]] — warm rain, deep shade, and fruit in every season — and because the drink itself was believed to come from the gods, it was reserved for priests, nobles and warriors. It was a [[divine]] gift rather than an everyday pleasure.","Preparing it was slow work. The seeds had to be fermented, dried, roasted and ground, and the [[texture]] of the final paste depended on how finely they were crushed. Skilled cooks could [[vary]] the flavour by adding vanilla, honey or flowers, and every household had its own recipe. The [[aroma]] that rose from a bowl of freshly ground cacao was unlike anything else in the market: rich, earthy, and faintly like the smell of damp soil. Not everyone enjoyed it. Some visitors complained of a strong [[odor]] they could not name, and a few found the taste so unfamiliar that they refused a second cup.","What those visitors noticed most, however, was the [[rate]] at which the drink seemed to restore energy. A single cup could keep a person walking for hours, and travellers began to carry [[condensed]] cakes of cacao with them, dissolving them in hot water when they needed strength. Back home, cooks [[combine|combined]] the paste with sugar and milk, and the bitter drink slowly became sweeter. The change was not [[rapid]] — it took more than a century — but it was complete.","Meanwhile, demand for the seeds reshaped whole landscapes. Forests were cleared to plant cacao in straight rows, and vast [[plantation|plantations]] spread across the tropics wherever the climate was warm and [[humid]] enough. The work was hard and often dangerous, and the profits were enormous. Today cacao is grown on three continents, and the drink that once belonged to the gods is sold in every corner shop.","[[contemporary|Contemporary]] chocolate comes in dozens of forms, from dark bars to [[soothing]] hot drinks, and chemists continue to study its [[subtle]] effects on mood and on the [[palate]]. One warning, though, has survived from the earliest accounts: the raw seeds are [[toxic]] to many animals, and to people in large quantities. The pleasure has always come with a price."],"exercises":[{"id":"b5u1-e1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What is the passage mainly about?","options":["How cacao is grown on modern plantations","The history of chocolate, from a bitter sacred drink to a sweet product","Why chocolate is dangerous for animals","The work of chemists who study flavour"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"The passage traces chocolate from its origins as a bitter drink in Central America through cultivation, preparation and trade to the sweet products sold today."},{"id":"b5u1-e2","type":"multiple-choice","question":"According to the passage, how was cacao originally drunk?","options":["Hot, with sugar and milk","Cold, thick and bitter","Warm, mixed with honey only","Diluted with fruit juice"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 2 describes the beverage as thick, bitter and often foamy, and says it was usually drunk cold."},{"id":"b5u1-e3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"Why did travellers begin to carry condensed cakes of cacao?","options":["They were easier to sell in markets","They could be dissolved in hot water when energy was needed","They tasted sweeter than the fresh paste","They were the only form that could be transported"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 4 says travellers carried condensed cakes and dissolved them in hot water when they needed strength."},{"id":"b5u1-e4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What can be inferred about chocolate in Europe from paragraph 4?","options":["It was immediately popular with everyone","Its taste changed gradually as sugar and milk were added","It was banned for more than a century","It was drunk only by priests and nobles"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"The passage says the change was not rapid — it took more than a century — and that cooks combined the paste with sugar and milk, so the flavour altered gradually."},{"id":"b5u1-e5","type":"multiple-choice","question":"As used in paragraph 6, the word \\"subtle\\" most nearly means…","options":["strong and easy to notice","not easy to notice","harmful in small amounts","lasting only a short time"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"The passage uses subtle for effects on mood that are slight and not obvious, which matches \'not easy to notice\'."},{"id":"b5u1-e6","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The ___ of fresh bread filled the whole street.\\"","options":["aroma","rate","palate","texture"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"An aroma is a scent or smell, which is exactly what fills a street when bread is baking."},{"id":"b5u1-e7","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Farmers in the valley ___ rice on terraces that have been used for centuries.\\"","options":["vary","combine","cultivate","condense"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"To cultivate plants is to care for them and help them grow, which is what farmers do with a rice crop."},{"id":"b5u1-e8","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The ___ spread of the infection surprised even the doctors.\\"","options":["subtle","humid","divine","rapid"],"answerIndex":3,"explanation":"Rapid means happening very quickly, which fits a spread that surprises even the doctors."},{"id":"b5u1-e9","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Some of the berries in that hedge are ___, so keep the children away from them.\\"","options":["soothing","toxic","condensed","contemporary"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Toxic means poisonous and very dangerous, which is why children should be kept away."},{"id":"b5u1-e10","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The label says the ___ should be served cold and shaken first.\\"","options":["odor","plantation","palate","beverage"],"answerIndex":3,"explanation":"A beverage is a drink, and a drink is the kind of thing that is served cold and shaken."}]},"2":{"id":"b5-u2","book":5,"unit":2,"title":"The Long Way Home","level":"B2","topic":"Survival at sea","generatedAt":"2026-09-23","wordCount":410,"paragraphs":["The [[steward]] had warned us that the crossing would be rough, and by the second night we understood what he meant. A fire had started somewhere below deck — a lamp knocked over, someone said, though nobody was sure. The crew shouted, the passengers crowded the rail, and within minutes the order came to abandon the ship. I have never been so [[horrified]] in my life.","There were nine of us in a boat built for six. We had a single [[paddle]], a coil of [[string]], and two tins of [[kerosene]] that nobody wanted to carry. The sea was [[fiery]] orange where the ship was burning, and we rowed until our arms ached, watching the flames sink lower until there was nothing left but a dark shape on the water. By morning the [[wreck]] had gone entirely.","We drifted for four days. Nobody spoke of [[escape]], because there was nowhere to escape to. The sun burned the [[flesh]] of our arms until it blistered. On the second morning a [[grapefruit]] floated past, and we shared it, [[sour]] and warm, in eight careful pieces. It was the best thing I have ever tasted.","What kept us going was the [[character]] of the two sailors among us. One, an old man who had served under an [[admiral]] in his youth, took charge without ever raising his voice. He marked our position each noon by the [[arc]] of the sun, and every evening he tied a [[loop]] in the string to count the days. He told stories to keep our spirits up, though I think he was as frightened as the rest of us.","On the fourth night we saw lights. We had no way of reaching them, and I lay in the [[middle]] of the boat, half asleep, listening to the water. My [[conscience]] would not let me rest: I had pushed past an old woman to get into that boat, and I knew it. Whatever happened next, I would have to live with that.","At dawn we were found. A fishing boat came alongside and the men aboard threw us a rope. Later I learned that we had drifted past a low island where farmers grew [[hay]] and kept cattle behind fences of wooden [[stake|stakes]], and that a single line of [[thorn]] bushes had been planted along the shore. It was those dark bushes that someone on the beach noticed first. We were lucky in a way I still cannot explain."],"exercises":[{"id":"b5u2-e1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What is the passage mainly about?","options":["How a ship was repaired after a fire","A survivor\'s account of several days adrift after a ship fire","The training of sailors in the nineteenth century","Why grapefruit is a useful food at sea"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"The narrator describes escaping a burning ship and drifting in an open boat for four days before being rescued."},{"id":"b5u2-e2","type":"multiple-choice","question":"Why did the passengers leave the ship?","options":["The ship had struck rocks near an island","A fire had started below deck","The engines had failed in a storm","The captain had ordered a practice drill"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 1 says a fire had started somewhere below deck and that the order came to abandon the ship."},{"id":"b5u2-e3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What did the old sailor do every evening?","options":["He sang to keep the others calm","He tied a loop in the string to count the days","He wrote down their position in a notebook","He divided the water into equal shares"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 4 states that each evening he tied a loop in the string to count the days."},{"id":"b5u2-e4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"Why does the narrator mention the old woman in paragraph 5?","options":["To explain how the boat was loaded","To show that he feels guilty about how he got into the boat","To praise the behaviour of the other passengers","To describe a person who was later rescued"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"He says his conscience would not let him rest because he had pushed past an old woman to get into the boat."},{"id":"b5u2-e5","type":"multiple-choice","question":"As used in paragraph 2, the word \\"fiery\\" most nearly means…","options":["burning strongly","reflecting light","difficult to look at","changing colour quickly"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"The sea is described as fiery orange where the ship was burning, so the word means burning strongly."},{"id":"b5u2-e6","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The milk has gone ___, so we should throw it away.\\"","options":["sour","middle","hay","string"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Sour describes a sharp, unpleasant taste, which is what milk develops when it is no longer fresh."},{"id":"b5u2-e7","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The farmer stored the ___ in the barn to feed the horses through the winter.\\"","options":["stake","hay","thorn","loop"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Hay is dry grass used to feed animals, which is why it is stored in a barn for winter."},{"id":"b5u2-e8","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"A ___ brought us blankets and a menu shortly after take-off.\\"","options":["steward","admiral","paddle","stake"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"A steward is a person who serves food and looks after passengers on planes and ships."},{"id":"b5u2-e9","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"He could not lie about it; his ___ would not allow him to.\\"","options":["character","conscience","escape","flesh"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Your conscience is your inner sense of right and wrong, which is what stops a person from lying."},{"id":"b5u2-e10","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"She tied the rope in a ___ so that it would not slip through the ring.\\"","options":["arc","thorn","loop","wreck"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"A loop is a line made into the shape of a circle, and a rope tied in a loop will not slip through a ring."}]},"3":{"id":"b5-u3","book":5,"unit":3,"title":"The Boy Who Talked Too Much","level":"B2","topic":"A fable about humility","generatedAt":"2026-09-23","wordCount":427,"paragraphs":["In a village at the foot of an [[immense]] mountain there lived a boy named Cato who could not stop talking about himself. He [[brag|bragged]] about his running, his memory and his skill with a sling; he bragged about things he had never done at all. The villagers, who had known him since he was a baby, mostly smiled and said nothing.","One autumn an old woman came down from the mountain and stayed for the winter. She had a quiet way of speaking that made people listen, and she never once raised her voice. When Cato began his usual performance at her table, she did not argue with him. She simply asked, in a tone barely [[audible]] above the fire, whether he had ever climbed to the top of the mountain. He had not.","\\"Then you have no idea what you are talking about,\\" she said. It was an [[indirect]] way of [[admonish|admonishing]] him, but he understood it perfectly, and for the first time in his life he felt the [[hint]] of shame. He [[disagree|disagreed]] with her, of course — loudly, at length, and with the confidence of an [[idiot]]. She let him finish. Then she said: \\"[[beware|Beware]] of the man who has no doubts. He is usually wrong.\\"","He decided to prove her wrong. Before dawn he set off, and the climb [[torment|tormented]] him for six hours: loose stone, thin air, a wind that [[switch|switched]] direction without warning. Twice he was [[conscious]] of nothing but his own breathing. At the summit, though, the view was [[awesome]] — a sea of cloud, and far below, the roofs of the village. He could [[pinpoint]] his own house by the yellow woodpile beside it.","Coming down, he met a young shepherd who had lost a goat. Cato gave him a [[thorough]] description of the path he had taken, the exact place where the animal had probably gone, and the two [[option|options]] for getting it back. The shepherd found the goat before dark. \\"How did you know?\\" he asked. Cato, for once, said nothing.","The [[eventual]] result of that day was not what Cato had expected. He had set out to silence an old woman and came back with a [[pastime]] he loved, and with a clearer sense of what he owed other people. He still talked too much — the [[echo]] of his own voice was, and remained, his favourite sound — but he no longer talked about himself. He talked about the mountain. Which, he would tell you, is the only subject that comes close to [[perfect]]."],"exercises":[{"id":"b5u3-e1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What is the passage mainly about?","options":["A boy who learns something about himself after climbing a mountain","The geography of a remote mountain range","A shepherd who loses a goat in bad weather","Why old people are wiser than young people"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"The story follows a boastful boy who climbs a mountain to prove an old woman wrong and comes back changed."},{"id":"b5u3-e2","type":"multiple-choice","question":"Why did Cato decide to climb the mountain?","options":["He wanted to find the shepherd\'s lost goat","He wanted to prove that the old woman was wrong","The villagers had asked him to fetch water","He was looking for a good place to practise with his sling"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 4 begins \'He decided to prove her wrong\', and the climb follows immediately."},{"id":"b5u3-e3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"How did the old woman respond to Cato\'s boasting?","options":["She argued with him in front of the villagers","She asked him a quiet question instead of arguing","She refused to speak to him at all","She praised his running and his memory"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 2 says she did not argue but simply asked, in a barely audible tone, whether he had ever climbed the mountain."},{"id":"b5u3-e4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What does the shepherd\'s question at the end of paragraph 5 suggest?","options":["That Cato had finally learned to tell the truth","That Cato\'s careful knowledge, not his boasting, was what helped","That the shepherd had already found the goat","That Cato was a better climber than the villagers thought"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"The shepherd is impressed because Cato\'s precise description allowed him to find the goat, which shows the value of observation rather than self-praise."},{"id":"b5u3-e5","type":"multiple-choice","question":"As used in paragraph 3, the word \\"indirect\\" most nearly means…","options":["not the straightest or easiest way of doing something","rude and unfriendly","difficult to understand","intended to cause offence"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"She admonishes him without saying so openly, so the criticism is indirect rather than direct."},{"id":"b5u3-e6","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The constant noise from the building site began to ___ the people living nearby.\\"","options":["admonish","pinpoint","torment","switch"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"To torment someone is to cause them to suffer, which is what constant noise does to the people living beside it."},{"id":"b5u3-e7","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Her whisper was barely ___ in the crowded hall.\\"","options":["awesome","audible","conscious","thorough"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"If something is audible it can be heard, and \'barely audible\' means it could hardly be heard at all."},{"id":"b5u3-e8","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Fishing is his favourite ___; he goes out on the lake every weekend.\\"","options":["option","hint","pastime","echo"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"A pastime is an activity you do often for fun, which fits a weekend hobby."},{"id":"b5u3-e9","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"An ___ wave swept the small boat towards the shore.\\"","options":["immense","indirect","eventual","perfect"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Immense means very large, and only a very large wave would sweep a boat towards the shore."},{"id":"b5u3-e10","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The teacher gave us a ___ about the answer without telling us directly.\\"","options":["hint","idiot","brag","disagree"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"A hint is information that suggests something without stating it, which matches \'without telling us directly\'."}]},"4":{"id":"b5-u4","book":5,"unit":4,"title":"The Emperor Who Sang","level":"C1","topic":"A fictional ruler and his reputation","generatedAt":"2026-09-23","wordCount":476,"paragraphs":["He came to power in an unusual way. The old emperor had no heir, and after his death the council could reach no [[consensus]] on a successor. For three weeks the empire drifted while the great families argued in the [[forum]] and the army waited. Then a young officer walked into the hall, sat down, and began to sing — quietly, and quite beautifully. The room fell silent. By evening the council had chosen him.","His name was Ivar, and he was twenty-six. Some historians describe the events of that week as a [[coup]], others as an accident, and a few as a [[conspiracy]] between the army and the merchants. Whatever the truth, he was crowned in a small [[chapel]] outside the walls the following morning, before the frost had melted, wearing a crown that was far too large for him.","He was, by all accounts, a strange ruler. He had a long grey [[beard]] which he refused to cut, and he kept a collection of animals, including one enormous [[beast]] from the southern forests that terrified visitors. He sang most days, sometimes for hours, and he had the habit of breaking into [[melody]] in the middle of serious discussions, which his advisers found either charming or infuriating.","His enemies were many. A [[commentator]] of that era wrote that the court was a nest of cynics, and he was not far wrong: several of the great families were openly [[cynical]] about a singer on the throne. In his fourth year an [[assassin]] was arrested in the palace gardens with a knife. The arrest [[inflame|inflamed]] opinion in the city, and crowds gathered outside the palace for three days. The emperor\'s response was characteristically odd — he invited the man to dinner, listened to his complaints, and let him go.","The empire prospered. He built a great library with a [[dome]] of blue tiles that could be seen from the hills, and he paid for lamps to [[illuminate]] the streets at night, so that citizens could walk home safely. He lowered taxes on grain and raised them on luxuries, and although the wealthy [[harass|harassed]] him constantly about this, he never changed his mind. Foreign visitors [[exclaim|exclaimed]] at the [[glamour]] of the court, at the music, at the gardens.","What destroyed him in the end was not a conspiracy but a piece of news. In his final year a message arrived from the eastern provinces, and whatever it said, his reaction was [[abrupt]] and complete: he stopped singing. He withdrew to the chapel, refused food, and was said to be in [[agony]] for weeks. He died in the spring, still young. Some said he had been [[con|conned]] by a courtier he trusted; others that he had simply been misinformed. The truth was never established, and perhaps it did not matter. What people remembered, in the end, was the voice."],"exercises":[{"id":"b5u4-e1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What is the passage mainly about?","options":["How an unlikely singer became ruler and what he was remembered for","The construction of a famous library","The history of a royal family over several centuries","Why rulers should not keep wild animals"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"The passage describes how Ivar came to power, how he ruled, and what people remembered about him after his death."},{"id":"b5u4-e2","type":"multiple-choice","question":"How was Ivar chosen as ruler?","options":["He inherited the throne from his father","The council chose him after he sang in front of them","He won a battle against the great families","The merchants elected him in the forum"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 1 describes how he walked into the hall, sang, and was chosen by the council by evening."},{"id":"b5u4-e3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What did the emperor do when the assassin was arrested?","options":["He had him executed in the palace gardens","He invited him to dinner and then released him","He sent him to the eastern provinces","He asked the council to decide his punishment"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 4 says he invited the man to dinner, listened to his complaints, and let him go."},{"id":"b5u4-e4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What does the final paragraph suggest about how Ivar is remembered?","options":["For the mystery of how he came to power","For his singing rather than for his achievements as a ruler","For the cruelty of his later years","For the library he built"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"The passage lists several possible explanations for his decline and then concludes that what people remembered was the voice."},{"id":"b5u4-e5","type":"multiple-choice","question":"As used in paragraph 1, the word \\"consensus\\" most nearly means…","options":["a general agreement among a group","a formal vote","a heated argument","a secret decision"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"The council could not agree on a successor, so consensus here means general agreement."},{"id":"b5u4-e6","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Candles were lit to ___ the ancient hall for the evening concert.\\"","options":["illuminate","harass","inflame","exclaim"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To illuminate something is to shine light on it, which is what candles do in a dark hall."},{"id":"b5u4-e7","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The police believed the ___ had entered the building through a side door.\\"","options":["beast","assassin","commentator","chapel"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"An assassin is someone who murders an important person, and such a person would enter secretly to avoid being seen."},{"id":"b5u4-e8","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The film captures the ___ of the 1920s, with its bright cars and crowded dance halls.\\"","options":["agony","consensus","glamour","beard"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"Glamour is a quality relating to riches, wealth and beauty, which matches bright cars and fashionable dance halls."},{"id":"b5u4-e9","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"She is too ___ to believe that the offer was made out of kindness.\\"","options":["abrupt","cynical","melody","dome"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"A cynical person is unwilling to believe that others have good intentions, which is exactly what the sentence describes."},{"id":"b5u4-e10","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The cathedral\'s great ___ is visible from every part of the city.\\"","options":["forum","dome","coup","beast"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"A dome is a curved roof, and a large curved roof on a cathedral would be visible from a distance."}]},"5":{"id":"b5-u5","book":5,"unit":5,"title":"Notes from a First Semester","level":"B2","topic":"Student life and study habits","generatedAt":"2026-09-23","wordCount":504,"paragraphs":["September. I made a [[resolution]] this month, and I am writing it down so that I cannot pretend I never did. This [[semester]] I will not leave everything to the last week. I will go to every [[lecture]]. I will read the books on the list instead of looking them up in [[cyberspace]] at midnight.","October. The library [[operate|operates]] on rules I still do not understand. There is a [[private]] study room on the fourth floor, and you have to book it with a card, and if you are one minute late it is given away. I sat there [[awhile]] on Tuesday, trying to [[edit]] the same paragraph, and achieved nothing. The older shelves still hold [[typewritten]] catalogues from the 1970s, bound in grey card. The [[gymnasium]] is next door, which is either a temptation or a reward depending on my mood.","November. We were given our first [[essay]] back today. The tutor had used a yellow pen to [[highlight]] almost every line of my introduction, and at the bottom she had written: \'You are not [[ignorant]] of the subject. You are ignorant of how to argue about it.\' I read the comment four times. Then I read the [[index]] of the book she recommended and understood what she meant. A good essay is not a list of facts; it is an argument, and every fact has to earn its place.","November, later. I [[faint|fainted]] in the second week of the month — nothing dramatic, just too little sleep and too much coffee — and woke up on the floor of the corridor with a crowd of people looking down at me. The doctor asked a series of [[weird]] questions and then told me, quite kindly, that a [[recent]] study had found students who skip breakfast are three times more likely to do exactly what I had done. I have eaten breakfast every day since.","December. Today we had a lecture on [[moral]] philosophy, and the lecturer asked us to [[evaluate]] a simple case: a man finds a wallet in the street. We spent ninety minutes on it. The strange thing is that by the end most of us had changed our minds about something. The course is [[global]] in its scope — we have read texts from four continents — and yet the questions are always the same. What should I do? What kind of person do I want to be?","January. The exams are over. My resolution did not survive November, and the moral of this diary is probably that resolutions are easy to make in September and hard to keep in December. But something has changed. I no longer read for the exam. I read because I want to know what happens next. I have started writing my notes by hand instead of typing them, and when I read them back I can hear my own voice. Next [[semester]] I will make the same [[resolution]]. I will probably break it again. That, I am beginning to think, is not the point."],"exercises":[{"id":"b5u5-e1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What is the passage mainly about?","options":["A student\'s diary of her first term and how her attitude changed","The rules of a university library","Why students should eat breakfast","A lecture on moral philosophy"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"The passage is a series of dated diary entries covering one term and ending with a changed attitude to study."},{"id":"b5u5-e2","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What did the tutor write at the bottom of the essay?","options":["That the essay was the best in the class","That the student was ignorant of the subject","That the student knew the subject but not how to argue about it","That the essay should be rewritten by hand"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"The comment was: \'You are not ignorant of the subject. You are ignorant of how to argue about it.\'"},{"id":"b5u5-e3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"Why did the narrator faint?","options":["She had too little sleep and too much coffee","She had been ill for several weeks","She had missed breakfast for a month","She had been working in the gymnasium"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"She describes it as \'nothing dramatic, just too little sleep and too much coffee\'."},{"id":"b5u5-e4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What does the last entry suggest about the narrator?","options":["She has decided to stop making resolutions","Her reading has changed even though she broke her resolution","She now studies only for exams","She regrets having chosen the course"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"She admits the resolution failed but says she now reads because she wants to know what happens next, so her attitude has changed regardless."},{"id":"b5u5-e5","type":"multiple-choice","question":"As used in paragraph 3, the word \\"ignorant\\" most nearly means…","options":["lacking knowledge","unwilling to learn","badly organised","easily confused"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"The tutor means the student has knowledge of the subject but lacks knowledge of how to argue, so ignorant means lacking knowledge."},{"id":"b5u5-e6","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The editor asked her to ___ the final chapter before it went to print.\\"","options":["edit","evaluate","highlight","faint"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To edit is to correct a piece of writing so that it is suitable to be published, which is what an editor asks for."},{"id":"b5u5-e7","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The garden is ___; only the family who live there may use it.\\"","options":["global","private","recent","moral"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"If something is private it is used by only one person or group, which matches a garden reserved for one family."},{"id":"b5u5-e8","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Before we buy the house, we should ___ how much the repairs will cost.\\"","options":["faint","lecture","evaluate","edit"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"To evaluate something is to study it so that a decision can be made, which is what happens before deciding to buy."},{"id":"b5u5-e9","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Climate change is a ___ problem that no single country can solve alone.\\"","options":["private","recent","moral","global"],"answerIndex":3,"explanation":"Global means happening all around the world, which is why no single country can solve it alone."},{"id":"b5u5-e10","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"It was a ___ feeling to walk into the wrong classroom and see thirty strangers.\\"","options":["weird","typewritten","ignorant","awhile"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Weird means very strange, which fits the uncomfortable experience of entering the wrong room."}]},"6":{"id":"b5-u6","book":5,"unit":6,"title":"What the Chimpanzees Taught Us","level":"C1","topic":"Animal behaviour research","generatedAt":"2026-09-23","wordCount":462,"paragraphs":["For nearly twenty years a research station in the [[jungle]] has recorded the daily life of a community of chimpanzees, and the results have changed how scientists think about animal minds. The work is slow and unglamorous. It depends on [[empirical]] evidence gathered hour by hour, and on the patience to sit still while nothing at all happens.","The first task was simply to [[accustom]] the animals to human presence. Chimpanzees are highly [[intelligent]] and extremely cautious, and a single careless movement can send the whole group into the trees. Researchers therefore spent months doing nothing, allowing the animals to approach on their own terms. [[compliance|Compliance]] with a strict set of field rules — no feeding, no touching, no following — is a condition of the permit, and those rules protect the animals as much as the scientists.","Chimpanzee society is political. Each community has an [[alpha]] male at the top, and his position is never entirely safe. Alliances form and break; two males may [[clash]] loudly for an hour and then sit side by side. When a group is threatened, its members [[congregate]] quickly, calling and embracing; when the danger passes, they [[disperse]] again into the forest to feed, often alone. Females with young tend to stay apart, and a young animal will [[cling]] to its mother\'s back for years, long after it can walk.","The question that most interests the researchers is whether chimpanzees can [[comprehend]] the intentions of others. The evidence is suggestive rather than conclusive. A male who wants support may [[arouse]] the interest of a potential ally by grooming him first, and an [[articulate]] gesture — an outstretched hand, a particular expression — can carry a great deal of meaning. Some animals clearly understand what others know and do not know, an ability that was once thought to belong only to humans.","Not everything observed fits the old [[classification]] of animals as either social or solitary. Chimpanzees are neither. They hunt together and share meat, yet they also compete for food, and a female may leave the group for weeks after a quarrel over [[custody]] of her own offspring. In the dry season, when other animals [[flock]] to the remaining waterholes and [[graze]] on the last green grass, the chimpanzees simply move on.","The work has also raised uncomfortable questions. When researchers describe the [[exclusion]] of a particular individual from the group, or the way a mother protects her young, they are using words that were once reserved for human behaviour. The animals [[embody]] a kind of social intelligence that is recognisably like our own, and yet is not our own. Whether that is a matter for celebration or for caution is still debated; the [[buzz]] in the field suggests that the next decade will settle it."],"exercises":[{"id":"b5u6-e1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What is the passage mainly about?","options":["How to obtain a permit for field research","What long-term observation of chimpanzees has revealed about their social lives","The diet of animals living in tropical forests","Why chimpanzees are difficult to photograph"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"The passage reports what researchers have learned about chimpanzee politics, alliances, communication and social behaviour."},{"id":"b5u6-e2","type":"multiple-choice","question":"Why did the researchers spend months doing nothing?","options":["They were waiting for permission to enter the forest","They wanted the animals to get used to their presence","They had not yet decided what to study","The chimpanzees were away in another part of the forest"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 2 says the first task was to accustom the animals to human presence and that researchers allowed them to approach on their own terms."},{"id":"b5u6-e3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What do chimpanzees do when a group is threatened?","options":["They scatter into the trees immediately","They gather together, calling and embracing","They send the alpha male forward alone","They fall silent until the danger passes"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 3 says that when a group is threatened its members congregate quickly, calling and embracing."},{"id":"b5u6-e4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What does the phrase \'recognisably like our own, and yet is not our own\' suggest?","options":["That chimpanzees are simply less intelligent than humans","That their social intelligence resembles ours but remains distinct","That researchers have misunderstood the evidence","That the animals have learned behaviour from humans"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"The phrase points to a similarity that is real but not an identity — the animals\' social intelligence is like ours without being the same."},{"id":"b5u6-e5","type":"multiple-choice","question":"As used in paragraph 5, the word \\"custody\\" most nearly means…","options":["the right to take care of someone","a period of separation","a share of food","a place of safety"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"The sentence describes a quarrel over custody of her own offspring, so custody means the right to take care of someone."},{"id":"b5u6-e6","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"After the ceremony the crowd began to ___ in every direction.\\"","options":["congregate","disperse","clash","cling"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"To disperse means to scatter everywhere, which is what a crowd does when an event ends."},{"id":"b5u6-e7","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"In summer the sheep ___ on the hillside above the village.\\"","options":["graze","flock","buzz","comprehend"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"When animals graze they feed on plants, which is what sheep do on a hillside."},{"id":"b5u6-e8","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"She is a remarkably ___ speaker who can explain difficult ideas in a few words.\\"","options":["empirical","alpha","articulate","jungle"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"An articulate person can express ideas clearly and effectively, which matches a speaker who explains difficult ideas briefly."},{"id":"b5u6-e9","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"It takes a few weeks to ___ yourself to a new climate.\\"","options":["accustom","disperse","embody","clash"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To accustom oneself to something is to get used to it, which is what happens over a few weeks in a new climate."},{"id":"b5u6-e10","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"In a wolf pack, the ___ male usually feeds first.\\"","options":["custody","alpha","buzz","exclusion"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"The alpha member of a group is the most dominant, so the alpha male would be the first to feed."}]},"7":{"id":"b5-u7","book":5,"unit":7,"title":"The Mill That Would Not Fall","level":"B2","topic":"A community campaign to save a building","generatedAt":"2026-09-23","wordCount":476,"paragraphs":["When the old mill on the river was finally demolished, most people in the town thought nothing of it. It had stood empty for twenty years, its roof half collapsed, and the land was said to be worth a great deal of money. Only the [[architect]] who had grown up beside it seemed to care, and for a long time nobody listened to her.","Her name was Ines, and she had one powerful argument: the mill was not simply a building. It was the last [[landmark]] on that stretch of the river, the only structure that older residents used to explain where they lived. The riverbank was also a [[habitat]] for otters and kingfishers, which she mentioned at every meeting. Her proposal was modest — keep the walls, build the new housing inside them — but it required someone to [[intervene]], and by the time the council met, the machines had already begun work.","What followed was, by local standards, a [[memorable]] few months. Ines wrote to the regional authority, and when that failed she began to investigate the paperwork. It took her a year, and it [[oblige|obliged]] her to open a separate bank [[account]] for the campaign, but she found something: the land had been sold twice, and the second sale was not valid. The [[deed]] was missing a signature. There was, in short, a [[legal]] problem with the whole project.","The council\'s response was to [[conceal]] the difficulty for as long as possible. No one would confirm anything, and two sets of minutes vanished from the archive. But a local journalist had been at the meetings, and she became the most important [[witness]] in what followed. Her notes, written in pencil in a small notebook, turned out to be more reliable than the official record. No one was ever charged with a [[crime]].","The case went to a tribunal. The [[offense]] was not dramatic — a missing signature, a carelessly filed document — but the effect was the same as a severe [[sentence]]: the project stopped. The developer [[proclaim|proclaimed]] the decision a disaster for the local economy, and predicted that nothing would ever be built there again.","What happened next was the part nobody had predicted. [[volunteer|Volunteers]] began arriving at weekends to clear the rubble; a [[rally]] on the riverbank drew four hundred people; and a retired engineer offered his time for free. Money, always the scarcest [[resource]], came in small amounts from hundreds of households. It took six years to [[resolve]] the legal questions and eight to finish the building. Ines, who had never wanted to be anyone\'s hero, was obliged to make a short speech at the opening. She thanked the town for its [[gratitude]], and then said the only thing she really believed: that a place is not the buildings in it, but the people who refuse to let it go."],"exercises":[{"id":"b5u7-e1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What is the passage mainly about?","options":["How a riverside mill was demolished and replaced by flats","How one resident saved a historic mill through research and community effort","The work of a local journalist in a small town","Why old buildings are expensive to repair"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"The passage follows Ines\'s campaign, from her first objections to the completed building eight years later."},{"id":"b5u7-e2","type":"multiple-choice","question":"Why was the second sale of the land not valid?","options":["The buyer had not paid the full price","The deed was missing a signature","The council had never approved it","The land belonged to the regional authority"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 3 says the deed was missing a signature, which created a legal problem with the whole project."},{"id":"b5u7-e3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What part did the local journalist play?","options":["She worked for the developer","She became the most important witness, with notes more reliable than the official record","She led the campaign against Ines","She wrote the missing signature into the deed"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 4 says she had been at the meetings and her notes turned out to be more reliable than the official record."},{"id":"b5u7-e4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What does Ines\'s final statement suggest about her view of the town?","options":["That its value lies in the commitment of its people rather than its buildings","That the new building is better than the old mill","That she regrets having spent eight years on the project","That the council should have acted more quickly"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"She says that a place is not the buildings in it, but the people who refuse to let it go."},{"id":"b5u7-e5","type":"multiple-choice","question":"As used in paragraph 3, the word \\"obliged\\" most nearly means…","options":["required","thankful","forbidden","encouraged"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"The campaign made it necessary for her to open a separate bank account, so obliged means required."},{"id":"b5u7-e6","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"She tried to ___ her disappointment behind a cheerful smile.\\"","options":["conceal","proclaim","intervene","resolve"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To conceal something is to hide it, which is what a cheerful smile does with disappointment."},{"id":"b5u7-e7","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The clock tower is the best-known ___ in the old quarter.\\"","options":["habitat","landmark","deed","rally"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"A landmark is an object that helps people find or remember a location, which is exactly what a well-known clock tower is."},{"id":"b5u7-e8","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The two neighbours finally met to ___ their disagreement about the fence.\\"","options":["conceal","oblige","volunteer","resolve"],"answerIndex":3,"explanation":"To resolve something means to find a solution, which is what the neighbours are trying to do about the fence."},{"id":"b5u7-e9","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"He sent a short letter to express his ___ for all their help.\\"","options":["gratitude","sentence","offense","witness"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Gratitude is a feeling of being thankful, which is what a letter of thanks expresses."},{"id":"b5u7-e10","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Several parents ___ to run the school library on Fridays.\\"","options":["conceal","volunteer","intervene","proclaim"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"To volunteer is to offer to do something for free, which matches parents running the library without payment."}]},"8":{"id":"b5-u8","book":5,"unit":8,"title":"The Player That Would Not Stop","level":"B2","topic":"A personal memory of an old music player","generatedAt":"2026-09-23","wordCount":496,"paragraphs":["My grandfather gave me the music player when I was twelve, and for the next four years it never left my pocket. It was a small grey [[device]], heavier than a phone, with a screen the size of a stamp and a [[crack]] across one corner that I had made within a week of getting it. He had bought it second-hand, and it came with a leather case that [[enclose|enclosed]] it completely.","It had no [[access]] to the internet and no games [[whatsoever]]; the only thing it did was play music. That was the point. I listened to the same forty songs in the same order, always in the same [[mode]] — shuffle off, volume fixed at eleven — and I did not care that the rest of the world had moved on.","I [[grip|gripped]] it [[constant|constantly]] — while walking to school, while waiting for the bus, while pretending to do homework. My [[conduct]] at home was, in retrospect, not good. My mother would ask a question and I would not hear it, because I had the headphones on, and she would have to come and stand in front of me. She used to say that the music had more [[influence]] on my behaviour than she did, and she was not entirely joking.","The trouble began when I was sixteen. The battery stopped holding its charge, and the player would [[halt]] in the middle of a song. The screen flickered. One evening the case [[snap|snapped]] shut on my fingers and the crack widened from a line into a shape like a river. I began to [[perspire]] every time I turned it on, convinced it was about to die.","I looked for a [[replace|replacement]]. The company had stopped making them years earlier, and the [[version|versions]] sold online were either broken or absurdly expensive. The only [[valid]] explanation, according to the forums I read at midnight, was that the battery had finally given up. There was an [[impending]] problem I could not ignore: the device had become part of how I thought, and I had no idea what I would do without it. My grandfather, when I complained, was unsympathetic. \'It is a machine,\' he said. \'Machines stop. That is the [[law]].\'","The last thing it did was [[sly]] and rather beautiful. On a Tuesday in March, during a song I had heard perhaps a thousand times, it stopped in the middle of a note and never started again. I still have it. It sits on a shelf in my room, and every so often I pick it up and press the button, just to check. There is a version of this story in which I learned something important about the difference between a thing and what it holds. I [[tend]] to think that version is a little too neat. But the player is still on the shelf, and I am still listening to those forty songs, and I would not claim that either fact is a coincidence."],"exercises":[{"id":"b5u8-e1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What is the passage mainly about?","options":["How to repair an old music player","The narrator\'s relationship with an old music player and what it meant to him","Why the narrator\'s mother disliked music","The history of a company that made electronic devices"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"The narrator describes how he used the player, how it broke down, and what he still feels about it."},{"id":"b5u8-e2","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What happened when the narrator was sixteen?","options":["He lost the player on a bus","The battery stopped holding its charge and the player began to stop mid-song","He was given a newer player as a present","The leather case was stolen"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 4 says the battery stopped holding its charge and the player would halt in the middle of a song."},{"id":"b5u8-e3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"How did the grandfather react when the narrator complained?","options":["He offered to buy a replacement","He said that machines stop, and that this is the law","He suggested looking for a repair shop","He told the narrator to grow up"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 5 quotes him saying \'It is a machine. Machines stop. That is the law.\'"},{"id":"b5u8-e4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What does the narrator mean when he says the neat version of the story is \'a little too neat\'?","options":["He thinks the story is too short to be interesting","He is unwilling to claim that he learned a simple, clear lesson","He cannot remember exactly what happened","He believes the player was never really broken"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"He rejects the tidy idea that he learned something important, and instead points to the facts he can be sure of."},{"id":"b5u8-e5","type":"multiple-choice","question":"As used in paragraph 6, the word \\"sly\\" most nearly means…","options":["sneaky and unexpected","loud and sudden","careless and clumsy","generous and kind"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"The player stops in the middle of a note without warning, so sly here describes something sneaky and unexpected."},{"id":"b5u8-e6","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The bus came to a sudden ___ at the corner of the square.\\"","options":["halt","crack","mode","law"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To halt is to stop moving, which is what a bus does suddenly at a corner."},{"id":"b5u8-e7","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"We had to ___ the broken window before the winter.\\"","options":["enclose","grip","replace","halt"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"To replace something is to put something else in its place, which is what must be done with a broken window."},{"id":"b5u8-e8","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Students have free ___ to the sports centre on weekday mornings.\\"","options":["access","conduct","influence","version"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Access is the right to enter or use something, which is what free entry to the sports centre gives students."},{"id":"b5u8-e9","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Runners ___ heavily in hot weather, so they need to drink often.\\"","options":["halt","perspire","replace","enclose"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"To perspire means to sweat, which is why runners need to drink often in hot weather."},{"id":"b5u8-e10","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Your ticket is only ___ for the date printed on it.\\"","options":["sly","valid","impending","constant"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"If something is valid it is correct or acceptable, so a ticket valid only for one date cannot be used on another."}]},"9":{"id":"b5-u9","book":5,"unit":9,"title":"The Slow Years","level":"B2","topic":"A rower\'s long road to success","generatedAt":"2026-09-23","wordCount":535,"paragraphs":["For the first three years of her career, Mira lost. Not occasionally — almost always. She was small, and in a sport where size matters she was written off as too [[feeble]] to compete at the top level, and she was too [[pessimistic]] to argue. Her coach, an old man who had once rowed for a national team, kept her on the water anyway. \'You are not slow,\' he told her. \'You are just early.\'","Her [[wage]] at that time was barely enough to cover the rent. She lived in a shared flat [[alongside]] two other athletes, all three of them eating enormous meals and going to bed at nine. Her [[appetite]] was a joke among them: she could eat more than anyone and still be hungry an hour later. What none of them could see was how many hours she spent alone on the water before the others were awake.","The turning point came at a small race in a coastal town where the water was flat and a light [[breeze]] blew from the west. She did not win. She came fourth, which was the best result of her life, and afterwards a journalist asked her to [[display]] her hands. They were covered in blisters. Her sister, who had come to [[assist]] with the bags, took the photograph that appeared in the newspaper the next morning.","Success, when it came, was not sudden. She became [[efficient]] rather than fast; her technique improved; she stopped trying to [[defy]] her own body and began to work with it. Her [[relationship]] with her coach changed too. For years he had been the one who decided everything; now they argued, and she won some of the arguments. He told her later that this was when he knew she would be [[successful]] — not when she started winning, but when she started disagreeing.","There were defeats as well. In her best year she lost a final to a rower ten years younger, and she was [[outraged]] in a way she had not been since she was twenty. She [[slap|slapped]] the water with her oar — a childish gesture, she admitted afterwards — and then sat in the boat for twenty minutes, refusing to speak to anyone. The next morning she [[smash|smashed]] her alarm clock against the wall and then, embarrassed, went out and bought a new one. Her rivals, [[whereas]] once they had ignored her, now waited for her at the pontoon.","She never became a household name. She won four major races in eight years, and when she retired she was given a small ceremony and a boat that she immediately put in her garage. At the ceremony she was [[lively]] and funny, and made a joke about the boat. She had learned, slowly, to [[forgive]] herself for the years she had lost, and to see them as the price of the years that followed. She had also learned that the [[majestic]] photographs and the magazine interviews have almost nothing to do with the sport. [[nor|Nor]], it turned out, did the medals. What she remembered was the water at five in the morning, grey and flat, and the sound of her own breathing, and nobody there at all."],"exercises":[{"id":"b5u9-e1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What is the passage mainly about?","options":["How a rower improved slowly and what she valued in the end","Why small athletes cannot succeed in sport","The rules of competitive rowing","A disagreement between an athlete and her sister"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"The passage follows Mira\'s slow development over many years and ends with what she remembers from her career."},{"id":"b5u9-e2","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What was Mira\'s result at the coastal race?","options":["She won the race","She came second","She came fourth, her best result so far","She did not finish"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"Paragraph 3 says she came fourth, which was the best result of her life."},{"id":"b5u9-e3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"According to her coach, when did he know she would be successful?","options":["When she won her first major race","When she started disagreeing with him","When she moved into a shared flat","When a photograph of her appeared in a newspaper"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"He says it was not when she started winning but when she started disagreeing."},{"id":"b5u9-e4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What does the final sentence suggest about what Mira valued most?","options":["The medals and the magazine interviews","The solitary early-morning training rather than the fame","The support of her coach and sister","The chance to beat younger rowers"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"She says the photographs, interviews and medals had little to do with the sport; what she remembered was the water at five in the morning and being alone."},{"id":"b5u9-e5","type":"multiple-choice","question":"As used in paragraph 1, the word \\"feeble\\" most nearly means…","options":["weak","slow","inexperienced","unwilling"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"The passage contrasts her small size with the demands of the sport, so feeble means weak."},{"id":"b5u9-e6","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The protesters were prepared to ___ the new rules and accept the consequences.\\"","options":["defy","assist","display","slap"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To defy someone is to refuse to do what they say, which is what the protesters are prepared to do."},{"id":"b5u9-e7","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The new machine is far more ___ than the old one and uses half the electricity.\\"","options":["feeble","efficient","lively","pessimistic"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"An efficient machine does not waste energy, which matches one that uses half the electricity."},{"id":"b5u9-e8","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"A long walk in the cold air gave us a healthy ___.\\"","options":["wage","breeze","appetite","relationship"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"Your appetite is your hunger for food, and exercise in cold air is well known for increasing it."},{"id":"b5u9-e9","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"It took her years to ___ her brother for what he had said.\\"","options":["display","smash","defy","forgive"],"answerIndex":3,"explanation":"To forgive someone is to stop being angry with them, which is what she gradually managed to do."},{"id":"b5u9-e10","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The factory agreed to raise the hourly ___ for all its workers.\\"","options":["wage","breeze","appetite","relationship"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"A wage is the money a person gets for doing a job, and an hourly wage is paid by the hour."}]},"10":{"id":"b5-u10","book":5,"unit":10,"title":"The Cabinet of Small Things","level":"C1","topic":"A self-taught collector and her museum","generatedAt":"2026-09-23","wordCount":499,"paragraphs":["The museum began in a single room above a shop, and for its first [[decade]] it was a private obsession rather than a public institution. Its founder was a schoolteacher who had spent her holidays collecting: shells, seeds, pressed flowers, the wings of insects, and [[numerous]] small stones that she could not identify and would not throw away.","Her method was simple. She would take an object, examine it, and try to [[classify]] it — to decide which group it belonged to and why. Where she could not decide, she drew. She had no [[sophisticated]] equipment, only a lens, a notebook and a great deal of patience, and the museum still holds hundreds of her [[diagram|diagrams]], most of them drawn in pencil on the backs of letters. A [[concept]] that she could not explain in words she could almost always explain in a drawing.","Her great subject was the difference between the [[animate]] and the inanimate world — between things that grow and things that do not. She spent years trying to [[isolate]] the exact moment when a seed stops being a mineral object and becomes a living one, and she never succeeded, because no such moment exists. But the attempt produced a body of careful observation that later researchers found invaluable.","Getting to her collecting sites was not easy. She would take the [[ferry]] across the bay, walk for hours, and come home with her pockets full. She kept a small folding knife and a magnifying glass in her coat at all times — [[handy]] tools that appear in almost every photograph of her. She refused to [[surrender]] any specimen to a museum in the capital, despite repeated [[plea|pleas]] from its director, and she could not [[refrain]] from correcting visitors who mislabelled her specimens. Her answer to every request was the same: it stays here, where it was found.","In her last years she was obliged to [[construct]] a proper catalogue, and this turned out to be the most difficult work of her life. She had to [[concede]] that some of her early labels were wrong, and that a [[particle]] of dust under a lens can look exactly like a seed. A formal [[review]] of the collection, carried out after her death, found more than nine hundred errors. It also found, in the margins, notes that corrected almost all of them.","The museum is larger now, and better funded, but the original room has been kept exactly as she left it. Visitors often remark on the [[longing]] visible in her letters — the sense of a person who wanted to understand everything and knew she never would — and on how [[upright]] she still looks in the photographs, standing beside a table of small grey stones. Whether the collection was [[worthwhile]] is not really a question anyone asks. It is the only place in the region where a child can bring an unknown object and be taken seriously, and that, as she would have said, is quite enough."],"exercises":[{"id":"b5u10-e1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What is the passage mainly about?","options":["How a self-taught collector\'s private obsession became a public museum","The geology of a coastal region","Why museums in the capital are better funded","How to identify a seed under a magnifying glass"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"The passage profiles the founder, her methods and her collection, and explains how the single room became a museum."},{"id":"b5u10-e2","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What did the founder do when she could not classify an object?","options":["She sent it to a museum in the capital","She threw it away","She drew it","She asked a researcher to identify it"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"Paragraph 2 says that where she could not decide, she drew, and that the museum still holds hundreds of her diagrams."},{"id":"b5u10-e3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What did the formal review of the collection find?","options":["That the collection was worth very little","More than nine hundred errors, and margin notes correcting almost all of them","That many specimens had been stolen","That the catalogue had never been completed"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 5 says the review found more than nine hundred errors and also found notes in the margins that corrected almost all of them."},{"id":"b5u10-e4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What does the passage suggest about the founder\'s attitude to her collection?","options":["She wanted it to be sold to the highest bidder","She believed objects should stay where they were found","She thought it should be moved to the capital","She was happy for visitors to relabel the specimens"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"She refused to surrender any specimen despite pleas from the capital museum, saying \'it stays here, where it was found\'."},{"id":"b5u10-e5","type":"multiple-choice","question":"As used in paragraph 6, the word \\"upright\\" most nearly means…","options":["standing up straight","honest and fair","old-fashioned","serious and formal"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"The word describes how she looks in photographs, standing beside a table, so it means standing up straight."},{"id":"b5u10-e6","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Librarians ___ books by subject so that readers can find them easily.\\"","options":["classify","concede","surrender","refrain"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To classify things is to put them into groups based on their type, which is what librarians do with books."},{"id":"b5u10-e7","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"In the end he had to ___ that his first estimate had been wrong.\\"","options":["construct","concede","classify","isolate"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"To concede is to admit against your wish that something is true, which fits admitting an estimate was wrong."},{"id":"b5u10-e8","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"A small torch is a ___ thing to keep in the car.\\"","options":["upright","handy","numerous","animate"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"If something is handy it is useful, which is why a torch is worth keeping in a car."},{"id":"b5u10-e9","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Doctors sometimes have to ___ a patient to stop an infection spreading.\\"","options":["isolate","construct","surrender","classify"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To isolate is to separate one person from a group, which is how an infection is prevented from spreading."},{"id":"b5u10-e10","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The extra hour of revision was ___ — it made the exam much easier.\\"","options":["worthwhile","sophisticated","numerous","upright"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"If something is worthwhile it is important or useful, which matches revision that made the exam easier."}]},"11":{"id":"b5-u11","book":5,"unit":11,"title":"The Voice from Kestrel Bay","level":"B2","topic":"Music, memory and a wartime career","generatedAt":"2026-09-23","wordCount":488,"paragraphs":["In the small port of Kestrel Bay there is a museum room devoted to a singer named Elena Voss, and the objects inside it are ordinary enough: a coat, a hand-written songbook, a pair of worn shoes. Yet the room explains something large — how a [[culture]] can survive years of hardship, and why a single voice sometimes has more [[impact]] than a fleet.","Elena was born in a fishing village and trained as a seamstress, not as a musician. Her voice was discovered almost by accident, at a village [[ceremony]] where she was asked to replace a singer who had lost her nerve at the last moment. The [[applause]] afterwards went on so long that the organisers had to ask people to sit down again. Within two years she had given up sewing altogether.","For the next decade she performed in halls across the region. Critics admired the [[detail]] of her storytelling: she noticed small things — the flicker of a lamp, the sound of a latch — and made her audiences notice them too. Her [[genre]] was difficult to name, since she drew on folk songs, chapel music and the popular tunes of the city, and it was precisely this [[diverse]] mixture that [[enchant|enchanted]] listeners who had expected something much narrower.","When the northern and southern provinces went to war, Elena refused to leave. She sang instead in crowded [[shelter|shelters]] and hospital wards, often with no accompaniment at all. Officials pressed her to make [[authoritative]] broadcasts that would lift morale, and she agreed on one condition: she would never be asked to [[lure]] young men towards the recruiting office. She was not [[armed]], she said, and her only weapon was a song.","Touring with a small [[alliance]] of musicians, she had to [[supply]] her own transport, and the roads were frequently blocked. The greatest [[obstacle]] was not the fighting but the paperwork, which nobody had thought to simplify. Somehow a borrowed truck was found and [[equip|equipped]] with a piano, and the group travelled by night and slept by day.","Her [[bravery]] lay less in the danger she accepted than in the songs she chose. She insisted on including the old laments, which some officers considered bad for morale. The single [[exception]] was a marching song she disliked; she sang it twice, and both times badly, on purpose. That quiet refusal was, in its own way, a [[defense]] of everything the war was supposed to be protecting.","Afterwards she was offered a national tour and a great deal of money, and she turned down both. She was not [[vain]], and she had no wish to be remembered as a monument; she went home, taught singing to children, and died in the village where she had been born. Visitors to the museum often ask why so few photographs of her exist. The answer is simple. She spent her life making sure that people listened, not that they looked."],"exercises":[{"id":"b5u11-e1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What does the museum room in Kestrel Bay help to explain?","options":["Why the harbour was rebuilt after the war","How a culture can survive years of hardship","Why the two provinces eventually made peace","How the singer learned to read music"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 1 states that the room explains how a culture can survive years of hardship and why a single voice can matter more than a fleet."},{"id":"b5u11-e2","type":"multiple-choice","question":"How did Elena\'s singing career begin?","options":["She was trained from childhood by a choir master","She replaced a singer who could not perform","She won a competition in the capital","She was discovered while working in a market"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 2 explains that she was asked to replace a singer who had lost her nerve at the last moment during a village ceremony."},{"id":"b5u11-e3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"Why was Elena\'s musical genre difficult to name?","options":["She changed style completely every year","She refused to perform anything written down","She combined folk, chapel and city music in one style","She performed only in languages few people understood"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"Paragraph 3 says she drew on folk songs, chapel music and popular city tunes, and that this diverse mixture was what enchanted her listeners."},{"id":"b5u11-e4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"Why did Elena sing the marching song badly?","options":["She had never learned the tune properly","She was unwell on both occasions","She disliked it and did not want to glorify the fighting","The orchestra kept playing at the wrong speed"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"Paragraph 6 says she disliked the song and sang it badly on purpose, a quiet refusal that she treated as a defense of what the war was meant to protect."},{"id":"b5u11-e5","type":"multiple-choice","question":"As used in paragraph 4, \\"lure\\" most nearly means…","options":["order someone officially","invite someone politely","carry someone away by force","persuade someone by using a trick"],"answerIndex":3,"explanation":"Elena refused to be used to trick young men into joining up; to lure someone is to persuade them to do something by using a trick."},{"id":"b5u11-e6","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The two villages formed an ___ to repair the bridge together.\\"","options":["alliance","exception","applause","detail"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"An alliance is a group of people or bodies working together, which is exactly what two villages repairing a bridge jointly would form."},{"id":"b5u11-e7","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The lack of a road was the biggest ___ they faced that winter.\\"","options":["ceremony","obstacle","genre","culture"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"An obstacle is an object or problem that stops you from doing something, so a missing road is a textbook obstacle."},{"id":"b5u11-e8","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"He was too ___ to admit that he had never learned to swim.\\"","options":["diverse","armed","authoritative","vain"],"answerIndex":3,"explanation":"Vain people are overly concerned with how they appear to others, which explains a refusal to admit an embarrassing gap in his skills."},{"id":"b5u11-e9","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The rescue team was ___ with two boats, a radio and a week of food.\\"","options":["equipped","enchanted","applauded","sheltered"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To equip someone is to give them the things they need for a task, so a team given boats, a radio and food has been equipped."},{"id":"b5u11-e10","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"When the storm broke, the walkers took ___ in a farmhouse.\\"","options":["impact","bravery","shelter","applause"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"A shelter is a place that is safe from danger or bad weather, and a farmhouse during a storm is exactly that."}]},"12":{"id":"b5-u12","book":5,"unit":12,"title":"The Wager of the Sun and the Wind","level":"B2","topic":"A fable about strength and gentleness","generatedAt":"2026-09-23","wordCount":468,"paragraphs":["Long before anyone thought to draw maps of the region, the sun and the north wind are said to have quarrelled about which of them was the stronger. Their [[conflict]] was never settled by [[violence]]. It was settled by a wager, and the wager concerned a single traveller.","At dawn a merchant left the town gates and set off along a straight [[avenue]] lined with plane trees, heading for the far side of the [[continent]]. He carried a heavy pack, his [[belly]] was empty, and he had a long way to go. Seeing him, the wind made a [[bid]] to end the argument at once: whichever of them could make the man take off his coat would be declared the winner. The sun, patient as always, agreed to the terms.","The wind went first. It [[blow|blew]] in great gusts, stripping the leaves from the trees and bending them until they [[lean|leaned]] almost to the ground. Dust filled the air and the whole [[terrain]] turned grey. But the harder the wind blew, the more tightly the traveller pulled his coat around him, and the more determined he became to keep walking. In the [[meantime]], the sun said nothing at all.","Then it was the sun\'s turn. It rose higher and simply shone. The traveller unbuttoned his coat, then carried it over his arm, and finally folded it into his pack. The wind, which had spent all its [[muscle]] and gained nothing, had to admit that warmth had done what force could not.","The merchant walked on. By noon the heat was [[harsh]], and he stopped where a river ran beside the road. The [[current]] was strong enough to carry a grown man off his feet, and a [[timid]] boy on the bank was afraid to cross. The merchant offered him an [[alternative]]: they would go over together, with the boy\'s bundle held high above the water.","They reached the far bank safely. What followed was a [[succession]] of small events that the merchant would remember far longer than the crossing itself. The boy, full of [[enthusiasm]], insisted on carrying part of the load. Later he played a piece of [[mischief]] on a sleeping ferryman, which the merchant did not find funny. \\"[[disrespect]] costs nothing to show and a great deal to repair,\\" he said, and the boy fell silent.","Years later, when the merchant was old, he told the story to his grandchildren. He had once [[rescue|rescued]] a boy from a river, he said, or perhaps the boy had rescued him from a dull afternoon; he was never quite sure which. The moral he drew was not the one the sun and the wind had drawn. Strength, he told them, comes in more forms than people expect, and the quietest of those forms is often the one that lasts."],"exercises":[{"id":"b5u12-e1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What were the sun and the wind arguing about?","options":["Which of them could travel the fastest","Which of them was the stronger","Which of them the merchant preferred","Which of them controlled the weather"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 1 says they quarrelled about which of them was the stronger, and settled it with a wager about a traveller."},{"id":"b5u12-e2","type":"multiple-choice","question":"Why did the traveller hold his coat more tightly?","options":["He was afraid of losing it in the dust","He wanted to hide the money he was carrying","The wind blew harder and made him cold","He had been warned about thieves on the road"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"Paragraph 3 explains that the harder the wind blew, the more tightly he pulled the coat around him — the wind\'s force produced the opposite of the intended result."},{"id":"b5u12-e3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What did the merchant do for the timid boy?","options":["He carried the boy across on his back","He offered to cross the river together with him","He paid a ferryman to take the boy over","He told the boy to wait until the water fell"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 5 says he offered an alternative: the two of them would cross together, holding the boy\'s bundle above the water."},{"id":"b5u12-e4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What does the merchant\'s final comment suggest about strength?","options":["It belongs only to those who are physically powerful","It takes many forms, and the quietest may last longest","It is useless without a great deal of luck","It should always be tested against other people"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 7 states that strength comes in more forms than people expect and that the quietest of those forms often lasts the longest."},{"id":"b5u12-e5","type":"multiple-choice","question":"As used in paragraph 3, \\"lean\\" most nearly means…","options":["bend in one direction","become thinner and weaker","rest against a wall","move very slowly"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"The trees are bent by the wind, so lean here means to bend in a particular direction rather than to rest on something."},{"id":"b5u12-e6","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"If the train is full, we can take the bus as an ___.\\"","options":["alternative","enthusiasm","succession","current"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"An alternative is something you can choose instead of your first choice, which is exactly what the bus would be if the train were full."},{"id":"b5u12-e7","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"That winter was ___: weeks of wind, no sun, and very little to eat.\\"","options":["timid","harsh","avenue","meantime"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Something harsh is very unpleasant, which fits a long winter of wind, darkness and hunger."},{"id":"b5u12-e8","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"She described the plan with such ___ that everyone in the room wanted to help.\\"","options":["disrespect","terrain","enthusiasm","mischief"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"Enthusiasm is a very strong good feeling about something, and it is what makes other people want to join in."},{"id":"b5u12-e9","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"A ___ of storms crossed the coast that month, one after another.\\"","options":["bid","belly","muscle","succession"],"answerIndex":3,"explanation":"A succession is a number of things following one after the other, which matches storms arriving in a series."},{"id":"b5u12-e10","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The ___ puppy hid behind the sofa whenever a visitor arrived.\\"","options":["timid","harsh","armed","vain"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Someone timid is afraid, shy or nervous, so hiding from visitors is typical timid behaviour."}]},"13":{"id":"b5-u13","book":5,"unit":13,"title":"Twenty-Six Miles and One Wrong Turn","level":"B2","topic":"A runner\'s diary of a city marathon","generatedAt":"2026-09-23","wordCount":457,"paragraphs":["At five in the morning my alarm went off and I lay still for a moment, listening to the city. The forecast had promised rain, and for once the forecast was wrong. I had trained for this race for eleven months, and I had a [[subconscious]] habit of checking my watch before I was fully awake.","Downstairs I [[brew|brewed]] coffee and ate the same breakfast I had eaten before every long run: porridge, a banana and a spoonful of honey. A nutritionist had told me it was [[nutritious]] and high in [[protein]], and by then I believed her. My flatmate, who is [[irritable]] before seven o\'clock, handed me a bag of [[stuff]] — plasters, a spare shirt, two energy bars — and went back to bed.","A [[van]] collected the runners from the square and drove us out of the city. Nobody spoke much; runners are not a talkative group before a race. Somebody\'s phone played music, and the woman in front of me asked for an [[autograph]] from a runner two seats back who had won a major marathon the year before. I watched the road and tried not to think about the hills.","The first ten miles were easy. Then the course turned into a long climb, and I began to [[lag]] behind the group I had been running with. A [[bead]] of sweat ran into my eye. My [[equipment]] was in perfect order — shoes, socks, a light vest — but my legs were not.","At mile twenty a driver sounded his [[horn]] at us and shouted something I could not hear. The marshals had [[warn|warned]] us about that corner, where the traffic had not been closed, and I understood why. Then came the [[nightmare]]: at a junction the signs had been turned around, and a group of us took the wrong road. For four minutes we ran east instead of north. A marshal on a bicycle [[zoom|zoomed]] past us, shouting, and we turned back.","The last mile is a blur. I remember a wall near the finish where runners were asked to write their names, and my [[signature]] is somewhere on it, small and shaky. Somebody put a medal round my neck, and I still keep it in a drawer. Crossing the line, I felt something close to [[destiny]]: as though the eleven months had been pointing at that afternoon all along.","The extra four minutes did not [[affect]] my time as much as I had feared. What [[charm|charmed]] me most, in the days that followed, was how many strangers had helped: the marshal on the bicycle, the man with the hose, the child handing out slices of orange. A [[workout]] is a private thing. A race belongs to everybody on the course."],"exercises":[{"id":"b5u13-e1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What does the narrator say about breakfast?","options":["It was a new recipe recommended by a friend","It was the same meal eaten before every long run","It was bought at the race venue","It was deliberately very light"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 2 says the narrator ate the same breakfast as before every long run: porridge, a banana and honey."},{"id":"b5u13-e2","type":"multiple-choice","question":"Why did the group take the wrong road?","options":["A marshal sent them the wrong way","They were following a runner who had already finished","The signs at a junction had been turned around","The road had been closed by the police"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"Paragraph 5 says that at a junction the signs had been turned around, so the group ran east instead of north."},{"id":"b5u13-e3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What had the marshals warned the runners about?","options":["A corner where the traffic had not been closed","The steep climb in the middle of the course","A shortage of water at the later stations","The heat forecast for the afternoon"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Paragraph 5 states that the marshals had warned them about the corner where the traffic had not been closed."},{"id":"b5u13-e4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What is the narrator\'s attitude in the final paragraph?","options":["Bitter about losing four minutes","Grateful for the help of strangers and for the shared nature of a race","Determined never to enter another race","Disappointed that the training had been wasted"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"The narrator notes that the lost time mattered less than feared and lists the strangers who helped, concluding that a race belongs to everybody on the course."},{"id":"b5u13-e5","type":"multiple-choice","question":"As used in paragraph 5, \\"nightmare\\" most nearly means…","options":["a frightening dream the night before the race","a serious mistake made by the organisers","a very unpleasant experience","a confusing set of instructions"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"The word introduces the moment the runners realised they had gone the wrong way, so here it means a very unpleasant experience rather than a literal dream."},{"id":"b5u13-e6","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Lentil soup is cheap, filling and extremely ___.\\"","options":["irritable","nutritious","subconscious","stale"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Something nutritious helps the body stay healthy, which is the sense needed for a description of good, wholesome food."},{"id":"b5u13-e7","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The smallest child began to ___ behind the rest of the group.\\"","options":["zoom","brew","lag","charm"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"To lag behind is to move slowly behind other moving people or objects, exactly what the smallest child does."},{"id":"b5u13-e8","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"He is always ___ when he has not slept properly.\\"","options":["irritable","nutritious","subconscious","fond"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Someone irritable becomes annoyed or angry very easily, which fits the effect of too little sleep."},{"id":"b5u13-e9","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The delay will ___ everyone who has a connection to catch.\\"","options":["affect","zoom","warn","lag"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To affect something is to have an influence on it, so a delay that changes everyone\'s plans affects them."},{"id":"b5u13-e10","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Climbing safely depends on good ___ and careful planning.\\"","options":["autograph","equipment","protein","destiny"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Equipment is the set of things used for a particular purpose, which is what climbing safely depends on."}]},"14":{"id":"b5-u14","book":5,"unit":14,"title":"The Bakery on Vine Street","level":"B2","topic":"Two brothers, one oven, and a broken jar","generatedAt":"2026-09-23","wordCount":440,"paragraphs":["The bakery on Vine Street had one oven, two brothers and a window with blue [[shutter|shutters]]. It had belonged to their father, and before him to a man nobody could remember. The street was short and steep, and almost everybody who lived on it bought their bread there.","Every morning at four, Anton [[sift|sifted]] the flour into a wide wooden bowl and added water, salt and yeast. The [[mixture]] sat under a cloth until it doubled. Then came the part that mattered: the [[dough]] had to be folded, pressed and turned until it was smooth and [[flexible]], and Anton did this with his whole arm, never with his [[fist]].","His brother Milo had different ideas. Milo wanted a second oven, a delivery bicycle, a sign above the door. \\"We could sell twice as much,\\" he said. Anton said nothing, which was his way of [[express|expressing]] disagreement, and Milo, who had a [[slight]] temper, would [[flush]] and leave the room.","One Thursday, in the middle of an argument, Milo pushed the table. A jar of honey fell and [[shatter|shattered]] on the stone floor. Anton\'s hand went down into the broken glass and he [[injure|injured]] two fingers badly. The loaves that were already rising were left too long; by noon they were [[stale]] and had to be thrown out. The whole week\'s baking was [[ruin|ruined]].","For three days they did not speak. The oven stood cold and the shop stayed shut. A [[lump]] of dough dried out on the counter until it began to [[crumble]], and neither of them could bring himself to throw it away. Anton looked at it every morning and said nothing, which was worse than shouting.","On the fourth day Milo came downstairs early and lit the fire. He worked alone and badly, and when Anton appeared at the door he said only, \\"I don\'t know how you do it.\\" That was enough. To [[reconcile]] does not always require an apology; sometimes it takes a brother admitting that he is lost.","They baked together that morning. Milo learned to fold the dough; Anton learned to [[utter]] the word \\"yes\\" occasionally. By spring there were new [[brick|bricks]] in the back wall and a second oven. Milo got his sign, though he had to agree that the shutters stayed blue. The customers noticed the difference at once, and said so.","On the first morning the new oven was lit, Milo [[sprinkle|sprinkled]] sugar over a tray of buns and watched them [[sparkle]] under the lamp. Anton, who had never been given to speeches, said that the bread tasted exactly as it always had, which from him was the highest praise available."],"exercises":[{"id":"b5u14-e1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What did Milo want that Anton resisted?","options":["A different recipe for the bread","A second oven and a sign above the door","To sell the bakery and move away","To hire an assistant from the village"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 3 lists Milo\'s wishes — a second oven, a delivery bicycle and a sign — while Anton responds with silence."},{"id":"b5u14-e2","type":"multiple-choice","question":"How was Anton injured?","options":["He burned his arm on the oven door","He cut his hand on broken glass from a fallen jar","He was hit by the delivery bicycle","He fell while carrying a sack of flour"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 4 says a jar of honey shattered on the floor and Anton\'s hand went into the broken glass."},{"id":"b5u14-e3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"Why were the loaves thrown away?","options":["They had been left too long and were stale","They had been made with the wrong flour","They were burnt in the fire","They had been ordered by a customer who never came"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Paragraph 4 explains that the rising loaves were left too long and were stale by noon, so they had to be thrown out."},{"id":"b5u14-e4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What does the passage suggest about how the brothers reconciled?","options":["Milo made a formal apology in front of the whole street","A relative persuaded them to talk","It took a small admission rather than a formal apology","They never really made it up"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"Paragraph 6 says that to reconcile does not always require an apology, and Milo\'s admission that he did not know how Anton managed was enough."},{"id":"b5u14-e5","type":"multiple-choice","question":"As used in paragraph 6, \\"reconcile\\" most nearly means…","options":["return to a friendly relationship","agree to share the profits","forgive a debt","accept a difficult job"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"The brothers had not spoken for three days and then began working together again, so reconcile means to return to a friendly relationship."},{"id":"b5u14-e6","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"You should ___ the flour before adding it to the bowl.\\"","options":["sift","shatter","flush","crumble"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To sift flour is to pass it through a sieve to remove lumps and large pieces, which is the standard instruction in baking."},{"id":"b5u14-e7","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"___ a little salt over the potatoes before you serve them.\\"","options":["Sprinkle","Shatter","Utter","Sift"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To sprinkle means to scatter something over something else, which is what one does with salt over potatoes."},{"id":"b5u14-e8","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The hose is ___ enough to bend around the corner of the house.\\"","options":["stale","flexible","slight","stiff"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Something flexible can bend easily without breaking, which is exactly the property needed for a hose to go round a corner."},{"id":"b5u14-e9","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Drop that mirror on the tiles and it will ___ into a hundred pieces.\\"","options":["reconcile","sprinkle","shatter","injure"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"To shatter something is to break it suddenly into many tiny pieces, which is what happens to a mirror dropped on tiles."},{"id":"b5u14-e10","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The bread had gone ___ after two days in the cupboard.\\"","options":["flexible","stale","slight","ruined"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Stale food is no longer fresh but dry and hard, which is what bread becomes after a couple of days."}]},"15":{"id":"b5-u15","book":5,"unit":15,"title":"A Small Passenger in Orbit","level":"C1","topic":"How animals made early spaceflight possible","generatedAt":"2026-09-23","wordCount":444,"paragraphs":["Long before anyone had walked on the Moon, engineers faced a problem that no laboratory could solve on its own. They could [[measure]] air pressure, temperature and radiation inside a sealed cabin. What they could not measure was what all of it would do to a living body.","So a [[variety]] of animals went first — mice, rabbits, and eventually dogs. The most [[beloved]] of them, at least in the records kept by one team, was a stray the technicians named Vesna. She had been picked up near the launch site, where stray dogs were common and, the engineers argued, already used to cold nights and irregular meals.","The training was long. [[prior|Prior]] to the launch, Vesna had to learn to sit still inside a container no larger than a cupboard, to wear a harness instead of a [[leash]], and to eat a gelatinous food she plainly disliked. She would [[complain]] loudly, in a high whistle, whenever the test room grew as hot as a [[furnace]].","The team had [[establish|established]] a strict routine. Every day the dogs were weighed, examined and returned to their cages. The engineers [[apply|applied]] sensors to the animals\' ribs with a soft paste that had to be washed off afterwards. It was easy to [[confuse]] the readings with electrical noise, and easier still to [[confuse]] a tired dog with an ill one.","The [[spaceship]] left the ground on schedule, and for a few hours the signals were clear. A recovery team was [[due]] to reach the landing zone within the hour. Inside the cabin, however, the temperature began to rise. The engineers on the ground were [[powerless]]: the capsule\'s [[climate]] control had failed, and there was nothing to be done from a desk a thousand miles away. In the [[midst]] of the noise and the numbers, one technician kept repeating the dog\'s name.","[[although|Although]] the flight lasted only a few hours, it changed the programme. The readings confirmed that a living creature could survive launch and weightlessness; they also exposed the [[misery]] that careless design could cause. The dogs that flew later were older, calmer, and had [[mature|matured]] under careful handling long before launch. Cooling systems were rebuilt, food was improved, and a little more room was found.","Crowds [[await|awaited]] the capsule\'s return, and when it came down safely the technicians who had spent two years on the project found they had very little to say. Vesna came home to a soft bed and a long retirement. The [[research]] she made possible is still cited in textbooks, though her name appears in only one of them. That seems, to those who knew her, an odd way to measure a life."],"exercises":[{"id":"b5u15-e1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What problem could laboratories not solve on their own?","options":["How to build a capsule that would not leak","What spaceflight would do to a living body","How to send radio signals over long distances","How to calculate the temperature inside a cabin"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 1 says they could measure pressure, temperature and radiation, but not what all of it would do to a living body."},{"id":"b5u15-e2","type":"multiple-choice","question":"Why were stray dogs considered suitable?","options":["They were smaller than other dogs","They had already been trained by the technicians","They were used to cold nights and irregular meals","They were less likely to make noise during tests"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"Paragraph 2 says the engineers argued that stray dogs were already used to cold nights and irregular meals."},{"id":"b5u15-e3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What was required when sensors were attached to the animals?","options":["A soft paste that had to be washed off afterwards","A harness that had to be replaced daily","A small container no larger than a cupboard","A gelatinous food the dogs disliked"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Paragraph 4 describes engineers applying sensors with a soft paste that had to be washed off afterwards."},{"id":"b5u15-e4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What did the flight described in the passage reveal?","options":["That weightlessness was impossible to survive","That survival was possible but that careless design could cause suffering","That animals were no longer needed in research","That cooling systems were unnecessary"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 6 states that the readings confirmed a living creature could survive launch and weightlessness, but also exposed the misery careless design could cause."},{"id":"b5u15-e5","type":"multiple-choice","question":"As used in paragraph 5, \\"powerless\\" most nearly means…","options":["without electrical power","unable to act","not holding authority","unaware of the problem"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"The engineers could do nothing from a desk a thousand miles away, so powerless here means unable to act rather than lacking electricity."},{"id":"b5u15-e6","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Keep the dog on a ___ near the main road.\\"","options":["leash","furnace","climate","midst"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"A leash is a rope or chain used to lead an animal, which is what keeps a dog under control near traffic."},{"id":"b5u15-e7","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"After the boiler broke, the workshop was as hot as a ___.\\"","options":["leash","furnace","spaceship","variety"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"A furnace is a place where heat is produced, so a room compared to one is extremely hot."},{"id":"b5u15-e8","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"She rang the hotel to ___ about the noise from the street.\\"","options":["complain","confuse","await","mature"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To complain is to say that you are unhappy about something, which is why she telephoned the hotel."},{"id":"b5u15-e9","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Thousands of supporters ___ the team\'s arrival at the airport.\\"","options":["complain","confuse","await","establish"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"To await something is to wait for it, which is what a crowd at an airport does before a team arrives."},{"id":"b5u15-e10","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The shop sells a wide ___ of teas from small farms.\\"","options":["variety","misery","research","leash"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"A variety of something is a group of many different kinds of it, which fits a shop offering many sorts of tea."}]},"16":{"id":"b5-u16","book":5,"unit":16,"title":"The Girl Who Rolled a Hoop","level":"B2","topic":"Starting a new school and finding a way in","generatedAt":"2026-09-23","wordCount":442,"paragraphs":["Gwen arrived at Mill Lane School in the middle of term, which is never easy. She was eleven, and she had never been the new girl before. Her family had moved three hours north for her father\'s work, and she had spent the whole journey rehearsing what she would say at the school gate.","The first week was worse than she had [[foresee|foreseen]]. She [[scrape|scraped]] her knee in the playground on the second day, and by the evening there was a dark [[bruise]] the size of a coin. A group of girls watched her without speaking. When she tried to join a game, someone said, \\"That\'s not how we do it here,\\" and turned away. Gwen did not [[plead]]; for the [[sake]] of avoiding another refusal, she walked to the wall and stood there.","At Mill Lane there was an old [[custom]] that the teachers had long since given up trying to stop. Every lunchtime, in the corner of the yard, children rolled a wooden [[hoop]] along a line of stones. The rules were complicated and unwritten. Nobody could explain where the game had come from; its [[source]] was lost, and nobody cared.","On the Friday, a boy called Sam lost his turn because a [[disobedient]] dog ran across the course. Sam was [[vehement]] about it — \\"It isn\'t fair\\" — and the others laughed. Gwen picked up the hoop and rolled it, badly, straight into the fence. There was a [[thump]] as it struck the wood. Then she laughed at herself, and the laughter around her changed direction.","From that afternoon things were different. She was not [[altogether]] accepted — that takes longer — but she was included. When her jacket [[rip|ripped]] on the fence, one of the girls offered to [[stitch]] it and did a better job than a shop would have. [[per|Per]] week the game produced at least one injury and one argument, and the two usually cancelled each other out.","What [[bind|bound]] the group together was not the game itself but the fact that it belonged to nobody in particular. Gwen, who had expected [[misfortune]] around every corner and had a rather [[negative]] view of her own chances, began to [[glimpse]] something else: a place where being new was only a temporary condition. It was, she decided later, the first place she had ever chosen for herself. The teacher on duty, a [[stern]] woman with a whistle, watched from the door. She never smiled, but she never once stopped the game either. Years later Gwen could still remember the sound of the hoop on the stones — a small, ordinary noise that had, for a while, meant everything."],"exercises":[{"id":"b5u16-e1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"Why did Gwen\'s family move?","options":["Her mother had been offered a new job","Her father\'s work required it","The family wanted a bigger house","Gwen had been unhappy at her old school"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 1 says her family had moved three hours north for her father\'s work."},{"id":"b5u16-e2","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What is the old custom at Mill Lane?","options":["Singing in the yard before lessons","Rolling a wooden hoop along a line of stones","Trading stones at the end of each week","Playing a game with a whistle"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 3 describes children rolling a wooden hoop along a line of stones in the corner of the yard every lunchtime."},{"id":"b5u16-e3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"Why did Sam lose his turn?","options":["He arrived late for the game","He argued with the teacher on duty","A disobedient dog ran across the course","He broke the hoop against the fence"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"Paragraph 4 states that Sam lost his turn because a disobedient dog ran across the course."},{"id":"b5u16-e4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What does the teacher\'s behaviour at the end suggest?","options":["She disapproved of the game but tolerated it","She had never noticed the game","She was planning to ban the game the following term","She was the person who had invented the game"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"The teacher is described as stern and never smiling, yet she never once stopped the game — a strict manner combined with quiet tolerance."},{"id":"b5u16-e5","type":"multiple-choice","question":"As used in paragraph 2, \\"plead\\" most nearly means…","options":["argue about the rules","ask for something she wanted very badly","complain to a teacher","answer back rudely"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Gwen chose not to beg the other girls to let her join, so plead here means to ask for something she wanted very badly."},{"id":"b5u16-e6","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"It is a local ___ to give children money at the new year.\\"","options":["custom","misfortune","bruise","thump"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"A custom is a way of doing things that has been the same for a long time, which is what an established local practice is."},{"id":"b5u16-e7","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Through the trees we could ___ the roof of the old house.\\"","options":["glimpse","rip","scrape","bind"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To glimpse something is to see it for a short time, which is what happens when a roof appears briefly between trees."},{"id":"b5u16-e8","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Losing the keys was a small ___ compared with everything else that week.\\"","options":["custom","misfortune","hoop","source"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"A misfortune is bad luck or an unlucky event, so losing keys is a minor misfortune next to larger problems."},{"id":"b5u16-e9","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The coach was ___ with the players, but everyone agreed he was fair.\\"","options":["stern","vehement","disobedient","negative"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"A stern person is very serious, which matches a strict coach who is nevertheless fair."},{"id":"b5u16-e10","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Be careful not to ___ the side of the car on the gate.\\"","options":["scrape","stitch","plead","foresee"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To scrape something is to rub it very hard against something sharp or rough, which is what happens when a car touches a gate."}]},"17":{"id":"b5-u17","book":5,"unit":17,"title":"What the Ledgers Recorded","level":"C1","topic":"A medieval epidemic and the birth of demography","generatedAt":"2026-09-23","wordCount":423,"paragraphs":["In the middle of the fourteenth century a disease moved across Europe faster than any army could have marched. It left no battlefield and took no prisoners, and the people who tried to describe it ran out of words.","The first sign, according to most accounts, was a [[fever]] that would not come down. Within a day or two the [[ailing]] patient could not stand. The [[physician|physicians]] had no [[diagnosis]] to offer and no cure; what they had was a set of theories, most of which were [[nonsense]]. Some blamed the air. Some blamed the stars. A few blamed their neighbours, which was the most dangerous theory of all.","The records that survive — parish registers, tax rolls, the notes of town clerks — are [[grim]] reading. They show a sharp [[dip]] in the number of births and a collapse in the number of marriages. Out of such lists the modern study of [[demography]] was born: historians can now estimate how many people died in a region without ever learning their names.","The [[distress]] of the period is easy to imagine and much harder to describe. What is difficult to recover is the ordinary response. People [[bathe|bathed]] the sick, burned [[herb|herbs]] in the streets, and [[mourn|mourned]] their dead in private, because public gatherings had been forbidden.","Medicine did not know where to look. Doctors of the period had been trained to [[critique]] the writings of ancient authorities rather than to examine a patient closely, and the disease exposed the limits of that training. Guards at the town gates wore a [[helmet]] against a threat that armour could not stop.","Two responses appeared everywhere. One was superstitious; the other was [[militant]]. Crowds attacked the households of outsiders, convinced that somebody must have caused the disease deliberately. It was a [[miracle]], in a sense, that the violence did not spread further than it did.","Looking back, historians make a point of saying that no two outbreaks were exactly [[alike]]. The disease did not respect the [[bounds]] of a parish or a province; it travelled along roads and rivers and arrived in cities and villages on the same day. Its effects on the [[gut]] were described in careful detail by people who had no idea what they were seeing. When the worst was over, wages had risen, land was cheaper, and the old certainties had been badly damaged. The chroniclers were [[appall|appalled]] by what they had lived through, and so they wrote it down. That is the only reason we know as much as we do."],"exercises":[{"id":"b5u17-e1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What was the first sign of the disease?","options":["A cough that lasted for weeks","A fever that would not come down","A pain in the stomach","A loss of hearing"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 2 says the first sign, according to most accounts, was a fever that would not come down."},{"id":"b5u17-e2","type":"multiple-choice","question":"How did the surviving records contribute to modern demography?","options":["They list the names of everyone who died","They explain how the disease spread along rivers","They allow historians to estimate deaths in a region without knowing names","They contain the first accurate medical diagnoses"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"Paragraph 3 says that from such lists the study of demography was born and that historians can now estimate how many died without learning their names."},{"id":"b5u17-e3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What does the passage say about doctors of the period?","options":["They were trained to critique ancient writings rather than examine patients closely","They refused to treat anyone with a fever","They had discovered the true cause of the disease","They worked only in the largest cities"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Paragraph 5 explains that doctors had been trained to critique the writings of ancient authorities rather than to examine a patient closely."},{"id":"b5u17-e4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What is the writer\'s attitude towards the chroniclers in the final paragraph?","options":["Critical of their wild theories","Approving, because they recorded what they saw","Indifferent, since their accounts are unreliable","Amused by their superstitions"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"The final sentence says the fact that they wrote it down is the only reason we know as much as we do, which is an approving judgement."},{"id":"b5u17-e5","type":"multiple-choice","question":"As used in paragraph 3, \\"dip\\" most nearly means…","options":["a short swim","a decline","a rise in prices","a written record"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"The word is used of the number of births, which fell sharply, so dip here means a decline or worsening."},{"id":"b5u17-e6","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"After two days of tests, the doctor\'s ___ was a mild infection.\\"","options":["diagnosis","demography","distress","dip"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"A diagnosis is a doctor\'s decision about the cause of an illness, which is what tests are carried out to reach."},{"id":"b5u17-e7","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The whole village gathered to ___ the loss of its oldest resident.\\"","options":["mourn","bathe","critique","appall"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To mourn means to feel or show great sadness when you lose someone, which is why a community gathers together."},{"id":"b5u17-e8","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"This ___ grows wild on the hillside and is used in cooking.\\"","options":["herb","bounds","helmet","gut"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"An herb is a plant used for cooking or medicine, which fits a plant gathered from a hillside for kitchen use."},{"id":"b5u17-e9","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"He talked complete ___ for ten minutes and nobody stopped him.\\"","options":["nonsense","miracle","physician","fever"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Nonsense is words or ideas that are silly or foolish, which is what an uninterrupted ten-minute speech of that kind would be."},{"id":"b5u17-e10","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The report painted a ___ picture of the town\'s finances.\\"","options":["grim","ailing","alike","militant"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Something grim is worrying, serious and frightening, which describes a report about failing finances."}]},"18":{"id":"b5-u18","book":5,"unit":18,"title":"Opening Night in the Basement","level":"B2","topic":"A school play, and an unexpected part","generatedAt":"2026-09-23","wordCount":442,"paragraphs":["Our school did not have a theatre. It had a [[basement]] with a low ceiling, a [[staircase]] that groaned, and forty folding chairs that had to be carried down before every performance.","I had joined the production as a favour to my cousin, who sang in the [[choir]] and had been given the part of the narrator. She had been in three productions before, but never with a speaking part. She had an [[accent]] from the north of the country that the director wanted to keep, and a habit of [[blink|blinking]] whenever she was nervous, which was constantly.","The play was a [[comic]] version of a very old story about a [[hermit]] who lives alone on a hill and refuses to speak to anybody. The [[script]] had been rewritten three times, and every rewrite seemed to [[complicate]] the plot rather than clarify it. Two scenes had been [[reserve|reserved]] for a song that nobody could sing.","My job was props. This meant [[search|searching]] the building for objects that could pass for something else: a [[leather]] bag for a merchant, a pair of white [[glove|gloves]] for a doctor, a wooden bowl, a broken clock that had stopped at ten past four. On the Wednesday I was sent on an [[errand]] to the shop at the corner to buy ribbon, and I came back with the wrong colour, which the director accepted [[justly]] as her own fault for not writing it down.","Then came the disaster. Two days before opening night our leading actor [[decline|declined]] to continue. He had [[ponder|pondered]] the problem for a week, he said, and had decided that he was not ready. The director went very quiet. There was a [[blank]] space where a plan should have been.","I was given the part. I had eleven lines and four days. My cousin, who was kinder than I deserved, sat with me on the staircase every evening and made me repeat the lines until they stopped sounding like a list. On the first night I [[slam|slammed]] the door too hard and the whole set shook, and somebody in the audience laughed — which, in a comedy, is not the worst thing that can happen.","We were not good, exactly. But we were better than we had any right to be, and when the last line was spoken the chairs were full. The chairs had to be carried back up the staircase on Sunday morning, which took twice as long as bringing them down. I still have the script somewhere, with my cousin\'s handwriting in the margins. She became a teacher; I became a [[barber]], of all things. Neither of us ever acted again."],"exercises":[{"id":"b5u18-e1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What did the school use as a theatre?","options":["The gymnasium","The basement","An outdoor stage in the yard","The choir room"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 1 says the school had no theatre, only a basement with a low ceiling and forty folding chairs."},{"id":"b5u18-e2","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What happened each time the script was rewritten?","options":["It became shorter","It removed the narrator\'s part","It made the plot more complicated rather than clearer","It added more songs for the choir"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"Paragraph 3 says every rewrite seemed to complicate the plot rather than clarify it."},{"id":"b5u18-e3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"Why did the narrator end up playing the leading part?","options":["The director had always wanted the narrator to play it","The original actor declined to continue","The cousin refused to perform alone","Nobody else had learned the lines"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 5 explains that two days before opening night the leading actor declined to continue, and the narrator was given the part."},{"id":"b5u18-e4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"How does the narrator look back on the performance?","options":["As a complete disaster that he would rather forget","As something modestly better than expected, in front of a full audience","As the beginning of a career in acting","As an unfair burden placed on him by the director"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 7 says they were not good exactly, but better than they had any right to be, and the chairs were full."},{"id":"b5u18-e5","type":"multiple-choice","question":"As used in paragraph 5, \\"decline\\" most nearly means…","options":["become worse in quality","refuse","move downwards slowly","postpone an event"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"The actor decided he was not ready and therefore refused to carry on, so decline here means to say no to something."},{"id":"b5u18-e6","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"I have to run an ___ for my grandmother this morning.\\"","options":["errand","accent","basement","script"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"An errand is a short trip taken to do a specific task, such as doing something for a relative."},{"id":"b5u18-e7","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The old ___ chair creaked loudly when he sat down.\\"","options":["blank","comic","leather","stern"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"Leather is a material made from animal skin, and a leather chair is the sort of object that creaks."},{"id":"b5u18-e8","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"She sat by the window to ___ the offer before replying.\\"","options":["slam","ponder","reserve","blink"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"To ponder something is to think about it carefully, which is what one does before deciding how to reply."},{"id":"b5u18-e9","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"He lived like a ___ for years, seeing almost nobody.\\"","options":["barber","hermit","choir","glove"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"A hermit lives alone and does not spend time with others, which matches a life of seeing almost nobody."},{"id":"b5u18-e10","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Please write your name in the ___ space at the top of the form.\\"","options":["blank","comic","justly","reserved"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Something blank has nothing written on it, which is exactly what a space waiting for a name is."}]},"19":{"id":"b5-u19","book":5,"unit":19,"title":"Seat 34B","level":"B2","topic":"A brother\'s first flight","generatedAt":"2026-09-23","wordCount":428,"paragraphs":["My brother Isaac had never flown before, and he had prepared for the journey the way other people prepare for an examination. He had read a whole [[chapter]] of a book by a well-known [[author]] on how to travel calmly; he had made a list; he had packed a lunch of bread, cheese, [[raisin|raisins]], [[pea|peas]], [[etc|etc.]] He had also watched four films about flying, which I suspect did more harm than good.","At the airport he [[state|stated]] very firmly that he was not nervous, which was how I knew that he was. He was also [[afflicted]] with a cough that had lasted three weeks and showed no sign of leaving.","We found our row. I had the window seat; Isaac had the middle, next to a large man in the [[aisle]] seat who fell asleep before the doors were closed. Isaac spent ten minutes working out how to [[flip]] the switch on his reading light.","The cabin crew [[notify|notified]] us that we would be delayed by an electrical [[breakdown]] somewhere in the terminal, which nobody explained further. Isaac, who had been [[idle]] for an hour and disliked it intensely, began to read the safety card as though it were a novel. I suggested that he buy a newspaper; he said he would rather watch the doors.","When we finally climbed, the cabin [[atmosphere]] changed. My ears clicked; Isaac\'s face went pale. He ate one [[pea]] from his box, put the [[tray]] table down, and immediately put it up again.","Then we hit turbulence. I have never seen anybody turn that particular colour. Isaac [[retain|retained]] his lunch, barely, and afterwards admitted that he had come close to needing to [[vomit]] in front of strangers. He held the armrests for the next twenty minutes and did not let go. He said it was the worst part of the whole day.","We landed in the middle of a storm. The runway lights were [[vivid]] in the rain, and the [[cargo]] doors were already open before the passengers had moved. We then waited on a bus for twenty minutes while the rain came down. It was an [[unfortunate]] end to a difficult afternoon.","Isaac did not fly again for six years. When he finally did, he sat in the same seat — 34B — and slept through the entire flight. \\"The trick,\\" he told me, \\"is not to think about it.\\" He said this as though he had discovered something, and perhaps he had. Fear, he had learned, does not [[connect]] to anything real; it simply sits there, waiting to be ignored."],"exercises":[{"id":"b5u19-e1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"How had Isaac prepared for the flight?","options":["By asking a pilot for advice","By reading a book chapter and making lists","By taking medicine for his cough","By flying a short distance the week before"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 1 says he had read a whole chapter of a book by a well-known author and had made a list."},{"id":"b5u19-e2","type":"multiple-choice","question":"Why was the flight delayed?","options":["The weather was too stormy to take off","A passenger had not boarded in time","An electrical breakdown somewhere in the terminal","The crew arrived late at the airport"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"Paragraph 4 says the cabin crew notified them of a delay caused by an electrical breakdown in the terminal."},{"id":"b5u19-e3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What happened to Isaac as the plane climbed?","options":["He fell asleep immediately","He began to read the safety card","His face went pale and his ears clicked","He asked to change seats"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"Paragraph 5 describes the cabin atmosphere changing, the narrator\'s ears clicking and Isaac\'s face going pale."},{"id":"b5u19-e4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What does the final paragraph suggest about Isaac?","options":["He never flew again after the difficult journey","He eventually coped with flying by deciding not to think about it","He still becomes ill on every flight","He now works for an airline"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Six years later he slept through the whole flight and said the trick was not to think about it, so he found a way to cope."},{"id":"b5u19-e5","type":"multiple-choice","question":"As used in paragraph 2, \\"stated\\" most nearly means…","options":["said in a definite way","wrote down formally","suggested without confidence","repeated several times"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"He stated very firmly that he was not nervous, so the word means to say something in a definite, confident way."},{"id":"b5u19-e6","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Please keep your bags out of the ___ so people can walk past.\\"","options":["aisle","cargo","tray","chapter"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"An aisle is the space between two rows that people use to walk along, so bags should be kept out of it."},{"id":"b5u19-e7","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The ship was carrying ___ from three different countries.\\"","options":["aisle","cargo","atmosphere","breakdown"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Cargo is the items carried by a ship or an aeroplane, which is what a ship transports between countries."},{"id":"b5u19-e8","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The machines stood ___ for weeks while the roof was repaired.\\"","options":["idle","vivid","unfortunate","afflicted"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"If something is idle it is not doing anything, so machines standing unused for weeks are idle."},{"id":"b5u19-e9","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"She gave a ___ description of the market at dawn.\\"","options":["vivid","idle","unfortunate","afflicted"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"A vivid description is bright, colourful and lively, which is what makes it easy to imagine a scene."},{"id":"b5u19-e10","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Please ___ the office if you change your address.\\"","options":["notify","retain","flip","connect"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To notify someone of something is to tell them about it, which is what an office asks you to do when your details change."}]},"20":{"id":"b5-u20","book":5,"unit":20,"title":"Nine Days on Kirenga","level":"B2","topic":"A mountain expedition, from the first camp to the summit","generatedAt":"2026-09-23","wordCount":459,"paragraphs":["The [[calendar]] had been on my kitchen wall for eight months, and by the end I knew it by heart. Every square marked a training walk, a rest day, or a [[deadline]] for booking equipment.","My [[ambition]] was simple: to stand on the summit of Kirenga, a mountain in East Africa that is not technically difficult and is nevertheless extremely hard. Our [[contingent]] was small — six walkers, two guides and a cook.","Day one was easy. We walked through farmland and then forest, and I felt [[impatient]] with the slow pace. The guides told us to keep it slow; they had seen too many people [[exert]] themselves on the first afternoon and go home on the second. \\"Pole, pole,\\" they said. Slowly, slowly.","On the third day we camped beside a lava [[cave]] that the porters used as a kitchen, and slept in a wooden [[cabin]] that smelled of smoke.","Day four was the hard one. We crossed open moorland where the wind came at us from the side. The porters [[haul|hauled]] our food and water up the mountain, while we carried only what we needed for the day. Even so, my pack felt heavier every hour.","Altitude does strange things. By day five I was [[constantly]] short of breath and my appetite had gone. I had read about [[nutrition]] on the mountain and had calculated every [[calorie]], but the numbers were useless when I could not face food.","Two of our group turned back on day six. One had twisted an [[ankle]] on loose rock; the other had a headache that would not lift. Our guides had [[forbid|forbidden]] us to continue if anyone showed signs of altitude sickness, and they meant it. At the park gate an [[ambulance]] waited for anyone who came down early, and that morning it left with two passengers.","We reached the last camp in the [[mid]]-afternoon. Two guides [[flank|flanked]] the slowest walker on the final slope, one on each side, and nobody was allowed to walk alone.","We left at midnight. It was colder than anything I had known. The cold had [[impair|impaired]] my judgement; I could not remember whether I had already taken a photograph. I was [[fond]] of my gloves in a way I had never been before. At about five o\'clock the sky began to lighten, and by six we were standing on the summit.","Coming down took two days. I remain an [[optimist]] about mountains: I believe that most people who fail do so because they started too fast, not because they were too weak. But I also know now that the summit is not the point. The point is the nine days, and the people who carried your food, and the fact that you came down again."],"exercises":[{"id":"b5u20-e1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What was the writer\'s ambition?","options":["To climb the mountain in the shortest possible time","To stand on the summit of Kirenga","To work as a guide on the mountain","To lead the largest group ever to reach the top"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 2 says the ambition was simple: to stand on the summit of Kirenga."},{"id":"b5u20-e2","type":"multiple-choice","question":"Why did the guides insist on a slow pace at the beginning?","options":["The path was too narrow for fast walking","They had seen people wear themselves out early and give up","The porters could not walk any faster","They wanted to arrive at camp after dark"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 3 says they had seen too many people exert themselves on the first afternoon and go home on the second."},{"id":"b5u20-e3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"Why did two members of the group turn back?","options":["They had run out of food and water","One had an injured ankle and the other a persistent headache","They were refused permission to continue by the park office","They had booked their return flight too early"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 7 states that one had twisted an ankle on loose rock and the other had a headache that would not lift."},{"id":"b5u20-e4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What does the writer conclude in the final paragraph?","options":["That the summit was not worth the effort","That the journey and the people involved mattered more than reaching the top","That most climbers fail because they are physically weak","That she will never attempt such a climb again"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"The writer says the summit is not the point; the point is the nine days, the people who carried the food, and coming down again."},{"id":"b5u20-e5","type":"multiple-choice","question":"As used in paragraph 9, \\"impair\\" most nearly means…","options":["weaken","improve gradually","confuse deliberately","protect from harm"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"The cold had made the writer\'s judgement less reliable, so impair here means to make something weaker or worse."},{"id":"b5u20-e6","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"We must finish the report before the ___ on Friday.\\"","options":["deadline","calendar","calorie","ambition"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"A deadline is the time by which something must be completed, which is what Friday represents for the report."},{"id":"b5u20-e7","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"A ___ is a unit used to measure the energy that food provides.\\"","options":["calorie","cabin","contingent","ankle"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"A calorie is a unit of heat used to measure how much energy a food will produce, which is exactly the definition required here."},{"id":"b5u20-e8","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The museum rules ___ visitors from touching the exhibits.\\"","options":["forbid","haul","flank","exert"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To forbid means to order someone not to do something, which is what a rule preventing contact with exhibits does."},{"id":"b5u20-e9","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"He grew ___ waiting for a bus that never came.\\"","options":["impatient","fond","optimistic","mid"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To be impatient is to become angry or anxious when something takes too long, which fits waiting for a bus that never arrives."},{"id":"b5u20-e10","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"I have always been ___ of early mornings on the coast.\\"","options":["fond","impatient","idle","constant"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To be fond of something is to like or cherish it, and the phrase \\"fond of\\" is the fixed expression needed here."}]},"21":{"id":"b5-u21","book":5,"unit":21,"title":"The Ledger and the Legend","level":"B2","topic":"A bank teller\'s hunch","generatedAt":"2026-09-23","wordCount":430,"paragraphs":["For eleven years Mara had worked behind the same counter, and in that time she had learned to read people faster than she read the forms they handed her. She was a [[teller]], and a careful one: patient, exact, and entirely [[trustworthy]]. Her manager had twice offered to [[promote]] her, and twice she had declined, saying that the counter suited her. Her [[background]] was in [[literature]], which her colleagues found funny, and she never explained it.","The branch itself belonged to another [[millennium]]. It had red brick walls, [[copper]] window frames that had gone green at the edges, and a vault door heavy enough to stop a train. In the staff room someone had once [[chronicle|chronicled]] the history of the building on a single sheet of paper, but nobody had [[update-v|updated]] it in thirty years, so the last entry still described a world of handwritten ledgers.","The stranger arrived on a wet Tuesday. He carried a leather case and a folder of photographs, and he wanted to move a large [[sum]] into a new account. The money, he said, came from a foundation that fought a rare tropical [[disease]]. He had a warm voice and an easy manner, and he told a long story about a village where the illness had first appeared — how it seemed to [[infect]] whole families, how it moved through the blood the way [[venom]] moves along a [[vein]].","It was a good story. That was the trouble. Mara had spent four years reading nineteenth-century novels, and she knew how a convincing tale works: you offer the listener one small, believable detail as [[bait]], and the rest follows. His foundation had no address. The photographs showed a place she could not identify. And yet something began to [[itch]] at the back of her mind, the way doubt always did when a detail refused to [[relate]] to anything she knew.","So she kept him talking. She asked about the village, the weather, the local [[religion]], and he answered everything smoothly, in the manner of a man reciting a well-known [[myth]]. Then she asked which bank had held the money before. He hesitated — only for a second, but she saw it. Later, when the police opened his case, they found no photographs of a village at all, only a printed list of names and account numbers. The story about the disease had been borrowed, like most [[folklore]], from somewhere else entirely. What he had been selling was not medicine but a legend, and legends, she thought afterwards, are simply stories that nobody has bothered to check."],"exercises":[{"id":"b5u21-e1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"Why had Mara twice refused promotion?","options":["She did not want the extra responsibility","She preferred working at the counter","She was not qualified for the role","She was planning to leave the bank"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 1 says she declined both offers, saying that the counter suited her."},{"id":"b5u21-e2","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What first made Mara uneasy about the stranger?","options":["His foundation had no address and his photographs showed an unidentifiable place","He refused to give his name","He asked to see the vault","He spoke rudely to the other customers"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Paragraph 4 lists two problems: the foundation had no address and the photographs showed a place she could not identify."},{"id":"b5u21-e3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What did the police find in the stranger\'s case?","options":["Photographs of a village","A printed list of names and account numbers","A large amount of cash","Medical supplies for the foundation"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 5 says they found no photographs of a village at all, only a printed list of names and account numbers."},{"id":"b5u21-e4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"As used in paragraph 4, the word \\"bait\\" most nearly means…","options":["a small convincing detail used to make a story believable","money offered to open an account","a legal document","a trap set by the police"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"The paragraph explains that you offer the listener one small, believable detail and the rest follows — that detail is the bait."},{"id":"b5u21-e5","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What does the final sentence suggest about Mara\'s attitude to stories?","options":["She thinks stories are always dishonest","She believes a story is only as good as the checking behind it","She prefers novels to real life","She regrets having studied literature"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"She calls legends simply stories that nobody has bothered to check, which shows she values verification over plausibility."},{"id":"b5u21-e6","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"A ___ employee is one you can rely on to keep a promise.\\"","options":["Itchy","Trustworthy","Venomous","Chronicled"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Someone who is trustworthy is honest and reliable, which is exactly what the sentence describes."},{"id":"b5u21-e7","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The snake\'s ___ can be dangerous even in very small amounts.\\"","options":["sum","vein","bait","venom"],"answerIndex":3,"explanation":"Venom is the poisonous substance produced by an animal such as a snake."},{"id":"b5u21-e8","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The cathedral has stood on this hill for more than a ___.\\"","options":["millennium","literature","folklore","religion"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"A millennium is a period of one thousand years, the only option that measures a span of time."},{"id":"b5u21-e9","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"She paid a large ___ of money for a very small flat.\\"","options":["vein","myth","sum","teller"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"A sum is a specific amount of money, which fits the phrase \\"a large ___ of money\\"."},{"id":"b5u21-e10","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The two problems ___ to one another more closely than they first appear to.\\"","options":["infect","update","promote","relate"],"answerIndex":3,"explanation":"To relate to something means to be connected with it, which is what the sentence describes."}]},"22":{"id":"b5-u22","book":5,"unit":22,"title":"The Scribe Who Copied Nothing","level":"B2","topic":"A short history of a trading city","generatedAt":"2026-09-23","wordCount":465,"paragraphs":["Along the river that divided the old [[empire]], the city of Vellis had grown rich on [[commerce]]. Boats arrived each morning loaded with [[goods]] — salt, timber, dyed cloth — and left again before dark. At the centre of the city stood the palace, and in the palace, in a small room with one window, sat a [[scribe]] named Ansel.","His room was [[cozy]] in winter and unbearable in summer, and it was there that he copied treaties, tax lists, and letters between rulers who never met. He had a careful hand and a patient one, though his [[temper]] was known to fray when a document arrived smudged. He was paid a modest amount for each page, and he had never once been invited into the [[throne]] room, which did not trouble him.","He was copying a trade agreement when he noticed the numbers. The agreement promised the northern provinces a fixed share of every cargo, but the total it named was larger than the city\'s whole [[economy]] could produce. Whoever had drawn it up intended to [[deplete]] the treasury and blame the provinces afterwards. Ansel set down his pen. To say nothing was to be [[neutral]], and neutrality, he had decided long ago, was a choice like any other.","He carried his warning to the chancellor, who [[mock|mocked]] him and told him to mind his ink. He carried it to a merchant he trusted, who advised him to [[heed]] his own business. A clerk suggested that anyone making such claims should be [[condemn|condemned]] as a troublemaker, and for a while Ansel was [[persecute|persecuted]] in the small ways that matter: his orders were delayed, his lamp oil was not replaced, his name was left off the list of guests at the autumn feast.","In the end it was the river that settled it. A flood destroyed half the warehouses, and with the warehouses gone the false agreement collapsed under its own arithmetic. The provinces, which had been ready to fight, found instead that they needed the city and the city needed them; out of the wreckage came an uneasy [[unity]] that no treaty had produced. There was no [[victor]]. Ansel was asked to [[reduce]] the old tax lists by a third and to draw up new ones, and he did it without complaint, though he refused the purse of [[charity]] offered by the merchants, saying that he had been paid for the pages he wrote. He had felt [[pity]] enough for the men who sat on thrones, and what he wanted was a page that would still make sense in a hundred years. Centuries later, students who [[hitchhike|hitchhiked]] along the river road and stopped to ask about the palace were shown the throne room and the gardens, but never the small room with one window."],"exercises":[{"id":"b5u22-e1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What did Ansel notice while copying the trade agreement?","options":["The total promised to the provinces was larger than the city could produce","The chancellor had signed it twice","The northern provinces had refused to trade","The figures had been written in a foreign script"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Paragraph 3 says the total named was larger than the city\'s whole economy could produce, which showed an intention to deplete the treasury."},{"id":"b5u22-e2","type":"multiple-choice","question":"How did the chancellor respond to Ansel\'s warning?","options":["He ordered an immediate investigation","He mocked him and told him to mind his ink","He thanked him privately","He sent him to the northern provinces"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 4 states that the chancellor mocked him and told him to mind his ink."},{"id":"b5u22-e3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What finally brought the false agreement down?","options":["A flood destroyed half the warehouses","The provinces went to war","The chancellor resigned","A merchant published the figures"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Paragraph 5 says the flood destroyed half the warehouses, and without them the agreement collapsed under its own arithmetic."},{"id":"b5u22-e4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"In paragraph 3, Ansel reflects that to say nothing was to be \\"neutral\\". What does this suggest about his view?","options":["Silence is always the safest course","Remaining silent is itself a decision with consequences","A scribe is required to remain neutral","He wanted to avoid taking sides in a private quarrel"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"He had decided that neutrality was a choice like any other, meaning that silence is itself a decision rather than an escape from one."},{"id":"b5u22-e5","type":"multiple-choice","question":"Why did Ansel refuse the merchants\' gift of money?","options":["He thought the amount was insultingly small","He said he had already been paid for the pages he wrote","He wanted a higher position instead","He did not trust the merchants who offered it"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 5 says he refused the purse of charity, saying that he had been paid for the pages he wrote."},{"id":"b5u22-e6","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The two countries agreed to stay ___ and trade with both sides.\\"","options":["neutral","cozy","depleted","heeded"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To be neutral is to refuse to support either side, which is what trading with both sides implies."},{"id":"b5u22-e7","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The warehouse fire destroyed thousands of pounds\' worth of ___.\\"","options":["pity","goods","temper","unity"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Goods are things that can be bought and sold, so they are what a warehouse stores and a fire destroys."},{"id":"b5u22-e8","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The council voted to ___ the new tax after farmers complained.\\"","options":["mock","heed","reduce","condemn"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"To reduce something is to make it smaller, so the council made the tax smaller."},{"id":"b5u22-e9","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"He was warned to ___ the advice of his doctor and rest.\\"","options":["deplete","hitchhike","persecute","heed"],"answerIndex":3,"explanation":"To heed something means to obey or follow it, which is what the warning asks him to do with the doctor\'s advice."},{"id":"b5u22-e10","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The old ruler sat on the ___ for forty years.\\"","options":["scribe","throne","victor","charity"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"A throne is the special chair in which a ruler sits, so it is the only option that fits the image."}]},"23":{"id":"b5-u23","book":5,"unit":23,"title":"The Day the Sky Went Dark","level":"C1","topic":"How the dinosaurs died out","generatedAt":"2026-09-23","wordCount":445,"paragraphs":["The question of what ended the age of the dinosaurs is an [[intellectual]] puzzle that has been argued over for a century. For a long time the [[majority]] of researchers believed that the great reptiles had simply declined as the climate cooled. That view was never entirely abandoned, but the [[controversy]] cooled sharply in the 1980s, when a thin layer of clay was found in rocks of the same age on several continents.","The clay tells a simple story. When you [[analyze]] its chemistry, you find iridium — a metal that is rare at the surface of the Earth but common in meteorites. The proportions are [[identical]] wherever the layer is sampled, from Italy to New Zealand, which is hard to explain by any local event. The most economical explanation is that an [[asteroid]] several kilometres wide struck the coast of what is now Mexico, throwing a [[vast]] cloud of dust and sulphate into the sky.","What followed was not a single disaster but a chain of them. Sunlight failed, plants died, and the animals that ate them starved. A second [[factor]] may have made matters worse: enormous volcanic eruptions were already pouring lava across what is now India, and the two events together left the atmosphere unable to [[regulate]] its own temperature. In [[stricken]] forests, nothing could grow.","Not everything died. Small [[mammal|mammals]], most of them the size of a mouse, survived on insects and seeds. Being small helped: they needed less food, they could hide, and they could shelter their [[offspring]] in burrows. With the large predators gone, they [[multiply|multiplied]] rapidly. Some of the survivors were plant-eaters, though the first of them were hardly [[vegetarian|vegetarians]] in the modern sense — they ate whatever the ruined landscape offered.","Birds, which had [[evolve|evolved]] from small feathered dinosaurs, came through as well. Their survival is the strongest argument against the old idea that the extinction was a slow, gradual decline: the fossil record is not [[accurate]] enough to date the crisis to a single year, but it is accurate enough to show that it happened quickly. Modern [[genetic]] work has [[reinforce|reinforced]] this picture. The [[genome]] of a bird still carries traces of a dinosaur ancestry, and the lineage appears to pass straight through the extinction rather than ending with it.","There is a lesson here that has nothing to do with dinosaurs. Remove one species and you do not lose one species; you lose a relationship. The same principle appears today whenever a [[pesticide]] is used too widely: the insects die, the birds that fed on them leave, and the whole food web thins out. The dinosaurs are gone. The mechanism that removed them is not."],"exercises":[{"id":"b5u23-e1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What is the main idea of the passage?","options":["Dinosaurs died out gradually over millions of years","An impact set off a chain of effects that ended the dinosaurs\' dominance","Birds are not related to dinosaurs at all","Volcanic eruptions alone killed the dinosaurs"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"The passage argues for the asteroid impact and describes the chain of consequences that followed it, while rejecting the purely gradual explanation."},{"id":"b5u23-e2","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What is found in the clay layer on several continents?","options":["Iridium, a metal rare at the Earth\'s surface","Fossilised dinosaur eggs","Volcanic ash from India only","Modern pesticide residues"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Paragraph 2 says the clay contains iridium, rare at the surface but common in meteorites."},{"id":"b5u23-e3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"According to the passage, why did small mammals survive?","options":["They were protected by the dinosaurs","They needed less food and could shelter their young","They could fly above the dust cloud","They lived only near the equator"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 4 explains that being small meant they needed less food, could hide, and could shelter their offspring in burrows."},{"id":"b5u23-e4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"In paragraph 3, the phrase \\"a second factor\\" refers to…","options":["the loss of sunlight","the volcanic eruptions in what is now India","the arrival of birds","the accuracy of the fossil record"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"The sentence introduces the enormous volcanic eruptions already pouring lava across what is now India."},{"id":"b5u23-e5","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What is the writer\'s purpose in the final paragraph?","options":["To argue that dinosaurs could still return","To show that the same mechanism of loss is still at work today","To criticise modern farming methods","To summarise the evidence for the asteroid theory"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"The writer uses the pesticide example to show that the mechanism that removed the dinosaurs is still operating, not to attack farmers."},{"id":"b5u23-e6","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The two brothers are not ___; they look quite different.\\"","options":["accurate","stricken","identical","vast"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"If two people are identical they are exactly the same, which is why their looking different rules it out."},{"id":"b5u23-e7","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Scientists can now read the complete ___ of many animals.\\"","options":["genome","mammal","asteroid","factor"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"A genome is the complete collection of genes in a living thing, which is what can now be read."},{"id":"b5u23-e8","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Rats ___ quickly when food is plentiful.\\"","options":["regulate","multiply","analyze","evolve"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"To multiply is to increase in number, which is what happens when food is plentiful."},{"id":"b5u23-e9","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"She has been a ___ since she was sixteen and eats no meat at all.\\"","options":["majority","offspring","controversy","vegetarian"],"answerIndex":3,"explanation":"A vegetarian is someone who does not eat meat, which matches the rest of the sentence."},{"id":"b5u23-e10","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The ___ of the city\'s residents supported the plan.\\"","options":["majority","genome","pesticide","intellectual"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"A majority is more than half of a group, so it fits the idea of most residents supporting the plan."}]},"24":{"id":"b5-u24","book":5,"unit":24,"title":"The Clinic at the Edge of the Sand","level":"B2","topic":"A village nurse\'s working life","generatedAt":"2026-09-23","wordCount":501,"paragraphs":["My grandmother trained as a nurse in a city hospital, and then, at twenty-three, she took a post in a village at the edge of a [[sandy]] plain, four hours from the nearest pharmacy. She stayed for thirty-one years.","The clinic was one room with a concrete floor and a shelf of [[earthen]] pots. There was no [[machine]] except a refrigerator that ran on a car battery, and no [[medication]] beyond what she could carry back from the city on the bus. What she had instead was time, and an appetite for detail that made her formidable. She would sit with a patient for an hour, asking about sleep, appetite, the colour of things, until a pattern emerged from what had first looked like noise.","She was [[skillful]] with her hands in a way that the hospital had never taught her. She could [[peel]] the bark of a root and prepare it in a [[bowl]] while carrying on a conversation about something else entirely. Her [[cooking]] was plain and good, and she fed people as often as she treated them, on the theory that a patient who eats is already making [[progress]].","Not everyone welcomed her. Some of the older men were [[hostile]] to the idea of a young woman giving orders, and there were weeks when she had to [[dominate]] a room simply to be heard. She was also stubbornly correct about facts, which did not help her case. When a local healer told a family to stop a child\'s [[prescription]] and rely on a charm, she said plainly that the advice was [[incorrect]] and explained why. It cost her a friend and saved the child. There was a permanent [[shortage]] of almost everything — gloves, clean water, time — and she learned to work inside the shortage rather than complain about it. She had watched, in the city, how easily a hospital can [[oppress]] the people it is meant to serve: by making them wait, by speaking over them, by turning an illness into paperwork.","The village was [[dense]] with people in the dry season, when the herds came in from the plain, and almost empty in the wet. She kept notes on every patient in a series of school exercise books, and she measured what people ate, their daily [[intake]] of salt and water, because she suspected it explained more than anyone had noticed. She treated the poor [[likewise]] and the rich, which in practice meant that the rich waited. What she gave people, more than medicine, was [[dignity]]: she never asked a difficult question in front of an audience, never discussed a body where it could be overheard. When she died, the district office sent a form asking for her qualifications, and someone wrote back that she had spent thirty-one years in one village and had never once been asked to leave. Her notebooks were never published. They remain in a cupboard, and they are the most [[obscure]] medical archive I know of, and probably the truest."],"exercises":[{"id":"b5u24-e1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What does the passage say the narrator\'s grandmother had instead of equipment?","options":["Time and an appetite for detail","A team of trained assistants","Money from the district office","Training in traditional medicine"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Paragraph 2 says that what she had instead was time and an appetite for detail that made her formidable."},{"id":"b5u24-e2","type":"multiple-choice","question":"How did she respond when a local healer told a family to stop a child\'s prescription?","options":["She said the advice was incorrect and explained why","She reported the healer to the district office","She said nothing and let the family decide","She offered the family money for the medicine"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Paragraph 4 says she said plainly that the advice was incorrect and explained why; it cost her a friend and saved the child."},{"id":"b5u24-e3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"Why did she measure people\'s daily intake of salt and water?","options":["To fill in the district\'s monthly forms","Because she suspected diet explained more than anyone had noticed","To decide who should receive free food","Because she was writing a book about the village"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 5 says she measured their intake because she suspected it explained more than anyone had noticed."},{"id":"b5u24-e4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"In paragraph 4, the writer says a hospital can \\"oppress\\" the people it is meant to serve. What is meant by this?","options":["It charges patients more than they can pay","It treats them in ways that make them powerless","It employs too many staff for the work available","It refuses to treat serious illnesses"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"The examples given — making people wait, speaking over them, turning illness into paperwork — all describe treatment that leaves patients powerless."},{"id":"b5u24-e5","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What is the writer\'s attitude towards the grandmother\'s notebooks?","options":["Disappointed that they were never published","Convinced they are the most truthful record of her work","Embarrassed by their lack of scientific method","Certain that they contain serious errors"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"The writer calls them the most obscure medical archive he knows of \\"and probably the truest\\"."},{"id":"b5u24-e6","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Add the flour to a large ___ and mix it well.\\"","options":["prescription","bowl","intake","shortage"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"A bowl is a round, deep dish, which is what flour would be mixed in."},{"id":"b5u24-e7","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The doctor wrote a ___ for a course of antibiotics.\\"","options":["shortage","dignity","prescription","progress"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"A prescription is a doctor\'s written permission to obtain medicine."},{"id":"b5u24-e8","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"There is a serious ___ of clean water in the camp.\\"","options":["shortage","intake","medication","cooking"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"A shortage means not having enough of something, which is exactly the situation described."},{"id":"b5u24-e9","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"She ___ the oranges and put the skins in a bin.\\"","options":["dominated","oppressed","likewise","peeled"],"answerIndex":3,"explanation":"To peel fruit is to remove its skin, which is what the sentence describes."},{"id":"b5u24-e10","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"His ___ of sugar is far higher than the recommended level.\\"","options":["intake","shortage","dignity","machine"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Your intake of something is the amount of it you take into your body, which fits a measured level of sugar."}]},"25":{"id":"b5-u25","book":5,"unit":25,"title":"The Man Who Counted Lizards","level":"B2","topic":"A field naturalist and a threatened species","generatedAt":"2026-09-23","wordCount":410,"paragraphs":["Elias Moreau was never a [[celebrity]], and the idea would have embarrassed him. For twenty-six years he taught [[zoology]] at a [[rural]] school, and he was [[esteemed]] less for his research than for the fact that he answered every letter he received, including the ones from children.","His subject was a small brown lizard that lived in a [[swamp]] an hour\'s walk from the school. Most people in the district had never seen one and did not believe it existed. To reach the colony he had to [[traverse]] ground that flooded every spring, and he did it four hundred times, usually alone. The [[species]] had been described only once, in a paper so vague that nobody could say for certain whether it still existed anywhere at all.","He was not a romantic. When a light [[aircraft]] began flying [[overhead]] on a survey route, he wrote to the company and asked for the photographs, then used them to map the wetland from above. That was [[decisive]] in a quiet way: the map showed that the swamp was three times larger than the official record claimed. A national [[institute]] took an interest, and money appeared, and with the money came a fence.","The fence saved the lizards and divided the village. Some farmers resented it; others were simply [[jealousy|jealous]] of the attention. Moreau had a [[principle]] about this that he repeated until people were tired of hearing it: the animals had no vote, so somebody had to speak for them, and that somebody should be whoever had done the reading. He also insisted that helping a species was not the same as [[nurture|nurturing]] it. The lizards were [[hardy]]; what they needed was ground to live on, not keepers. In autumn they [[migrate|migrated]] to a [[secluded]] corner of the marsh and waited out the winter there.","By the time he retired, the colony had doubled. The new visitor centre was built of [[concrete]] and glass and looked, he said, like a bus shelter for giants, and he refused to have his name on it. Two other species in the district had gone [[extinct]] during his lifetime, and he never pretended that one success cancelled that out. What he said instead, in the last interview he gave, was that the [[ethical]] question was not whether we should save a lizard, but whether we could honestly explain to anyone why we had decided not to. The fence is still standing. The lizards are still there."],"exercises":[{"id":"b5u25-e1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"Why was Moreau esteemed in the district?","options":["He had published famous research","He answered every letter he received, including from children","He had discovered a new species","He had worked for a national institute"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 1 says he was esteemed less for his research than for answering every letter he received, including the ones from children."},{"id":"b5u25-e2","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What did the aerial photographs reveal?","options":["The swamp was three times larger than the official record claimed","The lizard had moved to another valley","The wetland had already dried out","A new road was planned through the marsh"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Paragraph 3 says the map showed the swamp was three times larger than the official record claimed."},{"id":"b5u25-e3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"How did Moreau respond when the visitor centre was built?","options":["He asked for it to be named after him","He refused to have his name on it","He campaigned against it","He designed the building himself"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 5 says he compared it to a bus shelter for giants and refused to have his name on it."},{"id":"b5u25-e4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"In paragraph 4, Moreau\'s \\"principle\\" is that…","options":["animals should be kept in captivity for their own safety","someone must speak for those who cannot speak for themselves","scientists should avoid publicity of any kind","fences are the only way to protect wildlife"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"He said the animals had no vote, so somebody had to speak for them, and that somebody should be whoever had done the reading."},{"id":"b5u25-e5","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What does the final sentence suggest about Moreau\'s view of conservation?","options":["Saving one species justifies ignoring the others","The harder question is how we justify deciding not to act","Ethical questions are irrelevant to science","Extinct species can usually be replaced"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"He reframed the ethical question as whether we could honestly explain a decision not to save a species, which shifts the burden onto those who do nothing."},{"id":"b5u25-e6","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The dodo became ___ in the seventeenth century.\\"","options":["hardy","extinct","secluded","esteemed"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"If a species is extinct, none of its members are left, which is what happened to the dodo."},{"id":"b5u25-e7","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Every autumn the birds ___ south to warmer regions.\\"","options":["nurture","traverse","migrate","celebrate"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"To migrate means to move from one place to another, which is what birds do each autumn."},{"id":"b5u25-e8","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The explorers had to ___ a hundred miles of desert.\\"","options":["traverse","nurture","principle","migrate"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To traverse means to move or travel through an area, which fits crossing a stretch of desert."},{"id":"b5u25-e9","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The old professor is widely ___ for her work on insects.\\"","options":["extinct","rural","hardy","esteemed"],"answerIndex":3,"explanation":"If someone is esteemed, many people respect them, which is what the sentence describes."},{"id":"b5u25-e10","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"They built a wall of ___ to hold back the sea.\\"","options":["swamp","concrete","zoology","jealousy"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Concrete is a hard building material made from stones, so a wall of it could hold back the sea."}]},"26":{"id":"b5-u26","book":5,"unit":26,"title":"The Secondhand Boat","level":"B2","topic":"Learning a practical skill","generatedAt":"2026-09-23","wordCount":480,"paragraphs":["The boat was [[secondhand]] when I bought her, and she had been secondhand twice before that. She was twenty-two feet long, built of wood, and she leaked in a way that was neither a disaster nor quite acceptable. I paid less for her than I had paid for my bicycle.","I knew almost nothing. My [[knowledge]] of engines came from a single afternoon spent watching someone else repair a lawnmower, and I had no [[skill]] with tools at all. What I did have was a [[genuine]] desire to learn, and a shed to work in, and a stubborn dislike of being beaten by a machine. I bought a manual, which turned out to describe an engine slightly different from mine, and that taught me my first lesson: the book is a guide and not a promise.","The engine was the real problem. Its [[gears]] had been worn smooth, and there was [[grease]] everywhere — on the floor, on the walls, in the seams of my coat. The [[dock]] where she sat was a mile from my house, so I went there [[frequent|frequently]], sometimes twice a day, usually to discover that I had brought the wrong spanner. On the good days I got something done; on the bad days I simply stood in the cockpit, looked at the water, and made a list.","My [[tactic]] was simple and slightly obsessive: I photographed every part before I removed it, and wrote the number of each screw in a notebook. I [[designate|designated]] one shelf for parts that were clean and another for parts that were not, which sounds orderly and never stayed that way for long. Even so, I managed to [[omit]] one bolt from the record, and spent a whole evening working out where it had come from. There is a [[slot]] in the gearbox housing that will accept a bolt of two different lengths, and if you choose the wrong one you will [[distort]] the casing when you tighten it. I chose the wrong one. I did not make that mistake twice.","The [[context]] matters, I think. I was not restoring a valuable boat; I was learning to be the kind of person who could. Every hour spent on her [[offset]] the hours I spent at a desk, and by the second summer the balance had shifted. The engine started on the third pull. I could [[cause]] it to run smoothly, or nearly, and I could hear from the sound alone when something was wrong. One evening I dropped the [[anchor]] in the bay and sat there for an hour doing nothing at all. The [[energy]] that had gone into the work came back as something like quiet. Afterwards I rearranged my week [[accordingly]], giving the boat two days instead of one. The office and the shed [[overlap|overlapped]] more and more, until I stopped thinking of them as two separate lives."],"exercises":[{"id":"b5u26-e1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What did the writer know about engines before buying the boat?","options":["He had repaired several of them before","Almost nothing — he had once watched someone repair a lawnmower","He had trained as a mechanic","He had read many books on the subject"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 2 says his knowledge came from a single afternoon watching someone else repair a lawnmower."},{"id":"b5u26-e2","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What was the writer\'s tactic when taking the engine apart?","options":["He worked only from the manual","He photographed every part and recorded each screw","He asked a friend to help him","He replaced the engine with a new one"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 4 describes his tactic as photographing every part before removing it and writing the number of each screw in a notebook."},{"id":"b5u26-e3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What mistake did the writer make with the gearbox?","options":["He used the wrong kind of grease","He fitted a bolt of the wrong length and distorted the casing","He lost one of the gears","He left the slot uncovered"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 4 explains that choosing the wrong length of bolt distorts the casing when tightened, and he chose the wrong one."},{"id":"b5u26-e4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"In paragraph 5, the writer says every hour spent on the boat \\"offset\\" the hours at a desk. This means the work on the boat…","options":["took time away from his job","balanced out or made up for the office hours","cost more than he earned","was paid for by his employer"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"To offset means to cancel out the effect of something else, so the boat work balanced the desk work."},{"id":"b5u26-e5","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What is the main point of the passage?","options":["Restoring a boat is a waste of money","Learning a practical skill changed how the writer lived","Secondhand boats are always unreliable","Boat engines are simpler than they look"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"The passage ends with the writer no longer seeing the office and the shed as separate lives, which shows how the skill reshaped his life."},{"id":"b5u26-e6","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"He bought a ___ car because a new one was too expensive.\\"","options":["genuine","frequent","secondhand","decisive"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"Something secondhand has been owned by someone else, which explains why it was cheaper than new."},{"id":"b5u26-e7","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Put some ___ on the hinge so the door stops squeaking.\\"","options":["grease","slot","anchor","tactic"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Grease is an oily substance put on moving parts so they work smoothly, which is what a squeaking hinge needs."},{"id":"b5u26-e8","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The report was long, so I decided to ___ the final section.\\"","options":["offset","omit","distort","overlap"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"To omit something is to leave it out, which is what the writer did with the final section."},{"id":"b5u26-e9","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Insert the coin into the ___ and press the button.\\"","options":["dock","gear","slot","grease"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"A slot is a narrow opening in a machine or container, which is where a coin would be inserted."},{"id":"b5u26-e10","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Higher fuel costs were ___ by a fall in ticket prices.\\"","options":["omitted","distorted","caused","offset"],"answerIndex":3,"explanation":"To offset means to use one thing to cancel out the effect of another, which is what the fall in ticket prices did to the fuel costs."}]},"27":{"id":"b5-u27","book":5,"unit":27,"title":"The Forest That Makes Its Own Rain","level":"C1","topic":"Why rainforests matter beyond their borders","generatedAt":"2026-09-23","wordCount":406,"paragraphs":["A [[rainforest]] is not just a wet place with trees in it. It is a system that makes its own weather. Every day the forest releases water vapour through its leaves; that vapour rises, cools, and falls again as rain, often within a few hours and often in the same place. Cut the trees and the rain does not simply continue without them. It stops.","The largest of these systems lies near the [[equator]], where sunlight is strong and steady. Its [[capacity]] to store carbon is enormous — greater, hectare for hectare, than almost any other landscape on Earth. The [[formation]] of its soil is slow and the soil itself is surprisingly thin, which is why cleared land there recovers so badly. What looks like a deep, rich, [[wooded]] world is often a thin skin of life stretched over poor ground.","The damage works in two ways, and both are [[cumulative]]. First, clearing releases carbon; second, it removes the trees that would have absorbed it. Every year the forest [[deposit|deposits]] less carbon into its soils and [[distribute|distributes]] less water into the air. The [[frequency]] of drought rises. Since the forest produces a large share of the [[oxygen]] and rainfall that the region depends on, this is [[crucial]] not only for the animals that live there but for the farms hundreds of kilometres away.","Conservationists have learned that a single [[strategy]] is rarely enough. The [[objective]] is not to fence off a [[comprehensive]] reserve and hope; it is to make standing forest more [[beneficial]] to the people who live beside it than cleared land would be. Some schemes pay communities to [[conserve]] a watershed. Others certify timber. A [[comparative]] study of two neighbouring districts, one with payments and one without, found that the payments slowed clearing by roughly a third — a modest result, but a real one. National and [[federal]] governments matter too, because most of the money and nearly all of the law sits with them, and a rule that is not enforced is not a rule.","The forest is also a [[birthplace]]: a large fraction of the medicines we use were first identified in plants that grow nowhere else. Some of those plants look [[exotic]] and useless, and some of them are the reason a child in another hemisphere survives an infection. We do not know which. That uncertainty is the argument. A library you have not read is not worthless; it is unread."],"exercises":[{"id":"b5u27-e1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What is the main idea of the passage?","options":["Rainforests are wet because they are close to the sea","A rainforest creates its own rainfall, and losing it damages far more than the forest itself","Only federal governments can protect forests effectively","Exotic plants are the only reason to conserve forests"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"The passage explains that the forest generates its own rainfall and that its loss affects rainfall, carbon and medicine far beyond its own borders."},{"id":"b5u27-e2","type":"multiple-choice","question":"Why does the passage say cleared tropical land recovers badly?","options":["The soil is thin and its formation is slow","There are too many animals competing for space","The rain never stops long enough","The land is too flat to drain"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Paragraph 2 says the formation of the soil is slow and the soil itself is surprisingly thin, which is why cleared land recovers badly."},{"id":"b5u27-e3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"According to the passage, why is the damage described as \\"cumulative\\"?","options":["Because it happens only in a single season","Because clearing both releases carbon and removes the trees that would absorb it","Because it can be reversed easily if payments are made","Because it affects only small areas at a time"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 3 sets out two effects that add up: clearing releases carbon and removes the trees that would have absorbed it."},{"id":"b5u27-e4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What did the comparative study of two districts find?","options":["Payments slowed clearing by about a third","Payments had no measurable effect","Certified timber worked better than payments","Both districts cleared land at the same rate"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Paragraph 4 says the payments slowed clearing by roughly a third — modest, but real."},{"id":"b5u27-e5","type":"multiple-choice","question":"In the final paragraph, the comparison of the forest to \\"a library you have not read\\" suggests that…","options":["the forest has little practical value","unused natural resources may still hold unknown value","books and forests should both be protected by law","scientists have already identified all the useful plants"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"The writer\'s point is that not yet having read a library does not make it worthless, which applies the same reasoning to unstudied plants."},{"id":"b5u27-e6","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Eating more vegetables is ___ for your health.\\"","options":["comparative","cumulative","beneficial","exotic"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"If something is beneficial, it is good for you, which is what the sentence claims about vegetables."},{"id":"b5u27-e7","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The stadium has a ___ of eighty thousand people.\\"","options":["formation","capacity","frequency","strategy"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"The capacity of something is the amount it can hold, so it fits the number of people a stadium can contain."},{"id":"b5u27-e8","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The charity aims to ___ the remaining wild tigers.\\"","options":["distribute","deposit","conserve","pollute"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"To conserve something is to protect it from being destroyed, which is what a charity would do for wild tigers."},{"id":"b5u27-e9","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The teacher asked us to ___ the leaflets to the whole class.\\"","options":["conserve","deposit","distribute","regulate"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"To distribute something is to give it to a number of people, which is what happens with leaflets in a class."},{"id":"b5u27-e10","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Water is made of hydrogen and ___.\\"","options":["oxygen","equator","rainforest","formation"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Oxygen is the gas that combines with hydrogen to form water."}]},"28":{"id":"b5-u28","book":5,"unit":28,"title":"The First Witness","level":"C1","topic":"How one ancient account shaped the reporting of disease","generatedAt":"2026-09-23","wordCount":456,"paragraphs":["In the second year of a long siege, a disease arrived in a crowded city and killed perhaps a quarter of its people. We know this because one man wrote it down. His account is short, unsentimental, and unlike anything written before it, and historians of medicine still argue about what it proves.","The writer began by [[define|defining]] his purpose. He would not explain the illness as a punishment sent by the gods, as was usual at the time; he would describe what he had seen and let others judge. He had caught the [[plague]] himself and survived it. He wrote that people [[dread|dreaded]] it more than any other danger, because it struck the healthy and the careful as readily as anyone else, and that fear of it did more damage than the disease. The symptoms he describes are, [[presently]], still argued over by doctors, which is a strange kind of immortality for a paragraph written so long ago.","The details are [[horrifying]] and he does not soften them. The illness seemed [[random]]: it took the strong and spared the weak, and no treatment was of any [[avail]]. As it [[worsen|worsened]], people stopped visiting the sick, and the city\'s customs broke down. Temples and [[shrine|shrines]] filled with the dying and then with the dead. There were episodes of [[riot|rioting]] when rumours spread that the water supply had been poisoned. He notes, without comment, that those who nursed the sick were the most likely to die, and that many of them were also the only people who behaved well. Elsewhere he writes about [[solitude]] as though he knew it personally — the particular loneliness of being the one who survives.","Later writers [[paraphrase|paraphrased]] him for centuries, and in doing so they often lost the point. They kept the [[stark]] facts and dropped the method. What makes the account remarkable is not the suffering but the refusal to explain it away. He does not [[summon]] the gods, and he does not pretend to certainty. When he lists the symptoms he sounds [[incredulous]] of his own memory, checking himself, correcting a detail he has already written. Some scholars believe he [[scribble|scribbled]] the whole passage in a hurry and never revised it. Whatever the truth of that, the effect [[linger|lingers]]: you are reading a witness, not a moralist.","Modern epidemiology has [[expand|expanded]] on his work in ways he could not have imagined. We now know that a [[fundamental]] feature of such outbreaks is that they are caused by an [[organism]] that intends nothing at all — no anger, no message, no lesson. That is precisely what he had grasped without the vocabulary to say it. He could not name the cause. He could, and did, insist that it had one."],"exercises":[{"id":"b5u28-e1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What made the writer\'s account different from earlier descriptions of illness?","options":["He blamed the gods for the disease","He described what he had seen instead of explaining it as a punishment","He wrote the whole account in verse","He refused to mention the dead"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 2 says he would not explain the illness as a punishment sent by the gods, as was usual; he would describe what he had seen."},{"id":"b5u28-e2","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What does the passage say about who the illness killed?","options":["It killed only the very old","It seemed random, taking the strong as readily as the weak","It killed only those who nursed the sick","It spared everyone who left the city"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 3 says the illness seemed random: it took the strong and spared the weak."},{"id":"b5u28-e3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"According to the passage, what happened to the city\'s customs?","options":["They became stricter than before","They broke down as people stopped visiting the sick","They were written down for the first time","They were protected by the temples"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 3 says that as the illness worsened, people stopped visiting the sick and the city\'s customs broke down."},{"id":"b5u28-e4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"In paragraph 4, the writer says later writers paraphrased the account but \\"lost the point\\". What was the point they lost?","options":["The exact list of symptoms","The refusal to explain the disaster away","The location of the city","The name of the disease"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"The passage says they kept the stark facts and dropped the method, and that what makes the account remarkable is the refusal to explain it away."},{"id":"b5u28-e5","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What is the writer\'s main purpose in this passage?","options":["To argue that ancient medicine was superior to modern medicine","To show how a careful witness shaped the way we think about disease","To describe the symptoms of a modern illness","To criticise later historians for copying"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"The passage traces how one account, by insisting on observation rather than explanation, anticipated the modern approach to outbreaks."},{"id":"b5u28-e6","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"No amount of arguing was of any ___; the decision had already been made.\\"","options":["avail","solitude","riot","shrine"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Avail means help or benefit, so \\"of any avail\\" means of any use at all."},{"id":"b5u28-e7","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The weather is expected to ___ over the weekend, with heavy rain and strong winds.\\"","options":["linger","worsen","dread","paraphrase"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"To worsen is to get worse, which is what heavy rain and strong winds would do to the weather."},{"id":"b5u28-e8","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The machine picks numbers in a completely ___ order.\\"","options":["stark","fundamental","random","incredulous"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"If something is random, it happens without any pattern, which is what the sentence describes."},{"id":"b5u28-e9","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"She was ___ when she heard the news and refused to believe it.\\"","options":["horrifying","random","stark","incredulous"],"answerIndex":3,"explanation":"If someone is incredulous about something, they do not believe it is true, which matches her refusal to believe the news."},{"id":"b5u28-e10","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The teacher asked us to ___ the paragraph in our own words.\\"","options":["dread","summon","paraphrase","linger"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"To paraphrase is to restate someone else\'s writing in your own words, which is what the teacher asked for."}]},"29":{"id":"b5-u29","book":5,"unit":29,"title":"Eight Hundred Kilometres on Sunlight","level":"B2","topic":"A solar car competition and what it changes","generatedAt":"2026-09-23","wordCount":464,"paragraphs":["The machines that line up for the Continental Solar Challenge do not look like [[automobile|automobiles]]. They are flat, low, and covered in dark panels, and they carry one person lying almost flat. The event is less a race than a [[tournament]], with separate classes for different designs, and the winner is decided on speed and energy used together. For a few days every second year, these are the most efficient vehicles in the world, and the most efficient form of [[transportation]] on the planet.","The route crosses a whole [[territory]], from the northern coast to the southern desert, and the rules are strict about how much stored energy each team may use. Between checkpoints, cars must run on sunlight alone. The [[interval]] between arrivals can be hours, and a single cloud can decide the race. Drivers describe the [[phenomenon]] of driving into a [[parallel]] band of shadow — a corridor of shade cast by a distant storm — and watching their speed fall without touching anything.","Each team begins with the same [[guideline|guidelines]]: a maximum panel area, a minimum weight, a list of permitted materials. Within those limits, everything else is open. Teams [[modify]] the shape of the body, [[incorporate]] new battery chemistry, and try to [[enhance]] the efficiency of the motor by fractions of a percent. Most of the technical detail stays [[confidential]] until the race is over, and rival engineers then spend the following year reading each other\'s reports.","Not long ago the event was [[ridicule|ridiculed]] as a student hobby. That changed when [[corporate]] sponsors began to see it as a testing ground: a [[candidate]] technology that survives eight hundred kilometres of desert is more convincing than one that survives a laboratory. Engineers who once worked only on racing cars now [[interact]] with bus manufacturers and with teams designing [[mobile]] power units for clinics that have no connection to a grid. The panels on a racing car and the panels on a village roof are not the same product, but they share a supply chain, and the race has quietly made that chain cheaper. It has not removed a single gram of carbon from the air by itself; cars that run on [[solar]] power do not [[pollute]] while they drive, but they are built in factories that do. The point is the argument the race makes, not the emissions it saves.","Whether that argument wins is another matter. The [[era]] of cheap oil will not end because of a race in the desert. But the engineers who will design what comes after it are, at this moment, somewhere in a workshop, cutting foam and arguing about weight. That is how transitions actually begin: not with a speech, but with a team of six people trying to make a car one kilogram lighter."],"exercises":[{"id":"b5u29-e1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What is unusual about the cars in the Continental Solar Challenge?","options":["They are flat and low and carry one person lying down","They have no motors at all","They carry four passengers each","They run only at night"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Paragraph 1 describes them as flat, low, covered in dark panels, and carrying one person lying almost flat."},{"id":"b5u29-e2","type":"multiple-choice","question":"According to the passage, what can decide the race?","options":["The number of checkpoints on the route","A single cloud","The weight of the driver","The size of the battery"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 2 says a single cloud can decide the race."},{"id":"b5u29-e3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"Why have corporate sponsors become interested in the event?","options":["It is cheap to sponsor","It is a convincing testing ground for new technology","It guarantees media coverage","It replaces laboratory testing entirely"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 4 says sponsors see it as a testing ground, because a technology that survives the desert is more convincing than one that survives a laboratory."},{"id":"b5u29-e4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"In paragraph 4, the writer says solar cars \\"do not pollute while they drive, but they are built in factories that do\\". What point is being made?","options":["Solar cars are of no practical use","The environmental benefit of a technology depends on more than how it is used","Factories should be closed down","Races in the desert should be banned"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"The sentence draws attention to the manufacturing stage, showing that a clean vehicle can still carry an environmental cost."},{"id":"b5u29-e5","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What does the final paragraph suggest about how technological change begins?","options":["With government speeches","With small teams working on practical problems","With a sudden rise in oil prices","With international agreements"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"The writer says transitions begin not with a speech but with a team of six people trying to make a car a kilogram lighter."},{"id":"b5u29-e6","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The report is ___ and must not be shared outside the company.\\"","options":["mobile","parallel","confidential","solar"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"If something is confidential, it must be kept secret, which is why it cannot be shared."},{"id":"b5u29-e7","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The two roads run ___ to each other for several miles.\\"","options":["parallel","corporate","confidential","mobile"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"If two lines are parallel, they stay the same distance apart along their whole length."},{"id":"b5u29-e8","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"We had to ___ the design slightly to fit the smaller engine.\\"","options":["incorporate","modify","enhance","ridicule"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"To modify something is to change it a little, which is what fitting a smaller engine required."},{"id":"b5u29-e9","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The new software should ___ the speed of the whole system.\\"","options":["pollute","interact","enhance","ridicule"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"To enhance something is to make it better, which fits improving a system\'s speed."},{"id":"b5u29-e10","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Factory waste has ___ the river for decades.\\"","options":["enhanced","modified","incorporated","polluted"],"answerIndex":3,"explanation":"To pollute is to make air, water or land dirty, which is what factory waste does to a river."}]},"30":{"id":"b5-u30","book":5,"unit":30,"title":"What the Storm Left","level":"B2","topic":"Four generations on one farm","generatedAt":"2026-09-23","wordCount":407,"paragraphs":["The farm had been in the family for four generations, and the question of who would take it on had been answered badly each time. My great-grandfather was the [[heir]] nobody wanted: the third son, the one who had failed at school and been sent to a military academy to learn [[martial]] discipline, and who came home at nineteen with a [[sergeant|sergeant\'s]] manner and no interest in farming at all.","He arrived in the year the [[telegraph]] reached the district, which meant that news and prices travelled faster than a horse. That single wire changed everything. It told him what the harvest would fetch before he had sold it, and roughly what the [[bill|bills]] would come to before the merchants sent them. He walked the [[boundary]] of the property twice, counted the fields, and decided that the farm would pay if he treated it like a business. He was not a man who enjoyed being proved right; he simply found it easier than being wrong.","He was [[consistent]] in a way that nobody in the family had been. He planted the same crops in the same rotation for eleven years, kept [[poultry]] in the orchard to deal with the insects, and refused to buy the new chemical fertilisers, not from principle but because the [[organic]] method was cheaper and he did not believe the advertisements. He sold wool to a [[textile]] mill two towns away and bought almost nothing. My grandmother remembered that her entire [[wardrobe]] as a girl consisted of three dresses and two pairs of boots, and that she was still the best-dressed child in the school.","Then came the summer of the storms. There was a [[tornado]] in June that took the roof off the barn, and a [[cyclone]] in August that flattened the orchard, and — rare for that latitude — a [[typhoon]] in September that arrived with three days\' warning and still found everyone unprepared. The family [[scramble|scrambled]] to move the animals, and the [[chaos]] was total: gates left open, lambs in the kitchen, a piano in the yard. The neighbours [[wail|wailed]] when the last of the orchard went down, and my great-grandmother stood in the doorway and said nothing. My great-grandfather\'s [[stance]] never changed. He walked out into the [[sheer]] noise of the wind, closed a gate that had been left open, and came back. The farm was [[doomed]] by then, though it took another decade for anyone to admit it."],"exercises":[{"id":"b5u30-e1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"Why was the great-grandfather an unexpected heir?","options":["He was the third son, who had failed at school and been sent to a military academy","He was the eldest son of the family","He had been adopted as a small child","He was a distant cousin from another town"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Paragraph 1 describes him as the heir nobody wanted: the third son who had failed at school and been sent to a military academy."},{"id":"b5u30-e2","type":"multiple-choice","question":"How did the telegraph change the farm?","options":["It let him know prices and bills sooner","It brought more workers to the district","It made the storms easier to predict","It allowed him to sell the farm"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Paragraph 2 says it told him what the harvest would fetch and roughly what the bills would come to, before the merchants sent them."},{"id":"b5u30-e3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"Why did he refuse to buy the new chemical fertilisers?","options":["He could not afford them at any price","He believed the organic method was cheaper and distrusted the advertisements","The government had banned them in that district","His wife preferred the old methods"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 3 says he refused them not from principle but because the organic method was cheaper and he did not believe the advertisements."},{"id":"b5u30-e4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"In paragraph 4, the writer says the great-grandfather\'s \\"stance never changed\\". What does this show?","options":["He was calm and unshakeable in a crisis","He did not understand the danger they were in","He was angry with the rest of the family","He had already decided to sell the farm"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"While everyone else panicked, he walked out into the noise, closed a gate and came back — the same behaviour as always."},{"id":"b5u30-e5","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What does the passage suggest about the family\'s fortunes?","options":["The farm prospered steadily for four generations","The farm was already doomed before the storms, and the storms finished it","The storms saved the farm from its debts","The family sold the farm before the storms arrived"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"The final sentence says the farm was doomed by then, implying the decline predated the storms."},{"id":"b5u30-e6","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The river marks the ___ between the two countries.\\"","options":["bill","boundary","chaos","heir"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"A boundary is the line where one area of land stops and another begins, which is what a river can mark."},{"id":"b5u30-e7","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The ___ destroyed dozens of houses along the coast.\\"","options":["typhoon","telegraph","wardrobe","textile"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"A typhoon is a large tropical storm, and only a storm could destroy houses in this way."},{"id":"b5u30-e8","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Chickens and ducks are both kinds of ___.\\"","options":["textile","chaos","poultry","heir"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"Poultry is the collective term for birds such as chickens and ducks kept for meat and eggs."},{"id":"b5u30-e9","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"He has been ___ in his work: always careful, always on time.\\"","options":["doomed","sheer","organic","consistent"],"answerIndex":3,"explanation":"If a person is consistent, they always behave in the same way, which matches being always careful and always on time."},{"id":"b5u30-e10","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The children ___ for the door as soon as the bell rang.\\"","options":["scrambled","wailed","marched","scattered"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To scramble is to move somewhere quickly and desperately, which is what the children did when the bell rang."}]}}'),r=Object.fromEntries(Object.entries({1:o,2:n,3:i,4:s,5:h,6:JSON.parse('{"1":{"id":"b6-u1","book":6,"unit":1,"title":"The Beach That Filled a Lorry","level":"C1","topic":"Waste, recycling and coastal life","generatedAt":"2026-09-23","wordCount":445,"paragraphs":["For as long as anyone in Whitcombe could remember, the beach below the harbour had cleaned itself. Whatever visitors left behind was carried off by the tide, and by the following morning the sand looked untouched. It was a [[convenient]] arrangement, and for decades nobody thought to question it. Then, one October, the wind changed and the sea gave everything back at once.","A team from the [[municipal]] council had been surveying the coast for three summers, and that autumn they published their figures. The [[incidence]] of plastic along the tideline had tripled in five years. [[boxed|Boxed]] meals, bottles, straws and bags — objects made to be [[discard|discarded]] after a single use — accounted for the greater part of it. Among the rest was a [[stuffed]] toy, still smiling, and several thousand cotton buds.","The authors of the survey were careful not to exaggerate, yet their conclusions were hard to read. For a [[dolphin]] or a [[whale]], a plastic fragment can be [[fatal]]: once swallowed, it may block the stomach, and an animal that cannot feed will slowly starve. Seabirds [[choke]] on the smaller pieces, and the fish absorb whatever is left. The [[overuse]] of packaging, the report concluded, was not merely untidy; it was [[ecologically]] reckless.","What followed was a genuine change of habit, and it did not happen quickly. From the spring, food sold in the harbour had to arrive in [[reusable]] or [[biodegradable]] containers. Shops were asked to [[utilize]] paper bags wherever they could, and a deposit scheme encouraged customers to [[recycle]] glass rather than leave it on the sand. Anyone who preferred an [[alternate]] arrangement — bringing their own boxes, for instance — was actively welcomed rather than tolerated.","The rules had their critics. Some traders complained that the [[adverse]] effects on their takings would far outweigh any benefit to the coast, and one or two simply ignored the ban and hoped the matter would be forgotten. Then a storm in February filled the harbour wall with rubbish, and a photograph of a gull tangled in a carrier bag appeared on the front page of the local paper. The argument ended there. It was, everyone agreed, a [[tragic]] image — and a useful one.","Two years on, the beach still needs cleaning, but a single volunteer can now clear it in an afternoon. Behind the dunes a shallow [[pond]] has been dug, and frogs have found it without any help from anyone. Whitcombe is not a model town, and it would be dishonest to pretend that every visitor is now careful. But the lorry that used to arrive each Monday morning to carry the rubbish away now comes every third week."],"exercises":[{"id":"b6u1-e1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What is the passage mainly about?","options":["Why tourism declined in a small harbour town","How a coastal village came to change its rules on waste","The biology of whales and dolphins in northern waters","How to organise a volunteer beach clean-up"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"The passage describes a survey of coastal plastic, the rules the council introduced in response, and the effect of those rules two years later."},{"id":"b6u1-e2","type":"multiple-choice","question":"According to the survey, what had happened to plastic on the tideline?","options":["It had tripled in five years","It had disappeared after the October storm","It had been traced to a single local factory","It had been halved by the council\'s new rules"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Paragraph 2 states that the incidence of plastic along the tideline had tripled in five years."},{"id":"b6u1-e3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"Why does the writer call the photograph of the gull \'useful\'?","options":["It was sold to the local paper for a considerable sum","It showed that the gull had been rescued by volunteers","It persuaded people who had resisted the new rules","It replaced the need for further surveys of the coast"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"The writer says the argument ended there, which implies the image silenced the traders who had objected to the ban."},{"id":"b6u1-e4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"As used in paragraph 3, \\"fatal\\" most nearly means…","options":["unpleasant","expensive","unavoidable","deadly"],"answerIndex":3,"explanation":"The sentence describes an animal starving after swallowing plastic, so fatal means causing death."},{"id":"b6u1-e5","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What is the writer\'s attitude towards the new rules?","options":["Approving, though not sentimental about them","Doubtful that they can be enforced at all","Angry that the traders were not consulted","Indifferent to whether they work"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"The final paragraph notes that the town is not a model town, yet records a practical improvement — approval without idealising the outcome."},{"id":"b6u1-e6","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Skipping meals has an ___ effect on concentration.\\"","options":["adverse","alternate","reusable","municipal"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"An adverse effect is a harmful or unfavourable one, which is what skipping meals has on concentration."},{"id":"b6u1-e7","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Each passenger was handed a ___ meal before the coach left the station.\\"","options":["stuffed","boxed","tragic","ecologically"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Boxed means packed inside a container, which describes a prepared meal handed out to passengers."},{"id":"b6u1-e8","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Do not ___ the receipt — you may need it if you want a refund.\\"","options":["discard","recycle","utilize","choke"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To discard something is to throw it away, which is precisely what one should avoid doing with a receipt."},{"id":"b6u1-e9","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The architects decided to ___ the old warehouse as a gallery rather than demolish it.\\"","options":["discard","recycle","utilize","choke"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"To utilize something is to use it for a particular purpose, here as a gallery."},{"id":"b6u1-e10","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The children spent the afternoon catching frogs at the ___ behind the school.\\"","options":["pond","whale","dolphin","boxed"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"A pond is a small freshwater body, the only option that frogs could plausibly be found in."}]},"2":{"id":"b6-u2","book":6,"unit":2,"title":"The Argument in the Side Room","level":"C1","topic":"Museums, heritage and who owns the past","generatedAt":"2026-09-23","wordCount":531,"paragraphs":["In a side room of a northern museum, behind a door that most visitors never open, twenty-three carved figures sit in a row on a low platform. They are [[indigenous]] to an island nine hundred kilometres away, and they have not been there for two centuries. Whether they should go back is a question that has been argued, politely and otherwise, for longer than most of the people arguing have been alive.","The story begins with an [[amateur]]: a naval officer with a private income and a great deal of curiosity. He reached the island in 1802, found the figures half-buried in scrub, and was told — in terms he later admitted were [[ambiguous]] — that they belonged to the temple rather than to any man. He removed them anyway. There was no single moment of theft. There was a slow [[parade]] of permissions, misunderstandings and small [[concession|concessions]], and then there was a ship.","A hundred and fifty years earlier, a few thousand people had [[dwell|dwelt]] on that island. Their descendants number nine thousand today, and the carving tradition never died: young sculptors there still work the same stone, and the largest of the [[statue|statues]] they produce weighs four tonnes. For them, the twenty-three figures are not art objects at all. They are relatives.","In the north, the figures were cleaned, catalogued and admired. Sculptors studied their [[geometry]]; poets wrote about the way the marble [[gleam|gleams]] under a certain light. For the museum, they were a gift to the public. For the island, they were a wound. The [[decay]] that the officers claimed to be rescuing them from was, in truth, slow — and it was a decay the islanders had been managing with considerable skill for centuries.","Only in 1983 did the island [[attain]] the legal standing to make a formal request. That request rested on a principle which museums found uncomfortable: the [[autonomy]] of a nation over the objects made within its borders. The museum\'s answer has never varied. It has [[jurisdiction]] over the collection; the collection is held in trust for the world; and a single building in which civilisations can be compared side by side has a value that no scattered arrangement can match. Neither side is lying.","The dispute has [[enrich|enriched]] a number of lawyers and exhausted everyone else. It has also, slowly, [[enlighten|enlightened]] the public about how such collections were assembled — not by [[greed]] alone, though greed played its part, but by curiosity, national pride, and a comfortable belief that the two were in [[harmony]]. The earliest letters of complaint were [[anonymous]], and they were ignored for forty years.","Meanwhile the figures sit in the side room, lit by a single window. On the island, a [[virgin]] forest of cedar still covers the ridge above the temple, and the original quarries can be visited by anyone willing to make the climb. Those who do tend to say the same thing: that the carvings in the side room are beautiful, and that they are also, unmistakably, in the wrong place. Whether a tradition can [[flourish]] when its finest work is nine hundred kilometres away is the question the two sides have never managed to settle."],"exercises":[{"id":"b6u2-e1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What is the passage mainly about?","options":["A long-running dispute over where a group of carvings belongs","The techniques used by nineteenth-century naval officers","How a museum catalogue is compiled and maintained","The decline of stone carving on a remote island"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"The whole passage traces the argument between a northern museum and the island from which the twenty-three figures came."},{"id":"b6u2-e2","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What does the passage say about how the officer obtained the figures?","options":["He bought them from the temple authorities","He was given them as a gift by the islanders","He took them after receiving unclear instructions","He found them already packed for shipment"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"Paragraph 2 says he was told in terms he admitted were ambiguous that the figures belonged to the temple, and that he removed them anyway."},{"id":"b6u2-e3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What does the writer suggest by saying that the figures are \'relatives\' to the islanders?","options":["The carvers were ancestors of the present inhabitants","The islanders regard the figures as part of their community rather than as art","Several of the figures portray members of the same family","The islanders believe the figures were made recently"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"The sentence contrasts the museum\'s view of the figures as art objects with the islanders\' sense that they belong to them personally."},{"id":"b6u2-e4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"As used in paragraph 2, \\"parade\\" most nearly means…","options":["a public procession","a display of wealth","a military ceremony","a continuous series"],"answerIndex":3,"explanation":"The word introduces a list of things following one after another — permissions, misunderstandings, concessions — so it means a series rather than a procession."},{"id":"b6u2-e5","type":"multiple-choice","question":"How does the writer treat the two sides of the argument?","options":["As a dispute in which both sides make defensible claims","As a case in which the museum is plainly dishonest","As a question that has already been settled by law","As an argument driven entirely by money"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"The writer reports the museum\'s reasoning fairly, notes that neither side is lying, and allows each position its own logic."},{"id":"b6u2-e6","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The donation was made by an ___ supporter who left no name.\\"","options":["anonymous","amateur","indigenous","ambiguous"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Anonymous means that nobody knows who the person is, which fits a donor who leaves no name."},{"id":"b6u2-e7","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"After twenty years of practice, she finally ___ the standard required for the competition.\\"","options":["enriched","attained","dwelt","flourished"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"To attain something is to succeed in reaching it, which is what she does with the required standard."},{"id":"b6u2-e8","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The wooden beams had been left to ___ for decades before anyone noticed the damage.\\"","options":["gleam","flourish","decay","attain"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"Decay is the result of something being slowly broken down naturally, which is what happened to neglected timber."},{"id":"b6u2-e9","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The polished floor ___ in the morning light.\\"","options":["decayed","dwelt","attained","gleamed"],"answerIndex":3,"explanation":"To gleam is to shine or sparkle, which is what a polished surface does in bright light."},{"id":"b6u2-e10","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"His ___ for profit eventually cost him the trust of his partners.\\"","options":["greed","harmony","autonomy","geometry"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Greed is wanting more of something than one needs or should have, which explains the loss of trust."}]},"3":{"id":"b6-u3","book":6,"unit":3,"title":"One Bicycle, Four Countries","level":"C1","topic":"Global trade and how things are made","generatedAt":"2026-09-23","wordCount":504,"paragraphs":["A [[bicycle]] sold in a European city is rarely made in one place. Its frame may be welded in one country, its wheels assembled in another, and its gears shipped from a third. Trace a single machine from the shop window back to its sources and you will pass through perhaps twenty factories, six currencies and three legal systems. Nothing about this is [[abnormal]]; it is simply how most manufactured goods now reach us.","Consider the frame. The [[aluminum]] is smelted where electricity is cheapest, which is rarely where the ore is mined. The tubing is then exported to a plant [[adjacent]] to a deep-water port, where it is cut, shaped and painted with an [[artificial]] compound developed for aircraft and now used on bicycles. What is [[absent]] from the finished product, meanwhile, is any indication of where its parts were made.","Nothing in this chain is accidental. Each step is [[configure|configured]] to take advantage of some local condition: a tax rule, a wage level, a shipping route. Firms [[consolidate]] their shipments so that a container leaves full rather than half-empty, and they spread production [[evenly]] across several countries so that no single flood or strike can stop them. The logic is as much [[fiscal]] as practical.","The people who make this work are not usually the ones who invented the product. They are agents, customs officers and [[bureaucracy|bureaucracies]] — the unglamorous machinery that moves a component across a border and records it in four places. Someone has to [[broker]] the deals, absorb the risk and argue with inspectors. A founder with a brilliant design will still fail if the paperwork is wrong, which is why the [[entrepreneur]] of popular imagination, working alone in a garage, is such a misleading figure.","For consumers, the arrangement has one obvious [[convenience]]: choice. A buyer in a small town can order a machine assembled from parts made on three continents, and it will arrive within a week. For governments, the same arrangement raises harder questions. Should a company be allowed to [[deduct]] the cost of shipping from its taxable income? Is a foreign [[franchise]] subject to local labour law? And what, exactly, should regulators [[deem]] a domestic product?","Not everyone benefits equally, and the debate is often more [[ideological]] than economic. Supporters argue that open trade has lifted hundreds of millions of people out of poverty; critics reply that it has also allowed firms to choose the cheapest country for every task and the most generous one for every tax. Both claims are [[applicable]] to the bicycle in the street, which is cheap, well made, and impossible to describe as belonging to any single country.","The next stage may be quieter. In several plants a [[robot]] now performs the welding, and the number of workers needed to build a frame has fallen by half. Meanwhile a growing share of the design work is done by software that can be [[configure|configured]] in an afternoon and rented by the month. The chain is not shortening. It is changing shape."],"exercises":[{"id":"b6u3-e1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What is the passage mainly about?","options":["How a single product depends on factories in many countries","Why bicycles are cheaper than other forms of transport","The environmental cost of shipping aluminium","How to start a manufacturing business abroad"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"The passage follows the production of one bicycle through several countries in order to explain how global supply chains work."},{"id":"b6u3-e2","type":"multiple-choice","question":"According to the passage, where is aluminium smelted?","options":["Where the ore is mined","Where electricity is cheapest","Where the bicycles are finally sold","Where labour laws are strictest"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 2 states that the aluminium is smelted where electricity is cheapest, which is rarely where the ore is mined."},{"id":"b6u3-e3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"Why does the writer describe the lone inventor in a garage as \'a misleading figure\'?","options":["Because such inventors rarely have good designs","Because garages are unsuitable places to work","Because a product\'s success depends on many people beyond its inventor","Because most inventions are now made by governments"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"The paragraph stresses that agents, customs officers and bureaucracies move goods across borders, so the solitary inventor image hides the real machinery."},{"id":"b6u3-e4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"As used in paragraph 3, \\"consolidate\\" most nearly means…","options":["strengthen an organisation","join things into one","delay until later","check carefully"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"The sentence describes combining shipments so that a container leaves full, which is the sense of bringing things together into one."},{"id":"b6u3-e5","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What is the writer\'s attitude towards the trade system described?","options":["Uncritically enthusiastic","Dismissive of its economic effects","Analytical, presenting arguments from both sides","Anxious about the future of cycling"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"The writer reports the mechanics of the chain neutrally and then sets out both the supporters\' and the critics\' positions."},{"id":"b6u3-e6","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The hospital reported an ___ number of cases for the time of year, and doctors wanted to know why.\\"","options":["abnormal","adjacent","applicable","artificial"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Abnormal means different from what is normal or average, which is why the doctors were puzzled."},{"id":"b6u3-e7","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Two of the usual members were ___ from the meeting, so no vote could be taken.\\"","options":["adjacent","absent","evenly","fiscal"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Absent means not in the place where someone is expected to be, which explains why the vote failed."},{"id":"b6u3-e8","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The bank agreed to ___ the arrangement fee from the total transfer.\\"","options":["deem","deduct","broker","consolidate"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"To deduct means to subtract an amount, here the fee, from a larger sum."},{"id":"b6u3-e9","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The two firms decided to ___ their research teams into a single department.\\"","options":["consolidate","deduct","deem","configure"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To consolidate is to bring separate things together into one, which is what happens to the two research teams."},{"id":"b6u3-e10","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The flowers were arranged ___ around the edge of the table, with equal space between each one.\\"","options":["fiscally","ideologically","evenly","abnormally"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"Evenly means divided equally in amount or spacing, which matches the equal gaps described."}]},"4":{"id":"b6-u4","book":6,"unit":4,"title":"The Garden in the Dry Country","level":"C1","topic":"A short history of a community and its water","generatedAt":"2026-09-23","wordCount":454,"paragraphs":["The [[abbey]] stands on a low ridge above a plain that receives, in a good year, thirty centimetres of rain. The country around it is [[arid]] — not desert, but close enough that every tree in the cloister garden has to be watered by hand. When the community was founded in the twelfth century, the site must have looked like an act of either optimism or stubbornness.","The first monks arrived from the coast, travelling [[inland]] for weeks, and they chose the ridge for a practical reason: it [[adjoin|adjoins]] a shallow valley where, after rain, water collects beneath the gravel. They dug. Within a generation they had a well, a channel and a set of terraces that held [[moisture]] in the soil long after the surface had dried.","What they built next was more ambitious. A [[drought]] in the 1180s, followed by a second in the 1190s, threatened to [[deprive]] the community of everything it had planted. The abbot, rather than [[grumble]] about the weather, ordered a reservoir cut into the rock. It took eleven years. It was [[nonetheless]] finished, and it is still in use.","Water, once secured, changed the place entirely. The gardens became [[abundant]]; there was [[ample]] food for the community and enough left over to give away. Records from the 1240s list orchards, a mill and a fishpond, along with a stretch of [[prairie]] to the north that was good for hay and very little else. Timber was [[scarce]] in that country, so the roofs were built of stone and the beams were reused for generations. On the [[rugged]] ground beyond, where no plough could reach, sheep were kept.","The community\'s rules were strict. Members took an [[oath]] of stability, promising to remain for life, and they [[fast|fasted]] on far more days of the year than they feasted. To join, a candidate had to be [[eligible]] — of age, in reasonable health and free of debts — and the examination could take a year. Outsiders [[speculate|speculated]] endlessly about what went on behind the walls; the truth, recorded in a surviving account book, was mostly agriculture.","The [[cathedral]] in the nearest city grew rich on the same trade in wool, and the two institutions quarrelled for two centuries about water rights. A [[crisis]] arrived in 1348, when plague reached the plain and killed more than half the community. The abbey survived, but it never recovered its earlier scale. The reservoir did.","Today the site is a working farm. The channel still runs and the terraces are still planted. Visitors who come expecting ruins are usually surprised to find the gardens in better order than the church, which lost its roof in the eighteenth century and has never been given another one."],"exercises":[{"id":"b6u4-e1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What is the passage mainly about?","options":["The architecture of medieval churches","How a dry-land community secured and used water","The causes of a fourteenth-century plague","Why wool was more profitable than grain"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"The passage follows the community\'s struggle to find, store and distribute water, and the effect that water had on its life."},{"id":"b6u4-e2","type":"multiple-choice","question":"Why was the ridge chosen as the site of the abbey?","options":["It offered the best defence against attack","It was close to the nearest cathedral","Water collects beneath the gravel of a valley beside it","The land there was already cleared for farming"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"Paragraph 2 gives a practical reason: the ridge adjoins a shallow valley where water collects after rain."},{"id":"b6u4-e3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What does the writer imply by saying that the reservoir is still in use?","options":["The community\'s engineering has outlasted the community\'s own way of life","The abbey was rebuilt on the same plan in later centuries","The drought of the 1190s never really ended","The reservoir was restored by modern engineers"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"The abbey never recovered its earlier scale, yet its water system survives — the practical work outlived the institution."},{"id":"b6u4-e4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"As used in paragraph 4, \\"ample\\" most nearly means…","options":["expensive","varied","plentiful","wasted"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"The sentence says there was enough food and some left over to give away, so ample means more than enough."},{"id":"b6u4-e5","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What is the writer\'s attitude towards the monks\' achievements?","options":["Admiring of their practical persistence","Sceptical that the reservoir was ever needed","Critical of their treatment of outsiders","Puzzled by their decision to settle there"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"The writer presents eleven years of digging and the survival of the water system as evidence of patience and practical skill."},{"id":"b6u4-e6","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Nothing grows easily in this ___ region, where rain falls only a few times a year.\\"","options":["abundant","arid","ample","eligible"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Arid describes land that is hot, dry and receives very little rain, which matches the description exactly."},{"id":"b6u4-e7","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Supplies were so ___ that the crew had to ration every meal.\\"","options":["ample","abundant","scarce","rugged"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"Scarce means there is only a very small amount, which is why rationing was necessary."},{"id":"b6u4-e8","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The path was too ___ for the trucks, so everything had to be carried up by hand.\\"","options":["rugged","arid","abundant","eligible"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Rugged ground is rocky and difficult to travel over, which explains why vehicles could not use the path."},{"id":"b6u4-e9","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Only employees who have worked here for a full year are ___ for the training grant.\\"","options":["arid","eligible","abundant","rugged"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Eligible means permitted to have or do something, here because of a length of service requirement."},{"id":"b6u4-e10","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"He grumbled about the price of fuel all winter, but he ___ refused to sell the car.\\"","options":["nonetheless","inland","amply","scarce"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Nonetheless means in spite of what was just mentioned — his complaining did not change his decision."}]},"5":{"id":"b6-u5","book":6,"unit":5,"title":"A Litre of Pond Water","level":"C1","topic":"Ecology and microscopic life","generatedAt":"2026-09-23","wordCount":461,"paragraphs":["Fill a jar with water from a quiet pond, add nothing, and leave it on a windowsill for a week. By the end of that week the water will be green, and a single drop of it, seen through a microscope, will contain more living things than the jar\'s owner has fingers and toes. This is the oldest and cheapest demonstration in [[ecology]], and it still surprises people.","The first thing a beginner notices is movement. Rotifers, no larger than grains of sand, swim in circles; water fleas jerk upwards in a [[repetitive]] rhythm that looks mechanical until you realise it is an escape response. Then come the [[bizarre]] forms: a single cell shaped like a trumpet, another like a slow green spiral. Many of these organisms are strictly [[aquatic]] — they cannot survive out of water for more than a few minutes.","Every one of them is a cell, and every cell has a [[nucleus]], a membrane and a set of instructions. What is striking is how much of that machinery we share. A pond organism must [[digest]] food, respond to light and manage its own water balance; so must we. Its [[reproductive]] cycle may last hours rather than decades, but the underlying chemistry is recognisably the same.","Not everything in the jar is a free-living creature. Some of the smaller organisms are [[parasite|parasites]], attaching themselves to larger hosts and taking food from them. Others live in partnership. A [[prominent]] example is the green cell found inside many pond animals, which photosynthesises on behalf of its host and is paid in shelter — an arrangement that has [[undergo|undergone]] hundreds of millions of years of refinement and is one of evolution\'s more impressive [[feat|feats]].","The pond itself is a small part of the [[biosphere]], but it is a complete one. Sunlight enters, plants convert it, animals eat the plants, and bacteria return the nutrients to the water. The [[duration]] of the whole cycle, from a fallen leaf to a new leaf, may be less than a month. In a [[temperate]] climate the pond freezes in winter and seethes with life in May, and the difference between those two states is a matter of a few degrees [[celsius|Celsius]].","That is why the organisms in the jar are also a warning. A pond is [[vulnerable]] to almost everything: a new road, a fertiliser spill, a rise of two degrees. Species with [[tolerance]] for only a narrow range of conditions vanish first, and what remains is a much smaller and much [[coarse|coarser]] community of the tough and the common. A jar of pond water makes an odd [[companion]] for a winter evening, but a reliable one. The variety of life is not [[infinite|infinite]], and the jar is the quickest way to watch it shrink."],"exercises":[{"id":"b6u5-e1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What is the passage mainly about?","options":["How to build a microscope at home","The variety of life in a small body of fresh water","Why ponds freeze in temperate climates","The history of the science of ecology"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"The passage examines the organisms in a jar of pond water and uses them to make a broader point about ecology."},{"id":"b6u5-e2","type":"multiple-choice","question":"According to the passage, why do water fleas jerk upwards?","options":["To escape from a threat","To attract a mate","To reach the surface for air","To shake off parasites"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Paragraph 2 says the rhythm looks mechanical until you realise it is an escape response."},{"id":"b6u5-e3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What point does the writer make about the green cell inside pond animals?","options":["It is a parasite that harms its host","It can only survive in temperate climates","It is an example of a partnership that benefits both sides","It was discovered only recently"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"The cell photosynthesises for its host and receives shelter in return — a mutually beneficial arrangement."},{"id":"b6u5-e4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"As used in paragraph 4, \\"prominent\\" most nearly means…","options":["standing out physically","well known and important","easy to overlook","difficult to explain"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"The writer is introducing a notable, well-known example rather than describing something that stands out visually."},{"id":"b6u5-e5","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What does the writer suggest in the final paragraph?","options":["Ponds should be kept away from roads and farms","The variety of life is more limited than it appears","Microscopes are unreliable instruments","Only specialists can observe ecological change"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"The writer says variety is not infinite and that a jar is the quickest way to watch it shrink."},{"id":"b6u5-e6","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Otters are perfectly adapted to an ___ life, but they move awkwardly on land.\\"","options":["aquatic","arid","temperate","repetitive"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Aquatic describes plants or animals that live in water, which is where otters are at ease."},{"id":"b6u5-e7","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The lecture was so ___ that half the audience had stopped listening by the end.\\"","options":["bizarre","repetitive","vulnerable","aquatic"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Repetitive means repeated many times until it becomes boring, which explains the audience\'s loss of attention."},{"id":"b6u5-e8","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The team\'s recovery from a two-goal deficit was a remarkable ___.\\"","options":["feat","duration","nucleus","tolerance"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"A feat is an impressive or difficult achievement, which describes the comeback."},{"id":"b6u5-e9","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The ___ of the storm was only forty minutes, but the damage took weeks to repair.\\"","options":["nucleus","feat","duration","companion"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"The duration of an event is the length of time during which it happens."},{"id":"b6u5-e10","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Young plants are ___ to frost, so they are covered at night.\\"","options":["prominent","vulnerable","infinite","temperate"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Vulnerable means weak and without protection, which is why the plants need covering."}]},"6":{"id":"b6-u6","book":6,"unit":6,"title":"The Cloth That Changed the Valley","level":"C1","topic":"Technology and the textile trade","generatedAt":"2026-09-23","wordCount":433,"paragraphs":["In the [[medieval]] wool trade, cloth was made by hand, and the process was slow enough that a single length of good fabric could occupy a family for a month. [[weave|Weaving]] was the central skill, but it was only one of several: wool had to be carded, spun, dyed and finished, and each stage employed different people in different rooms.","By the eighteenth century the [[industry]] had spread across a [[peninsula]] in the north of the country, where fast streams provided power and damp air kept the threads from snapping. Its [[prestige]] rested on quality. Its weakness was [[dependence]] — on water, on weather, and above all on the hands of skilled workers who could not easily be replaced.","Then came the machines. The first were crude, and their [[drawback]] was obvious: they produced thread of uneven quality and broke down constantly. Within fifty years, however, the technology had improved so far that hand-weaving became [[obsolete]] across most of the region. The change was [[radical]]: the [[proportion]] of workers needed for each metre of cloth fell from dozens to two, and it happened within a single generation.","The consequences were [[spectacular]] and uneven. Output rose, prices fell, and a fabric that had once been a luxury became ordinary. At the same time the old [[managerial]] arrangements — a master, a handful of apprentices, a workshop — were replaced by factories in which hundreds of people answered to one owner. The [[component]] parts of a loom were now made by specialist firms, and a single broken part could halt an entire floor.","Owners could [[impose]] long hours and low wages because the alternative was no work at all. Historians still argue about what this meant. One account holds that the new system was [[capitalism]] in its purest form, [[oriented]] entirely towards profit and indifferent to the people it displaced. A rival account insists that the transformation raised living standards for everyone within two generations, and that the early suffering, real as it was, has been exaggerated. Neither side has managed to [[refute]] the other, largely because both are describing different decades.","What is not disputed is the scale of the change. The looms were fitted with [[brass]] bearings that had to be oiled daily, and their clatter could be heard from the road. The number of mills did not [[diminish]] slowly: it fell by two thirds in twenty years, and the streams that had once powered them were left to the fish. The [[archaic]] vocabulary of the trade survives in odd places — spinner, weaver, master — words that once described an entire life."],"exercises":[{"id":"b6u6-e1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What is the passage mainly about?","options":["The rise and decline of a cloth-making region","How wool is dyed using natural materials","The working conditions of medieval apprentices","Why damp air improves the quality of thread"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"The passage traces the trade from hand production, through mechanisation, to the collapse in the number of mills."},{"id":"b6u6-e2","type":"multiple-choice","question":"According to the passage, why did the industry settle on the peninsula?","options":["It was close to the largest market for cloth","Streams gave power and damp air protected the threads","The local wool was of unusually high quality","Skilled workers had been brought there from elsewhere"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 2 mentions fast streams providing power and damp air keeping threads from snapping."},{"id":"b6u6-e3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"Why does the writer mention the brass bearings and the noise of the looms?","options":["To explain why the mills were built near rivers","To show that the new machinery required constant maintenance and dominated its surroundings","To argue that brass was more valuable than cloth","To prove that the machines were poorly designed"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Daily oiling and noise audible from the road convey how demanding and intrusive the new factories were."},{"id":"b6u6-e4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"As used in paragraph 3, \\"radical\\" most nearly means…","options":["politically extreme","very new and far-reaching","gradual","unfair"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"The sentence describes a fall in the workforce from dozens to two within one generation, which is a far-reaching change."},{"id":"b6u6-e5","type":"multiple-choice","question":"How does the writer handle the disagreement between historians?","options":["By declaring one account correct","By refusing to mention either account","By setting out both accounts and noting that neither has been disproved","By arguing that the question cannot be researched"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"The writer presents two rival accounts and says neither side has managed to refute the other."},{"id":"b6u6-e6","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The village still uses an ___ system of land measurement that no official has applied for centuries.\\"","options":["archaic","oriented","radical","spectacular"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Archaic means very old or outdated, which fits a system that officials no longer use."},{"id":"b6u6-e7","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The main ___ of the plan is that it takes four years to show any benefit.\\"","options":["component","drawback","prestige","dependence"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"A drawback is a disadvantage, which is what a long delay before any benefit represents."},{"id":"b6u6-e8","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Typewriters were not abandoned overnight, but by the 1990s they had become entirely ___.\\"","options":["spectacular","obsolete","oriented","archaic"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Obsolete describes something no longer used because something better exists, which is what happened to typewriters."},{"id":"b6u6-e9","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The council cannot simply ___ its own rules on residents without consulting them.\\"","options":["refute","impose","diminish","weave"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"To impose means to force something on other people, which is what the sentence says the council must not do."},{"id":"b6u6-e10","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Interest in the project did not fade so much as ___ after the first results were published.\\"","options":["diminish","impose","refute","weave"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To diminish means to reduce or get smaller, which describes what happened to interest."}]},"7":{"id":"b6-u7","book":6,"unit":7,"title":"Entries from a Smallholding","level":"C1","topic":"A farmer\'s diary","generatedAt":"2026-09-23","wordCount":405,"paragraphs":["3 March. The [[preliminary]] survey of the lower field is finished, and the news is not good. The [[ditch]] along its eastern edge has silted up, which means the spring rain will sit exactly where the [[tomato|tomatoes]] are meant to go. I spent the morning with a [[spade]] clearing what I could reach, and the rest will have to wait for the digger.","12 April. We [[sow|sowed]] the first [[crop]] today: barley on the high ground, where the soil drains properly. My neighbour watched from the road with what I can only describe as [[contempt]]. He has farmed here for forty years and regards anyone under sixty as a hobbyist. I am trying to be [[indifferent]] to his opinion, with limited success.","2 May. The irrigation pump is installed at last. We can now [[irrigate]] the whole southern field in a single morning, which should [[maximize]] what we take from it. The cost was considerable, and I had to ask the bank to [[finance]] it. My [[accountant]] was encouraging about the figures. My father was not encouraging about anything.","20 June. Rain, and then more rain. The ground is [[saturate|saturated]] — you cannot cross it without sinking to the ankle. As a [[precaution]] I have stopped all work on the lower field until it dries out. At dinner a [[capitalist]] friend who has never grown anything in his life explained that farming is simply a business like any other. I did not argue. I have learned that the [[simplicity]] of that view is exactly what makes it so attractive to people who hold it.","8 July. A good week. The barley is up, the tomatoes are setting, and the small [[enterprise]] at the gate — boxes of vegetables, an honesty box, no staff — has covered its costs for the first time. What I actually [[dedicate]] most of my hours to, however, is not the growing but the paperwork: [[monetary]] questions, subsidy forms, inspections. The farm is a business, the business is mostly admin, and the admin is mostly waiting.","30 July. The [[upcoming]] harvest will decide whether any of this was worth it. I believe it was, though not for reasons that a spreadsheet would recognise. The figures are mediocre at best. But at the end of a day in the field I know precisely what I have done and why, and I have never had another job of which that could be said."],"exercises":[{"id":"b6u7-e1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What is the main purpose of the diary?","options":["To argue that small farms should receive larger subsidies","To record the practical and personal realities of running a smallholding","To explain how barley differs from other cereal crops","To complain about a neighbour\'s behaviour"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"The entries mix practical tasks — ditches, irrigation, paperwork — with the writer\'s reflections on the work."},{"id":"b6u7-e2","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What problem does the writer find in the lower field?","options":["The soil there is too dry for tomatoes","The ditch has silted up and rain will collect there","The pump cannot reach that part of the farm","The barley there has failed to grow"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"The first entry says the ditch has silted up, so spring rain will sit where the tomatoes are to be planted."},{"id":"b6u7-e3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What does the writer\'s reaction to his capitalist friend suggest?","options":["He agrees that farming is like any other business","He resents being lectured but chooses not to argue","He has decided to sell the farm","He thinks his friend should buy a farm"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"He says he did not argue and then remarks ironically on the simplicity of the friend\'s view, showing quiet disagreement."},{"id":"b6u7-e4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"As used in the entry for 30 July, \\"mediocre\\" most nearly means…","options":["better than expected","not recorded anywhere","of only average quality","impossible to calculate"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"The writer contrasts the mediocre figures with his own sense that the work was worthwhile."},{"id":"b6u7-e5","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What is the writer\'s attitude towards his work by the end of the passage?","options":["He doubts that the harvest will come at all","He values it for reasons that are not financial","He regrets borrowing money for the pump","He intends to give up farming next year"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"He says he knows what he has done and why, and that no other job allowed him that — a non-financial reward."},{"id":"b6u7-e6","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"A ___ report on the site suggested that further testing would be needed before building began.\\"","options":["preliminary","monetary","saturated","indifferent"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"A preliminary report comes before a more important stage, which matches testing done before building."},{"id":"b6u7-e7","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The farmer dug a narrow ___ along the edge of the field to carry water away.\\"","options":["spade","ditch","crop","enterprise"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"A ditch is a narrow channel cut into the ground, which is what carries water away from a field."},{"id":"b6u7-e8","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"In this climate, farmers must ___ their fields twice a week or the plants will die.\\"","options":["saturate","sow","irrigate","maximize"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"To irrigate means to supply water to land so that crops can grow, which is what the sentence describes."},{"id":"b6u7-e9","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"He was completely ___ to the criticism and carried on exactly as before.\\"","options":["indifferent","preliminary","monetary","upcoming"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Indifferent means having a lack of interest in something, which explains why nothing changed."},{"id":"b6u7-e10","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Wear gloves as a ___ against the chemicals in the cleaning fluid.\\"","options":["precaution","contempt","simplicity","enterprise"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"A precaution is an action intended to stop something bad from happening, which is the purpose of the gloves."}]},"8":{"id":"b6-u8","book":6,"unit":8,"title":"Two Brothers, One Bench","level":"C1","topic":"A profile of two very different careers","generatedAt":"2026-09-23","wordCount":451,"paragraphs":["The two of them grew up in the same house, slept in the same room and were, by every measure their school had available, equally able. Today one is an [[undergraduate]] in his second year, studying [[anthropology]]; the other is a welder with a workshop on the edge of town. Neither finds the other\'s choice strange, which is perhaps the most interesting thing about them.","The elder brother, Marek, was [[appoint|appointed]] to a research assistantship last spring. He spends his weekdays [[supervise|supervising]] first-year students and his evenings reading about how different cultures arrange space. His own project concerns a [[monument]] in the town square — a stone [[cube]] — and the way people behave around it. He has found that visitors [[situate]] themselves in almost perfect [[symmetry]] on either side of it, although nobody instructs them to.","His brother Tomasz is not convinced. He argues — and this is where most of their arguments begin — that a great deal of what Marek studies is [[crude]] guesswork dressed in long words. \'You watch ten people stand in a square,\' he says, \'and you write forty pages.\' Marek accepts the criticism with more patience than most researchers would. He calls it useful [[feedback]].","Tomasz\'s own work is not obviously simpler. He builds steel frames for buildings, and his [[competence]] is not in doubt: he has passed the same certification three [[consecutive]] years, and a firm in the city has twice tried to [[confer]] with him about a permanent position. He is [[muscular]] in the way that men who lift steel all day become, and he has the [[posture]] of someone who has spent years bent over a bench — one shoulder slightly forward, head a little low.","The two of them share a [[tattoo]], a small mark on the left forearm, done badly and cheaply when they were nineteen, and very little else. Marek worries about his brother\'s [[ignorance]] of history; Tomasz worries about Marek\'s of the physical world. Tomasz, whose interests are entirely [[masculine]] in the traditional sense — engines, weights, machines — reads poetry in the evenings, a fact he does not advertise.","When the council proposed a new building for the square, Marek wrote a letter about how it would change the way people moved through the space, and Tomasz sent a photograph of the drainage. Both were ignored, which they found funny rather than disappointing. What they agree on is harder to state. Both [[applaud]] the other\'s work without hesitation, and both are quick to admit that their own is partly guesswork. Whether that is [[compatible]] with success in either field is a question neither has answered. They are thirty-one and thirty-three, and they still meet on Sundays."],"exercises":[{"id":"b6u8-e1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What is the passage mainly about?","options":["Two brothers who took very different paths and respect each other\'s work","The rivalry between a researcher and a builder","Why welding is a better career than academic research","The history of a monument in a town square"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"The profile contrasts the brothers\' careers while emphasising that neither finds the other\'s choice strange and that each admires the other\'s work."},{"id":"b6u8-e2","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What has Marek observed about visitors to the monument?","options":["They avoid standing close to it","They arrange themselves symmetrically on either side of it","They cannot agree on what it commemorates","They walk around it in a fixed direction"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 2 states that visitors situate themselves in almost perfect symmetry on either side, without being instructed to."},{"id":"b6u8-e3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What does Tomasz\'s reading of poetry suggest about him?","options":["He is considering a change of career","He is not as narrowly defined by his interests as he appears","He is embarrassed by his brother\'s research","He intends to study anthropology himself"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"The writer notes that his interests are traditionally masculine and that he reads poetry without advertising it, which complicates the picture."},{"id":"b6u8-e4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"As used in paragraph 3, \\"crude\\" most nearly means…","options":["rude and offensive","not exact or detailed","recently completed","deliberately dishonest"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Tomasz is calling the research rough and imprecise rather than offensive, as the following sentence about writing forty pages shows."},{"id":"b6u8-e5","type":"multiple-choice","question":"How does the writer present the relationship between the brothers?","options":["As one of unresolved hostility","As warm, argumentative and mutually respectful","As distant but polite","As a professional partnership"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"They argue and criticise each other, yet they applaud each other\'s work and still meet every Sunday."},{"id":"b6u8-e6","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The crowd ___ for several minutes after the final performance.\\"","options":["applauded","appointed","conferred","supervised"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To applaud means to clap in order to show approval, which is what an audience does after a performance."},{"id":"b6u8-e7","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The committee ___ her as the new director in March.\\"","options":["applauded","appointed","conferred","situated"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"To appoint someone to a job is to give them that job, which is what the committee did."},{"id":"b6u8-e8","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The two systems are not ___ with each other, so data cannot be transferred between them.\\"","options":["compatible","consecutive","crude","muscular"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Compatible means able to exist or work together successfully, which these systems cannot do."},{"id":"b6u8-e9","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"She worked for eleven ___ days without a single break.\\"","options":["consecutive","compatible","crude","masculine"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Consecutive means happening one after another without interruption, which is what eleven unbroken days are."},{"id":"b6u8-e10","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Before making a decision, the ministers met privately to ___ with their advisers.\\"","options":["applaud","confer","supervise","situate"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"To confer with someone is to discuss something with them in order to reach a decision."}]},"9":{"id":"b6-u9","book":6,"unit":9,"title":"The Field on the Hillside","level":"C1","topic":"An animal sanctuary and the people who run it","generatedAt":"2026-09-23","wordCount":481,"paragraphs":["The [[sanctuary]] occupies four hectares of hillside, most of it given over to kennels, a kitchen and a fenced field. It was founded in 1998 by a woman who had inherited a house, a modest amount of money and no clear idea what to do with either. She began with a single [[hound]], a nervous animal that had been returned to three different homes.","The building that now serves as the main [[facility]] was once a barn. It was converted over two summers and now contains a clinic, a storeroom and a room where new arrivals are kept for their first fortnight. Everything is designed to [[cater]] to animals that have been badly treated: the doors are wide, the floors are warm, and nobody raises their voice. Staff are trained never to [[yell]], even when a dog has chewed through a lead.","The admission process is deliberately slow. Before an animal is accepted it must meet a set of [[criteria]] that include a veterinary report, a behavioural assessment and evidence that no other placement exists. That paperwork [[entitle|entitles]] the sanctuary to a small grant from the county, which covers roughly a third of its costs.","Volunteers are essential. A [[pedestrian]] walking past on the road above the field will often stop and watch, and a few come back as helpers. One of them, a retired teacher, has been coming for eleven years; everyone describes her as the most [[considerate]] person on the site, and she has a [[faculty]] for calming the most frightened animals simply by sitting near them. Volunteers [[escort]] every visitor around the site, partly for insurance reasons and partly because some of the animals react badly to strangers.","The work is not sentimental. The [[consumption]] of food is enormous — a [[heap]] of tinned meat arrives every Tuesday — and the bills are paid by donations. The founder is blunt about the limits of what she can do. \'We can\'t fix the whole [[hemisphere]],\' she says. \'We can fix one field on one hillside.\' Visitors sometimes find this [[impersonal]], and she has decided that honesty is more useful than comfort.","The animals themselves are indifferent to all of it. A new arrival will spend its first days in a corner, refusing food, and then, one morning, it will cross the room, [[lick]] the hand that feeds it, and lie down in the open. Staff call this the turning. It is the only [[external]] sign they have that the work is succeeding, and it is enough.","Above the gate hangs a single [[ornament]]: a brass bell taken from the collar of the first dog, who lived eleven years. [[spectator|Spectators]] at the gate often ask whether the animals are sad. The answer, most days, is that they are busy — there is a [[crust]] of bread to be soaked, a lead to be walked and a warm place to sleep."],"exercises":[{"id":"b6u9-e1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What is the passage mainly about?","options":["The daily operation of a home for rescued animals","The legal requirements for keeping dogs","Why volunteers leave animal charities","The cost of veterinary treatment"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"The passage describes how the sanctuary began, how it is run, and how the animals respond to it."},{"id":"b6u9-e2","type":"multiple-choice","question":"Why is the admission process slow?","options":["The staff are short of time","Animals must meet several conditions before being accepted","The county requires a waiting period of a year","There is not enough space in the kennels"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 3 lists the criteria — a veterinary report, a behavioural assessment and evidence that no other placement exists."},{"id":"b6u9-e3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What does the founder\'s remark about the hemisphere suggest?","options":["She believes the problem is impossible to solve","She accepts that her work is limited in scale but still worthwhile","She wants to open sanctuaries in other countries","She thinks the county should pay all of her costs"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"She contrasts the whole hemisphere with one field on one hillside, accepting the modest scale of what she can achieve."},{"id":"b6u9-e4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"As used in paragraph 5, \\"impersonal\\" most nearly means…","options":["not friendly, making people feel unimportant","not belonging to any particular person","difficult to understand","deliberately unkind"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"The sentence explains that visitors find her manner cold, and that she prefers honesty to comfort."},{"id":"b6u9-e5","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What is the writer\'s attitude towards the sanctuary?","options":["Sentimental and uncritical","Sympathetic, while noting that the work is unsentimental","Sceptical about the value of the volunteers","Mainly concerned with the cost of food"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"The writer clearly admires the work but repeatedly stresses its practical, unsentimental character."},{"id":"b6u9-e6","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The new hotel tries to ___ to families with young children as well as business guests.\\"","options":["cater","escort","lick","yell"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To cater to someone is to provide them with what they need or want, which is what the hotel is doing for two groups."},{"id":"b6u9-e7","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"It was very ___ of you to remember that she dislikes flowers.\\"","options":["considerate","impersonal","external","consecutive"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Considerate describes someone who pays attention to the needs of others, which is what remembering her preference shows."},{"id":"b6u9-e8","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Applicants who do not meet the ___ will not be shortlisted.\\"","options":["criteria","faculties","ornaments","heaps"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Criteria are the factors on which a judgement or decision is based, which is what applicants must satisfy."},{"id":"b6u9-e9","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"A ___ in a bright coat stepped into the road without looking.\\"","options":["spectator","pedestrian","sanctuary","hound"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"A pedestrian is a person walking on a street, which is what the sentence describes."},{"id":"b6u9-e10","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"By the end of the day there was a ___ of unwashed plates beside the sink.\\"","options":["crust","heap","faculty","consumption"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"A heap is a large pile of things, which is what accumulated beside the sink."}]},"10":{"id":"b6-u10","book":6,"unit":10,"title":"Twelve Thousand Pieces","level":"C1","topic":"A museum model and the man who built it","generatedAt":"2026-09-23","wordCount":570,"paragraphs":["In the corner of the [[naval]] museum\'s second room stands a glass case containing a model of a three-masted ship, built to a scale of one to forty-eight. It is 1.4 metres long, [[exact|exactly]] to the millimetre, and it contains, by the last count, just under twelve thousand separate pieces. Most visitors walk straight past it. A few stop, and those who do tend to stay for a long time.","The model was made between 1931 and 1938 by a man who had spent his working life at sea. He had no formal training in model-making and no [[innate]] talent that anyone had noticed. What he had was patience, a set of fine tools and an [[elaborate]] system of notes, written in a hand that grew shakier as the decade went on. Those notes are now kept in a canvas [[pouch]] in the museum\'s archive.","The hull is built from boxwood, and the deck fittings were turned on a watchmaker\'s lathe. The base is a slab of grey [[marble]], with the ship\'s name engraved on it. The [[mast|masts]] alone contain more than two hundred parts, each of which had to be shaped, fitted, and then removed again before the rigging could be attached.","The builder\'s [[dilemma]] was one that every model-maker recognises. A ship can be shown under sail or at anchor, but not both; the rigging differs, and the choice has to be made early. He chose sail, which meant that the model would represent the vessel at sea with the rest of the [[fleet]], and that the arrangement of every rope had to match the original drawings. He kept the wood [[saturated]] with water while bending it, so that the planks would hold their curve.","There was a second problem, which the notes discuss at length: how to [[facilitate]] inspection without spoiling the effect. A ship in a glass case cannot be turned over, and the underside of a hull is where much of the most interesting work is. His solution was to mount the model on a [[grid]] of thin rods, so that it appears to float, and to supply a set of mirrors in a [[cardboard]] box that visitors could borrow. The box is long gone. The rods remain.","The museum\'s current [[update-n|update]] to the display adds a screen, a short film, and a note explaining what the ship was used for. Records show that it carried timber [[import|imported]] from the north coast for nineteen years and was broken up in 1929, two years before the model was begun. From the notes one can [[infer]] that the builder was not commemorating a famous vessel. He was remembering one he had sailed on.","Children often ask whether the sails are real cloth. They are, and the ropes are twisted from thread, and the whole thing weighs less than a bag of sugar. What they rarely ask is how the builder managed it. The answer is in the notes, along with the illnesses and the bad years: eleven hundred hours, two winters of [[nausea]] that he blamed on the paint, and a single entry, in 1936, recording that he had dropped the model and begun again. He refuses to [[inflate]] the achievement, adding only that a patient person with a good lamp could do the same. The last item in the museum\'s [[acquisition]] records is a small [[accessory]] from the original case: a brass boat-hook, five centimetres long."],"exercises":[{"id":"b6u10-e1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What is the passage mainly about?","options":["A detailed model ship and the man who spent years making it","The history of a nineteenth-century trading vessel","How to build a model ship using boxwood","Why museums display models instead of real ships"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"The passage describes the model, the builder\'s methods and his notes, and the museum\'s presentation of it."},{"id":"b6u10-e2","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What was the builder\'s dilemma?","options":["He could not obtain the right kind of wood","He had to choose between showing the ship under sail or at anchor","He was unsure whether the ship should be shown with the fleet","He could not decide where in the museum to display it"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 4 explains that the rigging differs between the two states and the choice had to be made early."},{"id":"b6u10-e3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"Why did the builder mount the model on a grid of rods?","options":["To make it easier for visitors to examine it from below","To protect it from changes in temperature","Because the marble base was too heavy","Because the original case had been lost"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"The rods were part of his solution to the problem of facilitating inspection of a hull that cannot be turned over."},{"id":"b6u10-e4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"As used in paragraph 6, \\"infer\\" most nearly means…","options":["to prove beyond doubt","to conclude from available evidence","to write down in order","to remember with affection"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"The writer reaches a conclusion about the builder\'s motive on the basis of the notes, which is what inferring means."},{"id":"b6u10-e5","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What impression does the writer give of the builder?","options":["A gifted craftsman who boasted of his skill","A modest and stubbornly patient man","A professional model-maker employed by the museum","A wealthy collector of maritime objects"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"He had no formal training, refuses to inflate the achievement, and kept working despite illness and a serious accident."},{"id":"b6u10-e6","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The two sides have reached a ___: either they reduce costs or they lose the contract.\\"","options":["dilemma","acquisition","accessory","pouch"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"A dilemma is a difficult situation in which a choice has to be made, which is exactly what the sentence describes."},{"id":"b6u10-e7","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The boxes were packed in ___ rather than plastic, which made them easy to recycle.\\"","options":["marble","cardboard","mast","grid"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Cardboard is a stiff paper material used to make boxes, and it recycles easily."},{"id":"b6u10-e8","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The new bridge should ___ the movement of goods between the two towns.\\"","options":["facilitate","inflate","infer","import"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To facilitate something is to make it easier, which is what a new bridge does for the movement of goods."},{"id":"b6u10-e9","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The reading was taken from a ___ of numbered squares printed on the map.\\"","options":["grid","fleet","mast","pouch"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"A grid is a pattern of squares used to locate places, which matches the numbered squares on the map."},{"id":"b6u10-e10","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"A ___ of fishing boats left the harbour before dawn.\\"","options":["grid","fleet","acquisition","accessory"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"A fleet is a group of ships, which is what left the harbour together."}]},"11":{"id":"b6-u11","book":6,"unit":11,"title":"The Mark on the Wall","level":"C1","topic":"A school, a damaged mural, and a rushed judgement","generatedAt":"2026-09-23","wordCount":430,"paragraphs":["Our school had a [[chronic]] problem with [[graffiti]], and no amount of [[discipline]] seemed to make the slightest difference. Every Monday the caretaker would paint over whatever had appeared at the weekend, and by Friday the wall beside the sports hall would be marked again. We had stopped noticing it, which is perhaps why nobody noticed when something worse happened.","That wall was not an ordinary wall. A [[donor]] had paid for a long mural showing the town\'s history, and its central [[theme]] was deliberately plain: work, and the people who did it. Then, one October morning, someone [[stab|stabbed]] at the lower panel with something sharp, drawing a deep line through three painted figures. On the grass below lay the broken head of a garden tool. Its wooden [[shaft]] had been [[sever|severed]] cleanly from the metal.","By lunchtime everybody had a theory. [[gossip]] travelled faster than any official announcement. A [[fellow]] student in my year, Daniel, was named first, because he had been seen near the hall after a match and because he had once been caught carving his initials into a desk. Within a day the [[suspicion]] had hardened into certainty. His [[guardian]] was called in, and I still remember how small his [[kin]] looked, sitting together on the bench outside the office.","The school\'s response was brisk. An [[attorney]] was consulted, the caretaker\'s report was passed on, and for a while it looked as though the whole affair would [[terminate]] there, with a boy\'s name attached to it for good. What nobody had considered was the [[referee]] of the Saturday match, who had stayed behind to lock the equipment shed and had seen a figure at the wall. It was not Daniel.","The real [[stimulus]] for the truth was a photograph. The referee had taken it idly, to record the state of the pitch, and in the corner of the frame stood a young man who had [[graduate|graduated]] three years earlier and had come back for the weekend, a tool in his hand. He had meant no harm at all; he had wanted to add his own name to the mural and had badly misjudged the paint. He apologised, paid for the repair, and quietly offered to cover the [[tuition]] of any pupil whose family needed it.","Daniel returned to lessons the next morning. Nothing was said officially, which is its own kind of cruelty. I have thought about that week many times since, and about how easily a community will [[implicate]] a person it has already decided to distrust, and how little evidence that decision really requires."],"exercises":[{"id":"b6u11-e1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"Why was Daniel suspected of damaging the mural?","options":["He had been seen with a garden tool near the wall","He had a previous record of marking school property and had been near the hall after a match","He had argued publicly with the caretaker that week","He had been the last person to leave the sports hall"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 3 says Daniel was named first because he had been seen near the hall after a match and had once been caught carving his initials into a desk."},{"id":"b6u11-e2","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What was found on the grass beneath the damaged panel?","options":["A photograph of the pitch","A can of paint and a brush","The broken head of a garden tool with its wooden shaft cut away","A carved name that matched Daniel\'s handwriting"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"Paragraph 2 describes the broken head of a garden tool whose wooden shaft had been severed cleanly from the metal."},{"id":"b6u11-e3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What finally revealed the truth about the incident?","options":["A photograph taken by the referee for an unrelated reason","A confession made by the caretaker","Daniel\'s guardian produced a witness","The donor demanded a formal inquiry"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Paragraph 5 explains that the referee had taken a photograph to record the state of the pitch, and it showed a former pupil at the wall."},{"id":"b6u11-e4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"How does the narrator feel about the way the school handled the affair?","options":["Relieved that the police were never involved","Grateful that the donor paid for the repair","Critical, because the community condemned Daniel without real evidence","Proud that the school reached a decision so quickly"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"The final paragraph speaks of the community deciding to distrust someone and of how little evidence that decision requires, and calls the official silence \'its own kind of cruelty\'."},{"id":"b6u11-e5","type":"multiple-choice","question":"As used in the final paragraph, \\"implicate\\" most nearly means…","options":["to forgive someone for a small mistake","to involve someone in wrongdoing","to interview someone as a witness","to exclude someone from a group"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"To implicate someone is to show or suggest that they were involved in something bad — here, attaching guilt to a person the community had already chosen to distrust."},{"id":"b6u11-e6","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The hospital is looking for a ___ to fund the new children\'s ward.\\"","options":["donor","shaft","kin","theme"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"A donor is somebody who gives something, typically money, to an organisation — exactly what a hospital needs to fund a new ward."},{"id":"b6u11-e7","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"He suffers from ___ back pain that has never fully gone away.\\"","options":["chronic","temporary","sudden","donor"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Chronic describes something that persists or recurs over a long period, which matches pain that has never fully gone away. \'Temporary\' and \'sudden\' contradict that duration."},{"id":"b6u11-e8","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Before signing anything, she asked her ___ to check the terms of the contract.\\"","options":["referee","gossip","attorney","donor"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"An attorney advises people about the law, so this is the person one would ask to examine a contract."},{"id":"b6u11-e9","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Their university fees are high, but a scholarship covers most of the ___.\\"","options":["tuition","discipline","suspicion","stimulus"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Tuition is the money paid to attend a school or university, so a scholarship covering it reduces that cost."},{"id":"b6u11-e10","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Office ___ about the reorganisation spread long before any announcement was made.\\"","options":["discipline","graffiti","gossip","stimulus"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"Gossip is unverified information that circulates in conversation, which fits rumours spreading ahead of an official announcement."}]},"12":{"id":"b6-u12","book":6,"unit":12,"title":"The Long Walk Home","level":"C1","topic":"A soldier after a long war","generatedAt":"2026-09-23","wordCount":486,"paragraphs":["The war lasted longer than the ruler\'s [[reign]] had promised it would. By its final year, soldiers of the [[corps]] were a [[commonplace]] sight in every town, and the vocabulary of [[combat]] had seeped into ordinary speech: people spoke of fighting a bad harvest, of defending a price, of winning an argument as though something had been taken from somebody.","Saro had spent two winters as a [[captive]] in a hilltop [[compound]], where the walls were high and the days were identical. His [[foe]] had not been cruel, only indifferent. Once a week a guard, bored rather than [[aggressive]], would try to [[provoke]] him into an argument, and Saro had learned to [[meditate]] instead — to sit with his back against the cold stone until the man lost interest and walked away.","The compound had its own small [[arena]], a flattened circle of earth where the guards played a rough game at dusk. Above the gate hung a strip of iron that nobody had ever replaced, and the [[rust]] had eaten it into a thin brown lace. Saro studied it daily, because it was the only object there that visibly changed.","When the [[amnesty]] came, it arrived without ceremony. He walked home for eleven days. In his own town he found that his brother had died and that the family land had been sold, and the house was [[dumb]] in a way he could not explain, though he knew perfectly well that houses do not speak. He took work cutting firewood and was careless with the axe: twice he [[nick|nicked]] his thumb, and once he [[hack|hacked]] a log so badly that it split into four useless pieces.","What saved him was the [[auditorium]]. The town\'s old assembly hall had lost its roof, and the council wanted the site cleared. Saro volunteered, and he worked there alone for a whole season. The labour did what the silence of the compound had never managed: it [[distract|distracted]] him. He discovered that he could think about the past only while his hands were occupied, and so he kept them occupied from first light until dark.","The hall stood at the edge of a [[sacred]] grove, and priests had once performed their rites beneath the trees. Saro had no interest in the [[realm]] of the gods; he had seen it claimed too often on both sides of a battle line. But he understood the value of a place where people came simply to be quiet, and he repaired the stone path himself so that they could reach it in winter.","In the end the town rebuilt the hall as a market, and Saro was given a corner stall. He sold nothing in particular and was, by then, a mild and unremarkable man, so visitors found it hard to believe that he had ever carried a weapon. He never corrected them. There were things he preferred to leave exactly where they were."],"exercises":[{"id":"b6u12-e1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What is suggested about the war in the opening paragraph?","options":["It was shorter than anyone had expected","It lasted longer than the ruler had promised, and its language spread into daily life","It was fought entirely inside the capital","It ended as soon as the ruler died"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 1 states that the war lasted longer than the ruler\'s reign had promised and that the vocabulary of combat had seeped into ordinary speech."},{"id":"b6u12-e2","type":"multiple-choice","question":"How did Saro respond to the guard who tried to start arguments?","options":["He reported the guard to the commander","He refused to eat until the guard was removed","He stayed silent and meditated until the man gave up","He answered with insults of his own"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"Paragraph 2 says he learned to meditate, sitting with his back against the cold stone until the guard lost interest and walked away."},{"id":"b6u12-e3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"Why did Saro study the iron strip above the gate?","options":["It was the only object in the compound that visibly changed","He hoped to remove it and sell it","It carried a message from the guards","It reminded him of his brother\'s workshop"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Paragraph 3 says he studied it daily because it was the only object there that visibly changed."},{"id":"b6u12-e4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What did repairing the assembly hall do for Saro?","options":["It earned him enough money to buy back the family land","It gave him a position among the priests","It kept his hands busy so that he could bear thinking about the past","It allowed him to avoid meeting his former neighbours"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"Paragraph 5 explains that the labour distracted him and that he could think about the past only while his hands were occupied."},{"id":"b6u12-e5","type":"multiple-choice","question":"As used in paragraph 2, \\"indifferent\\" most nearly means…","options":["actively hostile","showing no particular interest or concern","unable to understand orders","unusually generous"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"The sentence contrasts indifference with cruelty and describes a foe who was neither kind nor unkind — simply unconcerned."},{"id":"b6u12-e6","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"After thirty years of neglect, the old railings had been eaten away by ___.\\"","options":["rust","combat","amnesty","realm"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Rust is the red-brown coating that forms on iron exposed to water and air, which is exactly what destroys neglected railings."},{"id":"b6u12-e7","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The children were told to stop shouting and to ___ in silence for five minutes.\\"","options":["provoke","meditate","hack","nick"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"To meditate is to focus or think deeply in silence, which is what the instruction asks for."},{"id":"b6u12-e8","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Deliberately annoying the dog until it barked was a foolish way to ___ it.\\"","options":["distract","provoke","meditate","nick"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"To provoke someone is to annoy them on purpose, especially to make them react — precisely what the sentence describes."},{"id":"b6u12-e9","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"For the villagers, the spring festival was a ___ occasion that had been observed for centuries.\\"","options":["sacred","aggressive","dumb","commonplace"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Something sacred is worshipped and respected, which fits a ceremony observed with reverence over many centuries."},{"id":"b6u12-e10","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The council granted ___ to several prisoners to mark the end of the conflict.\\"","options":["amnesty","arena","rust","corps"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Amnesty is a pardon granted to prisoners, so it is the word that fits releasing prisoners on a symbolic occasion."}]},"13":{"id":"b6-u13","book":6,"unit":13,"title":"Eleven Weeks on the Ridge","level":"C1","topic":"A field diary from a primate research camp","generatedAt":"2026-09-23","wordCount":436,"paragraphs":["The [[trail]] from the [[creek]] up to the ridge takes ninety minutes, and the final [[incline]] — forty metres of loose red earth that slides away underfoot — is where my [[fatigue]] turns into something closer to resentment. I sit at the top until my hands stop shaking, then I take out the notebook.","The [[canyon]] below holds a small community of [[primate|primates]]: long-armed, slow-moving and entirely indifferent to me. They live mainly on [[termite|termites]], which they fish out of the mounds with a [[twig]] stripped of its leaves. The technique looks [[primitive]], and that word is precisely the problem, because the sequence involved — select, strip, insert, wait, withdraw — demands decisions that nobody has yet managed to teach them by imitation. I have watched one female repeat it forty times and I still cannot [[perceive]] the instant at which she decides a particular hole is worth the effort. The [[sticky]] sap on the twig does much of the work; the patience does the rest.","I arrived with a [[bias]] I was not aware of. My training had given me an [[aesthetic]] taste for drama: I wanted confrontations, alliances, betrayals. What I actually find is administration. A senior female, whom the students privately call the [[executive]], spends her mornings settling disputes about sitting space. She is not [[arrogant]]; she simply has no doubt whatsoever about her position, and that certainty is more effective than any display of force. Watching her, I begin to suspect that most of the drama I was taught to look for is simply the noise made by animals who have not yet worked out who is in charge.","The camp\'s own [[welfare]] depends on unglamorous things: a new [[drill]] for the fence posts, dry boots, and a cook who refuses to let anybody skip breakfast. She also enjoys dismantling the [[stereotype]] that field scientists are fearless, and she is not gentle about it. When a [[nasty]] bite on my ankle swelled up, she treated it, mocked me at length, and then fed me, [[thereby]] proving her own theory that we are all useless in the forest and would starve without her.","Eleven weeks in, the notebook is full and I understand considerably less than I did in the first week, which I am assured is normal. What has changed is the question I am asking. I came here to discover what these animals do. I am leaving with a far harder question: how much of what I recorded was genuinely out there, and how much was the shape of my own expectations, pressed onto a hillside that never asked for them."],"exercises":[{"id":"b6u13-e1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What is the writer\'s main realisation by the end of the passage?","options":["The primates are less intelligent than her training had suggested","The camp is badly managed and needs better equipment","Her own expectations may have shaped what she recorded","Field research is impossible without local help"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"The final paragraph asks how much of what she recorded was genuinely out there and how much was the shape of her own expectations."},{"id":"b6u13-e2","type":"multiple-choice","question":"How do the primates obtain their food?","options":["They dig into the ground with their hands","They use a stripped twig to fish termites out of mounds","They catch insects in the creek","They wait for termites to climb onto sticky leaves"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 2 describes how they fish termites out of the mounds with a twig stripped of its leaves, relying on the sticky sap."},{"id":"b6u13-e3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"Why does the writer object to the word \\"primitive\\"?","options":["It suggests a technique that is simple when it actually requires a sequence of decisions","It is an insulting term that the local students dislike","It refers to animals rather than to tools","It is too technical for a field notebook"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Paragraph 2 says the word is precisely the problem because the technique demands decisions that nobody has managed to teach by imitation."},{"id":"b6u13-e4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What does the writer find instead of the drama she had expected?","options":["Constant aggression between rival groups","A rigid hierarchy enforced by violence","Ordinary administration, such as settling disputes over sitting space","Almost no social behaviour at all"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"Paragraph 3 says that what she actually finds is administration, and gives the example of the senior female settling disputes about sitting space."},{"id":"b6u13-e5","type":"multiple-choice","question":"As used in paragraph 3, \\"aesthetic\\" most nearly means…","options":["relating to a sense of beauty and taste","connected with scientific accuracy","concerned with physical strength","based on careful measurement"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"The writer says her training gave her a taste for drama, so \'aesthetic\' here refers to an appreciation of what she finds beautiful or satisfying."},{"id":"b6u13-e6","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"After twelve hours of walking, ___ made it impossible to continue.\\"","options":["fatigue","bias","welfare","creek"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Fatigue is extreme tiredness, which is what would prevent someone from continuing after a very long walk."},{"id":"b6u13-e7","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"I could not ___ any difference between the two paintings, however hard I looked.\\"","options":["perceive","provoke","welfare","incline"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To perceive something is to become aware of it, so it fits failing to notice any difference between two pictures."},{"id":"b6u13-e8","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The charity\'s main concern is the ___ of children in temporary housing.\\"","options":["welfare","incline","drill","twig"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Welfare is the health and happiness of a person or group, which is exactly what a charity would be concerned with."},{"id":"b6u13-e9","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The honey had dripped onto the table and left a ___ patch that attracted wasps.\\"","options":["sticky","arrogant","primitive","chronic"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Something sticky is covered with a substance that other things adhere to, which is why the patch attracted wasps."},{"id":"b6u13-e10","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The manager\'s ___ manner made him unpopular with a team that valued modesty.\\"","options":["arrogant","gracious","sticky","aesthetic"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"An arrogant person believes they are more important than others, which would clash with a team that values modesty."}]},"14":{"id":"b6-u14","book":6,"unit":14,"title":"The Bird Above the Bakery","level":"C1","topic":"A mountain village, an old prediction, and a small brown bird","generatedAt":"2026-09-23","wordCount":459,"paragraphs":["The village lay at the foot of a [[glacier]] that had been shrinking for a century. Its edge was a wall of blue-white ice, and from the far side of the [[globe]] the valley must have looked like a long scratch on the surface of the world.","Once a year, in the week before the thaw, the village performed a [[rite]] that nobody could explain and nobody dared to abandon. An old woman — the [[oracle]], as the children called her, though she insisted she had no [[supernatural]] gifts — would walk up to the ice, press one hand flat against it, and stand there for an hour. Then she would come down and tell the village what sort of summer to expect.","Cosmo was a small brown bird who had been [[provide|provided]] with his name by a girl who could not pronounce the one he arrived with. He had had a [[previous]] owner, a sailor, and he carried an [[inward]] conviction that the world was arranged around a single point: the chimney of the village bakery. From that chimney he would [[orbit]] the valley twice each morning, [[flap|flapping]] hard against the wind and [[hum|humming]] a note so low that only the dogs appeared to hear it.","That spring the oracle gave an [[overview]] of the coming months that nobody liked. She said the ice would break early. The baker, who kept a careful [[inventory]] of flour and salt, worked out what an early thaw would mean for the road, and decided to [[stall]] the delivery of his summer grain. On his [[behalf]], and on behalf of the other shopkeepers, the schoolteacher wrote to the town below.","What happened next was [[relevant]] to everybody. A [[horizontal]] crack appeared in the ice, fine as a hair, and then the same crack [[recur|recurred]] a metre to the left, and then again. Cosmo flew over it every morning without once looking down. On the fourth day the girl who had named him was given a [[preview]] of the summer ahead: a river where the path had been, and a [[loaf]] of bread that would have to be carried up the hill by hand, because no cart would ever get through again.","The village survived, as villages generally do. What changed was its habits. People began to write things down — the height of the ice, the date of the first melt, the year the bridge was finally taken by the water. What had once been one woman\'s private instinct became a shared record, and a record, unlike memory, can be argued with. Cosmo outlived the oracle by nine years. He was still circling the chimney at the end, still humming, and by then the dogs had long since stopped bothering to answer."],"exercises":[{"id":"b6u14-e1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What does the oracle do each year before the thaw?","options":["She leads a ceremony at the bakery","She presses her hand against the ice and then forecasts the summer","She measures the height of the glacier with a rope","She asks the schoolteacher to write to the town below"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 2 describes her walking to the ice, pressing one hand flat against it, standing there for an hour, and then telling the village what sort of summer to expect."},{"id":"b6u14-e2","type":"multiple-choice","question":"Why did the baker delay the delivery of his summer grain?","options":["He had run out of storage space in the bakery","The town below had cancelled its order","He had calculated what an early thaw would do to the road","He wanted to force the shopkeepers to pay more"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"Paragraph 4 says he worked out what an early thaw would mean for the road and therefore decided to stall the delivery."},{"id":"b6u14-e3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What is said about Cosmo\'s daily behaviour?","options":["He follows the girl to the ice every morning","He circles the valley twice each morning, centred on the bakery chimney","He refuses to fly while the dogs are watching","He migrates south before the thaw each year"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 3 says that from the chimney he would orbit the valley twice each morning, flapping against the wind and humming."},{"id":"b6u14-e4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What change came over the village after the events described?","options":["It abandoned the annual rite altogether","It moved to the town below","It began keeping written records rather than relying on one woman\'s instinct","It stopped trading with the town below"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"The final paragraph says people began to write things down and that what had been one woman\'s private instinct became a shared record."},{"id":"b6u14-e5","type":"multiple-choice","question":"As used in paragraph 4, \\"stall\\" most nearly means…","options":["to cancel something permanently","to delay something and continue it later","to sell something at a lower price","to store something in a safe place"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"To stall means to stop a process and continue it at a later time, which is what the baker does with the grain delivery."},{"id":"b6u14-e6","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The river is fed by meltwater from a ___ high in the mountains.\\"","options":["glacier","rite","inventory","loaf"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"A glacier is a large mass of ice that moves slowly, and its meltwater feeds rivers — the other options are unrelated to ice."},{"id":"b6u14-e7","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The same error ___ every time the software is updated, and nobody can explain why.\\"","options":["recurs","stalls","orbits","provides"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To recur means to happen more than once, which matches an error appearing again with every update."},{"id":"b6u14-e8","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"She accepted the award on ___ of the whole research team.\\"","options":["behalf","overview","preview","inventory"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"The fixed phrase \'on behalf of\' means representing someone else, which is what accepting an award for a team involves."},{"id":"b6u14-e9","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Only one paragraph of his report was ___ to our investigation, so we ignored the rest.\\"","options":["relevant","supernatural","horizontal","previous"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Something relevant is important to a particular situation, so a relevant paragraph is the only part worth reading."},{"id":"b6u14-e10","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The shop keeps a detailed ___ of everything in the storeroom.\\"","options":["inventory","rite","globe","oracle"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"An inventory is a supply or a full list of goods held, which is exactly what a shop records about its storeroom."}]},"15":{"id":"b6-u15","book":6,"unit":15,"title":"The Woman Who Bought a Forest","level":"C1","topic":"A profile of a reclusive conservationist","generatedAt":"2026-09-23","wordCount":459,"paragraphs":["Visitors arrive at the Renn Reserve expecting a zoo. There is an [[aquarium]] just inside the gate, a warm building for [[reptile|reptiles]], and a hand-painted sign that lists the opening hours with the cheerful [[arbitrary|arbitrariness]] of a place that has never employed a manager. Only when you sit down in the caf\xe9 does anybody explain what the reserve really is: four hundred hectares of forest, bought by one woman with money she had inherited and did not want.","Ada Renn was born in a coastal town and, by her own account, was a thoroughly [[naughty]] child. Her [[autobiography]], published when she was eighty-one, describes a girl who [[misplace|misplaced]] her school shoes so regularly that her mother eventually stopped buying new ones. What [[intrigue|intrigued]] her was never animals in general but a much narrower problem: how to keep a particular species alive in a landscape that was disappearing around it.","Her [[philanthropy]] was never tidy. She gave money to causes that embarrassed her friends, and she was a difficult [[recipient]] of awards, accepting them only when refusing would have seemed rude. She had no patience for [[convention]] — she wore the same coat for thirty years — and yet she was unfailingly [[gracious]] to anyone who came to her with a serious question, however junior that person happened to be.","The reserve\'s best-known resident is an elderly [[orangutan]] who arrived as an infant and has never learned to tolerate visitors. Ada\'s rule was that the animals came first, which meant that a school group could be turned away because the orangutan was asleep. Critics accused her of running a private kingdom; she replied that a kingdom would have been considerably cheaper. She refused to [[overload]] the site with cars, paths and caf\xe9s, and she spent an unreasonable sum [[insulate|insulating]] the reptile house so that the heating would never fail. When journalists came, she would let them [[probe]] her methods for an hour and then send them away with a map and no answers.","The results are not dramatic, which is rather the point. Two species have begun to [[thrive]] again; the forest has thickened; a bird that had not been recorded locally for forty years is now, if not common, at least no longer a surprise. Water quality in the stream has slowly [[improve|improved]]. Ada attributes none of this to herself. She says the reserve works because it stopped trying to be interesting and settled instead into the unglamorous [[norm]] of doing the same small things every year. She is ninety-four. Asked about her [[longevity]], she says it is mostly a matter of having something that makes her want to get up in the morning, and that her [[ultimate]] ambition is that nobody will remember who paid for the forest."],"exercises":[{"id":"b6u15-e1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What is the Renn Reserve actually revealed to be?","options":["A commercial zoo owned by a company","Four hundred hectares of forest bought by one woman","A research station funded by a university","A public park managed by the local council"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 1 explains that only in the caf\xe9 does anyone reveal what the reserve really is: four hundred hectares of forest bought by one woman."},{"id":"b6u15-e2","type":"multiple-choice","question":"According to her autobiography, what was Ada like as a child?","options":["Quiet and studious","Determined to become a scientist","Mischievous, and constantly losing her shoes","Frightened of animals"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"Paragraph 2 says she was a thoroughly naughty child who misplaced her school shoes so regularly that her mother stopped buying new ones."},{"id":"b6u15-e3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"Why might a school group be refused entry?","options":["They had not booked far enough in advance","The entrance fee was too high for them","Because the animals\' needs took priority — for example if the orangutan was asleep","Because the reptile house was being heated"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"Paragraph 4 states that Ada\'s rule was that the animals came first, so a group could be turned away because the orangutan was asleep."},{"id":"b6u15-e4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"How does Ada explain the reserve\'s success?","options":["Through generous donations from her friends","Through constant publicity and media attention","Through repeated small actions rather than trying to be interesting","Through the strict exclusion of all visitors"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"The final paragraph says she believes the reserve works because it stopped trying to be interesting and settled into the norm of doing the same small things every year."},{"id":"b6u15-e5","type":"multiple-choice","question":"As used in paragraph 3, \\"gracious\\" most nearly means…","options":["wealthy and well connected","kind and courteous to others","strict about rules and standards","reluctant to speak in public"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"The sentence contrasts her impatience with convention with her behaviour towards anyone asking a serious question, so \'gracious\' means kind and courteous."},{"id":"b6u15-e6","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The walls of the old house were filled with material to ___ it against the winter cold.\\"","options":["insulate","overload","probe","thrive"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To insulate something is to protect it from heat or cold, which is precisely the purpose of material in a wall cavity."},{"id":"b6u15-e7","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Her ___ has funded three libraries and a mobile clinic.\\"","options":["philanthropy","longevity","convention","norm"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Philanthropy is the act of helping others without expecting anything in return, and funding libraries and a clinic is exactly that."},{"id":"b6u15-e8","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The committee decided to ___ the matter further before reaching a conclusion.\\"","options":["probe","thrive","misplace","insulate"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To probe into something is to ask questions in order to discover facts, which is what a committee does before concluding."},{"id":"b6u15-e9","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Do not ___ the circuit by plugging in too many appliances at once.\\"","options":["overload","improve","intrigue","probe"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To overload something is to put more into it than it is designed to hold, which is what too many appliances do to a circuit."},{"id":"b6u15-e10","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Every ___ of the scholarship is asked to write a short report at the end of the year.\\"","options":["recipient","convention","norm","aquarium"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"A recipient is the person who receives something, so a recipient of a scholarship is the student who was awarded it."}]},"16":{"id":"b6-u16","book":6,"unit":16,"title":"The Register of Small Things","level":"C1","topic":"A first job in a museum basement","generatedAt":"2026-09-23","wordCount":429,"paragraphs":["I was [[punctual]] on my first morning — twenty minutes early, in fact, standing in the rain because nobody had thought to tell me which door to use. I had applied for the post on a whim, along with ninety other [[applicant|applicants]], and I suspect I was [[recruit|recruited]] chiefly because I could read nineteenth-century handwriting.","The museum\'s collection was far smaller than its [[renown]] suggested. Most of it lived in the basement: rows of wooden drawers, each with a paper [[register]] listing its contents, and inside the drawers a great many objects that nobody had examined for fifty years. The air smelled of dust and old varnish, and the only window was painted shut.","My first task was to check the [[chronology]] of a group of finds from a single excavation. A [[fossil]] had been catalogued as the oldest item in the group, which was impossible, since it [[precede|preceded]] the layer in which it was found. The error had been made in 1953 and repeated, in four different hands, ever since. Correcting it took two days and a telephone call to a geologist who had retired in 1998.","Among the [[artifact|artifacts]] was a carved [[tusk]], not strictly [[antique]] but old enough to matter, and a fragmentary [[epic]] in verse of which only a single [[excerpt]] survives. I spent a week establishing that the [[lyric]] passages in the middle were composed considerably later than the narrative frame around them. It was the kind of discovery that changes nothing and satisfies everything.","Then came the awkward business of an [[authentic|authenticity]] dispute. A collector, a former [[diplomat]] with a taste for grand objects, had lent us a piece said to have belonged to a [[monarch]], and the label described its [[majesty]] in terms that made the curators wince. The scientific case against it was strong, and to state that case publicly would have [[humiliate|humiliated]] the lender. He had paid a considerable sum for a private viewing, and when we quietly removed the piece from display he demanded a [[refund]]. In the end we wrote a cautious caption, returned the object, and refunded him in full, which is cheaper than a lawsuit and far cheaper than being wrong in print.","I stayed six years. What I learned there was not the subject I had studied at university but something far less teachable: that an object means very little on its own, and that the whole of a curator\'s work lies in the company it is given — what is placed beside it, what is written underneath it, and what is quietly left out."],"exercises":[{"id":"b6u16-e1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What was the writer\'s first task at the museum?","options":["To catalogue a newly donated tusk","To check the chronology of finds from one excavation","To rewrite the labels in the public galleries","To negotiate the return of a borrowed object"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 3 says the first task was to check the chronology of a group of finds from a single excavation."},{"id":"b6u16-e2","type":"multiple-choice","question":"Why was the catalogue entry for the fossil impossible?","options":["The fossil had been stolen from another museum","It had been listed as the oldest item although it preceded the layer it came from","It was in fact a modern copy","It had been catalogued twice under different names"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 3 explains that it could not be the oldest item because it preceded the layer in which it was found."},{"id":"b6u16-e3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"How was the authenticity dispute resolved?","options":["The museum published a strong public denial","The collector withdrew the loan and apologised","A cautious caption was written, the object returned, and the lender refunded","The curators displayed the piece with a warning label"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"Paragraph 5 states that they wrote a cautious caption, returned the object, and refunded him in full."},{"id":"b6u16-e4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What does the writer mean by \\"the kind of discovery that changes nothing and satisfies everything\\"?","options":["A discovery so important that it transformed the collection","A small scholarly finding that is nevertheless deeply satisfying","A finding that had to be kept secret from the public","A discovery that proved an earlier curator had lied"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"The phrase refers to establishing the date of the lyric passages — work of no public consequence that the writer nonetheless found completely rewarding."},{"id":"b6u16-e5","type":"multiple-choice","question":"As used in paragraph 2, \\"renown\\" most nearly means…","options":["the fame or reputation something has","the physical size of a building","the income a museum earns","the age of a collection"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"The sentence contrasts the museum\'s renown with the actual size of its collection, so \'renown\' refers to its reputation or fame."},{"id":"b6u16-e6","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Every ___ for the course must submit two letters of reference.\\"","options":["applicant","register","excerpt","tusk"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"An applicant is someone who formally requests to be considered, which is exactly who would submit references for a course."},{"id":"b6u16-e7","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The firm plans to ___ fifty graduates this year.\\"","options":["recruit","refund","humiliate","precede"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To recruit people is to select them to join an organisation, which is what a firm does when it takes on graduates."},{"id":"b6u16-e8","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The lecture began at nine, and he was ___ to the minute.\\"","options":["punctual","lyric","antique","authentic"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Someone who is punctual arrives at the right time, so being punctual to the minute fits arriving exactly at nine."},{"id":"b6u16-e9","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The anthology includes a short ___ from her final novel.\\"","options":["excerpt","refund","register","chronology"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"An excerpt is a short piece taken from a larger work, which is what an anthology would include from a novel."},{"id":"b6u16-e10","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"She was afraid the mistake would ___ her in front of the whole department.\\"","options":["humiliate","recruit","precede","register"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To humiliate someone is to make them feel ashamed and embarrassed, which is exactly what she fears happening publicly."}]},"17":{"id":"b6-u17","book":6,"unit":17,"title":"The Bone in the Bank","level":"C1","topic":"An excavation season in a remote basin","generatedAt":"2026-09-23","wordCount":475,"paragraphs":["We had been digging for eleven days at the [[periphery]] of the basin, where the rock changes from grey to a red so dark it looks wet. The [[locale]] had been chosen for us by a colleague who had never visited it, which is how most of our [[endeavor|endeavors]] begin. His reasoning was sound on paper: a river, a slope, a layer of sediment of roughly the right age. On the ground it was forty degrees in the shade, and the nearest drinking water was a two-hour drive away.","The work itself is simple and endless. You [[excavate]] with a [[shovel]] until the ground stops being soil and starts being story, and then you switch to a trowel and a brush. On the ninth day we struck a layer of [[jagged]] fragments — bone, most of it, shattered by water and by time. We wrapped the larger pieces in [[plaster]] and, where a specimen was too fragile to lift, we built a [[mold]] around it in the field. By the evening of the eleventh day we had something that looked, unmistakably, like part of a [[skeletal]] frame: a [[thigh]] bone, complete, with the joint still articulated.","Nobody in camp believed it at first. The claim was too large and the evidence was, frankly, [[dubious]]. Our team leader, whose [[ego]] had survived three decades of failed seasons, refused to make an [[outright]] statement to anybody. What we had was a possibility, not a fact, and he had learned the hard way that a [[vicious]] argument with a rival expedition is far easier to start than to finish. Two species, he pointed out, can [[coexist]] in a single layer without ever having met. It was not impossible to [[conceive]] of another explanation; it was simply harder. So we said nothing and kept digging, which is the least glamorous and most useful thing a field team can do.","The [[magnitude]] of what we eventually found took a further year to establish. There were [[engrave|engraved]] marks on two of the bones, and a strip of material that behaved like [[elastic]] whenever it got wet. I [[stumble|stumbled]] over the last piece on the final afternoon, half buried and almost exactly the colour of the ground. We were not the first people to stand in that valley, and for the first time in my career I understood how little that sentence usually means.","The paper appeared eighteen months later and was, by any measure, modest: eleven pages, four figures, and a conclusion phrased so cautiously that it took me three readings to see how strong it really was. That, I have come to think, is what the work teaches you. Not patience — everybody says patience — but the harder habit of describing only what you can actually see, and leaving the rest to whoever arrives next with a better trowel."],"exercises":[{"id":"b6u17-e1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"How was the dig site chosen?","options":["By a local guide who knew the area well","By a colleague who had never visited it, on the basis of sound reasoning on paper","By following a river upstream from the camp","After a rival expedition abandoned the area"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 1 says the locale was chosen by a colleague who had never visited it, whose reasoning was sound on paper but who had not experienced the conditions on the ground."},{"id":"b6u17-e2","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What had the team found by the evening of the eleventh day?","options":["A complete engraved tablet","A strip of elastic material","What appeared to be part of a skeletal frame, including a complete thigh bone","A plaster mold containing a whole skeleton"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"Paragraph 2 describes something that looked like part of a skeletal frame: a thigh bone, complete, with the joint still articulated."},{"id":"b6u17-e3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"Why did the team leader refuse to make an outright statement?","options":["He wanted to sell the story to a newspaper first","He did not believe the bones were real","The evidence was questionable, and he knew how easily a dispute with rivals could begin","He had been instructed to remain silent by the funding body"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"Paragraph 3 describes the evidence as dubious and explains that he knew a vicious argument with a rival expedition is easier to start than to finish."},{"id":"b6u17-e4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What does the writer say the work has taught them?","options":["That field research requires physical endurance above all","That rival expeditions should be avoided","The habit of describing only what can actually be observed","That modest findings are not worth publishing"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"The final paragraph says the work teaches the harder habit of describing only what you can actually see and leaving the rest to others."},{"id":"b6u17-e5","type":"multiple-choice","question":"As used in paragraph 3, \\"dubious\\" most nearly means…","options":["extremely valuable","questionable and not to be relied on","carefully measured","deliberately falsified"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"The team leader refuses to make a public claim precisely because the evidence cannot yet be trusted, so \'dubious\' means questionable."},{"id":"b6u17-e6","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Archaeologists began to ___ the site after a farmer uncovered a coin in his field.\\"","options":["excavate","engrave","stumble","coexist"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To excavate is to dig on land and remove earth in order to look for objects, which is what archaeologists do at a site."},{"id":"b6u17-e7","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The earthquake was of such ___ that it was felt two hundred kilometres away.\\"","options":["magnitude","periphery","mold","elastic"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Magnitude refers to great size or extent, and here it describes the scale of an earthquake that was felt far away."},{"id":"b6u17-e8","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The waistband is made of ___ so that it stretches to fit.\\"","options":["elastic","plaster","shovel","locale"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Elastic is a material that stretches when pulled, which is exactly what a waistband needs in order to fit different people."},{"id":"b6u17-e9","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"It is hard to ___ of a city of that size having no public transport at all.\\"","options":["conceive","excavate","stumble","engrave"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To conceive of something is to be able to imagine or believe it, so it fits finding such a situation hard to imagine."},{"id":"b6u17-e10","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"New housing estates have spread out to the ___ of the old town.\\"","options":["periphery","magnitude","ego","endeavor"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"The periphery is the outer edge of a place, so estates spreading outwards would reach the periphery of the town."}]},"18":{"id":"b6-u18","book":6,"unit":18,"title":"Five Days Under the Roof","level":"C1","topic":"A household diary, and a leak that will not wait","generatedAt":"2026-09-23","wordCount":447,"paragraphs":["Monday. The [[superintendent]] of the building rang at seven to say that water was coming through the ceiling of the flat below and that it appeared to be coming from our [[attic]]. I have lived in this house for twenty-two years and have never once climbed up there, which I mention only because it is about to become relevant.","Tuesday. I spent the morning under the roof, and I can report the following. The [[descent]] through the hatch is considerably more dangerous than the climb. The [[din]] from the street, which I had assumed would be fainter at roof level, is in fact louder. Somebody — I shall not speculate who — has stored a large iron [[kettle]] among the boxes, together with a mattress on which a person has evidently slept, and from which, judging by the noise, a person has also [[snore|snored]] extremely [[soundly]] for many nights. I came down at noon with dust in my hair and the distinct impression that the house has been keeping secrets from me.","Wednesday. My employer, who is [[outspoken]] to the point of recklessness, telephoned the plumber herself and made a considerable [[fuss]] about the cost. I attempted to explain that the pipe was older than either of us, which did not [[dissatisfy]] her so much as enrage her. She has been badly [[overwork|overworked]] for months — three charities, two committees, and a novel that nobody has read — and I suspect the ceiling was simply the final item on a very long list. The whole [[ordeal]] would have been shorter if she had let me handle it.","Thursday. The plumber arrived: a cheerful man who took one look at the attic and asked whether we had ever considered selling the house. He replaced a length of pipe, removed a [[chunk]] of damp plaster the size of a dinner plate, and charged a sum I have decided not to record. [[hence|Hence]] the [[particular]] difficulty with old houses — the repairs are never finished, only postponed.","Friday. I went back up to clear the last of it and found, to my surprise, that I was sorry to be leaving. The attic is not [[glamorous]], and there is nothing remotely [[gourmet]] about a flask of tea and a biscuit at ten in the morning. But the work carried an [[intrinsic]] value that my usual duties lack. I am not a [[civic]] figure; I have never worked for a [[ministry]]; nobody will ever thank me for a length of copper pipe. All the same, for five days I knew exactly what I was doing, which is more than most weeks can offer. I have kept the kettle. It seems only fair."],"exercises":[{"id":"b6u18-e1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What did the superintendent telephone about?","options":["A complaint from the neighbours about noise","Water coming through a ceiling, apparently from the attic","The cost of repairing an old copper pipe","A delivery left outside the building"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 1 says the superintendent reported that water was coming through the ceiling of the flat below and appeared to originate in the attic."},{"id":"b6u18-e2","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What did the narrator discover in the attic?","options":["A collection of unread novels","A hidden staircase to the floor below","An iron kettle and a mattress suggesting somebody had been sleeping there","A box of documents belonging to the ministry"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"Paragraph 2 mentions a large iron kettle stored among the boxes and a mattress on which a person had evidently slept."},{"id":"b6u18-e3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"Why did the employer react so strongly to the plumber\'s bill?","options":["She believed the narrator had caused the leak","She was already overworked and the repair became the final burden on a long list","She had wanted to sell the house for years","She did not trust the plumber\'s qualifications"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 3 says she had been badly overworked for months and the narrator suspects the ceiling was simply the final item on a very long list."},{"id":"b6u18-e4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"How did the narrator feel by Friday?","options":["Relieved that the house would now be sold","Angry that the plumber had charged so much","Sorry to finish, because the work had an intrinsic value the usual duties lacked","Determined to report the person who had been sleeping in the attic"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"The final paragraph says the narrator was sorry to be leaving and that the work carried an intrinsic value missing from ordinary duties."},{"id":"b6u18-e5","type":"multiple-choice","question":"As used in paragraph 5, \\"intrinsic\\" most nearly means…","options":["belonging to the essential nature of something","financially rewarding","temporary and easily lost","recognised by other people"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"The narrator contrasts intrinsic value with being thanked or recognised, so it refers to a worth that comes from the work itself."},{"id":"b6u18-e6","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"There was a terrible ___ coming from the kitchen, as though every pan had fallen at once.\\"","options":["din","chunk","attic","kettle"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"A din is loud, unpleasant, prolonged noise, which is what the sentence describes coming from the kitchen."},{"id":"b6u18-e7","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Please stop making such a ___ about a small change of plan.\\"","options":["fuss","descent","kettle","ministry"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"A fuss is excited or annoyed behaviour that serves no useful purpose, which fits an overreaction to a small change."},{"id":"b6u18-e8","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The hotel restaurant serves ___ dishes that cost three times what we usually pay.\\"","options":["gourmet","intrinsic","civic","outspoken"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Gourmet food is nicer and more expensive than ordinary food, which matches the description of the dishes and their price."},{"id":"b6u18-e9","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"After the baby finally slept ___, she tiptoed out of the room.\\"","options":["soundly","hence","intrinsically","outright"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"If something is done soundly in this sense, it is done completely or deeply, so sleeping soundly means sleeping deeply."},{"id":"b6u18-e10","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Losing his passport two days before the flight was a real ___.\\"","options":["ordeal","kettle","chunk","fuss"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"An ordeal is a bad or difficult experience, which is exactly what losing a passport before travelling amounts to."}]},"19":{"id":"b6-u19","book":6,"unit":19,"title":"How a City Learns to Wait","level":"C1","topic":"An essay on streets, manners and design","generatedAt":"2026-09-23","wordCount":443,"paragraphs":["A city\'s real character is easiest to read at seven in the morning, when its roads are at their most [[congested]]. On the avenue outside my window the traffic divides into three kinds. There is [[freight]], slow and enormous, moving between warehouses at hours chosen to avoid the daytime rush. There is the [[courier]] on a bicycle, who treats red lights as a suggestion. And there is the [[tram]], slower than all of them and yet, because it cannot swerve, in possession of the right of way by sheer physics.","What prevents this from becoming chaos is not law but [[etiquette]] — an unwritten set of rules that every road user absorbs without ever being taught. It is oddly [[intuitive]]: nobody explains that you should not stop in the middle of a crossing, and yet almost nobody does. Break the rules and you are [[liable]] to be shouted at, which is unpleasant but effective. I have come to think of good manners on the road as a [[privilege]] that must be continually renewed, because it vanishes the moment enough people decide it does not matter.","Money shapes the same streets in subtler ways. The new towers near the station are [[exclusive]] in the plainest sense: the rents are so far above the average that an entire category of resident has been quietly excluded. Their ground floors hold shops that charge a [[premium]] for an ordinary cotton [[garment]], and the buildings themselves seem designed to [[propel]] you through rather than to invite you to stop. Even there, however, people find ways to [[socialize]], because human beings will always colonise a bench.","Cities also make their residents unwell in ways that are difficult to measure. Traffic noise is strongly associated with [[insomnia]], and the effect is not merely mechanical; it is the mind\'s refusal to switch off. We [[obsess]] over small sounds at three in the morning. We [[suppress]] the irritation all day, and it returns at night. Nothing is more [[unsettle|unsettling]] than a noise you cannot identify, and nothing is easier to exaggerate. I have watched neighbours go entirely [[overboard]] about a ventilation fan three floors away.","Bad design does not merely annoy people; over time it [[deform|deforms]] the way they behave. Give a street no shade and no seats, and within a season people will stop walking along it. Remove the crossing, and pedestrians begin to cross wherever they please, and drivers begin to expect it. A city is not a machine that holds its shape. Left alone, it will [[warp]] slowly in whichever direction is easiest, and the only remedy is the most old-fashioned one available: somebody has to keep paying attention."],"exercises":[{"id":"b6u19-e1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"Which three kinds of traffic does the writer identify on the avenue?","options":["Buses, taxis and private cars","Freight vehicles, bicycle couriers and the tram","Delivery vans, motorcycles and pedestrians","Trams, trains and coaches"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 1 lists freight moving between warehouses, the courier on a bicycle, and the tram."},{"id":"b6u19-e2","type":"multiple-choice","question":"According to the writer, what prevents the morning traffic from becoming chaos?","options":["Strict enforcement by traffic police","The design of the tram network","Unwritten rules of behaviour that road users absorb without being taught","The high cost of driving into the city centre"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"Paragraph 2 states that what prevents chaos is not law but etiquette — unwritten rules that every road user absorbs."},{"id":"b6u19-e3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What does the writer say about the new towers near the station?","options":["They have improved the range of housing available to ordinary residents","Their high rents exclude many people and their design discourages people from lingering","They were designed to encourage socialising on the ground floors","They are cheaper than the surrounding buildings"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 3 describes them as exclusive, notes that a whole category of resident has been excluded, and says the buildings seem designed to propel you through rather than invite you to stop."},{"id":"b6u19-e4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What is the writer\'s main point in the final paragraph?","options":["Cities should be rebuilt from scratch every generation","Poor design gradually changes how people behave, and constant attention is needed","Pedestrians are mainly responsible for unsafe streets","Shade and seating are the most expensive elements of urban design"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"The final paragraph argues that bad design deforms behaviour over time and that the city will warp in the easiest direction unless somebody keeps paying attention."},{"id":"b6u19-e5","type":"multiple-choice","question":"As used in paragraph 2, \\"liable\\" most nearly means…","options":["legally responsible for damage","likely to experience something","forbidden by regulation","entitled to compensation"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"The sentence means that if you break the rules you are likely to be shouted at, so \'liable to\' here expresses likelihood."},{"id":"b6u19-e6","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The roads into the airport become badly ___ on Friday evenings.\\"","options":["congested","intuitive","exclusive","liable"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"If something is congested it is full or blocked, which is exactly what roads become at a busy time."},{"id":"b6u19-e7","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"He had never learned the ___ of dining with colleagues from another country.\\"","options":["etiquette","freight","premium","insomnia"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Etiquette is the set of rules about polite behaviour, which is what one needs to learn for formal meals."},{"id":"b6u19-e8","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"She has suffered from ___ since the move, and rarely sleeps more than four hours.\\"","options":["insomnia","etiquette","freight","privilege"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Insomnia is a condition in which a person has difficulty sleeping, which matches sleeping only four hours a night."},{"id":"b6u19-e9","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Customers pay a ___ for seats with extra legroom.\\"","options":["premium","garment","tram","courier"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"A premium is a payment higher than the usual rate, which is what airlines charge for better seats."},{"id":"b6u19-e10","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Leave the wooden frame in damp conditions and it will gradually ___.\\"","options":["warp","propel","socialize","suppress"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To warp is to become bent into the wrong shape, which is what damp conditions do to wood."}]},"20":{"id":"b6-u20","book":6,"unit":20,"title":"The White Slope","level":"C1","topic":"A morning in the upper valley, and what came down it","generatedAt":"2026-09-23","wordCount":442,"paragraphs":["The morning of the accident was [[overcast]], the kind of flat grey that sits low in the valley and makes everything look nearer than it is. My father called it a good day for staying indoors, and he meant it literally. He was not a superstitious man, but he had a habit of reading the weather as an [[omen]], and that morning he read it badly enough to keep us at the lodge until eleven.","Above us, snow had been [[accumulate|accumulating]] on the north face for a week with no wind to shift it. Everyone in the village knew what that meant; nobody seriously expected an [[avalanche]] in the first week of March. The [[ranger]] who patrolled the upper valley had [[seclude|secluded]] himself at the hut since January, checking his [[apparatus]] twice a day, and he had sent down two warnings that nobody had acted on. On his wall hung an [[aerial]] photograph of the slope taken in December, and beside it one from the previous week. The difference between the two was his entire argument.","What saved us was [[consistency]]. My father had walked the same route for nineteen years, always at the same hour, always stopping at the same boulder. On that particular morning he stopped a hundred metres short of it, because he had seen a [[speck]] of white move [[sideways]] on the slope above — a small thing, easily missed. Then the slope itself began to move. We watched a wall of snow cross the path we would have been standing on, take three pines out at the base, and come to rest in a heap of [[rubble]] that filled the lower gully completely.","I was [[discharge|discharged]] from the clinic the following evening, more or less [[intact]]: a broken wrist and a great deal of bruising. My father was not so fortunate, and for two days nobody could tell us whether he would live. I remember sitting in the corridor and [[sob|sobbing]] without any dignity whatsoever, and I remember a nurse telling me, with a gentleness I found almost [[poignant]], that he was [[mortal]] like everybody else and that this was the whole of the difficulty.","It is an [[episode]] I have never been able to discuss in a [[sober]] way. People tell me I was lucky, which is true and useless. What I actually took from it was something my [[upbringing]] had given me without my noticing: the habit of stopping, of looking up before crossing open ground, of trusting a small observation over a large expectation. My father recovered. He walks more slowly now, and he still reads the weather, and he is still usually right."],"exercises":[{"id":"b6u20-e1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"Why did the family stay at the lodge until eleven that morning?","options":["The road down the valley had been closed","The father read the overcast weather as a bad sign","The ranger had forbidden anyone to leave","They were waiting for a delivery of equipment"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 1 says the father had a habit of reading the weather as an omen and read it badly enough to keep them at the lodge until eleven."},{"id":"b6u20-e2","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What had the ranger been doing since January?","options":["Leading guided walks up the north face","Living alone at the hut, checking his equipment twice daily and sending warnings","Photographing the village for a local exhibition","Repairing the lower gully after an earlier fall"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 2 describes him as having secluded himself at the hut since January, checking his apparatus twice a day and sending down two warnings."},{"id":"b6u20-e3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What does the writer identify as having saved them?","options":["The ranger\'s warnings, which they finally acted on","A change in the wind direction at the last moment","The father\'s long habit of following the same route, which made him stop short of his usual rock","The nurse\'s decision to keep them at the clinic"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"Paragraph 3 attributes it to consistency: the father stopped a hundred metres short of his usual stopping place after noticing a speck of white move sideways."},{"id":"b6u20-e4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What does the writer say they took from the experience?","options":["A lasting fear of walking in the upper valley","A conviction that the ranger should have done more","A habit of pausing and trusting small observations over large expectations","A determination never to travel in March again"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"The final paragraph says the writer took the habit of stopping, looking up before crossing open ground, and trusting a small observation over a large expectation."},{"id":"b6u20-e5","type":"multiple-choice","question":"As used in paragraph 4, \\"poignant\\" most nearly means…","options":["causing a strong feeling of sadness","confusing and hard to follow","professionally detached","amusing in an unexpected way"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"The nurse\'s gentleness is described as almost poignant in the middle of a distressing scene, so the word refers to something that stirs deep sadness."},{"id":"b6u20-e6","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"After the storm, rescue teams searched the ___ for anyone trapped beneath the fallen walls.\\"","options":["rubble","speck","omen","apparatus"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Rubble is the pile of broken stone and wood left after a building is destroyed, which is where people would be trapped."},{"id":"b6u20-e7","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Despite the crash, the parcel inside was completely ___.\\"","options":["intact","mortal","overcast","aerial"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"If something is intact it is complete and undamaged, which is what the sentence says about the parcel."},{"id":"b6u20-e8","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Dust had begun to ___ on the shelves during the years the house stood empty.\\"","options":["accumulate","discharge","seclude","sob"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To accumulate something is to collect a lot of it over time, which is how dust builds up in an unused house."},{"id":"b6u20-e9","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The sky turned ___ and we hurried home before the rain.\\"","options":["overcast","poignant","intact","sober"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"When the sky is overcast it is full of clouds, which is the usual warning of approaching rain."},{"id":"b6u20-e10","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Sailors once regarded a red sunset as a favourable ___.\\"","options":["omen","ranger","apparatus","upbringing"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"An omen is a sign of what will happen in the future, which is exactly how a red sunset was interpreted."}]},"21":{"id":"b6-u21","book":6,"unit":21,"title":"The House That Kept Its Rules","level":"C1","topic":"Memory, service and a house in decline","generatedAt":"2026-09-23","wordCount":455,"paragraphs":["For forty years Mr. Aldridge had served as [[butler]] at Thornbury Hall, and in all that time he had never once arrived late. Even on this final morning, walking up the drive for the last time, he kept to his usual pace — unhurried, exact — as though the gravel itself were watching him. The house, he reflected, was a [[testament]] to a way of living that had quietly ended years earlier, without anyone ever announcing it. There had been no ceremony, no sale, no argument; the family had simply stopped coming, and the rooms had gone on waiting.","The building was badly [[dilapidated]]. [[slate|Slates]] had slid from the roof in the winter storms, and the [[timber]] beneath them had softened to the texture of wet bread. In the scullery a [[mound]] of old newspapers had grown against the wall, and the [[filth]] that gathered behind the pipes disgusted even him — a man who had spent his whole working life among other people\'s mess and had never once complained about it. The damp had reached the plaster in three rooms, and the smell of it met him at the door like a greeting.","Indoors, not a single room had been [[decorate|decorated]] since the 1970s. One [[faucet]] dripped into the sink with the patience of a clock, and the [[valve]] beneath it had rusted shut, so that nobody could have stopped the water even if they had wanted to. He set a fallen [[stool]] upright — the same stool on which he had eaten his lunch for four decades — and considered [[housekeeping]] as a discipline rather than a job: a way of holding chaos at arm\'s length for one more day.","His hands had begun to [[cramp]] on the long drive, and his fingertips were [[numb]] with the cold. He was not, by temperament, a [[reckless]] man; he had spent his career preventing accidents rather than causing them, and he had been good at it. Yet standing in the hall, listening to the water find its patient way into the sink, he could see the [[flaw]] in his own reasoning, the one he had never admitted aloud — that a house is not preserved by rules, only postponed by them.","At the gate he turned and allowed himself a [[grin]]: brief, private, almost boyish. The place would [[evoke]] nothing in the visitors who would come to photograph it next spring; they would see a ruin and reach for their cameras. He would remember the polish, the silver, the small daily [[bliss]] of a job done exactly right. Then he raised his hat in a silent [[farewell]] and walked down to the road, where a taxi had been waiting, as arranged, for precisely eleven minutes."],"exercises":[{"id":"b6u21-e1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What is the passage mainly about?","options":["A family deciding to sell a historic house","An ageing butler\'s last visit to the house he served","A dispute between a butler and his employers","The restoration of a country house by volunteers"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"The whole passage follows Mr. Aldridge walking through Thornbury Hall for the last time, remembering his forty years of service there."},{"id":"b6u21-e2","type":"multiple-choice","question":"Why could nobody stop the dripping water?","options":["The plumbing had been removed years earlier","The valve beneath the sink had rusted shut","There was no longer any water supply to the house","The faucet had been sealed with plaster"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 3 says the valve beneath the sink had rusted shut, so nobody could have stopped the water even if they had wanted to."},{"id":"b6u21-e3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What does the passage suggest about why the house was left to decay?","options":["The family lost a legal case over the property","A fire destroyed the upper floors","The family simply stopped coming, with no formal decision","The house was too far from the nearest town"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"Paragraph 1 states that there was no ceremony, no sale and no argument — the family had simply stopped coming and the rooms went on waiting."},{"id":"b6u21-e4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"As used in paragraph 4, the word \\"flaw\\" most nearly means…","options":["a crack in the plaster","a weakness in an argument or belief","a stain on a fabric","a missing document"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"The \\"flaw\\" here is in his own reasoning — the unadmitted weakness in the belief that rules can preserve a house."},{"id":"b6u21-e5","type":"multiple-choice","question":"Which phrase best describes Mr. Aldridge\'s attitude as he leaves?","options":["Bitter and resentful towards the family","Indifferent to the fate of the house","Affectionate but resigned to its decline","Eager to buy the property himself"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"He smiles privately and recalls the \\"bliss\\" of work well done, yet accepts that the house is a ruin and that a house is only \\"postponed\\" by rules — affectionate but resigned."},{"id":"b6u21-e6","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The old cinema, now ___ and boarded up, had once been the pride of the town.\\"","options":["dilapidated","numb","reckless","overjoyed"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"A dilapidated building is old and in bad condition, which fits a boarded-up cinema that has fallen into decay."},{"id":"b6u21-e7","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Those photographs ___ memories of summers we had long forgotten.\\"","options":["cramp","evoke","decorate","grin"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"To evoke a memory is to make it occur or come back to mind, which is exactly what old photographs do."},{"id":"b6u21-e8","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"He drove at ___ speed through the narrow village lanes, ignoring every warning sign.\\"","options":["numb","dilapidated","reckless","hearty"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"Reckless describes acting in an unsafe way without care for the consequences, which matches ignoring warning signs."},{"id":"b6u21-e9","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Her patience with the most difficult customers was a ___ to her training.\\"","options":["testament","mound","farewell","valve"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"A testament to something shows that it exists or is true, so her patience shows the quality of her training."},{"id":"b6u21-e10","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The ___ opened the door, took their coats and showed them into the dining room.\\"","options":["stool","slate","butler","cramp"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"A butler is the most important male servant in a wealthy house, the person who would greet guests and take their coats."}]},"22":{"id":"b6-u22","book":6,"unit":22,"title":"What the Handbook Didn\'t Say","level":"C1","topic":"A young soldier\'s first posting on a distant border","generatedAt":"2026-09-23","wordCount":441,"paragraphs":["When I [[enlist|enlisted]] at seventeen, I imagined the army would make a man of me. The recruiting officer handed me a [[handbook]] of two hundred pages and told me to learn it by heart. I read it twice on the train and understood almost nothing; the words described a war, but not the one I was about to meet. I still have it somewhere, with my name written inside the cover in blue ink that has long since faded to grey.","Our post stood on a [[frontier]] that no map quite agreed on. Beyond the wire stretched a [[tract]] of [[lush]] green valley, so beautiful in the early light that it seemed almost [[splendid]], and somewhere inside it moved small groups of [[guerilla|guerillas]] who knew every path and every hiding place. They used [[camouflage]] cut from the trees themselves, and we, in our bright [[outfit|outfits]], might as well have been waving flags at them. At night the valley filled with mist, and the mist made everything sound closer than it really was.","I was [[hesitant]] from the first night. I lay on a narrow [[cot]] listening to the generator and tried to [[contemplate]] what I would do if they came. What I had to [[contend]] with, in truth, was not the enemy but my own imagination. I could not sleep. Instead I kept a [[vigil]], hour after hour, with a [[torch]] I did not dare switch on. Fear, I discovered, is not dramatic; it is tedious, and it lasts.","On the fourth day a dog [[stray|strayed]] into the compound, thin and frightened, and I fed it from my own rations. It followed me everywhere, its [[paw|paws]] leaving small prints in the dust, and slept beside my boots. I began to talk to it, which the handbook had certainly not recommended, and it was the only thing that kept me from weeping. The dog had no name for the first three days, and then it had one.","The attack, when it came, was smaller than I had feared and over in twenty minutes. Afterwards I sat with the dog and felt [[weary]] beyond anything I had words for. I had learned something [[substantial]] that night, though it took me years to put it into a sentence: that I was not brave, and probably never would be, but that I could [[confront]] my fear and stay exactly where I was standing. That, it turned out, was the whole of the lesson. The dog stayed with me until the transport came, and then it did not. I have never told anyone that, and I am not entirely sure why I am telling it now."],"exercises":[{"id":"b6u22-e1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"Why did the handbook fail the narrator?","options":["It had been written in a language he could not read","It described a kind of war that was not the one he faced","It had been lost on the train journey","It was too short to be useful"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 1 says the words described a war, but not the one he was about to meet."},{"id":"b6u22-e2","type":"multiple-choice","question":"How did the narrator spend the night before the attack?","options":["Sleeping deeply on his cot","Patrolling the wire with a lit torch","Keeping a sleepless watch without using his torch","Writing letters home by the generator"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"Paragraph 3 says he could not sleep and instead kept a vigil hour after hour, with a torch he did not dare switch on."},{"id":"b6u22-e3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What does the description of the enemy\'s camouflage suggest?","options":["They had better equipment than the narrator\'s unit","They were poorly organised and easy to spot","They knew the terrain far better than the defenders","They avoided the valley altogether"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"They made camouflage from the trees and knew every path and hiding place, while the defenders wore bright outfits — a contrast that shows their knowledge of the terrain."},{"id":"b6u22-e4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"As used in paragraph 3, \\"contend with\\" most nearly means…","options":["argue publicly about","struggle to deal with","compete for a prize","quietly ignore"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"He says the thing he had to contend with was his own imagination — something he had to struggle against and overcome."},{"id":"b6u22-e5","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What conclusion does the narrator reach by the end of the passage?","options":["That he was braver than he had realised","That the handbook should be rewritten","That courage means staying in place despite fear","That soldiers should never keep pets"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"He states that he was not brave but could confront his fear and stay where he was standing — that was the whole lesson."},{"id":"b6u22-e6","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The nurse slept on a folding ___ beside the patient\'s bed.\\"","options":["cot","tract","torch","frontier"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"A cot is a small portable bed, which is what a nurse would use beside a patient."},{"id":"b6u22-e7","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"After twelve hours on their feet, the crew were too ___ to argue about anything.\\"","options":["lush","weary","hesitant","substantial"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Weary means tired, which explains why the crew had no energy left to argue."},{"id":"b6u22-e8","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"She was ___ about signing the contract until she had read every clause.\\"","options":["splendid","hesitant","weary","lush"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Hesitant means unsure or slow to act, which fits someone who waits until she has read every clause."},{"id":"b6u22-e9","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The river has long marked the ___ between the two provinces.\\"","options":["outfit","vigil","frontier","handbook"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"A frontier is a border between two regions, so a river marking the boundary fits exactly."},{"id":"b6u22-e10","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Hundreds of people held a candlelit ___ in the square until dawn.\\"","options":["vigil","tract","camouflage","paw"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"A vigil is a period of watchful attention at night for a specific purpose, which matches a candlelit gathering until dawn."}]},"23":{"id":"b6-u23","book":6,"unit":23,"title":"Chaos in Three Acts","level":"C1","topic":"A theatre review","generatedAt":"2026-09-23","wordCount":418,"paragraphs":["The new play at the Riverside Theatre is a [[satire]], and a sharp one. It opens [[backstage]], [[amid]] the ropes and dusty lamps, where a nervous stagehand is trying to explain to a visiting [[billionaire]] why the leading actor has locked himself in his dressing room and will not come out. The set is little more than scaffolding and a few chairs. Within ten minutes I had forgotten that I was watching actors at all.","The billionaire, played with magnificent comic timing, is a [[brute]] in an expensive suit. He shouts, he [[collide|collides]] with the furniture, he is [[clumsy]] with everything he touches, and he is entirely convinced that he is charming. The plot turns on a simple mistake. Someone has stored a [[flammable]] liquid beside the heating pipes, and by the interval the theatre is being [[evacuate|evacuated]] into the street, where a small [[mob]] of disappointed ticket-holders is already gathering. The audience laughs, then stops laughing, which is the surest sign that a comedy is doing its job.","What follows is a [[tumult]] of mistaken identity and rising [[uproar]]. The director has chosen to [[segregate]] the cast into two halves of the stage, and the effect is genuinely clever: we are forced to watch both stories at once, never quite sure which one is real and which is being performed for the billionaire\'s benefit. Two characters argue for a full page about a coat, and the argument is really about everything else.","It would be [[premature]] to call the play a masterpiece. The second act drags, and the ending arrives far too neatly. The second half is shorter than the first, and better. But the writing [[subject|subjects]] its characters to a level of [[scrutiny]] that few comedies dare, and it never [[underestimate|underestimates]] how much an audience can hold in mind at once. The play asks us to consider the possibility that the [[culprit]] is not the man who struck the match but the world that sold him the fuel.","Some critics will [[resent]] its sharpness; others will [[testify]] that they have not laughed so uncomfortably in years. Both reactions are, in a sense, the point. Go expecting chaos and you will not be disappointed; go expecting answers and you will leave annoyed. Either way, you will be arguing about it the next morning, which is more than can be said for most of what opens in this city. The Riverside has staged three new plays this season; this is the only one that has stayed with me."],"exercises":[{"id":"b6u23-e1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What is the writer\'s main purpose in this passage?","options":["To summarise the plot of a novel","To review a new satirical play","To complain about ticket prices at a theatre","To interview a stagehand about his work"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"The passage assesses a new play — its staging, writing and effect on the audience — which is the work of a theatre review."},{"id":"b6u23-e2","type":"multiple-choice","question":"Why is the theatre evacuated?","options":["A fire starts in the dressing room","A flammable liquid was left beside the heating pipes","The mob forces the doors open","The billionaire demands that everyone leave"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 2 says someone had stored a flammable liquid beside the heating pipes, and by the interval the theatre is being evacuated."},{"id":"b6u23-e3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What does the writer think the director\'s staging choice achieves?","options":["It hides the weakness of the second act","It gives the billionaire more time on stage","It forces the audience to follow two stories at once","It makes the play shorter and cheaper to stage"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"The writer calls the decision to segregate the cast genuinely clever because we must watch both stories at once, unsure which is real."},{"id":"b6u23-e4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"As used in paragraph 4, the verb \\"subjects\\" most nearly means…","options":["makes a topic of study","forces to experience","places under a ruler","changes the wording of"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"The writing subjects its characters to scrutiny — it forces them to undergo that close examination."},{"id":"b6u23-e5","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What is the writer\'s overall attitude towards the play?","options":["Admiring but not uncritical","Completely hostile","Politely indifferent","Excited and entirely uncritical"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"The writer praises the satire, the staging and the writing, but also notes that the second act drags and the ending is too neat."},{"id":"b6u23-e6","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Keep ___ liquids well away from the stove.\\"","options":["flammable","premature","clumsy","cosmopolitan"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Flammable means able to catch fire easily, which is why such liquids must be kept away from a stove."},{"id":"b6u23-e7","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Firefighters ordered residents to ___ the building immediately.\\"","options":["collide","evacuate","testify","resent"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"To evacuate is to leave a place of danger for a place of safety, exactly what firefighters would order."},{"id":"b6u23-e8","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"He was too ___ to carry the tray without spilling something.\\"","options":["clumsy","flammable","premature","splendid"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Clumsy means awkward in handling things, which explains why he spilled the contents of the tray."},{"id":"b6u23-e9","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The company\'s accounts came under close ___ after the audit.\\"","options":["uproar","scrutiny","tumult","satire"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Scrutiny is careful examination, so accounts coming under close scrutiny means they were examined very carefully."},{"id":"b6u23-e10","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Never ___ how much a determined beginner can learn in a single year.\\"","options":["underestimate","evacuate","segregate","collide"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To underestimate is to think less of someone than they really are, which fits the warning about a determined beginner."}]},"24":{"id":"b6-u24","book":6,"unit":24,"title":"The Kite and the Storm","level":"C1","topic":"How an amateur scientist measured the upper air","generatedAt":"2026-09-23","wordCount":423,"paragraphs":["In the 1880s, [[meteorology]] was still a science of educated guesswork. Observers wrote down the temperature and the direction of the wind at ground level, and then argued about what the numbers meant. Elinor Vance, a schoolteacher with no laboratory and very little money, thought the arguing was the problem. Nobody, she pointed out, had ever measured the air that actually produces the weather.","Her first instrument was [[ingenious]] and almost free: a box kite carrying a sealed glass tube and a thin copper wire. When a storm approached, the wire carried a faint current and the needle jumped. She had, without quite realising it, begun to record an [[electromagnetic]] field from a thousand feet up. Her notebooks filled with [[meteorological]] readings taken at heights no one had ever sampled before.","The work was [[dreary]] in the ordinary sense — cold mornings, wet paper, fingers that would not warm up — but she was [[persistent]], and persistence in science is worth more than brilliance. Every [[defect]] in her apparatus, every cracked tube and bent frame, she recorded in detail and then rebuilt [[anew]]. Her assistants learned to [[duplicate]] each instrument exactly, so that two readings could be compared and errors traced to their source.","Her great insight concerned [[propulsion]]. A kite rises because air moves across its surface; a heavier craft might do the same if it could [[accelerate]] to the right speed and then [[glide]] on the lift it produced. She built models of balsa and silk and [[simulate|simulated]] the airflow with smoke from a pipe, watching how the streamlines [[penetrate|penetrated]] the gaps between the wings.","She was, in this as in everything, quietly [[innovative]]. Long before most physicists spoke easily of the [[electron]], she suspected that electricity was not a fluid but something made of particles far too small to see, and she said so in a paper that almost nobody read. The word [[innovation]] does not appear in a single review of her work, and reviewers were slow to be [[stimulate|stimulated]] by any of it. What [[spur|spurred]] her on was neither fame nor money but a private conviction that the sky was readable, and that reading it would one day save lives.","When a young engineer finally managed to [[launch]] a glider of her design from a hillside, she watched it in silence for a long time. Then she wrote a single line in her notebook: \'It flew.\' She was right, and every forecast we now take for granted descends, in a long and complicated line, from that stubborn kite."],"exercises":[{"id":"b6u24-e1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What is the passage mainly about?","options":["The history of the box kite as a toy","How an amateur scientist advanced the measurement of weather","The rivalry between two nineteenth-century physicists","Why gliders were dangerous in the 1880s"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"The passage follows Elinor Vance\'s kite-borne instruments and her wider ideas about airflow, showing how an outsider improved weather measurement."},{"id":"b6u24-e2","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What did her first instrument consist of?","options":["A sealed glass tube and a thin copper wire carried by a kite","A balloon filled with hot air and a small clock","A wooden tower with a wind gauge on top","A glass jar buried in the soil"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Paragraph 2 describes a box kite carrying a sealed glass tube and a thin copper wire."},{"id":"b6u24-e3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"How did she study the movement of air over a wing?","options":["By dropping models from a tower","By simulating the airflow with smoke from a pipe","By measuring the temperature of the wing","By copying designs published by other engineers"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 4 says she simulated the airflow with smoke from a pipe and watched how the streamlines penetrated the gaps between the wings."},{"id":"b6u24-e4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"As used in paragraph 3, the word \\"dreary\\" most nearly means…","options":["badly paid","dull, cold and depressing","difficult to understand","dangerous"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"The writer lists cold mornings, wet paper and cold fingers — the work was dull and depressing rather than dangerous or badly paid."},{"id":"b6u24-e5","type":"multiple-choice","question":"Why does the writer quote the notebook line \\"It flew\\"?","options":["To show that she had given up on her theories","To mark the quiet moment when her design was proved right","To suggest that the glider was built by someone else","To show that she preferred flying to writing"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"After years of persistence, the brief note records the moment her design worked — a restrained but decisive piece of evidence."},{"id":"b6u24-e6","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The car began to ___ as it reached the open road.\\"","options":["glide","accelerate","penetrate","simulate"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"To accelerate is to increase in speed, which is what a car does on reaching an open road."},{"id":"b6u24-e7","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Her ___ questions eventually forced the committee to explain itself.\\"","options":["persistent","dreary","premature","clumsy"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"A persistent person keeps on working and does not give up, which is why her repeated questions eventually worked."},{"id":"b6u24-e8","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The eagle seemed to ___ across the valley without moving its wings.\\"","options":["accelerate","launch","glide","duplicate"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"To glide is to move through the air with little or no effort, which is exactly what the eagle appeared to be doing."},{"id":"b6u24-e9","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"A good teacher knows how to ___ curiosity rather than merely fill notebooks.\\"","options":["stimulate","simulate","penetrate","duplicate"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To stimulate something is to cause or increase activity in it, so stimulating curiosity means encouraging it to grow."},{"id":"b6u24-e10","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Please ___ the file before making any changes to it.\\"","options":["duplicate","launch","spur","glide"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To duplicate something is to copy it, which is why you copy a file before altering it."}]},"25":{"id":"b6-u25","book":6,"unit":25,"title":"Four Entries from the Valley","level":"C1","topic":"A nurse\'s diary from a remote clinic","generatedAt":"2026-09-23","wordCount":428,"paragraphs":["Monday. The bus left at six, and I had packed my bag [[beforehand]] so there was nothing to do but stand at the window and wait. The road into the valley is [[winding]] and narrow, and by the third hour everything outside had become [[blurred]] — trees, walls, the occasional farmhouse, all smeared into one grey band. I [[zip|zipped]] my coat to the chin and slept until we stopped. I had been warned that the road was bad; nobody had warned me that it would be beautiful. The driver stopped twice to let sheep cross, and neither of us minded.","The clinic occupies what was once a [[manor]], and above it, on the ridge, stands a [[monastery]] whose bells I can hear at seven each morning. The [[exterior]] is shabby and the paint has gone entirely, but inside the place is warm and startlingly [[hospitable]]. Sister Anne met me at the door with both arms [[outstretched]] and a [[hearty]] welcome that could probably have been heard in the village. There are fourteen beds and, on the day I arrived, nineteen patients.","Tuesday. There is a [[nursery]] on the ground floor, four cots and a window facing the hills. In the afternoon the temperature fell to five [[degree|degrees]] [[centigrade]] and the children\'s breath [[condense|condensed]] on the glass. They [[chatter|chattered]] constantly, in a dialect I only half understand, and their [[humor]] improved the moment I produced the [[parcel]] my sister had sent — sweets, mostly, and a small radio. I learned four new words that afternoon and forgot three of them by dinner.","Wednesday. One of the older patients began to [[deteriorate]] overnight. I sat with her while a [[concerto]] played quietly on the radio and watched her [[profile]] against the lamp. She did not want to talk and I did not make her. Sometimes the whole of nursing is simply staying in the room, hour after hour, and letting the room do the rest. The radio reception comes and goes with the weather, and the concerto kept dissolving into static.","Thursday. She was better this morning, which nobody had predicted. I walked up to the monastery in the afternoon and stood in the courtyard, listening to the bells and to the wind coming down off the ridge. I had come here expecting to teach something, and I am leaving with the uncomfortable sense that I have been taught instead — about patience, mostly, and about how much a stranger\'s kindness can carry. On the bus home I realised that I had written almost nothing in this diary about medicine."],"exercises":[{"id":"b6u25-e1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What kind of text is this?","options":["A formal report on a clinic\'s finances","A series of diary entries by a nurse on a short placement","A letter of complaint about a bus journey","A travel advertisement for a mountain valley"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"The passage is organised as dated entries (Monday to Thursday) written in the first person by a nurse at a valley clinic."},{"id":"b6u25-e2","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What is on the ground floor of the clinic?","options":["A kitchen and a dining room","A chapel with a painted ceiling","A nursery with four cots","A storeroom full of old furniture"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"The Tuesday entry says there is a nursery on the ground floor, with four cots and a window facing the hills."},{"id":"b6u25-e3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What did the parcel contain?","options":["Sweets and a small radio","Warm clothes and letters","Medical supplies for the clinic","Books and writing paper"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"The Tuesday entry lists sweets, mostly, and a small radio, sent by the writer\'s sister."},{"id":"b6u25-e4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"As used in paragraph 3, the word \\"humor\\" most nearly means…","options":["jokes and funny stories","mood or spirits","a sense of what is amusing","the noise of a crowd"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Here their humor improved when the parcel arrived — the word refers to the children\'s mood or spirits rather than to comedy."},{"id":"b6u25-e5","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What does the writer conclude at the end of the passage?","options":["That the clinic is badly managed","That she intends to return permanently","That she has been taught as much as she has taught","That she regrets making the journey"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"She says she came expecting to teach and is leaving with the sense that she has been taught instead — about patience and kindness."},{"id":"b6u25-e6","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"If you prepare the vegetables ___, the cooking will take half the time.\\"","options":["beforehand","afterwards","centigrade","winding"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"If something is done beforehand, it is done in advance, which is what preparing vegetables early means."},{"id":"b6u25-e7","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"His handwriting ___ so much that the last page was almost unreadable.\\"","options":["condensed","deteriorated","chattered","zipped"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"To deteriorate is to become steadily worse, which fits handwriting that grows less readable."},{"id":"b6u25-e8","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The villagers were so ___ that we were never once allowed to pay for a meal.\\"","options":["hospitable","blurred","hearty","outstretched"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Hospitable describes someone who is friendly and generous to guests or strangers, which explains their refusal to let us pay."},{"id":"b6u25-e9","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"She ran to meet him with ___ arms.\\"","options":["winding","outstretched","blurred","hearty"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Outstretched means extended to full length, which is how arms are held open to welcome someone."},{"id":"b6u25-e10","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Without my glasses, the numbers on the board were completely ___.\\"","options":["hospitable","blurred","centigrade","hearty"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Something blurred is not seen clearly, which is exactly the effect of reading a board without glasses."}]},"26":{"id":"b6-u26","book":6,"unit":26,"title":"Twelve Years of Arguing","level":"C1","topic":"How a small town rewrote its own rules","generatedAt":"2026-09-23","wordCount":429,"paragraphs":["Sherman is not a large town and has never pretended to be an important one. Yet the archive in its library holds a document that students of local history still travel to read: a [[charter]] of ninety-four pages, [[amend|amended]] more than two hundred times, which sets out in stubborn detail what the town may and may not do. It is kept in a glass case, and visitors are asked not to lean on the case.","The story begins with a mayor who was, by any reasonable standard, [[infamous]]. He had promised at his first public meeting to [[abolish]] the town\'s old system of charges, and then discovered within a month that he could not. He was [[lame]] in one leg and [[limp|limped]] noticeably, a fact his critics used against him for years, though it had nothing whatever to do with his abilities. His supporters, meanwhile, tried to [[flatter]] him into resigning quietly, and failed.","What followed was twelve years of argument. Committees were formed, and reports were [[disseminate|disseminated]] through the local paper. A [[poll]] of residents found that most of them wanted the rules simplified but could not agree on how. A new [[constitution]] was drafted, and a small committee of eleven volunteers — a shopkeeper, two teachers, a nurse, a farmer and several others — was asked to [[tackle]] the practical questions the lawyers had left open.","The debates were not always dignified. Meetings ran so long that the audience sometimes slipped into a [[trance]], and there was one famous [[outburst]] from a resident who had waited three hours for his turn to speak. A period of [[secrecy]] followed, which later caused considerable [[remorse]] among those who had insisted on it. Some members wanted to [[censor]] the minutes; others wanted every word printed. In the end, every word was printed, which is why the archive is now so long. Reading it today, you can hear the arguments in the punctuation.","Was it worth it? The town became unexpectedly [[cosmopolitan]] for its size: families arrived from six countries while the document was being written, and the new rules had to [[aspire]] to something larger than the habits of the people who wrote them. The mayor never recovered his reputation, and his stubbornness grew almost [[pathological]] in his final years. But the [[phenomenal]] effort of those eleven volunteers outlasted him. Their real achievement was not the document. It was the discovery that a small place can argue its way, slowly and painfully, towards fairness. The charter has been amended eleven times since, and each amendment is shorter than the last."],"exercises":[{"id":"b6u26-e1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What is the passage mainly about?","options":["The career of a successful town mayor","A long public effort to rewrite a town\'s governing document","The history of a library archive","A dispute between two neighbouring towns"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"The passage traces twelve years of committees, drafts and debate that produced the town\'s amended charter and new constitution."},{"id":"b6u26-e2","type":"multiple-choice","question":"How long did the argument over the rules last?","options":["Six months","Two years","Twelve years","Ninety-four years"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"Paragraph 3 opens with the statement that what followed was twelve years of argument."},{"id":"b6u26-e3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What was decided about the minutes of the meetings?","options":["They were destroyed after a year","They were censored before publication","Every word was printed","Only the final decisions were recorded"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"Paragraph 4 says some wanted to censor the minutes, but in the end every word was printed — which is why the archive is so long."},{"id":"b6u26-e4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"As used in paragraph 5, the verb \\"aspire\\" most nearly means…","options":["breathe in deeply","aim at or seek to reach","refuse to accept","explain in detail"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"The new rules had to aspire to something larger — that is, they had to aim at a higher standard than the habits of their authors."},{"id":"b6u26-e5","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What does the writer consider the volunteers\' real achievement?","options":["The length of the finished document","The removal of the mayor from his post","The discovery that a small place can argue its way towards fairness","The money saved by simplifying the rules"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"The final paragraph states that their real achievement was not the document but the discovery that a small place can argue its way towards fairness."},{"id":"b6u26-e6","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The committee voted to ___ the outdated charge entirely.\\"","options":["amend","abolish","tackle","disseminate"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"To abolish something is to put an end to it, so abolishing a charge means removing it completely."},{"id":"b6u26-e7","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"You may ___ the contract only with the written consent of both parties.\\"","options":["amend","censor","flatter","aspire"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To amend something is to change it in order to improve or correct it, which is what altering a contract clause means."},{"id":"b6u26-e8","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"He felt deep ___ for the way he had spoken to her.\\"","options":["secrecy","remorse","outburst","trance"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Remorse is a strong feeling of sadness and regret, which is what he feels about his words."},{"id":"b6u26-e9","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The new manager was hired to ___ the company\'s mounting debts.\\"","options":["flatter","tackle","limp","censor"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"To tackle something is to deal with it in a determined and efficient way, which is what a new manager would be expected to do with debts."},{"id":"b6u26-e10","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The building is ___ for its leaking roof and freezing corridors.\\"","options":["infamous","cosmopolitan","phenomenal","lame"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Infamous means well known for something bad, which fits a building known for its dreadful conditions."}]},"27":{"id":"b6-u27","book":6,"unit":27,"title":"The Words She Would Not Cut","level":"C1","topic":"A literary editor and the languages she published","generatedAt":"2026-09-23","wordCount":434,"paragraphs":["Marta Rein edited a small literary quarterly for nineteen years, and in that time she [[delete|deleted]] more of her own writing than she ever published. She was [[earnest]] to the point of awkwardness, and she had a [[solemn]] manner that made new contributors nervous until they realised how gently she actually treated them.","Her magazine was, by design, [[inclusive]]. Submissions arrived in six languages; she read in three of them herself, was passably [[fluent]] in a fourth, and employed translators for the rest. She had grown up [[monolingual]] in a village where every family spoke the same dialect, and she never forgot how it had felt to meet a second language for the first time — the sudden sense that the world was considerably larger than her own street.","She had begun her career cataloguing [[imperial]] records in a university library, work that taught her how much of any language\'s history is borrowed, and she never lost the habit of tracing a word back to its origins. Her early notebooks are dense with [[linguistic]] detail: where a word came from, when it entered the language, how its meaning had quietly shifted. A [[legislature]] in a distant province had once tried to forbid the teaching of minority languages in schools; she devoted an entire issue to the question, printing the arguments of both sides and letting readers decide.","Not everyone approved. A rival editor accused her of [[undermine|undermining]] the tradition, of publishing work that was, in his phrase, not really ours. She answered him in print with far more [[tact]] than he deserved. Language, she wrote, is not a wall; it is a market. Nobody loses a word by learning another.","She was especially careful with writers whose [[nationality]] was held against them. She had seen how a sentence could be read differently depending on a surname, how a [[racial]] or [[ethnic]] assumption could decide whether a story was called universal or merely local. She kept a file of such cases and showed it, [[firsthand]], to anyone who doubted her. When a rival [[prosecute|prosecuted]] a former contributor over a disputed contract, she raised money for his defence and organised a public reading in [[solidarity]].","Some readers said she was no [[patriot]]; she replied that the word belonged to anyone who wanted their country\'s language to keep growing. In private she was a little of a [[coward]] about confrontation — she avoided it for weeks, and then wrote the finest essay of her career — but she never once tried to [[exclude]] a writer because a manuscript was difficult. Difficult, she said, was the whole point."],"exercises":[{"id":"b6u27-e1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What is the passage mainly about?","options":["The economics of running a small magazine","A literary editor\'s principles and the criticism they attracted","The history of a university library","A legal dispute over a publishing contract"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"The passage profiles Marta Rein\'s editorial choices — multilingual, inclusive publishing — and the objections those choices provoked."},{"id":"b6u27-e2","type":"multiple-choice","question":"How many of the submitted languages could Marta read herself?","options":["Six, with no need for translators","Two only","Three well, and a fourth passably","None; she relied entirely on translators"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"Paragraph 2 says she read in three languages herself, was passably fluent in a fourth, and employed translators for the rest."},{"id":"b6u27-e3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"How did she respond when a contributor was prosecuted?","options":["She advised him to settle out of court","She removed his work from the archive","She raised money for his defence and organised a public reading","She wrote an article attacking him"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"Paragraph 5 says she raised money for his defence and organised a public reading in solidarity."},{"id":"b6u27-e4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"As used in paragraph 4, the word \\"tact\\" most nearly means…","options":["a refusal to reply","sensitivity in dealing with people","skill in public speaking","a sense of humour"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"She answered with more tact than he deserved — that is, with more care not to offend than his attack warranted."},{"id":"b6u27-e5","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What did Marta mean by \\"Language is not a wall; it is a market\\"?","options":["Languages should be bought and sold","Languages grow through contact rather than being damaged by it","Only professional writers should translate","Some languages are more valuable than others"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"She follows the image with \\"Nobody loses a word by learning another\\", so the market image expresses gain through exchange, not loss."},{"id":"b6u27-e6","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"After two years in the city she was ___ in the local dialect.\\"","options":["fluent","earnest","solemn","inclusive"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"If someone is fluent in a language, they can speak it very well, which is the result of two years of practice."},{"id":"b6u27-e7","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The new policy would ___ part-time staff from the pension scheme.\\"","options":["exclude","prosecute","undermine","disseminate"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To exclude someone is to keep them out of a group, which is what the policy would do to part-time staff."},{"id":"b6u27-e8","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Constant criticism can ___ a child\'s confidence.\\"","options":["flatter","undermine","amend","testify"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"To undermine someone is to weaken their efforts or confidence, which is the effect of constant criticism."},{"id":"b6u27-e9","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"He made an ___ attempt to apologise, and she believed him.\\"","options":["earnest","imperial","monolingual","firsthand"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"Earnest means honest and sincere, which is why his apology was believed."},{"id":"b6u27-e10","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The editor had to ___ the final paragraph because it repeated an earlier point.\\"","options":["delete","flatter","prosecute","exclude"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To delete something is to remove written material, which is what an editor does with a redundant paragraph."}]},"28":{"id":"b6-u28","book":6,"unit":28,"title":"The Stunt at Platform Four","level":"C1","topic":"A staged kidnapping that goes badly wrong","generatedAt":"2026-09-23","wordCount":410,"paragraphs":["The plan was not a crime. That was the first thing Leo insisted on, and he insisted on it repeatedly, usually with his [[forefinger]] raised for emphasis. It was a [[stunt]] — a piece of street theatre designed to attract the attention of a film producer who was known to take the 6:40 train from the old [[depot]] every Thursday evening.","The idea was [[inherent|inherently]] silly, which is precisely why Leo loved it. He would be \'abducted\' on the platform, bundled into a van, and released half an hour later, and the whole thing would be filmed. There would be no [[ransom]], no threats, nobody hurt. His friend Mira was [[tolerant]] of the scheme for about a week and then stopped answering her phone.","On the day itself, several things went wrong. The van\'s engine was [[constrain|constrained]] by a faulty fuel pump and would not start. The hired actor — a man who claimed to have played a [[vampire]] in three films — arrived forty minutes late, [[yawn|yawning]] and entirely unapologetic, and spent the first ten minutes trying to [[emulate]] a gangster he had seen on television. The platform was cold, and a [[moist]] wind came up off the river.","It was the [[janitor]] who finally called the police, because he saw a man being [[restrain|restrained]] beside the ticket office and did not find it amusing. By then Leo was sitting on the ground with a dry mouth and no [[saliva]] to spare, having had to [[spit]] dust from his lips before he could speak at all, and having been [[prod|prodded]] in the ribs by an actor who had taken the role far too seriously. \'He was trying to [[intimidate]] me,\' Leo said afterwards, which was true, and which he had not expected at all.","The officers were patient and completely unimpressed. \'[[nope|Nope]],\' said the younger one, when Leo began to explain. \'That is not how any of this works.\' Leo had to [[sprint]] the length of the platform to catch the producer, who had watched the entire performance from a carriage window and was now getting back onto the train. He did not get the part. What he got instead was a formal warning, a small fine, and a lesson he has repeated ever since: that the courage to attempt something foolish is not the same as the sense to know when to stop. The doctor later confirmed that his [[guts]] were undamaged, though his pride was another matter."],"exercises":[{"id":"b6u28-e1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What is the passage mainly about?","options":["A genuine kidnapping that was solved by police","A staged kidnapping that went badly wrong","An actor\'s successful audition for a film","The daily routine of a railway janitor"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Leo\'s plan is explicitly not a crime but a stunt, and the passage follows the comic failure of that staged event."},{"id":"b6u28-e2","type":"multiple-choice","question":"Why did the van fail to start?","options":["The battery had been stolen","It had a faulty fuel pump","The keys had been left at the depot","It had been blocked in by another vehicle"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 3 says the van\'s engine was constrained by a faulty fuel pump and would not start."},{"id":"b6u28-e3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"Who alerted the police?","options":["The film producer","Mira, by telephone","The janitor at the station","The hired actor"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"Paragraph 4 states that it was the janitor who finally called the police after seeing a man being restrained."},{"id":"b6u28-e4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"As used in paragraph 3, the verb \\"emulate\\" most nearly means…","options":["criticise strongly","copy in order to be like","argue with","compete against"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"The actor was trying to emulate a gangster he had seen on television — that is, to imitate him because he admired the performance."},{"id":"b6u28-e5","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What lesson does Leo take from the day?","options":["That film producers cannot be trusted","That actors are always unreliable","That daring is not the same as good judgement","That police officers have no sense of humour"],"answerIndex":2,"explanation":"The final paragraph states his lesson directly: the courage to attempt something foolish is not the same as the sense to know when to stop."},{"id":"b6u28-e6","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The family refused to pay the ___ that the thieves demanded.\\"","options":["ransom","stunt","depot","saliva"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"A ransom is a sum of money paid to a kidnapper to set a person free, which is what the thieves were demanding."},{"id":"b6u28-e7","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The ___ repaired the broken lock before the children arrived.\\"","options":["vampire","janitor","forefinger","guerilla"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"A janitor is a person who makes repairs and takes care of a building, so he would be the one to fix a lock."},{"id":"b6u28-e8","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"She tried to hide a ___ behind her hand during the lecture.\\"","options":["yawn","prod","sprint","spit"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"A yawn is the act of opening the mouth wide and breathing in, typically from tiredness — hence hiding it behind a hand."},{"id":"b6u28-e9","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"They hoped the size of the crowd would ___ him into staying silent.\\"","options":["emulate","intimidate","restrain","yawn"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"To intimidate means to frighten others, which is what the crowd\'s size was meant to do."},{"id":"b6u28-e10","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"A tight budget will ___ what we can achieve this year.\\"","options":["constrain","emulate","sprint","prod"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To constrain something is to limit its development, which is exactly what a tight budget does."}]},"29":{"id":"b6-u29","book":6,"unit":29,"title":"The Bridge at Hallow Field","level":"C1","topic":"An old story about a village, a landowner and a crossing","generatedAt":"2026-09-23","wordCount":432,"paragraphs":["There is an old story in the valley about a landowner, a stream, and a [[petition]] written on the back of a laundry list. It is not a story with a villain in it, which is partly why people still bother to tell it.","The landowner lived in a [[mansion]] on the hill, with a long stone [[terrace]] running the length of its southern wall and a view over two hundred [[hectare|hectares]] of good [[pasture]]. He was not a cruel man, but he was a careless one, and careless men cause almost as much damage as cruel ones. The road from the village to the market crossed his land, and every spring the stream below it rose and carried the crossing away. In winter the road was mud, in summer it was dust, and in spring it was simply gone.","So the farmers [[assemble|assembled]] in the church one autumn, twenty-three of them, and signed a request for a stone bridge. They [[allege|alleged]] nothing and demanded nothing; they simply set out the arithmetic — three carts lost in five years, one broken leg, one serious [[casualty]] among the horses. Then they walked up the hill in their good coats and handed the paper to the man at his own front door. The laundry list was long enough, and the back of it was all they had.","He read it, and for a long moment said nothing at all. The problem was not money but [[foul]] pride: he did not enjoy being asked for anything. Yet his housekeeper, who had been with the family for forty years, reminded him of the [[hospitality]] his mother had always shown the village, and something in him shifted. He sent word down the hill the following morning.","The bridge took two summers. The farmers dug the footings and [[erect|erected]] the wooden frame; a mason from the coast cut the [[slab|slabs]] and set the [[arch]]. The landowner paid for the stone and asked only that the crossing be kept clear of [[turf]] and rubbish. When it was finished he came down to stand on it, and the villagers — who [[outnumber|outnumbered]] him by a very good deal — were [[overjoyed]] to see him look, for once, entirely uncertain of himself.","In later years the [[adjoining]] field was [[renovate|renovated]] and planted, and the road was [[revise|revised]] on the parish map. The bridge is still there. It [[heighten|heightens]] nothing and commemorates nobody; it is simply a bridge, and that is exactly what the farmers asked for. Travellers cross it without noticing, which is the highest compliment a bridge can be paid."],"exercises":[{"id":"b6u29-e1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What is the passage mainly about?","options":["A legal dispute over the ownership of a field","How a village obtained a bridge from a careless landowner","The construction of a mansion on a hill","A feud between two neighbouring families"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"The passage follows the villagers\' petition, the landowner\'s change of mind, and the building of the bridge over two summers."},{"id":"b6u29-e2","type":"multiple-choice","question":"How many farmers signed the request?","options":["Eleven","Twenty-three","Forty","Two hundred"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 3 says twenty-three of them assembled in the church and signed the request."},{"id":"b6u29-e3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What did the landowner ask in return for paying for the stone?","options":["That the farmers work his pasture each spring","That the crossing be kept clear of turf and rubbish","That his name be carved on the bridge","That the road be moved off his land"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 5 states that he asked only that the crossing be kept clear of turf and rubbish."},{"id":"b6u29-e4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"As used in paragraph 4, the word \\"foul\\" most nearly means…","options":["dirty and smelly","unpleasant and mean-spirited","against the rules","noisy and disorderly"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"The phrase is \\"foul pride\\" — the writer is describing an unattractive, mean-spirited quality rather than a physical smell."},{"id":"b6u29-e5","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What does the final sentence suggest about the bridge?","options":["It has become an important monument","Its value lies in being purely practical","It was badly built and will not last","It is admired mainly by visitors"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"The writer says it heightens nothing and commemorates nobody — its worth is simply that it does the job the farmers asked for."},{"id":"b6u29-e6","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"More than a thousand residents signed the ___ against the new road.\\"","options":["petition","arch","slab","hectare"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"A petition is a written request asking an authority to do something, which is what the residents were signing."},{"id":"b6u29-e7","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The crew were asked to ___ in the car park at dawn.\\"","options":["assemble","erect","renovate","outnumber"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To assemble means to get together in one place, which is what the crew were asked to do in the car park."},{"id":"b6u29-e8","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"Fortunately, there were no ___ in the accident.\\"","options":["casualties","terraces","pastures","arches"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"A casualty is a person killed or injured in an accident, so no casualties means nobody was hurt."},{"id":"b6u29-e9","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"They plan to ___ the old schoolhouse into a small library.\\"","options":["renovate","erect","allege","heighten"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To renovate a building is to repair it and give it a new purpose, which fits turning a schoolhouse into a library."},{"id":"b6u29-e10","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"In the final minutes, the attacking players began to ___ the defenders.\\"","options":["outnumber","assemble","renovate","revise"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To outnumber a group is to have a greater number than it, which is what the attackers began to do."}]},"30":{"id":"b6-u30","book":6,"unit":30,"title":"The Long Way Round","level":"C1","topic":"A journey inland and a road that is not what it seems","generatedAt":"2026-09-23","wordCount":424,"paragraphs":["The map on the wall of the roadhouse [[depict|depicted]] a straight line between two towns and a dotted line that wandered north for sixty kilometres before rejoining it. The dotted line was labelled, in very small letters, [[shortcut]]. Everyone at the bar laughed when I asked about it, which should probably have told me something. I ordered a cold drink and studied the map for a while, the way you study a person you do not entirely trust.","I had come inland with a [[dual]] purpose: to photograph the [[outback]] for a magazine, and to establish whether the old mining road was still passable. The [[bulk]] of the region is flat, red and almost empty of people. It [[comprise|comprises]] a handful of cattle stations, a [[grove]] of dead trees around a dry well, and one [[pier]] on a river that has not reached the sea in forty years.","The heat was the first obstacle. My [[thermometer]] read a hundred and nine [[fahrenheit|Fahrenheit]], which is forty-three degrees in the scale I grew up with, and I had drunk most of my water before eight in the morning. I scanned the horizon with a pair of [[binoculars]] and saw nothing at all — no cattle, no buildings, no movement, only saltbush and shimmering air. The [[tariff]] at the roadhouse had included unlimited water, and I began to understand why.","There is a [[paradox]] at the heart of journeys like this one. The [[shortcut]] is never shorter. The dotted road was sandy, unmarked and crossed two dry creek beds; although it saved sixty kilometres on paper, the time it cost [[outweigh|outweighed]] the distance. The sealed road was longer, faster and utterly without incident, and my decision to take it was [[analogous]] to every other sensible decision I have ever failed to make on time.","Distance on a map does not correspond to time on a road, or [[vice-versa|vice versa]]. I took the long way round and [[fulfill|fulfilled]] both halves of my purpose by the following evening. At the roadhouse the barman asked whether I had discovered the [[whereabouts]] of the old mine, where they once hauled [[ore]] out by the truckload. The mine itself has been closed for fifty years. I said I had not, and he nodded as though this were the correct answer. Then he [[tilt|tilted]] his head towards the window, where the light was turning orange over the plain, and said the thing I have thought about ever since. \'Nobody takes the shortcut twice,\' he said. \'That is why the road is still there.\'"],"exercises":[{"id":"b6u30-e1","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What is the passage mainly about?","options":["A traveller\'s decision to take the main road rather than a shortcut","The history of a mining town in the outback","A dispute over the price of water at a roadhouse","How to read a topographic map correctly"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"The passage recounts an inland journey and the traveller\'s choice of the longer sealed road over the dotted shortcut."},{"id":"b6u30-e2","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What did the thermometer read?","options":["Forty-three Fahrenheit","A hundred and nine Fahrenheit","Sixty degrees Fahrenheit","A hundred and nine Celsius"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 3 says the thermometer read a hundred and nine Fahrenheit, which the writer converts to forty-three degrees in the scale he grew up with."},{"id":"b6u30-e3","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What does the region comprise, according to the writer?","options":["Several large cities and a harbour","Cattle stations, a grove of dead trees and one pier","A national park and a river delta","Mines, farms and a railway line"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"Paragraph 2 lists a handful of cattle stations, a grove of dead trees around a dry well, and one pier on a river that no longer reaches the sea."},{"id":"b6u30-e4","type":"multiple-choice","question":"As used in paragraph 4, the verb \\"outweigh\\" most nearly means…","options":["be greater than","be measured against","be lifted carefully","be paid for in advance"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"The time the shortcut cost outweighed the distance — the cost was greater than the saving."},{"id":"b6u30-e5","type":"multiple-choice","question":"What does the barman\'s remark imply about the shortcut?","options":["It is closed for repairs","It is a mistake that everyone makes once","It is used only by mining trucks","It is shorter than the map suggests"],"answerIndex":1,"explanation":"\\"Nobody takes the shortcut twice\\" implies that people try it once, learn their lesson, and then use the main road — which is why the road is still there."},{"id":"b6u30-e6","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The ___ in the greenhouse showed thirty degrees by noon.\\"","options":["thermometer","tariff","paradox","pier"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"A thermometer is a device that measures temperature, which is what is being read in the greenhouse."},{"id":"b6u30-e7","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The ___ of the work was finished well before the deadline.\\"","options":["bulk","grove","ore","whereabouts"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"The bulk of something is its size or main part, so \\"the bulk of the work\\" means most of it."},{"id":"b6u30-e8","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"We walked to the end of the ___ and watched the fishing boats come in.\\"","options":["pier","grove","shortcut","paradox"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"A pier is a structure extending into a body of water, which is where you would stand to watch boats."},{"id":"b6u30-e9","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"She worked for years to ___ her ambition of becoming a surgeon.\\"","options":["fulfill","tilt","depict","comprise"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To fulfill something is to achieve or complete it, which is what she did with her ambition."},{"id":"b6u30-e10","type":"vocab-in-context","question":"Choose the word that best completes the sentence: \\"The mural ___ the town as it looked a century ago.\\"","options":["depicts","outweighs","tilts","comprises"],"answerIndex":0,"explanation":"To depict something is to show or portray it, often through art, which is what the mural does."}]}}')}).flatMap(e=>{let[t,a]=e;return Object.entries(a).map(e=>{let[a,o]=e;return["".concat(t,"-").concat(a),o]})})),d=Object.keys(r).length}}]);